// A-level Mathematics — Model Answers
// Key format: "paperId::partCode".
window.MATHS_MODEL_ANSWERS = {
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::1": "dy/dx = −2/x³. Write y = x⁻². Differentiating: dy/dx = −2x⁻³ = −2/x³.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::2": "y = 5 × 5^x. A stretch in the y-direction with scale factor 5 multiplies every y-value by 5, so the transformed graph is y = 5 × (5^x) = 5 × 5^x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::3": "4. The sequence Uₙ = sin(nπ/2) takes the repeating values U₁ = sin(π/2) = 1, U₂ = sin π = 0, U₃ = sin(3π/2) = −1, U₄ = sin(2π) = 0, U₅ = 1, … The block of values repeats every 4 terms, so the period is 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::4": "Let y = e^(x−4). Taking natural logs: ln y = x − 4, so x = 4 + ln y. Swapping variables, f⁻¹(x) = 4 + ln x. The domain of f⁻¹ is the range of f. Since e^(x−4) > 0 for all x ∈ ℝ, the range of f is (0, ∞), so the domain of f⁻¹ is x > 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::5(a)": "x = 4 × 2^(−t) + 3 and y = 3 × 2^t − 5. Differentiate using d/dt(2^t) = (ln 2)2^t:\ndy/dt = 3(ln 2)2^t = (3 ln 2)2^t,\ndx/dt = 4 × (−ln 2)2^(−t) = (−4 ln 2)2^(−t).\nBy the chain rule, dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = (3 ln 2)2^t / ((−4 ln 2)2^(−t)) = −(3/4) × 2^t/2^(−t) = −(3/4) × 2^(2t), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::5(b)": "From x = 4 × 2^(−t) + 3: 2^(−t) = (x − 3)/4. From y = 3 × 2^t − 5: 2^t = (y + 5)/3.\nSince 2^t × 2^(−t) = 1: ((y + 5)/3)((x − 3)/4) = 1, so (y + 5)(x − 3) = 12.\nExpand: xy − 3y + 5x − 15 = 12 ⇒ xy + 5x − 3y = 27.\nThus a = 5, b = −3, c = 27, giving xy + 5x − 3y = 27.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::6(a)": "1/√(4 + x) = (4 + x)^(−½) = (4(1 + x/4))^(−½) = ½ (1 + x/4)^(−½).\nBinomial expansion of (1 + u)^(−½) = 1 + (−½)u + ((−½)(−3/2)/2)u² + … = 1 − ½u + (3/8)u² + … with u = x/4:\n= 1 − ½(x/4) + (3/8)(x/4)² + … = 1 − x/8 + (3/128)x² + …\nMultiply by ½: 1/√(4 + x) ≈ ½ − x/16 + 3x²/256.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::6(b)": "Replace x with −x³ in the part (a) expansion ½ − x/16 + 3x²/256:\n1/√(4 − x³) ≈ ½ − (−x³)/16 + 3(−x³)²/256 = ½ + x³/16 + 3x⁶/256.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::6(c)": "Using the part (b) expansion as the integrand:\n∫₀¹ 1/√(4 − x³) dx ≈ ∫₀¹ (½ + x³/16 + 3x⁶/256) dx = [x/2 + x⁴/64 + 3x⁷/1792]₀¹\n= ½ + 1/64 + 3/1792 = 0.5 + 0.015625 + 0.0016741… = 0.5172991 (to 7 decimal places).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::6(d)(i)": "In the expansion ½ + x³/16 + 3x⁶/256 + … every term is positive for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 (each successive binomial term remains positive). So adding more terms only increases the value of the partial sum used in the integral. Therefore using more terms gives a larger approximation, which means Edward's few-term value is an underestimate (and his more accurate value increases towards the true value).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::6(d)(ii)": "The binomial expansion of (1 + (−x³/4))^(−½) is only valid when |−x³/4| < 1, i.e. |x³| < 4, i.e. |x| < ³√4 ≈ 1.587. The integral ∫₋₂⁰ uses the lower limit x = −2, where |x| = 2 > ³√4. There the series does not converge, so the expansion does not represent the function and the approximation is invalid.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::7(a)": "Gradient of AB = (17 − 10)/(8 − 15) = 7/(−7) = −1.\nGradient of BC = (10 − (−7))/(15 − (−2)) = 17/17 = 1.\nProduct of gradients = (−1)(1) = −1, so AB ⊥ BC, hence angle ABC is a right angle.\n(Alternatively, AB² = 7² + 7² = 98, BC² = 17² + 17² = 578, AC² = 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676; since AB² + BC² = 98 + 578 = 676 = AC², by the converse of Pythagoras angle ABC = 90°.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::7(b)(i)": "The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is a right angle (angle in a semicircle). Since angle ABC = 90° and A, B, C all lie on the circle with the right angle at B, the chord AC must be a diameter of the circle.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::7(b)(ii)": "AC is a diameter, so the centre is the midpoint of AC: centre = ((8 + (−2))/2, (17 + (−7))/2) = (3, 5). The radius is ½ × AC = ½ × √676 = ½ × 26 = 13.\nDistance² from the centre (3, 5) to D(−8, −2): (3 − (−8))² + (5 − (−2))² = 11² + 7² = 121 + 49 = 170.\nSince 170 > 169 = 13², the distance √170 ≈ 13.04 is greater than the radius 13. Therefore D lies outside the circle.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::8(a)": "OA = OB = r (radii). C is the midpoint of OB, so OC = r/2.\nArea of triangle OAC = ½ × OA × OC × sin(angle AOC) = ½ × r × (r/2) × sin θ = (r²/4) sin θ.\nArea of sector OAB = ½ r²θ.\nGiven area OAC = ¼ × area of sector OAB:\n(r²/4) sin θ = ¼ × ½ r²θ = (1/8) r²θ.\nDivide both sides by r²/4: sin θ = ½ θ, i.e. 2 sin θ = θ. Hence θ = 2 sin θ, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::8(b)": "Let f(θ) = θ − 2 sin θ, so f(θ) = 0 at the required root. Then f′(θ) = 1 − 2 cos θ.\nNewton-Raphson: θ_(n+1) = θₙ − (θₙ − 2 sin θₙ)/(1 − 2 cos θₙ), with θ₁ = π.\nf(π) = π − 0 = π, f′(π) = 1 − 2(−1) = 3, so θ₂ = π − π/3 = 2π/3 = 2.094395…\nθ₃ = θ₂ − (θ₂ − 2 sin θ₂)/(1 − 2 cos θ₂). With θ₂ = 2.094395: 2 sin θ₂ = 1.732051, so numerator = 0.362344; 1 − 2 cos θ₂ = 1 − 2(−0.5) = 2.0; θ₃ = 2.094395 − 0.362344/2.0 = 2.094395 − 0.181172 = 1.913223…\nSo θ₃ = 1.91322 (to 5 decimal places).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::8(c)": "True value θ = 1.89549, approximation θ₃ = 1.91322.\nPercentage error = |θ₃ − θ|/θ × 100 = |1.91322 − 1.89549|/1.89549 × 100 = 0.01773/1.89549 × 100 = 0.935% (AWRT 0.94%).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::9(a)": "For an arithmetic sequence Sₙ = (n/2)(2a + (n − 1)d).\nS₆ = (6/2)(2a + 5d) = 3(2a + 5d) = 6a + 15d.\nS₃₆ = (36/2)(2a + 35d) = 18(2a + 35d) = 36a + 630d.\nGiven S₃₆ = (S₆)²: 36a + 630d = (6a + 15d)².\nExpand the right side: (6a + 15d)² = 36a² + 2(6a)(15d) + 225d² = 36a² + 180ad + 225d².\nSo 36a + 630d = 36a² + 180ad + 225d². Divide every term by 9:\n4a + 70d = 4a² + 20ad + 25d², as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::9(b)": "The sixth term is a + 5d = 25, so d = (25 − a)/5.\nSubstitute into the result from (a), 4a + 70d = 4a² + 20ad + 25d²:\n4a + 70((25 − a)/5) = 4a² + 20a((25 − a)/5) + 25((25 − a)/5)²\n4a + 14(25 − a) = 4a² + 4a(25 − a) + (25 − a)²\n4a + 350 − 14a = 4a² + 100a − 4a² + 625 − 50a + a²\n350 − 10a = a² + 50a + 625\n0 = a² + 60a + 275\n(a + 5)(a + 55) = 0\na = −5 or a = −55 (giving d = 6 or d = 16 respectively).\nThe smallest possible value of a is −55.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::10(a)": "The half-life is 5.7 hours, so when t = 5.7, m = ½ m₀: ½ = e^(−5.7k), giving k = ln 2 / 5.7 = 0.1216047…\nTwo strong cups at 8 am give m₀ = 2 × 200 = 400 mg. Midday is t = 4 hours later.\nm = 400 e^(−0.1216047 × 4) = 400 e^(−0.486419) = 400 × 0.614824 = 245.93 mg (≈ 246 mg; AWRT 250 mg to 2 significant figures).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::10(b)": "To keep the total below 480 mg after drinking another 200 mg cup, the caffeine already present must drop below 480 − 200 = 280 mg.\n400 e^(−0.1216047 t) < 280 ⇒ e^(−0.1216047 t) < 0.7 ⇒ −0.1216047 t < ln 0.7 = −0.356675 ⇒ t > 2.933 hours (2.93303…).\n2.933 hours = 2 hours 56 minutes. Adding to 8 am gives 10:56 am, so the earliest time she could drink another cup is 10:56 am.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::10(c)": "The model uses an average half-life of 5.7 hours, but different people eliminate caffeine at different rates (depending on body mass, metabolism, etc.), so the actual mass remaining will differ from the predicted value. (Equally, the amount of caffeine in a particular \"strong cup of coffee\" may not be exactly 200 mg.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::11(a)(i)": "Oil production is zero when V = 0: 10 + 100(T/30)³ − 50(T/30)⁴ = 0.\nWrite as 50(T/30)⁴ = 10 + 100(T/30)³, i.e. 50 T⁴/30⁴ = 10 + 100 T³/30³.\nSince 30⁴ = 810000 and 50/810000 = 1/16200, this is T⁴/16200 = 10 + T³/270.\nMultiply by 16200: T⁴ = 162000 + 60 T³. Divide by T (T ≠ 0): T³ = 162000/T + 60T².\nTake the cube root: T = ³√(60T² + 162000/T), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::11(a)(ii)": "Using T_(n+1) = ³√(60Tₙ² + 162000/Tₙ) with T₀ = 38:\nT₁ = ³√(60(38)² + 162000/38) = ³√(86640 + 4263.16) = ³√90903.16 = 44.963.\nT₂ = ³√(60(44.963)² + 162000/44.963) = ³√(121299.9 + 3603.0) = ³√124902.9 = 49.987.\nT₃ = ³√(60(49.987)² + 162000/49.987) = ³√(149922.0 + 3240.8) = ³√153162.8 = 53.504.\nSo T₁ = 44.963, T₂ = 49.987, T₃ = 53.504 (to 3 decimal places).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::11(a)(iii)": "Here t is the time in years since 1 January 1980, so t = 38 corresponds to the year 1980 + 38 = 2018, the current/present year. Starting the iteration from T₀ = 38 begins the search from the present year, which is sensible since we expect production to fall to zero some years after the present.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::11(b)": "Set the country's use equal to world production:\n4.5 × 1.063^t = 10 + 100(t/30)³ − 50(t/30)⁴.\nThe year 2029 corresponds to t = 2029 − 1980 = 49.\nAt t = 49: world production = 10 + 100(49/30)³ − 50(49/30)⁴ = 10 + 100(4.3565) − 50(7.1156) = 10 + 435.65 − 355.78 = 89.87 (≈ 89.89); country use = 4.5 × 1.063^49 = 4.5 × 19.96 = 89.81. These are essentially equal.\nAt t = 50: world production = 10 + 100(50/30)³ − 50(50/30)⁴ = 10 + 462.96 − 385.80 = 87.16; country use = 4.5 × 1.063^50 = 95.47.\nThe difference (world − country) changes sign between t = 49 (positive, ≈ +0.08) and t = 50 (negative, ≈ −8.3), so the two are equal for some t in (49, 50). Since t = 49 to 50 lies within the year 2029 (1980 + 49 = 2029), the country's use of oil and the world production of oil are equal during the year 2029.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::12(a)": "By the factor theorem, (2x + 1) is a factor of p(x) if p(−½) = 0.\np(−½) = 30(−½)³ − 7(−½)² − 7(−½) + 2 = 30(−1/8) − 7(1/4) + 7/2 + 2 = −15/4 − 7/4 + 14/4 + 8/4 = (−15 − 7 + 14 + 8)/4 = 0/4 = 0.\nSince p(−½) = 0, by the factor theorem (2x + 1) is a factor of p(x).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::12(b)": "Since (2x + 1) is a factor, divide: p(x) = 30x³ − 7x² − 7x + 2 = (2x + 1)(15x² − 11x + 2).\nFactorise the quadratic 15x² − 11x + 2: find factors of 15×2 = 30 summing to −11, namely −5 and −6: 15x² − 5x − 6x + 2 = 5x(3x − 1) − 2(3x − 1) = (5x − 2)(3x − 1).\nTherefore p(x) = (2x + 1)(5x − 2)(3x − 1).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::12(c)": "Starting from (30 sec²x + 2 cos x)/7 = sec x + 1. Since cos x = 1/sec x, 2 cos x = 2/sec x. Multiply both sides by 7 sec x:\n30 sec³x + 2 = 7 sec²x + 7 sec x ⇒ 30 sec³x − 7 sec²x − 7 sec x + 2 = 0.\nThis is exactly p(sec x) = 0, where p is from part (b). So (2 sec x + 1)(5 sec x − 2)(3 sec x − 1) = 0, giving sec x = −½, sec x = 2/5 or sec x = 1/3.\nBut for all real x, sec x = 1/cos x satisfies |sec x| ≥ 1 (its range is (−∞, −1] ∪ [1, ∞)). Each of −½, 2/5 and 1/3 lies strictly between −1 and 1, so none can equal sec x. Therefore the equation has no real solutions.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::13": "Place the circle x² + y² = 16 (radius 4) centred at the origin. Let the rectangle have a vertex (x, y) in the first quadrant, with x > 0, y > 0. By symmetry its width is 2x and its height is 2y, so its area is A = (2x)(2y) = 4xy.\nThe vertex lies on the circle, so y = √(16 − x²). Hence A = 4x√(16 − x²), for 0 < x < 4.\nDifferentiate: dA/dx = 4√(16 − x²) + 4x · (−x)/√(16 − x²) = 4√(16 − x²) − 4x²/√(16 − x²) = (4(16 − x²) − 4x²)/√(16 − x²) = (64 − 8x²)/√(16 − x²).\nSet dA/dx = 0: 64 − 8x² = 0 ⇒ x² = 8 ⇒ x = 2√2 (taking x > 0).\nTo confirm a maximum, check the sign of dA/dx: for x = 2 (< 2√2), 64 − 8(4) = 32 > 0 (A increasing); for x = 2.9 (> 2√2), 64 − 8(8.41) = −3.3 < 0 (A decreasing). So x = 2√2 gives a maximum.\nThen y = √(16 − 8) = √8 = 2√2, and A = 4(2√2)(2√2) = 4 × 8 = 32.\nThe maximum area of the rectangle is 32 square inches.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::14(a)": "In the construction P lies on the horizontal line through E and Q is the intersection of EF with the perpendicular RF. The angle OQR equals the angle FQE (vertically opposite), and angle ORQ = angle FEQ = 90°, so triangle FEQ is similar to the right-angled triangle giving angle QFE = A. Equivalently, since angle PEF = 90° and the slope of OE makes angle A, the angle EFP = A. Therefore in right-angled triangle PEF, PF/EF = cos(angle EFP) = cos A. Also, in right-angled triangle OEF, EF/OF = sin(angle EOF) = sin B. Multiplying, (PF/EF) × (EF/OF) = cos A × sin B = cos A sin B, which is exactly the second term in Line 5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::14(b)": "Line 4: = (DE/OE) × (OE/OF) + (PF/EF) × (EF/OF).\nHere DE/OE = sin A (in right-angled triangle ODE) and OE/OF = cos B (in right-angled triangle OEF), so the first product is sin A cos B. The second product, as shown in part (a), is cos A sin B.\nLine 5: = sin A cos B + cos A sin B.\nHence sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::14(c)": "The whole argument is built on the diagram, which is constructed from right-angled triangles ODE and OEF. For the angles DOE = A and EOF = B to be interior angles of these right-angled triangles, both A and B must be acute. So the geometric proof only establishes the identity when A and B are acute angles; it does not cover obtuse or reflex angles.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::14(d)": "Assume sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B holds for all A and B. Replace B with −B:\nsin(A + (−B)) = sin A cos(−B) + cos A sin(−B).\nUsing the even/odd identities cos(−B) = cos B and sin(−B) = −sin B:\nsin(A − B) = sin A cos B + cos A (−sin B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B.\nHence sin(A − B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::15(a)": "The curve is y = x³ − 48x.\nAt A, x = −4: y_A = (−4)³ − 48(−4) = −64 + 192 = 128.\nAt B, x = −4 + h: y_B = (−4 + h)³ − 48(−4 + h).\nExpand (−4 + h)³ = −64 + 48h − 12h² + h³, and −48(−4 + h) = 192 − 48h. So y_B = (−64 + 48h − 12h² + h³) + (192 − 48h) = 128 − 12h² + h³.\nGradient of AB = (y_B − y_A)/((−4 + h) − (−4)) = (128 − 12h² + h³ − 128)/h = (h³ − 12h²)/h = h² − 12h.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p1::15(b)": "As h → 0, point B moves towards point A, and the gradient of the chord AB approaches the gradient of the tangent to the curve at A. So the gradient of the curve at A is lim_(h→0)(h² − 12h). (We take the limit; we must not simply put h = 0 in the chord formula.)\nlim_(h→0)(h² − 12h) = 0² − 12(0) = 0.\nSince the gradient of the curve at A equals 0, A is a stationary point on the curve.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::1": "The statement x = 2 ⇒ x² = 4 is correct: if x equals 2 then squaring gives x² = 4. The converse x² = 4 ⇒ x = 2 is false because x² = 4 also allows x = −2, so neither x² = 4 ⇒ x = 2, x² = 4 ⇔ x = 2, nor x² = 4 ⇒ x = −2 (which excludes x = 2) is a correct two-way/forced statement. Answer: x = 2 ⇒ x² = 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::2": "In the binomial expansion of (1 + 2x)⁷, the term in x² is C(7,2)(2x)² = 21 × 4x² = 84x². So the coefficient of x² is 84.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::3": "The total shaded area is the sum of the magnitudes of the two regions.\nFor −2 ≤ x ≤ 0 the curve is below the x-axis: ∫₋₂⁰ x³ dx = [x⁴/4]₋₂⁰ = 0 − (16/4) = −4, so this area is |−4| = 4.\nFor 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 the curve is above the x-axis: ∫₀⁴ x³ dx = [x⁴/4]₀⁴ = 256/4 = 64.\nTotal shaded area = 4 + 64 = 68.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::4(a)": "C is the parabola y = x² − 6x + k, which opens upwards (positive x² coefficient). Since x² − 6x + k = 0 has two distinct positive roots, the curve crosses the x-axis at two points, both on the positive x side. The vertex lies below the x-axis (between the two roots, at x = 3), and the y-intercept is y = k, which is positive (above the origin). So the sketch is an upward parabola with its minimum below the x-axis, both x-intercepts positive, and a positive y-intercept.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::4(b)": "For two distinct real roots the discriminant must be positive:\nb² − 4ac > 0 ⇒ (−6)² − 4(1)(k) > 0 ⇒ 36 − 4k > 0 ⇒ k < 9.\nFor both roots to be positive, the product of the roots = k (= c/a) must be positive, equivalently the curve must cross the positive y-axis, so k > 0. (The sum of the roots = 6 > 0 is automatically satisfied.)\nCombining both conditions: 0 < k < 9.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::5": "A number is prime if it has no factors other than 1 and itself. To test whether 23 is prime it is enough to check for prime factors up to √23, since any composite number must have a factor ≤ its square root.\n√23 ≈ 4.8, so the only primes to test are 2 and 3.\n23 is odd, so 2 is not a factor. 2 + 3 = 5 is not a multiple of 3 (and 23 = 3 × 7 + 2), so 3 is not a factor.\nSince no prime less than or equal to √23 divides 23, it has no factors other than 1 and 23. Therefore 23 is a prime number.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::6": "Differentiate (x + y − 2)² = e^(y) − 1 implicitly with respect to x:\n2(x + y − 2)(1 + dy/dx) = e^(y) · dy/dx.\nAt a stationary point dy/dx = 0, so the equation becomes 2(x + y − 2)(1) = 0, giving x + y − 2 = 0.\nSubstitute x + y − 2 = 0 into the original equation: (0)² = e^(y) − 1, so e^(y) = 1 ⇒ y = 0.\nThen x + 0 − 2 = 0 ⇒ x = 2.\nThe stationary point is (2, 0).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::7": "Since dy/dx = (x − 1)eˣ, integrate to find f(x):\nf(x) = ∫(x − 1)eˣ dx.\nIntegrate by parts with u = x − 1 (u′ = 1) and dv = eˣ dx (v = eˣ):\n∫(x − 1)eˣ dx = (x − 1)eˣ − ∫eˣ dx = (x − 1)eˣ − eˣ + c = (x − 2)eˣ + c.\nThe range of f is {y ≥ e}, so the least value of y is e. The minimum occurs where dy/dx = 0: (x − 1)eˣ = 0 ⇒ x = 1 (since eˣ > 0). So the curve passes through (1, e).\nSubstitute: e = (1 − 2)e¹ + c = −e + c ⇒ c = 2e.\nTherefore f(x) = (x − 2)eˣ + 2e.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::8(a)": "Write √3 sin x − 3 cos x in the form R sin(x − α), with R > 0:\nR sin(x − α) = R sin x cos α − R cos x sin α.\nComparing coefficients: R cos α = √3 and R sin α = 3.\nR² = (√3)² + 3² = 3 + 9 = 12, so R = √12 = 2√3.\ntan α = 3/√3 = √3, and cos α, sin α are both positive, so α = π/3.\nHence y = √3 sin x − 3 cos x + 4 = 2√3 sin(x − π/3) + 4.\nStarting from y = sin x:\n1) a translation by the vector [π/3, 0] (π/3 in the positive x-direction), giving y = sin(x − π/3);\n2) a stretch parallel to the y-axis with scale factor 2√3, giving y = 2√3 sin(x − π/3);\n3) a translation by the vector [0, 4] (4 in the positive y-direction), giving y = 2√3 sin(x − π/3) + 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::8(b)(i)": "From part (a), √3 sin x − 3 cos x + 4 = 2√3 sin(x − π/3) + 4.\nSince −1 ≤ sin(x − π/3) ≤ 1, the denominator lies between 4 − 2√3 and 4 + 2√3, which are both positive.\n1/(2√3 sin(x − π/3) + 4) is least when the denominator is greatest, i.e. when sin(x − π/3) = 1, giving the denominator 2√3 + 4.\nLeast value = 1/(2√3 + 4).\nRationalise by multiplying top and bottom by (2√3 − 4):\n1/(2√3 + 4) = (2√3 − 4)/((2√3)² − 4²) = (2√3 − 4)/(12 − 16) = (2√3 − 4)/(−4) = (4 − 2√3)/4 = (2 − √3)/2.\nSo the least value is (2 − √3)/2, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::8(b)(ii)": "From part (a), √3 sin x − 3 cos x + 4 = 2√3 sin(x − π/3) + 4, and the denominator is always positive (at least 4 − 2√3 > 0).\nThe fraction is greatest when the denominator is least, i.e. when sin(x − π/3) = −1, giving the denominator 4 − 2√3.\nGreatest value = 1/(4 − 2√3). Rationalise by multiplying top and bottom by (4 + 2√3):\n1/(4 − 2√3) = (4 + 2√3)/(16 − 12) = (4 + 2√3)/4 = (2 + √3)/2.\nSo the greatest value is (2 + √3)/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::9(a)": "The rate of sales dx/dt is directly proportional to (8 − t)/x, so dx/dt = k(8 − t)/x for some constant k.\nAfter two hours t = 2, x = 336 and dx/dt = 72:\n72 = k(8 − 2)/336 = 6k/336.\nSolving: k = 72 × 336/6 = 4032.\nTherefore dx/dt = 4032(8 − t)/x, and multiplying both sides by x:\nx(dx/dt) = 4032(8 − t), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::9(b)": "From part (a), x(dx/dt) = 4032(8 − t). Separate variables:\n∫x dx = ∫4032(8 − t) dt.\nLHS = ½x². RHS = 4032(8t − t²/2) + c.\nSo ½x² = 4032(8t − ½t²) + c.\nWhen the stall opens, t = 0 and x = 0 (no sales yet), so 0 = 0 + c ⇒ c = 0.\nHence ½x² = 4032(8t − ½t²) = 4032(16t − t²)/2, giving x² = 4032(16t − t²) = 4032t(16 − t), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::9(c)(i)": "The trader closes when the rate of sales falls below £24 per hour, so consider dx/dt = 24.\nFrom x(dx/dt) = 4032(8 − t): 24x = 4032(8 − t) ⇒ x = 168(8 − t).\nSubstitute into x² = 4032t(16 − t): (168(8 − t))² = 4032t(16 − t).\n28224(8 − t)² = 4032t(16 − t). Divide by 4032: 7(8 − t)² = t(16 − t).\n7(64 − 16t + t²) = 16t − t² ⇒ 448 − 112t + 7t² = 16t − t² ⇒ 8t² − 128t + 448 = 0 ⇒ t² − 16t + 56 = 0.\nt = (16 ± √(256 − 224))/2 = (16 ± √32)/2 = 8 ± 2√2, so t = 5.171… or t = 10.828…\nThe earliest time is t ≈ 5.171 hours = 5 hours 10 minutes after opening.\nThe stall opens at 09.30, so the earliest closing time is 09.30 + 5 h 10 min = 14.40.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::9(c)(ii)": "At 09.30, t = 0 and the total sales x = 0 (no sales have yet been made). The model dx/dt = 4032(8 − t)/x then has a zero denominator, so the rate of sales is undefined. Because division by zero occurs when x = 0, the model cannot be applied at the moment the stall opens, so it is not valid at 09.30.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::10": "Convert the final speed: 1.28 cm s⁻¹ = 0.0128 m s⁻¹. The snail starts from rest (u = 0) and accelerates uniformly for t = 1.8 s.\na = (v − u)/t = (0.0128 − 0)/1.8 = 0.00711… ≈ 0.0071 m s⁻².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::11": "The rod is uniform and rests on the support at its midpoint C, so the rod's own weight acts at C and produces no moment about C.\nTaking moments about C (clockwise = anticlockwise):\n4g × 0.6 = 1.5g × x.\nDivide by g: 4 × 0.6 = 1.5x ⇒ 2.4 = 1.5x ⇒ x = 1.6 m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::12(a)": "Acceleration is the gradient of the velocity–time graph, so find the gradient of each straight section:\nt = 0 to 4: (−2 − 0)/4 = −0.5\nt = 4 to 6: (2 − (−2))/2 = 2\nt = 6 to 11: 0\nt = 11 to 12: (0 − 2)/1 = −2\nt = 12 to 13: (−4 − 0)/1 = −4\nt = 13 to 16: 0\nt = 16 to 20: (0 − (−4))/4 = 1\nThe steepest section is from t = 12 to t = 13, where the velocity falls by 4 m/s in 1 s, giving an acceleration of −4 m s⁻².\nMaximum magnitude of the acceleration = 4 m s⁻².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::12(b)": "The object is at its starting position when its displacement, the signed area between the v–t graph and the t-axis from t = 0, is zero.\nFinding t₁:\nt = 0 to 4: triangle below the axis, area ½ × 4 × 2 = 4, so displacement −4.\nt = 4 to 5: the velocity rises from −2 to 0, triangle below the axis, area ½ × 1 × 2 = 1, so displacement −5.\nt = 5 to 6: the velocity rises from 0 to 2, triangle above the axis, area ½ × 1 × 2 = 1, so displacement −4.\nFrom t = 6 the velocity is 2 m/s, so 4 more units of area are needed: 2 × (t₁ − 6) = 4 ⇒ t₁ = 8.\nSo t₁ = 8.\nFinding t₂:\nt = 8 to 11: rectangle above the axis, area 3 × 2 = 6, so displacement +6.\nt = 11 to 12: triangle above the axis, area ½ × 1 × 2 = 1, so displacement +7.\nt = 12 to 13: triangle below the axis, area ½ × 1 × 4 = 2, so displacement +5.\nFrom t = 13 the velocity is −4 m/s, so 5 units of area below the axis are needed: 4 × (t₂ − 13) = 5 ⇒ t₂ − 13 = 1.25 ⇒ t₂ = 14.25.\nSo t₁ = 8 and t₂ = 14.25.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::13(a)": "The maximum (limiting) friction available is F_max = μmg = 0.85 × 20 × 9.8 = 166.6 N.\nThe boy applies a horizontal force of 150 N. Since 150 < 166.6, the applied force is less than the maximum friction, so friction can balance it and the crate does not move. The crate remains stationary.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::13(b)": "Resolve vertically (no vertical motion): R + 150 sin 15° = 20g, so\nR = 20 × 9.8 − 150 sin 15° = 196 − 38.82… = 157.177 N.\nThe maximum friction is F_max = μR = 0.85 × 157.177 = 133.6 N.\nThe horizontal component of the pull is 150 cos 15° = 144.9 ≈ 145 N.\nSince 145 > 133.6, the horizontal driving force exceeds the maximum available friction, so the crate begins to move (it does not remain stationary).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::14(a)": "AB = OB − OA = [−1, 2, 7] − [3, 5, 1] = [−1 − 3, 2 − 5, 7 − 1] = [−4, −3, 6].",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::14(b)": "Find the opposite edge DC = OC − OD = [0, 7, 6] − [4, 10, 0] = [−4, −3, 6].\nSince AB = DC = [−4, −3, 6], the side AB is parallel to and equal in length to DC, which means ABCD is a parallelogram.\nNow compare the lengths of two adjacent sides. |AB| = √((−4)² + (−3)² + 6²) = √(16 + 9 + 36) = √61.\nAD = OD − OA = [4, 10, 0] − [3, 5, 1] = [1, 5, −1], so |AD| = √(1² + 5² + (−1)²) = √(1 + 25 + 1) = √27 = 3√3.\nSince |AB| = √61 ≈ 7.81 and |AD| = 3√3 ≈ 5.20 are not equal, the adjacent sides have different lengths, so ABCD is a parallelogram but not a rhombus.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::15(a)": "For minibus A, acceleration a = 0.138t². Integrate to get velocity (starts from rest, so c = 0):\nv = ∫0.138t² dt = 0.046t³.\nIntegrate again for displacement (s = 0 at t = 0, so k = 0):\ns = ∫0.046t³ dt = 0.0115t⁴.\nSet s = 100: 0.0115t⁴ = 100 ⇒ t⁴ = 8695.65… ⇒ t = (8695.65)^(1/4) = 9.657 s.\nThe time taken for A to travel 100 m is 9.657 s (4 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::15(b)": "For minibus B, acceleration a = 0.024t³. Integrate (from rest, c = 0):\nv = ∫0.024t³ dt = 0.006t⁴.\nIntegrate again (s = 0 at t = 0, k = 0):\ns = ∫0.006t⁴ dt = 0.0012t⁵.\nSet s = 100: 0.0012t⁵ = 100 ⇒ t⁵ = 83333.3… ⇒ t = (83333.3)^(1/5) = 9.642 s.\nComparing the times: B takes 9.642 s and A takes 9.657 s. Since 9.642 < 9.657, minibus B travels 100 m in the shorter time, so the company should buy minibus B.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::15(c)": "The models assume each minibus starts moving instantly at t = 0, but in reality there is a driver reaction time before the vehicle sets off. The two times (9.642 s for B and 9.657 s for A) are very close, so a difference in reaction times could change the outcome. If driver B has a longer reaction time than driver A, then in practice A might cover the 100 m faster than B, meaning the company's decision to buy B could be wrong.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::16(a)": "Consider the vertical motion to the highest point, where the vertical velocity is zero. Taking up as positive, with initial vertical speed u sin 35° and acceleration −g:\nv = u sin 35° − gt, and v = 0 at t = 1.5 s:\n0 = u sin 35° − 9.81 × 1.5.\nSo u sin 35° = 14.715, giving u = 14.715/sin 35° = 14.715/0.5736 = 25.7 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::16(b)": "The particle lands 10 m below its starting point, so the vertical displacement is s = −10 m (taking up as positive). Using s = ut + ½at² vertically with vertical launch speed u sin 35° = 25.7 sin 35° = 14.74 m s⁻¹ and a = −9.81:\n−10 = (25.7 sin 35°)t − ½ × 9.81 × t²\n−10 = 14.74t − 4.905t².\nRearranging: 4.905t² − 14.74t − 10 = 0. Solving (taking the positive root):\nt = [14.74 + √(14.74² + 4 × 4.905 × 10)]/(2 × 4.905) = [14.74 + √(217.3 + 196.2)]/9.81 = [14.74 + 20.33]/9.81 = 3.571.\nThe total time of flight is approximately 3.57 seconds.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::17(a)(i)": "Treat the buggy and roller-skater as one system, total mass 410 + 72 = 482 kg, with acceleration 0.2 m s⁻². The horizontal forces are the driving force 300 N forward and the resistance forces 140 N (buggy) and R (skater) backward.\nNewton's second law: 300 − 140 − R = 482 × 0.2.\n160 − R = 96.4 ⇒ R = 63.6 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::17(a)(ii)": "Apply Newton's second law to the roller-skater alone (mass 72 kg, acceleration 0.2 m s⁻²). The forces on her are the rope tension T forward and resistance R = 63.6 N backward:\nT − R = 72 × 0.2.\nT − 63.6 = 14.4 ⇒ T = 78 N.\nThe tension in the rope is 78 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::17(b)": "A necessary assumption is that the rope is light (has no mass), so the tension is the same throughout it. (Equivalently one could assume the rope is inextensible, so the buggy and skater have the same acceleration, or that the rope is horizontal.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::17(c)(i)": "After releasing the rope, the only horizontal force on the skater (mass 72 kg) is the resistance R = 63.6 N, which decelerates her.\nDeceleration: −63.6 = 72a ⇒ a = −0.883 m s⁻².\nShe starts this phase at 6 m s⁻¹ and decelerates to rest. Using v² = u² + 2as with v = 0, u = 6:\n0 = 6² − 2 × 0.883 × s ⇒ s = 36/(2 × 0.883) = 20.4 m.\nThe skater travels 20.4 m before stopping. Since 20.4 > 20 (the distance to the stationary buggy), she does not stop in time — she reaches and hits the buggy.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p2::17(c)(ii)": "When the roller-skater releases the rope, the tension that was pulling back on the buggy is removed. The buggy still has its driving force but now no rope tension opposing it, so the resultant forward force on the buggy increases. This gives the buggy a greater acceleration, which is the change in motion (a sudden increase in acceleration / speeding up) that the driver noticed.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::1": "The circle (x − 4)² + (y + 4)² = 9 has centre (4, −4) and radius² = 9, so r = 3. Area = πr² = π(3)² = 9π.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::2": "y = x⁵ + 4x³ + 7x + q, so dy/dx = 5x⁴ + 12x² + 7. At x = 0: dy/dx = 0 + 0 + 7 = 7. The gradient is 7.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::3": "L: 2x + 3y = 7 ⇒ y = −(2/3)x + 7/3, so its gradient is −2/3. A perpendicular line has gradient 3/2. Rearranging the options, 3x − 2y = 7 gives y = (3/2)x − 7/2, which has gradient 3/2. So 3x − 2y = 7 is perpendicular to L.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::4": "y = |2x + a| is the graph of y = 2x + a with the part below the x-axis reflected up, giving a V-shape. The vertex is where 2x + a = 0, i.e. x = −a/2, so the vertex is at (−a/2, 0) on the negative x-axis. The y-intercept is at x = 0: y = |a| = a, so the graph crosses the positive y-axis at (0, a). The V opens upwards with both arms (gradients −2 on the left, +2 on the right) and nothing below the x-axis; mark the x-intercept −a/2 and the y-intercept a.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::5": "For small x, use the small-angle approximations sin θ ≈ θ and tan θ ≈ θ (in radians). Here sin(x/2) ≈ x/2 and tan(x/3) ≈ x/3. Substituting:\ny ≈ 5 + 4(x/2) + 12(x/3) = 5 + 2x + 4x = 6x + 5.\nThis is of the form y = mx + c (with m = 6, c = 5), which is a straight line. Hence, for small values of x, the graph can be approximated by the straight line y = 6x + 5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::6(a)": "f(x) = x/√(2x − 2). The square root requires 2x − 2 ≥ 0, and since √(2x − 2) is in the denominator it cannot be zero, so we need 2x − 2 > 0, i.e. x > 1. The maximum possible domain is {x ∈ ℝ : x > 1}.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::6(b)": "Write f(x) = x/(2x − 2)^(1/2). Quotient rule with u = x (u′ = 1) and v = (2x − 2)^(1/2) (v′ = ½(2x − 2)^(−1/2)·2 = (2x − 2)^(−1/2)):\nf′(x) = [v·u′ − u·v′]/v² = [(2x − 2)^(1/2)·1 − x·(2x − 2)^(−1/2)] / (2x − 2).\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (2x − 2)^(1/2):\nf′(x) = [(2x − 2) − x] / (2x − 2)^(3/2) = (x − 2)/(2x − 2)^(3/2), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::6(c)": "A point of inflection occurs where f″(x) = 0 and f″ changes sign. With f′(x) = (x − 2)(2x − 2)^(−3/2), differentiate using the product rule:\nf″(x) = (2x − 2)^(−3/2) + (x − 2)·(−3/2)(2x − 2)^(−5/2)·2 = (2x − 2)^(−3/2) − 3(x − 2)(2x − 2)^(−5/2).\nFactor out (2x − 2)^(−5/2):\nf″(x) = (2x − 2)^(−5/2)[(2x − 2) − 3(x − 2)] = (2x − 2)^(−5/2)(−x + 4).\nSetting f″(x) = 0: since (2x − 2)^(−5/2) ≠ 0 for x > 1, we need −x + 4 = 0, so x = 4. (x = 1 is the domain endpoint and is excluded.)\nFor 1 < x < 4, −x + 4 > 0 so f″ > 0; for x > 4, −x + 4 < 0 so f″ < 0. The sign changes, so x = 4 is a point of inflection, and it is the only one. Hence the graph has exactly one point of inflection (at x = 4).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::6(d)": "The graph is convex where f″(x) > 0. From part (c), f″(x) = (2x − 2)^(−5/2)(4 − x), which is positive when 4 − x > 0 and x > 1, i.e. for 1 < x < 4. So the graph is convex for 1 < x < 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::7(a)": "logₐ y = 2 logₐ 7 + logₐ 4 + ½.\nUse n logₐ x = logₐ xⁿ: 2 logₐ 7 = logₐ 49.\nWrite ½ = ½ logₐ a = logₐ a^(1/2) = logₐ √a.\nSo logₐ y = logₐ 49 + logₐ 4 + logₐ √a = logₐ(49 × 4 × √a) = logₐ(196√a).\nTherefore y = 196√a.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::7(b)": "The error is at the final line. The original equation 2 logₐ x = logₐ 9 − logₐ 4 requires x > 0 for logₐ x to be defined. When the student writes x² = 9/4 and takes square roots to get x = 3/2 or x = −3/2, the value x = −3/2 must be rejected because logₐ(−3/2) cannot be evaluated (the logarithm of a negative number is undefined). So −3/2 is not a valid solution; only x = 3/2 is.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::8(a)": "Start with the LHS: (sin 2x)/(1 + tan²x). Using the identity 1 + tan²x = sec²x:\nLHS = sin 2x / sec²x = sin 2x · cos²x.\nUsing sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x:\nLHS = 2 sin x cos x · cos²x = 2 sin x cos³x = RHS.\nHence (sin 2x)/(1 + tan²x) ≡ 2 sin x cos³x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::8(b)": "Using part (a) with x replaced by 2θ: (sin 4θ)/(1 + tan²2θ) = 2 sin 2θ cos³2θ. Therefore\n∫ (4 sin 4θ)/(1 + tan²2θ) dθ = ∫ 4·(2 sin 2θ cos³2θ) dθ = ∫ 8 sin 2θ cos³2θ dθ.\nLet u = cos 2θ, so du/dθ = −2 sin 2θ, giving sin 2θ dθ = −½ du.\n∫ 8 sin 2θ cos³2θ dθ = ∫ 8 u³ (−½) du = −4 ∫ u³ du = −u⁴ + c = −cos⁴2θ + c.\nSo the integral is −cos⁴2θ + c.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::9(a)": "The area of each tile is half the area of the previous one. If the largest tile has side w, its area is w². The second tile has area w²/2, so its side is √(w²/2) = w/√2 = (√2 w)/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::9(b)": "Each tile’s side is 1/√2 times the previous one, so the side lengths form a geometric sequence with first term a = w and common ratio r = 1/√2. The total length is the sum of all side lengths. Since |r| = 1/√2 ≈ 0.707 < 1, the series converges and the sum to infinity is:\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = w/(1 − 1/√2).\nNumerically, 1 − 1/√2 ≈ 1 − 0.7071 = 0.2929, so S∞ ≈ w/0.2929 ≈ 3.41w.\nSince 3.41w < 3.5w, the total length of any finite number of tiles (which is less than S∞) is always less than 3.5w, no matter how many tiles are in the pattern.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::9(c)": "To refine the model, add an extra 3 mm (0.3 cm) of length for each tile to account for the gap between adjacent tiles. Since the geometric series can contain unlimited tiles, the sum of these constant 3 mm gaps grows without bound (n × 0.3 cm has no upper limit as n increases). So the total length will no longer have an upper limit — it may now exceed 3.5w.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::10": "Proof by contradiction. Assume, for contradiction, that ³√2 is rational. Then it can be written as ³√2 = a/b, where a and b are integers with no common factors (the fraction is in its lowest terms).\nCubing both sides: 2 = a³/b³, so a³ = 2b³. Thus a³ is even, which means a is even (if a were odd, a³ would be odd). Write a = 2k for some integer k.\nThen (2k)³ = 2b³ ⇒ 8k³ = 2b³ ⇒ b³ = 4k³, so b³ is even, which means b is even.\nBut now both a and b are even, so they share the common factor 2 — contradicting the assumption that a/b is in its lowest terms.\nThis contradiction shows the assumption is false, so ³√2 cannot be written as a ratio of integers. Therefore ³√2 is irrational.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::11": "The probabilities must sum to 1: k + 2k + 4k + 2k + k = 10k = 1, so k = 1/10.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::12": "Frequencies = frequency density × class width: [155,160): 0.8 × 5 = 4; [160,170): 2.4 × 10 = 24; [170,180): 2.0 × 10 = 20; [180,190): 1.0 × 10 = 10. Total = 58, so the median is the 29.5th value. Cumulative frequencies: 4 (up to 160), 28 (up to 170), 48 (up to 180). The 29.5th value falls between 28 and 48, so it lies in the class interval [170, 180).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::13": "Sarah’s claim is incorrect. The figures in the row ‘Other takeaway food brought home’ appear as 0 because the recorded values have been rounded to the nearest whole number, but the actual underlying values are small non-zero numbers (this is known from the Large Data Set, where the figures are given to several decimal places). If the unrounded values were used, the percentage change from 2011 to 2014 would indeed work out to about −29%. So there is a real change, and the −29% figure is correct — Sarah’s claim is incorrect.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::14(a)": "Let P = ‘studies physics’ and G = ‘studies geography’. From the 24 students: P(P) = 12/24 = 1/2, P(G) = 8/24 = 1/3, and P(P ∩ G) = 4/24 = 1/6.\nFor independence we need P(P ∩ G) = P(P) × P(G). Here P(P) × P(G) = (1/2)(1/3) = 1/6, which equals P(P ∩ G) = 1/6.\nSince P(P ∩ G) = P(P) × P(G), the two events are independent.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::14(b)": "Let M = studies mathematics, B = studies biology. Given P(M) = 1/5, P(B) = 1/6, and P(B | M) = 3/8.\nFirst find P(M ∩ B) = P(B | M) × P(M) = (3/8)(1/5) = 3/40.\nThen, by the addition rule:\nP(M ∪ B) = P(M) + P(B) − P(M ∩ B) = 1/5 + 1/6 − 3/40.\nUsing a common denominator of 120: 1/5 = 24/120, 1/6 = 20/120, 3/40 = 9/120.\nP(M ∪ B) = (24 + 20 − 9)/120 = 35/120 = 7/24.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::15(a)": "Let X be the number of faulty bulbs out of the 6 bought, each with probability 0.15 of being faulty. Then X ~ B(6, 0.15) (binomial with n = 6, p = 0.15).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::15(b)": "P(all six faulty) = P(X = 6) = 0.15⁶ = 0.000011390625 ≈ 0.0000114.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::15(c)": "P(X ≥ 2) = 1 − P(X ≤ 1) = 1 − [P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)].\nP(X = 0) = 0.85⁶ ≈ 0.37715; P(X = 1) = 6 × 0.15 × 0.85⁵ ≈ 0.39933; so P(X ≤ 1) ≈ 0.77648.\nTherefore P(X ≥ 2) = 1 − 0.7764 ≈ 0.224.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::15(d)": "For X ~ B(6, 0.15), the mean is np = 6 × 0.15 = 0.9.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::15(e)": "Two necessary assumptions for the binomial model B(6, 0.15) to be valid:\n1. The probability of any given bulb being faulty is fixed (constant at 0.15 for each bulb).\n2. Whether one bulb is faulty is independent of whether any other bulb is faulty.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(a)(i)": "Mean of X = Σx / n = 165.6 / 120 = 1.38.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(a)(ii)": "Variance = Σx²/n − (mean)² = 261.8/120 − 1.38² = 2.18167 − 1.9044 = 0.27727. Standard deviation = √0.27727 ≈ 0.527 (between 0.526 and 0.529).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(b)(i)": "Model X ~ N(1.38, 0.527²). Then P(0.5 < X < 1.5) = P(X < 1.5) − P(X < 0.5). Standardising with μ = 1.38, σ ≈ 0.527: z₁ = (1.5 − 1.38)/0.527 ≈ 0.228, z₂ = (0.5 − 1.38)/0.527 ≈ −1.670. P(0.5 < X < 1.5) ≈ Φ(0.228) − Φ(−1.670) ≈ 0.5902 − 0.0475 ≈ 0.542 (between 0.5417 and 0.5428).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(b)(ii)": "X is modelled by a continuous (normal) distribution, and the probability of a continuous random variable taking any single exact value is zero. Therefore P(X = 1) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(c)": "Check how far the model extends below 0: mean − 3 standard deviations ≈ 1.38 − 3(0.527) ≈ 1.38 − 1.58 ≈ −0.20 (between −0.1998 and −0.207). This is negative, but a volume of carbonated drink cannot be negative. Since the normal model assigns non-negligible probability to impossible negative values, a normal distribution is not entirely suitable to model this data.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::16(d)": "Y ~ N(μ, 0.21²) with P(Y > 0.75) = 0.10. Standardising: P(Z > (0.75 − μ)/0.21) = 0.10. The z-value with 10% in the upper tail is z = 1.2816. So:\n(0.75 − μ)/0.21 = 1.2816 ⇒ 0.75 − μ = 0.21 × 1.2816 = 0.2691 ⇒ μ = 0.75 − 0.2691 = 0.4809.\nTherefore μ ≈ 0.481 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::17(a)": "Let X = number of matches Suzanne wins out of 10. Under the null hypothesis X ~ B(10, 0.5). Since we are testing whether the racket has made a difference (either way), this is a two-tailed test.\nH₀: p = 0.5, H₁: p ≠ 0.5, at the 10% level (so 5% in each tail).\nObserved: 7 wins. P(X ≥ 7) = 1 − P(X ≤ 6) = 1 − 0.8281 = 0.1719 ≈ 0.172.\nCompare with 0.05 (the 5% in the upper tail): 0.172 > 0.05, so the result is not significant. There is insufficient evidence to reject H₀.\nConclusion: there is not enough evidence at the 10% level to say that Suzanne’s new tennis racket has made a difference to her probability of winning a match.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::17(b)": "Let Y = number of matches won out of 20. Under the null hypothesis Y ~ B(20, 0.5). To conclude the racket has improved her performance, this is a one-tailed (upper-tail) test at the 10% level, so we need the smallest y with P(Y ≥ y) < 0.10.\nP(Y ≥ 13) = 1 − P(Y ≤ 12) = 1 − 0.8684 = 0.1316 > 0.10 (not significant).\nP(Y ≥ 14) = 1 − P(Y ≤ 13) = 1 − 0.9423 = 0.0577 < 0.10 (significant).\nSo the minimum number of matches she must win is 14.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::18(a)(i)": "The first 750 available people were surveyed, so the sampling method is opportunistic (convenience) sampling.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::18(a)(ii)": "The sample is not random — taking the first 750 available people is an opportunistic sample, which is unlikely to be representative of the whole region. A hypothesis test requires a random sample for the inference to be valid, so this sample should not be used to conduct a hypothesis test.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2018-p3::18(b)": "Test whether the mean has decreased from 66.5 g. Let μ be the mean consumption after the campaign.\nH₀: μ = 66.5, H₁: μ < 66.5 (one-tailed, 10% level).\nThe sample mean of n = 750 is x̄ = 65.4, with population standard deviation σ = 21.2. The standardised test statistic is:\nz = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n) = (65.4 − 66.5)/(21.2/√750) = −1.1/0.77418 ≈ −1.42.\nThe critical value for a one-tailed test at the 10% level is z = −1.28. Since −1.42 < −1.28, the test statistic lies in the critical region, so we reject H₀.\nConclusion: there is sufficient evidence at the 10% level that the advertising campaign has decreased (reduced) the mean consumption of chocolate per person per week.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::1": "−4 log₁₀(√a). Using log laws: −4 log₁₀(√a) = −4 · ½ log₁₀(a) = −2 log₁₀(a). The other three expressions all simplify to +2 log₁₀(a): 2 log₁₀(a) directly; log₁₀(a²) = 2 log₁₀(a); −2 log₁₀(1/a) = −2(−log₁₀ a) = 2 log₁₀(a). So −4 log₁₀(√a) is the odd one out.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::2": "dy/dx = ke^(kx). Differentiating y = e^(kx) by the chain rule gives dy/dx = k·e^(kx).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::3": "6.4 cm². Area of a sector = ½r²θ = ½ × 4² × 0.8 = ½ × 16 × 0.8 = 6.4 cm².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::4": "Gradient of AB is −5/4, so the perpendicular bisector has gradient 4/5.\nFind the midpoint: A and B both lie on 5x + 4y = 17, and the midpoint has x = (−1 + 3)/2 = 1. Substituting x = 1 into 5x + 4y = 17 gives 5 + 4y = 17 ⇒ y = 3. Midpoint M = (1, 3).\nPerpendicular bisector: y − 3 = (4/5)(x − 1) ⇒ y = (4/5)x − 4/5 + 3 = (4/5)x + 11/5.\nSo the perpendicular bisector is y = (4/5)x + 11/5  (equivalently 5y − 4x = 11).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::5(a)": "Sum of first 16 terms: S₁₆ = (16/2)(2a + (16−1)d) = 8(2a + 15d).\nSetting this equal to 260: 8(2a + 15d) = 260 ⇒ 2(2a + 15d) = 65 ⇒ 4a + 30d = 65, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::5(b)": "Sum of first 60 terms: S₆₀ = (60/2)(2a + 59d) = 30(2a + 59d) = 315 ⇒ 2a + 59d = 10.5 ⇒ 20a + 590d = 105.\nFrom part (a): 4a + 30d = 65 ⇒ 20a + 150d = 325.\nSubtract: (20a + 590d) − (20a + 150d) = 105 − 325 ⇒ 440d = −220 ⇒ d = −0.5.\nThen 4a + 30(−0.5) = 65 ⇒ 4a − 15 = 65 ⇒ a = 20.\nS₄₁ = (41/2)(2×20 + 40×(−0.5)) = (41/2)(40 − 20) = (41/2)(20) = 410.\nThe sum of the first 41 terms is 410.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::5(c)": "With a = 20 and d = −0.5, the nth term is Uₙ = 20 + (n−1)(−0.5) = 20.5 − 0.5n. This is positive while 20.5 − 0.5n > 0, i.e. n < 41, and Uₙ = 0 is not reached exactly: U₄₁ = 20.5 − 20.5 = 0 and U₄₂ = −0.5 < 0. So terms up to the 41st are non-negative and every term from the 42nd onwards is negative. Adding these negative terms reduces the running total, so the sum is greatest at n = 41. Hence S₄₁ = 410 is the maximum value of Sₙ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::6(a)": "f(x) = ½(x² + 1) for x ≥ 0. At x = 0, f(0) = ½(0 + 1) = ½, and f is increasing for x ≥ 0, so f(x) takes all values from ½ upwards. Range: {y : y ≥ ½}, i.e. [½, ∞).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::6(b)(i)": "Let y = ½(x² + 1). Then 2y = x² + 1 ⇒ x² = 2y − 1 ⇒ x = √(2y − 1) (positive root, since the domain of f is x ≥ 0). Swapping variables: f⁻¹(x) = √(2x − 1), with domain x ≥ ½ (the range of f).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::6(b)(ii)": "The range of f⁻¹ equals the domain of f, which is x ≥ 0. So the range of f⁻¹ is {y : y ≥ 0}, i.e. [0, ∞).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::6(c)": "A reflection in the line y = x. The graph of an inverse function is the reflection of the original graph in y = x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::6(d)": "The graphs of f and f⁻¹ meet on the line y = x, so solve f(x) = x: ½(x² + 1) = x ⇒ x² + 1 = 2x ⇒ x² − 2x + 1 = 0 ⇒ (x − 1)² = 0 ⇒ x = 1. Then y = 1. Point of intersection: (1, 1).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::7(a)": "Sketch y = 1/x as a decreasing curve in the first quadrant, with the x- and y-axes as asymptotes (it stays positive and gets close to both axes). Sketch y = sec 2x = 1/cos 2x: it has a value 1 at x = 0 and increases to +∞ as x → π/4⁻ (vertical asymptote where cos 2x = 0). For small x > 0, y = 1/x is large and lies above sec 2x near x = 0 but both start near each other; on the first branch (0 < x < π/4) the two curves cross exactly once. After x = π/4, sec 2x takes large negative then values that do not meet the positive, decreasing 1/x curve again. Hence the graphs intersect at exactly one point for x > 0, so 1/x = sec 2x has exactly one solution for x > 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::7(b)": "Let f(x) = 1/x − sec 2x. Then\nf(0.4) = 1/0.4 − sec(0.8) = 2.5 − 1/cos(0.8) = 2.5 − 1.4353... = 1.06... > 0,\nf(0.6) = 1/0.6 − sec(1.2) = 1.6667 − 1/cos(1.2) = 1.6667 − 2.7597... = −1.09... < 0.\nSince f is continuous on [0.4, 0.6] and f(0.4) > 0 while f(0.6) < 0, there is a change of sign, so by the change-of-sign rule the solution of 1/x = sec 2x lies between 0.4 and 0.6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::7(c)": "Starting from 1/x = sec 2x = 1/cos 2x. Taking reciprocals (both sides non-zero): x = cos 2x. Therefore 2x = cos⁻¹ x, and dividing by 2 gives x = ½ cos⁻¹ x, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::7(d)(i)": "Using x_(n+1) = ½ cos⁻¹ xₙ with x₁ = 0.4 (radians):\nx₂ = ½ cos⁻¹(0.4) = ½ × 1.15928 = 0.5796 (4 dp),\nx₃ = ½ cos⁻¹(0.5796) = ½ × 0.95259 = 0.4763 (4 dp),\nx₄ = ½ cos⁻¹(0.4763) = ½ × 1.07449 = 0.5372 (4 dp).\nSo x₂ = 0.5796, x₃ = 0.4763, x₄ = 0.5372.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::7(d)(ii)": "On the given axes, draw the line y = x and the curve y = ½ cos⁻¹ x. Starting at x₁ = 0.4 on the x-axis: go vertically up to the curve (height x₂ = 0.5796), then horizontally across to the line y = x, then vertically to the curve (height x₃ = 0.4763), then horizontally to the line, then vertically to the curve (height x₄ = 0.5372). Because the curve has negative gradient at the intersection, this produces a cobweb (spiralling) diagram that converges towards the root near 0.515. Mark the positions of x₂, x₃ and x₄ on the x-axis where each horizontal step meets the line y = x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::8(a)": "P(n) = ∑(k=0 to n) k³ − ∑(k=0 to n−1) k³. The two sums are identical except the first includes the k = n term, so P(n) = n³.\nP(3) = 3³ = 27.\nP(10) = 10³ = 1000.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::8(b)": "Since P(n) = n³, solve n³ = 1.25 × 10⁸ = 125 000 000.\nn = ³√(125 000 000) = ³√(125 × 10⁶) = 5 × 100 = 500.\nSo n = 500.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::9": "Proof by contradiction. Suppose, for contradiction, that the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is rational.\nLet the rational number be a/b (a, b integers, b ≠ 0) and let x be irrational, and suppose a/b + x is rational, say a/b + x = c/d (c, d integers, d ≠ 0).\nThen x = c/d − a/b = (bc − ad)/(bd).\nSince a, b, c, d are integers, bc − ad and bd are integers (and bd ≠ 0), so x = (bc − ad)/(bd) is a ratio of two integers, i.e. x is rational.\nThis contradicts the assumption that x is irrational. Therefore the original supposition is false, and the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::10": "The volume increases at a constant rate, so dV/dt = k for some constant k.\nFor a sphere V = (4/3)πr³, so dV/dr = 4πr².\nBy the chain rule, dV/dt = (dV/dr)(dr/dt), hence k = 4πr² · dr/dt.\nRearranging: dr/dt = k/(4πr²) = (k/4π) · (1/r²).\nSince k/(4π) is constant, dr/dt ∝ 1/r²; that is, the rate of increase of the radius is inversely proportional to r².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::11": "Step 4: For the gradient of the curve at A, let h → 0 (not h = 0). Using the standard limits, as h → 0, (cos h − 1)/h → 0 and sin h / h → 1.\nStep 5: Hence the gradient of the curve at A is\nsin(π/2) × 0 + cos(π/2) × 1 = 1 × 0 + 0 × 1 = 0.\nThe correction is that sin h / h → 1 (not 0); Jodie wrongly set both limits to 0 and put h = 0 rather than taking the limit h → 0. The cos(π/2) = 0 term then makes the second product zero, giving gradient 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::12(a)": "Use the identity cot²x = cosec²x − 1. Substituting into 2cot²x + 2cosec²x = 1 + 4cosecx:\n2(cosec²x − 1) + 2cosec²x = 1 + 4cosecx\n2cosec²x − 2 + 2cosec²x = 1 + 4cosecx\n4cosec²x − 2 = 1 + 4cosecx\n4cosec²x − 4cosecx − 3 = 0.\nThis is of the form a cosec²x + b cosecx + c = 0 with a = 4, b = −4, c = −3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::12(b)": "Solve 4cosec²x − 4cosecx − 3 = 0. Factorising: (2cosecx − 3)(2cosecx + 1) = 0, so cosecx = 3/2 or cosecx = −½.\nReject cosecx = −½ because |cosecx| ≥ 1 for all x, so −½ is impossible.\nTake cosecx = 3/2 ⇒ sin x = 2/3. Then cot²x = cosec²x − 1 = (3/2)² − 1 = 9/4 − 1 = 5/4, so tan²x = 4/5.\nThus |tan x| = 2/√5 = 2√5/5. Since x is obtuse (in the second quadrant), tan x is negative.\nTherefore tan x = −2√5/5  (equivalently −2/√5).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::13": "y = e^(3x−5)/x² is undefined at x = 0, so x ≠ 0.\nDifferentiate by the quotient rule:\ndy/dx = [3e^(3x−5)·x² − e^(3x−5)·2x] / x⁴ = e^(3x−5)(3x² − 2x)/x⁴ = e^(3x−5)·x(3x − 2)/x⁴ = e^(3x−5)(3x − 2)/x³.\nStationary points occur where dy/dx = 0: e^(3x−5)(3x − 2)/x³ = 0 ⇒ e^(3x−5)(3x − 2) = 0 (for x ≠ 0).\nSince e^(3x−5) > 0 for all x (it is never zero), the only solution is 3x − 2 = 0 ⇒ x = 2/3.\nThis x = 2/3 is in the domain (x ≠ 0), and it is the only value making dy/dx zero. Therefore C has exactly one stationary point, at x = 2/3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::14(a)(i)": "With n = 4 trapezia (strips), the number of ordinates (y-values) needed is n + 1 = 5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::14(a)(ii)": "Strip width h = (4 − 0)/4 = 1. Evaluate y = 2x³/(x² + 1) at x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4:\nx=0: y=0; x=1: y=2/2=1; x=2: y=16/5=3.2; x=3: y=54/10=5.4; x=4: y=128/17=7.52941...\nTrapezium rule: Area ≈ (h/2)[y₀ + y₄ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)] = (1/2)[0 + 7.52941 + 2(1 + 3.2 + 5.4)] = (1/2)[7.52941 + 2(9.6)] = (1/2)[7.52941 + 19.2] = (1/2)(26.72941) = 13.3647...\nArea ≈ 13.36 (to 2 dp).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::14(b)": "Exact area A = ∫₀⁴ 2x³/(x² + 1) dx. Substitute u = x² + 1, so du = 2x dx and x² = u − 1. When x = 0, u = 1; when x = 4, u = 17.\nThen 2x³/(x²+1) dx = x²·(2x dx)/(x²+1) = (u − 1)/u du.\nSo A = ∫₁¹⁷ (u − 1)/u du = ∫₁¹⁷ (1 − 1/u) du = [u − ln u]₁¹⁷ = (17 − ln 17) − (1 − ln 1) = (17 − ln 17) − 1 = 16 − ln 17.\nHence the exact area of A is 16 − ln 17.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::14(c)": "As n → ∞ the trapezia fit the curve more and more closely, so Caroline's estimate gets closer to the exact area under the curve.\nHer answer tends to the exact value of ∫₀⁴ 2x³/(x² + 1) dx = 16 − ln 17 (≈ 13.17).\n(With n = 4 her estimate of 13.36 is larger than this, so as n increases the estimate decreases towards 16 − ln 17.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::15(a)(i)": "A high tide occurs at t = 0. Height h = 3 − 2·∛(t − 3) at t = 0:\nh = 3 − 2·∛(0 − 3) = 3 − 2·∛(−3) = 3 − 2(−1.44225) = 3 + 2.8845 = 5.88 metres (AWRT 5.9 m).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::15(a)(ii)": "Low tides occur when v = 0: 4 − (2t/3 − 2)² = 0 ⇒ (2t/3 − 2)² = 4 ⇒ 2t/3 − 2 = ±2.\nSo 2t/3 = 4 ⇒ t = 6, or 2t/3 = 0 ⇒ t = 0 (the high tide at 2 am).\nThe first low tide after 2 am is at t = 6 hours, i.e. at 2 am + 6 h = 8 am.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::15(a)(iii)": "At the low tide t = 6: h = 3 − 2·∛(6 − 3) = 3 − 2·∛3 = 3 − 2(1.44225) = 3 − 2.8845 = 0.12 metres (AWRT 0.115–0.116 m).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::15(b)": "v = 4 − (2t/3 − 2)² is maximised when the squared term is zero, i.e. when 2t/3 − 2 = 0 ⇒ t = 3 (giving maximum velocity v = 4 knots).\nHeight at t = 3: h = 3 − 2·∛(3 − 3) = 3 − 2·∛0 = 3 − 0 = 3 metres.\nSo the height when the tide flows with maximum velocity is 3 metres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::15(c)": "The model is only valid over a limited time (about one tidal cycle). For t > 6 the height h = 3 − 2·∛(t − 3) keeps decreasing without bound, whereas a real tide rises again after low tide; also v = 0 only at t = 0 and t = 6 within this interval, so the model does not reproduce the repeating pattern of further high and low tides. Hence the model breaks down after the first cycle and should only be used for roughly the first 6 hours.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::16(a)": "y = e^(−x)(sin x + cos x). By the product rule:\ndy/dx = (−e^(−x))(sin x + cos x) + e^(−x)(cos x − sin x)\n= e^(−x)[−(sin x + cos x) + (cos x − sin x)]\n= e^(−x)[−sin x − cos x + cos x − sin x]\n= e^(−x)(−2 sin x) = −2e^(−x) sin x.\nSo dy/dx = −2e^(−x) sin x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::16(b)": "From part (a), d/dx [e^(−x)(sin x + cos x)] = −2e^(−x) sin x. Integrating both sides:\n∫ −2e^(−x) sin x dx = e^(−x)(sin x + cos x) + k.\nDividing by −2:\n∫ e^(−x) sin x dx = −½ e^(−x)(sin x + cos x) + c.\nThis is of the form a·e^(−x)(sin x + cos x) + c with a = −½ (a rational number).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::16(c)(i)": "A₁ is the area for the first region, where e^(−x) sin x ≥ 0, i.e. 0 ≤ x ≤ π:\nA₁ = ∫₀^π e^(−x) sin x dx = [−½ e^(−x)(sin x + cos x)]₀^π.\nAt x = π: −½ e^(−π)(sin π + cos π) = −½ e^(−π)(0 − 1) = ½ e^(−π).\nAt x = 0: −½ e^0(sin 0 + cos 0) = −½(0 + 1) = −½.\nA₁ = ½ e^(−π) − (−½) = ½ e^(−π) + ½ = (1 + e^(−π))/2.\nSo A₁ = (1 + e^(−π))/2  (equivalently (e^π + 1)/(2e^π)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::16(c)(ii)": "A₂ is the area of the second region, π ≤ x ≤ 2π, where e^(−x) sin x ≤ 0, so A₂ = −∫_π^(2π) e^(−x) sin x dx.\nUsing the antiderivative: −[−½ e^(−x)(sin x + cos x)]_π^(2π) = ½[e^(−x)(sin x + cos x)]_π^(2π).\nAt x = 2π: e^(−2π)(0 + 1) = e^(−2π). At x = π: e^(−π)(0 − 1) = −e^(−π).\nA₂ = ½[e^(−2π) − (−e^(−π))] = ½(e^(−2π) + e^(−π)) = ½ e^(−π)(e^(−π) + 1).\nTherefore A₂/A₁ = [½ e^(−π)(1 + e^(−π))] / [½(1 + e^(−π))] = e^(−π), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p1::16(c)(iii)": "Since A_(n+1)/Aₙ = e^(−π) for all n, the areas A₁, A₂, A₃, ... form a geometric series with first term A₁ = (1 + e^(−π))/2 and common ratio r = e^(−π). As |r| = e^(−π) < 1, the total area is the sum to infinity:\nTotal = A₁/(1 − r) = [(1 + e^(−π))/2] / (1 − e^(−π)).\nMultiply numerator and denominator by e^π:\n= (e^π(1 + e^(−π))/2) / (e^π(1 − e^(−π))) = ((e^π + 1)/2) / (e^π − 1) = (1 + e^π) / (2(e^π − 1)).\nHence the total area enclosed between the curve and the x-axis is (1 + e^π)/(2(e^π − 1)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::1": "y = 1 − |x + 2| is y = −|x + 2| + 1: take the basic modulus graph y = |x|, shift 2 left (vertex at x = −2), reflect in the x-axis (so it opens downwards), then shift up 1. The result is an inverted-V (Λ) with maximum at (−2, 1), cutting the x-axis at x = −3 and x = −1. This is graph D.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::2": "a^(2/3) × a^(2/5) = a^(2/3 + 2/5) = a^(10/15 + 6/15) = a^(16/15). Taking the square root: (a^(16/15))^(1/2) = a^(8/15).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::3": "A function has an inverse only if it is one-to-one over its domain. On x ∈ ℝ: x³, 2x + 1 and eˣ are all one-to-one (strictly monotonic). But f(x) = x² is many-to-one (e.g. f(2) = f(−2) = 4), so it has no inverse. Answer: f(x) = x².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::4": "Since (x + 2) is a common factor, by the factor theorem x = −2 is a root of both quadratics.\nFor x² + bx + c: (−2)² + b(−2) + c = 0 ⇒ 4 − 2b + c = 0.\nFor x² + dx + e: (−2)² + d(−2) + e = 0 ⇒ 4 − 2d + e = 0.\nSubtract the first from the second: (4 − 2d + e) − (4 − 2b + c) = 0 ⇒ −2d + e + 2b − c = 0 ⇒ 2b − 2d = c − e ⇒ 2(b − d) = c − e.\nMultiplying both sides by −1: 2(d − b) = e − c, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::5": "Separate the variables: t dt = (ln x)/x² dx, so ∫ t dt = ∫ (ln x)/x² dx.\nLHS = t²/2.\nRHS by parts with u = ln x (u′ = 1/x) and dv = x⁻² dx (v = −1/x):\n∫ (ln x)/x² dx = −(ln x)/x − ∫ (−1/x)(1/x) dx = −(ln x)/x + ∫ x⁻² dx = −(ln x)/x − 1/x.\nSo t²/2 = −(ln x)/x − 1/x + c.\nApply x = 1, t = 2: 4/2 = −(ln 1)/1 − 1/1 + c ⇒ 2 = 0 − 1 + c ⇒ c = 3.\nThus t²/2 = −(ln x)/x − 1/x + 3, and multiplying by 2:\nt² = 6 − 2(ln x)/x − 2/x = 6 − 2((1 + ln x)/x).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::6": "Write y = a sin x + b cos x = R sin(x + α) where R = √(a² + b²). The maximum value of y is R, so R = 4, giving a² + b² = 16, with a = R cos α = 4 cos α and b = R sin α = 4 sin α.\nThe curve passes through (π/3, 2√3): 4 sin(π/3 + α) = 2√3, so sin(π/3 + α) = √3/2. Taking π/3 + α = 2π/3 gives α = π/3 (consistent with the rising curve in the diagram).\nThen a = 4 cos(π/3) = 4 × ½ = 2 and b = 4 sin(π/3) = 4 × (√3/2) = 2√3.\nCheck: a² + b² = 4 + 12 = 16 ✓. So a = 2 and b = 2√3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::7(a)": "A suitable sketch: a cubic such as y = (x + 2)(x)(x − 2) = x³ − 4x, which has a positive coefficient of x³ and three distinct real roots at x = −2, 0, 2. The curve comes up from the bottom-left, has a local maximum, dips to a local minimum, and rises to the top-right (an N-shape), crossing the x-axis at three distinct points.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::7(b)(i)": "f(x) = x³ + 3px² + q, so f′(x) = 3x² + 6px = 3x(x + 2p). Setting f′(x) = 0 gives x = 0 or x = −2p. Since x = 0 is a stationary point and the curve crosses the y-axis at x = 0, there is a turning point where the curve crosses the y-axis.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::7(b)(ii)": "The stationary points are at x = 0 and x = −2p. Since p > 0, −2p < 0; checking f′ either side (or f″(x) = 6x + 6p, so f″(0) = 6p > 0 and f″(−2p) = −6p < 0) shows x = 0 is a local minimum and x = −2p is a local maximum.\nValues: minimum f(0) = q; maximum f(−2p) = (−2p)³ + 3p(−2p)² + q = −8p³ + 12p³ + q = 4p³ + q.\nFor three distinct real roots, the maximum must be above the x-axis and the minimum below it:\nmaximum > 0: 4p³ + q > 0 ⇒ q > −4p³;\nminimum < 0: q < 0.\nTherefore −4p³ < q < 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::8(a)": "Start from V = pqᵗ and take logarithms base 10 of both sides:\nlog₁₀ V = log₁₀(pqᵗ).\nBy the multiplication law: log₁₀(pqᵗ) = log₁₀ p + log₁₀(qᵗ).\nBy the power law: log₁₀(qᵗ) = t log₁₀ q.\nHence log₁₀ V = log₁₀ p + t log₁₀ q, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::8(b)": "Comparing log₁₀ V = log₁₀ p + t log₁₀ q with a straight line (y = c + mt), the intercept is log₁₀ p and the gradient is log₁₀ q.\nFrom the line of best fit: intercept ≈ 3.90, gradient ≈ (5.28 − 3.90)/(40 − 0) ≈ 1.38/40 = 0.0345.\nThen p = 10^3.90 ≈ 7940 (3 s.f.) and q = 10^0.0345 ≈ 1.08 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::8(c)": "Using the model V = 7940 × 1.08ᵗ, set V = 500000:\n1.08ᵗ = 500000/7940 = 62.97…\nt = log₁₀(62.97…)/log₁₀(1.08) = 53.82… (this is beyond t = 40, as expected).\nSo the house reaches £500 000 about 53.8 years after 2 January 1970.\n53 years after 2 January 1970 is 2 January 2023 and 54 years after is 2 January 2024, so t = 53.8 falls during 2023.\nThe house is first worth £500 000 during 2023.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::8(d)": "The prediction is unreliable because it uses the model far beyond the range of the data (the valuations only go up to t = 40, i.e. 2010, but the prediction is for t ≈ 54). Extrapolating this far assumes the exponential trend continues, which may not happen — house prices can change due to market conditions, economic downturns, or other factors — so the answer to part (c) is not likely to be reliable.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::9(a)": "√(4 − 2x²) = √[4(1 − x²/2)] = 2(1 − x²/2)^(1/2).\nUsing the binomial expansion (1 + u)^(1/2) ≈ 1 + ½u for small u, with u = −x²/2:\n2(1 − x²/2)^(1/2) ≈ 2[1 + ½(−x²/2)] = 2[1 − x²/4] = 2 − x²/2.\nSo the first two terms are 2 − x²/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::9(b)": "The binomial expansion of (1 + u)^(1/2) is valid for |u| < 1. Here u = −x²/2, so we need |−x²/2| < 1, i.e. x²/2 < 1 ⇒ x² < 2 ⇒ |x| < √2. So the expansion is valid for −√2 < x < √2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::9(c)": "For small x, cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2, so √(cos x) ≈ √(1 − x²/2). Using part (a) with the role of 2x² played by x² (i.e. √(1 − x²/2) ≈ 1 − x²/4):\n∫₀^0.4 √(cos x) dx ≈ ∫₀^0.4 (1 − x²/4) dx = [x − x³/12]₀^0.4 = 0.4 − (0.4)³/12 = 0.4 − 0.064/12 = 0.4 − 0.0053333… ≈ 0.39467.\nThe approximation is justified because 0.4 radians is a small angle, so cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2 holds well over [0, 0.4].",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::9(d)": "This method may not be suitable because 1.4 radians is not a small angle, so the small-angle approximation cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2 (and hence √(cos x) ≈ 1 − x²/4) breaks down over part of the interval [0, 1.4]. The upper limit 1.4 exceeds the range where the expansion is valid (the limiting value ≈ 0.664), so the resulting approximation would be inaccurate.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::10": "For 12 ≤ t ≤ 20 the velocity decreases from 18 m s⁻¹ to 0, so the speed is decreasing — the particle is decelerating. (It is not stationary for 9 ≤ t ≤ 12, where v = 18; displacement is the area under the graph, which is not zero at t = 6; and the speed at t = 4 is +12 m s⁻¹, not −12.) Correct statement: \"The particle was decelerating for 12 ≤ t ≤ 20.\"",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::11": "On the point of moving, friction is at its maximum: F = μR, where R is the normal reaction = the weight W (horizontal surface, force parallel to surface). So 600 = 0.6 × W ⇒ W = 600/0.6 = 1000 N. The weight of the crate is 1000 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::12": "Moving at constant speed means zero acceleration, so the resultant force is zero. Adding the forces: (400 + p)i + (180 − 180)j = 0. The j-components already cancel. For the i-component: 400 + p = 0 ⇒ p = −400.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::13(a)": "The particle is released from rest, so initial velocity u = 0, and falls under gravity with acceleration a = g over a distance s = h. Using v² = u² + 2as with consistent (downward positive) signs:\nv² = 0² + 2gh = 2gh.\nTaking the positive root (it is moving when it reaches the ground): v = √(2gh).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::13(b)": "Using v = √(2gh) with g = 9.8 and h = 18:\nv = √(2 × 9.8 × 18) = √352.8 ≈ 18.8 m s⁻¹.\nThe model predicts a landing speed of about 18.8 m s⁻¹, but the machine measured 20 m s⁻¹. Since 18.8 ≠ 20 (and 18.8 < 20), the measured value does not agree with the theoretical value, so the teacher’s claim that the velocity machine is faulty is correct.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::14(a)": "The rod has length 20 cm with overhang AB = 6 cm beyond the shelf edge A. The rod is uniform, so its weight mg acts at the centre, which is 10 cm from B, i.e. 10 − 6 = 4 cm = 0.04 m from A on the shelf side. The 0.28 kg object hangs at the midpoint of AB, which is 3 cm = 0.03 m from A on the overhang side.\nTaking moments about A (the edge of the shelf):\nclockwise (overhang, the hanging object) = anticlockwise (rod weight):\n0.28g × 0.03 = mg × 0.04.\nDivide by g: 0.28 × 0.03 = 0.04m ⇒ 0.0084 = 0.04m ⇒ m = 0.21, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::14(b)": "With the object removed, the rod (m = 0.21 kg, weight acting 0.04 m from A on the shelf side) is loaded by n objects of 0.048 kg each, hung 1 cm from B, i.e. 6 − 1 = 5 cm = 0.05 m from A on the overhang side. The rod stays horizontal provided it does not tip about A; the limiting case is when the moments balance:\n0.21g × 0.04 = 0.048g × 0.05 × n.\nDivide by g: 0.0084 = 0.0024n ⇒ n = 0.0084/0.0024 = 3.5.\nSince n must be a whole number and the rod must not tip, the maximum value is n = 3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::14(c)": "One valid assumption: the rod is uniform, so its weight acts at its midpoint (centre of the rod). (Other acceptable assumptions: the rod is rigid; the rod is in limiting equilibrium / on the point of tipping.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::15(a)": "Side vectors: AB = OB − OA = [−210 − 410, 530 − 710] = [−620, −180]; CD = OD − OC = [590 − (−340), −40 − (−310)] = [930, 270].\nNotice CD = [930, 270] = −1.5 × [−620, −180] = −1.5 AB, so AB and CD are parallel (one is a scalar multiple of the other). Hence ABCD has a pair of parallel sides (AB ∥ DC), so it is a trapezium.\nBut |CD| = 1.5 |AB| (and they point in opposite directions), so AB ≠ DC. Also the other pair: BC = OC − OB = [−130, −840] and AD = OD − OA = [180, −750]; these are not scalar multiples (−130/180 ≠ −840/750), so they are not parallel. Therefore only one pair of sides is parallel and the parallel sides are unequal, so ABCD is a trapezium but not a parallelogram.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::15(b)": "The displacement from B to C is BC = OC − OB = [−340 − (−210), −310 − 530] = [−130, −840] (metres).\nThe journey takes 50 s at constant speed, so the velocity is BC/50 = [−2.6, −16.8] m s⁻¹.\nSpeed = |velocity| = √((−2.6)² + (−16.8)²) = √(6.76 + 282.24) = √289 = 17 m s⁻¹.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::16(a)": "v = 11.71 − 11.68e^(−0.9t) − 0.03e^(0.3t). Differentiate:\ndv/dt = −11.68(−0.9)e^(−0.9t) − 0.03(0.3)e^(0.3t) = 10.512e^(−0.9t) − 0.009e^(0.3t).\nMaximum v occurs where dv/dt = 0: 10.512e^(−0.9t) = 0.009e^(0.3t).\nDividing: 10.512/0.009 = e^(0.3t + 0.9t) = e^(1.2t) ⇒ e^(1.2t) = 1168 ⇒ 1.2t = ln 1168 ⇒ t ≈ 5.886 s.\nSubstitute: v = 11.71 − 11.68e^(−0.9×5.886) − 0.03e^(0.3×5.886) ≈ 11.71 − 11.68(0.00499) − 0.03(5.86) ≈ 11.71 − 0.058 − 0.176 ≈ 11.5 m s⁻¹.\nThis is a maximum: it is the only stationary point with t > 0, and d²v/dt² = −9.4608e^(−0.9t) − 0.0027e^(0.3t) < 0 at t = 5.886 (both terms negative), confirming a maximum.\nMaximum value of v ≈ 11.5 (1 d.p.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::16(b)": "Distance s = ∫ v dt = ∫ [11.71 − 11.68e^(−0.9t) − 0.03e^(0.3t)] dt.\nIntegrating term by term: s = 11.71t − 11.68 × (e^(−0.9t)/−0.9) − 0.03 × (e^(0.3t)/0.3) + c = 11.71t + 12.978e^(−0.9t) − 0.1e^(0.3t) + c.\nAt the start the athlete has run no distance: s = 0 when t = 0, so 0 = 0 + 12.978(1) − 0.1(1) + c ⇒ c = −12.878.\nTherefore s = 11.71t + 12.978e^(−0.9t) − 0.1e^(0.3t) − 12.878.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::16(c)": "Substitute t = 9.8 into s: s = 11.71(9.8) + 12.978e^(−0.9×9.8) − 0.1e^(0.3×9.8) − 12.878 ≈ 114.758 + (tiny) − 1.88 − 12.878 ≈ 99.99 m.\nThe model predicts the athlete covers about 99.99 m in 9.8 s, which is extremely close to the actual 100 m. So the model is accurate / a good model for this race.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::17(a)": "Let R be the normal reaction and F the friction (acting backwards, opposing motion). The rope tension T acts at angle θ above the horizontal.\nResolving vertically (no vertical acceleration): R + T sin θ = Mg.\nResolving horizontally with Newton’s second law: T cos θ − F = Ma.\nThe sledge is moving, so friction is at its maximum: F = μR.\nFrom the vertical equation, R = Mg − T sin θ. Substitute into the horizontal equation:\nT cos θ − μ(Mg − T sin θ) = Ma\nT cos θ − μMg + μT sin θ = Ma\nT cos θ + μT sin θ = Ma + μMg\nT(cos θ + μ sin θ) = M(a + μg)\nT = M(a + μg)/(cos θ + μ sin θ), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p2::17(b)": "The formula in part (a) was derived assuming the sledge is moving, so that friction acts at its limiting value F = μR. However, in part (b) the sledge remains at rest. When a body is stationary (and not on the point of slipping), the friction is whatever is needed for equilibrium and satisfies F ≤ μR rather than F = μR. So friction may not be acting at its limiting value, the relationship from part (a) does not necessarily apply, and the value Lizzie obtains for μ may therefore be incorrect.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::1": "f(x) = arcsin x is the inverse sine function. The sine function only takes values between −1 and 1, so arcsin can only accept inputs in that range. The maximum possible domain is therefore {x ∈ ℝ : −1 ≤ x ≤ 1}.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::2": "100!/(98! × 3!) = (100 × 99 × 98!)/(98! × 3!) = (100 × 99)/3! = 9900/6 = 1650.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::3": "Check uₙ = 2 − 0.9ⁿ⁻¹: as n increases, 0.9ⁿ⁻¹ decreases towards 0, so 2 − 0.9ⁿ⁻¹ increases towards 2 (u₁ = 1, u₂ = 1.1, u₃ = 1.19, …). This is increasing for n ≥ 1. The other options either decrease or oscillate/decrease, so the answer is uₙ = 2 − 0.9ⁿ⁻¹.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::4": "First sketch y = (1 − 2x)(x + 3). This is a quadratic with a negative x² coefficient (∩-shaped), crossing the x-axis where 1 − 2x = 0 (x = 0.5) and x + 3 = 0 (x = −3). It opens downwards with its maximum between the roots.\nThen sketch the line y = x + 3, which passes through (−3, 0) and (0, 3).\nThe region R is the set of points satisfying BOTH y ≤ (1 − 2x)(x + 3) (on or below the parabola) and y − x ≤ 3, i.e. y ≤ x + 3 (on or below the line). Shade the area enclosed beneath the parabola and beneath the line, using solid boundary lines, and label it R.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::5": "Complete the square for the circle: x² − 6x + y² − 8y = 264 ⇒ (x − 3)² − 9 + (y − 4)² − 16 = 264 ⇒ (x − 3)² + (y − 4)² = 289. So the radius is r = √289 = 17.\nThe shaded minor segment = area of sector − area of triangle, both with angle θ = 0.9 rad:\nSector = ½ r²θ = ½ × 17² × 0.9 = ½ × 289 × 0.9 = 130.05.\nTriangle = ½ r² sin θ = ½ × 289 × sin 0.9 = 144.5 × 0.78333 = 113.19.\nSegment = 130.05 − 113.19 = 16.86… ≈ 16.9 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::6(a)": "Take the 3–4–5 right-angled triangle and scale it by 2 to get a = 6, b = 8, c = 10. Then a² + b² = 36 + 64 = 100 = c², so it is right-angled, and all three sides are even. (Any all-even Pythagorean triple, e.g. 12, 16, 20, also works.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::6(b)": "Suppose, for contradiction, that a, b and c are all odd. Write the two shorter sides as a = 2m + 1 and b = 2n + 1 for integers m, n.\nThen a² + b² = (2m + 1)² + (2n + 1)² = (4m² + 4m + 1) + (4n² + 4n + 1) = 4m² + 4m + 4n² + 4n + 2 = 2(2m² + 2m + 2n² + 2n + 1), which is even.\nBy Pythagoras, c² = a² + b², so c² is even. If c² is even then c must be even (an odd number squared is odd). This contradicts the assumption that c is odd.\nTherefore it is not possible for all of a, b and c to be odd.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::7(a)": "Write (4x + 3)/(x − 1)² = A/(x − 1) + B/(x − 1)². Multiplying through by (x − 1)²:\n4x + 3 = A(x − 1) + B.\nSubstitute x = 1: 4(1) + 3 = B ⇒ B = 7.\nCompare coefficients of x: 4 = A.\nSo (4x + 3)/(x − 1)² = 4/(x − 1) + 7/(x − 1)².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::7(b)": "Using part (a), ∫₃⁴ (4x + 3)/(x − 1)² dx = ∫₃⁴ [4/(x − 1) + 7/(x − 1)²] dx.\nIntegrate: 4 ln(x − 1) − 7/(x − 1).\nEvaluate from 3 to 4:\nAt x = 4: 4 ln 3 − 7/3.\nAt x = 3: 4 ln 2 − 7/2.\nDifference: (4 ln 3 − 7/3) − (4 ln 2 − 7/2) = 4(ln 3 − ln 2) + (−7/3 + 7/2) = 4 ln(3/2) + 7/6.\nWrite 4 ln(3/2) = ln(3/2)⁴ = ln(81/16).\nSo the integral = 7/6 + ln(81/16), giving p = 7/6 and q = 81/16 (both rational).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::8(a)": "θ = 5(4 + λe^(−kt)).\nAt t = 0, θ = 75: 75 = 5(4 + λ) ⇒ 15 = 4 + λ ⇒ λ = 11.\nAt t = 2, θ = 68: 68 = 5(4 + 11e^(−2k)) ⇒ 13.6 = 4 + 11e^(−2k) ⇒ 11e^(−2k) = 9.6 ⇒ e^(−2k) = 9.6/11.\nSo −2k = ln(9.6/11) ⇒ k = −½ ln(9.6/11) = 0.068066…\nAt t = 15: θ = 5(4 + 11e^(−0.068066 × 15)) = 5(4 + 11e^(−1.0210)) = 5(4 + 11 × 0.36025) = 5(4 + 3.9628) = 5 × 7.9628 ≈ 39.8 °C (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::8(b)(i)": "As time becomes large (t → ∞), e^(−kt) → 0, so θ → 5(4 + 0) = 20. The model predicts the liquid cools towards this limiting value, which is the room temperature. So the room temperature of the laboratory is 20 °C.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::8(b)(ii)": "1 °C above room temperature means θ = 20 + 1 = 21.\n21 = 5(4 + 11e^(−0.068066t)) ⇒ 4.2 = 4 + 11e^(−0.068066t) ⇒ 11e^(−0.068066t) = 0.2 ⇒ e^(−0.068066t) = 0.2/11 = 0.018182.\n−0.068066t = ln(0.018182) = −4.0073 ⇒ t = 4.0073/0.068066 ≈ 58.9 minutes (about 59 minutes).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::8(c)": "The model approaches a room temperature of 20 °C. In a different place the room temperature could be different (warmer or cooler), so the limiting value in the model — and possibly the cooling rate — would need to be changed to fit the new surroundings.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::9(a)": "The curve has equation x²y² + xy⁴ = 12.\nWhere it would meet the y-axis, x = 0: LHS = 0²·y² + 0·y⁴ = 0, but this must equal 12, so 0 = 12 — impossible. Hence the curve does not cross the y-axis.\nWhere it would meet the x-axis, y = 0: LHS = x²·0² + x·0⁴ = 0, but this must equal 12, so again 0 = 12 — impossible. Hence the curve does not cross the x-axis.\nSince both lead to a contradiction, the curve does not intersect either coordinate axis.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::9(b)(i)": "Differentiate x²y² + xy⁴ = 12 implicitly with respect to x, using the product rule on each term:\nd/dx(x²y²) = 2xy² + x²·2y·(dy/dx) = 2xy² + 2x²y(dy/dx).\nd/dx(xy⁴) = y⁴ + x·4y³·(dy/dx) = y⁴ + 4xy³(dy/dx).\nSo 2xy² + 2x²y(dy/dx) + y⁴ + 4xy³(dy/dx) = 0.\nCollect the dy/dx terms: (2x²y + 4xy³)(dy/dx) = −(2xy² + y⁴).\ndy/dx = −(2xy² + y⁴)/(2x²y + 4xy³).\nFactor y from numerator and denominator: = −y(2xy + y³)/[y(2x² + 4xy²)] = −(2xy + y³)/(2x² + 4xy²), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::9(b)(ii)": "For a stationary point dy/dx = 0, so the numerator must be zero: 2xy + y³ = 0 ⇒ y(2x + y²) = 0.\nFrom part (a) the curve never meets y = 0, so y ≠ 0; hence 2x + y² = 0, i.e. y² = −2x.\nSubstitute into the curve equation x²y² + xy⁴ = 12. Writing xy⁴ = xy²·y² = xy²(−2x) = −2x²y²:\nx²y² + (−2x²y²) = −x²y² = 12.\nBut −x²y² ≤ 0 for all real x and y, so it can never equal 12. This is a contradiction, so there are no stationary points on the curve.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::9(b)(iii)": "Substitute y = 1 into x²y² + xy⁴ = 12: x²(1) + x(1) = 12 ⇒ x² + x − 12 = 0 ⇒ (x + 4)(x − 3) = 0 ⇒ x = −4 or x = 3.\nSince x > 0, take x = 3, so the point is (3, 1).\nGradient there: dy/dx = −(2xy + y³)/(2x² + 4xy²) = −(2·3·1 + 1)/(2·9 + 4·3·1) = −(6 + 1)/(18 + 12) = −7/30.\nTangent through (3, 1): y − 1 = −(7/30)(x − 3).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::10": "The points lie close to a straight line that slopes downwards from upper-left to lower-right, so as one variable increases the other decreases and the points are tightly clustered about the line. This is a strong negative correlation.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::11": "Lenny is told to interview a fixed number (50) from a particular category of the population (women aged 18–29), without using a random selection mechanism. Choosing a set number from specified groups in this way is quota sampling.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::12(a)": "The ten heights are 162, 169, 172, 156, 146, 161, 159, 164, 157, 160.\nMean: x̄ = (162 + 169 + 172 + 156 + 146 + 161 + 159 + 164 + 157 + 160)/10 = 1606/10 = 160.6.\nStandard deviation: s.d ≈ 6.8 cm.\nThe lower outlier boundary is x̄ − 2 × s.d = 160.6 − 2 × 6.8 = 160.6 − 13.6 = 147.\nAnn’s height is 146, and 146 < 147, so her height lies more than 2 standard deviations below the mean. Therefore Ann’s height is an outlier.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::12(b)": "Ann’s height (146 cm) is the lowest value and lies well below the mean. Discarding it removes a low value, so the mean would increase (to about 162.2 cm). It also removes the value that is furthest from the mean, so the data become less spread out and the standard deviation would decrease (to about 5.03 cm).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(a)(i)": "The number of falls in a day is X ~ B(30, 0.2). The mean of a binomial distribution is np = 30 × 0.2 = 6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(a)(ii)": "For X ~ B(30, 0.2), the variance is np(1 − p) = 30 × 0.2 × 0.8 = 4.8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(b)(i)": "P(X = 10) = C(30, 10) × 0.2¹⁰ × 0.8²⁰ = 30045015 × 0.2¹⁰ × 0.8²⁰ ≈ 0.0355.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(b)(ii)": "P(X ≥ 5) = 1 − P(X ≤ 4). From the binomial distribution B(30, 0.2), P(X ≤ 4) = 0.255. So P(X ≥ 5) = 1 − 0.255 = 0.745.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(c)(i)": "Each day, P(falls off at least 5 times) = 0.745 (from part (b)(ii)), and the days are independent. So the probability he falls off at least 5 times on each of the 5 days is 0.745⁵ ≈ 0.229.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::13(c)(ii)": "Using the same probability 0.2 for all 5 days is unrealistic because Patrick is practising: with repeated attempts over 5 days he is likely to improve, so the chance of falling off should decrease over time (or, conversely, tiredness could change it). The probability is therefore unlikely to stay constant.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::14(a)(i)": "From the table, 10 teachers suffer from back trouble and have a high exercise level, out of 120 teachers. So P(back trouble and high exercise) = 10/120 = 1/12 ≈ 0.0833.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::14(a)(ii)": "The number of teachers suffering from depression is 9 + 2 + 1 = 12 (across low, medium and high exercise). So P(depression) = 12/120 = 1/10 = 0.1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::14(a)(iii)": "There are 50 teachers with a low exercise level, of whom 38 suffer from stress. So P(stress | low exercise) = 38/50 = 19/25 = 0.76.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::14(b)": "Among the 50 low-exercise teachers, 14 suffer from back trouble and 38 suffer from stress. Since 14 + 38 = 52, which is greater than 50, at least 2 teachers must suffer from both back trouble and stress. Because these two events can happen to the same teacher (their intersection is not empty), they are not mutually exclusive.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::15": "Jamal’s claim is that larger rainfall gives greater yield, i.e. positive correlation, so this is a one-tailed test at the 5% level with sample size 10. The critical value from the table (1-tailed, 5%) is 0.549.\nHis sample correlation coefficient is 0.567. Since 0.567 > 0.549, the result is significant: there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to reject the assumption of no correlation. Jamal should conclude that there is significant evidence that the larger the rainfall, the greater the yield of wheat.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::16(a)": "Reason 1: The vertical (Salt g) axis does not start at zero. Cutting the axis exaggerates the apparent drop, so the graph makes the decrease look much larger than it really is.\nReason 2: The data from the Large Data Set is the amount of salt purchased, not the amount actually consumed. Some food (and therefore salt) is thrown away or wasted, so a fall in purchasing does not directly show that consumption decreased greatly.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::16(b)": "This is a two-tailed test of the population mean. H₀: μ = 78.9, H₁: μ ≠ 78.9, at the 5% level.\nUnder H₀ the sample mean is normally distributed with standard error σ/√n = 25.0/√918 = 25.0/30.30 = 0.8251.\nTest statistic: z = (80.4 − 78.9)/0.8251 = 1.5/0.8251 = 1.82.\nFor a two-tailed test at 5%, the critical value is z = 1.96.\nSince 1.82 < 1.96, the test statistic does not lie in the critical region, so we do not reject H₀. There is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to conclude that the mean amount of sugar purchased per person in England has changed between 2014 and 2018.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::16(c)": "Testing at the 10% significance level means there is a 10% probability of rejecting the null hypothesis even when it is actually true (a Type I error). So rejecting H₀ does not prove that a change has occurred — the rejection could have happened by chance with probability up to 10%. Therefore it is not necessarily true that the mean amount of fat purchased has changed.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::17(a)": "Let X ~ N(μ, σ²).\nP(X < 30) = 0.1: standardising, (30 − μ)/σ = z₁ where Φ(z₁) = 0.1, so z₁ = −1.2816. Hence 30 − μ = −1.2816σ … (1).\nP(X > 32.5) = 0.8 means P(X < 32.5) = 0.2: (32.5 − μ)/σ = z₂ where Φ(z₂) = 0.2, so z₂ = −0.8416. Hence 32.5 − μ = −0.8416σ … (2).\nSubtract (1) from (2): (32.5 − μ) − (30 − μ) = −0.8416σ − (−1.2816σ) ⇒ 2.5 = 0.44σ ⇒ σ = 2.5/0.44 = 5.68.\nFrom (1): μ = 30 + 1.2816σ = 30 + 1.2816 × 5.68 = 30 + 7.28 ≈ 37.3.\nSo the mean is about 37.3 g and the standard deviation is about 5.68 g.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::17(b)(i)": "X is a continuous (normal) random variable, so the probability of it taking any single exact value is 0; that is, P(X = 35) = 0. Therefore P(X ≠ 35) = 1 − 0 = 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::17(b)(ii)": "Using μ ≈ 37.3 and σ ≈ 5.68, standardise: z = (35 − 37.3)/5.68 = −2.3/5.68 = −0.405.\nP(X < 35) = P(Z < −0.405) ≈ 0.344.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2019-p3::17(c)": "Let Y be the number of brownies, out of a batch of 13, with mass less than 35 g. The probability that a single brownie is less than 35 g is p = P(X < 35) ≈ 0.344 (from part (b)(ii)), and the masses are independent, so Y ~ B(13, 0.344).\n“No more than 3” means P(Y ≤ 3). Evaluating the binomial cumulative probability gives P(Y ≤ 3) ≈ 0.294.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::1": "Roots of (x − 3)(2x + 7) = 0 are x = 3 and x = −7/2. The parabola opens upwards, so (x − 3)(2x + 7) > 0 outside the roots.\nAnswer: {x : x < −7/2 or x > 3}.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::2": "y = ln(5x) = ln 5 + ln x. Differentiating: dy/dx = 0 + 1/x = 1/x.\nAnswer: dy/dx = 1/x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::3": "Multiplying every term of a geometric sequence by −2 multiplies its sum to infinity by −2.\nNew sum to infinity = −2 × (−3) = 6.\nAnswer: 6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::4": "In proof by contradiction you assume the negation of the statement to be proved. The statement is \"every irrational × non-zero rational is irrational\"; its negation is that some such product is rational.\nAnswer: \"There exists a non-zero rational and an irrational whose product is rational.\"",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::5(a)": "L: 3y − 4x = 21 ⇒ y = (4/3)x + 7, gradient 4/3. Perpendicular gradient = −3/4.\nThrough P(15, 2): y − 2 = −(3/4)(x − 15) ⇒ 4y − 8 = −3x + 45 ⇒ 3x + 4y = 53.\nAnswer: 4y + 3x = 53 (i.e. y = −3/4 x + 53/4).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::5(b)": "Find the foot of the perpendicular by solving 3y − 4x = 21 and 3x + 4y = 53 simultaneously.\nFrom these: x = 3, y = 11, giving the point (3, 11).\nShortest distance = distance from P(15, 2) to (3, 11) = √((15 − 3)² + (2 − 11)²) = √(144 + 81) = √225 = 15.\nAnswer: 15.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::6(a)": "Ninth term: a + 8d = 3.\nS₂₁ = (21/2)(2a + 20d) = 42 ⇒ 2a + 20d = 4 ⇒ a + 10d = 2.\nSubtracting: (a + 10d) − (a + 8d) = 2 − 3 ⇒ 2d = −1 ⇒ d = −0.5.\nThen a = 3 − 8(−0.5) = 3 + 4 = 7.\nAnswer: first term a = 7, common difference d = −0.5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::6(b)": "Sₙ = (n/2)(2(7) + (n−1)(−0.5)) = (n/2)(14 − 0.5(n−1)).\nTₙ = (n/2)(2(−18) + (n−1)(0.75)) = (n/2)(−36 + 0.75(n−1)).\nSet Tₙ = Sₙ; divide by n/2 (n ≠ 0): −36 + 0.75(n−1) = 14 − 0.5(n−1).\n1.25(n − 1) = 50 ⇒ n − 1 = 40 ⇒ n = 41.\nAnswer: n = 41.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::7(a)": "Rearrange to f(x) = x³ − x² + x − 3 = 0.\nf(1.5) = 3.375 − 2.25 + 1.5 − 3 = −0.375 < 0.\nf(1.6) = 4.096 − 2.56 + 1.6 − 3 = 0.136 > 0.\nSince f is continuous and changes sign between 1.5 and 1.6, the root α lies between 1.5 and 1.6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::7(b)": "x² = x³ + x − 3 ⇒ x³ = x² − x + 3.\nDivide both sides by x: x² = x − 1 + 3/x.\nThis is the required form.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::7(c)": "Using x_(n+1) = √(xₙ − 1 + 3/xₙ) with x₁ = 1.5:\nx₂ = √(1.5 − 1 + 3/1.5) = √(2.5) = 1.5811\nx₃ = √(1.5811 − 1 + 3/1.5811) = 1.5743\nx₄ = √(1.5743 − 1 + 3/1.5743) = 1.5748\nAnswer: x₂ = 1.5811, x₃ = 1.5743, x₄ = 1.5748.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::7(d)": "Since x₃ = 1.5743 and x₄ = 1.5748 both round (to 3 dp) to values within a 0.001-wide band, α lies between 1.574 and 1.575.\nAnswer: 1.574 ≤ α ≤ 1.575.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::8(a)": "9sin²θ + sin 2θ = 8. Use sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ:\n9sin²θ + 2 sin θ cos θ = 8.\nWrite 8 = 8(sin²θ + cos²θ) using sin²θ + cos²θ = 1:\n9sin²θ + 2 sin θ cos θ = 8sin²θ + 8cos²θ.\nDivide every term by sin²θ:\n9 + 2cot θ = 8 + 8cot²θ.\nRearrange: 8cot²θ − 2cot θ − 1 = 0. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::8(b)": "Solve 8cot²θ − 2cot θ − 1 = 0. Factorising: (4cot θ + 1)(2cot θ − 1) = 0 ⇒ cot θ = −1/4 or cot θ = 1/2.\nSo tan θ = −4 or tan θ = 2.\ntan θ = 2: θ = 1.11 or θ = 1.11 + π = 4.25.\ntan θ = −4: θ = π − 1.326 = 1.82 or 1.82 + π = 4.96.\nAnswer: θ = 1.11, 1.82, 4.25, 4.96 (to 2 dp).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::8(c)": "Replacing θ by (2x − π/4), the equation 9sin²(2x − π/4) + sin(4x − π/2) = 8 has the same solutions as part (b) in terms of the argument:\n2x − π/4 = 1.107… or 2x − π/4 = 1.815… (the values in 0 < 2x − π/4 range).\nSolving: 2x = π/4 + 1.107… ⇒ x = 0.945… ≈ 0.9; and 2x = π/4 + 1.815… ⇒ x = 1.300… ≈ 1.3.\nAnswer: x = 0.9, 1.3 (to 1 dp).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(a)": "P = A × 10^(kt). Take log₁₀ of both sides:\nlog₁₀ P = log₁₀(A × 10^(kt)) = log₁₀ A + log₁₀ 10^(kt) = log₁₀ A + kt.\nSo log₁₀ P = kt + log₁₀ A, which is of the form Y = mt + c (gradient k, intercept log₁₀ A). Hence the graph of log₁₀ P against t is linear.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(b)(i)": "log₁₀ 156 = 2.19 (t = 15) and log₁₀ 260 = 2.41 (t = 25).\nCompleted table: t = 0,5,10,15,20,25 → log₁₀ P = 1.88, 1.97, 2.08, 2.19, 2.31, 2.41.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(b)(ii)": "Plot the six points (0,1.88), (5,1.97), (10,2.08), (15,2.19), (20,2.31), (25,2.41) and draw a ruled straight line of best fit through them from t = 0 to t = 25. The points lie close to a straight line, confirming the model.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(c)(i)": "The gradient of log₁₀ P against t equals k. Using the endpoints of the line of best fit:\nk ≈ (2.41 − 1.88)/(25 − 0) = 0.53/25 = 0.0212 ≈ 0.02. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(c)(ii)": "The intercept of the line is log₁₀ A = 1.88 (value at t = 0), so A = 10^1.88 ≈ 75.\nAnswer: A = 75.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(d)": "In 2030, t = 50. With A = 75 and k = 0.02:\nP = 75 × 10^(0.02 × 50) = 75 × 10¹ = 750.\nP is measured in millions of tonnes, so the prediction is 750 million tonnes.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(e)": "Set P = 8000: 8000 = 75 × 10^(0.02t) ⇒ 10^(0.02t) = 8000/75 = 106.67.\n0.02t = log₁₀(106.67) = 2.0280 ⇒ t = 101.4.\nP first exceeds 8000 when t ≥ 102, i.e. year = 1980 + 102 = 2082.\nAnswer: 2082.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::9(f)": "The model is being used to extrapolate far beyond the range of the data (1980–2005). It is unreasonable to assume exponential growth will continue indefinitely; for example, global plastics production may decrease in the future (e.g. for environmental reasons), so the model is unlikely to give reliable predictions.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::10(a)": "y = tan x = sin x / cos x. By the quotient rule with u = sin x, v = cos x:\ndy/dx = (v u′ − u v′)/v² = (cos x · cos x − sin x · (−sin x))/cos²x = (cos²x + sin²x)/cos²x.\nSince cos²x + sin²x = 1, dy/dx = 1/cos²x = sec²x. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::10(b)": "The horizontal line meets the curve where tan²x = 1 (at x = ±π/4), so the line is y = 1.\nShaded area = (rectangle area) − (area under y = tan²x between −π/4 and π/4).\nRectangle: width π/4 − (−π/4) = π/2, height 1, so area = π/2.\n∫_{−π/4}^{π/4} tan²x dx = ∫_{−π/4}^{π/4} (sec²x − 1) dx = [tan x − x]_{−π/4}^{π/4} = (1 − π/4) − (−1 + π/4) = 2 − π/2.\nShaded area = π/2 − (2 − π/2) = π − 2. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::11": "Separate variables: (1/y²) dy = (1/6) x² dx.\nIntegrate: ∫ y⁻² dy = (1/6) ∫ x² dx ⇒ −1/y = x³/18 + c.\nUse (1, 6): −1/6 = 1/18 + c ⇒ c = −1/6 − 1/18 = −3/18 − 1/18 = −4/18 = −2/9.\nSo −1/y = x³/18 − 2/9, i.e. y = 1/(2/9 − x³/18).\nSince −1/y is undefined when y = 0, y can never be 0, so C never meets the x-axis.\nThe y-axis intercept is at x = 0: −1/y = −2/9 ⇒ y = 9/2 = 4.5.\nTherefore C meets the coordinate axes at exactly one point: (0, 4.5).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::12(a)": "Find P (on positive x-axis, y = 0): (x + 0)² = 0 + 2x + 8 ⇒ x² − 2x − 8 = 0 ⇒ (x − 4)(x + 2) = 0 ⇒ x = 4 (positive). So P = (4, 0).\nDifferentiate implicitly: 2(x + y)(1 + dy/dx) = 4 dy/dx + 2.\nAt P(4, 0): 2(4)(1 + dy/dx) = 4 dy/dx + 2 ⇒ 8 + 8 dy/dx = 4 dy/dx + 2 ⇒ 4 dy/dx = −6 ⇒ dy/dx = −3/2. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::12(b)": "Tangent gradient at P is −3/2, so the normal gradient is the negative reciprocal, 2/3.\nNormal through P(4, 0): y − 0 = (2/3)(x − 4) ⇒ 3y = 2x − 8 ⇒ 2x − 3y = 8.\nAnswer: 2x − 3y = 8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::13(a)": "By the factor theorem, (5x + 1) is a factor of P(x) if P(−1/5) = 0.\nP(−1/5) = 125(−1/5)³ + 150(−1/5)² + 55(−1/5) + 6 = 125(−1/125) + 150(1/25) − 11 + 6 = −1 + 6 − 11 + 6 = 0.\nSince P(−1/5) = 0, (5x + 1) is a factor of P(x). (proved)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::13(b)": "Dividing P(x) = 125x³ + 150x² + 55x + 6 by (5x + 1) gives the quadratic factor 25x² + 25x + 6.\n25x² + 25x + 6 factorises as (5x + 2)(5x + 3) (since (5x+2)(5x+3) = 25x² + 25x + 6).\nP(x) = (5x + 1)(5x + 2)(5x + 3).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::13(c)": "250n³ + 300n² + 110n + 12 = 2(125n³ + 150n² + 55n + 6) = 2 P(n) = 2(5n + 1)(5n + 2)(5n + 3).\nThe numbers (5n + 1), (5n + 2), (5n + 3) are three consecutive whole numbers. Among any three consecutive integers, one is divisible by 3 and at least one is even (divisible by 2).\nSo the product (5n + 1)(5n + 2)(5n + 3) is divisible by 2 × 3 = 6, and multiplying by the factor 2 outside gives divisibility by 2 × 2 × 3 = 12.\nHence 250n³ + 300n² + 110n + 12 is a multiple of 12 for positive whole n. (proved)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::14(a)": "C crosses the positive x-axis where y = 0 (and t ≠ 0): 4t² − t³ = 0 ⇒ t²(4 − t) = 0 ⇒ t = 4.\ndx/dt = 2t + 1, dy/dt = 8t − 3t².\ndy/dx = (8t − 3t²)/(2t + 1). At t = 4: dy/dx = (32 − 48)/(9) = −16/9.\nAnswer: gradient = −16/9.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::14(b)(i)": "b is the value of x at the upper limit, where the curve returns to the x-axis, i.e. at t = 4.\nx = t² + t = 16 + 4 = 20. Answer: b = 20.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::14(b)(ii)": "With x = t² + t, dx = (2t + 1) dt. Limits: x = 0 ⇒ t = 0; x = 20 ⇒ t = 4.\nA = ∫₀²⁰ y dx = ∫₀⁴ (4t² − t³)(2t + 1) dt.\nExpand: (4t² − t³)(2t + 1) = 8t³ + 4t² − 2t⁴ − t³ = 4t² + 7t³ − 2t⁴.\nTherefore A = ∫₀⁴ (4t² + 7t³ − 2t⁴) dt. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::14(b)(iii)": "A = ∫₀⁴ (4t² + 7t³ − 2t⁴) dt = [4t³/3 + 7t⁴/4 − 2t⁵/5]₀⁴.\nAt t = 4: 4(64)/3 + 7(256)/4 − 2(1024)/5 = 256/3 + 448 − 2048/5.\nCommon denominator 15: 1280/15 + 6720/15 − 6144/15 = 1856/15 ≈ 123.7.\nAnswer: A = 1856/15 ≈ 124.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::15(a)": "For small x: sin x ≈ x and cos 2x ≈ 1 − (2x)²/2 = 1 − 2x².\nsin x − sin x cos 2x = sin x(1 − cos 2x) ≈ x(1 − (1 − 2x²)) = x(2x²) = 2x³.\nTherefore sin x − sin x cos 2x ≈ 2x³ for small x. (shown)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::15(b)": "Area = ∫₀^0.25 √(8(sin x − sin x cos 2x)) dx ≈ ∫₀^0.25 √(8 · 2x³) dx = ∫₀^0.25 √(16x³) dx = ∫₀^0.25 4x^(3/2) dx.\n= 4[ (2/5) x^(5/2) ]₀^0.25 = (8/5)(0.25)^(5/2) = (8/5)(1/2)⁵ = (8/5)(1/32) = 1/20.\nWrite as powers: (8/5)(1/32) = 2³ · 5⁻¹ · 2⁻⁵ = 2⁻² × 5⁻¹.\nAnswer: Area ≈ 2⁻² × 5⁻¹, so m = −2 and n = −1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::15(c)(i)": "The result 2x³ ≈ sin x − sin x cos 2x is only valid when x is small (close to 0). On the interval from 6.3 to 6.4 the values of x are not small, so 2x³ is not a good approximation for sin x − sin x cos 2x there. Hence ∫_{6.3}^{6.4} 2x³ dx is not a suitable approximation.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p1::15(c)(ii)": "The function sin x − sin x cos 2x is periodic with period 2π, so its integral over an interval shifted by a whole multiple of 2π is unchanged.\nShift the limits down by 2π: a = 6.3 − 2π ≈ 0.017 and b = 6.4 − 2π ≈ 0.117. These are small values, so the small-angle approximation is valid and\n∫_{6.3}^{6.4} (sin x − sin x cos 2x) dx = ∫_a^b (sin x − sin x cos 2x) dx ≈ ∫_a^b 2x³ dx,\nwith a = 6.3 − 2π ≈ 0.017 and b = 6.4 − 2π ≈ 0.117.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::1": "b² − 4ac = 0 means the quadratic has a repeated (double) root, so the curve touches the x-axis at exactly one point. Since a, b, c are all non-zero, that touch point is not at the origin (c ≠ 0 means the y-intercept is non-zero, and b ≠ 0 means the vertex is off the y-axis). The only sketch consistent with touching the x-axis at a single non-origin point is B. Answer: B.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::2": "At a point of inflection the concavity changes, which requires the second derivative to be zero there. Hence f″(7) = 0 must be true. (f′(7) = a could be any value, and b = f″(7) = 0.) Answer: f″(7) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::3": "u(n+1) = −uₙ with u₁ = a gives the alternating sequence a, −a, a, −a, … Pairs (a) + (−a) = 0. Among 95 terms there are 47 such pairs (94 terms) summing to 0, plus the 95th term = a. So ∑ = a. Answer: a.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::4(a)": "y = |3x − 6| = 3|x − 2| is a V-shape with its vertex at (2, 0) on the positive x-axis. It is symmetric about the line x = 2, touches the x-axis at x = 2, and has gradient ±3 (steeper than y = |2x|). It rises into the first and second quadrants and cuts the y-axis at (0, 6). The two graphs intersect at one point on each side as found in part (b).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::4(b)": "Solve |3x − 6| = |2x|.\nCase 3x − 6 = 2x: x = 6, then y = |2×6| = 12, giving (6, 12).\nCase 3x − 6 = −2x: 5x = 6, x = 1.2, then y = |2×1.2| = 2.4, giving (1.2, 2.4).\n(Checking the other sign cases gives no additional valid solutions.)\nPoints of intersection: (6, 12) and (1.2, 2.4).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::5": "Write 5(x − 3) = A(4 − 3x) + B(2x − 11).\nSubstitute x = 11/2 (to eliminate B): 5(11/2 − 3) = A(4 − 33/2) ⇒ 5(5/2) = A(−25/2) ⇒ 25/2 = −25A/2 ⇒ A = −1.\nSubstitute x = 4/3 (to eliminate A): 5(4/3 − 3) = B(8/3 − 11) ⇒ 5(−5/3) = B(−25/3) ⇒ −25/3 = −25B/3 ⇒ B = 1.\nSo A = −1 and B = 1, giving  −1/(2x − 11) + 1/(4 − 3x).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::6": "Take natural logs of both sides of 5^x = 3^(x+4):\nx ln5 = (x + 4) ln3.\nExpand: x ln5 = x ln3 + 4 ln3.\nCollect x terms: x ln5 − x ln3 = 4 ln3, so x(ln5 − ln3) = 4 ln3.\nSince 4 ln3 = ln(3⁴) = ln81,\nx = ln81 / (ln5 − ln3), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::7(a)(i)": "Complete the square: x² − 6x + y² − 8y = p ⇒ (x − 3)² − 9 + (y − 4)² − 16 = p ⇒ (x − 3)² + (y − 4)² = p + 25. Centre = (3, 4).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::7(a)(ii)": "From (x − 3)² + (y − 4)² = 25 + p, the radius² = 25 + p, so radius = √(25 + p).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::7(b)": "The circle has centre (3, 4), radius √(25 + p). It cuts the axes at exactly three points in two ways:\n(1) It passes through the origin (so one axis-crossing is shared): substitute (0,0): 0 + 0 − 0 − 0 = p ⇒ p = 0. (Then radius = 5, and the circle passes through O, giving exactly three axis intercepts.)\n(2) It touches one axis: by symmetry it touches the x-axis when radius = y-coordinate of centre = 4, i.e. √(25 + p) = 4 ⇒ 25 + p = 16 ⇒ p = −9.\nThe two possible values are p = 0 and p = −9.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::8(a)": "Mistake 1: The algebra after Step 3 is wrong — (3m + 1)((3m + 1)² − 1) expands to (3m + 1)(9m² + 6m), not (3m + 1)(9m²); Kai did not expand the brackets correctly.\nMistake 2: The proof by exhaustion is incomplete — Kai only considered n = 3m and n = 3m + 1, but never considered numbers of the form n = 3m + 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::8(b)": "Corrected Step 4 (case n = 3m + 1):\nn³ − n = (3m + 1)((3m + 1)² − 1) = (3m + 1)(9m² + 6m) = 3(3m + 1)(3m² + 2m), which is a multiple of 3.\nNew step (case n = 3m + 2):\nn³ − n = (3m + 2)((3m + 2)² − 1) = (3m + 2)(9m² + 12m + 3) = 3(3m + 2)(3m² + 4m + 1), which is a multiple of 3.\nSince every positive integer is of the form 3m, 3m + 1 or 3m + 2, and in all three cases n³ − n is a multiple of 3, the result is proved for all positive integers n.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::9(a)": "P is at the end of OP at angle θ to the x-axis, so the x-coordinate of P is d cos θ. The rod PQ makes the same angle, so QP is inclined at 2θ to OP’s direction; the horizontal displacement from P to Q is d sin(2θ − π/2) (the horizontal component of the second rod). Adding the two horizontal contributions gives the pen’s x-coordinate:\nx = d cos θ + d sin(2θ − π/2) = d(cos θ + sin(2θ − π/2)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::9(b)": "Use sin(2θ − π/2) = −cos 2θ. Then\nx = d(cos θ − cos 2θ).\nReplace cos 2θ = 2cos²θ − 1:\nx = d(cos θ − (2cos²θ − 1)) = d(1 + cos θ − 2cos²θ), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::9(c)": "x = 9d/8 − d(cos θ − ¼)². The subtracted term d(cos θ − ¼)² is ≥ 0, so x is greatest when it is zero, i.e. when cos θ = ¼. Then the greatest value is x = 9d/8, occurring at cos θ = ¼.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::9(d)": "In triangle OPQ, OP = PQ = d and the angle OPQ = θ.\nBy the cosine rule:\nOQ² = d² + d² − 2 × d × d × cos θ\nOQ² = 2d² − 2d² cos θ\nFrom part (c), x is at its maximum when cos θ = ¼, so\nOQ² = 2d² − 2d² × ¼\nOQ² = 2d² − ½d² = 3d²/2\nOQ = √(3d²/2) = d√3/√2 = (√6/2)d",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::10(a)": "h(x) = √x / (x − 3). The numerator √x requires x ≥ 0, and the denominator requires x − 3 ≠ 0, i.e. x ≠ 3. So the domain is {x : x ≥ 0, x ≠ 3}.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::10(b)": "The sign-change (change of sign) argument only guarantees a root if the function is continuous on the interval. Here h is not continuous at x = 3, which lies in 1 < x < 4 (the function has a vertical asymptote there and the values jump from −∞ to +∞). So the change of sign between x = 1 and x = 4 is due to this discontinuity, not a crossing of zero — there is no value of x in (1, 4) with h(x) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::10(c)": "Differentiate h(x) = √x/(x − 3) using the quotient rule:\nh′(x) = [½ x^(−½)(x − 3) − √x] / (x − 3)².\nSet the numerator to zero: ½ x^(−½)(x − 3) − √x = 0. Multiply by 2√x: (x − 3) − 2x = 0 ⇒ −x − 3 = 0 ⇒ x = −3.\nThe only stationary point is at x = −3, which is not in the domain (x ≥ 0). So on its domain h has no turning points. On x ≥ 0, x ≠ 3: for 0 ≤ x < 3, h(x) ≤ 0 and decreasing; for x > 3, h(x) > 0 and decreasing — h is one-to-one (each y-value comes from at most one x). Therefore h has an inverse function.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::11": "v = dr/dt = d/dt(3e^(0.5t)) = 3 × 0.5 e^(0.5t) = 1.5e^(0.5t). Answer: v = 1.5e^(0.5t).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::12": "The j (vertical) component of the velocity is the speed times the sine of the 30° angle above the horizontal: v₂ = 6 sin30°. Answer: v₂ = 6 sin30°.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::13": "Using v² = u² + 2as with u = 13, v = 17, s = 40:\n17² = 13² + 2a(40) ⇒ 289 = 169 + 80a ⇒ 80a = 120 ⇒ a = 1.5 m s⁻².\nResultant force F = ma = 1200 × 1.5 = 1800 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::14": "The distance travelled is the area under the velocity–time graph between t = 12 and t = 36.\nSplit the area into four trapezia using readings from the graph:\nt = 12, v = 5.8;  t = 18, v = 5.2;  t = 25, v = 6.2;  t = 30, v = 6;  t = 36, v = 3.8\n12 to 18: (6/2)(5.8 + 5.2) = 3 × 11 = 33\n18 to 25: (7/2)(5.2 + 6.2) = 3.5 × 11.4 = 39.9\n25 to 30: (5/2)(6.2 + 6) = 2.5 × 12.2 = 30.5\n30 to 36: (6/2)(6 + 3.8) = 3 × 9.8 = 29.4\nTotal distance ≈ 33 + 39.9 + 30.5 + 29.4 = 132.8 m\n132.8 m ≈ 130 m, so Noosha's estimate is reasonable: she is correct.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::15(a)": "T = 12 N. The cyclist (and the whole system) moves at constant speed, so there is no acceleration and the trailer is in equilibrium. The only horizontal forces on the trailer are the tension T (forwards) and the 12 N resistance (backwards), so T must equal 12 N to balance the resistance.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::15(b)": "One assumption: the connecting rod is rigid and lies parallel to the direction of travel (so the full tension acts horizontally). [Other acceptable answers: the rod is light/inextensible.]",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::15(c)": "Consider the whole system (cyclist + bicycle + trailer) at constant speed, so the net force is zero: driving force = total resistance.\n40 = T + R, where R is the external resistance on the cyclist and bicycle and T = 12 N is transmitted to the trailer.\n40 = 12 + R ⇒ R = 28 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::16": "Let the string be x m from B. Taking moments about the point of suspension (with weights w at A, 3w at B, and the rod’s weight 0.2g acting at the midpoint, 3 m from each end):\nClockwise (about suspension): 3w·x (the 3w weight, x from suspension).\nAnticlockwise: w(6 − x) + 0.2g(3 − x).\nEquilibrium: w(6 − x) + 0.2g(3 − x) = 3w·x.\nExpand: 6w − wx + 0.6g − 0.2gx = 3wx.\nCollect: 6w + 0.6g = 3wx + wx + 0.2gx = 4wx + 0.2gx = x(4w + 0.2g).\nSo x = (6w + 0.6g)/(4w + 0.2g) = (3w + 0.3g)/(2w + 0.1g), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::17(a)": "Andy uses the simple model of constant acceleration a = g, released from rest (u = 0). Using v = u + at with t = 2:\nv = 0 + g × 2 = 2g m s⁻¹. This is how Andy obtained his prediction of 2g m s⁻¹.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::17(b)": "a = dv/dt = g − 0.1v. Separate variables:\n∫ 1/(g − 0.1v) dv = ∫ 1 dt.\nLeft side: −10 ln(g − 0.1v) (since d/dv[−10 ln(g − 0.1v)] = 1/(g − 0.1v)).\nSo −10 ln(g − 0.1v) = t + c.\nAt t = 0, v = 0: −10 ln g = c.\nThus −10 ln(g − 0.1v) + 10 ln g = t ⇒ −10 ln((g − 0.1v)/g) = t ⇒ (g − 0.1v)/g = e^(−0.1t).\nSo g − 0.1v = g e^(−0.1t) ⇒ 0.1v = g(1 − e^(−0.1t)) ⇒ v = 10g(1 − e^(−0.1t)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::17(c)": "For Andy’s model v = gt, which grows without limit as t → ∞ (v keeps increasing). For Amy’s refined model v = 10g(1 − e^(−0.1t)); as t → ∞, e^(−0.1t) → 0, so v → 10g, i.e. the velocity approaches an upper limit (terminal velocity) of 10g. So the refined model is more realistic, tending to a maximum speed rather than increasing indefinitely.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::18(a)": "Take upwards as positive, with acceleration −g vertically and no horizontal acceleration.\nP: initial velocity components u cos θ (horizontal) and u sin θ (vertical).\nQ: initial velocity components 2u cos 2θ (horizontal) and 2u sin 2θ (vertical).\nTime of flight for P: when it lands, vertical displacement s = 0.\ns = ut + ½at² ⇒ 0 = (u sin θ)t_P − ½g t_P²\nt_P ≠ 0, so t_P = 2u sin θ / g\nTime of flight for Q, in the same way:\n0 = (2u sin 2θ)t_Q − ½g t_Q² ⇒ t_Q = 4u sin 2θ / g\nHorizontal distances (constant horizontal velocity):\nP: XY = u cos θ × t_P = u cos θ × 2u sin θ / g = 2u² sin θ cos θ / g\nQ: XY = 2u cos 2θ × t_Q = 2u cos 2θ × 4u sin 2θ / g = 8u² sin 2θ cos 2θ / g\nBoth land at Y, so the horizontal distances are equal:\n2u² sin θ cos θ / g = 8u² sin 2θ cos 2θ / g\n2 sin θ cos θ = 8 sin 2θ cos 2θ\nUsing sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ:\nsin 2θ = 8 sin 2θ cos 2θ\n2θ is acute, so sin 2θ ≠ 0; dividing by sin 2θ:\n1 = 8 cos 2θ\ncos 2θ = 1/8, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::18(b)": "From part (a), cos 2θ = 1/8.\ncos 2θ = 2cos²θ − 1 ⇒ 2cos²θ − 1 = 1/8 ⇒ cos²θ = 9/16\nθ is acute, so cos θ > 0: cos θ = 3/4.\nBoth particles travel the same horizontal distance XY, so\nu cos θ × t_P = 2u cos 2θ × t_Q\nt_P cos θ = t_Q × 2 cos 2θ\nSubstitute t_P = 0.4, cos θ = 3/4, cos 2θ = 1/8:\n0.4 × 3/4 = t_Q × 2 × 1/8\n0.3 = t_Q / 4\nt_Q = 1.2\nQ takes 1.2 seconds to travel from X to Y.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::18(c)": "One modelling assumption: the acceleration due to gravity g is constant throughout the motion. [Equivalently: X and Y are at the same height / the ground is level, or the particles are point masses.]",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::19(a)(i)": "Amba and Jo move along parallel paths, so their velocity vectors point in the same direction, i.e. they are parallel. Two parallel vectors are scalar multiples of each other, so Amba’s velocity must equal k(2.8i + 9.6j) for some scalar constant k.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::19(a)(ii)": "Amba’s speed = |k(2.8i + 9.6j)| = k√(2.8² + 9.6²) = k√(7.84 + 92.16) = k√100 = 10k.\nGiven Amba’s speed is 8 m s⁻¹: 10k = 8 ⇒ k = 0.8. Verified.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::19(b)": "Amba’s velocity = 0.8(2.8i + 9.6j) = (2.24i + 7.68j) m s⁻¹.\nDisplacement over 4 s: 4 × (2.24i + 7.68j) = (8.96i + 30.72j) m.\nPosition at t = 4: (2i − 7j) + (8.96i + 30.72j) = (10.96i + 23.72j) m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p2::19(c)": "In 4 seconds, Jo travels: speed = √(2.8² + 9.6²) = 10 m s⁻¹, so distance = 4 × 10 = 40 m. Amba travels: speed 8 m s⁻¹, so distance = 8 × 4 = 32 m, in the same (parallel) direction.\nThe separation is 5 m at both t = 0 and t = 4. The two parallel displacement vectors (40 m and 32 m) with the equal 5 m end-gaps form an isosceles trapezium; the perpendicular distance d between the two lines of motion satisfies\nd² + ((40 − 32)/2)² = 5²,\nso d = √(25 − 4²) = √(25 − 16) = √9 = 3.\nThe shortest distance between the two parallel lines of motion is 3 m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::1": "The graph of y = arccos x has domain −1 ≤ x ≤ 1 and range 0 ≤ y ≤ π. The end point P is at the left of the domain, x = −1, where y = arccos(−1) = π.\nSo P = (−1, π).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::2": "(x+3)(6−2x)/((x−3)(3+x))\nFactor out −2 from (6−2x): 6 − 2x = −2(x − 3).\n= (x+3)·(−2)(x−3) / [(x−3)(x+3)]\nCancel (x+3) and (x−3): = −2.\nAnswer: −2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::3": "The limit lim(h→0) [f(x+h) − f(x)]/h is the definition of the derivative f′(x).\nFor f(x) = 3x², f′(x) = 6x.\nAnswer: 6x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::4(a)": "Using the binomial expansion of (2x − 3)¹⁰ in descending powers of x:\nTerm in x¹⁰: (2x)¹⁰ = 1024x¹⁰\nTerm in x⁹: ¹⁰C₁ (2x)⁹(−3) = 10 × 512x⁹ × (−3) = −15360x⁹\nTerm in x⁸: ¹⁰C₂ (2x)⁸(−3)² = 45 × 256x⁸ × 9 = 103680x⁸\nSo the first three terms are 1024x¹⁰ − 15360x⁹ + 103680x⁸.\nHence p = −15360 and q = 103680.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::4(b)": "General term of (2x − 3/x)¹⁰ is ¹⁰Cᵣ (2x)^(10−r) (−3/x)^r = ¹⁰Cᵣ 2^(10−r)(−3)^r x^(10−r−r) = ¹⁰Cᵣ 2^(10−r)(−3)^r x^(10−2r).\nConstant term needs 10 − 2r = 0 ⇒ r = 5.\nConstant term = ¹⁰C₅ × 2⁵ × (−3)⁵ = 252 × 32 × (−243) = −1959552.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::5(a)(i)": "Area of a sector = ½ r²θ = ½ × 5² × 0.7 = ½ × 25 × 0.7 = 8.75 m².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::5(a)(ii)": "Perimeter = arc length + two radii = rθ + 2r = 5 × 0.7 + 2 × 5 = 3.5 + 10 = 13.5 m.\nCost = 13.5 × £1.80 = £24.30.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::5(b)(i)": "Area = ½ r²θ = 20 ⇒ θ = 40/r².\nPerimeter P = rθ + 2r = r(40/r²) + 2r = 40/r + 2r.\nCost C = 1.80 × P = 1.8(40/r + 2r).\nSince 1.8 = 18/10 = 9/5, factor: 1.8(40/r + 2r) = (9/5)(40/r) + (9/5)(2r) = 72/r + (18/5)r.\nWriting (18/5) outside: (18/5)(20/r + r) = (18/5)(20/r) + (18/5)r = 72/r + (18/5)r. ✓\nHence C = (18/5)(20/r + r), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::5(b)(ii)": "C = (18/5)(20/r + r) = 72/r + (18/5)r = 72r⁻¹ + (18/5)r.\nDifferentiate: dC/dr = −72/r² + 18/5.\nFor a minimum, set dC/dr = 0: −72/r² + 18/5 = 0 ⇒ 18/5 = 72/r² ⇒ r² = 72 × 5/18 = 20.\nSo r = √20 ≈ 4.472 ≈ 4.5.\nSecond derivative: d²C/dr² = 144/r³. When r = √20 > 0, d²C/dr² = 144/r³ > 0, so this stationary point is a minimum.\nHence the minimum cost occurs when r ≈ 4.5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::6": "Let me factorise the numerator 10 + 5x − 2x^(1/2) − x^(3/2). Group:\n10 + 5x − 2√x − x√x = 5(2 + x) − √x(2 + x) = (2 + x)(5 − √x).\nDenominator is 5 − √x.\nSo the expression = (2 + x)(5 − √x)/(5 − √x) = 2 + x (valid since x ≠ 25 means 5 − √x ≠ 0).\nAnswer: 2 + x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::7(a)": "Wₙ = 30 × 0.98^(n−1).\nW₂ = 30 × 0.98^(1) = 30 × 0.98 = 29.4 millilitres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::7(b)": "Each minute the amount dripping reduces by 2%, so it is multiplied by 0.98 each minute. The amounts form a geometric sequence with first term 30 (the first minute) and common ratio 0.98.\nThe nth term of a geometric sequence is (first term) × (ratio)^(n−1) = 30 × 0.98^(n−1).\nComparing with Wₙ = A × 0.98^(n−1), we have A = 30.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::7(c)": "The increase after 15 minutes is the sum of the first 15 terms:\nS₁₅ = 30(1 − 0.98¹⁵)/(1 − 0.98) = 30(1 − 0.98¹⁵)/0.02.\n0.98¹⁵ ≈ 0.738569.\nS₁₅ = 30(0.261431)/0.02 = 30 × 13.07155 ≈ 392.15.\nTo the nearest millilitre, the increase ≈ 392 millilitres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::7(d)": "The maximum extra water (assuming it never rains again) is the sum to infinity of the geometric series:\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = 30/(1 − 0.98) = 30/0.02 = 1500 millilitres = 1.5 litres.\nThe bucket started one third full: ⅓ × 12 = 4 litres.\nMaximum amount of water = 4 + 1.5 = 5.5 litres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::7(e)": "Reason 1: The sum to infinity assumes the dripping continues forever, but in reality the puddle dries up and the drips stop, so the geometric model overestimates the water added.\nReason 2: Over several hours water is lost from the bucket through evaporation (and possibly wind, or animals drinking it), so the actual amount is less than 5.5 litres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::8": "Use integration by parts with u = x, u′ = 1, v′ = cos x, v = sin x:\n∫ x cos x dx = x sin x − ∫ sin x dx = x sin x + cos x.\nEvaluate between π/4 and π/3:\n[x sin x + cos x] from π/4 to π/3\n= (π/3 · sin(π/3) + cos(π/3)) − (π/4 · sin(π/4) + cos(π/4))\n= (π/3 · √3/2 + ½) − (π/4 · √2/2 + √2/2)\n= (√3 π/6 + ½) − (√2 π/8 + √2/2).\nCollect the π terms: √3/6 − √2/8 = (4√3 − 3√2)/24.\nCollect the constants: ½ − √2/2 = (1 − √2)/2.\nSo ∫ = ((4√3 − 3√2)/24)π + (1 − √2)/2 = aπ + b.\nHence a = (4√3 − 3√2)/24 and b = (1 − √2)/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::9(a)(i)": "f(x) = x⁴ + 5x³.\nf′(x) = 4x³ + 15x².\nf″(x) = 12x² + 30x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::9(a)(ii)": "Stationary points at x = 0 and x = −15/4 (where f′(x) = x²(4x + 15) = 0).\nAt x = −15/4: f″(−15/4) = 12(225/16) + 30(−15/4) = 168.75 − 112.5 = 56.25 = 225/4 > 0, so x = −15/4 is a local minimum.\nAt x = 0: f″(0) = 0, so the second-derivative test is inconclusive. Examine f′(x) = x²(4x + 15): for x slightly less than 0 and slightly greater than 0 (but greater than −15/4), x² > 0 and (4x + 15) > 0, so f′(x) > 0 on both sides. The gradient does not change sign, so x = 0 is a (stationary) point of inflection, not a maximum or minimum.\nConclusion: minimum at x = −15/4 and a stationary point of inflection at x = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::9(b)": "f is increasing where f′(x) ≥ 0. f′(x) = 4x³ + 15x² = x²(4x + 15). Since x² ≥ 0 always, the sign is governed by (4x + 15): this is ≥ 0 when x ≥ −15/4.\nSo f is increasing for x > −15/4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::9(c)(i)": "g(x) = x⁴ − 5x³ = (−x)⁴ + 5(−x)³ = f(−x).\nReplacing x by −x is a reflection in the y-axis.\nSo the single transformation is a reflection in the y-axis.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::9(c)(ii)": "g′(x) = 4x³ − 15x² = x²(4x − 15). Since x² ≥ 0, g is increasing where 4x − 15 ≥ 0, i.e. x ≥ 15/4.\nSo g is an increasing function for x > 15/4.\n(This is consistent with g being f reflected in the y-axis: f increasing for x > −15/4 becomes g increasing for x > 15/4.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::10": "A correlation (product-moment) coefficient must always lie between −1 and 1. A value of −1.2 lies outside this range, so it cannot be a valid correlation coefficient.\nThe correct box is: Definitely incorrect.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::11": "For X ~ B(n, p): mean = np = 225 and variance = np(1 − p) = 144.\nDivide: np(1 − p)/np = 144/225, so 1 − p = 0.64, giving p = 0.36.\nAnswer: 0.36.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::12": "Step 1: Number every name in the register from 1 to 8000.\nStep 2: Work out the sampling interval k = 8000 ÷ 100 = 80.\nStep 3: Randomly choose a starting number between 1 and 80 (the first person selected).\nStep 4: Then select every 80th name after that starting point (the starting number, then +80, +160, … ), giving 100 names in total.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::13(a)(i)": "CO₂ values (n = 13): 154, 146, 138, 159, 138, 130, 146, 146, 192, 122, 175, 140, 146\nΣx = 1932\nMean = 1932/13 = 148.6 (1 d.p.)\nΣx² = 154² + 146² + 138² + 159² + 138² + 130² + 146² + 146² + 192² + 122² + 175² + 140² + 146² = 291222\nVariance = Σx²/n − (mean)² = 291222/13 − (1932/13)² = 22401.69… − 22086.53… = 315.16…\nStandard deviation = √315.16… = 17.8 (3 s.f.)\nMean = 148.6, standard deviation = 17.8",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::13(a)(ii)": "Outlier boundaries are mean ± 2 × standard deviation:\nUpper: 148.6 + 2 × 17.8 = 148.6 + 35.6 = 184.2.\nLower: 148.6 − 2 × 17.8 = 148.6 − 35.6 = 113.\nAny value outside [113, 184.2] is an outlier. Checking the data, 192 > 184.2, and all other values lie between 113 and 184.2.\nSo 192 is the only outlier.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::13(b)": "The Mass of 0 in the last line must be an error: every car has a mass (and there is also the mass of a driver), so a recorded mass of 0 is impossible. This part of Maria’s claim is correct.\nHowever, the blank Particulate Emissions value is not necessarily an error. In the Large Data Set, particulate emissions are only recorded for some cars, so a missing/blank entry is to be expected for certain vehicles.\nSo Maria is only partly right: there is one definite error (the mass), but the blank cell need not be an error.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::14(a)": "P(A∪B) = 1 − P(A′∩B′) = 1 − 0.2 = 0.8.\nAddition rule: P(A∪B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A∩B).\nWith P(B) = 2P(A) and P(A∩B) = 0.1:\n0.8 = P(A) + 2P(A) − 0.1 ⇒ 0.9 = 3P(A) ⇒ P(A) = 0.3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::14(b)": "P(B|A) = P(A∩B)/P(A) = 0.1/0.3 = 1/3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::14(c)": "P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 2 × 0.3 = 0.6.\nIf A and B were independent we would need P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B) = 0.3 × 0.6 = 0.18.\nBut P(A∩B) = 0.1 ≠ 0.18.\nTherefore A and B are not independent.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::15": "Let X be the time (minutes) to solve and escape; assume X is normally distributed with known σ = 11.3.\nH₀: μ = 65;  H₁: μ ≠ 65 (two-tailed test at the 2% level).\nSample mean: x̄ = 6780/100 = 67.8.\nTest statistic: z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n) = (67.8 − 65)/(11.3/√100) = 2.8/1.13 = 2.4779 ≈ 2.48.\nCritical value for a two-tailed test at 2% (1% in each tail) is z = 2.3263 ≈ 2.33.\nSince 2.48 > 2.33, the test statistic lies in the critical region, so reject H₀.\nConclusion: There is sufficient evidence at the 2% significance level to suggest that the mean time to solve the puzzles and escape has changed.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::16(a)": "The probabilities must sum to 1.\nP(X=0) = c(7−0) = 7c, P(X=1) = c(7−2) = 5c, P(X=2) = c(7−4) = 3c, P(X=3) = c(7−6) = c, P(X=4) = k.\nSum: 7c + 5c + 3c + c + k = 1 ⇒ 16c + k = 1, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::16(b)": "P(X ≥ 3) = P(X=3) + P(X=4) = c + k = 5/8.\nWe also have 16c + k = 1.\nSubtract the first from the second: (16c + k) − (c + k) = 1 − 5/8 ⇒ 15c = 3/8 ⇒ c = 3/120 = 1/40.\nThen k = 5/8 − c = 5/8 − 1/40 = 25/40 − 1/40 = 24/40 = 3/5.\nSo c = 1/40 and k = 3/5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::17(a)": "Assumption 1: Each game has only two outcomes (James either wins or does not win), and there is a fixed number of games (10).\nAssumption 2: The probability of James winning is constant at 0.6 for every game, and the games are independent of one another.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::17(b)": "Y ~ B(10, 0.6). P(Y = 4) = ¹⁰C₄ (0.6)⁴(0.4)⁶ = 210 × 0.1296 × 0.004096 ≈ 0.1115.\nP(Y = 4) ≈ 0.111.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::17(c)": "P(Y ≥ 4) = 1 − P(Y ≤ 3).\nFrom the binomial distribution B(10, 0.6), P(Y ≤ 3) = 0.05476.\nP(Y ≥ 4) = 1 − 0.05476 = 0.94524 ≈ 0.945.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::17(d)": "Let X be the number of wins in 15 games; under the claim test if p has increased.\nH₀: p = 0.6;  H₁: p > 0.6 (one-tailed at 5%), with X ~ B(15, 0.6).\nObserved: 12 wins. Find P(X ≥ 12) = 1 − P(X ≤ 11).\nFrom B(15, 0.6): P(X ≤ 11) = 0.9095, so P(X ≥ 12) = 1 − 0.9095 = 0.0905 ≈ 0.0906.\nCompare with significance level 0.05: 0.0906 > 0.05, so the result is not significant — do not reject H₀.\nConclusion: There is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to support James’s claim that he has increased his probability of winning.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::18(a)(i)": "X is a continuous (normal) random variable. The probability that a continuous random variable takes any single exact value is 0.\nSo P(X = 372) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::18(a)(ii)": "X ~ N(372, 3.5²). Standardise: z = (368 − 372)/3.5 = −4/3.5 = −1.1429.\nP(X > 368) = P(Z > −1.1429) = P(Z < 1.1429) ≈ 0.873.\nSo the probability is approximately 0.87.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::18(b)(i)": "Y ~ N(μ, σ²) with P(Y < 346) = 0.975.\nThe z-value corresponding to a left-tail area of 0.975 is z = 1.96 (from the inverse normal distribution).\nStandardising: (346 − μ)/σ = 1.96.\nMultiplying through by σ: 346 − μ = 1.96σ, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2021n-p3::18(b)(ii)": "From part (b)(i): 346 − μ = 1.96σ  …(1)\nGiven P(Y < 336) = 0.14, the z-value for a left-tail area of 0.14 is z = −1.08 (inverse normal).\nStandardise: (336 − μ)/σ = −1.08 ⇒ 336 − μ = −1.08σ  …(2)\nSubtract (2) from (1): (346 − μ) − (336 − μ) = 1.96σ − (−1.08σ) ⇒ 10 = 3.04σ ⇒ σ = 10/3.04 ≈ 3.29.\nFrom (1): μ = 346 − 1.96σ = 346 − 1.96(3.29) = 346 − 6.45 ≈ 339.55 ≈ 340.\nSo σ ≈ 3.29 and μ ≈ 340.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::1": "x = cos θ and y = sin θ. Using the identity cos²θ + sin²θ = 1, we have x² + y² = 1. The Cartesian equation is x² + y² = 1 (a unit circle).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::2": "Uₙ = (−1)ⁿ gives the terms −1, 1, −1, 1, −1, … which repeat every 2 terms. The period of the sequence is 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::3": "A stretch of scale factor 2 parallel to the y-axis replaces y by y, multiplying the function value by 2: y = 2 log₄ x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::4": "f(x) is a downward-opening (negative leading coefficient) quadratic, so f′(x) = 2ax + b is a straight line with negative gradient. The correct graph is the straight line that decreases (negative gradient) — the steeply decreasing line with a positive y-intercept.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::5": "y = (x − 2)⁴, so dy/dx = 4(x − 2)³.\nAt x = 0: dy/dx = 4(−2)³ = −32, and y = (0 − 2)⁴ = 16.\nThe tangent passes through (0, 16) with gradient −32:\ny − 16 = −32(x − 0)\ny = −32x + 16.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::6(a)": "(1 − x/2)^(½) = 1 + (½)(−x/2) + … = 1 − x/4 + …\nThe first two terms are 1 − x/4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::6(b)": "For small x, sin 4x ≈ 4x.\n√(cos x) = (cos x)^(½) ≈ (1 − x²/2)^(½). Using the result from part (a) with the variable replaced by x²: (1 − (x²)/2)^(½) ≈ 1 − x²/4.\nSo sin 4x + √(cos x) ≈ 4x + 1 − x²/4 = 1 + 4x − ¼x².\nHence A = 1, B = 4, C = −¼.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::7": "cot(x − π/2) = tan x. The graph of y = tan x for 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π consists of three increasing branches with vertical asymptotes at x = π/2 and x = 3π/2 (and behaviour governed by period π). Sketch: passing through the origin region, three separate increasing branches between consecutive asymptotes, with vertical asymptotes drawn at x = π/2 and x = 3π/2; the curve crosses zero at x = 0, π, 2π. (Equivalently, the cotangent graph translated π/2 to the right.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::8(a)(i)": "L₁: 5x + 3y = 15 meets the y-axis at x = 0 ⇒ y = 5, so P = (0, 5).\nL₁ has gradient −5/3, so the perpendicular PQ has gradient 3/5.\nPQ: y = (3/5)x + 5, i.e. 3x − 5y = −25.\nQ lies on L₂: 5x + 3y = 83. Solving simultaneously:\nFrom 3x − 5y = −25 and 5x + 3y = 83: multiply to eliminate — 9x − 15y = −75 and 25x + 15y = 415, adding gives 34x = 340 ⇒ x = 10, then 5(10) + 3y = 83 ⇒ 3y = 33 ⇒ y = 11.\nQ = (10, 11).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::8(a)(ii)": "PQ² = (10 − 0)² + (11 − 5)² = 100 + 36 = 136.\nPQ = √136 = √(4 × 34) = 2√34.\nHence PQ = k√34 with k = 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::8(b)(i)": "C touches both L₁ and L₂, so its centre is the same distance from each line. The lines are parallel, so the centre lies on the line midway between them.\nL₁: 5x + 3y = 15 and L₂: 5x + 3y = 83\nMidway line: 5x + 3y = (15 + 83)/2 = 49\nThe centre (a, −17) lies on this line:\n5a + 3(−17) = 49\n5a − 51 = 49\n5a = 100\na = 20",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::8(b)(ii)": "Since L₁ and L₂ are both tangents, the radius equals half the perpendicular distance between them, which is ½ PQ = ½(2√34) = √34, so r² = 34.\nWith centre (20, −17): (x − 20)² + (y + 17)² = 34.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::9(a)": "For an arithmetic sequence consecutive differences are equal:\n(5x + 1) − (2x + 5) = (6x + 7) − (5x + 1)\n3x − 4 = x + 6\n2x = 10\nx = 5.\nThis is a linear equation, so x = 5 is the only solution. Hence x = 5 is the only value giving an arithmetic sequence.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::9(b)(i)": "With x = 5, the first term is 2x + 5 = 2(5) + 5 = 15.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::9(b)(ii)": "The terms are 15, 26, 37, so the common difference is d = 26 − 15 = 11.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::9(c)": "a = 15, d = 11. Sₙ = (N/2)(2(15) + (N − 1)(11)) = (N/2)(11N + 19).\nWe need Sₙ < 100000: (N/2)(11N + 19) < 100000 ⇒ 11N² + 19N − 200000 < 0.\nSolving 11N² + 19N − 200000 = 0: N = (−19 + √(19² + 4·11·200000))/(2·11) = (−19 + √8800361)/22 ≈ (−19 + 2966.5)/22 ≈ 133.97.\nSo Sₙ < 100000 holds up to N = 133 and S₁₃₄ > 100000. Hence N = 133.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::10(a)": "Area of sector OAB = ½ r²θ.\nIn the right-angled triangle OAC, OA = r, so OC = r cos θ and AC = r sin θ.\nArea of triangle OAC = ½ × OC × AC = ½ (r cos θ)(r sin θ) = ½ r² sin θ cos θ.\nGiven area of triangle = ½ × area of sector:\n½ r² sin θ cos θ = ½ × (½ r²θ)\nr² sin θ cos θ = ½ r²θ\n2 sin θ cos θ = θ\nUsing sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ: sin 2θ = θ, i.e. θ = sin 2θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::10(b)": "Let f(θ) = θ − sin 2θ.\nf(π/5) = π/5 − sin(2π/5) ≈ 0.6283 − 0.9511 = −0.3227 < 0.\nf(2π/5) = 2π/5 − sin(4π/5) ≈ 1.2566 − 0.5878 = 0.6688 > 0.\nf is continuous and changes sign between π/5 and 2π/5, so by the change-of-sign rule a solution of θ = sin 2θ lies in [π/5, 2π/5].",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::10(c)(i)": "f(θ) = θ − sin 2θ, f′(θ) = 1 − 2 cos 2θ.\nθ_(n+1) = θₙ − (θₙ − sin 2θₙ)/(1 − 2 cos 2θₙ).\nθ₁ = π/5 = 0.6283…\nθ₂ = 0.6283 − (0.6283 − sin(1.2566))/(1 − 2 cos(1.2566)) = 1.4732575…\nθ₃ = 1.4732575 − (1.4732575 − sin(2.945515))/(1 − 2 cos(2.945515)) = 1.0413241…\nθ₃ = 1.041 (to 3 decimal places).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::10(c)(ii)": "Apply more iterations of the Newton-Raphson method (keep re-iterating, computing θ₄, θ₅, … ), which gives successively more accurate approximations to θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::10(c)(iii)": "f′(θ) = 1 − 2 cos 2θ. At θ₁ = π/6: f′(π/6) = 1 − 2 cos(π/3) = 1 − 2(½) = 0.\nThe first approximation is at a stationary point of f, so the tangent there is horizontal and never crosses the x-axis. The Newton-Raphson formula divides by f′(θ₁) = 0, so it is undefined and cannot produce the next approximation, hence it does not lead to a solution for θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::11(a)": "By the factor theorem, (x + 2) is a factor of p(x) if p(−2) = 0.\np(−2) = (−2)³ + (b + 2)(−2)² + 2(b + 2)(−2) + 8\n= −8 + 4(b + 2) − 4(b + 2) + 8\n= −8 + 8 = 0.\nSince p(−2) = 0 for every value of b, (x + 2) is a factor of p(x) for all values of b.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::11(b)(i)": "p(x) = (x + 2)(x² + bx + 4). Since the graph meets the x-axis at exactly two points, one root is repeated. A possible graph is a positive cubic (going from bottom-left to top-right) that crosses the x-axis once and touches it (a repeated root) at another point, with a positive y-intercept at (0, 8). For example, a curve crossing at x = −2 and touching the axis at a positive x-value, turning back up. (Sketch: positive cubic, single crossing at x = −2, a turning point just touching the x-axis elsewhere, y-intercept 8.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::11(b)(ii)": "p(x) = (x + 2)(x² + bx + 4). The factor (x + 2) gives the root x = −2. For the graph to meet the x-axis at exactly two points, the quadratic x² + bx + 4 must have a repeated root (a touching point), so its discriminant is zero:\nb² − 4(1)(4) = 0 ⇒ b² − 16 = 0 ⇒ b = ±4.\nIf b = 4, then x² + 4x + 4 = (x + 2)², giving p(x) = (x + 2)³, which meets the x-axis at only one point — rejected.\nIf b = −4, then x² − 4x + 4 = (x − 2)², giving p(x) = (x + 2)(x − 2)², which meets the x-axis at exactly two points (x = −2 and x = 2).\nTherefore b = −4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::12(a)(i)": "S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 1/(1 − ½) = 1/(½) = 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::12(a)(ii)": "sin 30° = ½, so ∑(n=1 to ∞)(sin 30°)ⁿ = ∑(n=1 to ∞)(½)ⁿ = ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + … This is geometric with first term ½ and ratio ½: sum = (½)/(1 − ½) = 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::12(b)": "The sum from n = 0 is geometric with first term 1 and ratio cos θ:\n∑(n=0 to ∞)(cos θ)ⁿ = 1/(1 − cos θ) = 2 − √2.\nSo 1 − cos θ = 1/(2 − √2) = (2 + √2)/((2 − √2)(2 + √2)) = (2 + √2)/2 = 1 + √2/2.\nThus cos θ = 1 − (1 + √2/2) = −√2/2.\nThe smallest positive θ with cos θ = −√2/2 is θ = 3π/4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::13(a)": "The cave meets the floor at O = (0, 0) and P = (16, 0) since OP = 16. Substituting (16, 0) into x² + y² = a√x − y:\n16² + 0² = a√16 − 0\n256 = 4a\na = 64.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::13(b)": "With a = 64: x² + y² = 64√x − y. Differentiate implicitly with respect to x:\n2x + 2y(dy/dx) = 64/(2√x) − dy/dx\n2x + 2y(dy/dx) = 32/√x − dy/dx.\nAt the maximum height, dy/dx = 0:\n2x = 32/√x ⇒ x√x = 16 ⇒ x^(3/2) = 16 ⇒ x = 16^(2/3) = 6.3496…\nSubstitute x = 6.3496… into x² + y² = 64√x − y:\n40.318 + y² = 64(2.5199) − y ⇒ y² + y − 120.96 = 0 ⇒ y = (−1 + √(1 + 483.84))/2 = (−1 + 22.018)/2 = 10.509…\nTaking the upper part of the curve, the maximum height ≈ 10.5 metres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::13(c)": "The model assumes a perfectly smooth curve, but the entrance to a real cave is unlikely to be perfectly smooth (it may have dents and irregularities), so the equation only approximates the true shape.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::14(a)": "With 5 ordinates, h = (4 − 1)/4 = 0.75. Evaluate y = (2x − 8) ln x:\nx = 1: y = (−6)(0) = 0\nx = 1.75: y = (−4.5) ln 1.75 = −2.51827\nx = 2.5: y = (−3) ln 2.5 = −2.74887\nx = 3.25: y = (−1.5) ln 3.25 = −1.76798\nx = 4: y = (0) ln 4 = 0\nTrapezium rule: A ≈ (0.75/2)[0 + 0 + 2(−2.51827 − 2.74887 − 1.76798)] = 0.375 × 2 × (−7.03512) = −5.276…\nThe region is below the x-axis, so the estimated area ≈ 5.28 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::14(b)": "∫(2x − 8) ln x dx by parts with u = ln x, dv = (2x − 8) dx ⇒ du = (1/x) dx, v = x² − 8x:\n= (x² − 8x) ln x − ∫(x² − 8x)(1/x) dx = (x² − 8x) ln x − ∫(x − 8) dx = (x² − 8x) ln x − x²/2 + 8x.\nEvaluate from 1 to 4:\nAt x = 4: (16 − 32) ln 4 − 8 + 32 = −16 ln 4 + 24 = −32 ln 2 + 24 (since ln 4 = 2 ln 2).\nAt x = 1: (1 − 8)(0) − ½ + 8 = 15/2.\nDefinite integral = (−32 ln 2 + 24) − 15/2 = −32 ln 2 + 33/2.\nThe shaded region lies below the x-axis, so its area is the magnitude:\nArea = 32 ln 2 − 33/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::15(a)(i)": "y = cosec θ = 1/sin θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::15(a)(ii)": "y = (sin θ)⁻¹. Differentiating using the chain rule:\ndy/dθ = −1(sin θ)⁻² × cos θ = −cos θ/sin²θ.\nWrite this as −(1/sin θ)(cos θ/sin θ) = −cosec θ cot θ.\nHence dy/dθ = −cosec θ cot θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::15(a)(iii)": "With y = cosec θ, y² − 1 = cosec²θ − 1 = cot²θ (Pythagorean identity).\nSo √(y² − 1)/y = √(cot²θ)/cosec θ = cot θ/cosec θ, taking the positive root since 0 < θ < π/2 means cot θ > 0.\ncot θ/cosec θ = (cos θ/sin θ) × sin θ = cos θ.\nTherefore √(y² − 1)/y = cos θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::15(b)(i)": "Let x = 2 cosec u. Then dx/du = −2 cosec u cot u, so dx = −2 cosec u cot u du.\nx² − 4 = 4 cosec²u − 4 = 4(cosec²u − 1) = 4 cot²u, so √(x² − 4) = 2 cot u (positive for x > 2).\nAlso x² = 4 cosec²u.\nSubstituting:\n∫ 1/(x²√(x² − 4)) dx = ∫ [−2 cosec u cot u]/(4 cosec²u · 2 cot u) du = ∫ −2 cosec u cot u/(8 cosec²u cot u) du = ∫ −1/(4 cosec u) du = −¼ ∫ sin u du.\nHence the integral can be written as k ∫ sin u du with k = −¼.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p1::15(b)(ii)": "From part (i), the integral = −¼ ∫ sin u du = −¼(−cos u) + c = ¼ cos u + c.\nNow x = 2 cosec u, so cosec u = x/2 and, using the result of part (a)(iii) with y = cosec u, cos u = √((x/2)² − 1)/(x/2) = √(x²/4 − 1)/(x/2).\nSimplify: √(x²/4 − 1) = √((x² − 4)/4) = √(x² − 4)/2, so cos u = (√(x² − 4)/2)/(x/2) = √(x² − 4)/x.\nTherefore ¼ cos u + c = ¼ · √(x² − 4)/x + c = √(x² − 4)/(4x) + c.\nHence ∫ 1/(x²√(x² − 4)) dx = √(x² − 4)/(4x) + c for x > 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::1": "(x−4)²+(y+5)²=36\n\nThe centre is (4,−5), so the terms are (x−4)² and (y+5)²; the radius is 6, so r²=36. The correct box is the third one.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::2": "−1\n\nThe expression lim_(h→0)[sin(π+h)−sinπ]/h is, by definition, the derivative of sin x evaluated at x=π. Since d/dx(sin x)=cos x, the value is cos π = −1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::3": "The increasing concave (log-shaped) curve.\n\nA concave function has f″(x) < 0 (gradient decreasing). Of the four graphs, the one resembling y = ln x — increasing but bending over (gradient getting smaller) — is concave. (The upward parabola and the increasing convex curve are convex; the curve with two outward-rising branches is convex on each branch.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::4": "The largest angle is opposite the longest side. AB is the shortest side, and AC=6.1, BC=8.7, so BC is the longest side and the largest angle is angle A (=BAC).\n\nUse the sine rule to find angle ACB (=θ) first, opposite AB... but more directly: the angle at A is opposite BC (the longest side). Using the sine rule with the known angle B=38° (opposite AC=6.1):\nsinC / AB ... instead find angle C opposite side AB. Easier: sin(C)/... Use sinθ/8.7 = sin38°/6.1, where θ is the angle opposite BC.\nWait — angle opposite BC=8.7 is angle A. So sinA/8.7 = sin38°/6.1 ⇒ sinA = 8.7 sin38°/6.1 = 0.8779 ⇒ A = 61.41° or 118.59°.\nSince A must be the largest angle (opposite the longest side) and B=38°, take the obtuse value: A = 180° − 61.41° = 118.59°.\nLargest angle = 119° (to the nearest degree).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::5(a)": "Expand (2+5x)⁴ using the binomial theorem:\n(2+5x)⁴ = 2⁴ + C(4,1)2³(5x) + C(4,2)2²(5x)² + C(4,3)2(5x)³ + (5x)⁴\n= 16 + 4·8·5x + 6·4·25x² + 4·2·125x³ + 625x⁴\n= 16 + 160x + 600x² + 1000x³ + 625x⁴.\nComparing, A = 16 and B = 600.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::5(b)": "From (a): (2+5x)⁴ = 16 + 160x + 600x² + 1000x³ + 625x⁴.\nReplacing x by −x: (2−5x)⁴ = 16 − 160x + 600x² − 1000x³ + 625x⁴.\nSubtracting:\n(2+5x)⁴ − (2−5x)⁴ = (160x + 160x) + (1000x³ + 1000x³) = 320x + 2000x³.\nHence C = 320 and D = 2000.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::5(c)": "Using part (b), the integrand simplifies:\n∫((2+5x)⁴ − (2−5x)⁴) dx = ∫(320x + 2000x³) dx\n= 320·x²/2 + 2000·x⁴/4 + c\n= 160x² + 500x⁴ + c.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::6(a)": "Take n = 12 (greater than... actually take a two-or-more digit number whose square exceeds 100). 12² = 144, whose last digit is 4. But the digit sum 1+2 = 3 ≠ 4. So adding the digits does not give the last digit of the square. This is a counter example, so Asif’s conclusion is not correct.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::6(b)": "By Claire’s method, only the last digit of the number being squared matters. The last digit of 23456789 is 9, and 9² = 81, which has last digit 1.\nTherefore the last digit of 23456789² is 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::6(c)": "By Claire’s method, the last digit of a square depends only on the last digit (0–9) of the number squared. Square each:\n0²=0 (ends 0), 1²=1 (1), 2²=4 (4), 3²=9 (9), 4²=16 (6), 5²=25 (5), 6²=36 (6), 7²=49 (9), 8²=64 (4), 9²=81 (1).\nThe possible last digits of a square are therefore 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 9 only. The digit 8 never appears. By exhaustion, no square number can have a last digit of 8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::7(a)": "Q lies on the curve y = 15 − x² with x = q, so Q = (q, 15−q²) and by symmetry P = (−q, 15−q²). The base PQ is horizontal with length 2q, and the height of the triangle (from O at the origin up to the line PQ) is 15−q².\nArea A = ½ × base × height = ½ × 2q × (15−q²) = q(15−q²) = 15q − q³.\nThe height 15−q² must be positive (P, Q above O), so 15−q² > 0, giving q < √15. With q > 0 this gives 0 < q < √15, so c = √15.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::7(b)": "A = 15q − q³ on 0 < q < √15.\nDifferentiate: dA/dq = 15 − 3q².\nMaximum where dA/dq = 0: 15 − 3q² = 0 ⇒ q² = 5 ⇒ q = √5 (taking the positive root in range).\nSecond derivative: d²A/dq² = −6q = −6√5 < 0, confirming a maximum.\nMaximum area = 15√5 − (√5)³ = 15√5 − 5√5 = 10√5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::8(a)": "The graph of y = 1/x² has two symmetric branches, one for x>0 and one for x<0, both lying entirely above the x-axis (y>0 for all x≠0). As x→0 the curve rises steeply with the y-axis (x=0) as a vertical asymptote, and as x→±∞ the curve approaches the x-axis (y=0) from above as a horizontal asymptote. The curve does not touch either axis. (It is the standard “double-spike” above the x-axis.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::8(b)": "Both Beth and Paul are correct.\nBeth (y-direction): 9/x² = 9 × (1/x²), which is the graph of y = 1/x² stretched in the y-direction by scale factor 9.\nPaul (x-direction): 9/x² = 1/(x²/9) = 1/(x/3)², which is the graph of y = 1/x² with x replaced by x/3 — a stretch in the x-direction by scale factor 3.\nBecause of the x⁻² form, a horizontal stretch by 3 and a vertical stretch by 9 produce the same curve, so both descriptions are valid.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::9": "log₂ x³ − log₂ y² = 9.\nCombine the logs: log₂(x³/y²) = 9.\nRemove the log (raise 2 to each side): x³/y² = 2⁹ = 512.\nSo x³ = 512 y².\nTake cube roots: x = (512)^(1/3) (y²)^(1/3) = 8 y^(2/3).\nHence x = 8 y^(2/3), with A = 8 (an integer) and p = 2/3 (a rational number).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::10(a)(i)": "Initially (t=0) there are 25 damaged plants, so A = 25. A 32% daily increase gives B = 1.32. Thus x = 25 × 1.32ᵗ.\nAt t = 5: x = 25 × 1.32⁵ = 25 × 4.0073… = 100.18….\nRounding to a whole number of plants, x = 101 plants damaged after 5 days.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::10(a)(ii)": "The model x = 25 × 1.32ᵗ is exponential, so it grows without limit as t increases. This is unrealistic because the greenhouse contains only 900 tomato plants, so the number of damaged plants can never exceed 900; eventually the model predicts more damaged plants than exist.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::10(b)(i)": "Separate variables in dx/dt = x(900−x)/2700:\n2700/(x(900−x)) dx = dt, so ∫ 2700/(x(900−x)) dx = ∫ dt.\nWrite 2700/(x(900−x)) in partial fractions: 2700/(x(900−x)) = A/x + B/(900−x).\nMultiplying: 2700 = A(900−x) + Bx.\nx = 0: 2700 = 900A ⇒ A = 3. x = 900: 2700 = 900B ⇒ B = 3.\nHence ∫ (3/x + 3/(900−x)) dx = ∫ dt, with A = 3 and B = 3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::10(b)(ii)": "Integrate both sides of ∫(3/x + 3/(900−x)) dx = ∫ dt:\n3 ln x − 3 ln(900−x) = t + constant, i.e. t = 3(ln x − ln(900−x)) + c.\nUse the initial condition: at t = 0, x = 25 (the initial 25 damaged plants), and 900−25 = 875:\n0 = 3(ln25 − ln875) + c ⇒ c = −3 ln(25/875) = −3 ln(1/35) = 3 ln35 = 10.67… .\nTherefore t = 3(ln x − ln(900−x)) + 10.67, which can be written t = 3 ln(35x/(900−x)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::10(b)(iii)": "Half the plants damaged means x = 450.\nt = 3 ln(35 × 450 / (900 − 450)) = 3 ln(15750/450) = 3 ln35 = 10.67… .\nSince t must be a whole number of days reached, it takes 11 days from when the damage is first noticed for half (450) of the plants to be damaged.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::11": "1.63 m s⁻².\nWeight W = mg, so g = W/m = 345/212 = 1.627… ≈ 1.63 m s⁻².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::12": "a = 0.6u.\nUsing v = u + at with v = 4u, initial velocity u, t = 5:\n4u = u + 5a ⇒ 3u = 5a ⇒ a = 3u/5 = 0.6u.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::13(a)": "Resolve the initial velocity vertically: u_y = 7 sin θ.\nAt the maximum height the vertical velocity is 0. Using v² = u² + 2as vertically with v = 0, u = 7 sin θ, a = −g = −9.8, s = h:\n0 = (7 sin θ)² − 2(9.8)h\n2(9.8)h = 49 sin²θ\nh = 49 sin²θ / 19.6 = 2.5 sin²θ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::13(b)": "h = 2.5 sin²θ increases as sin²θ increases. On 0° ≤ θ ≤ 60°, sin θ is greatest at θ = 60° (sin 60° = √3/2).\nh_max = 2.5 sin²60° = 2.5 × (√3/2)² = 2.5 × 3/4 = 1.875 = 1.9 m (= 15/8 m).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::13(c)": "Nisha is not correct. The projectile is modelled as a particle and air resistance is ignored, so the result h = 2.5 sin²θ depends only on the launch speed (7 m s⁻¹) and angle θ — not on the size of the ball. Making the ball larger does not change the maximum height in this model.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::14(a)": "The rod is uniform and rests on its midpoint (the support), so its own weight acts at the support and produces no moment. Take moments about the support (80 mm from each end).\nThe coin (mass 12 g, diameter 28 mm, radius 14 mm) is placed with its curved edge above A, so its centre is 14 mm in from A, i.e. 80 − 14 = 66 mm from the support.\nThe load m hangs at B, 80 mm from the support.\nFor equilibrium (taking moments, distances in mm, masses in g):\n12 × 66 = m × 80\n792 = 80m\nm = 9.9 grams.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::14(b)": "It is assumed that the £2 coin is uniform, so that its mass (weight) acts at its geometric centre — that is, the centre of mass lies at the middle of the coin, a distance of one radius (14 mm) from its edge at A.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::15": "Let t seconds be the next time after t = 0 when v = 0.\nDisplacement is the area between the graph and the t-axis (positive above the axis, negative below).\nArea above the axis: triangle with base t and height 4\n= ½ × t × 4 = 2t.\nArea below the axis:\ntriangle from time t to 10 with depth 4: ½ × (10 − t) × 4 = 2(10 − t)\nrectangle from 10 to 15 with depth 4: 5 × 4 = 20\ntotal below = 2(10 − t) + 20.\nTotal displacement = area above − area below = −7:\n2t − [2(10 − t) + 20] = −7\n2t + 7 = 2(10 − t) + 20\n2t + 7 = 40 − 2t\n4t = 33\nt = 8.25\nCheck: 2(8.25) − [2(1.75) + 20] = 16.5 − 23.5 = −7 ✓\nThe velocity is next zero at t = 8.25 s.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::16(a)": "P starts at rest and accelerates with (3i − 4j), so P’s direction of motion is parallel to (3, −4), gradient −4/3.\nQ moves in a straight line through (1, −1) and (10, c), so Q’s direction is (10−1, c−(−1)) = (9, c+1).\nFor the paths to be parallel, (9, c+1) must be parallel to (3, −4): (9, c+1) = k(3, −4).\nFrom the i-component: 9 = 3k ⇒ k = 3. Then c + 1 = −4k = −12 ⇒ c = −13.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::16(b)(i)": "P is initially at rest at (−4i + 5j) with constant acceleration (3i − 4j). Using r = r₀ + u t + ½ a t² with u = 0:\nr = (−4i + 5j) + ½(3i − 4j)t²   metres.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::16(b)(ii)": "P’s position is (−4 + 1.5t², 5 − 2t²). Eliminating t² (= (x+4)/1.5) gives P’s path as a straight line of gradient −4/3:\ny − 5 = −4/3 (x − (−4)) ⇒ y = −4/3 x − 16/3 + 5 = −4/3 x − 1/3.\nQ’s path passes through (1, −1) with the same gradient −4/3:\ny + 1 = −4/3 (x − 1) ⇒ y = −4/3 x + 4/3 − 1 = −4/3 x + 1/3.\nBoth lines have gradient −4/3 (parallel), but their y-intercepts differ (−1/3 for P, +1/3 for Q). Parallel lines with different intercepts never coincide, so the paths of P and Q are not collinear (they do not lie on the same straight line).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::17": "r = eᵗ cos t i + eᵗ sin t j.\nVelocity (differentiate each component by the product rule):\nd/dt(eᵗ cos t) = eᵗ cos t − eᵗ sin t = eᵗ(cos t − sin t)\nd/dt(eᵗ sin t) = eᵗ sin t + eᵗ cos t = eᵗ(sin t + cos t)\nSo ṙ = eᵗ(cos t − sin t) i + eᵗ(sin t + cos t) j.\nAcceleration (differentiate again):\nd/dt[eᵗ(cos t − sin t)] = eᵗ(cos t − sin t) + eᵗ(−sin t − cos t) = eᵗ(−2 sin t) = −2eᵗ sin t\nd/dt[eᵗ(sin t + cos t)] = eᵗ(sin t + cos t) + eᵗ(cos t − sin t) = eᵗ(2 cos t) = 2eᵗ cos t\nSo r̈ = −2eᵗ sin t i + 2eᵗ cos t j.\nMagnitude:\na = |r̈| = √[(−2eᵗ sin t)² + (2eᵗ cos t)²] = √[4e^(2t)(sin²t + cos²t)] = √[4e^(2t)] = 2eᵗ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::18(a)": "Let A and B be the angles the strings OA (0.8 m, shorter) and OB (1.2 m, longer) make with the ceiling. O is 0.6 m below the ceiling, so\nsin A = 0.6/0.8 = 0.75 ⇒ A = 48.59°\nsin B = 0.6/1.2 = 0.5 ⇒ B = 30°\nResolving horizontally (O is in equilibrium):\nT_OA cos A = T_OB cos B\nT_OA = T_OB × cos 30°/cos 48.59°\n= T_OB × 0.8660/0.6614\n= 1.309 T_OB\n(Exactly, cos 30° = √3/2 and cos A = √7/4, so T_OA = (2√21/7) T_OB.)\nSince 1.309 > 1.3, T_OA > 1.3 T_OB: the tension in the shorter string is over 30% more than the tension in the longer string.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::18(b)": "T_OB = 2g. From (a), T_OA = 1.309 T_OB = 1.309 × 2g = 2.619g.\nResolving vertically (weight balanced by the vertical components of the tensions):\nmg = T_OA sin A + T_OB sin B\nmg = 2.619g sin 48.59° + 2g sin 30°\nmg = 2.619g × 0.75 + 2g × 0.5\nmg = 1.964g + g = 2.964g\nm = 2.96…, so m = 3 kg (to 1 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::19(a)": "Resolve perpendicular to the ramp (no acceleration that way): R = mg cos 25° = 20 × 9.8 × cos 25° = 196 cos 25° = 177.6 N.\nThe crate moves up the ramp, so friction acts down the ramp with magnitude F = μR.\nResolve along the ramp (up the slope positive), Newton’s second law:\nT − mg sin 25° − μR = ma\n230 − 196 sin 25° − μ(196 cos 25°) = 20 × 1.2\n230 − 82.83 − 177.6μ = 24\n147.17 − 177.6μ = 24\n177.6μ = 123.17\nμ = 0.6935… ≈ 0.69.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::19(b)(i)": "The crate starts from rest (u = 0), accelerates at a = 1.2 m s⁻² and takes t = 3.8 s to reach the top of the ramp. The distance it travels up the ramp (from A to the top) is:\ns = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 1.2 × 3.8² = 0.6 × 14.44 = 8.664 m.\nThe whole ramp is 10 m long (from O at the bottom to the top), and the crate started at A, travelling the top 8.664 m. So the distance from O to A is:\nOA = 10 − 8.664 = 1.336 ≈ 1.3 m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p2::19(b)(ii)": "The crate is modelled as a particle (so its dimensions are ignored and all forces act at a single point), other than the assumption that air resistance is negligible.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::1": "|x| < 4.\nThe binomial expansion of (1 − x/4)^(½) is valid when the modulus of the term is less than 1: |x/4| < 1, i.e. |x| < 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::2": "∫₀⁵ (5x − x²) dx.\nThe curves meet where x² − 7x + 7 = 7 − 2x ⇒ x² − 5x = 0 ⇒ x = 0 or x = 5.\nArea = ∫₀⁵ [(7 − 2x) − (x² − 7x + 7)] dx = ∫₀⁵ (5x − x²) dx.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::3": "x = −1.\nf⁻¹(x) = (x − 1)/2. Solving f(x) = f⁻¹(x): 2x + 1 = (x − 1)/2 ⇒ 4x + 2 = x − 1 ⇒ 3x = −3 ⇒ x = −1.\n(For a linear function, f(x) = f⁻¹(x) on the line y = x, giving 2x + 1 = x ⇒ x = −1.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::4": "∫(x² + x^(½)) dx = x³/3 + x^(3/2)/(3/2) + c = x³/3 + (2x^(3/2))/3 + c.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::5(a)": "y = sin 2x has period 180°, so over 0° ≤ x ≤ 360° there are two complete sine cycles.\nKey features: passes through (0°, 0) going up to a maximum of 1 at x = 45°, back to 0 at x = 90°, minimum −1 at x = 135°, 0 at x = 180°, maximum 1 at x = 225°, 0 at x = 270°, minimum −1 at x = 315°, and 0 at x = 360°.\nThe curve crosses the x-axis at 0°, 90°, 180°, 270°, 360°, with amplitude 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::5(b)": "A = ±1, i.e. A = 1 or A = −1.\nThe horizontal line y = A meets y = sin 2x in exactly two points (over 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°) only when it is tangent at the peaks/troughs, i.e. A = 1 (touching the two maxima) or A = −1 (touching the two minima).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::6(a)": "The length is the horizontal extent. Since x = −2t² is most negative at the largest t, evaluate at t = 9.5:\nx = −2 × 9.5² = −2 × 90.25 = −180.5.\nLength = 180.5 cm.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::6(b)(i)": "dy/dt = 9 − 1.4t and dx/dt = −4t.\ndy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = (9 − 1.4t)/(−4t).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::6(b)(ii)": "The maximum y (top of the curve) occurs where dy/dx = 0, i.e. where the numerator is 0:\n9 − 1.4t = 0 ⇒ t = 9/1.4 = 45/7 ≈ 6.43 (which is in 0 < t < 9.5).\nThen y = 9(6.43) − 0.7(6.43)² ≈ 57.86 − 28.93 ≈ 28.9 cm.\nThe full width is twice this (top half plus its mirror image): width = 2 × 28.9 ≈ 58 cm.\nOne third of the length = 180.5 ÷ 3 ≈ 60.2 ≈ 58 cm.\nSince the width (≈ 58 cm) is approximately equal to one third of the length (≈ 60 cm), the width is approximately one third of the length.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::7(a)(i)": "Gradient b = (4.49 − 1.94)/(3.46 − 1.76) = 2.55/1.7 = 1.5.\nUsing Mercury (1.76, 1.94): 1.94 = a + 1.5(1.76) ⇒ a = 1.94 − 2.64 = −0.7.\nEquation: log₁₀ T = −0.7 + 1.5 log₁₀ d (so a = −0.7, b = 1.5).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::7(a)(ii)": "From log₁₀ T = −0.7 + 1.5 log₁₀ d:\nlog₁₀ T − 1.5 log₁₀ d = −0.7 ⇒ log₁₀(T/d^(1.5)) = −0.7.\nT/d^(1.5) = 10^(−0.7) ⇒ T = 10^(−0.7) × d^(1.5).\nSo T = K d^n with K = 10^(−0.7) ≈ 0.2 and n = 1.5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::7(b)": "Using T = 0.2 d^(1.5) with T = 60 000:\n60 000 = 0.2 d^(1.5) ⇒ d^(1.5) = 300 000 ⇒ d = 300 000^(2/3) ≈ 4488.5.\nAverage distance ≈ 4500 million kilometres (i.e. about 4.5 × 10⁹ km).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::8(a)": "V = πh³/12, so dV/dh = 3πh²/12 = πh²/4.\nWater is added at 8 cm³/s, so after t seconds V = 8t. At t = 3, V = 24.\nThen πh³/12 = 24 ⇒ h³ = 288/π ⇒ h = (288/π)^(1/3).\nSo dV/dh = (π/4)(288/π)^(2/3) = (π/4) × 288^(2/3)/π^(2/3) = (1/4) π^(1/3) × 288^(2/3).\nNow 288^(2/3) = (288²)^(1/3) = 82944^(1/3), so dV/dh = (1/4) ∛(π × 82944) = (1/4) ∛(82944π) = ∛(82944π/64) = ∛(1296π).\nSince 1296π = 216 × 6π and 216 = 6³: dV/dh = ∛(216 × 6π) = 6·∛(6π), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::8(b)": "By the chain rule: dh/dt = (dV/dt) × (dh/dV) = (dV/dt) ÷ (dV/dh).\ndV/dt = 8 and at t = 3, dV/dh = 6·∛(6π).\ndh/dt = 8 / (6·∛(6π)). With 6π ≈ 18.85, ∛(18.85) ≈ 2.661, so 6 × 2.661 ≈ 15.97.\ndh/dt ≈ 8 / 15.97 ≈ 0.501 cm s⁻¹ (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::9(a)": "From a² − 4b − 2 = 0 we get a² = 4b + 2 = 2(2b + 1).\nSo a² is a multiple of 2, i.e. a² is even.\nIf a² is even then a must be even (since the square of an odd number is odd).\nTherefore a is even.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::9(b)": "Since a is even, write a = 2p for some integer p. Then a² = 4p².\nSubstituting into a² = 2(2b + 1): 4p² = 2(2b + 1) ⇒ 2p² = 2b + 1.\nThe left side 2p² is even, so 2b + 1 must be even.\nBut 2b + 1 is always odd (one more than the even number 2b). This is a contradiction: 2b + 1 cannot be both even and odd.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::9(c)": "The contradiction shows the original assumption was impossible.\nTherefore there are no integers a and b satisfying a² − 4b − 2 = 0; the equation has no solutions in integers.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(a)": "x = −2.5.\nThe denominator 2x + 5 = 0 when x = −2.5, so f is undefined there and x = −2.5 is excluded from the domain.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(b)": "A function is many-to-one if two or more different x-values map to the same y-value.\nFrom the graph, the curve y = f(x) has a turning shape, so a horizontal line can cross it in two places; for example two distinct x-values give the same output (such as where the curve doubles back). Hence f is many-to-one.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(c)(i)": "At P and Q the curve meets y = x, so f(x) = x:\n(x² + 10)/(2x + 5) = x ⇒ x² + 10 = x(2x + 5) = 2x² + 5x.\nRearranging: 0 = x² + 5x − 10, i.e. x² + 5x − 10 = 0, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(c)(ii)": "Solving x² + 5x − 10 = 0 by the quadratic formula:\nx = (−5 ± √(25 + 40))/2 = (−5 ± √65)/2.\nP (the lower point) has x = (−5 − √65)/2, and Q (the upper point) has x = (−5 + √65)/2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(d)": "Differentiate f(x) = (x² + 10)/(2x + 5) by the quotient rule:\nf'(x) = [2x(2x + 5) − 2(x² + 10)]/(2x + 5)² = (4x² + 10x − 2x² − 20)/(2x + 5)² = (2x² + 10x − 20)/(2x + 5)².\nStationary points occur where f'(x) = 0, i.e. where the numerator is 0:\n2x² + 10x − 20 = 0 ⇒ x² + 5x − 10 = 0.\nThis is exactly the equation from part (c), whose roots are the x-coordinates of P and Q, namely x = (−5 ± √65)/2.\nTherefore P and Q occur at the x-values that make f'(x) = 0, so P and Q are stationary points of the curve.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::10(e)": "P and Q are the stationary points, and since they lie on y = x their y-coordinates equal their x-coordinates: y = (−5 ± √65)/2.\nThe lower stationary value is (−5 − √65)/2 (a local maximum of the lower branch) and the upper stationary value is (−5 + √65)/2 (a local minimum of the upper branch); the curve takes no values strictly between them.\nRange: { f(x) : f(x) ≤ (−5 − √65)/2 } ∪ { f(x) : f(x) ≥ (−5 + √65)/2 }, i.e. (−∞, (−5 − √65)/2] ∪ [(−5 + √65)/2, ∞).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::11": "0.59.\nFor X ~ N(14, 0.35) the second parameter is the variance, so the standard deviation = √0.35 = 0.5916… ≈ 0.59 (2 d.p.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::12": "Positively skewed.\nFrom the five-figure summary: median − Q1 = 6 − 4 = 2, while Q3 − median = 17 − 6 = 11. The longer spread above the median (and longer upper whisker) indicates positive skew.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::13": "Reason 1 (sample of vehicles): The Large Data Set is only a sample of vehicles in the UK — it does not include all UK regions or all types of vehicle (and has some missing CO₂ data), so it cannot show what happened for all vehicles.\nReason 2 (data years): The Large Data Set only contains data for the years 2002 and 2016, with nothing in between, so it cannot show that emissions declined in every year from 2002 to 2016.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::14(a)": "Assumption 1: The probability that any given call is a complaint is constant (fixed) for every call.\nAssumption 2: The calls are independent of one another (whether one call is a complaint does not affect any other). [Equivalently: each call has only two outcomes — complaint or not — and there is a fixed number of calls, n = 20.]",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::14(b)(i)": "X ~ B(20, 0.3).\nP(X = 1) = C(20, 1)(0.3)¹(0.7)¹⁹ = 20 × 0.3 × 0.7¹⁹ = 0.00684 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::14(b)(ii)": "P(X < 4) = P(X ≤ 3) = P(0) + P(1) + P(2) + P(3) = 0.107 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::14(b)(iii)": "P(X ≥ 10) = 1 − P(X ≤ 9) = 1 − 0.952 = 0.048 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::14(c)": "For Y ~ B(10, p), variance = np(1 − p) = 10p(1 − p), and standard deviation 1.5 gives variance 1.5² = 2.25.\n10p(1 − p) = 2.25 ⇒ 10p − 10p² = 2.25 ⇒ 10p² − 10p + 2.25 = 0.\np = (10 ± √(100 − 90))/20 = (10 ± √10)/20.\nSo p = 0.658… or p = 0.341…, i.e. p ≈ 0.66 or p ≈ 0.34.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::15(a)": "The population is all adults on the electoral register of the town.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::15(b)(i)": "Systematic sampling (every 100th adult is selected).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::15(b)(ii)": "It is quick and easy to carry out (cheap to collect the sample) and, with a randomly chosen start, it is fair/free from selection bias and spreads the sample evenly through the register.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::16(a)": "Work from the centre outwards (using all three = 17):\nC∩D only = 48 − 17 = 31; C∩T only = 21 − 17 = 4; D∩T only = 32 − 17 = 15.\nC only = 153 − 31 − 4 − 17 = 101; D only = 70 − 31 − 15 − 17 = 7; T only = 45 − 4 − 15 − 17 = 9.\nTotal in the three circles = 101 + 31 + 7 + 4 + 17 + 15 + 9 = 184, so none of the three = 240 − 184 = 56.\nVenn diagram: C only 101, C∩D only 31, D only 7, C∩T only 4, C∩D∩T 17, D∩T only 15, T only 9, outside 56.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::16(b)(i)": "P(cat only) = 101/240 ≈ 0.421.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::16(b)(ii)": "At least two types = (C∩D only) + (C∩T only) + (D∩T only) + (all three) = 31 + 4 + 15 + 17 = 67.\nP = 67/240 ≈ 0.279.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::16(b)(iii)": "Households with no tortoise = 240 − 45 = 195.\nAmong these, owning a cat or dog or both = C only + D only + C∩D only = 101 + 7 + 31 = 139.\nP(cat or dog or both | no tortoise) = 139/195 ≈ 0.713.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::16(c)": "P(C) = 153/240, P(T) = 45/240, and P(C ∩ T) = (C∩T only + all three)/240 = (4 + 17)/240 = 21/240 = 0.0875.\nIf independent, P(C ∩ T) would equal P(C)P(T) = (153/240)(45/240) = 0.1196.\nSince 0.0875 ≠ 0.1196, owning a cat and owning a tortoise are NOT independent.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::17": "Let μ be the mean working hours per week. Test H₀: μ = 34 against H₁: μ > 34 (one-tailed), at the 2.5% level.\nUnder H₀ the sample mean X̄ ~ N(34, 4.5²/30).\nStandardising the observed mean 36.2: z = (36.2 − 34)/(4.5/√30) = 2.2/0.8216 = 2.68.\nP(X̄ ≥ 36.2) = P(Z ≥ 2.68) = 0.0037 ≈ 0.004.\nSince 0.004 < 0.025 (equivalently 2.68 > 1.96), reject H₀.\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 2.5% significance level to support the manager's claim that the mean working hours per week have increased.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(a)": "For a normal distribution, about 95% of values lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean.\nMean ± 2 sd = 1.78 ± 2(0.23) = 1.78 ± 0.46, giving the interval 1.32 to 2.24 metres.\nThis is very close to the stated interval 1.33 to 2.22 metres containing 95% of athletes.\nSince height is continuous and the data fit the 95%-within-2-sd property well, a normal distribution may be a suitable model.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(b)(i)": "For a continuous random variable, the probability of any single exact value is 0.\nP(X = 1.82) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(b)(ii)": "X ~ N(1.78, 0.23²).\nP(1.70 < X < 1.90) = P((1.70 − 1.78)/0.23 < Z < (1.90 − 1.78)/0.23) = P(−0.348 < Z < 0.522) = 0.335 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(b)(iii)": "The two athletes are chosen independently, so multiply the probabilities:\nP(both between 1.70 and 1.90) = 0.335² = 0.112 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(c)": "Mean estimate: h̄ = ∑h / n = 69.2 / 40 = 1.73 metres.\nStandard deviation estimate (using n − 1): s = √(∑(h − h̄)² / (n − 1)) = √(2.81/39) = √0.07205 = 0.265 metres (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::18(d)": "Comparing the Summer Olympics (mean 1.78, sd 0.23) with the Winter Olympics (mean 1.73, sd 0.265):\nOn average, Summer Olympic male athletes are taller than Winter Olympic male athletes (1.78 > 1.73).\nThe Summer athletes' heights are less varied/more consistent (smaller spread) than the Winter athletes' heights (0.23 < 0.265).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2022-p3::19": "Let p be the proportion of customers registered for an Internet banking account. Test H₀: p = 0.42 against H₁: p > 0.42 (one-tailed), at the 10% level.\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(35, 0.42), where X is the number registered in the sample of 35. Observed value x = 18.\nP(X ≥ 18) = 1 − P(X ≤ 17) = 1 − 0.832 = 0.168.\n(Equivalently the critical region is X ≥ 19, since P(X ≥ 19) = 0.097 < 0.10 but P(X ≥ 18) = 0.168 > 0.10.)\nSince 0.168 > 0.10 (and 18 is not in the critical region), do not reject H₀.\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 10% significance level to suggest that the proportion of customers registered for an Internet banking account has increased since the campaign.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::1": "The required term is C(7,7)·(2x)⁷·(−3)⁰ = 1·128x⁷·1 = 128x⁷.\nCoefficient of x⁷ = 128.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::2": "y = 2x³ ⇒ dy/dx = 3·2x² = 6x².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::3": "A stretch parallel to the x-axis with scale factor 2 replaces x by x/2.\nSo y = ln(x/2).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::4": "For small θ, cos 2θ ≈ 1 − (2θ)²/2 = 1 − 4θ²/2 = 1 − 2θ².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::5(a)": "h = (4 − 1)/5 = 0.6.\nArea ≈ (h/2)[y₀ + y₅ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃ + y₄)]\n= (0.6/2)[2.90988 + 0.09329 + 2(1.26485 + 0.62305 + 0.32374 + 0.17263)]\n= 0.3[3.00317 + 2(2.38427)]\n= 0.3 × 7.77171\n= 2.331513…\n≈ 2.3315 (4 d.p.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::5(b)": "20/(eˣ − 1) = 4 × 5/(eˣ − 1), so the integral is 4 times the area in part (a).\n∫₁⁴ 20/(eˣ − 1) dx ≈ 4 × 2.3315 = 9.326 ≈ 9.3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::6": "2 log₁₀ x = log₁₀ x² (power rule).\nlog₁₀ 4 + log₁₀(x + 8) = log₁₀ 4(x + 8) (addition rule).\nSo log₁₀ x² = log₁₀ 4(x + 8) ⇒ x² = 4(x + 8) = 4x + 32.\nx² − 4x − 32 = 0 ⇒ (x − 8)(x + 4) = 0 ⇒ x = 8 or x = −4.\nThe original equation contains log₁₀ x, which requires x > 0. For x = −4, log₁₀(−4) is undefined (the log of a negative number does not exist), so x = −4 is rejected.\nTherefore the equation has exactly one solution, x = 8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::7(a)": "Common denominator (3 + 5√n)(5√n − 3):\n7/(3 + 5√n) − 7/(5√n − 3) = [7(5√n − 3) − 7(3 + 5√n)] / [(3 + 5√n)(5√n − 3)]\nNumerator: 7(5√n − 3) − 7(3 + 5√n) = 35√n − 21 − 21 − 35√n = −42.\nDenominator: (5√n + 3)(5√n − 3) = 25n − 9.\nSo the expression = −42/(25n − 9).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::7(b)": "For any positive integer n, 25n − 9 is an integer and −42 is an integer (with 25n − 9 ≠ 0). A ratio of two integers is rational, so −42/(25n − 9) is a rational number for all positive integer values of n.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::8": "Use integration by parts with u = x, dv = sin 4x dx, so du = dx and v = −¼ cos 4x.\n∫ x sin 4x dx = −(x/4) cos 4x − ∫ −¼ cos 4x dx\n= −(x/4) cos 4x + ¼·(¼ sin 4x)\n= −(x/4) cos 4x + (1/16) sin 4x.\nEvaluate from 0 to π/2:\nAt x = π/2: −(π/8) cos 2π + (1/16) sin 2π = −π/8·(1) + 0 = −π/8.\nAt x = 0: 0 + 0 = 0.\n∫₀^(π/2) x sin 4x dx = −π/8 − 0 = −π/8. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::9(a)(i)": "Midpoint of PQ = ((−6 + 12)/2, (15 + 19)/2) = (3, 17).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::9(a)(ii)": "Gradient of PQ = (19 − 15)/(12 − (−6)) = 4/18 = 2/9.\nPerpendicular gradient = −9/2.\nThrough the midpoint (3, 17): y − 17 = −9/2(x − 3).\n2(y − 17) = −9(x − 3) ⇒ 2y − 34 = −9x + 27 ⇒ 9x + 2y = 61.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::9(b)(i)": "The centre is equidistant from P and Q, so it lies on the perpendicular bisector 9x + 2y = 61. It also lies on 2x − 5y = −30.\nSolve simultaneously: from 9x + 2y = 61 and 2x − 5y = −30.\nMultiply: 5(9x + 2y) = 305 ⇒ 45x + 10y = 305; 2(2x − 5y) = −60 ⇒ 4x − 10y = −60.\nAdd: 49x = 245 ⇒ x = 5, then 2(5) − 5y = −30 ⇒ 10 − 5y = −30 ⇒ y = 8.\nCentre (5, 8).\nr² = (5 − (−6))² + (8 − 15)² = 11² + (−7)² = 121 + 49 = 170.\nEquation: (x − 5)² + (y − 8)² = 170.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::9(b)(ii)": "Centre (5, 8), r = √170 ≈ 13.04.\nx-axis: (x − 5)² + 64 = 170 ⇒ (x − 5)² = 106 > 0 ⇒ two real points.\ny-axis: 25 + (y − 8)² = 170 ⇒ (y − 8)² = 145 > 0 ⇒ two real points.\nSo the circle meets the coordinate axes at n = 4 points.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::10(a)(i)": "A has y = 0.5, so sin a° = 0.5 with A in the second quadrant region (−360 to 0, where sine is positive on the left lobe). The relevant solution is a = −180 − 30 = −210.\na = −210.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::10(a)(ii)": "sin(180° − a°) = sin a° = 0.5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::10(b)(i)": "sin(b° − 180°) = −sin b°.\nB has y = sin b° = −3/7, so sin(b° − 180°) = −(−3/7) = 3/7.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::10(b)(ii)": "B is the point (b, −3/7) on y = sin x°, so sin b° = −3/7.\ncos²b° + sin²b° = 1\ncos²b° = 1 − (−3/7)² = 1 − 9/49 = 40/49\ncos b° = ±√40/7 = ±2√10/7.\nFrom the graph, B lies between x = 180 and x = 270, so 180 < b < 270. In this interval cos x° is negative.\nTherefore cos b° = −2√10/7.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::11(a)": "u₂ = p·u₁ + 70 = 400p + 70.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::11(b)(i)": "u₃ = p·u₂ + 70 = p(400p + 70) + 70 = 400p² + 70p + 70.\nSet u₃ = 382: 400p² + 70p + 70 = 382 ⇒ 400p² + 70p − 312 = 0.\nDivide by 2: 200p² + 35p − 156 = 0. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::11(b)(ii)": "From (b)(i), 200p² + 35p − 156 = 0.\np = [−35 ± √(35² + 4 × 200 × 156)]/(2 × 200) = [−35 ± √126025]/400 = (−35 ± 355)/400\nSo p = 0.8 or p = −0.975.\nIf p = −0.975: u₂ = −320, u₃ = 382, u₄ = −0.975 × 382 + 70 = −302.45, u₅ = 364.88875. The terms go down and up, so the sequence is not decreasing; reject p = −0.975.\nSo p = 0.8:\nu₂ = 0.8 × 400 + 70 = 390\nu₃ = 0.8 × 390 + 70 = 382 ✓\nu₄ = 0.8 × 382 + 70 = 305.6 + 70 = 375.6\nu₅ = 0.8 × 375.6 + 70 = 300.48 + 70 = 370.48\n(400 > 390 > 382 > 375.6 > 370.48, so the sequence is decreasing.)\nu₄ = 375.6 and u₅ = 370.48.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::11(c)(i)": "As n → ∞, uₙ₊₁ and uₙ both tend to L, so L = pL + 70, i.e. L = 0.8L + 70 (taking p = 0.8).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::11(c)(ii)": "L = 0.8L + 70 ⇒ 0.2L = 70 ⇒ L = 350.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::12(a)": "At t = 0: f = 1 − cos 0 = 1 − 1 = 0; c = 8 − 4 sin 0 = 8 − 0 = 8.\nInitial distance = c − f = 8 − 0 = 8 metres. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::12(b)": "d = c − f = (8 − 4 sin t) − (1 − cos t) = 7 + cos t − 4 sin t.\nWrite cos t − 4 sin t = R cos(t + α) = R cos α cos t − R sin α sin t.\nCompare: R cos α = 1, R sin α = 4.\nR = √(1² + 4²) = √17; tan α = 4/1 ⇒ α = arctan 4 = 1.3258… ≈ 1.33 (rad).\nTherefore d = 7 + √17 cos(t + 1.33).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::12(c)": "The minimum of cos(t + α) is −1, so the minimum distance is 7 − √17 (since √17 ≈ 4.123 < 7).\n7 − √17 = 7 − 4.1231… = 2.8769… ≈ 2.88 metres (288 cm).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::13(a)": "arccos a = 0 ⇒ a = cos 0 = 1. So a = 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::13(b)(i)": "On the same axes:\n• y = cos x for 0 ≤ x ≤ π/2: a decreasing curve from (0, 1) to (π/2, 0).\n• y = x for 0 ≤ x ≤ π/2: a straight line through the origin with gradient 1, from (0, 0) to (π/2, π/2).\nThe arccos curve (from (0, π/2) to (1, 0)) and the cos curve are reflections of each other in the line y = x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::13(b)(ii)": "x − cos x = 0 means x = cos x. Applying arccos to both sides (valid on this domain, where arccos is the inverse of cos): arccos x = arccos(cos x) = x = cos x. Hence the same x satisfies cos x = arccos x. Geometrically, y = cos x and y = arccos x are reflections in y = x, so they meet on the line y = x — exactly where x = cos x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::13(c)": "Let f(x) = x − cos x, f′(x) = 1 + sin x. Newton-Raphson: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (xₙ − cos xₙ)/(1 + sin xₙ).\nx₀ = 0.\nx₁ = 0 − (0 − 1)/(1 + 0) = 1.\nx₂ = 1 − (1 − cos 1)/(1 + sin 1) = 1 − (1 − 0.54030)/(1 + 0.84147) = 1 − 0.45970/1.84147 = 0.75036.\nx₃ = 0.75036 − (0.75036 − cos 0.75036)/(1 + sin 0.75036) = 0.75036 − (0.75036 − 0.73140)/(1 + 0.68170) = 0.75036 − 0.01896/1.68170 = 0.73911.\nx₃ ≈ 0.7391 (4 d.p.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::14(a)(i)": "y = 2ˣ = e^(x ln 2), so dy/dx = ln 2·e^(x ln 2) = 2ˣ ln 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::14(a)(ii)": "Since d/dx(2ˣ) = 2ˣ ln 2, reversing gives ∫ 2ˣ dx = 2ˣ/ln 2 + c.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::14(b)(i)": "The eight rectangles span x = −4 to x = 0, each of width 4/8 = 0.5. The largest (rightmost) rectangle has its left edge at x = −0.5, so its height is y(−0.5) = 2^(−1/2) = 1/√2.\nArea = width × height = 0.5 × 1/√2 = 1/(2√2) = √2/4. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::14(b)(ii)": "The areas form a geometric sequence with first term a = √2/4 (largest) and common ratio r = √2/2 (each rectangle to the left is smaller).\nS₈ = a(1 − r⁸)/(1 − r) = (√2/4)(1 − (√2/2)⁸)/(1 − √2/2).\n(√2/2)⁸ = (2^(1/2)/2)⁸ = 2⁴/2⁸ = 16/256 = 1/16.\nS₈ = (√2/4)(1 − 1/16)/(1 − √2/2) = (√2/4)(15/16)/((2 − √2)/2) = (15√2/64)·(2/(2 − √2)) = (15√2/32)/(2 − √2).\nRationalise: ×(2 + √2)/(2 + √2): (15√2(2 + √2))/(32(4 − 2)) = (15√2(2 + √2))/(64) = (30√2 + 30)/64 = (15√2 + 15)/32 = (15/32)(1 + √2).\nTotal area = (15/32)(1 + √2).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::14(b)(iii)": "As n → ∞ the rectangle approximation tends to the exact area:\nA = ∫₋₄⁰ 2ˣ dx = [2ˣ/ln 2]₋₄⁰ = (1/ln 2)(2⁰ − 2⁻⁴) = (1/ln 2)(1 − 1/16) = (15/16)/ln 2 = 15/(16 ln 2).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::15(a)": "Differentiate x² + 2y³ − 4xy = 0 implicitly:\n2x + 6y²(dy/dx) − 4(y + x dy/dx) = 0\n2x + 6y²(dy/dx) − 4y − 4x(dy/dx) = 0.\nAt the stationary point P, dy/dx = 0:\n2x − 4y = 0 ⇒ x = 2y.\nSubstitute x = 2y into the curve equation:\n(2y)² + 2y³ − 4(2y)(y) = 4y² + 2y³ − 8y² = 2y³ − 4y² = 0.\nFactorise: 2y²(y − 2) = 0 ⇒ y²(y − 2) = 0. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::15(b)": "From y²(y − 2) = 0, y = 0 or y = 2. y = 0 gives x = 2y = 0, i.e. the origin (where the curve crosses itself), not the stationary point shown. So y = 2, and x = 2y = 4.\nP = (4, 2).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::16(a)": "1/(16 − 9x²) ≡ A/(4 − 3x) + B/(4 + 3x).\nMultiply by (4 − 3x)(4 + 3x) = 16 − 9x²: 1 ≡ A(4 + 3x) + B(4 − 3x).\nx = 4/3: 1 = A(4 + 4) = 8A ⇒ A = 1/8.\nx = −4/3: 1 = B(4 + 4) = 8B ⇒ B = 1/8.\nSo A = 1/8 and B = 1/8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::16(b)(i)": "Rate in = 0.16, rate out = 0.36d², so dV/dt = 0.16 − 0.36d².\nThe base area = 1.6 × 1.25 = 2 m², so V = 2d, giving d = V/2.\ndV/dt = 0.16 − 0.36(V/2)² = 0.16 − 0.36·V²/4 = 0.16 − 0.09V².\n= (16 − 9V²)/100. ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::16(b)(ii)": "Separate variables: dt = 100/(16 − 9V²) dV.\nUsing part (a) scaled by 100: 100/(16 − 9V²) = (100/8)[1/(4 − 3V) + 1/(4 + 3V)] = (25/2)[1/(4 − 3V) + 1/(4 + 3V)].\nIntegrate: t = (25/2)[(−1/3)ln(4 − 3V) + (1/3)ln(4 + 3V)] + c = (25/6)[ln(4 + 3V) − ln(4 − 3V)] + c.\nWhen t = 0, V = 0: 0 = (25/6)(ln 4 − ln 4) + c ⇒ c = 0.\nt = (25/6) ln((4 + 3V)/(4 − 3V)).   (Equivalently t = (100/24)[ln(4 + 3V) − ln(4 − 3V)].)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p1::16(b)(iii)": "The container is full when the depth d = 0.5 m, so V = 2d = 2 × 0.5 = 1 m³.\nt = (25/6) ln((4 + 3)/(4 − 3)) = (25/6) ln(7/1) = (25/6) ln 7 = (100/24) ln 7.\nln 7 = 1.94591…, so t = 4.1667 × 1.94591 = 8.108… minutes.\nTo the nearest minute, t ≈ 8 minutes.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::1": "The parabola opens upwards with roots at x = 2 and x = 5, so y > 0 outside the roots.\nax² + bx + c > 0 for x < 2 or x > 5.\nAnswer: {x : x < 2} ∪ {x : x > 5}.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::2": "∫₀³ f(x) dx = ∫₀⁶ f(x) dx − ∫₃⁶ f(x) dx = 20 − (−10) = 30.\nAnswer: 30.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::3": "The circle (x − 5)² + (y − 13)² = 16 has radius² = 16, so radius = √16 = 4.\nAnswer: 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::4(a)": "y = x²/8 + 4√x = x²/8 + 4x^(1/2).\ndy/dx = 2x/8 + 4 · ½ x^(−1/2) = x/4 + 2x^(−1/2).\nSo dy/dx = x/4 + 2/√x.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::4(b)": "At P, x = 4: dy/dx = 4/4 + 2/√4 = 1 + 1 = 2, so the gradient is 2.\nTangent through (4, 10): y − 10 = 2(x − 4) ⇒ y = 2x − 8 + 10 = 2x + 2.\nEquation of tangent: y = 2x + 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::4(c)": "Stationary points need dy/dx = 0, i.e. x/4 + 2/√x = 0.\nThe domain requires √x, so x > 0. For all x > 0, both x/4 > 0 and 2/√x > 0, hence dy/dx = x/4 + 2/√x > 0.\nSince dy/dx is strictly positive throughout the domain it is never zero, so the curve has no stationary points.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::5(a)": "This is an arithmetic sequence with first term 10 and common difference 4.\nNumber of lengths on day n = 10 + 4(n − 1) = 4n + 6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::5(b)(i)": "Distance on day n = 25(4n + 6) metres. Require 25(4n + 6) ≥ 3000 ⇒ 4n + 6 ≥ 120 ⇒ 4n ≥ 114 ⇒ n ≥ 28.5.\nSince n must be a whole number, the minimum is n = 29.\nZiad needs 29 days of training.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::5(b)(ii)": "Total lengths over the first 29 days = (29/2)[2(10) + (29 − 1)(4)] = (29/2)(20 + 112) = (29/2)(132) = 1914 lengths.\nTotal distance = 1914 × 25 = 47 850 metres.\nSince 47 850 < 50 000, the coach is NOT correct.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::6(a)(i)": "At N = 0, log₁₀ V = 0.057(0) + 1.76 = 1.76, and V = a here, so log₁₀ a = 1.76.\nTherefore a = 10^(1.76) = 57.54… = 57.5 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::6(a)(ii)": "Comparing log₁₀ V = 0.057N + 1.76 with log₁₀(a b^N) = log₁₀ a + N log₁₀ b, the gradient gives log₁₀ b = 0.057.\nSo b = 10^(0.057) = 1.140… = 1.14 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::6(b)": "Each year the value is multiplied by b = 1.14, an increase of b − 1 = 0.14.\nYearly percentage increase = 100(1.14 − 1) = 14%.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::6(c)(i)": "2025 is N = 16 years after 2009.\nV = 57.5 × 1.14^(16) = 57.5 × 8.137… = 467.9 (£ million).\nPredicted average weekly value ≈ £467.9 million (about £467 900 000).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::6(c)(ii)": "N = 16 (2025) is far outside the range of the data (2009–2016), so using the model here is extrapolation.\nReal sales could behave very differently — for example a sudden change due to a pandemic, supply shortages, or a return to in-person shopping — so the prediction may be unreliable.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::7(a)": "h(x) = g(f(x)) = g(√(10 − 2x)) = 1/√(10 − 2x).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::7(b)": "We need 10 − 2x > 0 (so that the square root is defined and non-zero, avoiding division by zero).\n10 − 2x > 0 ⇒ x < 5.\nDomain of h: x < 5.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::7(c)": "Let y = 1/√(10 − 2x). Then √(10 − 2x) = 1/y, so squaring gives 10 − 2x = 1/y².\nRearrange: 2x = 10 − 1/y² ⇒ x = 5 − 1/(2y²).\nInterchanging variables, h⁻¹(x) = 5 − 1/(2x²), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::8(a)": "1/(1 − cos θ) + 1/(1 + cos θ) = [(1 + cos θ) + (1 − cos θ)] / [(1 − cos θ)(1 + cos θ)]\n= 2 / (1 − cos²θ)\n= 2 / sin²θ   (using sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ)\n= 2 cosec²θ.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::8(b)": "From part (a) the equation is 2 cosec²θ = A.\nSince sin²θ ≤ 1, cosec²θ = 1/sin²θ ≥ 1, so 2 cosec²θ ≥ 2.\nThus a real solution exists only when A ≥ 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::8(c)": "Using part (a): 2 cosec²θ = 16 ⇒ cosec²θ = 8.\nSince 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ, cot²θ = 8 − 1 = 7, so cot θ = ±√7.\nθ is obtuse (90° < θ < 180°), where cos θ < 0 and sin θ > 0, so cot θ < 0.\nTherefore cot θ = −√7.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::9(a)": "(1 + x)^(−1/2) = 1 + (−½)x + [(−½)(−3/2)/2!]x² + …\n= 1 − ½x + (3/8)x² + …",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::9(b)": "The binomial expansion of (1 + x)^(−1/2) is only valid for |x| < 1.\nThe student used x = 2, which lies outside this interval, so the series does not converge and the approximation is invalid.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::9(c)": "Put x = −1/4: (1 + x)^(−1/2) = (3/4)^(−1/2) = (4/3)^(1/2) = 2/√3.\nExpansion: 1 − ½(−1/4) + (3/8)(−1/4)² = 1 + 1/8 + (3/8)(1/16) = 1 + 1/8 + 3/128 = 147/128.\nSo 2/√3 ≈ 147/128, giving 1/√3 ≈ ½ × 147/128 = 147/256 = 0.574 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::10(a)": "(a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::10(b)": "Take a negative rational, e.g. a = −2. Its reciprocal is −1/2.\nSum = −2 + (−1/2) = −5/2 = −2.5, which is NOT greater than 2.\nThis counter-example shows Peter is incorrect.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::10(c)": "Assume the opposite: a/b + b/a ≤ 2, with a, b distinct positive numbers.\nMultiplying by ab > 0 gives a² + b² ≤ 2ab, so a² − 2ab + b² ≤ 0, i.e. (a − b)² ≤ 0.\nBut a and b are distinct, so a − b ≠ 0 and therefore (a − b)² > 0 — a contradiction.\nHence the assumption is false and a/b + b/a > 2.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::11": "The decoration hangs in equilibrium, so the tension balances the weight.\nT = mg = 0.2 × g = 0.2g N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::12": "Displacement is the signed area under the velocity–time graph; over 0 ≤ t ≤ 4 it must equal 0.\nThe correct graph starts at v = +5 (t = 0), falls linearly to v = −5 at t = 2, then rises linearly back to v = +5 at t = 4. The positive area (0 to 1) and negative area (1 to 3) and positive area (3 to 4) cancel to give zero net displacement.\nAnswer: the V-shaped graph going from +5 down to −5 and back to +5 (graph 3).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::13(a)": "Taking downward as positive, the ball has u (at M), v = 3u (at N) and acceleration a = g.\nUsing v = u + at: 3u = u + g t.\nSo 2u = g t, giving t = 2u/g seconds.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::13(b)": "Let MN = s. Using v² = u² + 2as with v = 3u, a = g:\n(3u)² = u² + 2g s ⇒ 9u² = u² + 2g s ⇒ 8u² = 2g s ⇒ s = 4u²/g.\nSo the distance MN = 4u²/g. Point N lies above the Earth’s surface, and M is above N, so the height of M above the Earth, h, is greater than MN.\nTherefore h > 4u²/g.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::14": "v = ∫a dt = ∫(3kt² − 2kt + 1) dt = kt³ − kt² + t + c.\nAt t = 0, v = 1, so c = 1: v = kt³ − kt² + t + 1.\nAt t = 3, v = 10: 10 = k(27) − k(9) + 3 + 1 = 18k + 4.\nSo 18k = 6, giving k = 1/3.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::15": "Mass m = W/g = 0.65/9.8 = 0.06633 kg. Normal reaction R = weight = 0.65 N (horizontal surface).\nFriction = μR = 0.4 × 0.65 = 0.26 N.\nNewton’s 2nd law along the motion: D − 0.26 = m a = 0.06633 × 0.91 = 0.06036.\nD = 0.26 + 0.0604 = 0.320 N.\nSo D ≈ 0.32 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::16": "Resultant force F₁ + F₂ = (1.6 + k)i + (5k − 5)j.\nF = ma: (1.6 + k)i + (5k − 5)j = m(3.2i + 12j).\ni: 1.6 + k = 3.2m;  j: 5k − 5 = 12m.\nDivide: (5k − 5)/(1.6 + k) = 12/3.2 = 3.75 ⇒ 5k − 5 = 3.75(1.6 + k) = 6 + 3.75k.\n1.25k = 11 ⇒ k = 8.8.   (Then m = 3.25.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::17(a)(i)": "Let the plank have mass m kg, weight m g acting at the centre, 3.5 m from P. X is 1.4 m from P; Y is 7 − 2 = 5 m from P. Reaction at X is 4g.\nTaking moments about Y: 4g × (5 − 1.4) = m g × (5 − 3.5)\n4g × 3.6 = m g × 1.5 ⇒ 14.4 = 1.5m ⇒ m = 9.6 kg.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::17(a)(ii)": "Resolving vertically: R_X + R_Y = weight = 9.6g.\n4g + R_Y = 9.6g ⇒ R_Y = 5.6g N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::17(b)": "Now Y is 7 − 1.4 = 5.6 m from P (2.1 m beyond the centre). Take moments about X (1.4 m from P): weight acts 3.5 − 1.4 = 2.1 m from X, support Y is 5.6 − 1.4 = 4.2 m from X.\nR_Y × 4.2 = 9.6g × 2.1 ⇒ R_Y = (9.6g × 2.1)/4.2 = 4.8g N.\nSince 4.8g ≠ 5.6g, the reaction at Y has decreased, so the claim that it remains unchanged is WRONG.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::18(a)": "Speed = |(3i + √3 j)| = √(3² + (√3)²) = √(9 + 3) = √12 = 2√3 m s⁻¹ (≈ 3.46 m s⁻¹).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::18(b)": "A→B velocity (3, √3) makes angle tan⁻¹((√3)/3) = 30° above due east (the i-direction).\nB→C velocity (−3, √3) makes angle tan⁻¹((√3)/3) = 30° above due west, i.e. 150° measured from east.\nThe particle’s direction turns from 30° to 150°, an exterior turn of 120°, so the interior angle of the triangle at B is 180° − 120° = 60°.\nThus the acute angle ABC = 60°.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::18(c)": "The triangle is equilateral and T returns after 9 s, so each of the three equal sides takes 3 s.\nDisplacement from B to C = (time) × (velocity B→C) = 3 × (−3, √3) = (−9, 3√3).\nOC = OB + BC = (1, 0) + (−9, 3√3) = (−8, 3√3).\nPosition vector of C = (−8, 3√3) metres, i.e. −8i + 3√3 j.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::19(a)": "Horizontal component of the string tension = 2 cos 40° = 1.532 N.\nTreating engine + trailer as one system (total mass 2.2 kg), with total resistance 0.8 + R (R = trailer resistance):\n2 cos 40° − (0.8 + R) = 2.2 × 0.06\n1.532 − 0.8 − R = 0.132 ⇒ R = 1.532 − 0.8 − 0.132 = 0.600 N.\nSo the trailer’s total resistance is approximately 0.6 N.   ∎",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::19(b)(i)": "After the string breaks there is no driving force; the only horizontal forces are the resistances 0.8 N (engine) and 0.6 N (trailer), total 1.4 N, decelerating the 2.2 kg system.\nWhole system: −1.4 = 2.2 a ⇒ a = −7/11 m s⁻² (deceleration 7/11).\nFor the trailer (0.7 kg) the rod force T acts forward and resistance 0.6 N backward:\nT − 0.6 = 0.7 a = 0.7 × (−7/11) = −0.4454…\nT = 0.6 − 0.4454 = 0.1545 N = 17/110 N.\nThe tension in the rod is 17/110 N ≈ 0.155 N.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::19(b)(ii)": "Deceleration a = −7/11 m s⁻², initial speed u = 0.5 m s⁻¹, final speed v = 0.\nUsing v² = u² + 2a h: 0 = 0.5² + 2(−7/11)h ⇒ (14/11)h = 0.25 ⇒ h = 0.25 × 11/14 = 11/56.\nh = 11/56 ≈ 0.20 m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::19(c)": "A suitable modelling assumption about the rod is that the rod is horizontal (equivalently, the rod is rigid / light), so the tension acts along the line of motion.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p2::20": "Resolve the throw: horizontal u_H = 14 cos 60° = 7 m s⁻¹; vertical u_V = 14 sin 60° = 7√3 m s⁻¹.\nVertical motion of the ball (up positive), caught 0.3 m up from a release height 1.8 m so net vertical displacement s = 0.3 − 1.8 = −1.5 m, a = −g = −9.8:\n−1.5 = 7√3 t − 4.9 t² ⇒ 4.9 t² − 12.124 t − 1.5 = 0 ⇒ t = 2.592 s (taking the positive root).\nHorizontal distance the ball travels = u_H × t = 7 × 2.592 = 18.144 m, which is where the dog catches it.\nThe dog starts 0.2 s later, from rest (u = 0), so its running time is t − 0.2 = 2.392 s, covering the same 18.144 m:\n18.144 = ½ a (2.392)² = 2.86083 a ⇒ a = 18.144 / 2.86083 = 6.3.\nThe dog’s acceleration a = 6.3 m s⁻².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::1": "The standard graph y = |x| has its vertex at the origin. The vertex here is at (2, −3): it has moved 2 to the right (replace x by x−2) and 3 down (subtract 3). So the equation is y = |x − 2| − 3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::2": "The trapezium rule joins points on the curve by straight chords. It overestimates the area when the curve is convex (bends upwards), because then the curve lies below its chords and the trapezia stick out above it. That is the second graph (the curve that increases and bends upwards, like exponential growth).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::3": "f′(3) = 0 means the tangent to the curve at (3, 7) is horizontal. The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so the normal is vertical. A vertical line through (3, 7) is x = 3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::4": "(5 − ³√x)/x² = 5/x² − x^(1/3)/x² = 5x⁻² − x^(1/3 − 2) = 5x⁻² − x^(−5/3).\nComparing with 5x^(p) − x^(q): p = −2 and q = −5/3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::5": "y = 3e^(2x), so dy/dx = 3 × 2e^(2x) = 6e^(2x) = 2(3e^(2x)) = 2y.\nWhen y = 10, dy/dx = 2 × 10 = 20.\nThe gradient is 20.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::6(a)": "y = x²(2x + a) = 2x³ + ax². This is a positive cubic. Roots: x²(2x + a) = 0 gives a repeated root at x = 0 (so the curve touches the x-axis at the origin, which is a turning point) and a single root at 2x + a = 0, i.e. x = −a/2 (which is negative since a > 0). \nSketch: a positive cubic that comes up from bottom-left, crosses the x-axis at x = −a/2, rises to a local maximum, comes back down to touch the x-axis at the origin (local minimum at (0,0)), then rises steeply to the top-right.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::6(b)(i)": "p(x) = x²(2x + a) + 36. Since (x + 3) is a factor, by the factor theorem p(−3) = 0.\np(−3) = (−3)²(2 × (−3) + a) + 36 = 9(−6 + a) + 36 = −54 + 9a + 36 = 9a − 18.\nSetting this to 0: 9a − 18 = 0 ⇒ 9a = 18 ⇒ a = 2.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::6(b)(ii)": "Adding 36 to every y-value moves the whole curve up by 36. So it is a translation by the vector (0, 36) (i.e. 36 units in the positive y-direction, 36j).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::6(b)(iii)": "From part (a), y = x²(2x + 2) has a minimum at the origin (0, 0), touching the x-axis there. From part (b)(ii), adding 36 translates this curve up by 36, so the lowest point is lifted to (0, 36), above the x-axis. The translated curve therefore crosses the x-axis only once — it has exactly one real root, at x = −3 (since x + 3 is the factor).\nWriting x²(2x+2)+36 = (x+3)(2x² + bx + c): the linear factor (x+3) gives the single real root x = −3, so the quadratic factor 2x² + bx + c must have no real roots. Hence its discriminant is negative:\nb² − 4(2)(c) < 0 ⇒ b² − 8c < 0 ⇒ b² < 8c.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::7(a)": "PQ = 5 is a diameter line, so the radius of each sector is r = PQ/2 = 2.5 m. The points P, O, Q lie on a straight line (angle POQ = π). The rhombus has angle ROS = θ at O, so the two sectors (ORP and OQS) together subtend the remaining angle π − θ.\nArea of the two sectors = ½r²(π − θ) = ½(2.5)²(π − θ) = (25/8)(π − θ).\nArea of rhombus OSTR (side r, with angle θ at O) = r² sin θ = (2.5)² sin θ = (25/4) sin θ.\nTotal area A = (25/8)(π − θ) + (25/4) sin θ = (25/8)[(π − θ) + 2 sin θ] = (25/8)(π − θ + 2 sin θ), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::7(b)(i)": "A = (25/8)(π − θ + 2 sin θ).\ndA/dθ = (25/8)(−1 + 2 cos θ).\nFor a stationary value, dA/dθ = 0 ⇒ −1 + 2 cos θ = 0 ⇒ cos θ = ½ ⇒ θ = π/3 (taking the value in the valid range).\nTo confirm it is a maximum: d²A/dθ² = (25/8)(−2 sin θ) = −(25/4) sin θ. At θ = π/3, sin(π/3) = √3/2 > 0, so d²A/dθ² = −(25/4)(√3/2) ≈ −5.41 < 0. Since the second derivative is negative, A has a maximum at θ = π/3.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::7(b)(ii)": "Substitute θ = π/3 into A = (25/8)(π − θ + 2 sin θ):\nA = (25/8)(π − π/3 + 2 sin(π/3)) = (25/8)(2π/3 + 2 × √3/2) = (25/8)(2π/3 + √3) m².\n(Numerically ≈ 11.97 m².)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::7(c)": "Increasing PQ from 5 m to 10 m doubles the radius (from 2.5 m to 5 m). The optimal angle does not depend on the radius, so the answer to (b)(i) is unchanged: the maximum still occurs at θ = π/3.\nArea scales with the square of the radius, so doubling the radius multiplies the area by 4. The answer to (b)(ii) is therefore quadrupled: A_max = (25/2)(2π/3 + √3) m².",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::8": "Let u = x⁵ + 2. Then du/dx = 5x⁴, so du = 5x⁴ dx, i.e. dx = du/(5x⁴).\nLimits: when x = 0, u = 2; when x = 1, u = 3.\nAlso x⁵ = u − 2, so x⁹ = x⁵ · x⁴ = (u − 2)x⁴.\n∫₀¹ x⁹/(x⁵+2)³ dx = ∫₂³ [(u−2)x⁴ / u³] · [1/(5x⁴)] du = (1/5)∫₂³ (u − 2)/u³ du\n= (1/5)∫₂³ (u⁻² − 2u⁻³) du = (1/5)[ −u⁻¹ + u⁻² ]₂³\n= (1/5)[ (−1/3 + 1/9) − (−1/2 + 1/4) ] = (1/5)[ (−2/9) − (−1/4) ] = (1/5)[ −2/9 + 1/4 ]\n= (1/5)[ (−8 + 9)/36 ] = (1/5)(1/36) = 1/180, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::9(a)": "y = t + 2/t.\nAt P (t = 0.2): y = 0.2 + 2/0.2 = 0.2 + 10 = 10.2 m.\nAt Q (t = 3): y = 3 + 2/3 = 11/3 ≈ 3.667 m.\nDifference in height = 10.2 − 11/3 = 10.2 − 3.667 = 6.53 m (3 s.f.).\nSince 6.53 < 7, the difference in height is less than 7 m, so the slide meets the safety requirement.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::9(b)(i)": "x = t − 1/t + 4.8 ⇒ dx/dt = 1 + 1/t² = 1 + t⁻².\ny = t + 2/t ⇒ dy/dt = 1 − 2/t² = 1 − 2t⁻².\ndy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = (1 − 2t⁻²)/(1 + t⁻²).\n(Multiplying top and bottom by t²: dy/dx = (t² − 2)/(t² + 1).)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::9(b)(ii)": "R is the lowest point of the curve, where dy/dx = 0 (equivalently dy/dt = 0).\n1 − 2t⁻² = 0 ⇒ t² = 2 ⇒ t = √2 (positive value in range).\nThe height at R is y = t + 2/t = √2 + 2/√2 = √2 + √2 = 2√2.\nRS is the vertical distance from R down to the ground, so RS = y = 2√2 ≈ 2.83 m.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::9(b)(iii)": "The gradient of the slide is dy/dx = (t² − 2)/(t² + 1). At Q, t = 3:\ndy/dx = (9 − 2)/(9 + 1) = 7/10 = 0.7.\nThe acute angle with the horizontal: tan α = |0.7| = 0.7 ⇒ α = arctan(0.7) = 34.99° ≈ 35° (nearest degree).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::10": "A product moment correlation coefficient r must satisfy −1 ≤ r ≤ 1. The value −6/5 = −1.2 lies outside this interval, so it is not a possible value.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::11": "For mutually exclusive events A and B, the events cannot both happen, so their intersection is empty. Therefore P(A ∩ B) = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(a)": "One valid assumption (in context): the probability that any given driver passes the test is constant at 0.4 (and the same for every driver). Equivalently, the outcomes of the 32 drivers are independent of one another.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(b)": "X ~ B(32, 0.4). P(X = 7) = C(32,7)(0.4)⁷(0.6)²⁵ = 0.0157 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(c)": "X ~ B(32, 0.4). P(X ≤ 16) = 0.908 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(d)": "P(X > 12) = 1 − P(X ≤ 12) = 1 − 0.4618 = 0.538 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(e)": "Mean = np = 32 × 0.4 = 12.8 drivers.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::12(f)": "Variance = np(1 − p) = 32 × 0.4 × 0.6 = 7.68.\nStandard deviation = √7.68 = 8√3/5 ≈ 2.77.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::13(a)": "Because the first coin is replaced, the two draws are independent with P(bronze) = 0.2 and P(silver) = 0.8.\nP(both bronze) = 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.04.\nP(both silver) = 0.8 × 0.8 = 0.64.\nP(both the same type) = 0.04 + 0.64 = 0.68.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::13(b)": "P(both bronze) = 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.04.\nP(at least one bronze) = 1 − P(no bronze) = 1 − 0.8 × 0.8 = 1 − 0.64 = 0.36.\nP(both bronze | at least one bronze) = P(both bronze)/P(at least one bronze) = 0.04/0.36 = 1/9 ≈ 0.111.\n(Both coins bronze automatically means at least one is bronze, so the numerator is just P(both bronze).)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::14(a)": "The mass is modelled by a continuous (normal) distribution, and for any continuous distribution the probability of taking one exact value is 0. So P(mass = 24 500) = 0, and therefore P(mass ≠ 24 500) = 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::14(b)": "H₀: μ = 24 500; H₁: μ ≠ 24 500 (two-tailed test at the 5% level).\nSample mean: X̄ = 641 520 / 24 = 26 730 kg.\nUnder H₀, X̄ ~ N(24 500, 5200²/24), so the standard error is 5200/√24 = 1061.4.\nTest statistic: z = (26 730 − 24 500)/1061.4 = 2.101.\nThis is a two-tailed test, so compare with z = 1.96 (5% level): 2.101 > 1.96. (Equivalently P(X̄ ≥ 26 730) = 0.0178 < 0.025.)\nSince the test statistic falls in the critical region, reject H₀.\nConclusion: there is sufficient evidence, at the 5% level, to suggest that the mean daily mass of aluminium cans recycled has changed.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::14(c)": "The claim is not justified. A different random sample of 24 days would in general have a different total mass and hence a different sample mean. That different sample mean could fall on the other side of the critical value, so the hypothesis test might lead to a different conclusion. We cannot be certain that the result would be the same, so the claim is not valid (the outcome is not definite).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::15(a)(i)": "Interquartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile = 1570 − 1167 = 403.\nLower limit = median − 1.5 × IQR = 1393 − 1.5 × 403 = 1393 − 604.5 = 788.5.\nUpper limit = median + 1.5 × IQR = 1393 + 1.5 × 403 = 1393 + 604.5 = 1997.5.\nSo masses must lie in [788.5, 1997.5]. Checking the eight masses: 950, 989, 1247, 1415, 1506, 1680, 1833 are all inside this interval; only 2040 lies outside (2040 > 1997.5). Hence exactly one mass (2040) should be removed.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::15(a)(ii)": "The mass 2040 kg is an outlier.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::15(b)": "The probabilities must sum to 1:\n0.14 + 0.37 + 0.9k + 0.25 + 0.4k + 1.7k + 0 = 1\n0.76 + 3k = 1 ⇒ 3k = 0.24 ⇒ k = 0.08.\nP(1 ≤ N < 5) = P(N=1) + P(N=2) + P(N=3) + P(N=4) = 0.37 + 0.9k + 0.25 + 0.4k = 0.62 + 1.3k.\n= 0.62 + 1.3 × 0.08 = 0.62 + 0.104 = 0.724.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::15(c)(i)": "The Large Data Set contains cars from two different years and a fixed number of makes (5 makes). To take a quota sample of 200 cars reflecting the different years and makes, split the sample evenly: choose 100 cars from each of the two years, and within each year choose 20 cars of each of the 5 makes (5 × 20 = 100 per year). This gives 2 × 5 × 20 = 200 cars, with the year and make proportions reflecting the structure of the LDS.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::15(c)(ii)": "A disadvantage of quota sampling is that it is non-random: the cars within each quota are not selected randomly, so the sample can be biased and may not be representative of the whole population.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::16(a)(i)": "D ~ N(6.5, 0.73²). Standardise: z = (5.2 − 6.5)/0.73 = −1.781.\nP(D < 5.2) = P(Z < −1.781) = 0.0375 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::16(a)(ii)": "z = (7 − 6.5)/0.73 = 0.6849.\nP(D > 7) = P(Z > 0.6849) = 0.2467 (4 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::16(a)(iii)": "P(5 < D < 8) = P(D < 8) − P(D < 5).\nz₁ = (5 − 6.5)/0.73 = −2.055, z₂ = (8 − 6.5)/0.73 = 2.055.\nP(5 < D < 8) = Φ(2.055) − Φ(−2.055) = 0.9601 (4 s.f.).\nSo about 96.0% of apples are accepted.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::16(b)": "Let the diameter be N(μ, σ²).\n60% have diameter less than 5.9: P(D < 5.9) = 0.6 ⇒ (5.9 − μ)/σ = z where Φ(z) = 0.6 ⇒ z = 0.2533. So (5.9 − μ)/σ = 0.2533 … (1)\n20% have diameter greater than 6.1: P(D > 6.1) = 0.2 ⇒ P(D < 6.1) = 0.8 ⇒ (6.1 − μ)/σ = 0.8416 … (2)\nFrom (1): 5.9 − μ = 0.2533σ. From (2): 6.1 − μ = 0.8416σ.\nSubtract (1) from (2): 0.2 = (0.8416 − 0.2533)σ = 0.5883σ ⇒ σ = 0.34 (2 s.f.).\nThen μ = 5.9 − 0.2533 × 0.34 = 5.9 − 0.0861 = 5.81 (3 s.f.).\nMean μ ≈ 5.81 cm, standard deviation σ ≈ 0.34 cm.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2023-p3::17": "Let p = proportion of new local businesses making a profit in their first year.\nH₀: p = 0.7; H₁: p > 0.7 (one-tailed test at the 2.5% level).\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(25, 0.7), where X = number making a profit. Observed value: X = 21.\nP(X ≥ 21) = 1 − P(X ≤ 20) = 1 − 0.9095 = 0.0905.\nSince 0.0905 > 0.025, the result is not in the critical region (the critical region is X ≥ 23, as P(X ≥ 23) = 0.00896 < 0.025 while P(X ≥ 22) = 0.0334 > 0.025).\nTherefore do not reject H₀.\nConclusion: there is insufficient evidence, at the 2.5% level, to suggest an increase in the proportion of new local businesses making a profit in their first year.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::1": "Since (x − 3) is a factor, g(3) = 0.\ng(3) = 3(3)³ − 20(3)² + (k + 17)(3) + k = 81 − 180 + 3k + 51 + k\n= −48 + 4k = 0\n⇒ 4k = 48 ⇒ k = 12.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::2(a)": "(1 − 9x)^(½) = 1 + (½)(−9x) + [(½)(−½)/2!](−9x)² + [(½)(−½)(−3/2)/3!](−9x)³ + …\n= 1 − (9/2)x + (−1/8)(81x²) + (1/16)(−729x³) + …\n= 1 − (9/2)x − (81/8)x² − (729/16)x³ + …",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::2(b)": "The expansion of (1 − 9x)^(½) is only valid for |9x| < 1, i.e. |x| < 1/9 (−1/9 < x < 1/9).\nThe value x = −2/9 lies outside this interval (since 2/9 > 1/9), so the series does not converge there and cannot be used to approximate √3.\n(Putting x = −2/9 gives (1 − 9(−2/9))^(½) = (1 + 2)^(½) = √3, but x = −2/9 is outside the range of validity.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::3(a)": "f(x) = x + tan(½x). Working in radians:\nf(3.6) = 3.6 + tan(1.8) = 3.6 − 4.286… = −0.686… < 0\nf(3.7) = 3.7 + tan(1.85) = 3.7 − 3.489… = 0.211… > 0\nf is continuous on (π, 3π/2) and there is a change of sign between x = 3.6 and x = 3.7, so the root α lies in the interval [3.6, 3.7].",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::3(b)": "f(x) = x + tan(½x)\nf'(x) = 1 + ½sec²(½x)\n(since d/dx[tan(½x)] = ½sec²(½x)).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::3(c)": "Newton–Raphson: x₁ = 3.7 − f(3.7)/f'(3.7).\nf(3.7) = 3.7 + tan(1.85) = 0.2110…\nf'(3.7) = 1 + ½sec²(1.85) = 1 + ½·(1/cos²1.85) = 7.58…\nx₁ = 3.7 − 0.2110/7.58 = 3.7 − 0.0278… = 3.672 (3 d.p.).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::4": "Let y = x². For a small change h in x:\ngradient of chord = [(x + h)² − x²]/h\n= [x² + 2xh + h² − x²]/h\n= [2xh + h²]/h\n= 2x + h.\nAs h → 0, this tends to the gradient of the tangent:\ndy/dx = lim(h→0)(2x + h) = 2x.\nHence dy/dx = 2x.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::5(a)": "f(x) = (2x − 3)/(x² + 4). Using the quotient rule with u = 2x − 3 (u' = 2) and v = x² + 4 (v' = 2x):\nf'(x) = [2(x² + 4) − (2x − 3)(2x)] / (x² + 4)²\n= [2x² + 8 − (4x² − 6x)] / (x² + 4)²\n= (−2x² + 6x + 8) / (x² + 4)².\nSo a = −2, b = 6, c = 8.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::5(b)": "f is decreasing where f'(x) < 0. The denominator (x² + 4)² > 0, so we need the numerator negative:\n−2x² + 6x + 8 < 0\n⇒ x² − 3x − 4 > 0  (dividing by −2, reversing the inequality)\n⇒ (x − 4)(x + 1) > 0.\nThe critical values are x = −1 and x = 4, and the expression is positive outside the roots.\nHence f is decreasing for x < −1 or x > 4.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::6(a)": "For y = 3|x − 2| + 5, the modulus term |x − 2| has its minimum value 0 when x = 2, giving y = 5.\nThe vertex is therefore P(2, 5).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::6(b)": "Solve 16 − 4x = 3|x − 2| + 5.\nRight branch (x ⩾ 2): 16 − 4x = 3(x − 2) + 5 = 3x − 1 ⇒ 17 = 7x ⇒ x = 17/7 ≈ 2.43 (valid, since 17/7 > 2).\nLeft branch (x < 2): 16 − 4x = 3(2 − x) + 5 = 11 − 3x ⇒ 5 = x, but x = 5 is not < 2, so reject.\nThe only solution is x = 17/7.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::6(c)": "The line l: y = kx + 4 passes through (0, 4), which is below the vertex P(2, 5).\nThe two \"limiting\" gradients give a single (boundary) intersection:\n• Through P(2, 5): 5 = 2k + 4 ⇒ k = ½.\n• Parallel to the right branch (gradient 3): k = 3.\nFor l to meet the graph at 2 distinct points, k must lie strictly between these: ½ < k < 3.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::7(a)": "dH/dt = −0.12e^(−0.2t).\nIntegrating: H = −0.12 · (1/−0.2)e^(−0.2t) + c = 0.6e^(−0.2t) + c.\nInitially the tank is full: at t = 0, H = 1.5:\n1.5 = 0.6e⁰ + c = 0.6 + c ⇒ c = 0.9.\nHence H = 0.6e^(−0.2t) + 0.9, so A = 0.6 and B = 0.9.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::7(b)": "Set H = 1.2:\n1.2 = 0.6e^(−0.2t) + 0.9 ⇒ 0.6e^(−0.2t) = 0.3 ⇒ e^(−0.2t) = ½.\n−0.2t = ln(½) ⇒ t = −5ln(½) = 5ln2 = 3.4657… hours.\n0.4657 × 60 ≈ 28 min.\nSo the depth reaches 1.2 m after 3 hours 28 minutes.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::7(c)": "As t → ∞, e^(−0.2t) → 0, so H → 0.9.\nThe water level falls to the height of the hole, so the hole is 0.9 m (90 cm) from the bottom of the tank.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::8(a)": "g(2) = 5/(2(2) − 9) = 5/(−5) = −1.\nfg(2) = f(−1) = 4 − 3(−1)² = 4 − 3 = 1.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::8(b)": "Let y = 5/(2x − 9).\ny(2x − 9) = 5 ⇒ 2xy − 9y = 5 ⇒ 2xy = 5 + 9y ⇒ x = (5 + 9y)/(2y).\nSwapping variables: g⁻¹(x) = (5 + 9x)/(2x),  x ≠ 0.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::8(c)": "(i) gf(x) = g(4 − 3x²)\n= 5/(2(4 − 3x²) − 9)\n= 5/(8 − 6x² − 9)\n= 5/(−1 − 6x²)\ngf(x) = −5/(1 + 6x²)\n(ii) Since x² ≥ 0 for all real x, 1 + 6x² ≥ 1, with equality when x = 0.\nSo 0 < 5/(1 + 6x²) ≤ 5: it equals 5 when x = 0 and tends to 0 (but never reaches it) as x gets large.\nTherefore −5 ≤ gf(x) < 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::8(d)": "f(x) = h(x): 4 − 3x² = 2x² − 6x + k\n⇒ 0 = 5x² − 6x + (k − 4).\nFor no real solutions, discriminant < 0:\n(−6)² − 4(5)(k − 4) < 0\n36 − 20k + 80 < 0\n116 < 20k\nk > 116/20 = 5.8.\nSo k > 5.8.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::9(a)": "For a geometric sequence the common ratio is constant:\n9^(7−2k) / 3^(4k−5) = 3^(2(k−1)) / 9^(7−2k).\nWrite 9 = 3²: 3^(14−4k) / 3^(4k−5) = 3^(2k−2) / 3^(14−4k).\n3^(14−4k−(4k−5)) = 3^((2k−2)−(14−4k))\n3^(19−8k) = 3^(6k−16).\nEqual bases ⇒ 19 − 8k = 6k − 16 ⇒ 35 = 14k ⇒ k = 35/14 = 5/2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::9(b)": "With k = 5/2:\nfirst term a = 3^(4(5/2)−5) = 3^(10−5) = 3⁵ = 243.\nsecond term = 9^(7−5) = 9² = 81, so r = 81/243 = 1/3.\nSince |r| = 1/3 < 1, S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 243/(1 − 1/3) = 243/(2/3) = 729/2 = 364.5.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::10(a)": "The curve crosses the x-axis where y = 0:\n8x − x^(5/2) = 0 ⇒ x(8 − x^(3/2)) = 0.\nFor A (x ≠ 0): x^(3/2) = 8 ⇒ x = 8^(2/3) = 4.\nHence the x coordinate of A is 4. (Check: y = 8(4) − 4^(5/2) = 32 − 32 = 0.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::10(b)": "y = 8x − x^(5/2) ⇒ dy/dx = 8 − (5/2)x^(3/2).\nAt A (x = 4): gradient = 8 − (5/2)(4^(3/2)) = 8 − (5/2)(8) = 8 − 20 = −12.\nTangent at A(4, 0): y − 0 = −12(x − 4) ⇒ y = −12x + 48 ⇒ 12x + y = 48.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::10(c)": "l₁: 12x + y = 48 and l₂: y = 8x meet where 12x + 8x = 48 ⇒ x = 2.4, y = 19.2.\nl₂ meets the curve at O(0, 0) and l₁ meets the curve at A(4, 0). The curve lies below both lines between x = 0 and x = 4, so R is the triangle O, (2.4, 19.2), A minus the area under the curve from x = 0 to x = 4.\nTriangle: base OA = 4, height 19.2\nArea = ½ × 4 × 19.2 = 38.4 (= 192/5).\nArea under the curve:\n∫(8x − x^(5/2)) dx = 4x² − (2/7)x^(7/2)\n∫₀⁴ (8x − x^(5/2)) dx = [4x² − (2/7)x^(7/2)]₀⁴\n= (4 × 16 − (2/7) × 128) − 0\n= 64 − 256/7\n= 192/7.\nArea of R = 38.4 − 192/7 = 192/5 − 192/7 = 192(7 − 5)/35 = 384/35.\nThe exact area of R is 384/35.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::11": "OA = OB = 5 cm (radii of the semicircle, centre O) and AB = 5 cm (radius of the arc OB, centre A).\nSo triangle OAB is equilateral and angle BAO = angle AOB = π/3.\nHence angle BOC = π − π/3 = 2π/3.\nThe arc OB (centre A) bulges into the sector BOC, so\nArea of R = (sector BOC, centre O) − (segment between chord OB and arc OB).\nSector BOC = ½ × 5² × 2π/3 = 25π/3.\nSegment = (sector OAB, centre A) − (triangle OAB)\n = ½ × 5² × π/3 − ½ × 5² × sin(π/3)\n = 25π/6 − 25√3/4.\nArea of R = 25π/3 − (25π/6 − 25√3/4)\n = 25π/3 − 25π/6 + 25√3/4\n = 25π/6 + 25√3/4.\nArea of R = ((25/4)√3 + (25/6)π) cm², so a = 25/4 and b = 25/6.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::12(a)": "K = √(140² + 480²) = √(19600 + 230400) = √250000 = 500.\ntanα = 480/140 = 24/7 ⇒ α = 73.7397…° = 73.74° (2 d.p.).\nSo 140cosθ − 480sinθ = 500cos(θ + 73.74°).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::12(b)": "(i) From part (a), 140cos(30t)° − 480sin(30t)° = 500cos(30t + 73.74)°, so\nR = A + 500cos(30t + 73.74)°\nThe maximum of cos is 1, so the maximum number of rabbits is A + 500.\nA + 500 = 1500 ⇒ A = 1000\nR = 1000 + 500cos(30t + 73.74)°\n(equivalently R = 1000 + 140cos(30t)° − 480sin(30t)°)\n(ii) The minimum of cos is −1, so the minimum number of rabbits is\nR_min = 1000 − 500 = 500",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::12(c)": "Middle of April corresponds to t = 3.5 months.\nR = 1000 + 500cos(30(3.5) + 73.74)° = 1000 + 500cos(178.74°) = 1000 + 500(−0.99975…) ≈ 500.1.\nThis is essentially the minimum value (500), so the model agrees with the actual minimum occurring in mid-April.\nHence the model is valid / reliable / appropriate for the number of rabbits.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::12(d)": "F = 100 + 70sin(30t + 70)° is minimum when sin(30t + 70)° = −1, i.e. 30t + 70 = 270 ⇒ 30t = 200 ⇒ t = 200/30 = 20/3 ≈ 6.667 months.\nNumber of rabbits at this time:\nR = 1000 + 500cos(30(20/3) + 73.74)° = 1000 + 500cos(200 + 73.74)° = 1000 + 500cos(273.74°)\n= 1000 + 500(0.06525…) ≈ 1032.6, i.e. R ≈ 1032 (or 1033) rabbits.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::13(a)": "Let x = a·sin²θ. Then dx/dθ = 2a·sinθcosθ, so dx = 2a·sinθcosθ dθ.\nLimits: x = 0 ⇒ θ = 0; x = a ⇒ sin²θ = 1 ⇒ θ = π/2.\nx^(½) = (a·sin²θ)^(½) = √a·sinθ (sinθ ⩾ 0 on [0, π/2]).\n√(a − x) = √(a − a·sin²θ) = √a·√(cos²θ) = √a·cosθ.\nSo ∫₀ᵃ x^(½)√(a − x) dx = ∫₀^(π/2) (√a·sinθ)(√a·cosθ)(2a·sinθcosθ) dθ\n= 2a² ∫₀^(π/2) sin²θcos²θ dθ.\nUsing sinθcosθ = ½sin2θ ⇒ sin²θcos²θ = ¼sin²2θ:\n= 2a²·¼ ∫₀^(π/2) sin²2θ dθ = ½a² ∫₀^(π/2) sin²2θ dθ.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::13(b)": "Use sin²2θ = (1 − cos4θ)/2:\n½a² ∫₀^(π/2) sin²2θ dθ = ½a² · ½ ∫₀^(π/2) (1 − cos4θ) dθ\n= ¼a² [θ − (sin4θ)/4]₀^(π/2)\n= ¼a² [(π/2 − (sin2π)/4) − (0 − 0)]\n= ¼a² (π/2)\n= (1/8)πa².\nHence ∫₀ᵃ x^(½)√(a − x) dx = (1/8)πa², so k = 1/8.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::14(a)": "The rate of increase of radius is inversely proportional to √r:\ndr/dt = k/√r,  where k is a positive constant.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::14(b)": "Find k using the data at t = 10: dr/dt = 0.9 when r = 16:\n0.9 = k/√16 = k/4 ⇒ k = 3.6.\nSo dr/dt = 3.6/√r. Separate variables:\n∫ √r dr = ∫ 3.6 dt ⇒ (2/3)r^(3/2) = 3.6t + c ⇒ r^(3/2) = 5.4t + (3/2)c.\nUse r = 16 at t = 10: 16^(3/2) = 64 = 5.4(10) + C ⇒ 64 = 54 + C ⇒ C = 10.\nHence r^(3/2) = 5.4t + 10.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::14(c)": "At t = 20: r^(3/2) = 5.4(20) + 10 = 108 + 10 = 118.\nr = 118^(2/3) = (118²)^(1/3) = (13924)^(1/3) = 24.10… cm.\nTo the nearest millimetre, r = 24.1 cm = 241 mm.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::14(d)": "A suitable limitation in context, for example: the model has no upper bound (r increases without limit as t increases), which is unrealistic — in reality the balloon would burst, OR a real balloon is not perfectly spherical.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::15(i)": "Complete the square:\nk² − 4k + 5 = (k² − 4k + 4) + 1 = (k − 2)² + 1.\nSince (k − 2)² ⩾ 0 for all real k, we have (k − 2)² + 1 ⩾ 1 > 0.\nHence k² − 4k + 5 is positive for all real values of k.\n(Equivalently, the discriminant is 16 − 20 = −4 < 0 with positive leading coefficient, so the quadratic is always positive.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p1::15(ii)": "Assume there are positive integers x and y with (3x + 2y)(2x − 5y) = 28.\nSince x and y are positive integers, 3x + 2y is an integer with 3x + 2y ≥ 3(1) + 2(1) = 5, and 2x − 5y is an integer.\nThe product is positive and 3x + 2y > 0, so 2x − 5y > 0 as well. So 3x + 2y and 2x − 5y are a pair of positive factors of 28 with 3x + 2y ≥ 5.\nThe positive factor pairs of 28 are 1 × 28, 2 × 14, 4 × 7, 7 × 4, 14 × 2, 28 × 1, so 3x + 2y must be 7, 14 or 28 (the cases 3x + 2y = 1, 2 or 4 are impossible because 3x + 2y ≥ 5).\nSolve each case:\n• 3x + 2y = 14, 2x − 5y = 2 ⇒ x = 74/19, y = 22/19: not integers.\n• 3x + 2y = 7, 2x − 5y = 4: 5 × first + 2 × second gives 19x = 43, so x = 43/19, and then 2y = 7 − 129/19 = 4/19, so y = 2/19: not integers.\n• 3x + 2y = 28, 2x − 5y = 1: 5 × first + 2 × second gives 19x = 142, so x = 142/19, and then 2y = 28 − 426/19 = 106/19, so y = 53/19: not integers.\nEvery possible case gives values of x and y that are not integers, which contradicts the assumption.\nHence there are no positive integers x and y such that (3x + 2y)(2x − 5y) = 28.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::1(a)": "By the cosine rule in triangle ABC with the included angle BAC = 30°:\n BC² = AB² + AC² − 2·AB·AC·cos(BAC)\n   = 6² + 15² − 2 × 6 × 15 × cos 30°\n   = 36 + 225 − 180 × (√3/2)\n   = 261 − 155.885 = 105.115.\nSo BC = √105.115 = 10.25 cm, i.e. BC = 10.3 cm (to 3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::1(b)": "Work in triangle ABD, where AB = 6, BD = 4 and angle BAD = angle BAC = 30°.\nBy the sine rule: sin(ADB)/AB = sin(BAD)/BD, i.e. sin(ADB)/6 = sin 30°/4.\nSo sin(ADB) = 6 × 0.5/4 = 0.75.\nHence ADB = 48.59° or ADB = 180° − 48.59° = 131.41°.\nThe possible values of the angle ADB are 48.6° and 131°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::2(a)(i)": "Combine over a common denominator (3 − 2√x)(3 + 2√x) = 9 − 4x:\n 1/(3 − 2√x) + 1/(3 + 2√x) = [(3 + 2√x) + (3 − 2√x)]/(9 − 4x)\n   = 6/(9 − 4x).\nSo it is of the form a/(b + cx) with a = 6, b = 9, c = −4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::2(a)(ii)": "Using part (i), the equation becomes 6/(9 − 4x) = 2.\nThen 6 = 2(9 − 4x) = 18 − 8x, so 8x = 12, giving x = 3/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::2(b)": "Let u = 2^y. Then 2^(2y) = (2^y)² = u², so the equation becomes\nu² − 7u − 8 = 0.\nFactorising: (u − 8)(u + 1) = 0, so u = 8 or u = −1.\nThat is, 2^y = 8 or 2^y = −1.\n2^y = 8 ⇒ y = log₂8 = 3.\n2^y = −1 has no solutions, because 2^y > 0 for all real y, so this root is rejected.\nHence the only solution is y = 3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::3(a)": "f(x) = x² + 2x.\nf(x + h) − f(x) = [(x + h)² + 2(x + h)] − [x² + 2x]\n   = x² + 2xh + h² + 2x + 2h − x² − 2x\n   = 2xh + h² + 2h.\nDividing by h: [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h = 2x + h + 2.\nTaking the limit as h → 0: f′(x) = lim (h → 0)(2x + h + 2) = 2x + 2.\nHence f′(x) = 2x + 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::3(b)": "Integrating dy/dx = 2x + 2: y = x² + 2x + c.\nThe curve passes through (−1, 5): 5 = (−1)² + 2(−1) + c = 1 − 2 + c = c − 1, so c = 6.\nHence the equation of the curve is y = x² + 2x + 6.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::4(a)": "A = i + j and B = 3i + 5j, so AB = B − A = 2i + 4j.\nLength AB = √(2² + 4²) = √(4 + 16) = √20 = 2√5 (≈ 4.47).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::4(b)": "C = pi + pj, so BC = C − B = (p − 3)i + (p − 5)j.\n|BC|² = (p − 3)² + (p − 5)². Setting |BC| = |AB|, so |BC|² = 20:\n (p − 3)² + (p − 5)² = 20\n p² − 6p + 9 + p² − 10p + 25 = 20\n 2p² − 16p + 14 = 0 ⇒ p² − 8p + 7 = 0 ⇒ (p − 1)(p − 7) = 0.\nSince p > 1, take p = 7. Hence the position vector of C is 7i + 7j.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::4(c)": "M is the midpoint of AC: M = ½[(i + j) + (7i + 7j)] = ½(8i + 8j) = 4i + 4j.\nVector BM = M − B = (4i + 4j) − (3i + 5j) = i − j.\nVector MD = 2 × BM = 2i − 2j.\nSo D = M + MD = (4i + 4j) + (2i − 2j) = 6i + 2j.\nThe position vector of D is 6i + 2j.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::4(d)": "ABCD is a kite.\nReason: it has two pairs of equal adjacent sides. From parts (a) and (b), AB = CB = 2√5. With D = 6i + 2j (from part (c)), AD = |D − A| = |5i + j| = √26 and CD = |D − C| = |−i − 5j| = √26, so AD = CD. Thus the two pairs of adjacent sides BA = BC and DA = DC are equal, which is the defining property of a kite (equivalently, BD is a line of symmetry of the quadrilateral).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::5(a)(i)": "f(x) = |ax − b| + c. The modulus part is zero at the vertex, so the vertex is at x = b/a. Given the vertex is at (3, 1), we have b/a = 3 and the vertex height gives c = 1.\nThe graph crosses the y-axis at (0, 7): f(0) = |−b| + 1 = b + 1 = 7, so b = 6.\nThen from b/a = 3 we get a = b/3 = 6/3 = 2.\nHence a = 2, b = 6 and c = 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::5(a)(ii)": "f is a many-to-one function: it is not one-to-one (for example f(0) = |−6| + 1 = 7 and f(6) = |12 − 6| + 1 = 7, so f(0) = f(6)). A function only has an inverse if it is one-to-one, so f⁻¹(x) does not exist.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::5(b)(i)": "For x ≥ q/p, px − q ≥ 0, so g(x) = |px − q| = px − q.\nLet y = px − q. Then x = (y + q)/p, so g⁻¹(x) = (x + q)/p.\nSince g maps x ≥ q/p onto values ≥ 0, the range of g (and hence the domain of g⁻¹) is x ≥ 0.\nSo g⁻¹(x) = (x + q)/p, with domain x ≥ 0.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::5(b)(ii)": "The graphs of g(x) = px − q (for x ≥ q/p) and its inverse g⁻¹(x) = (x + q)/p are reflections of one another in the line y = x. They fail to meet when the gradient p of g is less than or equal to 1 (so the line of g is shallower than y = x and lies below it on the relevant domain). Hence the equation g(x) = g⁻¹(x) has no solutions for 0 < p ≤ 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::6(a)": "y = e^(x²+3x). By the chain rule, dy/dx = (2x + 3)e^(x²+3x).\nStationary points where dy/dx = 0: (2x + 3)e^(x²+3x) = 0.\nSince e^(x²+3x) > 0 for all x, we need 2x + 3 = 0, so x = −3/2.\nThe only stationary point has x-coordinate x = −3/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::6(b)": "From dy/dx = (2x + 3)e^(x²+3x), differentiate again using the product rule:\n d²y/dx² = 2·e^(x²+3x) + (2x + 3)·(2x + 3)e^(x²+3x)\n   = [2 + (2x + 3)²]e^(x²+3x).\nA curve is convex where d²y/dx² > 0 for all x in the interval.\nNow (2x + 3)² ≥ 0 for all x, so 2 + (2x + 3)² ≥ 2 > 0, and e^(x²+3x) > 0 for all x.\nTherefore d²y/dx² = [2 + (2x + 3)²]e^(x²+3x) > 0 for every value of x, so the curve is convex for all values of x.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::7(a)": "Start from cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B and replace B with −B:\n cos(A − B) = cos(A + (−B)) = cos A cos(−B) − sin A sin(−B).\nUsing the even/odd properties cos(−B) = cos B and sin(−B) = −sin B:\n cos(A − B) = cos A cos B − sin A(−sin B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B.\nHence cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::7(b)": "With cos 30° = √3/2 and sin 30° = ½:\n cos(θ + 30°) = √3/2 cos θ − ½ sin θ,  cos(θ − 30°) = √3/2 cos θ + ½ sin θ.\nMultiplying (difference of two squares):\n f(θ) = (√3/2 cos θ)² − (½ sin θ)² = ¾ cos²θ − ¼ sin²θ.\nUsing sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ:\n f(θ) = ¾ cos²θ − ¼(1 − cos²θ) = ¾ cos²θ − ¼ + ¼ cos²θ = cos²θ − ¼.\nHence f(θ) = cos²θ − ¼.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::7(c)(i)": "f(θ) = cos²θ − ¼. Since 0 ≤ cos²θ ≤ 1, f is greatest when cos²θ = 1, giving the maximum value 1 − ¼ = ¾.\ncos²θ = 1 means cos θ = ±1, i.e. θ = 0°, 180°, … . The smallest positive value of θ at which this occurs is θ = 180°.\nMaximum value ¾, at θ = 180°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::7(c)(ii)": "f(θ) = cos²θ − ¼ is smallest when cos²θ = 0, giving the minimum value 0 − ¼ = −¼.\ncos²θ = 0 means cos θ = 0, i.e. θ = 90°, 270°, … . The smallest positive value of θ at which this occurs is θ = 90°.\nMinimum value −¼, at θ = 90°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::8(a)": "Take out the factor 4: (4 + 3x)^(3/2) = 4^(3/2)(1 + ¾x)^(3/2) = 8(1 + ¾x)^(3/2).\nExpand (1 + ¾x)^(3/2) using the binomial series with n = 3/2:\n (1 + ¾x)^(3/2) = 1 + (3/2)(¾x) + [(3/2)(1/2)/2!](¾x)² + …\n   = 1 + (9/8)x + (3/8)(9/16)x² + …\n   = 1 + (9/8)x + (27/128)x² + … .\nMultiplying by 8:\n (4 + 3x)^(3/2) = 8 + 9x + (27/16)x² + … .\nThe first three terms are 8 + 9x + (27/16)x².",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::8(b)": "The binomial expansion of (1 + ¾x)^(3/2) is valid when |¾x| < 1, i.e. |x| < 4/3.\nSo the expansion in part (a) is valid for |x| < 4/3 (that is, −4/3 < x < 4/3).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::8(c)": "Using part (a), (4 + 3x)^(3/2) = 8 + 9x + (27/16)x² + … and (1 + ax)² = 1 + 2ax + a²x².\nThe coefficient of x² in the product is:\n (8)(a²) + (9)(2a) + (27/16)(1) = 8a² + 18a + 27/16.\nSet this equal to 107/16:\n 8a² + 18a + 27/16 = 107/16 ⇒ 8a² + 18a − 80/16 = 0 ⇒ 8a² + 18a − 5 = 0.\nFactorising: (2a + 5)(4a − 1) = 0, so a = −5/2 or a = ¼.\nThe possible values are a = −5/2 and a = ¼.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::9(a)": "dB/dF is the rate of change of the number of bees with respect to the number of wildflower plants — i.e. how many extra bees there are per additional wildflower plant at that instant.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::9(b)": "Differentiate each with respect to t:\n dB/dt = d/dt(20 + 2t + cos 3t) = 2 − 3 sin 3t.\n dF/dt = d/dt(50e^(0.1t)) = 5e^(0.1t).\nBy the chain rule, dB/dF = (dB/dt)/(dF/dt) = (2 − 3 sin 3t)/(5e^(0.1t)).\nAt t = 4 (working in radians):\n dB/dF = (2 − 3 sin 12)/(5e^(0.4)) = (2 − 3(−0.53657))/(5 × 1.49182)\n   = (2 + 1.60971)/7.4591 = 3.60971/7.4591 = 0.484 (to 3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::9(c)": "The study takes place over the summer, so for t > 12 weeks the summer is ending: the wildflowers will stop flowering and the bee population cannot keep increasing as the model predicts. The seasonal change means the assumed relationship between bees and plants will no longer hold, so the model is unlikely to be valid for t > 12.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::10(a)": "f(0) = e⁰/(4(0)² − 1) + 2 = 1/(−1) + 2 = 1, and f(1) = e/(4 − 1) + 2 = e/3 + 2 ≈ 2.9.\nBoth f(0) and f(1) are positive, so there is no sign change between x = 0 and x = 1. The sign-change method relies on a change of sign to bracket a root, so it will fail to locate α here (also, there is a discontinuity at x = ½ between these points, so they are not on the same part of the curve).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::10(b)": "f(x) = 0: e^(x)/(4x² − 1) + 2 = 0, so e^(x)/(4x² − 1) = −2.\nMultiply: e^(x) = −2(4x² − 1) = −8x² + 2.\nRearrange for x²: 8x² = 2 − e^(x), so 16x² = 4 − 2e^(x).\nTaking the positive square root (α > 0): 4x = √(4 − 2e^(x)), hence x = ¼√(4 − 2e^(x)).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::10(c)": "Using xₙ₊₁ = ¼√(4 − 2e^(xₙ)) with x₁ = 0.3:\nx₂ = ¼√(4 − 2e^0.3) = 0.28507…\nx₃ = ¼√(4 − 2e^0.28507…) = 0.28943…\nx₄ = 0.28817…\nx₅ = 0.28853…\nx₆ = 0.28843…\nx₇ = 0.28846…\nx₈ = 0.28845…\nx₇ and x₈ both round to 0.2885 to 4 significant figures, so the iteration has converged to this accuracy.\nα = 0.2885 (4 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::10(d)": "F(x) = ln(2 − 8x²). Differentiating (chain rule):\n F′(x) = (1/(2 − 8x²))·(−16x) = −16x/(2 − 8x²).\nAt x = 0.3: F′(0.3) = −16(0.3)/(2 − 8(0.09)) = −4.8/(2 − 0.72) = −4.8/1.28 = −3.75.\nSince |F′(0.3)| = 3.75 > 1, the iteration x_(n+1) = F(x_n) is repelled from a root near 0.3 (the magnitude of the gradient exceeds 1, so successive iterates diverge). Therefore this iterative formula will not converge to α.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::11(a)": "Take logs (base 10) of S = ab^(t):\n log₁₀S = log₁₀(ab^(t)) = log₁₀a + t·log₁₀b.\nWriting Y = log₁₀S and X = t, this is Y = (log₁₀b)X + log₁₀a, which is the equation of a straight line with gradient log₁₀b and intercept log₁₀a.\nThe graph of log₁₀S against t is a straight line (the plotted points are collinear), which is exactly what this model predicts. So the graph suggests that S = ab^(t) is an appropriate model.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::11(b)": "From part (a) the gradient of the line equals log₁₀b and the intercept equals log₁₀a.\nThe gradient of the line of best fit is about 0.06, so log₁₀b ≈ 0.06, giving b = 10^(0.06) = 1.15.\nThe intercept (value of log₁₀S at t = 0) is about 2.08, so log₁₀a ≈ 2.08, giving a = 10^(2.08) ≈ 120.\nThese agree with a = 120 and b = 1.15, so the graph supports the estimates.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::11(c)": "Using S = 120 × 1.15^(t) with t = 7:\n S = 120 × 1.15⁷ = 120 × 2.66002 = 319.2.\nSince S is a number of items, the model predicts about 319 items sold in the seventh month (accept 320).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::11(d)(i)": "The total number of items sold is the sum of the monthly sales 120 × 1.15^(t) for t = 1, 2, 3, …, which is a geometric series with first term a = 120 × 1.15 = 138 and common ratio r = 1.15.\nThe sum of the first t months is S_t = 138(1.15^(t) − 1)/(1.15 − 1).\nRequire S_t > 70 000:\n 138(1.15^(t) − 1)/0.15 = 70 000\n 1.15^(t) − 1 = 70 000 × 0.15/138 = 76.087\n 1.15^(t) = 77.087.\nTaking logs: t = ln 77.087/ln 1.15 = 4.3451/0.13976 = 31.09.\nSince t must be a whole number of months, the total first exceeds 70 000 after 32 months.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::11(d)(ii)": "This prediction is unlikely to be reliable. It relies on the model holding for 32 months, far beyond the 6 months of recorded data, so it is a large extrapolation. In practice a constant 15% monthly growth is unlikely to continue indefinitely — the market may become saturated, demand may fall, or competition may change — so the predicted figure should be treated with caution.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::12(a)": "Let u = e^(x) − 2. Then du/dx = e^(x), so du = e^(x) dx, giving dx = du/e^(x) = du/(u + 2) (since e^(x) = u + 2).\nThe numerator: 7e^(x) − 8 = 7(u + 2) − 8 = 7u + 14 − 8 = 7u + 6.\nThe denominator: (e^(x) − 2)² = u².\nTherefore\n ∫ (7e^(x) − 8)/(e^(x) − 2)² dx = ∫ (7u + 6)/u² · du/(u + 2) = ∫ (7u + 6)/(u²(u + 2)) du,\nas required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p1::12(b)": "Resolve into partial fractions: (7u + 6)/(u²(u + 2)) = A/u + B/u² + C/(u + 2).\nThen 7u + 6 = Au(u + 2) + B(u + 2) + Cu².\n u = 0: 6 = 2B ⇒ B = 3.\n u = −2: −8 = 4C ⇒ C = −2.\n Comparing u² coefficients: 0 = A + C ⇒ A = 2.\nSo (7u + 6)/(u²(u + 2)) = 2/u + 3/u² − 2/(u + 2).\nIntegrate: ∫ (2/u + 3/u² − 2/(u + 2)) du = 2 ln u − 3/u − 2 ln(u + 2) (+ const).\nLimits: when x = ln4, u = e^(ln4) − 2 = 4 − 2 = 2; when x = ln6, u = 6 − 2 = 4.\nEvaluate from u = 2 to u = 4:\n At u = 4: 2 ln 4 − 3/4 − 2 ln 6.\n At u = 2: 2 ln 2 − 3/2 − 2 ln 4.\nDifference = (2 ln 4 − 3/4 − 2 ln 6) − (2 ln 2 − 3/2 − 2 ln 4)\n   = 4 ln 4 − 2 ln 2 − 2 ln 6 + 3/4\n   = 3/4 + ln(4⁴/(2²·6²)) = 3/4 + ln(256/144) = 3/4 + ln(16/9).\nHence the integral equals 3/4 + ln(16/9), so a = 3/4 and b = 16/9.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::1(a)": "Use strip width h = (2 − 0)/4 = 0.5, with x = 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2.\nCompute y = x²e^(−x):\n y₀ = 0\n y₁ = 0.25·e^(−0.5) = 0.151633\n y₂ = 1·e^(−1) = 0.367879\n y₃ = 2.25·e^(−1.5) = 0.502035\n y₄ = 4·e^(−2) = 0.541341\nTrapezium rule:\n Area ≈ ½ × 0.5 × [y₀ + y₄ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)]\n   = 0.25 × [0 + 0.541341 + 2(1.021547)]\n   = 0.25 × 2.584435 = 0.646109\nSo the estimate is 0.646 (to 3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::1(b)": "Use more strips of equal width (a smaller strip width h), i.e. divide [0, 2] into more than 4 trapezia, so the straight tops follow the curve more closely.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::1(c)": "On the part of the curve that is convex (curving upward), the straight tops of the trapezia lie above the curve, so the rule over-estimates there; on the part that is concave (curving downward) the trapezia lie below the curve, so the rule under-estimates there. Because the interval 0 ≤ x ≤ 2 contains a point of inflection, both effects occur, so it cannot be decided from the diagram whether the overall estimate is too big or too small.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::2(a)(i)": "y is inversely proportional to x⁶: y = a/x⁶.\nz is directly proportional to ∛y: z = b·y^(1/3) = b·(a/x⁶)^(1/3) = b·a^(1/3)/x².\nCombining the constants, z = k/x², where k = b·a^(1/3).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::2(a)(ii)": "Since z = k/x² is an even function with z → ∞ as x → 0 and z → 0 as x → ±∞, symmetric about the z-axis, the graph is Fig. 1.1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::2(b)": "Using z = k/x²: when z = 3, x = 4, so 3 = k/16, giving k = 48.\nThus z = 48/x². When z = 12: 12 = 48/x² ⇒ x² = 4 ⇒ x = ±2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::3(a)": "Take the primes 2 and 3. Their product is 2 × 3 = 6, which is even, not odd. This is a counterexample, so the statement \"the product of two prime numbers is always odd\" is false.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::3(b)(i)": "x² = 3x ⇐ x = 3.\n(If x = 3 then x² = 9 = 3×3 = 3x, so the implication holds from right to left. But x² = 3x also allows x = 0, so the forward implication fails; hence ⇐.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::3(b)(ii)": "x > 4 ⇔ x³ > 64.\n(The function x³ is strictly increasing, and 4³ = 64, so x > 4 exactly when x³ > 64; the statement works both ways.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::3(b)(iii)": "x° = 45° ⇒ tan x° = 1.\n(If x = 45 then tan 45° = 1, so the forward implication holds. The reverse fails because tan x° = 1 also for x = 225, etc.; hence ⇒.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::3(c)": "Let the two odd numbers be 2m + 1 and 2n + 1 for integers m, n.\nSum of squares = (2m + 1)² + (2n + 1)²\n   = (4m² + 4m + 1) + (4n² + 4n + 1)\n   = 4m² + 4m + 4n² + 4n + 2\n   = 2(2m² + 2m + 2n² + 2n + 1).\nThe factor 2 shows the sum is a multiple of 2.\nThe bracket 2m² + 2m + 2n² + 2n + 1 is even + 1 = odd, so it is not divisible by 2. Therefore 2 × (odd) is divisible by 2 but not by 4. Hence the sum of the squares of any two odd numbers is always a multiple of 2 but never a multiple of 4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::4(a)": "u₁ = 2.\nu₂ = 1 − 1/u₁ = 1 − 1/2 = 0.5.\nu₃ = 1 − 1/u₂ = 1 − 1/0.5 = 1 − 2 = −1.\nu₄ = 1 − 1/u₃ = 1 − 1/(−1) = 1 + 1 = 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::4(b)": "Since u₄ = u₁ = 2, the sequence repeats: 2, 0.5, −1, 2, 0.5, −1, … . It is periodic with period 3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::4(c)": "The terms repeat in blocks of three: 2, 0.5, −1, which sum to 2 + 0.5 + (−1) = 1.5.\nAfter 47 complete blocks (141 terms) the running total is 47 × 1.5 = 70.5.\nAdding the next term u₁₄₂ = 2 gives 72.5; adding u₁₄₃ = 0.5 gives 73.\nSo Σ (n = 1 to 143) u_(n) = 73, hence k = 143.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::5": "The point (4, 8) lies on y = ax² + b√x:\n  16a + 2b = 8.   …(1)\nDifferentiate: dy/dx = 2ax + b/(2√x).\nAt x = 4: gradient of curve = 8a + b/4.\nThe normal x + 13y = 108 has gradient −1/13, so the tangent gradient there is 13:\n  8a + ¼b = 13.   …(2)\nFrom (1): 8a + b = 4. Subtract this from (2)×1: (8a + ¼b) − (8a + b) = 13 − 4 ⇒ −¾b = 9 ⇒ b = −12.\nThen 8a − 12 = 4 ⇒ 8a = 16 ⇒ a = 2.\nSo a = 2 and b = −12.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::6(a)": "f(¼) = 4(¼)³ − 25(¼)² − 58(¼) + 16\n   = 4(1/64) − 25(1/16) − 58/4 + 16\n   = 1/16 − 25/16 − 232/16 + 256/16\n   = (1 − 25 − 232 + 256)/16 = 0/16 = 0.\nSince f(¼) = 0, x = ¼ is a root of f(x) = 0.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::6(b)": "Since x = ¼ is a root, (4x − 1) is a factor. Dividing:\n  4x³ − 25x² − 58x + 16 = (4x − 1)(x² − 6x − 16).\nCheck: (4x − 1)(x² − 6x − 16) = 4x³ − 24x² − 64x − x² + 6x + 16 = 4x³ − 25x² − 58x + 16. ✓\nSo f(x) = (4x − 1)(x² − 6x − 16).  [The quadratic factorises further as (x − 8)(x + 2).]",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::6(c)": "Let X = e^(y). Then 4e^(3y) − 25e^(2y) − 58e^(y) + 16 = 4X³ − 25X² − 58X + 16 = f(X).\nFrom part (b), f(X) = (4X − 1)(X − 8)(X + 2) = 0, so X = ¼, X = 8 or X = −2.\nSince X = e^(y) > 0, reject X = −2.\n e^(y) = ¼ ⇒ y = ln(¼) = −2 ln 2.\n e^(y) = 8 ⇒ y = ln 8 = 3 ln 2.\nHence y = −2 ln 2 and y = 3 ln 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::7(a)": "Gradient of AB = (10 − 7)/(h − 1) = 3/(h − 1).\nSetting this equal to 2: 3/(h − 1) = 2 ⇒ 3 = 2(h − 1) ⇒ 2h = 5 ⇒ h = 5/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::7(b)": "B = (5/2, 10) is the midpoint of AC, with A = (1, 7).\nIf C = (x, y) then (1 + x)/2 = 5/2 ⇒ x = 4, and (7 + y)/2 = 10 ⇒ y = 13.\nSo C = (4, 13).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::7(c)": "The line through A, B, C has gradient 2 and passes through A(1, 7): y − 7 = 2(x − 1), i.e. y = 2x + 5.\nFor intersections with y = x² − 4x + k:\n  2x + 5 = x² − 4x + k ⇒ x² − 6x + (k − 5) = 0.\nFor two distinct points the discriminant must be positive:\n  (−6)² − 4(k − 5) > 0 ⇒ 36 − 4k + 20 > 0 ⇒ 56 > 4k ⇒ k < 14.\nSo the set of possible values is {k : k < 14}.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::8(a)": "|x| > x holds exactly when x is negative. So the set of values is x < 0, i.e. (−∞, 0).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::8(b)(i)": "With |n| ≤ 9 and n an integer, n ranges over −9, …, 9. |2n − 1| is largest when 2n − 1 is most negative, at n = −9: |2(−9) − 1| = |−19| = 19. Maximum value is 19.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::8(b)(ii)": "|2n − 1| is smallest when 2n − 1 is closest to 0. For integer n, 2n − 1 is always odd, so the smallest possible |2n − 1| is 1 (at n = 0 giving −1, or n = 1 giving 1). Minimum value is 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::8(c)(i)": "|½x − 1| = |2x − 3| means ½x − 1 = ±(2x − 3).\nCase 1: ½x − 1 = 2x − 3 ⇒ 2 = (3/2)x ⇒ x = 4/3.\nCase 2: ½x − 1 = −(2x − 3) = −2x + 3 ⇒ (5/2)x = 4 ⇒ x = 8/5.\nSo x = 4/3 or x = 8/5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::8(c)(ii)": "The right-hand side 2x − 3 has no modulus, so it can only equal |½x − 1| where 2x − 3 ≥ 0, i.e. x ≥ 3/2.\nTest the two candidates from part (c)(i):\n x = 4/3 ≈ 1.33 < 3/2: here 2x − 3 = 2(4/3) − 3 = −1/3 < 0, but |½x − 1| ≥ 0, so this cannot be a solution — reject it.\n x = 8/5 = 1.6 ≥ 3/2: here 2x − 3 = 2(8/5) − 3 = 1/5 ≥ 0, and |½(8/5) − 1| = |−1/5| = 1/5, which matches.\nHence the equation |½x − 1| = 2x − 3 has only one solution, x = 8/5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::9(a)(i)": "d = 1.9 + 1.1 cos(30t − 60)°. The cosine has minimum value −1, so the minimum depth is d = 1.9 − 1.1 = 0.8 metres.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::9(a)(ii)": "The minimum occurs when cos(30t − 60)° = −1, i.e. 30t − 60 = 180 (the first such value).\nThen 30t = 240, so t = 8. The minimum depth first occurs at t = 8 hours after midnight.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::9(b)": "The boat cannot enter when d < 1:\n 1.9 + 1.1 cos(30t − 60)° < 1 ⇒ cos(30t − 60)° < −0.8182.\nSolving cos(30t − 60)° = −0.8182 gives 30t − 60 = ±144.90° (principal solutions, plus multiples of 360°).\n 30t − 60 = 144.90 ⇒ t = 6.830 h ≈ 06:50.\n 30t − 60 = 215.10 ⇒ t = 9.170 h ≈ 09:10.\nBy the 12-hour symmetry (period 12 h), the next interval is 12 h later: about 18:50 to 21:10.\nSo the boat will not be able to enter the river between 06:50 and 09:10, and between 18:50 and 21:10.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::9(c)": "In the new model d = e^(−cp)(1.9 + 1.1 cos(30t − 60)°), the factor e^(−cp) decreases as p (the distance travelled) increases. This scales the whole depth down as the boat goes further, so both the average depth and the size of the tidal variation get smaller along the river. This reflects the real situation in which the river becomes shallower as the boat travels along it, so the model is improved.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::10": "For a geometric progression the ratio of consecutive terms is constant:\n (x + 9)/(2x + 3) = (2x − 6)/(x + 9).\nCross-multiplying: (x + 9)² = (2x + 3)(2x − 6)\n x² + 18x + 81 = 4x² − 6x − 18\n 0 = 3x² − 24x − 99 ⇒ x² − 8x − 33 = 0 ⇒ (x − 11)(x + 3) = 0.\nSo x = 11 or x = −3.\nIf x = −3: terms are −3, 6, −12, with r = −2 — not convergent, so reject.\nIf x = 11: terms are 25, 20, 16, with first term a = 25 and r = 20/25 = 4/5 (|r| < 1, convergent).\nSum to infinity: S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 25/(1 − 4/5) = 25/(1/5) = 125.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::11(a)": "ln(1 − cos 2x) is undefined where 1 − cos 2x ≤ 0, and since 1 − cos 2x ≥ 0 always, it is undefined exactly where 1 − cos 2x = 0, i.e. cos 2x = 1.\nThis gives 2x = 2kπ, so x = kπ for k ∈ ℤ (… , −2π, −π, 0, π, 2π, …).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::11(b)": "y = 5 ln(1 − cos 2x).\ndy/dx = 5 · (2 sin 2x)/(1 − cos 2x) = 10 sin 2x/(1 − cos 2x).\nStationary points where the numerator is zero (and the function is defined): sin 2x = 0 with 1 − cos 2x ≠ 0.\nsin 2x = 0 ⇒ 2x = nπ; the smallest positive x giving a defined point is x = π/2 (since x = 0 is excluded).\nAt x = π/2: 1 − cos π = 1 − (−1) = 2, so y = 5 ln 2.\nHence P = (½π, 5 ln 2).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::11(c)(i)": "From dy/dx = 10 sin 2x/(1 − cos 2x), differentiate by the quotient rule:\n d²y/dx² = [20 cos 2x(1 − cos 2x) − 10 sin 2x(2 sin 2x)]/(1 − cos 2x)²\n   = [20 cos 2x − 20 cos²2x − 20 sin²2x]/(1 − cos 2x)²\n   = [20 cos 2x − 20]/(1 − cos 2x)²   (using sin²2x + cos²2x = 1)\n   = −20(1 − cos 2x)/(1 − cos 2x)² = −20/(1 − cos 2x).\nNow y = 5 ln(1 − cos 2x) gives 1 − cos 2x = e^(y/5), so\n d²y/dx² = −20/e^(y/5) = −20 e^(−(1/5)y).\nTherefore d²y/dx² + 20 e^(−(1/5)y) = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::11(c)(ii)": "Since e^(−(1/5)y) > 0 for all y, we have 20 e^(−(1/5)y) > 0, so d²y/dx² = −20 e^(−(1/5)y) < 0 for every x. As the second derivative is negative at every stationary point, all of the stationary points on the curve are maxima.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::12(a)": "The shaded region is bounded on the right by the curve, on the left by the y-axis, below by the x-axis and above by y = 2, so its area is ∫ x dy between the appropriate y-limits.\nUsing parametric form: area = ∫ x (dy/dt) dt.\nHere x = 2/(2t + 1)⁴ and dy/dt = d/dt(2t² + 3t) = 4t + 3.\nSo area = ∫ [2/(2t + 1)⁴](4t + 3) dt = ∫ (8t + 6)/(2t + 1)⁴ dt.\nLimits (in t): the x-axis corresponds to y = 0, i.e. 2t² + 3t = 0 ⇒ t = 0 (t ≥ 0), so a = 0.\nThe line y = 2 gives 2t² + 3t = 2 ⇒ 2t² + 3t − 2 = 0 ⇒ (2t − 1)(t + 2) = 0 ⇒ t = ½ (rejecting t = −2), so b = ½.\nHence area = ∫₀^(½) (8t + 6)/(2t + 1)⁴ dt, with a = 0 and b = ½.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p1::12(b)": "Substitute u = 2t + 1, so du = 2 dt, i.e. dt = ½ du, and 8t + 6 = 4(2t + 1) + 2 = 4u + 2.\nWhen t = 0, u = 1; when t = ½, u = 2.\nArea = ∫₁² (4u + 2)/u⁴ · ½ du = ½∫₁² (4u⁻³ + 2u⁻⁴) du = ∫₁² (2u⁻³ + u⁻⁴) du.\nIntegrate: ∫(2u⁻³ + u⁻⁴) du = −u⁻² − ⅓u⁻³.\nEvaluate from 1 to 2:\n at u = 2: −1/4 − ⅓·(1/8) = −1/4 − 1/24 = −7/24.\n at u = 1: −1 − 1/3 = −4/3 = −32/24.\nArea = (−7/24) − (−32/24) = 25/24.\nSo the exact area of the shaded region is 25/24.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::1(a)": "Write 3x⁴ − 2/x² = 3x⁴ − 2x⁻². Differentiate term by term: d/dx(3x⁴) = 12x³ and d/dx(−2x⁻²) = +4x⁻³.\nSo the derivative is 12x³ + 4x⁻³ (= 12x³ + 4/x³).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::1(b)": "Write 4√x − 9 = 4x^(½) − 9. Differentiate: d/dx(4x^(½)) = 4 · ½ x^(−½) = 2x^(−½), and the constant −9 differentiates to 0.\nSo the derivative is 2x^(−½) = 2/√x.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::2": "The vector has magnitude 6 at 60° above the positive x-axis, so a = 6cos60° and b = 6sin60°.\ncos60° = ½ and sin60° = √3/2.\nTherefore a = 6 × ½ = 3 and b = 6 × √3/2 = 3√3.\nExact values: a = 3, b = 3√3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::3(a)": "By the factor theorem, (x − 3) is a factor of f(x) iff f(3) = 0.\nf(3) = 3³ − 3² − 5(3) − 3 = 27 − 9 − 15 − 3 = 0.\nSince f(3) = 0, (x − 3) is a factor of f(x).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::3(b)": "Since (x − 3) is a factor, divide: f(x) = (x − 3)(x² + 2x + 1).\nThe quadratic factorises: x² + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)².\nTherefore f(x) = (x − 3)(x + 1)² completely factorised.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::3(c)": "y = (x − a)(x − 1)(x + 1) is a cubic with a positive x³ coefficient, so it rises from bottom left to top right.\nWhatever the value of a, y = 0 when x = 1 and when x = −1, so every correct graph must meet the x-axis at x = 1 and at x = −1. The third root is x = a.\nFig. 1.1: the curve crosses the x-axis at 0, 1 and 2. It does not pass through (−1, 0), so it cannot be a graph of y = (x − a)(x − 1)(x + 1) for any a.\n⇒ No value of a\nFig. 1.2: the curve crosses the x-axis at −1, 1 and 2, with a positive cubic shape. The roots −1 and 1 are always present, so the third root is a = 2, giving y = (x − 2)(x − 1)(x + 1).\n⇒ a = 2\nFig. 1.3: the curve crosses at x = −1 and touches the x-axis at x = 1, so x = 1 is a repeated root. This happens when a = 1, giving y = (x − 1)²(x + 1).\n⇒ a = 1",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::4(a)": "Let g(x) = xe^(1−3x) − 0.2. Evaluate either side of x = 0.79:\ng(0.785) ≈ 0.785·e^(1−2.355) − 0.2 ≈ 0.785·e^(−1.355) − 0.2 ≈ +0.0009 (> 0).\ng(0.795) ≈ 0.795·e^(1−2.385) − 0.2 ≈ 0.795·e^(−1.385) − 0.2 ≈ −0.0009 (< 0).\nThere is a change of sign between x = 0.785 and x = 0.795, so the root lies in this interval and rounds to x = 0.79 (2 d.p.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::4(b)": "y = xe^(1−3x). By the product rule, dy/dx = (1)e^(1−3x) + x(−3)e^(1−3x) = e^(1−3x)(1 − 3x).\nSince e^(1−3x) is never zero, set 1 − 3x = 0, giving x = 1/3.\nSo the exact x-coordinate of the maximum point is x = 1/3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::4(c)": "Area = ∫₀¹ xe^(1−3x) dx. Integrate by parts with u = x, dv = e^(1−3x) dx, so du = dx, v = −(1/3)e^(1−3x).\n∫₀¹ xe^(1−3x) dx = [−(x/3)e^(1−3x)]₀¹ − ∫₀¹ −(1/3)e^(1−3x) dx\n= [−(x/3)e^(1−3x)]₀¹ + (1/3)∫₀¹ e^(1−3x) dx\n= [−(x/3)e^(1−3x)]₀¹ + (1/3)·[−(1/3)e^(1−3x)]₀¹\n= [−(x/3)e^(1−3x) − (1/9)e^(1−3x)]₀¹.\nAt x = 1: −(1/3)e⁻² − (1/9)e⁻². At x = 0: −0 − (1/9)e¹ = −(1/9)e.\nArea = (−(1/3)e⁻² − (1/9)e⁻²) − (−(1/9)e) = (1/9)e − (4/9)e⁻².\nEquivalently, area = (1/9)e − (4/9)e⁻² = (e³ − 4)/(9e²).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::5(a)": "The rate of change of the population with respect to time is dP/dt. The proposed model says the rate of decline per year is (1/80)P². Because the population is declining, the rate of change is negative, so dP/dt = −(1/80)P².",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::5(b)": "Separate variables: ∫ P⁻² dP = ∫ −(1/80) dt.\nIntegrating: −1/P = −t/80 + c.\nAt t = 0, P = 120: −1/120 = c, so c = −1/120.\nThus −1/P = −t/80 − 1/120 ⇒ 1/P = t/80 + 1/120.\nCommon denominator 240: 1/P = 3t/240 + 2/240 = (3t + 2)/240.\nTherefore P = 240/(3t + 2).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::5(c)": "Set P = 10: 10 = 240/(3t + 2) ⇒ 3t + 2 = 24 ⇒ 3t = 22 ⇒ t = 22/3.\nSo it takes t = 22/3 = 7⅓ years (about 7.33 years) for the population to reach 10.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::5(d)": "From P = 240/(3t + 2), as t increases P decreases towards 0 but never equals 0. When t ≥ 160, 3t + 2 ≥ 482, so P = 240/(3t + 2) ≤ 240/482 < 1, i.e. the model predicts a population strictly between 0 and 1. A real population must be a whole number, so a value below 1 means in reality the species would have become extinct (P = 0). Hence the model is not valid for large values of t — it never reaches zero whereas in practice the population would.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::6(a)(i)": "cos 3θ = cos(2θ + θ) = cos2θ cosθ − sin2θ sinθ.\nUsing cos2θ = 2cos²θ − 1 and sin2θ = 2sinθ cosθ:\n= (2cos²θ − 1)cosθ − (2sinθ cosθ)sinθ\n= (2cos²θ − 1)cosθ − 2sin²θ cosθ\n= (2cos²θ − 1)cosθ − 2(1 − cos²θ)cosθ\n= 2cos³θ − cosθ − 2cosθ + 2cos³θ\n= 4cos³θ − 3cosθ. ∎",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::6(a)(ii)": "Since 4cos³θ − 3cosθ = cos 3θ, the equation 4cos³θ − 3cosθ − √2/2 = 0 becomes cos 3θ = √2/2.\nFor 0° ≤ θ ≤ 180° we need 0° ≤ 3θ ≤ 540°. cos⁻¹(√2/2) = 45°, so 3θ = 45°, 315°, 405° (the values in range giving θ in range).\nTherefore θ = 15°, 105°, 135°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::6(b)(i)": "Expand (x + √2/2)(4x² − 2√2x − 1):\n= x·4x² + x·(−2√2x) + x·(−1) + (√2/2)·4x² + (√2/2)·(−2√2x) + (√2/2)·(−1)\n= 4x³ − 2√2x² − x + 2√2x² − 2x − √2/2\n= 4x³ + (−2√2x² + 2√2x²) + (−x − 2x) − √2/2\n= 4x³ − 3x − √2/2. ∎",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::6(b)(ii)": "From (b)(i), 4x³ − 3x − √2/2 = (x + √2/2)(4x² − 2√2x − 1) = 0.\nEither x + √2/2 = 0 ⇒ x = −√2/2,\nor 4x² − 2√2x − 1 = 0 ⇒ x = [2√2 ± √(8 + 16)]/8 = [2√2 ± √24]/8 = [2√2 ± 2√6]/8 = (√2 ± √6)/4.\nThe exact roots are x = −√2/2, x = (√2 + √6)/4 and x = (√2 − √6)/4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::6(c)": "Setting x = cosθ in 4x³ − 3x = √2/2 reproduces 4cos³θ − 3cosθ = √2/2, i.e. cos 3θ = √2/2. So the roots of 4x³ − 3x − √2/2 = 0 are exactly x = cosθ for the solutions θ = 15°, 105°, 135° found in (a)(ii).\nFrom (a)(ii): cos 15° > 0, while cos 105° < 0 and cos 135° < 0.\nFrom (b)(ii) the roots are −√2/2 < 0, (√2 − √6)/4 < 0, and (√2 + √6)/4 > 0.\nMatching the single positive root to the single positive cosine value: cos 15° must equal (√2 + √6)/4.\nTherefore cos 15° = (√2 + √6)/4. ∎",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::7(a)": "A has first term a₁ = 4 and common difference d, so aᵢ = 4 + (i − 1)d and a₁₀₀ = 4 + 99d.\nB has 100 terms with b₁₀₀ = 4 and b₁ = a₁₀₀ = 4 + 99d. The common difference of B is (b₁₀₀ − b₁)/99 = (4 − (4 + 99d))/99 = −d.\nSo bᵢ = b₁ + (i − 1)(−d) = (4 + 99d) − (i − 1)d.\nThe condition bᵢ = 10 + aᵢ gives:\n(4 + 99d) − (i − 1)d = 10 + 4 + (i − 1)d\n99d − (i − 1)d − (i − 1)d = 10\n99d − 2(i − 1)d = 10\n2(i − 1)d = 99d − 10\ni − 1 = 99/2 − 5/d\ni = 101/2 − 5/d. ∎",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::7(b)": "i = 101/2 − 5/d must be a positive integer (a term index). 101/2 = 50.5, so we need 50.5 − 5/d to be a whole number, i.e. 5/d must be a half-odd-integer (…, 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, …).\n5/d = 0.5 gives d = 10; 5/d = 2.5 gives d = 2. Other half-integer values of 5/d give non-integer d (e.g. 5/d = 1.5 ⇒ d = 10/3) which is not allowed since d is a positive integer.\nHence the equation bᵢ = 10 + aᵢ is only possible when d = 2 or d = 10.\n(Check: d = 2 ⇒ i = 50.5 − 2.5 = 48; d = 10 ⇒ i = 50.5 − 0.5 = 50, both valid integers in 1–100.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::8(a)(i)": "X ~ B(15, 0.3). P(X = 4) = (15 choose 4)(0.3)⁴(0.7)¹¹ = 1365 × 0.0081 × 0.0197… = 0.2186… ≈ 0.219 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::8(a)(ii)": "P(X ≥ 4) = 1 − P(X ≤ 3). For X ~ B(15, 0.3), P(X ≤ 3) = 0.2969…, so P(X ≥ 4) = 1 − 0.2969… = 0.7031… ≈ 0.703 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::8(b)(i)": "For X ~ B(15, 0.3) with P(orange) = 0.3 and P(not orange) = 0.7:\nP(X = r) = (15 choose r)(0.7)^(15−r)(0.3)^r, for r = 0, 1, 2, …, 15.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::8(b)(ii)": "The binomial expansion of (0.7 + 0.3)ⁿ with n = 15 is Σ_{r=0}^{15} (15 choose r)(0.7)^(15−r)(0.3)^r. Each individual term (15 choose r)(0.7)^(15−r)(0.3)^r is exactly the expression for P(X = r) from part (b)(i). So P(X = r) is the (r + 1)th term of the expansion of (0.7 + 0.3)¹⁵, and since 0.7 + 0.3 = 1 the terms (all the probabilities) sum to 1ⁿ = 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::9(a)": "M ~ N(1002, 2.25), so σ = √2.25 = 1.5. Standardise: z₁ = (1000 − 1002)/1.5 = −1.333…, z₂ = (1005 − 1002)/1.5 = 2.\nP(1000 < M < 1005) = Φ(2) − Φ(−1.333) = 0.97725 − 0.09121 = 0.886 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::9(b)": "Standardise both conditions. P(mass > 502) = 0.20 ⇒ (502 − μ)/σ = z with Φ(z) = 0.80, so (502 − μ)/σ = 0.84162.\nP(mass < 499) = 0.30 ⇒ (499 − μ)/σ = z with Φ(z) = 0.30, so (499 − μ)/σ = −0.52440.\nSubtract: (502 − μ) − (499 − μ) = (0.84162 + 0.52440)σ ⇒ 3 = 1.36602σ ⇒ σ = 2.1962… ≈ 2.20 (2 d.p.).\nThen μ = 502 − 0.84162σ = 502 − 0.84162(2.1962) = 500.151… ≈ 500.15 (2 d.p.).\nSo μ = 500.15 and σ = 2.20.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::9(c)(i)": "For a normal curve the points of inflection occur at one standard deviation either side of the mean. The curve peaks near x = 2 (the mean), and reading the graph the inflections are at approximately x = 0.5 and x = 3.5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::9(c)(ii)": "The points of inflection lie one standard deviation from the mean. They are at about x = 0.5 and x = 3.5, which are 1.5 units either side of the mean (≈ 2). Therefore the standard deviation is approximately σ ≈ 1.5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::10(a)": "Let ρ be the population product-moment correlation coefficient between the heating spend h and the number of customers c.\nH₀: ρ = 0\nH₁: ρ ≠ 0\n(two-tailed, because we are testing for correlation without a stated direction)\nThere are 12 months of data, so n = 12. Significance level 1%, two-tailed.\nFrom the table, n = 12, 2-tail 1%: critical value = 0.7079.\nSample value r = −0.798, so |r| = 0.798.\n0.798 > 0.7079, so the result is significant.\nReject H₀.\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 1% level of linear correlation between the amount spent on heating (h) and the number of customers (c).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::10(b)": "Two main features of the scatter diagram:\n1. The points would lie fairly close to a straight line (indicating strong linear correlation, since |r| = 0.798 is close to 1).\n2. The line/trend would have a negative gradient (downward sloping from top-left to bottom-right), reflecting the negative value of r — more customers tends to go with lower heating spend.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::10(c)": "The statement is flawed because correlation does not imply causation. The negative value of r shows only an association between c and t, not that spending less on heating will increase customers. A likely third factor — the season / outside temperature — affects both variables (in warmer months heating spend is low and footfall may be higher; in colder months heating spend is high and fewer customers visit). So reducing heating spend would not cause more customers; the manager’s conclusion is not justified.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::10(d)": "0.7155 is in the n = 10 row, in the column for a 1-tail test at 1% (equivalently a 2-tail test at 2%).\nSo it means: if there is no correlation in the population (ρ = 0), then for a random sample of 10 pairs,\nP(r ≥ 0.7155) = 0.01\nor equivalently P(|r| ≥ 0.7155) = 0.02.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::11(a)(i)": "Train probably had the greatest overall percentage growth. In the chart, the cells for Train fall in the highest shaded band (+50% < PI, or higher) for the most local authorities, so its percentage increases are consistently the largest.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::11(a)(ii)": "The answer is not definite because the chart only shows the band (range) into which each percentage increase falls, not the exact figures. Two methods could both be shaded in the “highest” band yet have very different actual percentage increases, so we cannot be certain which mode truly had the greatest growth.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::11(b)": "Two reasons the student is wrong:\n1. The chart shows percentage increases for each mode, not the actual numbers of people, and we do not know the 2001 base numbers using each mode — so the separate percentage increases cannot be combined into a meaningful total percentage change for the LA.\n2. The chart only gives the band each percentage increase lies in, not the exact value, so any total calculated from it would be inaccurate.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::11(c)": "The numbers using underground/metro/light rail/tram in these LAs in 2001 were all very small (under 50, in one case just 4). With such small starting numbers, even a very large percentage increase corresponds to only a tiny change in actual numbers of people. So the percentage-increase chart gives a misleading impression of the trend and is not very helpful for investigating the real change in numbers.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::11(d)": "From the chart, take the midpoints of the relevant bands: the increase in D (“Driving a car or van”) is about 20% and the increase in H (“Work mainly at or from home”) is about 40%.\nIncrease in D = 0.2·D₂₀₀₁ and increase in H = 0.4·H₂₀₀₁. We are told increase in D ≈ 3.5 × increase in H:\n0.2·D = 3.5 × 0.4·H ⇒ 0.2D = 1.4H ⇒ D/H = 1.4/0.2 = 7.\nTherefore D : H ≈ 7 : 1 in 2001.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::12(a)": "With two fair dice the totals are not equally likely: e.g. a total of 2 (student A) occurs only as (1,1), probability 1/36, whereas a total of 3 (student B) occurs as (1,2) or (2,1), probability 2/36.\nSince P(A) = 1/36 ≠ 2/36 = P(B), the 11 students do not all have the same chance of being chosen. Therefore the sampling method is not random.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::12(b)(i)": "A suitable instruction: “If the two dice show a pair that is not listed in the table (for example a double such as (2,2), (3,3), …, or (6,6)), ignore that throw and throw the two dice again, repeating until a listed pair appears.” This makes the 11 listed outcomes the only ones used.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::12(b)(ii)": "Using Sasha’s method, the 11 listed ordered pairs are each equally likely (each unordered combination split into its ordered forms), so each of the 11 students is chosen with equal probability. Student E corresponds to the pair (3,1), so P(choosing student E) = 1/11.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::13(a)": "Under H₀, X ~ B(200, 0.2), approximated by Y ~ N(np, npq) = N(40, 32) where np = 40 and npq = 200(0.2)(0.8) = 32, so σ = √32 = 5.657.\nP(Y > y) = 0.05 ⇒ (y − 40)/5.657 = 1.6449 ⇒ y = 40 + 1.6449(5.657) = 40 + 9.305 = 49.3 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::13(b)": "We reject H₀ for large X. Using the exact binomial X ~ B(200, 0.2) (the normal value y ≈ 49.3 suggests the critical region is around X = 50):\nP(X ≥ 50) = 0.0493… < 0.05, so X = 50 lies in the 5% critical region.\nP(X ≥ 49) = 0.0692… > 0.05, so X = 49 does not lie in the critical region.\nTherefore the smallest value of X that would result in rejecting the null hypothesis is X = 50.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p2::14": "First find p. The probabilities sum to 1:\np + (1/6)p + p² + 1/2 = 1 ⇒ p² + (7/6)p − 1/2 = 0 ⇒ 6p² + 7p − 3 = 0 ⇒ (3p − 1)(2p + 3) = 0.\nSo p = 1/3 or p = −3/2; reject p = −3/2 (a probability cannot be negative). Hence p = 1/3, giving:\nP(X=1) = 1/3, P(X=2) = (1/6)(1/3) = 1/18, P(X=3) = (1/3)² = 1/9, P(X=4) = 1/2.\n(Check: 6/18 + 1/18 + 2/18 + 9/18 = 18/18 = 1.)\nWe want P(X₂ = 2X₁ | X₂ > X₁) = P(X₂ = 2X₁ and X₂ > X₁) / P(X₂ > X₁). Since X₂ = 2X₁ automatically gives X₂ > X₁, the numerator is P(X₂ = 2X₁).\nNumerator: pairs (X₁, X₂) with X₂ = 2X₁ are (1,2) and (2,4):\nP(X₁=1)P(X₂=2) + P(X₁=2)P(X₂=4) = (1/3)(1/18) + (1/18)(1/2) = 1/54 + 1/36.\nCommon denominator 108: = 2/108 + 3/108 = 5/108.\nDenominator P(X₂ > X₁) = Σ_{j>i} P(X₁=i)P(X₂=j):\n(X₁=1): P(1)[P(2)+P(3)+P(4)] = (1/3)(1/18 + 1/9 + 1/2) = (1/3)(1/18 + 2/18 + 9/18) = (1/3)(12/18) = (1/3)(2/3) = 2/9.\n(X₁=2): P(2)[P(3)+P(4)] = (1/18)(1/9 + 1/2) = (1/18)(2/18 + 9/18) = (1/18)(11/18) = 11/324.\n(X₁=3): P(3)·P(4) = (1/9)(1/2) = 1/18.\nSum: 2/9 + 11/324 + 1/18 = 72/324 + 11/324 + 18/324 = 101/324.\nTherefore P(X₂ = 2X₁ | X₂ > X₁) = (5/108) / (101/324) = (5/108)(324/101) = (5 × 3)/101 = 15/101 ≈ 0.149 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::1(a)": "(2a²)³ = 2³(a²)³ = 8a⁶.\nThen 8a⁶ × ¾a⁻¹ = 8 × ¾ × a⁶⁻¹ = 6a⁵.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::1(b)": "Factorise numerator and the quadratic factor:\n 4x² − 9 = (2x − 3)(2x + 3),\n 2x² + 5x − 12 = (2x − 3)(x + 4).\nSo (4x² − 9)/[(2x² + 5x − 12)(2x + 3)] = [(2x − 3)(2x + 3)]/[(2x − 3)(x + 4)(2x + 3)].\nCancel the common factors (2x − 3) and (2x + 3):\n = 1/(x + 4).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::2": "The area of the sector AOB with radius (3x + 1) and angle 2 radians is\n A = ½r²θ = ½(3x + 1)²(2) = (3x + 1)².\nThe condition A < 44x − 7 gives\n (3x + 1)² < 44x − 7\n 9x² + 6x + 1 < 44x − 7\n 9x² − 38x + 8 < 0.\nFactorising: (9x − 2)(x − 4) < 0.\nThe critical values are x = 2/9 and x = 4, and the quadratic is negative between the roots, so\n 2/9 < x < 4.\n(Note 3x + 1 > 0 here, so the radius is valid.)\nIn set notation: {x : 2/9 < x < 4}.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::3(a)": "Write (3 − 2x)⁻² = [3(1 − (2/3)x)]⁻² = 3⁻²(1 − (2/3)x)⁻² = (1/9)(1 − (2/3)x)⁻².\nBinomial expansion of (1 + u)⁻² with u = −(2/3)x:\n (1 + u)⁻² = 1 + (−2)u + [(−2)(−3)/2!]u² + … = 1 − 2u + 3u² + …\nWith u = −(2/3)x:\n = 1 − 2(−(2/3)x) + 3((2/3)x)² + … = 1 + (4/3)x + 3(4/9)x² + … = 1 + (4/3)x + (4/3)x² + ….\nMultiplying by 1/9:\n (3 − 2x)⁻² = 1/9 + (4/27)x + (4/27)x² + ….",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::3(b)": "The binomial expansion of (1 − (2/3)x)⁻² is valid when |(2/3)x| < 1, i.e. |x| < 3/2.\nSo the set of values is {x : |x| < 3/2}, equivalently −3/2 < x < 3/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::3(c)": "Using part (a), (a + x)/(3 − 2x)² = (a + x)(1/9 + (4/27)x + (4/27)x² + …).\nThe coefficient of x comes from a × (4/27) + 1 × (1/9):\n coefficient of x = (4/27)a + 1/9 = (4/27)a + 3/27.\nSetting this equal to zero:\n (4/27)a + 3/27 = 0 ⇒ 4a + 3 = 0 ⇒ a = −3/4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::4(a)": "Using cot²x = cos²x/sin²x and cosec x = 1/sin x:\n 2(cos²x/sin²x) − 9(1/sin x) − 3 = 0.\nMultiply through by sin²x:\n 2cos²x − 9sin x − 3sin²x = 0.\nReplace cos²x = 1 − sin²x:\n 2(1 − sin²x) − 9sin x − 3sin²x = 0\n 2 − 2sin²x − 9sin x − 3sin²x = 0\n 2 − 5sin²x − 9sin x = 0.\nMultiplying by −1 and rearranging:\n 5sin²x + 9sin x − 2 = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::4(b)(i)": "Replacing x by 2θ in the result of part (a):\n 5sin²2θ + 9sin 2θ − 2 = 0.\nFactorise: (5sin 2θ − 1)(sin 2θ + 2) = 0.\nSince −1 ≤ sin 2θ ≤ 1, sin 2θ = −2 is impossible and is rejected, so\n sin 2θ = 0.2.\nFor 0 < θ < π we have 0 < 2θ < 2π:\n 2θ = arcsin(0.2) = 0.20135… or 2θ = π − 0.20135… = 2.94024….\nHence θ = 0.10068… or θ = 1.47012….\nTo 3 decimal places: θ = 0.101 and θ = 1.470.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::4(b)(ii)": "Using the small angle approximation sin 2θ ≈ 2θ in 5sin²2θ + 9sin 2θ − 2 = 0:\n 5(2θ)² + 9(2θ) − 2 = 0\n 20θ² + 18θ − 2 = 0\n 10θ² + 9θ − 1 = 0\n (10θ − 1)(θ + 1) = 0.\nTaking the positive root, θ = 0.1.\nThe exact smallest positive solution from part (b)(i) is 0.10068…, which is 0.10 to 2 decimal places, and the approximation also gives 0.10 to 2 decimal places. So the approximate solution is accurate to 2 decimal places.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::5(a)(i)": "y = (x³ − 2x²)ln x.\nProduct rule (u = x³ − 2x², v = ln x):\n dy/dx = (x³ − 2x²)(1/x) + (3x² − 4x)ln x = (x² − 2x) + (3x² − 4x)ln x.\nDifferentiate again:\n d²y/dx² = (2x − 2) + (6x − 4)ln x + (3x² − 4x)(1/x)\n   = (2x − 2) + (3x − 4) + (6x − 4)ln x\n   = 5x − 6 + (6x − 4)ln x.\nAt the point of inflection M, d²y/dx² = 0:\n 5x − 6 + (6x − 4)ln x = 0\n 5x = 6 − (6x − 4)ln x = 6 + (4 − 6x)ln x.\nHence x = [6 + (4 − 6x)ln x]/5, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::5(a)(ii)": "Use the iteration xₙ₊₁ = [6 + (4 − 6xₙ)ln xₙ]/5 with x₁ = 1.1:\n x₂ = [6 + (4 − 6.6)ln 1.1]/5 = 1.150438…\n x₃ = 1.118643…\n x₄ = 1.139191…\n x₅ = 1.126105…\n x₆ = 1.134521…\nThe iterates settle around 1.13, so the x-coordinate of M is 1.13 (to 2 decimal places).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::5(b)": "On 1 ≤ x ≤ 2 the curve lies below the x-axis (it crosses at x = 1 and x = 2, where ln x = 0 and x³ − 2x² = 0 respectively), so the shaded area is\n A = −∫₁² (x³ − 2x²)ln x dx.\nIntegrate by parts with u = ln x, dv = (x³ − 2x²)dx, so du = (1/x)dx, v = x⁴/4 − 2x³/3:\n ∫(x³ − 2x²)ln x dx = (x⁴/4 − 2x³/3)ln x − ∫(x⁴/4 − 2x³/3)(1/x)dx\n   = (x⁴/4 − 2x³/3)ln x − ∫(x³/4 − 2x²/3)dx\n   = (x⁴/4 − 2x³/3)ln x − (x⁴/16 − 2x³/9).\nEvaluate from 1 to 2.\n At x = 2: (16/4 − 16/3)ln 2 − (16/16 − 16/9) = (4 − 16/3)ln 2 − (1 − 16/9) = (−4/3)ln 2 + 7/9.\n At x = 1: 0 − (1/16 − 2/9) = −(9 − 32)/144 = 23/144.\nSo ∫₁² (x³ − 2x²)ln x dx = [(−4/3)ln 2 + 7/9] − 23/144 = (−4/3)ln 2 + 112/144 − 23/144 = (−4/3)ln 2 + 89/144.\nHence the area is\n A = −[(−4/3)ln 2 + 89/144] = (4/3)ln 2 − 89/144.\nSo p = 4/3, q = 2, r = 89/144.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::6(a)": "From the parametric equations,\n sin t = (x − 4k)/k and cos t = (y − 2)/4.\nUsing sin²t + cos²t = 1:\n ((x − 4k)/k)² + ((y − 2)/4)² = 1,\ni.e. (x − 4k)²/k² + (y − 2)²/16 = 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::6(b)(i)": "A circle requires equal denominators for the two squared terms in (x − 4k)²/k² + (y − 2)²/16 = 1, so k² = 16.\nThen the equation is (x − 4k)² + (y − 2)² = 16, a circle of radius √16 = 4.\nSo the radius is r = 4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::6(b)(ii)": "The circle (x − 4k)² + (y − 2)² = 16 has centre (4k, 2).\nFrom k² = 16, k = 4 or k = −4, giving 4k = 16 or 4k = −16.\nSo the possible centres are (16, 2) and (−16, 2).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::7": "Differentiate 5x − 2xy + 2y² − k = 0 implicitly with respect to x:\n 5 − 2(y + x(dy/dx)) + 4y(dy/dx) = 0\n 5 − 2y − 2x(dy/dx) + 4y(dy/dx) = 0.\nSolving for dy/dx:\n dy/dx = (2y − 5)/(4y − 2x).\nThe tangents at P and Q are parallel to the y-axis, so dy/dx is infinite there, meaning the denominator is zero:\n 4y − 2x = 0 ⇒ x = 2y.\nSubstitute x = 2y into the curve:\n 5(2y) − 2(2y)y + 2y² − k = 0\n 10y − 4y² + 2y² − k = 0\n −2y² + 10y − k = 0 ⇒ 2y² − 10y + k = 0.\nThe two y-coordinates are the roots y = [10 ± √(100 − 8k)]/4, so their difference is\n √(100 − 8k)/2 = 3 ⇒ √(100 − 8k) = 6 ⇒ 100 − 8k = 36 ⇒ k = 8.\nThen 2y² − 10y + 8 = 0 ⇒ y² − 5y + 4 = 0 ⇒ (y − 1)(y − 4) = 0, so y = 1 or y = 4.\nUsing x = 2y, the x-coordinates of P and Q are x = 2 and x = 8.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::8(a)": "Using s = ut + ½at² with u = i + 3j, a = −5i + 2j, t = 2:\n s = (i + 3j)(2) + ½(−5i + 2j)(2²)\n   = (2i + 6j) + ½(−5i + 2j)(4)\n   = (2i + 6j) + (−10i + 4j)\n   = −8i + 10j.\nSo the displacement is (−8i + 10j) m.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::8(b)": "Using v = u + at with u = i + 3j, a = −5i + 2j, t = 2:\n v = (i + 3j) + (−5i + 2j)(2) = (i + 3j) + (−10i + 4j) = −9i + 7j.\nSpeed = |v| = √((−9)² + 7²) = √(81 + 49) = √130 = 11.4017… ≈ 11.4 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::9(a)": "Resolve the two forces into North and East components.\n 17 N on bearing 050°: East = 17sin50° = 13.022, North = 17cos50° = 10.928.\n 12 N on bearing 340°: East = 12sin340° = −12sin20° = −4.104, North = 12cos340° = 12cos20° = 11.276.\nResultant components:\n East = 13.022 − 4.104 = 8.918 N (= 17sin50° − 12sin20°),\n North = 10.928 + 11.276 = 22.204 N (= 17cos50° + 12cos20°).\nMagnitude = √(8.918² + 22.204²) = √(79.5 + 493.0) = √572.5 = 23.93 N ≈ 23.9 N.\nBearing measured clockwise from North: θ = arctan(East/North) = arctan(8.918/22.204) = 21.9° ≈ 022°.\nSo the resultant force is 23.9 N on a bearing of 022°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::9(b)": "For the three forces to be in equilibrium, F must be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the resultant of the other two.\nSo the magnitude of F is 23.9 N.\nIts bearing is opposite to 022°, i.e. 022° + 180° = 202°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::10(a)": "The hanging object of mass 5 kg is in equilibrium, so the tension in the rope is\n T = 5g = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N.\nThe block is in equilibrium. Resolving horizontally, the horizontal component of the rope tension balances the horizontal component of the force X:\n T cos50° = X cos20°.\nSo X = T cos50°/cos20° = 49cos50°/cos20° = 49 × 0.6428/0.9397 = 33.5 N (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::10(b)": "Resolving vertically for the block (the ground exerts a 147 N normal contact force upward, and both X and the rope tension have upward vertical components):\n X sin20° + T sin50° + 147 = mg.\nWith X = 33.518 and T = 49:\n 33.518 sin20° + 49 sin50° + 147 = mg\n 11.46 + 37.54 + 147 = mg\n 196.0 = m(9.8)\n m = 196.0/9.8 = 20.\nSo m = 20.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::11(a)": "Take moments about A, where the lamina rests on the ground. The smooth wall at D exerts a horizontal normal reaction R_D. The weight 0.5g acts at the centre of the uniform rectangular lamina.\nWith AD = 6 m inclined at 50° to the horizontal, the moment of R_D about A is 6 R_D sin50° (R_D is horizontal and its perpendicular distance from A is the vertical height of D, which is 6 sin50°).\nThe moment of the weight about A equals 0.5g times the horizontal distance from A to the line of action of the weight, which works out to (3cos50° − ½sin50°) (using the dimensions of the rectangle), giving a weight moment of 1.5g cos50° − 0.5g sin50°.\nSetting the moments equal:\n 6 R_D sin50° = 1.5g cos50° − 0.5g sin50°\n R_D = (1.5g cos50° − 0.5g sin50°)/(6 sin50°)\n   = (1.5 × 9.8 × 0.6428 − 0.5 × 9.8 × 0.766)/(6 × 0.766)\n   = (9.449 − 3.754)/4.596 = 5.695/4.596 = 1.239 N.\nSo R_D = 1.24 N (3 s.f.), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::11(b)": "At A the lamina touches the rough ground; let R_A be the normal reaction and F_A the friction.\nResolve vertically: R_A = 0.5g = 0.5 × 9.8 = 4.9 N.\nResolve horizontally: the only horizontal forces are the friction F_A and the wall reaction R_D, so\n F_A = R_D = 1.24 N.\nThe lamina is in limiting equilibrium, so F_A = μR_A:\n 1.24 = μ × 4.9 ⇒ μ = 1.24/4.9 = 0.253.\nSo the coefficient of friction is 0.253 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::12(a)": "For 0 ≤ t ≤ 6: v = (1/12)kt(t − 3) is a positive (upward-opening) quadratic in t through the origin and t = 3, dipping below the t-axis between t = 0 and t = 3 (minimum turning point in the fourth quadrant at t = 1.5), then rising to a positive value at t = 6.\nFor 6 ≤ t ≤ 9: v = 54k/t² is a decreasing positive curve (of 1/t² type) that stays above the t-axis.\nThe two pieces join at t = 6, where (1/12)k(6)(3) = (3/2)k and 54k/36 = (3/2)k agree. The graph passes through the origin, crosses up at t = 3, peaks at t = 6 and then decreases towards t = 9. (Mark t = 3 and t = 6 on the t-axis.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::12(b)": "On 0 ≤ t ≤ 6, v = (1/12)k(t² − 3t), so the acceleration is\n a = dv/dt = (1/12)k(2t − 3).\nThis is zero when 2t − 3 = 0, i.e. t = 1.5.\n(On the second branch a = dv/dt = −108k/t³ ≠ 0.)\nSo the acceleration is zero at t = 1.5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::12(c)": "Distance is the integral of |v|. On 0 ≤ t ≤ 6, v = (1/12)k(t² − 3t) is negative for 0 < t < 3 and positive for 3 < t < 6.\nUsing ∫(t² − 3t)dt = t³/3 − 3t²/2:\n ∫₀³ (1/12)k(t² − 3t)dt = (1/12)k[t³/3 − 3t²/2]₀³ = (1/12)k(9 − 13.5) = (1/12)k(−4.5) = −3k/8.\nThe magnitude of this is 3k/8.\n ∫₃⁶ (1/12)k(t² − 3t)dt = (1/12)k[(72 − 54) − (9 − 13.5)] = (1/12)k(18 + 4.5) = (1/12)k(22.5) = 15k/8.\nFor 6 ≤ t ≤ 9:\n ∫₆⁹ 54k/t² dt = 54k[−1/t]₆⁹ = 54k(−1/9 + 1/6) = 54k(1/18) = 3k.\nTotal distance = 3k/8 + 15k/8 + 3k = 18k/8 + 3k = (9/4)k + 3k = (21/4)k.\nSetting this equal to 84:\n (21/4)k = 84 ⇒ k = 84 × 4/21 = 16.\nSo k = 16.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::13(a)": "Here sin θ = 0.6 and cos θ = 0.8 (θ ≈ 36.87°).\nNormal reaction: R = mg cos θ = 0.8mg.\nFriction (opposing motion up the plane): F = μR = 0.25 × 0.8mg = 0.2mg.\nNewton's second law up the plane (both gravity component and friction act down the plane):\n −mg sin θ − μmg cos θ = ma\n a = −g(0.6) − 0.25g(0.8) = −9.8(0.6) − 0.25(9.8)(0.8) = −5.88 − 1.96 = −7.84 m s⁻².\nUsing v² = u² + 2as with u = 6, s = AB = 1.375:\n v² = 6² + 2(−7.84)(1.375) = 36 − 21.56 = 14.44.\nSo v = √14.44 = 3.8 m s⁻¹, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2024-p3::13(b)": "Time from A to B (v = u + at with v = 3.8, u = 6, a = −7.84):\n 3.8 = 6 − 7.84 t₁ ⇒ t₁ = 2.2/7.84 = 0.2806 s.\nThe height of B above the horizontal ground is AB sin θ = 1.375 × 0.6 = 0.825 m.\nAt B the particle leaves the slope with speed 3.8 m s⁻¹ directed up the plane at angle θ (sin θ = 0.6, cos θ = 0.8):\n vertical component (up) = 3.8 × 0.6 = 2.28 m s⁻¹.\nFor the projectile from B until it lands at C, take up as positive; the vertical displacement is −0.825 m:\n −0.825 = 2.28 t₂ − ½(9.8)t₂²\n 4.9 t₂² − 2.28 t₂ − 0.825 = 0.\nSolving: t₂ = [2.28 + √(2.28² + 4 × 4.9 × 0.825)]/(2 × 4.9) = [2.28 + √21.368]/9.8 = (2.28 + 4.6225)/9.8 = 0.7043 s.\nTotal time T = t₁ + t₂ = 0.2806 + 0.7043 = 0.985 s.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::1(a)": "We need gg(5), so first compute g(5):\n g(5) = (2·5 + 5)/(5 − 3) = 15/2.\nNow apply g again:\n gg(5) = g(15/2) = (2·(15/2) + 5)/((15/2) − 3) = (15 + 5)/(15/2 − 6/2) = 20/(9/2) = 20 × 2/9 = 40/9.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::1(b)": "Write g(x) = (2x + 5)/(x − 3) = (2(x − 3) + 11)/(x − 3) = 2 + 11/(x − 3).\nFor x ≥ 5, x − 3 ≥ 2, so 11/(x − 3) is positive and decreasing as x increases.\nAt x = 5: g(5) = 2 + 11/2 = 15/2, the greatest value.\nAs x → ∞, 11/(x − 3) → 0 but is always positive, so g(x) → 2 but never equals 2.\nRange: 2 < g(x) ≤ 15/2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::1(c)": "Let y = (2x + 5)/(x − 3) and make x the subject:\ny(x − 3) = 2x + 5\nxy − 3y = 2x + 5\nxy − 2x = 3y + 5\nx(y − 2) = 3y + 5\nx = (3y + 5)/(y − 2).\nSo g⁻¹(x) = (3x + 5)/(x − 2).\nThe domain of g⁻¹ is the range of g. From part (b), the range of g is 2 < g(x) ≤ 15/2.\nDomain of g⁻¹: 2 < x ≤ 15/2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::2(a)": "First find the vector AB:\n AB = OB − OA = (4 − 2)i + (−2 − 3)j + (3 − (−4))k = 2i − 5j + 7k.\nSince AB = BD, we have:\n OD = OB + BD = OB + AB = (4i − 2j + 3k) + (2i − 5j + 7k) = 6i − 7j + 10k.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::2(b)": "Find vector AC:\n AC = OC − OA = (a − 2)i + (5 − 3)j + (−2 − (−4))k = (a − 2)i + 2j + 2k.\nCompute |AC|²:\n |AC|² = (a − 2)² + 2² + 2² = (a − 2)² + 8.\nSet |AC| = 4:\n (a − 2)² + 8 = 16 ⟹ (a − 2)² = 8 ⟹ a − 2 = ±2√2.\nSo a = 2 + 2√2 or a = 2 − 2√2.\nSince a < 0, and 2 + 2√2 ≈ 4.83 > 0, we reject that solution.\n2 − 2√2 ≈ −0.83 < 0, so a = 2 − 2√2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::3(a)": "We need two irrational numbers m ≠ n whose product is rational.\nLet m = √3 and n = √12 = 2√3. Both are irrational and m ≠ n.\nTheir product: mn = √3 × √12 = √(3 × 12) = √36 = 6, which is rational.\nThis counter-example shows the statement is false.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::3(b)": "(i) The graph of y = |x| + 3 is a V-shape with vertex at (0, 3). For x ≥ 0 the graph is the line y = x + 3 (gradient +1); for x < 0 it is the line y = −x + 3 (gradient −1). The graph lies entirely above y = 3.\n\n(ii) We consider three cases:\n• x ≥ 0: |x| + 3 = x + 3 and |x + 3| = x + 3 (since x + 3 > 0), so |x| + 3 = |x + 3|. ✓\n• −3 ≤ x < 0: |x| + 3 = −x + 3 and |x + 3| = x + 3 (since x + 3 ≥ 0). Then |x| + 3 − |x + 3| = (−x + 3) − (x + 3) = −2x ≥ 0. ✓\n• x < −3: |x| + 3 = −x + 3 and |x + 3| = −(x + 3) = −x − 3. Then |x| + 3 − |x + 3| = (−x + 3) − (−x − 3) = 6 > 0. ✓\nIn all cases |x| + 3 ≥ |x + 3|.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::4(i)": "Split the sum into three parts:\n Σ(r=1 to 16) 3 = 3 × 16 = 48.\n Σ(r=1 to 16) 5r = 5 × (16 × 17)/2 = 5 × 136 = 680.\n Σ(r=1 to 16) 2ʳ = 2(2¹⁶ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 2¹⁷ − 2 = 131072 − 2 = 131070.\nTotal = 48 + 680 + 131070 = 131798. ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::4(ii)": "Compute the first few terms using uₙ₊₁ = 1/uₙ:\nu₁ = 2/3\nu₂ = 1/(2/3) = 3/2\nu₃ = 1/(3/2) = 2/3 = u₁.\nSo the sequence is periodic with period 2: odd-numbered terms are 2/3 and even-numbered terms are 3/2.\nIn the first 100 terms there are 50 odd-numbered and 50 even-numbered terms, so\nΣ(r = 1 to 100) uᵣ = 50(2/3) + 50(3/2)\n = 100/3 + 75\n = 325/3.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::5(a)": "The Newton-Raphson formula is x_{n+1} = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f'(xₙ).\nHere f(x) = 2x³ + x² − 1, so f'(x) = 6x² + 2x.\nx_{n+1} = xₙ − (2xₙ³ + xₙ² − 1)/(6xₙ² + 2xₙ)\n = [xₙ(6xₙ² + 2xₙ) − (2xₙ³ + xₙ² − 1)] / (6xₙ² + 2xₙ)\n = [6xₙ³ + 2xₙ² − 2xₙ³ − xₙ² + 1] / (6xₙ² + 2xₙ)\n = (4xₙ³ + xₙ² + 1) / (6xₙ² + 2xₙ). ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::5(b)": "Using x₁ = 1:\n x₂ = (4(1)³ + (1)² + 1)/(6(1)² + 2(1)) = (4 + 1 + 1)/(6 + 2) = 6/8 = 3/4.\nUsing x₂ = 3/4:\n Numerator: 4(3/4)³ + (3/4)² + 1 = 4(27/64) + 9/16 + 1 = 27/16 + 9/16 + 16/16 = 52/16.\n Denominator: 6(3/4)² + 2(3/4) = 6(9/16) + 3/2 = 54/16 + 24/16 = 78/16.\n x₃ = (52/16)/(78/16) = 52/78 = 2/3.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::5(c)": "The Newton-Raphson formula requires dividing by f'(xₙ). At x₁ = 0:\n f'(0) = 6(0)² + 2(0) = 0.\nDivision by zero is undefined, so the formula cannot be applied. Geometrically, the tangent to the curve at x = 0 is horizontal and never meets the x-axis.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::6(a)": "(i) f(2) = −3(2)³ + 8(2)² − 9(2) + 10\n= −24 + 32 − 18 + 10\n= 0\n\n(ii) Since f(2) = 0, by the factor theorem (x − 2) is a factor of f(x).\nDivide f(x) by (x − 2), or compare coefficients:\n−3x³ + 8x² − 9x + 10 = (x − 2)(ax² + bx + c)\nx³: a = −3\nx²: b − 2a = 8 ⇒ b + 6 = 8 ⇒ b = 2\nconstant: −2c = 10 ⇒ c = −5\nCheck x term: c − 2b = −5 − 4 = −9 ✓\n\nf(x) = (x − 2)(−3x² + 2x − 5)\n(equivalently (2 − x)(3x² − 2x + 5))",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::6(b)": "The equation −3y⁶ + 8y⁴ − 9y² + 10 = 0 is f(y²) = 0, where x = y².\nFrom part (a): f(x) = (x − 2)(−3x² + 2x − 5) = 0.\nCase 1: x − 2 = 0 ⟹ y² = 2 ⟹ y = ±√2. This gives 2 real solutions.\nCase 2: −3x² + 2x − 5 = 0. Discriminant = 4 − 4(−3)(−5) = 4 − 60 = −56 < 0, so no real values of x, hence no real values of y.\nTherefore there are exactly two real solutions: y = √2 and y = −√2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::6(c)": "Multiply both sides of 3tan³θ − 8tan²θ + 9tanθ − 10 = 0 by −1:\n −3tan³θ + 8tan²θ − 9tanθ + 10 = 0, i.e. f(tanθ) = 0.\nThe real solution is tanθ = 2 (from part (b); the quadratic factor has no real roots).\nThe function tan has period π. On the interval 7π ≤ θ < 10π the length is 3π, which contains exactly 3 complete periods of tan.\nIn each period there is exactly one value of θ with tanθ = 2.\nSo there are 3 real solutions.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::7(i)": "4sinx = secx means 4sinx = 1/cosx.\nMultiplying both sides by cosx (valid for 0 ≤ x < π/2 since cosx > 0):\n 4sinx cosx = 1 ⟹ 2sin2x = 1 ⟹ sin2x = 1/2.\nFor 0 ≤ x < π/2, we have 0 ≤ 2x < π.\nWithin this range: 2x = π/6 or 2x = 5π/6.\nSo x = π/12 or x = 5π/12.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::7(ii)": "Write 5sinθ − 5cosθ in the form R sin(θ − α):\n R sin(θ − α) = R sinθ cosα − R cosθ sinα.\nMatching coefficients: R cosα = 5 and R sinα = 5.\nSo R = √(5² + 5²) = √50 = 5√2 and tanα = 5/5 = 1, giving α = 45°.\nThe equation becomes:\n 5√2 sin(θ − 45°) = 2 ⟹ sin(θ − 45°) = 2/(5√2) = √2/5.\nLet φ = arcsin(√2/5) ≈ 16.4°.\nFor 0 ≤ θ < 360°, so −45° ≤ θ − 45° < 315°:\n θ − 45° = 16.4° ⟹ θ ≈ 61.4°.\n θ − 45° = 180° − 16.4° = 163.6° ⟹ θ ≈ 208.6°.\nSolutions: θ ≈ 61.4° and θ ≈ 208.6°.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::8(a)": "The ball starts at x = 0 (H = 0) and lands at x = 40 (H = 0), so H = a·x(x − 40) for some constant a.\nThe maximum height is 12 m, occurring at x = 20 (midpoint by symmetry):\n 12 = a·20·(20 − 40) = a·20·(−20) = −400a ⟹ a = −3/100.\nSo H = −(3/100)x(x − 40) = (3/100)x(40 − x).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::8(b)": "The bar is 3 m high. Set H = 3:\n (3/100)x(40 − x) = 3 ⟹ x(40 − x) = 100 ⟹ 40x − x² = 100 ⟹ x² − 40x + 100 = 0.\nUsing the quadratic formula:\n x = (40 ± √(1600 − 400))/2 = (40 ± √1200)/2 = 20 ± √300 = 20 ± 10√3.\nThe greatest horizontal distance is x = 20 + 10√3 ≈ 20 + 17.32 ≈ 37.3 m.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::8(c)": "One limitation is that the model assumes the ground is perfectly flat and horizontal, which may not reflect the actual playing surface. (Other valid answers: the ball is modelled as a particle with no size; air resistance is ignored; the model assumes the kick is taken from ground level.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::9": "By the definition of the derivative:\n d/dθ (cosθ) = lim_{h→0} [cos(θ + h) − cosθ] / h.\nExpand cos(θ + h) using the addition formula:\n cos(θ + h) = cosθ cosh − sinθ sinh.\nSubstitute:\n [cos(θ+h) − cosθ]/h = [cosθ cosh − sinθ sinh − cosθ] / h\n = cosθ · (cosh − 1)/h − sinθ · sinh/h.\nTake the limit as h → 0, using the given results (cosh−1)/h → 0 and sinh/h → 1:\n d/dθ (cosθ) = cosθ · 0 − sinθ · 1 = −sinθ. ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::10(a)": "Let r mm be the radius of the mint and t minutes be the time elapsed.\nThe rate of decrease of the radius is inversely proportional to r²:\n dr/dt = −k/r²  (k > 0; negative because r is decreasing).\nSeparate variables:\n r² dr = −k dt.\nIntegrate both sides:\n r³/3 = −kt + c.\nApply initial condition t = 0, r = 5:\n 125/3 = c.\nApply condition t = 4, r = 3:\n 27/3 = −4k + 125/3 ⟹ 9 = −4k + 125/3 ⟹ 4k = 125/3 − 27/3 = 98/3 ⟹ k = 49/6.\nThe equation linking r and t is:\n r³/3 = −(49/6)t + 125/3,  i.e.  r³ = 125 − (49/2)t.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::10(b)": "The mint dissolves completely when r = 0:\n 0 = 125 − (49/2)t ⟹ t = 250/49 minutes.\n250/49 ≈ 5.1020 minutes.\nThe fractional part: 0.1020 × 60 ≈ 6.1 seconds ≈ 6 seconds.\nTotal time: 5 minutes 6 seconds.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::10(c)": "One limitation is that the mint is assumed to remain perfectly spherical throughout the process, which is unlikely in practice. (Other valid answers: the rate may not be exactly inversely proportional to r²; saliva concentration and sucking intensity vary over time.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::11(a)": "Write 1 + 11x − 6x² ≡ A(x − 3)(1 − 2x) + B(1 − 2x) + C(x − 3).\nSubstitute x = 3:\n 1 + 33 − 54 = B(1 − 6) ⟹ −20 = −5B ⟹ B = 4.\nSubstitute x = 1/2:\n 1 + 11/2 − 6/4 = C(1/2 − 3) ⟹ 1 + 5.5 − 1.5 = −(5/2)C ⟹ 5 = −(5/2)C ⟹ C = −2.\nCompare coefficients of x²:\n −6 = A(−2) ⟹ A = 3.\nSo A = 3, B = 4, C = −2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::11(b)": "From part (a): f(x) = 3 + 4/(x − 3) − 2/(1 − 2x).\nDifferentiate:\n f'(x) = −4/(x − 3)² − 4/(1 − 2x)².\n(Using d/dx[1/(1−2x)] = 2/(1−2x)², so −2 × 2/(1−2x)² = −4/(1−2x)².)\nFor x > 3:\n (x − 3)² > 0 so −4/(x − 3)² < 0.\n (1 − 2x)² > 0 so −4/(1 − 2x)² < 0.\nTherefore f'(x) < 0 for all x > 3, which proves that f is a strictly decreasing function.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::12(a)": "Start with the right-hand side:\n tanθ sin2θ = (sinθ/cosθ)(2sinθ cosθ) = 2sin²θ.\nUsing the double angle identity cos2θ = 1 − 2sin²θ, we get 2sin²θ = 1 − cos2θ.\nTherefore tanθ sin2θ = 1 − cos2θ, i.e. 1 − cos2θ ≡ tanθ sin2θ. ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::12(b)": "Substitute the identity 1 − cos2x = tanx sin2x from part (a) into the left-hand side:\n (sec²x − 5)(1 − cos2x) = (sec²x − 5) tanx sin2x.\nThe equation becomes:\n (sec²x − 5) tanx sin2x = 3tan²x sin2x.\nCase 1 — sin2x = 0: This gives 2x = 0 (for −π/2 < x < π/2), so x = 0. Check: both sides equal 0. ✓\nCase 2 — sin2x ≠ 0: Divide both sides by sin2x:\n (sec²x − 5) tanx = 3tan²x.\nIf tanx ≠ 0, divide by tanx:\n sec²x − 5 = 3tanx.\nReplace sec²x = 1 + tan²x:\n 1 + tan²x − 5 = 3tanx ⟹ tan²x − 3tanx − 4 = 0 ⟹ (tanx − 4)(tanx + 1) = 0.\nSo tanx = 4 or tanx = −1.\n tanx = 4 ⟹ x = arctan4 ≈ 1.326 rad (within (−π/2, π/2)).\n tanx = −1 ⟹ x = −π/4.\n(If tanx = 0 back in Case 2: (sec²x − 5)·0 = 0 which is satisfied, giving x = 0 already found.)\nAll solutions: x = 0, x = −π/4, x ≈ 1.326.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::13": "Step 1 — Normal at P(e, e).\nDifferentiate y = xlnx: dy/dx = lnx + 1.\nAt x = e: dy/dx = ln e + 1 = 2, so the gradient of the normal is −1/2.\nNormal l: y − e = −½(x − e) ⟹ y = −x/2 + 3e/2.\nMeets x-axis (y = 0): x = 3e.\n\nStep 2 — Identify the region R.\nThe curve y = xlnx is zero at x = 1 (since 1·ln1 = 0) and passes through P(e, e).\nR is bounded by: the curve from (1, 0) to (e, e), the normal from (e, e) to (3e, 0), and the x-axis from (1, 0) to (3e, 0).\n\nStep 3 — Area under the normal (triangle).\nThe normal meets the x-axis at (3e, 0) and the curve at (e, e).\nThis is a right triangle with base 2e (from x = e to x = 3e along the x-axis) and height e.\nArea of triangle = ½ × 2e × e = e².\n\nStep 4 — Area under the curve from x = 1 to x = e.\n∫₁ᵉ xlnx dx. Integrate by parts with u = lnx, dv = x dx:\n = [x²lnx/2]₁ᵉ − ∫₁ᵉ (x²/2)(1/x) dx\n = [x²lnx/2]₁ᵉ − ∫₁ᵉ x/2 dx\n = [x²lnx/2 − x²/4]₁ᵉ\n = (e²·1/2 − e²/4) − (1·0/2 − 1/4)\n = (e²/4) − (−1/4) = e²/4 + 1/4.\n\nStep 5 — Total area of R.\nArea R = (area under curve) + (area of triangle)\n = (e²/4 + 1/4) + e²\n = 5e²/4 + 1/4.\nSo A = 5/4 and B = 1/4.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::14(a)": "At t = 0:\n N = 900/(3 + 7e⁰) = 900/(3 + 7) = 900/10 = 90.\nThere are 90 mice at the start of the study.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::14(b)": "N = 900/(3 + 7e^(−0.25t)) = 900(3 + 7e^(−0.25t))⁻¹.\nBy the chain rule:\ndN/dt = −900(3 + 7e^(−0.25t))⁻² × 7(−0.25)e^(−0.25t)\n = 900(0.25)(7e^(−0.25t))/(3 + 7e^(−0.25t))².\nNow write this in terms of N. From N = 900/(3 + 7e^(−0.25t)):\n3 + 7e^(−0.25t) = 900/N, so 7e^(−0.25t) = 900/N − 3.\nSubstituting:\ndN/dt = 900(0.25)(900/N − 3)/(900/N)²\n = 900(0.25)(900/N − 3) × N²/810000\n = 0.25(900/N − 3) × N²/900\n = 0.25(900N − 3N²)/900\n = (900N − 3N²)/3600\n = 3N(300 − N)/3600\n = N(300 − N)/1200, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::14(c)": "From part (b), dN/dt = N(300 − N)/1200. This is a quadratic in N, maximised when d/dN[N(300 − N)] = 300 − 2N = 0, i.e. N = 150.\nSet N = 150 in the population model:\n 150 = 900/(3 + 7e^{−0.25T}) ⟹ 3 + 7e^{−0.25T} = 6 ⟹ 7e^{−0.25T} = 3 ⟹ e^{−0.25T} = 3/7.\nTaking logarithms:\n −0.25T = ln(3/7) ⟹ T = −4ln(3/7) = 4ln(7/3).\nT = 4ln(7/3) ≈ 3.37 months.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p2::14(d)": "As t → ∞, e^{−0.25t} → 0, so N → 900/3 = 300.\nThe maximum number of mice according to the model is P = 300.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::1(a)": "Cloud cover C is measured in oktas on a scale 0 to 8, giving 9 equally likely values.\nUnder a discrete uniform distribution: P(C = c) = 1/9 for c = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::1(b)": "Cloud cover less than 50% corresponds to C < 4.5, i.e. C ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} (4 values out of 9).\nP(C < 4) = 4/9 ≈ 0.444.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::1(c)": "The model predicts P(C < 4) = 4/9 ≈ 0.444, but the observed proportion is 0.315, which is noticeably lower.\nThis suggests Helen's discrete uniform model is not suitable for the Hurn 2015 data.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::1(d)": "Cloud cover varies by season and location, so the probabilities are not equal across all values.\nA suitable refinement is to use a non-uniform discrete distribution, for example assigning higher probabilities to larger cloud cover values (or using the observed relative frequencies as probabilities).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::2(a)": "Let ρ be the population product moment correlation coefficient between w and t.\nH₀: ρ = 0,  H₁: ρ < 0  (one-tailed test at 5% significance level).\nFor n = 8, the critical value at the 5% level for a one-tailed test is −0.6215.\nSince r = −0.915 < −0.6215, the result is significant.\nWe reject H₀. There is sufficient evidence at the 5% level of a negative correlation between weekly sales figures and average weekly temperature.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::2(b)": "As temperature increases, people are more likely to visit the beach or spend time outdoors rather than shopping, which reduces weekly sales.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::2(c)": "Yes, the correlation coefficient is consistent with Tessa's suggestion. The value r = −0.915 is close to −1, indicating a strong linear relationship between w and t, which supports the use of a linear regression model.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::2(d)": "The explanatory variable is t (average weekly temperature), because weekly sales (w) are likely to depend on temperature — temperature influences shopping behaviour, not the other way around.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::2(e)": "The gradient −171 means that for every 1 °C rise in average weekly temperature, the predicted weekly sales decrease by £171.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(a)": "Two assumptions needed for Peta's B(10, 0.1) model:\n1. The probability of a dart hitting the target is constant (the same value 0.1) for every throw and every child.\n2. Each throw is independent — whether a throw hits or misses does not affect the outcome of any other throw.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(b)": "H ~ B(10, 0.1).\nP(H ≥ 4) = 1 − P(H ≤ 3).\nFrom tables or a calculator: P(H ≤ 3) = 0.9872.\nP(H ≥ 4) = 1 − 0.9872 = 0.0128.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(c)": "Under Peta's assumptions the throws are independent with constant probability 0.1, so F follows a geometric distribution.\nP(F = 5) = (probability of missing 4 times) × (probability of hitting on the 5th throw)\n= 0.9⁴ × 0.1 = 0.6561 × 0.1 = 0.0656.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(d)": "Thomas' model: P(F = n) = 0.01 + (n − 1)α for n = 1, 2, …, 10.\nThe 10 probabilities must sum to 1:\nΣₙ₌₁¹⁰ [0.01 + (n − 1)α] = 1\n10 × 0.01 + α × Σₙ₌₁¹⁰ (n − 1) = 1\n0.1 + α × (0 + 1 + 2 + … + 9) = 1\n0.1 + 45α = 1\n45α = 0.9\nα = 0.02.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(e)": "Using Thomas' model with α = 0.02:\nP(F = 5) = 0.01 + (5 − 1) × 0.02 = 0.01 + 0.08 = 0.09.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::3(f)": "In Peta's model the probability of hitting the target is constant (0.1) on every throw, regardless of the throw number.\nIn Thomas' model the probability increases with each successive throw — later throws are more likely to hit.\nPeta's model implies the child does not improve; Thomas' model implies improvement with practice.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(a)": "Charlie used convenience (opportunity) sampling — he simply asked workers who happened to arrive during a particular 10-minute window.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(b)": "An alternative non-random method is quota sampling.\nCharlie would divide the workforce into categories (strata), for example by department or gender, and then select workers from each category until a fixed quota (totalling 40) is reached — for instance, asking the first 5 people from each of 8 departments.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(c)": "Taruni used a census — she collected data from every member of the company rather than just a sample.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(d)": "From the box plot: LQ = 26, UQ = 58.\nIQR = 58 − 26 = 32 minutes.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(e)": "Mean: x̄ = Σx / n = 4133 / 95 = 43.5 minutes (awrt 43.5).\nStandard deviation: s = √(Σx²/n − x̄²) = √(202294/95 − 43.505…²)\n= √(2129.41… − 1892.69…) = √236.72… ≈ 15.4 minutes (awrt 15.4).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(f)": "The data contains outliers (visible on the box plot) and is positively skewed. Outliers inflate the mean and standard deviation, making them unrepresentative.\nIt is better to use the median and IQR, which are resistant to outliers and give a more accurate picture of the typical journey time.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::4(g)": "After the changes, Rana's journey fell from 75 min to 35 min and David's from 60 min to 33 min.\nBoth values moved from above the median (≈40 min) to below it, so more than half the data now lies below the old median — the median decreases.\nSimilarly, both original values were above the lower quartile but below (or near) the upper quartile of 58. With two values moving down, more than 75% of the data now lies below the old upper quartile — the upper quartile decreases.\nThe two values that change are the median (Q2) and the upper quartile (Q3); both decrease.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::5(a)": "L ~ N(18, 4²).\nP(L > 16) = P(Z > (16 − 18)/4) = P(Z > −0.5) = Φ(0.5) ≈ 0.691.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::5(b)": "Alice's calculator has been used for 16 hours. Each battery has already survived 16 hours, so we need the conditional probability that each battery lasts a further 4 hours (i.e. lasts beyond 20 hours in total).\nP(L > 20 | L > 16) = P(L > 20) / P(L > 16).\nP(L > 20) = P(Z > (20 − 18)/4) = P(Z > 0.5) = 1 − 0.6915 = 0.3085.\nP(L > 16) = 0.6915 (from part (a)).\nConditional probability = 0.3085 / 0.6915 ≈ 0.4462.\nFor the calculator to keep working, all 4 batteries must survive: P = (0.4462)⁴ ≈ 0.0396.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::5(c)": "After 16 hours, Alice replaces 2 of the 4 old batteries with 2 new ones at random. The calculator now contains 2 old batteries (that have survived 16 h) and 2 new batteries.\nFor the calculator to survive the remaining 4 hours, all 4 batteries must last:\n• Each old battery: P(L > 20 | L > 16) = 0.4462 (from (b)).\n• Each new battery: P(L > 4) = P(Z > (4 − 18)/4) = P(Z > −3.5) ≈ 0.9998.\nP(calculator survives) = (0.4462)² × (0.9998)²\n= 0.19909… × 0.99960… ≈ 0.199 (to 3 s.f.). ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::5(d)": "H₀: μ = 18,  H₁: μ > 18  (one-tailed test at 5% significance level).\nUnder H₀, x̄ ~ N(18, 4²/20) = N(18, 0.8).\nTest statistic: Z = (19.2 − 18) / √0.8 = 1.2 / 0.8944 ≈ 1.342.\nCritical value at 5% (one-tailed): z = 1.6449.\nSince 1.342 < 1.6449, the result is not significant (p-value ≈ 0.0899 > 0.05).\nWe do not reject H₀. There is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to support Alice's belief that the mean battery lifetime is more than 18 hours.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::6": "Integrate the velocity vector to find the position vector:\nr = ∫(t^{−½} i − 4t j) dt = 2t^{½} i − 2t² j + C.\nAt t = 1 (point A): r_A = 2(1) i − 2(1) j + C = (2i − 2j) + C.\nAt t = 4 (point B): r_B = 2(2) i − 2(16) j + C = (4i − 32j) + C.\nDisplacement vector AB = r_B − r_A = (4 − 2)i + (−32 + 2)j = 2i − 30j.\nDistance AB = |AB| = √(2² + (−30)²) = √(4 + 900) = √904 = 2√226 m.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::7(a)": "From tan α = 3/4: sin α = 3/5 and cos α = 4/5.\nResolve vertically (↑): R + 40 sin α = 20g\nR = 20 × 9.8 − 40 × 3/5 = 196 − 24 = 172 N.\nFriction: F = μR = 0.14 × 172 = 24.08 N.\nApply Newton's second law horizontally (→): 40 cos α − F = 20a\n40 × 4/5 − 24.08 = 20a\n32 − 24.08 = 20a\n7.92 = 20a\na = 0.396 m s⁻².",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::7(b)": "When the crate is pushed (Figure 2), the thrust in the handle has a downward vertical component.\nThis increases the normal reaction R (compared with pulling), since the floor must now support both the weight and the downward component of the push.\nA larger R means a larger limiting friction force F = μR.\nWith greater friction opposing the motion, the net horizontal force is smaller and therefore the acceleration is less.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::8(a)": "Use r = ut + ½at² with u = (2i − 3j), r = (7i − 10j) at t = 2:\n(7i − 10j) = 2(2i − 3j) + ½ × a × 4\n(7i − 10j) = (4i − 6j) + 2a\n2a = (3i − 4j)\na = 1.5i − 2j m s⁻².\n|a| = √(1.5² + 2²) = √(2.25 + 4) = √6.25 = 2.5 m s⁻². ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::8(b)": "First find the velocity at A (t = 2 from O):\nv_A = u + at = (2i − 3j) + 2(1.5i − 2j) = (2 + 3)i + (−3 − 4)j = 5i − 7j m s⁻¹.\nFrom A, with new constant acceleration (4i + 8.8j):\nv_B = v_A + t(4i + 8.8j) = (5 + 4t)i + (8.8t − 7)j.\nFor north-east direction the i and j components of velocity must be equal:\n5 + 4t = 8.8t − 7\n12 = 4.8t\nt = 2.5 s.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::9(a)": "Model the plank as a uniform rod; take moments about A to eliminate the unknown wall reaction.\nAnticlockwise moment from tension T: T × 2a × sin α (perpendicular distance from A to the line of T = 2a sin α).\nClockwise moments: weight of plank Mg acts at the centre (distance a from A); block 3Mg acts at P (distance x from A).\nMoment equation:\nT × 2a × sin α = Mg × a + 3Mg × x\nWith tan α = 3/4 ⟹ sin α = 3/5:\nT × 2a × 3/5 = Mg(a + 3x)\nT × 6a/5 = Mg(a + 3x)\nT = 5Mg(a + 3x) / (6a). ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::9(b)": "Resolve horizontally for the plank:\nHorizontal component of T = T cos α = T × 4/5.\nThis equals the horizontal reaction at A, given as 2Mg:\nT × 4/5 = 2Mg ⟹ T = 5Mg/2.\nSubstitute into the expression from (a):\n5Mg(a + 3x)/(6a) = 5Mg/2\n(a + 3x)/(6a) = 1/2\na + 3x = 3a\n3x = 2a\nx = 2a/3.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::9(c)": "With x = 2a/3, find T: T = 5Mg(a + 3 × 2a/3)/(6a) = 5Mg(a + 2a)/(6a) = 5Mg × 3a/(6a) = 5Mg/2.\nResolve vertically for the plank:\nY + T sin α = Mg + 3Mg (total downward load)\nY + (5Mg/2)(3/5) = 4Mg\nY + 3Mg/2 = 4Mg\nY = 5Mg/2.\nThe horizontal component of the wall force is X = 2Mg.\ntan β = Y/X = (5Mg/2) / (2Mg) = 5/4.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::9(d)": "The tension T = 5Mg(a + 3x)/(6a) is an increasing function of x (as x increases, T increases).\nFor the rope not to break: T ≤ 5Mg.\n5Mg(a + 3x)/(6a) ≤ 5Mg\n(a + 3x)/(6a) ≤ 1\na + 3x ≤ 6a\n3x ≤ 5a\nx ≤ 5a/3.\nTherefore the block P must be placed no more than 5a/3 from A along the plank. If P is placed further than 5a/3 from A, the tension would exceed 5Mg and the rope would break.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::10(a)": "Consider the vertical motion from A to the highest point, taking upwards as positive.\nAt the highest point the vertical component of velocity is zero. The initial vertical component is U sin α, and the ball rises 3 − 2 = 1 m.\nUsing v² = u² + 2as with v = 0, u = U sin α, a = −g, s = 1:\n0² = (U sin α)² − 2g × 1\nU² sin²α = 2g\nU² = 2g/sin²α.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::10(b)": "Let the angle be α with tan α = 1/4 (to be shown). Set up equations of motion from A.\nHorizontal: 20 = Ut cos α  ⟹  t = 20/(U cos α).   …(1)\nVertical (displacement from A to T is 0.75 − 2 = −1.25 m):\n−1.25 = Ut sin α − ½gt²   …(2)\nSubstitute (1) into (2):\n−1.25 = 20 tan α − ½g × 400/(U² cos²α).\nSubstitute U² = 2g/sin²α:\n−1.25 = 20 tan α − 200g sin²α / (2g cos²α)\n−1.25 = 20 tan α − 100 tan²α.\nRearrange: 100 tan²α − 20 tan α − 1.25 = 0.\nMultiply through by 4: 400 tan²α − 80 tan α − 5 = 0.\nFactorise: (4 tan α − 1)(100 tan α + 5) = 0.\nSo tan α = 1/4 or tan α = −1/20.\nSince α > 0 (ball thrown upward), tan α = 1/4 and α = arctan(1/4) ≈ 14°.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::10(c)": "One limitation: the ball is modelled as a particle, so its actual size is ignored. In reality the ball has dimensions, meaning it could reach the target even if its centre does not pass exactly through T.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p3::10(d)": "With tan α = 1/4: sin α = 1/√17, cos α = 4/√17.\nFrom U² = 2g/sin²α = 2 × 9.8 × 17 = 333.2, so U = √333.2 ≈ 18.255 m s⁻¹.\nt = 20/(U cos α) = 20 / (18.255 × 4/√17) = 20 / (18.255 × 0.9701) = 20 / 17.706 ≈ 1.13 s.\n(Accept t ≈ 1.1 s.)",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::1": "f(x) = 3x³ + 2ax² − 4x + 5a. Since (x + 3) is a factor, f(−3) = 0.\nf(−3) = 3(−27) + 2a(9) − 4(−3) + 5a = −81 + 18a + 12 + 5a = 23a − 69.\nSet f(−3) = 0: 23a − 69 = 0  ⟹  a = 3.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::2(a)": "Rearrange: cos x − 2x − ½ = 0 ⇔ cos x = 2x + ½\nSo the roots are the x-coordinates of the intersections of y = cos x and the line y = 2x + ½.\nDraw y = 2x + ½ on Diagram 1: it has y-intercept ½ and gradient 2 (for example it passes through (0, ½) and (½, 1½)).\nThe line meets y = cos x exactly once, just to the right of the y-axis (between x = 0 and x = ¼).\nIt cannot meet the curve anywhere else: the gradient of y = cos x is −sin x, which is never more than 1, while the line has gradient 2, so the line rises faster than the curve everywhere and can cross it only once. (Also −1 ≤ cos x ≤ 1, while the line is above y = 1 for x > ¼ and below y = −1 for x < −¾.)\nThere is only one point of intersection, so cos x − 2x − ½ = 0 has exactly one real root.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::2(b)": "For small x, cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2. Substitute into cos x − 2x − ½ = 0:\n1 − x²/2 − 2x − ½ = 0\n½ − 2x − x²/2 = 0\nMultiply by −2: x² + 4x − 1 = 0\nx = (−4 ± √(16 + 4))/2 = (−4 ± √20)/2 = −2 ± √5\nx = −2 + √5 ≈ 0.236 or x = −2 − √5 ≈ −4.236\nα is small (from (a) the root lies between 0 and ¼), so reject −2 − √5.\nα = −2 + √5 = 0.236 (3 d.p.)",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::3(a)": "y = (5x² + 10x)/(x + 1)²\nQuotient rule with u = 5x² + 10x, v = (x + 1)²:\ndu/dx = 10x + 10, dv/dx = 2(x + 1)\ndy/dx = [(x + 1)²(10x + 10) − (5x² + 10x) × 2(x + 1)] / (x + 1)⁴\nCancel a factor of (x + 1):\ndy/dx = [(x + 1)(10x + 10) − 2(5x² + 10x)] / (x + 1)³\nNumerator: (10x² + 20x + 10) − (10x² + 20x) = 10\ndy/dx = 10/(x + 1)³\nSo A = 10 and n = 3.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::3(b)": "From (a), dy/dx = 10/(x + 1)³.\nThe numerator 10 is positive, so dy/dx < 0 exactly when (x + 1)³ < 0.\n(x + 1)³ < 0 ⇔ x + 1 < 0 ⇔ x < −1\ndy/dx < 0 for x < −1",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::4(a)": "Write 1/√(4−x) = (4−x)^(−½) = [4(1 − x/4)]^(−½) = 4^(−½)(1 − x/4)^(−½) = ½(1 − x/4)^(−½).\nExpand (1 − x/4)^(−½) using binomial series with u = −x/4, n = −½:\n= 1 + (−½)(−x/4) + (−½)(−3/2)/2 × (x/4)² + …\n= 1 + x/8 + (3/4)(x²/16)/1 + …  →  1 + x/8 + 3x²/128.\nMultiply by ½: 1/√(4−x) ≈ ½ + x/16 + 3x²/256.\nFirst three terms: ½ + x/16 + 3x²/256.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::4(b)(i)": "The expansion is valid only when |x/4| < 1, i.e. |x| < 4.\nSince |−14| = 14 > 4, the value x = −14 lies outside the range of validity, so should not be used.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::4(b)(ii)": "Of the two valid values (x = 2 and x = −½, both satisfying |x| < 4), x = −½ is closer to zero.\nThe closer x is to zero, the smaller the higher-order terms in the expansion and hence the more accurate the approximation. So x = −½ gives the more accurate result.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::5(a)": "f(x) = 2x² + 4x + 9.\nFactor 2 from the first two terms: 2(x² + 2x) + 9.\nComplete the square: x² + 2x = (x + 1)² − 1.\nSo f(x) = 2[(x + 1)² − 1] + 9 = 2(x + 1)² − 2 + 9 = 2(x + 1)² + 7.\nTherefore a = 2, b = 1, c = 7.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::5(b)": "y = 2(x + 1)² + 7 is a U-shaped parabola (positive leading coefficient).\nMinimum (vertex) at (−1, 7).\nY-intercept: set x = 0: y = 2(1) + 7 = 9, point (0, 9).\nThe discriminant is 4² − 4(2)(9) = 16 − 72 = −56 < 0, so no x-intercepts.\nSketch: U-shape with vertex (−1, 7) and y-intercept (0, 9), entirely above the x-axis.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::5(c)": "(i) Expand g(x):\ng(x) = 2(x − 2)² + 4x − 3 = 2x² − 8x + 8 + 4x − 3 = 2x² − 4x + 5\n\nCompare with f(x − 2):\nf(x − 2) = 2(x − 2)² + 4(x − 2) + 9 = 2(x − 2)² + 4x + 1\n\nSo g(x) = 2(x − 2)² + 4x + 1 − 4 = f(x − 2) − 4\n\n(Check: f(x) = 2(x + 1)² + 7 has minimum (−1, 7); g(x) = 2(x − 1)² + 3 has minimum (1, 3), which is 2 right and 4 down.)\n\nThe transformation is a translation by the vector (2, −4), i.e. 2 units in the positive x-direction and 4 units in the negative y-direction.\n\n(ii) From part (a), 2x² + 4x + 9 = 2(x + 1)² + 7, so\nh(x) = 21/(2(x + 1)² + 7)\n\nSince 2(x + 1)² ≥ 0, the denominator is always ≥ 7, with its minimum value 7 when x = −1.\nSo the maximum value of h(x) is 21/7 = 3.\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so h(x) > 0, and as x → ±∞ the denominator → ∞ so h(x) → 0 but never reaches 0.\n\nRange: 0 < h(x) ≤ 3",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::6(a)": "5sin2θ = 9tanθ. Write sin2θ = 2sinθcosθ and tanθ = sinθ/cosθ:\n10sinθcosθ = 9sinθ/cosθ.\nMultiply through by cosθ and rearrange: sinθ(10cos²θ − 9) = 0.\nCase 1: sinθ = 0 ⟹ θ = 0°, ±180°.\nCase 2: cos²θ = 9/10 ⟹ cosθ = ±3/√10.\ncosθ = 3/√10 ⟹ θ ≈ ±18.4°.\ncosθ = −3/√10 ⟹ θ ≈ ±161.6°.\nAll solutions in [−180°, 180°]: θ = 0°, ±18.4°, ±161.6°, ±180°.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::6(b)": "5sin(2x−50°) = 9tan(x−25°) is equivalent to the equation in part (a) with θ = x − 25°.\nFrom part (a), solutions for θ: 0°, ±18.4°, ±161.6°, ±180°.\nFor smallest positive x, try the smallest θ giving x > 0:\nθ = −18.4° ⟹ x = 25° − 18.4° = 6.6° (positive ✓).\nSmallest positive solution: x ≈ 6.6°.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::7(a)": "Use V = Ae^(kt). At t = 0: V = 20000, so A = 20000.\nAt t = 1: V = 16000: 16000 = 20000e^k ⟹ e^k = 0.8 ⟹ k = ln0.8 ≈ −0.2231.\nModel: V = 20000e^(−0.2231t)  (or equivalently V = 20000 × 0.8^t).",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::7(b)": "V(10) = 20000e^(−0.2231 × 10) = 20000e^(−2.231) ≈ 20000 × 0.1074 ≈ £2148.\nThis is close to the actual resale value of £2000 (about 7% overestimate).\nThe model is reasonably reliable for this prediction.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::7(c)": "Car B depreciates more slowly than car A. In the model V = 20000e^(kt), use a less negative value of k (i.e. k closer to zero) so the exponential decays more slowly. For example, use k = −0.15 instead of −0.2231.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::8(a)": "y = x(x + 2)(x − 4) = x(x² − 2x − 8) = x³ − 2x² − 8x.\nFor −2 < x < 0 the curve is above the x-axis (e.g. at x = −1, y = (−1)(1)(−5) = 5 > 0), so\nArea R₁ = ∫₋₂⁰ (x³ − 2x² − 8x) dx.\nIntegrating: ∫(x³ − 2x² − 8x) dx = ¼x⁴ − ⅔x³ − 4x².\nArea R₁ = [¼x⁴ − ⅔x³ − 4x²]₋₂⁰\n= (0) − (¼(−2)⁴ − ⅔(−2)³ − 4(−2)²)\n= 0 − (4 + 16/3 − 16)\n= 0 − (−20/3)\n= 20/3.\nSo the exact area of R₁ is 20/3.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::8(b)": "R₂ lies below the x-axis for 0 < x < b, so the integral from 0 to b is negative and equals −(area of R₂).\nSince area R₂ = area R₁ = 20/3:\n∫₀ᵇ (x³ − 2x² − 8x) dx = −20/3\n[¼x⁴ − ⅔x³ − 4x²]₀ᵇ = ¼b⁴ − ⅔b³ − 4b² − 0\nSo ¼b⁴ − ⅔b³ − 4b² = −20/3.\nMultiply by 12: 3b⁴ − 8b³ − 48b² = −80\n⇒ 3b⁴ − 8b³ − 48b² + 80 = 0.\nNow factorise. Substituting b = −2: 3(16) − 8(−8) − 48(4) + 80 = 48 + 64 − 192 + 80 = 0, so (b + 2) is a factor.\nDividing: 3b⁴ − 8b³ − 48b² + 80 = (b + 2)(3b³ − 14b² − 20b + 40).\nSubstituting b = −2 into the cubic: 3(−8) − 14(4) − 20(−2) + 40 = −24 − 56 + 40 + 40 = 0, so (b + 2) is a factor again.\nDividing: 3b³ − 14b² − 20b + 40 = (b + 2)(3b² − 20b + 20).\nCheck: (b + 2)(3b² − 20b + 20) = 3b³ − 20b² + 20b + 6b² − 40b + 40 = 3b³ − 14b² − 20b + 40 ✓\nHence 3b⁴ − 8b³ − 48b² + 80 = (b + 2)²(3b² − 20b + 20), and so b satisfies\n(b + 2)²(3b² − 20b + 20) = 0.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::8(c)": "Diagram: sketch y = x(x + 2)(x − 4) (as in Figure 2), crossing the x-axis at −2, 0 and 4, and draw a vertical line at x = 5.442, to the right of x = 4. Shade:\n• the region above the x-axis from x = −2 to x = 0 (this is R₁, area 20/3);\n• the region below the x-axis from x = 0 to x = 4;\n• the region above the x-axis from x = 4 to x = 5.442.\nThe root 5.442 is the value of b for which ∫₋₂ᵇ y dx = 0, i.e. ∫₀ᵇ y dx = −20/3 with b beyond x = 4.\nSo between x = −2 and x = 5.442 the total area above the x-axis equals the total area below the x-axis:\n(area from −2 to 0) + (area from 4 to 5.442) = (area from 0 to 4).\nEquivalently, the area below the axis between x = 1.225 and x = 4 equals the area above the axis between x = 4 and x = 5.442 (each is 36).\nIt is not a valid value of b for R₂ because R₂ requires 0 < b < 4.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::9(a)": "loga − logb = log(a−b). Apply log subtraction law: log(a/b) = log(a−b).\nSince log is one-to-one: a/b = a − b.\nMultiply both sides by b: a = ab − b².\nRearrange: a − ab = −b² ⟹ a(1−b) = −b² ⟹ a = b²/(b−1). ✓",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::9(b)": "We need b > 1.\nFor a = b²/(b−1) to be defined: b ≠ 1.\nFor a > 0 (since a > b > 0): b²/(b−1) > 0. Since b > 0 implies b² > 0, we need b − 1 > 0 ⟹ b > 1.\nAlso a − b = b/(b−1) > 0 ✓ (so log(a−b) is defined).\nFull restriction: b > 1.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::10(i)": "Proof by exhaustion — consider n even and n odd.\nCase 1 (n even): n = 2m. n² + 2 = 4m² + 2 = 4m² + 2. Remainder on dividing by 4 is 2, so not divisible by 4.\nCase 2 (n odd): n = 2m+1. n² + 2 = 4m²+4m+1+2 = 4(m²+m)+3. Remainder is 3, so not divisible by 4.\nIn both cases n² + 2 is not divisible by 4. Since every integer is either even or odd, the result holds for all n ∈ ℤ. ∎",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::10(ii)": "The statement '|3x − 28| ≥ x − 9' is sometimes true.\nCounter-example (FALSE): x = 9.5. |3(9.5)−28| = |28.5−28| = 0.5. x−9 = 0.5. So |3x−28| = x−9, meaning the statement is actually true here... try x = 9.4: |3(9.4)−28| = |0.2| = 0.2 < 0.4 = x−9. FALSE.\nExample (TRUE): x = 12. |3(12)−28| = 8 ≥ 3 = x−9. TRUE.\nSince the statement is true for some values and false for others, it is sometimes true.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::11(a)": "Kilometres 1–4 each take 6 minutes: total = 24 minutes.\n5th km: 6 × 1.05 = 6.3 min. 6th km: 6 × 1.05² = 6.615 min.\nTotal = 24 + 6.3 + 6.615 = 36.915 min = 36 min 54.9 s ≈ 36 min 55 s.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::11(b)": "The time for the rth km (r ≥ 5) forms a geometric sequence starting from r = 5.\nTime for rth km = 6 × 1.05^(r−4) minutes.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::11(c)": "First 4 km: 24 min. Km 5 to 20 (16 terms): geometric series, first term = 6×1.05 = 6.3, ratio = 1.05.\nSum = 6.3(1.05^16 − 1)/(1.05 − 1).\n1.05^16 ≈ 2.18287.\nSum = 6.3 × 1.18287/0.05 ≈ 6.3 × 23.657 ≈ 149.04 min.\nTotal ≈ 24 + 149.04 = 173.04 min ≈ 173 min 3 s.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::12(a)": "f(x) = 10e^(−0.25x) sinx. Apply product rule:\nf'(x) = 10[−0.25e^(−0.25x) sinx + e^(−0.25x) cosx] = 10e^(−0.25x)[−0.25 sinx + cosx].\nAt turning points f'(x) = 0; since e^(−0.25x) > 0:\n−0.25 sinx + cosx = 0 ⟹ cosx = 0.25 sinx ⟹ tanx = 4. ✓",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::12(b)": "H(t) = |10e^(−0.25t) sint|. The amplitude 10e^(−0.25t) decreases exponentially. The graph shows a sequence of arches above the t-axis (at each bounce t = nπ the function touches zero). Each successive arch is lower than the previous one, with the arches approaching zero as t → ∞.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::12(c)": "Between the 1st and 2nd bounces (π < t < 2π), a maximum of H occurs at t = π + arctan4 ≈ π + 1.326 ≈ 4.467.\nH(4.467) = 10e^(−0.25×4.467)|sin(4.467)| = 10e^(−1.117) × |sin(π+1.326)| ≈ 10 × 0.3273 × 0.9701 ≈ 3.18 m.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::12(d)": "The model has bounces at t = nπ, equally spaced in time. In reality, as the ball loses energy the time between bounces decreases (the ball moves slower and returns sooner). So the model incorrectly assumes equal time intervals between bounces, making it unreliable for predicting bounce times.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::13(a)": "(i) There is a vertical asymptote x = 2, so the denominator (2x − q)(x + 3) must be zero when x = 2 (the curve is not defined there).\nThe factor (x + 3) is not zero at x = 2, so 2x − q = 0 when x = 2:\n2(2) − q = 0 ⇒ q = 4\n\n(ii) With q = 4, y = (p − 3x)/((2x − 4)(x + 3)).\nThe curve passes through (3, ½), so substitute x = 3, y = ½:\n½ = (p − 3×3)/((2×3 − 4)(3 + 3))\n½ = (p − 9)/((2)(6))\n½ = (p − 9)/12\np − 9 = 6\np = 15",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::13(b)": "From (a), q = 4 and p = 15, so y = (15 − 3x)/((2x − 4)(x + 3)).\nThe curve meets the x-axis where 15 − 3x = 0, i.e. x = 5. So R lies between x = 3 and x = 5 (the curve is above the axis there, e.g. y = ½ at x = 3).\nPartial fractions:\n(15 − 3x)/((2x − 4)(x + 3)) = A/(2x − 4) + B/(x + 3)\n15 − 3x = A(x + 3) + B(2x − 4)\nx = 2: 9 = 5A ⇒ A = 9/5 = 1.8\nx = −3: 24 = −10B ⇒ B = −12/5 = −2.4\nSo y = 1.8/(2x − 4) − 2.4/(x + 3).\nIntegrating:\n∫ y dx = 0.9 ln(2x − 4) − 2.4 ln(x + 3)\nArea R = [0.9 ln(2x − 4) − 2.4 ln(x + 3)]₃⁵\n= (0.9 ln 6 − 2.4 ln 8) − (0.9 ln 2 − 2.4 ln 6)\n= 3.3 ln 6 − 2.4 ln 8 − 0.9 ln 2\nUsing ln 6 = ln 2 + ln 3 and ln 8 = 3 ln 2:\n= 3.3 ln 3 + 3.3 ln 2 − 7.2 ln 2 − 0.9 ln 2\n= 3.3 ln 3 − 4.8 ln 2.\nSo the area of R is 3.3 ln 3 − 4.8 ln 2 (≈ 0.298), i.e. a = −4.8 and b = 3.3.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::14(a)": "x = 4sin2y. Differentiate implicitly w.r.t. y: dx/dy = 8cos2y, so dy/dx = 1/(8cos2y).\nAt the origin: x = 0 ⟹ sin2y = 0 ⟹ y = 0. cos(0) = 1.\ndy/dx = 1/(8 × 1) = 1/8.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::14(b)": "(i) For points close to the origin, y is small, so 2y is small and sin 2y ≈ 2y.\nThen x = 4 sin 2y ≈ 4(2y), so\nx ≈ 8y (equivalently y ≈ x/8)\n\n(ii) In part (a), dx/dy = 8 cos 2y, so dy/dx = 1/(8 cos 2y), which at the origin (y = 0) gives dy/dx = 1/8.\nThe line found in (b)(i), y = x/8, has gradient 1/8.\nSo the value of dy/dx found in (a) is the gradient of the line x = 8y: both have gradient 1/8. Near the origin the curve is approximated by this straight line, which is its tangent at O.",
  "math-edx-9ma0-2019-p1::14(c)": "x = 4 sin 2y ⇒ dx/dy = 8 cos 2y ⇒ dy/dx = 1/(8 cos 2y).\nFrom x = 4 sin 2y, sin 2y = x/4.\nUsing sin²2y + cos²2y = 1: cos²2y = 1 − (x/4)² = (16 − x²)/16.\nFor −π/4 < y < π/4 we have −π/2 < 2y < π/2, so cos 2y > 0 and\ncos 2y = √(1 − (x/4)²) = √(16 − x²)/4.\ndy/dx = 1/(8√(1 − (x/4)²)) = 1/(8 × √(16 − x²)/4) = 1/(2√(16 − x²)).\nSo dy/dx = 1/(2√(16 − x²)), giving a = 2 and b = 16.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::1(a)": "y = f(x) + 2 is a translation of y = f(x) by 2 units up (vector (0, 2)).\nThe x-coordinate is unchanged and 2 is added to the y-coordinate:\n(−2, −5) → (−2, −5 + 2)\nImage of P: (−2, −3)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::1(b)": "y = |f(x)| reflects any part of the curve below the x-axis in the x-axis, so a negative y-coordinate becomes positive and x is unchanged.\nP has y = −5 < 0, so\n(−2, −5) → (−2, |−5|)\nImage of P: (−2, 5)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::1(c)": "y = 3f(x − 2) + 2 is made of three transformations of y = f(x):\n• f(x − 2): translation 2 units right, so x: −2 → −2 + 2 = 0\n• 3f(...): vertical stretch, scale factor 3, so y: −5 → 3 × (−5) = −15\n• + 2: translation 2 units up, so y: −15 → −15 + 2 = −13\nCheck: when x = 0, x − 2 = −2 and f(−2) = −5, so y = 3(−5) + 2 = −13.\nImage of P: (0, −13)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::2": "By the factor theorem, (x + 2) is a factor of f(x) ⇔ f(−2) = 0.\nf(−2) = (−2 − 4)((−2)² − 3(−2) + k) − 42\n      = (−6)(4 + 6 + k) − 42\n      = −6(k + 10) − 42\nSet f(−2) = 0:\n−6(k + 10) − 42 = 0\n−6(k + 10) = 42\nk + 10 = −7\nk = −17",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::3(a)": "Complete the square in x and in y:\nx² − 10x + y² + 16y = 80\n(x − 5)² − 25 + (y + 8)² − 64 = 80\n(x − 5)² + (y + 8)² = 169\nCompare with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²:\n(i) Centre (5, −8)\n(ii) r² = 169, so radius = 13",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::3(b)": "Distance from O to the centre C(5, −8):\nOC = √(5² + (−8)²) = √(25 + 64) = √89 (≈ 9.43)\nSince √89 < 13, O lies inside the circle.\nThe point on the circle furthest from O lies on the line through O and the centre, on the far side of the centre from O. So\nOP = OC + radius\nOP = 13 + √89",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::4(a)": "As δx → 0, the sum of the strips (2/x)δx from x = 2.1 to x = 6.3 becomes the integral of 2/x between those limits:\nlim (δx→0) Σ (from x = 2.1 to 6.3) (2/x) δx = ∫ from 2.1 to 6.3 of (2/x) dx",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::4(b)": "Using part (a):\n∫ from 2.1 to 6.3 of (2/x) dx = [2 ln x] from 2.1 to 6.3\n= 2 ln 6.3 − 2 ln 2.1\n= 2 ln(6.3/2.1)\n= 2 ln 3\n= ln 3²\n= ln 9\nSo the limit equals ln k with k = 9.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::5(a)": "Substitute each data pair into h² = at + b.\nt = 2, h = 2.60: 2.6² = 2a + b ⇒ 2a + b = 6.76   (1)\nt = 10, h = 5.10: 5.1² = 10a + b ⇒ 10a + b = 26.01   (2)\n(2) − (1): 8a = 19.25 ⇒ a = 2.40625\nSubstitute in (1): b = 6.76 − 2(2.40625) = 6.76 − 4.8125 = 1.9475\nTo 3 s.f.: a = 2.41, b = 1.95\nModel: h² = 2.41t + 1.95",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::5(b)": "Substitute t = 20 into the model:\nh² = 2.41 × 20 + 1.95 = 48.2 + 1.95 = 50.15\nh = √50.15 = 7.08 m (3 s.f.)\nThe model predicts a height of 7.08 m, while the actual height was 7 m.\nThese values are very close (within about 1%), so the model is a good model for the height of the tree.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::6(a)": "f′(x) < 0 where the curve is decreasing.\nReading from Figure 1, the curve rises to the maximum at (2, 8), falls to the minimum at (6, 0), then rises again.\nSo C is decreasing between the two turning points:\n2 < x < 6",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::6(b)": "Consider horizontal lines y = k meeting C:\n• 0 < k < 8: the line cuts C three times (once on each rising section and once on the falling section).\n• k = 0 or k = 8: the line touches C at a turning point and also cuts it once more, so it meets C twice.\n• k > 8: the line is above the maximum, so it meets C only once (on the right-hand rising section).\n• k < 0: the line is below the minimum, so it meets C only once (on the left-hand rising section).\nSo y = k meets C at only one point when k > 8 or k < 0.\nIn set notation: {k : k > 8} ∪ {k : k < 0}",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::6(c)": "C passes through the origin, so x is a factor of f(x).\nC has a minimum on the x-axis at (6, 0), so x = 6 is a repeated root and (x − 6)² is a factor.\nf(x) is cubic, so\ny = ax(x − 6)²\nThe maximum (2, 8) lies on C:\n8 = a × 2 × (2 − 6)²\n8 = a × 2 × 16 = 32a\na = ¼\nEquation of C: y = ¼x(x − 6)²",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::7(i)": "Assume, for contradiction, that there exist integers p and q such that pq is even and both p and q are odd.\nSince p and q are odd, write p = 2m + 1 and q = 2n + 1, where m and n are integers.\nThen\npq = (2m + 1)(2n + 1)\n   = 4mn + 2m + 2n + 1\n   = 2(2mn + m + n) + 1\n2mn + m + n is an integer, so pq is one more than an even number, i.e. pq is odd.\nThis contradicts the assumption that pq is even.\nHence the assumption is false: if pq is even, then at least one of p or q is even.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::7(ii)": "Expand the given inequality:\n(x + y)² < 9x² + y²\nx² + 2xy + y² < 9x² + y²\n2xy < 8x²\nNow divide both sides by 2x. Since x < 0, 2x is negative, so the inequality sign reverses:\ny > 8x²/(2x)\ny > 4x\nas required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::8(a)": "The car stops at the second set of lights at t = T, so v = 0 there.\nv = (10 − 0.4t) ln(t + 1) = 0\nEither ln(t + 1) = 0 ⇒ t = 0 (the start), or 10 − 0.4t = 0 ⇒ t = 25.\nSo T = 25",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::8(b)": "v = (10 − 0.4t) ln(t + 1)\nDifferentiate using the product rule:\ndv/dt = −0.4 ln(t + 1) + (10 − 0.4t) × 1/(t + 1)\ndv/dt = −0.4 ln(t + 1) + (10 − 0.4t)/(t + 1)\nAt the maximum speed, dv/dt = 0:\n0.4 ln(t + 1) = (10 − 0.4t)/(t + 1)\nMultiply by 2.5:\nln(t + 1) = (25 − t)/(t + 1)\n(t + 1) ln(t + 1) = 25 − t\nt ln(t + 1) + ln(t + 1) = 25 − t\nt + t ln(t + 1) = 25 − ln(t + 1)\nt(1 + ln(t + 1)) = 25 − ln(t + 1)\nt = (25 − ln(t + 1))/(1 + ln(t + 1))\nWrite the numerator as 26 − (1 + ln(t + 1)):\nt = (26 − (1 + ln(t + 1)))/(1 + ln(t + 1))\nt = 26/(1 + ln(t + 1)) − 1\nFigure 2 shows a single turning point, a maximum, so the maximum speed occurs when t = 26/(1 + ln(t + 1)) − 1.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::8(c)": "(i) t₁ = 7\nt₂ = 26/(1 + ln 8) − 1 = 7.44309...\nt₃ = 26/(1 + ln 8.44309...) − 1 = 7.29783...\nt₃ = 7.298 (3 d.p.)\n(ii) Continue iterating:\nt₄ = 7.34405...\nt₅ = 7.32920...\nt₆ = 7.33396...\nt₇ = 7.33243...\nt₈ = 7.33292...\nThe values settle at 7.33 (to 3 s.f.).\nTime taken to reach maximum speed = 7.33 seconds",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::9(a)": "|PQ→| = √(2² + 3² + (−4)²) = √(4 + 9 + 16) = √29\n|QR→| = √(5² + 0² + (−2)²) = √(25 + 0 + 4) = √29\nSo |PQ→| = |QR→| = √29.\nPQRS is a parallelogram with two adjacent sides equal, so all four sides are equal.\nHence PQRS is a rhombus.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::9(b)": "Find the diagonals. Since PQRS is a parallelogram, PS→ = QR→.\nPR→ = PQ→ + QR→ = (2i + 3j − 4k) + (5i − 2k) = 7i + 3j − 6k\nQS→ = −PQ→ + PS→ = −(2i + 3j − 4k) + (5i − 2k) = 3i − 3j + 2k\nLengths:\n|PR→| = √(7² + 3² + (−6)²) = √(49 + 9 + 36) = √94\n|QS→| = √(3² + (−3)² + 2²) = √(9 + 9 + 4) = √22\nThe diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular bisectors of each other, so\nArea = ½ × |PR→| × |QS→|\n     = ½ × √94 × √22\n     = ½ × √2068\n     = ½ × 2√517\n     = √517",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::10(a)": "At the start of the study t = 0:\nN_b = 45 + 220e⁰ = 45 + 220 = 265\nN_b is measured in thousands, so there were 265 thousand (265 000) bees at the start.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::10(b)": "N_b = 45 + 220e^(0.05t)\nRate of increase:\ndN_b/dt = 220 × 0.05e^(0.05t) = 11e^(0.05t)\nAt t = 10:\ndN_b/dt = 11e^(0.05 × 10) = 11e^(0.5) = 18.1 (3 s.f.)\nSo the number of bees was increasing at a rate of about 18.1 thousand per year, i.e. approximately 18 thousand per year.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::10(c)": "At t = T the numbers are equal:\n45 + 220e^(0.05t) = 10 + 800e^(−0.05t)\n220e^(0.05t) + 35 − 800e^(−0.05t) = 0\nMultiply through by e^(0.05t):\n220(e^(0.05t))² + 35e^(0.05t) − 800 = 0\nLet u = e^(0.05t): 220u² + 35u − 800 = 0\nu = (−35 ± √(35² + 4 × 220 × 800))/(2 × 220) = (−35 ± √705225)/440\nu = 1.8290... or u = −1.9881...\ne^(0.05t) > 0, so reject the negative root: e^(0.05t) = 1.8290...\n0.05t = ln 1.8290...\nt = 20 ln 1.8290... = 12.0758...\nT = 12.08 (2 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::11(a)": "Substitute x = ½ into both equations.\nC₁: y = 2(½)³ + 10 = 2 × ⅛ + 10 = ¼ + 10 = 41/4 = 10.25\nC₂: y = 42(½) − 15(½)² − 7 = 21 − 15/4 − 7 = 41/4 = 10.25\nBoth curves pass through (½, 41/4), so the curves intersect at x = ½.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::11(b)": "At the points of intersection:\n42x − 15x² − 7 = 2x³ + 10\n2x³ + 15x² − 42x + 17 = 0\nFrom (a), x = ½ is a root, so (2x − 1) is a factor.\nDivide: 2x³ + 15x² − 42x + 17 = (2x − 1)(x² + 8x − 17)\nCheck: (2x − 1)(x² + 8x − 17) = 2x³ + 16x² − 34x − x² − 8x + 17 = 2x³ + 15x² − 42x + 17 ✓\nThe other intersection P satisfies x² + 8x − 17 = 0.\nComplete the square:\n(x + 4)² − 16 − 17 = 0\n(x + 4)² = 33\nx = −4 ± √33\nSince x > 0, reject x = −4 − √33 (negative).\nx coordinate of P: x = −4 + √33",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::12": "Integrate by parts with u = ln x and dv/dx = x³, so du/dx = 1/x and v = x⁴/4.\n∫ x³ ln x dx = (x⁴/4) ln x − ∫ (x⁴/4)(1/x) dx\n             = (x⁴/4) ln x − ∫ (x³/4) dx\n             = (x⁴/4) ln x − x⁴/16 (+ c)\nApply the limits 1 and e², using ln e² = 2, ln 1 = 0 and (e²)⁴ = e⁸:\n∫ from 1 to e² of x³ ln x dx = [(x⁴/4) ln x − x⁴/16] from 1 to e²\n= (e⁸/4 × 2 − e⁸/16) − (1/4 × 0 − 1/16)\n= (e⁸/2 − e⁸/16) + 1/16\n= (8e⁸ − e⁸)/16 + 1/16\n= (7/16)e⁸ + 1/16\nSo a = 7/16 and b = 1/16.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::13(i)": "Write the sum of the first n terms forwards and backwards:\nSₙ = a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) + ... + (a + (n − 2)d) + (a + (n − 1)d)\nSₙ = (a + (n − 1)d) + (a + (n − 2)d) + (a + (n − 3)d) + ... + (a + d) + a\nAdd the two lines term by term. Each pair sums to 2a + (n − 1)d, for example\na + (a + (n − 1)d) = 2a + (n − 1)d\n(a + d) + (a + (n − 2)d) = 2a + (n − 1)d\nThere are n such pairs, so\n2Sₙ = n[2a + (n − 1)d]\nSₙ = (n/2)[2a + (n − 1)d]",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::13(ii)(a)": "The savings form an arithmetic series with a = 10 and d = 9.20 − 10 = −0.8.\nAfter n weeks the total is £64:\n64 = (n/2)[2 × 10 + (n − 1)(−0.8)]\n64 = (n/2)(20 − 0.8(n − 1))\n128 = n(20 − 0.8n + 0.8)\n128 = 20.8n − 0.8n²\n0.8n² − 20.8n + 128 = 0\nDivide by 0.8:\nn² − 26n + 160 = 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::13(ii)(b)": "n² − 26n + 160 = 0\n(n − 10)(n − 16) = 0\nn = 10 or n = 16",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::13(ii)(c)": "James takes 10 weeks.\nReason: he has saved exactly £64 after 10 weeks, so he would not keep saving for a further 6 weeks. (After week 13 the amounts saved would become negative, e.g. week 14 would be 10 − 13 × 0.8 = −£0.40, so n = 16 does not make sense.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::14(a)": "Expand both sides with the compound angle formulae:\n2 sin(x − 60°) = 2 sin x cos 60° − 2 cos x sin 60°\ncos(x − 30°) = cos x cos 30° + sin x sin 30°\nSo\n2 sin x cos 60° − 2 cos x sin 60° = cos x cos 30° + sin x sin 30°\nUse exact values cos 60° = ½, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, sin 30° = ½:\n2 sin x × ½ − 2 cos x × √3/2 = cos x × √3/2 + sin x × ½\nsin x − √3 cos x = (√3/2) cos x + ½ sin x\nCollect terms:\n½ sin x = √3 cos x + (√3/2) cos x = (3√3/2) cos x\nDivide by cos x and use sin x/cos x = tan x:\n½ tan x = 3√3/2\ntan x = 3√3",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::14(b)": "Let x = 2θ + 60°. Then x − 60° = 2θ and x − 30° = 2θ + 30°, so\n2 sin 2θ = cos(2θ + 30°) is 2 sin(x − 60°) = cos(x − 30°).\nBy part (a): tan(2θ + 60°) = 3√3\nFor 0 ≤ θ < 180°: 60° ≤ 2θ + 60° < 420°\nPrincipal value: arctan(3√3) = 79.107°...\ntan has period 180°, so\n2θ + 60° = 79.107°, 259.107° (the next, 439.1°, is out of range)\n2θ = 19.107°, 199.107°\nθ = 9.553°..., 99.553°...\nθ = 9.6°, 99.6° (1 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::15(a)": "Area of each sector face (ABC and DEF) = ½r²θ = ½r²(0.8) = 0.4r²\nVolume = cross-section area × length:\n0.4r²h = 240 ⇒ h = 240/(0.4r²) = 600/r²\nSurface area: two sector faces, two rectangular faces (ABED and ACFD, each r × h) and the curved face (arc length × h):\nArc BC = rθ = 0.8r, so curved face area = 0.8rh\nS = 2(0.4r²) + 2rh + 0.8rh = 0.8r² + 2.8rh\nSubstitute h = 600/r²:\nS = 0.8r² + 2.8r × 600/r²\nS = 0.8r² + 1680/r",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::15(b)": "S = 0.8r² + 1680r⁻¹\ndS/dr = 1.6r − 1680r⁻² = 1.6r − 1680/r²\nAt a stationary point dS/dr = 0:\n1.6r = 1680/r²\nr³ = 1680/1.6 = 1050\nr = ∛1050 = 10.16...\nr = 10.2 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::15(c)": "dS/dr = 1.6r − 1680r⁻²\nd²S/dr² = 1.6 + 3360r⁻³ = 1.6 + 3360/r³\nAt the stationary point r³ = 1050:\nd²S/dr² = 1.6 + 3360/1050 = 1.6 + 3.2 = 4.8\nd²S/dr² = 4.8 > 0, so this value of r (r ≈ 10.2) gives the minimum surface area of the toy.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::16(a)": "Area R = ∫ y dx = ∫ y (dx/dt) dt\nx = 8 sin²t ⇒ dx/dt = 16 sin t cos t\nUsing sin 2t = 2 sin t cos t:\ny (dx/dt) = (2 sin 2t + 3 sin t) × 16 sin t cos t\n          = (4 sin t cos t + 3 sin t) × 16 sin t cos t\n          = 64 sin²t cos²t + 48 sin²t cos t\nNow 64 sin²t cos²t = 16(2 sin t cos t)² = 16 sin²2t\nand cos 4t = 1 − 2 sin²2t ⇒ sin²2t = (1 − cos 4t)/2, so\n16 sin²2t = 8(1 − cos 4t) = 8 − 8 cos 4t\nHence y (dx/dt) = 8 − 8 cos 4t + 48 sin²t cos t\nLimits: at x = 0, sin²t = 0 ⇒ t = 0; at x = 4, 8 sin²t = 4 ⇒ sin²t = ½ ⇒ sin t = 1/√2 ⇒ t = π/4 (as 0 ≤ t ≤ π/2).\nSo Area of R = ∫ from 0 to π/4 of (8 − 8 cos 4t + 48 sin²t cos t) dt\na = π/4",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p1::16(b)": "Integrate term by term (48 sin²t cos t is of the form f′(t)[f(t)]² with f(t) = sin t):\n∫ (8 − 8 cos 4t + 48 sin²t cos t) dt = 8t − 2 sin 4t + 16 sin³t\nApply the limits 0 and π/4:\nAt t = π/4: 8(π/4) − 2 sin π + 16(sin π/4)³ = 2π − 0 + 16(√2/2)³ = 2π + 16 × (2√2/8) = 2π + 4√2\nAt t = 0: 0 − 0 + 0 = 0\nArea of R = 2π + 4√2",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::1": "|3 − 2x| = 7 + x means either 3 − 2x = 7 + x or −(3 − 2x) = 7 + x.\n\nCase 1: 3 − 2x = 7 + x\n−3x = 4\nx = −4/3\n\nCase 2: −(3 − 2x) = 7 + x\n2x − 3 = 7 + x\nx = 10\n\nCheck both: at x = −4/3, |3 + 8/3| = 17/3 and 7 − 4/3 = 17/3 ✓\nAt x = 10, |3 − 20| = 17 and 7 + 10 = 17 ✓\n\nx = −4/3 or x = 10",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::2(a)": "Sketch: an exponential growth curve lying entirely above the x-axis (in quadrants 1 and 2 only).\n• For large negative x the curve flattens out and approaches the x-axis (the x-axis is an asymptote, y → 0), but never touches it.\n• It crosses the y-axis at (0, 1), since 4⁰ = 1.\n• For x > 0 it rises increasingly steeply.\n\nThere is no intersection with the x-axis, because 4ˣ > 0 for all x.\n\nIntercept: (0, 1)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::2(b)": "4ˣ = 100\nTake logs of both sides:\nx log 4 = log 100\nx = log 100 / log 4 (= log₄ 100)\nx = 3.3219…\n\nx = 3.32 (2 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::3(a)": "(i) a₁ = 3\na₂ = 8 − a₁ = 8 − 3 = 5\na₃ = 8 − a₂ = 8 − 5 = 3\na₄ = 8 − a₃ = 5, …\n\nIn general aₙ₊₂ = 8 − aₙ₊₁ = 8 − (8 − aₙ) = aₙ, so the terms repeat 3, 5, 3, 5, … and the sequence is periodic.\n\n(ii) The sequence repeats every 2 terms, so its order is 2.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::3(b)": "The terms go 3, 5, 3, 5, …, so the odd-numbered terms are 3 and the even-numbered terms are 5.\n\nFrom n = 1 to 85 there are 42 complete pairs (3 + 5) followed by a₈₅ = 3 (85 is odd).\n\nΣ aₙ (n = 1 to 85) = 42 × (3 + 5) + 3\n= 336 + 3\n= 339",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::4": "y = 2x²\nGradient of the chord between (x, 2x²) and (x + h, 2(x + h)²):\n\n(2(x + h)² − 2x²)/h\n= (2x² + 4xh + 2h² − 2x²)/h\n= (4xh + 2h²)/h\n= 4x + 2h\n\ndy/dx = lim (h → 0) (4x + 2h) = 4x\n\nSo dy/dx = 4x, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::5(a)": "The strip width is h = 4.5 − 3 = 1.5\n\nTrapezium rule:\n∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ 2x dx ≈ (h/2) × [y₀ + y₄ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)]\n= (1.5/2) × [1.63 + 2.63 + 2(2 + 2.26 + 2.46)]\n= 0.75 × [4.26 + 2 × 6.72]\n= 0.75 × [4.26 + 13.44]\n= 0.75 × 17.7\n= 13.275\n\n≈ 13.3",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::5(b)": "(i) Using the power law, log₃ (2x)¹⁰ = 10 log₃ 2x, so\n\n∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ (2x)¹⁰ dx = 10 × ∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ 2x dx\n≈ 10 × 13.275\n= 132.75\n≈ 133\n\n(ii) log₃ 18x = log₃ (9 × 2x) = log₃ 9 + log₃ 2x = 2 + log₃ 2x, so\n\n∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ 18x dx = ∫ from 3 to 9 of 2 dx + ∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ 2x dx\n= [2x] from 3 to 9 + ∫ from 3 to 9 of log₃ 2x dx\n= (18 − 6) + 13.275\n= 12 + 13.275\n= 25.275\n≈ 25.3",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::6(a)": "f(x) = 8 sin(½x) − 3x + 9\nf′(x) = 8 × ½ cos(½x) − 3 = 4 cos(½x) − 3\n\nAt a turning point f′(x) = 0:\n4 cos(½x) − 3 = 0\ncos(½x) = 3/4\n\n½x = cos⁻¹(3/4) = 0.7227…, or 2π − 0.7227… = 5.5605…, or 2π + 0.7227… = 7.0059…, …\nx = 1.445…, 11.12…, 14.01…, …\n\nFrom Figure 2, the first turning point (x = 1.445…) is the maximum close to the y-axis, the second (x = 11.12…) is the minimum to the right of α, and P is the next turning point, a local maximum.\n(Check: f″(x) = −2 sin(½x), and at x = 14.01…, sin(7.0059…) = sin(0.7227…) > 0, so f″ < 0, confirming a maximum.)\n\nSo the x coordinate of P is x = 14.0 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::6(b)": "f(4) = 4.274 > 0 and f(5) = −1.212 < 0, so there is a change of sign between x = 4 and x = 5.\n\nf(x) = 8 sin(½x) − 3x + 9 is continuous on [4, 5] (it is made of sine and linear functions, so its graph has no breaks).\n\nSince f is continuous and changes sign on [4, 5], the curve must cross the x-axis in this interval, so α lies in [4, 5].",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::6(c)": "Newton–Raphson: x₁ = x₀ − f(x₀)/f′(x₀), with f′(x) = 4 cos(½x) − 3\n\nf(5) = 8 sin 2.5 − 15 + 9 = −1.2122…\nf′(5) = 4 cos 2.5 − 3 = −6.2045…\n\nx₁ = 5 − (8 sin 2.5 − 15 + 9)/(4 cos 2.5 − 3)\n= 5 − (−1.2122…)/(−6.2045…)\n= 5 − 0.19537…\n= 4.8046…\n\nx₁ = 4.80 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::7(a)": "√(4 − 9x) = (4 − 9x)^(½) = 4^(½) (1 − 9x/4)^(½) = 2(1 − 9x/4)^(½)\n\nUsing (1 + u)ⁿ = 1 + nu + n(n − 1)u²/2! + n(n − 1)(n − 2)u³/3! + … with n = ½ and u = −9x/4:\n\n(1 − 9x/4)^(½) = 1 + (½)(−9x/4) + [(½)(−½)/2!](−9x/4)² + [(½)(−½)(−3/2)/3!](−9x/4)³ + …\n= 1 − 9x/8 + (−1/8)(81x²/16) + (1/16)(−729x³/64) + …\n= 1 − 9x/8 − 81x²/128 − 729x³/1024 + …\n\nMultiplying by 2:\n√(4 − 9x) = 2 − 9x/4 − 81x²/64 − 729x³/512 + …",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::7(b)": "Overestimate.\n\nWith x = 1/9 > 0, every term after the first (−9x/4, −81x²/64, −729x³/512, and all later terms) is negative. The approximation stops after four terms, so it leaves out further terms that would all be subtracted. The approximation is therefore larger than the true value of √(4 − 9 × 1/9) = √3, so it is an overestimate.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::8": "The curve meets the x-axis where (x − 2)(x − 4) = 0, so at x = 2 and x = 4. These are the limits for R.\n\nWrite y as a sum of powers of x:\ny = (x − 2)(x − 4)/(4√x) = (x² − 6x + 8)/(4x^(½))\n= ¼x^(3/2) − (3/2)x^(½) + 2x^(−½)\n\nIntegrate:\n∫ (¼x^(3/2) − (3/2)x^(½) + 2x^(−½)) dx\n= ¼ × (2/5)x^(5/2) − (3/2) × (2/3)x^(3/2) + 2 × 2x^(½)\n= (1/10)x^(5/2) − x^(3/2) + 4x^(½) (+ c)\n\nApply the limits 2 and 4:\nAt x = 4: (1/10)(32) − 8 + 4(2) = 16/5\nAt x = 2: (1/10)(4√2) − 2√2 + 4√2 = (2/5)√2 + 2√2 = (12/5)√2\n\n∫ from 2 to 4 of y dx = 16/5 − (12/5)√2\n\nThis is negative because R lies below the x-axis, so\n\nArea of R = (12/5)√2 − 16/5\n(a = 12/5, b = −16/5)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::9(a)": "Maximum height: the greatest value of |A sin(bt + α)°| is |A|, so |A| = 50 and we can take A = 50.\n\nPeriod: Figure 5 shows that one revolution (720 s) corresponds to one arch of the modulus graph, i.e. the angle bt + α increases by 180°.\nSo 720b = 180, giving b = ¼.\n\nStart: when t = 0, H = 1:\n1 = 50 sin(α)°\nsin(α)° = 1/50\nα = sin⁻¹(0.02) = 1.1459…° = 1.15 (3 s.f.)\n\nModel: H = |50 sin(¼t + 1.15)°|",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::9(b)": "In the original model the minimum value of |A sin(bt + α)°| is 0, so the model predicts the passenger's height is 0 m at the bottom of each revolution, i.e. the passenger touches the ground. In reality the lowest point of the wheel is above the ground. Adding the positive constant d raises the whole graph by d m, so the minimum height is d m and the passenger never touches the ground.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::10(a)": "f⁻¹(3/2) is the value of x for which f(x) = 3/2:\n\n(8x + 5)/(2x + 3) = 3/2\n2(8x + 5) = 3(2x + 3)\n16x + 10 = 6x + 9\n10x = −1\nx = −1/10 (which satisfies x > −3/2)\n\nf⁻¹(3/2) = −1/10",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::10(b)": "Write the numerator in terms of the denominator:\n8x + 5 = 4(2x + 3) − 12 + 5 = 4(2x + 3) − 7\n\nSo f(x) = [4(2x + 3) − 7]/(2x + 3) = 4 − 7/(2x + 3)\n\nA = 4, B = −7",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::10(c)": "The range of g⁻¹ is the domain of g.\n\n0 ≤ g⁻¹(x) ≤ 4",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::10(d)": "fg⁻¹(x) = f(g⁻¹(x)), and from (c) the values of g⁻¹(x) fill the interval [0, 4].\n\nFrom (b), f(x) = 4 − 7/(2x + 3). For x > −3/2, as x increases 2x + 3 increases, so 7/(2x + 3) decreases and f(x) increases. So f is increasing on [0, 4] and its least and greatest values there are at the end points:\n\nf(0) = (8 × 0 + 5)/(2 × 0 + 3) = 5/3\nf(4) = (8 × 4 + 5)/(2 × 4 + 3) = 37/11\n\nRange of fg⁻¹: 5/3 ≤ fg⁻¹(x) ≤ 37/11",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::11": "Every natural number n is either even or odd.\n\nCase 1: n is even, n = 2k for some integer k.\nn(n² + 5) = 2k(4k² + 5)\nThis is 2 × (an integer), so it is even.\n\nCase 2: n is odd, n = 2k + 1 for some integer k.\nn² + 5 = 4k² + 4k + 1 + 5 = 4k² + 4k + 6 = 2(2k² + 2k + 3)\nn(n² + 5) = 2(2k + 1)(2k² + 2k + 3)\nThis is 2 × (an integer), so it is even.\n\nIn both cases n(n² + 5) is even, so n(n² + 5) is even for all n ∈ ℕ.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::12(a)": "f(x) = e^(3x)/(4x² + k)\nQuotient rule with u = e^(3x), u′ = 3e^(3x), v = 4x² + k, v′ = 8x:\n\nf′(x) = (v u′ − u v′)/v²\n= [(4x² + k) × 3e^(3x) − e^(3x) × 8x]/(4x² + k)²\n= e^(3x)(12x² + 3k − 8x)/(4x² + k)²\n= (12x² − 8x + 3k) × e^(3x)/(4x² + k)²\n\nSo f′(x) = (12x² − 8x + 3k) g(x), where g(x) = e^(3x)/(4x² + k)²",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::12(b)": "Stationary points occur where f′(x) = 0. Since e^(3x) > 0 and (4x² + k)² > 0 (k > 0), g(x) is never 0, so we need\n12x² − 8x + 3k = 0\nto have at least one real root.\n\nDiscriminant ≥ 0:\n(−8)² − 4(12)(3k) ≥ 0\n64 − 144k ≥ 0\nk ≤ 64/144 = 4/9\n\nk is a positive constant, so\n\n0 < k ≤ 4/9",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::13(a)": "AB = OB − OA = (4j + 6k) − (4i − 3j + 5k) = −4i + 7j + k\nAC = OC − OA = (−16i + pj + 10k) − (4i − 3j + 5k) = −20i + (p + 3)j + 5k\n\nA, B and C are collinear, so AC is a multiple of AB.\nComparing i components: −20 = 5 × (−4), and k components: 5 = 5 × 1, so AC = 5AB.\nThen the j components give p + 3 = 5 × 7 = 35\n\np = 32",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::13(b)": "D lies on OB extended, so OD = λOB = 4λj + 6λk for some λ.\n\nCD = OD − OC = (4λj + 6λk) − (−16i + 32j + 10k)\n= 16i + (4λ − 32)j + (6λ − 10)k\n\nCD is parallel to OA = 4i − 3j + 5k. Comparing i components, CD = 4 × OA, so\n4λ − 32 = 4 × (−3) = −12 ⇒ λ = 5\n6λ − 10 = 4 × 5 = 20 ⇒ λ = 5 (consistent)\n\nOD = 20j + 30k\n|OD| = √(20² + 30²) = √1300 = 10√13",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::14(a)": "3/((2x − 1)(x + 1)) = P/(2x − 1) + Q/(x + 1)\n3 = P(x + 1) + Q(2x − 1)\n\nx = −1: 3 = −3Q ⇒ Q = −1\nx = ½: 3 = (3/2)P ⇒ P = 2\n\n3/((2x − 1)(x + 1)) = 2/(2x − 1) − 1/(x + 1)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::14(b)": "dV/dt = 3V/((2t − 1)(t + 1))\n\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ (1/V) dV = ∫ 3/((2t − 1)(t + 1)) dt\n\nUsing (a):\n∫ (1/V) dV = ∫ (2/(2t − 1) − 1/(t + 1)) dt\nln V = ln(2t − 1) − ln(t + 1) + c\n\nWhen t = 2, V = 3:\nln 3 = ln 3 − ln 3 + c ⇒ c = ln 3\n\nln V = ln(2t − 1) − ln(t + 1) + ln 3 = ln[3(2t − 1)/(t + 1)]\n\nV = 3(2t − 1)/(t + 1)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::14(c)": "(i) Oxygen starts being produced when V = 0:\n3(2t − 1)/(t + 1) = 0 ⇒ 2t − 1 = 0 ⇒ t = ½ hour\nTime delay = 30 minutes\n\n(ii) V = 3(2t − 1)/(t + 1) = 3(2 − 1/t)/(1 + 1/t)\nAs t → ∞, 1/t → 0, so V → 3 × 2/1 = 6\nLimit = 6 m³",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::15(a)": "In a geometric series the ratio of consecutive terms is constant:\n(5 + 2 sin θ)/(12 cos θ) = 6 tan θ/(5 + 2 sin θ)\n\nCross-multiply:\n(5 + 2 sin θ)² = 6 tan θ × 12 cos θ = 72 tan θ cos θ\n\nSince tan θ cos θ = sin θ:\n(5 + 2 sin θ)² = 72 sin θ\n25 + 20 sin θ + 4 sin²θ = 72 sin θ\n\n4 sin²θ − 52 sin θ + 25 = 0, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::15(b)": "4 sin²θ − 52 sin θ + 25 = 0\n(2 sin θ − 1)(2 sin θ − 25) = 0\nsin θ = ½ or sin θ = 25/2\n\nsin θ = 25/2 is impossible since −1 ≤ sin θ ≤ 1, so sin θ = ½.\n\nsin θ = ½ gives θ = π/6 or 5π/6. θ is obtuse (π/2 < θ < π), so\n\nθ = 5π/6",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::15(c)": "With θ = 5π/6: cos θ = −√3/2, sin θ = ½\n\nFirst term: a = 12 cos θ = 12 × (−√3/2) = −6√3\nSecond term: 5 + 2 sin θ = 5 + 1 = 6\nCommon ratio: r = 6/(−6√3) = −1/√3\n\n|r| = 1/√3 < 1, so the sum to infinity exists.\n\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = −6√3/(1 + 1/√3)\nMultiply numerator and denominator by √3:\nS∞ = −18/(√3 + 1)\nRationalise the denominator:\nS∞ = −18(√3 − 1)/((√3 + 1)(√3 − 1)) = −18(√3 − 1)/2 = −9(√3 − 1)\n\nS∞ = 9(1 − √3), so k = 9",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::16(a)": "x = 2 tan t + 1 ⇒ dx/dt = 2sec²t\ny = 2sec²t + 3 ⇒ dy/dt = 2 × 2 sec t × sec t tan t = 4sec²t tan t\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = 4sec²t tan t/(2sec²t) = 2 tan t\n\nAt t = π/4: tan(π/4) = 1, sec²(π/4) = 2\nx = 2(1) + 1 = 3, y = 2(2) + 3 = 7, dy/dx = 2\n\nGradient of the normal = −1/2\nNormal at P(3, 7):\ny − 7 = −½(x − 3)\ny = −½x + 3/2 + 7\n\ny = −½x + 17/2, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::16(b)": "From x = 2 tan t + 1: tan t = (x − 1)/2\nFrom y = 2sec²t + 3: sec²t = (y − 3)/2\n\nUsing sec²t = 1 + tan²t:\n(y − 3)/2 = 1 + ((x − 1)/2)²\ny − 3 = 2 + 2(x − 1)²/4\ny − 3 = 2 + (x − 1)²/2\n\ny = ½(x − 1)² + 5, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p2::16(c)": "C is the part of y = ½(x − 1)² + 5 between the end points:\nt = −π/4: x = 2(−1) + 1 = −1, y = 2(2) + 3 = 7, giving (−1, 7)\nt = π/3: x = 2√3 + 1, y = 2(4) + 3 = 11, giving (1 + 2√3, 11)\nSo C is the parabola arc for −1 ≤ x ≤ 1 + 2√3.\n\nIntersections with y = −½x + k:\n½(x − 1)² + 5 = −½x + k\n(x − 1)² + 10 = −x + 2k\nx² − 2x + 1 + 10 + x − 2k = 0\nx² − x + (11 − 2k) = 0\n\nLower limit (line is a tangent): discriminant = 0\n(−1)² − 4(1)(11 − 2k) = 0\n1 − 44 + 8k = 0 ⇒ k = 43/8\nThe repeated root is x = ½, which lies on C, so for k = 43/8 there is only one point of contact; for k > 43/8 the discriminant is positive and there are two distinct roots.\n\nUpper limit (line through the end point (−1, 7)):\n7 = −½(−1) + k ⇒ k = 13/2\nThe roots of x² − x + (11 − 2k) = 0 add to 1, so they are x and 1 − x. When k = 13/2 the equation is x² − x − 2 = 0, giving x = −1 and x = 2, both on C (−1 ≤ x ≤ 1 + 2√3), so there are still two intersection points.\nFor k > 13/2 the smaller root is less than −1, which is off C, so there is at most one intersection point.\n(The larger root never passes the other end of C here, because it is at most 2 < 1 + 2√3 while the smaller root is ≥ −1.)\n\n43/8 < k ≤ 13/2",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::1": "Expand the numerator and split the fraction:\nx^(½)(2x − 5)/3 = (2x^(3/2) − 5x^(½))/3 = (2/3)x^(3/2) − (5/3)x^(½)\n\nIntegrate each term by adding 1 to the power and dividing by the new power:\n∫ (2/3)x^(3/2) dx = (2/3) × x^(5/2)/(5/2) = (2/3)(2/5)x^(5/2) = (4/15)x^(5/2)\n∫ (5/3)x^(½) dx = (5/3) × x^(3/2)/(3/2) = (5/3)(2/3)x^(3/2) = (10/9)x^(3/2)\n\nSo\n∫ x^(½)(2x − 5)/3 dx = (4/15)x^(5/2) − (10/9)x^(3/2) + c",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::2(a)": "Since (x − a) is a factor of f(x), the factor theorem gives f(a) = 0.\n\nf(a) = 4a³ + 5a² − 10a + 4a = 0\n4a³ + 5a² − 6a = 0\n\nTake out the common factor a:\na(4a² + 5a − 6) = 0, as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::2(b)": "(i) From part (a), a(4a² + 5a − 6) = 0\nFactorise the quadratic: a(4a − 3)(a + 2) = 0\nSo a = 0, a = ¾ or a = −2.\na is a positive constant, so a = ¾.\n\n(ii) With a = ¾, f(x) = 4x³ + 5x² − 10x + 4 × ¾ = 4x³ + 5x² − 10x + 3\n\nf(x) = 3 ⇒ 4x³ + 5x² − 10x + 3 = 3\n⇒ 4x³ + 5x² − 10x = 0\n⇒ x(4x² + 5x − 10) = 0\n\nSo x = 0, or 4x² + 5x − 10 = 0.\n\nQuadratic formula:\nx = (−5 ± √(5² − 4 × 4 × (−10)))/(2 × 4) = (−5 ± √(25 + 160))/8 = (−5 ± √185)/8\n\nThe exact solutions are x = 0, x = (−5 + √185)/8 and x = (−5 − √185)/8.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::3(a)": "OA = 5i + 3j + 2k, so\n|OA| = √(5² + 3² + 2²) = √(25 + 9 + 4) = √38, as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::3(b)": "OB = 2i + 4j + ak, so\n|OB| = √(2² + 4² + a²) = √(20 + a²)\n\nWe need |OB| > |OA|:\n√(20 + a²) > √38\n20 + a² > 38\na² > 18\n\na is a positive integer. a = 4 gives a² = 16 < 18 (|OB| = √36 < √38), while a = 5 gives a² = 25 > 18 (|OB| = √45 > √38).\n\nThe smallest value is a = 5.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::4(a)": "At the stationary point f′(α) = 0:\n2α + ½cos α = 0\n\nα is small, so use cos α ≈ 1 − α²/2:\n2α + ½(1 − α²/2) = 0\n2α + ½ − α²/4 = 0\n\nMultiply by −4:\nα² − 8α − 2 = 0\n\nα = (8 ± √(64 + 8))/2 = (8 ± √72)/2 = 4 ± 3√2\n\nSo α = 4 − 3√2 = −0.2426… or α = 4 + 3√2 = 8.243…\nα is small, so reject 8.243 (the approximation is only valid for small angles).\n\nα = −0.243 (3 d.p.)",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::4(b)": "Gradient of the tangent at P(0, 3):\nf′(0) = 2(0) + ½cos 0 = ½ × 1 = ½\n\nTangent through (0, 3) with gradient ½:\ny − 3 = ½(x − 0)\n\ny = ½x + 3  (so m = ½, c = 3)",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::5(a)": "There are 5 strips between x = 3 and x = 4, so h = (4 − 3)/5 = 0.2.\n\nTrapezium rule: area ≈ ½h{y₀ + y₅ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃ + y₄)}\n\n½ × 0.2 × {a + 13.5 + 2(16.8 + b + 20.2 + 18.7)} = 17.59\n0.1 × {a + 13.5 + 2(55.7 + b)} = 17.59\na + 13.5 + 111.4 + 2b = 175.9\na + 2b + 124.9 = 175.9\na + 2b = 51, as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::5(b)": "Sum of the y values:\na + 16.8 + b + 20.2 + 18.7 + 13.5 = 97.2\na + b + 69.2 = 97.2\na + b = 28\n\nFrom part (a): a + 2b = 51\nSubtract: (a + 2b) − (a + b) = 51 − 28 ⇒ b = 23\nThen a = 28 − 23 = 5\n\na = 5 and b = 23",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::6(a)": "log₂ √x = log₂ x^(½) = ½ log₂ x = ½a",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::6(b)": "x² + 8x = x(x + 8), so\nlog₂(x² + 8x) = log₂ x(x + 8) = log₂ x + log₂(x + 8) = a + b",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::6(c)": "Write the bracket as a single fraction:\n8 + 64/x = (8x + 64)/x = 8(x + 8)/x\n\nSo\nlog₂(8 + 64/x) = log₂ 8 + log₂(x + 8) − log₂ x\n\nSince 2³ = 8, log₂ 8 = 3, so\nlog₂(8 + 64/x) = 3 + b − a",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::7(a)": "For x > 2, √(x − 2) takes every positive value (but never 0, since x ≠ 2).\nSo 3 + √(x − 2) takes every value greater than 3.\n\nRange: f(x) > 3",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::7(b)": "Let y = 3 + √(x − 2) and make x the subject:\n√(x − 2) = y − 3\nx − 2 = (y − 3)²\nx = (y − 3)² + 2\n\nSo f⁻¹(x) = (x − 3)² + 2\n\nThe domain of f⁻¹ is the range of f, so\nf⁻¹(x) = (x − 3)² + 2, x > 3",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::7(c)": "f(6) = 3 + √(6 − 2) = 3 + √4 = 3 + 2 = 5\n\ngf(6) = g(5) = 15/(5 − 3) = 15/2",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::7(d)": "f(a² + 2) = 3 + √(a² + 2 − 2) = 3 + √(a²)\ng(a) = 15/(a − 3)\n\nThe left side is greater than 3, so g(a) > 0, which needs a > 3. Hence a is positive and √(a²) = a.\n\n3 + a = 15/(a − 3)\n(a + 3)(a − 3) = 15\na² − 9 = 15\na² = 24\na = ±√24 = ±2√6\n\na = −2√6 is rejected: then g(a) = 15/(−2√6 − 3) is negative, but f(a² + 2) = 3 + 2√6 is positive, so they are not equal.\n\na = 2√6",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::8(a)": "Arc length s = rθ for sector OCDE, with s = 27.6 m and θ = 2.3 rad:\nOC × 2.3 = 27.6\nOC = 27.6/2.3 = 12 m, as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::8(b)": "AOG is a straight line, so the angles at O along it add to π:\nangle AOB + angle COE + angle GOF = π\n\nTriangles ABO and GFO are congruent, so angle AOB = angle GOF:\n2 × angle AOB = π − 2.3\nangle AOB = (π − 2.3)/2 = 0.8415…/2 = 0.42079…\n\nangle AOB = 0.421 rad (3 d.p.), as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::8(c)": "Area of sector OCDE:\n½r²θ = ½ × 12² × 2.3 = 165.6 m²\n\nLength OB:\nThe front edge is BC + arc CDE + EF = 35, and BC = EF (congruent triangles), so\nBC = (35 − 27.6)/2 = 3.7 m\nOB = OC + CB = 12 + 3.7 = 15.7 m\n\nLength OA:\nOA = OG (congruent triangles) and AOG = 15 m, so OA = 7.5 m\n\nArea of triangle AOB:\n½ × OA × OB × sin(angle AOB) = ½ × 7.5 × 15.7 × sin(0.42079…) = 24.05 m² (2 d.p.)\nTriangle GFO has the same area.\n\nTotal area = 165.6 + 2 × 24.05 = 213.7 m²\n\nTotal area of the stage = 214 m² (to the nearest m²)",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::9(a)": "In a geometric sequence the ratio of consecutive terms is constant:\n(12 − 3k)/(3k + 4) = (k + 16)/(12 − 3k)\n\nCross-multiply:\n(12 − 3k)² = (3k + 4)(k + 16)\n144 − 72k + 9k² = 3k² + 48k + 4k + 64\n144 − 72k + 9k² = 3k² + 52k + 64\n6k² − 124k + 80 = 0\n\nDivide by 2:\n3k² − 62k + 40 = 0, as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::9(b)": "(i) Solve 3k² − 62k + 40 = 0:\n(3k − 2)(k − 20) = 0\nk = ⅔ or k = 20\n\nk = ⅔: terms are 6, 10, 50/3, so r = 10/6 = 5/3. |r| > 1, so the sequence would diverge.\nk = 20: terms are 64, −48, 36, so r = −48/64 = −¾. |r| = ¾ < 1, so the sequence converges.\n\nThe sequence converges, so k = 20 (because it gives r = −¾ with |r| < 1).\n\n(ii) With k = 20: first term a = 3(20) + 4 = 64 and r = −¾.\n\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = 64/(1 − (−¾)) = 64/(7/4) = 256/7",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::10(a)": "Complete the square on x and y:\nx² + 6kx = (x + 3k)² − 9k²\ny² − 2ky = (y − k)² − k²\n\nSo\n(x + 3k)² − 9k² + (y − k)² − k² + 7 = 0\n(x + 3k)² + (y − k)² = 10k² − 7\n\n(i) Centre (−3k, k)\n\n(ii) Radius √(10k² − 7)",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::10(b)": "Substitute y = 2x − 1 into x² + y² + 6kx − 2ky + 7 = 0:\nx² + (2x − 1)² + 6kx − 2k(2x − 1) + 7 = 0\nx² + 4x² − 4x + 1 + 6kx − 4kx + 2k + 7 = 0\n5x² + (2k − 4)x + 2k + 8 = 0\n\nTwo distinct intersection points means two distinct real roots, so b² − 4ac > 0:\n(2k − 4)² − 4 × 5 × (2k + 8) > 0\n4k² − 16k + 16 − 40k − 160 > 0\n4k² − 56k − 144 > 0\nk² − 14k − 36 > 0\n\nCritical values: k² − 14k − 36 = 0\nk = (14 ± √(196 + 144))/2 = (14 ± √340)/2 = 7 ± √85\n\nThe quadratic in k has positive k² coefficient, so it is positive outside the roots:\nk < 7 − √85 or k > 7 + √85",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::11(a)": "The initial value is when t = 0. From the graph, log₁₀ V = 3 when t = 0, so\nV = 10³ = 1000\n\nInitial value = £1000",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::11(b)": "Take logs of V = abᵗ:\nlog₁₀ V = log₁₀ a + t log₁₀ b\nThis is a straight line with intercept log₁₀ a and gradient log₁₀ b.\n\nGradient = (2.79 − 3)/(10 − 0) = −0.021, so log₁₀ b = −0.021\nb = 10^(−0.021) = 0.95279… = 0.953 (3 s.f.)\n\nIntercept: log₁₀ a = 3, so a = 10³ = 1000\n\nV = 1000 × 0.953ᵗ",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::11(c)": "After exactly 2 years, t = 24 months:\nV = 1000 × 0.953²⁴ = 314.94… ≈ £315\n(Using the unrounded b = 10^(−0.021) gives log₁₀ V = 3 − 0.021 × 24 = 2.496, so V ≈ £313.)\n\nThe model predicts about £315, which is close to the actual value of £320 (a difference of under 2%).\nSo the model is reliable (a good model) for predicting the value after 2 years.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::12": "Gradient of the chord between x and x + h:\ndy/dx = lim (h → 0) of (sin(x + h) − sin x)/h\n\nUse sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B:\n(sin(x + h) − sin x)/h = (sin x cos h + cos x sin h − sin x)/h\n\nGroup the terms:\n= sin x × ((cos h − 1)/h) + cos x × ((sin h)/h)\n\nAs h → 0, (cos h − 1)/h → 0 and (sin h)/h → 1, so this tends to\nsin x × 0 + cos x × 1 = cos x\n\nHence dy/dx = cos x.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::13(a)": "H = a − b(t − 20)² has its maximum when t = 20, and the maximum height is 60 m, so a = 60.\n\nAt the start, t = 0 and H = 2:\n2 = 60 − b(0 − 20)²\n2 = 60 − 400b\n400b = 58\nb = 0.145\n\nH = 60 − 0.145(t − 20)²",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::13(b)": "When t = 40:\nH = 60 − 0.145(40 − 20)² = 60 − 0.145 × 400 = 60 − 58 = 2\n\nHeight = 2 m",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::13(c)": "29cos(9t + α)° varies between −29 and 29, so H varies between β − 29 and β + 29.\n\nMaximum height 60 m: β + 29 = 60 ⇒ β = 31\n(Check: minimum β − 29 = 2 m, the starting height.)\n\nAt t = 0, H = 2:\n29cos α + 31 = 2 ⇒ cos α = −1 ⇒ α = 180 (with 0 ≤ α < 360)\n\nH = 29cos(9t + 180)° + 31",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::13(d)": "The cosine model is periodic (period 360/9 = 40 seconds), so it repeats and models the carriage going round multiple circuits during the 2 minutes, returning to 2 m at the start of each circuit.\n\nThe quadratic model only describes one rise and fall: after t = 40 its height becomes negative (e.g. at t = 120, H = 60 − 0.145 × 100² = −1390 m), which is impossible.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::14": "Every n ∈ ℕ is either even or odd. Let k be an integer.\n\nCase 1: n is even, n = 2k.\n(n + 1)³ − n³ = (2k + 1)³ − (2k)³\n= 8k³ + 12k² + 6k + 1 − 8k³\n= 12k² + 6k + 1\n= 2(6k² + 3k) + 1\n6k² + 3k is an integer, so this is one more than an even number: it is odd.\n\nCase 2: n is odd, n = 2k + 1.\n(n + 1)³ − n³ = (2k + 2)³ − (2k + 1)³\n= (8k³ + 24k² + 24k + 8) − (8k³ + 12k² + 6k + 1)\n= 12k² + 18k + 7\n= 2(6k² + 9k + 3) + 1\n6k² + 9k + 3 is an integer, so this is odd.\n\nIn both cases (n + 1)³ − n³ is odd, so (n + 1)³ − n³ is odd for all n ∈ ℕ.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::15(a)": "Write f(x) = u/v with u = 7xe^x and v = (e^(3x) − 2)^(½).\n\nProduct rule: du/dx = 7(xe^x + e^x) = 7e^x(x + 1)\nChain rule: dv/dx = ½ × 3e^(3x) × (e^(3x) − 2)^(−½) = (3/2)e^(3x)(e^(3x) − 2)^(−½)\n\nQuotient rule:\nf′(x) = (v du/dx − u dv/dx)/v²\n= [(e^(3x) − 2)^(½) × 7e^x(x + 1) − 7xe^x × (3/2)e^(3x)(e^(3x) − 2)^(−½)]/(e^(3x) − 2)\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by 2(e^(3x) − 2)^(½):\nf′(x) = [14e^x(x + 1)(e^(3x) − 2) − 21xe^x e^(3x)]/(2(e^(3x) − 2)^(3/2))\n= 7e^x[2(x + 1)(e^(3x) − 2) − 3xe^(3x)]/(2(e^(3x) − 2)^(3/2))\n\nSimplify the bracket:\n2(x + 1)(e^(3x) − 2) − 3xe^(3x) = 2xe^(3x) + 2e^(3x) − 4x − 4 − 3xe^(3x)\n= e^(3x)(2 − x) − 4x − 4\n\nSo\nf′(x) = 7e^x(e^(3x)(2 − x) − 4x − 4)/(2(e^(3x) − 2)^(3/2))\n\nA = −4 and B = −4",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::15(b)": "At a turning point f′(x) = 0. Since 7e^x > 0 and the denominator 2(e^(3x) − 2)^(3/2) > 0 for x > ln ∛2, the bracket must be zero:\ne^(3x)(2 − x) − 4x − 4 = 0\n2e^(3x) − xe^(3x) − 4x − 4 = 0\n2e^(3x) − 4 = xe^(3x) + 4x\n2e^(3x) − 4 = x(e^(3x) + 4)\n\nx = (2e^(3x) − 4)/(e^(3x) + 4), as required.",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::15(c)": "Staircase diagram on Diagram 1:\nStart at x₁ = 1 on the x-axis and draw a vertical line up to the curve y = (2e^(3x) − 4)/(e^(3x) + 4) (this height is x₂ ≈ 1.50).\nFrom that point draw a horizontal line across to the line y = x (this gives x = x₂).\nDraw a vertical line up from there to the curve again, then a horizontal line across to y = x, and repeat.\nThe steps get smaller and climb towards the intersection of the curve and the line at x = β, forming a staircase that converges to β (all lines lie to the right of x = 1).",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::15(d)": "(i) x₂ = (2e^(3×1) − 4)/(e^(3×1) + 4) = (2e³ − 4)/(e³ + 4) = 1.5017756…\nx₂ = 1.502 (3 d.p.)\n\n(ii) Continue iterating:\nx₃ = 1.87301…\nx₄ = 1.95709…\nx₅ = 1.96654…\nx₆ = 1.96747…\nx₇ = 1.96756…\nx₈ = 1.96757…\nThe iterates converge to 1.96757…, so\nβ = 1.968 (3 d.p.)",
"math-edx-9ma0-2023-p1::15(e)": "Let h(x) = (2e^(3x) − 4)/(e^(3x) + 4) − x. A root of h(x) = 0 is a root of the equation.\n\nUse the interval [0.4315, 0.4325]:\nh(0.4315) = −0.000297… < 0\nh(0.4325) = 0.000947… > 0\n\nThere is a change of sign, and h(x) is continuous on [0.4315, 0.4325], so there is a root in 0.4315 < x < 0.4325.\n\nHence α = 0.432 (3 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::1(a)": "f(x) = x³ + 2x² − 8x + 5\nDifferentiate once:\nf′(x) = 3x² + 4x − 8\nDifferentiate again:\nf″(x) = 6x + 4",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::1(b)": "(i) f″(x) = 0\n6x + 4 = 0\nx = −4/6 = −2/3\n\n(ii) f(x) is concave where f″(x) ≤ 0:\n6x + 4 ≤ 0\nx ≤ −2/3\nSo f(x) is concave for x ≤ −2/3.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::2(a)": "u₁ = 35,  uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 7cos(nπ/2) − 5(−1)ⁿ\n\n(i) n = 1:\nu₂ = 35 + 7cos(π/2) − 5(−1)¹\nu₂ = 35 + 0 + 5 = 40, as required.\n\n(ii) n = 2:\nu₃ = 40 + 7cos(π) − 5(−1)²\nu₃ = 40 − 7 − 5 = 28\n\nn = 3:\nu₄ = 28 + 7cos(3π/2) − 5(−1)³\nu₄ = 28 + 0 + 5 = 33\n\nu₃ = 28 and u₄ = 33",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::2(b)": "(i) The sequence is periodic with order 4, so u₅ = u₁:\nu₅ = 35\n(Check: u₅ = 33 + 7cos(2π) − 5(−1)⁴ = 33 + 7 − 5 = 35.)\n\n(ii) The terms repeat in blocks of four: 35, 40, 28, 33.\nSum of one block = 35 + 40 + 28 + 33 = 136\n25 terms = 6 complete blocks (24 terms) + u₂₅, and u₂₅ = u₁ = 35.\nΣ uᵣ (r = 1 to 25) = 6 × 136 + 35\n= 816 + 35\n= 851",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::3(a)": "log₂(x + 3) + log₂(x + 10) = 2 + 2log₂ x\n\nLeft-hand side, using the addition law:\nlog₂(x + 3) + log₂(x + 10) = log₂[(x + 3)(x + 10)]\n\nRight-hand side, using 2 = log₂ 4 and 2log₂ x = log₂ x²:\n2 + 2log₂ x = log₂ 4 + log₂ x² = log₂(4x²)\n\nSo log₂[(x + 3)(x + 10)] = log₂(4x²)\n⇒ (x + 3)(x + 10) = 4x²\n⇒ x² + 13x + 30 = 4x²\n⇒ 3x² − 13x − 30 = 0, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::3(b)": "(i) 3x² − 13x − 30 = 0\n(3x + 5)(x − 6) = 0\nx = 6 or x = −5/3\n\n(ii) x = −5/3 is not a solution of the original equation.\nReason: substituting x = −5/3 into the term 2log₂ x gives log₂(−5/3), and the logarithm of a negative number is not defined (not real).\n(x = 6 is valid: x + 3, x + 10 and x are all positive.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::4(a)": "H = Ae^(−Bt) + 30\nInitially t = 0 and H = 85:\n85 = Ae⁰ + 30 = A + 30\nA = 55",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::4(b)": "H = 55e^(−Bt) + 30\ndH/dt = −55Be^(−Bt)\n\nInitially the coffee is cooling at 7.5 °C per minute, so at t = 0, dH/dt = −7.5:\n−55B e⁰ = −7.5\n55B = 7.5\nB = 7.5/55 = 3/22 = 0.13636…\nB = 0.136 (3 d.p.)\n\nH = 55e^(−0.136t) + 30",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::5(a)": "P(3, −10) is a turning point, so dy/dx = 0 when x = 3:\n2(3)³ − 9(3)² + 5(3) + k = 0\n54 − 81 + 15 + k = 0\n−12 + k = 0\nk = 12, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::5(b)": "dy/dx = 2x³ − 9x² + 5x + 12\nIntegrate:\ny = ½x⁴ − 3x³ + (5/2)x² + 12x + c\n\nThe curve passes through P(3, −10):\n½(81) − 3(27) + (5/2)(9) + 12(3) + c = −10\n40.5 − 81 + 22.5 + 36 + c = −10\n18 + c = −10\nc = −28\n\ny = ½x⁴ − 3x³ + (5/2)x² + 12x − 28\nC crosses the y-axis where x = 0: y = −28\nCoordinates: (0, −28)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::6(a)": "OA = 12i, OB = 16j, OC = 50i + 136j, OD = 22i + 24j\n\nAD = OD − OA = (22i + 24j) − 12i = 10i + 24j\nBC = OC − OB = (50i + 136j) − 16j = 50i + 120j\n\nBC = 50i + 120j = 5(10i + 24j) = 5 AD\nSince BC is a scalar multiple of AD, AD is parallel to BC.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::6(b)": "Side vectors:\nAB = OB − OA = −12i + 16j\nBC = 50i + 120j\nCD = OD − OC = −28i − 112j\nDA = OA − OD = −10i − 24j\n\nLengths (metres):\n|AB| = √(12² + 16²) = √400 = 20\n|BC| = √(50² + 120²) = √16900 = 130\n|CD| = √(28² + 112²) = √13328 = 28√17 ≈ 115.447\n|DA| = √(10² + 24²) = √676 = 26\n\nOne lap = 20 + 130 + 28√17 + 26 = 176 + 28√17 ≈ 291.447 m\nTwo laps ≈ 582.89 m = 0.58289 km\n\nTime = 5 minutes = 5/60 hour = 1/12 hour\nAverage speed = 0.58289 ÷ (1/12) = 0.58289 × 12 ≈ 6.9947 km/h\n\nAverage speed ≈ 6.99 km/h",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::7(a)": "x³ + 2xy + 3y² = 47\nDifferentiate implicitly with respect to x (product rule on 2xy):\n3x² + (2y + 2x dy/dx) + 6y dy/dx = 0\n\nCollect the dy/dx terms:\n(2x + 6y) dy/dx = −(3x² + 2y)\n\ndy/dx = −(2y + 3x²)/(2x + 6y)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::7(b)": "At P(−2, 5):\ndy/dx = −(2(5) + 3(−2)²)/(2(−2) + 6(5))\n= −(10 + 12)/(−4 + 30)\n= −22/26 = −11/13\n\nGradient of the normal = −1 ÷ (−11/13) = 13/11\n\nNormal through (−2, 5):\ny − 5 = (13/11)(x + 2)\n11y − 55 = 13x + 26\n13x − 11y + 81 = 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::8(a)": "2cos θ + 8sin θ ≡ Rcos(θ − α) = Rcos θ cos α + Rsin θ sin α\nCompare coefficients:\nRcos α = 2,  Rsin α = 8\n\nR = √(2² + 8²) = √68 = 2√17\n\ntan α = 8/2 = 4\nα = arctan 4 = 1.3258… = 1.326 (3 d.p.)\n\n2cos θ + 8sin θ = 2√17 cos(θ − 1.326)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::8(b)": "The sequence is arithmetic with first term a = cos x and common difference d = sin x.\n\nS₉ = (9/2)[2a + 8d]\n= (9/2)[2cos x + 8sin x]\n= 4.5(2cos x + 8sin x)\n\nUsing part (a):\nS₉ = 4.5 × 2√17 cos(x − α) = 9√17 cos(x − α), where α = 1.3258…\n\n(i) The maximum value of cos(x − α) is 1, so\nmaximum S₉ = 9√17\n\n(ii) The maximum occurs when cos(x − α) = 1, i.e. x − α = 0, 2π, …\nSmallest positive x = α = 1.33 (3 s.f.)\n(This is not a multiple of π, so it is allowed.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::9(a)": "x = t² + 6t − 16, y = 6ln(t + 3), t > −3\n\nComplete the square:\nx = (t + 3)² − 9 − 16 = (t + 3)² − 25\nx + 25 = (t + 3)²\n\nSince t > −3, t + 3 > 0, so\nt + 3 = (x + 25)^½\n\nSubstitute into y:\ny = 6ln[(x + 25)^½] = 6 × ½ ln(x + 25)\ny = 3ln(x + 25)\n\nSo A = 3 and B = 25 (with x > −25).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::9(b)": "P is where C cuts the y-axis, so x = 0:\ny = 3ln(0 + 25) = 3ln 25 = 6ln 5\nP = (0, 6ln 5)\n\nFrom y = 3ln(x + 25):\ndy/dx = 3/(x + 25)\nAt x = 0: dy/dx = 3/25\n\nTangent at P:\ny − 6ln 5 = (3/25)(x − 0)\n25y − 150ln 5 = 3x\n−3x + 25y = 150ln 5\n\nThis is of the form ax + by = c ln 5 with a = −3, b = 25, c = 150.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::10(a)": "f(x) = (3kx − 18)/((x + 4)(x − 2))\n\nLet (3kx − 18)/((x + 4)(x − 2)) ≡ A/(x + 4) + B/(x − 2)\n3kx − 18 ≡ A(x − 2) + B(x + 4)\n\nx = 2:  6k − 18 = 6B  ⇒  B = k − 3\nx = −4: −12k − 18 = −6A  ⇒  A = 2k + 3\n\nf(x) = (2k + 3)/(x + 4) + (k − 3)/(x − 2)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::10(b)": "∫ f(x) dx = (2k + 3)ln|x + 4| + (k − 3)ln|x − 2|\n\nApply the limits −3 and 1:\n[(2k + 3)ln|x + 4| + (k − 3)ln|x − 2|] from −3 to 1\n= [(2k + 3)ln 5 + (k − 3)ln 1] − [(2k + 3)ln 1 + (k − 3)ln 5]\n= (2k + 3)ln 5 − (k − 3)ln 5\n= (k + 6)ln 5\n\nSet equal to 21:\n(k + 6)ln 5 = 21\nk + 6 = 21/ln 5\nk = 21/ln 5 − 6\n(k ≈ 7.05, which is positive as required.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::11(a)": "The base is 20 m by 10 m, so\nV = 20 × 10 × h = 200h\ndV/dh = 200\n\nThe rate of change of V is inversely proportional to √h:\ndV/dt = k/√h for some constant k.\n\nChain rule:\ndh/dt = (dh/dV) × (dV/dt) = (1/200) × k/√h = (k/200)/√h\n\nSo dh/dt = λ/√h, where λ = k/200 is a constant.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::11(b)": "dh/dt = λ/√h\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ h^(½) dh = ∫ λ dt\n(2/3)h^(3/2) = λt + c\n\nt = 0, h = 1.44:\n(2/3)(1.44)^(3/2) = c\n(2/3)(1.728) = c\nc = 1.152\n\nt = 8, h = 3.24:\n(2/3)(3.24)^(3/2) = 8λ + 1.152\n(2/3)(5.832) = 8λ + 1.152\n3.888 = 8λ + 1.152\n8λ = 2.736\nλ = 0.342\n\nSo (2/3)h^(3/2) = 0.342t + 1.152\nMultiply by 3/2:\nh^(3/2) = 0.513t + 1.728\n\nA = 0.513, B = 1.728",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::11(c)": "The tank is full when h = 5:\n5^(3/2) = 0.513t + 1.728\n11.1803… = 0.513t + 1.728\n0.513t = 9.4523…\nt = 18.4256…\n\nThe tank is completely full after 18.4 minutes (to 3 s.f.).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::12(a)": "At t = 0:\nN_A = |0 − 3| + 4 = 3 + 4 = 7\nN_B = 8 − |0 − 6| = 8 − 6 = 2\n\nN_A − N_B = 7 − 2 = 5 (thousand)\n\nThe initial difference is 5000 subscribers.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::12(b)": "T = 3\nReason: N_A = |t − 3| + 4 has its minimum at t = 3; this is the point when company A had its lowest number of subscribers, and after it the number of subscribers starts to increase.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::12(c)": "Remove the modulus signs on each side of t = 3.\n\nFor 0 ≤ t < 3:\nN_A = −(t − 3) + 4 = 7 − t\nN_B = 8 − (6 − 2t) = 2 + 2t\nN_A = N_B: 7 − t = 2t + 2 ⇒ 3t = 5 ⇒ t = 5/3\n\nFor t ≥ 3:\nN_A = (t − 3) + 4 = t + 1\nN_B = 8 − (2t − 6) = 14 − 2t\nN_A = N_B: t + 1 = 14 − 2t ⇒ 3t = 13 ⇒ t = 13/3\n\nAt t = 3, N_A = 4 and N_B = 8, so N_A < N_B between the two critical values.\nAt t = 0, N_A = 7 > N_B = 2, and for large t, N_A increases while N_B decreases.\nSo N_A > N_B outside the critical values:\nt < 5/3 or t > 13/3\n\nIn set notation (with t ≥ 0):\n{t ∈ ℝ : 0 ≤ t < 5/3} ∪ {t ∈ ℝ : t > 13/3}",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::12(d)": "N_B = 8 − |2t − 6| becomes negative when |2t − 6| > 8, i.e. for t > 7.\nA negative number of subscribers is impossible, so the model for company B is not valid for t > 7.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::13(a)": "(3 + x)⁻² = 3⁻²(1 + x/3)⁻² = (1/9)(1 + x/3)⁻²\n\n(1 + x/3)⁻² = 1 + (−2)(x/3) + [(−2)(−3)/2!](x/3)² + …\n= 1 − 2x/3 + 3(x²/9) + …\n= 1 − 2x/3 + x²/3 + …\n\n(3 + x)⁻² = (1/9)(1 − 2x/3 + x²/3 + …)\n= 1/9 − 2x/27 + x²/27 + …",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::13(b)": "6x/(3 + x)² = 6x(3 + x)⁻² ≈ 6x(1/9 − 2x/27 + x²/27)\n= 2x/3 − 4x²/9 + 2x³/9\n\n∫ (2x/3 − 4x²/9 + 2x³/9) dx = x²/3 − 4x³/27 + x⁴/18\n\nApply limits 0.2 to 0.4:\nAt x = 0.4: (0.4)²/3 − 4(0.4)³/27 + (0.4)⁴/18 = 0.053333… − 0.0094815… + 0.0014222… = 0.0452741…\nAt x = 0.2: (0.2)²/3 − 4(0.2)³/27 + (0.2)⁴/18 = 0.013333… − 0.0011852… + 0.0000889… = 0.0122370…\n\nI ≈ 0.0452741 − 0.0122370 = 0.0330370… (exactly 223/6750)\n\nI ≈ 0.03304 (4 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::13(c)": "Let u = 3 + x, so x = u − 3 and dx = du.\nLimits: x = 0.2 ⇒ u = 3.2; x = 0.4 ⇒ u = 3.4\n\n∫ from 0.2 to 0.4 of 6x/(3 + x)² dx = ∫ from 3.2 to 3.4 of 6(u − 3)/u² du\n= ∫ from 3.2 to 3.4 of (6/u − 18/u²) du\n= [6ln u + 18/u] from 3.2 to 3.4\n\n= (6ln 3.4 + 18/3.4) − (6ln 3.2 + 18/3.2)\n= 6ln(3.4/3.2) + 90/17 − 45/8\n= 6ln(17/16) + (720 − 765)/136\n= 6ln(17/16) − 45/136\n\n(So a = 6, b = 17/16, c = −45/136; value ≈ 0.032865.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::14(a)": "2tan θ(8cos θ + 23sin² θ) = 8sin 2θ(1 + tan² θ)\n\nUse tan θ = sin θ/cos θ, 1 + tan² θ = sec² θ and sin 2θ = 2sin θ cos θ.\nMultiply both sides by cos² θ (cos θ ≠ 0 since tan θ is defined):\n\nLHS × cos² θ = 2sin θ cos θ(8cos θ + 23sin² θ) = sin 2θ(8cos θ + 23sin² θ)\nRHS × cos² θ = 8sin 2θ sec² θ cos² θ = 8sin 2θ\n\nSo sin 2θ(8cos θ + 23sin² θ) = 8sin 2θ\nUsing sin² θ = 1 − cos² θ:\nsin 2θ(8cos θ + 23 − 23cos² θ) − 8sin 2θ = 0\nsin 2θ(−23cos² θ + 8cos θ + 15) = 0\nsin 2θ(23cos² θ − 8cos θ − 15) = 0\n\nA = 23, B = −8, C = −15",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::14(b)": "By part (a), the equation is equivalent to\nsin 2x(23cos² x − 8cos x − 15) = 0, for 360° ≤ x ≤ 540°, x ≠ 450°\n\nEither sin 2x = 0:\n720° ≤ 2x ≤ 1080°, so 2x = 720°, 900°, 1080°\nx = 360°, 450°, 540°\nx = 450° is excluded, so x = 360° or 540°.\n\nOr 23cos² x − 8cos x − 15 = 0:\n(23cos x + 15)(cos x − 1) = 0\ncos x = 1 or cos x = −15/23\n\ncos x = 1 ⇒ x = 360° (already found).\ncos x = −15/23: principal value arccos(−15/23) = 130.71°\nIn the range 360° to 540°: x = 360° + 130.71° = 490.71°\n(The other solution, 720° − 130.71° = 589.29°, is out of range.)\n\nSolutions: x = 360°, 491° (3 s.f.), 540°",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p2::15": "Assume that sin x − cos x < 1 when x is an obtuse angle.\n(For obtuse x, sin x > 0 and cos x < 0, so sin x − cos x > 0 and squaring keeps the inequality.)\n⇒ (sin x − cos x)² < 1\n⇒ sin² x − 2sin x cos x + cos² x < 1\nUsing sin² x + cos² x = 1:\n⇒ 1 − 2sin x cos x < 1\n⇒ −2sin x cos x < 0\n\nBut x is obtuse (90° < x < 180°), so sin x > 0 and cos x < 0.\nHence sin x cos x < 0, so −2sin x cos x > 0.\nThis contradicts −2sin x cos x < 0.\n\nSo the assumption is false, and therefore sin x − cos x ≥ 1 when x is obtuse.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::1(a)": "A is the circle containing the regions 0.13 and 0.25.\nP(A) = 0.13 + 0.25\nP(A) = 0.38",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::1(b)": "From the Venn diagram:\nP(B) = 0.25 + 0.05 + 0.3 = 0.6\nP(C) = 0.3 + p\nP(B ∩ C) = 0.3\n\nB and C are independent, so P(B ∩ C) = P(B) × P(C):\n0.3 = 0.6 × (0.3 + p)\n0.3 + p = 0.5\np = 0.2\n\nThe probabilities in the Venn diagram sum to 1:\n0.13 + 0.25 + 0.05 + 0.3 + p + q = 1\n0.73 + 0.2 + q = 1\nq = 0.07\n\np = 0.2 and q = 0.07",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::1(c)": "P(A | B′) = P(A ∩ B′)/P(B′)\n\nA ∩ B′ is the part of A outside B: P(A ∩ B′) = 0.13\nP(B′) = 1 − P(B) = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4\n(check: 0.13 + p + q = 0.13 + 0.2 + 0.07 = 0.4)\n\nP(A | B′) = 0.13/0.4 = 13/40 = 0.325",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::2(a)": "The prizes must be placed in the packets independently of each other (each packet's chance of containing a prize is not affected by any other packet in the box).\n\nEquivalently: the probability that a packet contains a prize must be constant (the same, 1/7) for every packet.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::2(b)": "T ~ B(40, 1/7)\n\n(i) P(T = 6) = ⁴⁰C₆ × (1/7)⁶ × (6/7)³⁴\nP(T = 6) = 0.17273…\nP(T = 6) = 0.173 (3 s.f.)\n\n(ii) Fewer than 3 means T ≤ 2:\nP(T < 3) = P(T ≤ 2)\n= (6/7)⁴⁰ + 40 × (1/7) × (6/7)³⁹ + ⁴⁰C₂ × (1/7)² × (6/7)³⁸\n= 0.061587…\nP(T < 3) = 0.0616 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::2(c)": "Let K = number of the 5 boxes that have fewer than 3 packets containing a prize.\nEach box independently has probability 0.061587… (from (b)(ii)), so\nK ~ B(5, 0.061587…)\n\nP(K = 2) = ⁵C₂ × (0.061587…)² × (1 − 0.061587…)³\n= 10 × 0.0037930… × 0.82638…\n= 0.031344…\n\nP(K = 2) = 0.0313 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::2(d)": "Let p = proportion of packets containing a prize.\nH₀: p = 1/7\nH₁: p < 1/7\n(one-tailed test at the 5% level)\n\nLet X = number of packets in the sample of 110 that contain a prize.\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(110, 1/7)\n\nThe observed value is X = 9, so find the probability of 9 or fewer:\nP(X ≤ 9) = 0.038292…\nP(X ≤ 9) = 0.0383 (3 s.f.)\n\n0.0383 < 0.05, so the result is significant: reject H₀.\n\nThere is evidence to support Kamil's claim that the proportion of packets containing a prize is less than 1/7.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::3(a)": "The 29 values recorded as 'tr' (trace) are not numbers, so they must be replaced with a numerical value before any calculation can be done.\n\nTrace means less than 0.05 mm of rain, so each 'tr' should be replaced by a value between 0 and 0.05, for example 0.025 mm (the midpoint).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::3(b)": "n = 184, Σx = 390, Σx² = 4336\n\n(i) Mean:\nx̄ = Σx/n = 390/184 = 195/92 = 2.1195…\nMean ≈ 2.12 mm\n\n(ii) Standard deviation:\nσ² = Σx²/n − x̄²\nσ² = 4336/184 − (2.1195…)²\nσ² = 23.5652… − 4.4925…\nσ² = 19.0727…\nσ = √19.0727… = 4.3672…\nStandard deviation ≈ 4.37 mm",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::3(c)": "(i) The large data set only contains data for the months May to October, so the 184 days do not represent the whole year.\n\n(ii) The missing months are the winter months (November to April), when there is likely to be more rain. So the mean from (b)(i) is expected to be an underestimate: the actual annual mean Daily Total Rainfall for Leeming in 1987 is likely to be higher than 2.12 mm.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::4(a)": "Let N = height (cm) of an adult man from region A, N ~ N(175.4, 6.8²)\n\nP(N > 180) = P(Z > (180 − 175.4)/6.8)\n= P(Z > 0.6765)\n= 0.24937…\n\nProportion taller than 180 cm ≈ 0.249",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::4(b)": "Let μ = mean height (cm) of adult men from region B.\nH₀: μ = 175.4\nH₁: μ ≠ 175.4\n(two-tailed test at the 5% level, so 2.5% in each tail)\n\nUnder H₀, the sample mean of 52 heights is\nS̄ ~ N(175.4, 6.8²/52), i.e. variance 0.8892…, standard deviation 0.94299…\n\nThe observed mean 177.2 is above 175.4, so use the upper tail:\nP(S̄ > 177.2) = P(Z > (177.2 − 175.4)/0.94299…)\n= P(Z > 1.9088…)\n= 0.02814…\n\n0.0281 > 0.025, so the result is not significant: do not reject H₀.\n\nThere is insufficient evidence to support the student's claim that the mean height of adult men from region B is different from that of region A.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::4(c)": "The test in (b) is two-tailed, so the p-value is double the one-tail probability:\np-value = 2 × P(S̄ > 177.2)\n= 2 × 0.02814…\n= 0.05628…\n\np-value ≈ 0.0563",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::5(a)": "By the definition of conditional probability,\nP(S ∩ {X = x}) = P(S | {X = x}) × P(X = x) = (k/x) × P(X = x)\n\nTisam's model says P(S ∩ {X = x}) is the same for every x, so for x = 50 and x = 80:\nP(S ∩ {X = 50}) = P(S ∩ {X = 80})\n(k/50) × b = (k/80) × c\n\nDivide both sides by k (k ≠ 0) and multiply by 80:\nc = (80/50)b\nc = (8/5)b",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::5(b)": "Using the same idea as (a), (k/x) × P(X = x) is the same for all four values of x:\n(k/20)a = (k/50)b = (k/80)c = (k/100)d\n\nSo a/20 = b/50 = c/80 = d/100, giving\na = (20/50)b = (2/5)b\nc = (8/5)b (from (a))\nd = (100/50)b = 2b\n\nThe probabilities sum to 1:\na + b + c + d = 1\n(2/5)b + b + (8/5)b + 2b = 1\n5b = 1\nb = 1/5\n\nThen\na = (2/5) × (1/5) = 2/25\nc = (8/5) × (1/5) = 8/25\nd = 2 × (1/5) = 2/5\n\nProbability distribution of X:\nx: 20, 50, 80, 100\nP(X = x): 2/25, 1/5, 8/25, 2/5",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::5(c)": "For Nav, P(S | {X = 100}) = 0.3, so in the model\nk/100 = 0.3 ⇒ k = 30\n\nBut then for x = 20 the model gives\nP(S | {X = 20}) = 30/20 = 1.5\n\nA probability cannot be greater than 1, so the model cannot describe Nav playing the game when X = 20.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(a)": "Frequency = frequency density × class width.\n\nFrom the histogram, the bars between 10 and 30 hours are:\n10 to 12 (part of the 7–12 bar): density 4.2, width 2 ⇒ 2 × 4.2 = 8.4\n12 to 16: density 4, width 4 ⇒ 4 × 4 = 16\n16 to 20: density 3.5, width 4 ⇒ 4 × 3.5 = 14\n20 to 30 (part of the 20–40 bar): density 1, width 10 ⇒ 10 × 1 = 10\n\nEstimated number of patients with 10 < T < 30:\n8.4 + 16 + 14 + 10 = 48.4\n\nP(10 < T < 30) ≈ 48.4/90 = 121/225 = 0.538 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(b)": "Tomas' model is not suitable.\nThe histogram is not symmetric: it is positively skewed, with a long tail stretching from 20 to 40 hours, whereas a normal distribution is symmetric (bell-shaped) about its mean.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(c)": "Integrate by parts with\nu = x, so du/dx = 1\ndv/dx = e^(−x), so v = −e^(−x)\n\n∫ xe^(−x) dx = −xe^(−x) − ∫ (−e^(−x)) dx\n= −xe^(−x) + ∫ e^(−x) dx\n= −xe^(−x) − e^(−x) + c\n\nSo\n∫₀ⁿ xe^(−x) dx = [−xe^(−x) − e^(−x)]₀ⁿ\n= (−ne^(−n) − e^(−n)) − (−0 × e⁰ − e⁰)\n= −ne^(−n) − e^(−n) + 1\n= 1 − (n + 1)e^(−n)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(d)": "The frequency polygon covers all 90 patients, so the area under Xiang's curve over 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 must be 90 (x in tens of hours, so 0 to 40 hours):\nk ∫₀⁴ xe^(−x) dx = 90\n\nUsing (c) with n = 4:\n∫₀⁴ xe^(−x) dx = 1 − 5e^(−4) = 0.90842…\n\nk(1 − 5e^(−4)) = 90\nk = 90/(1 − 5e^(−4))\nk = 99.0729…\n\nSo k = 99 to the nearest integer.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(e)(i)": "T ~ N(14.9, 9.3²)\n\nP(10 < T < 30) = P((10 − 14.9)/9.3 < Z < (30 − 14.9)/9.3)\n= P(−0.5269 < Z < 1.6237)\n= 0.64863…\n\nP(10 < T < 30) ≈ 0.649",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(e)(ii)": "x is in tens of hours, so 10 < T < 30 corresponds to 1 < x < 3.\n\nEstimated number of patients = 99 ∫₁³ xe^(−x) dx\nUsing (c):\n∫₁³ xe^(−x) dx = ∫₀³ xe^(−x) dx − ∫₀¹ xe^(−x) dx\n= (1 − 4e^(−3)) − (1 − 2e^(−1))\n= 2e^(−1) − 4e^(−3)\n= 0.53661…\n\nNumber of patients = 99 × 0.53661… = 53.12…\n\nP(10 < T < 30) ≈ 53.12…/90 = 0.590 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p31::6(f)": "Xiang's curve is only defined for 0 ≤ x ≤ 4, i.e. up to 40 hours, so the model cannot be used for patients who stay in hospital longer than 40 hours (some patients might stay longer than 40 hours).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::1(a)": "u = 0, a = 3.2 m s⁻², t = 5 s\nv = u + at\nv = 0 + 3.2 × 5\nv = 16 m s⁻¹",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::1(b)": "u = 0, a = 3.2 m s⁻², t = 5 s\ns = ut + ½at²\ns = 0 × 5 + ½ × 3.2 × 5²\ns = 1.6 × 25\ns = 40 m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::2(a)": "The plane is horizontal and the only vertical forces are the weight and the normal reaction.\nResolving vertically:\nR = 5g\nR = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::2(b)": "Newton's second law in the direction of motion (resultant force = mass × acceleration):\n28 − F = 5 × 1.4\n28 − F = 7\nF = 21",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::2(c)": "The particle is moving, so friction is limiting: F = μR\n21 = μ × 49\nμ = 21/49 = 3/7 = 0.4285…\nμ = 0.43 (2 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::3(a)": "At t = 0:\nv = (0 − 0 + 7)i + (0 − 3)j = 7i − 3j\n\nSpeed = |v| = √(7² + (−3)²)\n= √(49 + 9)\n= √58\n= 7.62 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::3(b)": "P moves parallel to (i + j) when the i- and j-components of v are equal (ratio 1 : 1):\nt² − 3t + 7 = 2t² − 3\n0 = t² + 3t − 10\n0 = (t + 5)(t − 2)\nt = 2 or t = −5\n\nt ≥ 0, so reject t = −5.\nt = 2 only (check: v = 5i + 5j at t = 2, which is parallel to i + j)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::3(c)": "a = dv/dt\nv = (t² − 3t + 7)i + (2t² − 3)j\na = (2t − 3)i + 4tj m s⁻²",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::3(d)": "The acceleration is perpendicular to i when its i-component is zero:\n2t − 3 = 0\nt = 1.5\n\n(At t = 1.5, a = 6j, which is perpendicular to i.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::4(a)": "u = (−16i − 3j) m s⁻¹, a = (2.4i + j) m s⁻², t = 5\nv = u + at\nv_B = (−16i − 3j) + 5(2.4i + j)\nv_B = (−16 + 12)i + (−3 + 5)j\nv_B = −4i + 2j\n\nSpeed at B = √((−4)² + 2²)\n= √20 = 2√5\n= 4.47 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::4(b)": "Using A as the starting position (t = 0):\nr = r_A + ut + ½at²\nAt t = T, P is at C:\n4i + cj = (44i − 10j) + (−16i − 3j)T + ½(2.4i + j)T²\n\nEquate i-components:\n4 = 44 − 16T + 1.2T²\n1.2T² − 16T + 40 = 0\nMultiply by 5:\n6T² − 80T + 200 = 0\n3T² − 40T + 100 = 0\n(3T − 10)(T − 10) = 0\nT = 10/3 or T = 10\n\nT > 5, so T = 10.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::4(c)": "Equate j-components of\n4i + cj = (44i − 10j) + (−16i − 3j)T + ½(2.4i + j)T²\nwith T = 10:\nc = −10 + (−3)(10) + ½ × 1 × 10²\nc = −10 − 30 + 50\nc = 10",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::5(a)": "Horizontal motion: there is no horizontal acceleration, so the horizontal velocity is constant at 28cos α.\nHorizontal distance from O to A is 40 m:\n28cos α × T = 40\nT = 40/(28cos α)\nT = 10/(7cos α)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::5(b)": "Vertical motion (taking up as positive): initial vertical velocity 28sin α, acceleration −g, displacement 20 m at time T:\ns = ut + ½at²\n20 = (28sin α)T − ½gT²\n\nSubstitute T = 10/(7cos α):\n20 = 28sin α × 10/(7cos α) − ½ × 9.8 × 100/(49cos²α)\n20 = 40 tan α − 10 sec²α\n\nUse sec²α = 1 + tan²α:\n20 = 40 tan α − 10(1 + tan²α)\n20 = 40 tan α − 10 − 10tan²α\n10tan²α − 40 tan α + 30 = 0\nDivide by 10:\ntan²α − 4tan α + 3 = 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::5(c)": "Solve the equation from (b):\ntan²α − 4tan α + 3 = 0\n(tan α − 1)(tan α − 3) = 0\ntan α = 1 or tan α = 3\n\nThe greatest height comes from the steeper projection, so use the larger value:\ntan α = 3, so sin α = 3/√10 (α = 71.565…°)\n(With tan α = 3, the ball reaches its highest point before A, so this height is reached between O and A.)\n\nAt the greatest height the vertical velocity is zero:\nv² = u² + 2as\n0 = (28sin α)² − 2gH\n0 = (28 × 3/√10)² − 2 × 9.8 × H\n0 = 705.6 − 19.6H\nH = 36\n\nGreatest possible height = 36 m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::5(d)": "The model treats the ball as a particle, so it ignores the size (dimensions) of the ball and any spin of the ball.\n\n(Other acceptable limitations: it ignores wind; the motion may not stay in a single vertical plane; the value used for g is only approximate.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::6(a)": "The friction at A acts to the right (towards the wall).\n\nReason: the normal reaction of the smooth wall at B acts horizontally to the left (away from the wall). The only other horizontal force is the friction at A, so for equilibrium it must act to the right to balance it. (Equivalently, the rod tends to slip away from the wall at A, and friction opposes this.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::6(b)": "The rod is uniform, so its weight Mg acts at its midpoint, a distance a from A along the rod.\nThe normal reaction S at B is horizontal; B is at height 2a sin θ above A.\nFriction and the normal reaction at A pass through A, so they have no moment about A.\n\nTaking moments about A:\nS × 2a sin θ = Mg × a cos θ\nS = Mga cos θ/(2a sin θ)\nS = ½Mg cot θ",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::6(c)": "Let R be the normal reaction and F the friction at A.\n\nResolve vertically: R = Mg\nResolve horizontally: F = S\n\ntan θ = 3/4, so cot θ = 4/3 and, from (b),\nS = ½Mg × 4/3 = (2/3)Mg\n\nThe rod is in limiting equilibrium, so F = μR:\n(2/3)Mg = μMg\nμ = 2/3",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::6(d)": "The resultant force at A is made up of the friction F = (2/3)Mg (horizontal) and the normal reaction R = Mg (vertical), which are perpendicular.\n\nMagnitude = √(F² + R²)\n= √((2Mg/3)² + (Mg)²)\n= Mg √(4/9 + 1)\n= Mg √(13/9)\n= (√13/3)Mg\n≈ 1.20Mg",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2023-p32::6(e)": "The new value of S is larger.\n\nReason: with the centre of mass closer to B, the weight acts further (horizontally) from A, so the moment of the weight about A is larger. Taking moments about A, S × 2a sin θ must equal this larger moment, and with θ unchanged S must be larger.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::1(a)": "y = 4x³ − 7x² + 5x − 10\nDifferentiate term by term (reduce each power by one):\n(i) dy/dx = 3·4x² − 2·7x + 5\ndy/dx = 12x² − 14x + 5\n(ii) Differentiate again:\nd²y/dx² = 2·12x − 14\nd²y/dx² = 24x − 14",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::1(b)": "From (a), d²y/dx² = 24x − 14\nSet d²y/dx² = 0:\n24x − 14 = 0\n24x = 14\nx = 14/24 = 7/12",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::2(a)": "The repayments form an arithmetic sequence with first term a = 400 and common difference d = 390 − 400 = −10.\nuₙ = a + (n − 1)d\nu₁₂ = 400 + (12 − 1)(−10)\n= 400 + 11 × (−10)\n= 400 − 110\n= 290\nSo Jamie repays £290 in month 12.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::2(b)": "The total repaid after N payments equals the loan of £8100.\nSₙ = ½n(2a + (n − 1)d), with a = 400, d = −10:\n8100 = ½N(2 × 400 + (N − 1)(−10))\n16200 = N(800 − 10N + 10)\n16200 = N(810 − 10N)\n16200 = 810N − 10N²\n10N² − 810N + 16200 = 0\nDivide by 10:\nN² − 81N + 1620 = 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::2(c)": "N² − 81N + 1620 = 0\nFactorise (45 × 36 = 1620 and 45 + 36 = 81):\n(N − 45)(N − 36) = 0\nN = 36 or N = 45\nThe total repaid first reaches £8100 after the 36th payment, so the loan is cleared then. N = 45 is rejected: the monthly payment 410 − 10n would be negative from month 42 onwards, which is not possible.\nN = 36",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::3": "P(3, −2)\n(i) y = f(x − 2) is a translation by 2 units in the positive x-direction: add 2 to the x-coordinate.\n(3 + 2, −2) = (5, −2)\n(ii) y = f(2x) is a stretch parallel to the x-axis with scale factor ½: halve the x-coordinate.\n(3 ÷ 2, −2) = (1.5, −2)\n(iii) y = 3f(−x) + 5:\nf(−x) reflects in the y-axis: (3, −2) → (−3, −2)\n3f(−x) stretches parallel to the y-axis, scale factor 3: (−3, −2) → (−3, −6)\n+5 translates up 5 units: (−3, −6) → (−3, −1)\nImage: (−3, −1)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::4(a)": "uₙ₊₁ = kuₙ − 5, u₁ = 6\nu₂ = k(6) − 5 = 6k − 5\nu₃ = k·u₂ − 5 = k(6k − 5) − 5\nGiven u₃ = −1:\nk(6k − 5) − 5 = −1\n6k² − 5k − 5 = −1\n6k² − 5k − 4 = 0",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::4(b)": "(i) 6k² − 5k − 4 = 0\n(3k − 4)(2k + 1) = 0\nk = 4/3 or k = −½\nk is a positive constant, so k = 4/3.\n(ii) u₁ = 6\nu₂ = (4/3) × 6 − 5 = 8 − 5 = 3\nu₃ = −1 (given; check: (4/3) × 3 − 5 = −1)\nΣ (r = 1 to 3) uᵣ = 6 + 3 + (−1) = 8",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::5": "For small θ (in radians):\ntan2θ ≈ 2θ\ncos3θ ≈ 1 − (3θ)²/2 = 1 − 9θ²/2\nNumerator: θtan2θ ≈ θ × 2θ = 2θ²\nDenominator: 1 − cos3θ ≈ 1 − (1 − 9θ²/2) = 9θ²/2\nθtan2θ/(1 − cos3θ) ≈ 2θ² ÷ (9θ²/2) = 4θ²/(9θ²) = 4/9",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::6(a)": "(i) f(x) = e^(4x² − 1)\nBy the chain rule, d/dx of e^u is (du/dx)e^u, with u = 4x² − 1, du/dx = 8x:\nf′(x) = 8xe^(4x² − 1)\n(ii) g(x) = 8lnx\ng′(x) = 8/x",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::6(b)": "At x = α, f′(x) = g′(x):\n8xe^(4x² − 1) = 8/x\ne^(4x² − 1) = 1/x²\nTake natural logs of both sides:\n4x² − 1 = ln(1/x²)\n4x² − 1 = −2lnx  (since ln(1/x²) = −lnx² = −2lnx for x > 0)\n4x² + 2lnx − 1 = 0\nSo α satisfies 4x² + 2lnx − 1 = 0.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::6(c)": "(i) x₁ = 0.6\nx₂ = √((1 − 2ln0.6)/4)\n= √((1 + 1.021651...)/4)\n= √0.505413...\n= 0.7109 (4 d.p.)\n(ii) Continue iterating:\nx₃ = 0.64853..., x₄ = 0.68302..., x₅ = 0.66379..., x₆ = 0.67446..., ...\nThe iterates oscillate and converge to 0.670641...\nα = 0.6706 (4 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::7(a)": "AB = OB − OA\n= (5i + 6j + 8k) − (2i − 3j + 5k)\n= 3i + 9j + 3k",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::7(b)": "P lies on l with |AP| = 2|BP|. There are two possible positions.\nCase 1: P beyond B (so B is the midpoint of AP), AP = 2AB:\nOP = OA + 2AB\n= (2i − 3j + 5k) + 2(3i + 9j + 3k)\n= 2i − 3j + 5k + 6i + 18j + 6k\n= 8i + 15j + 11k\nCheck: |AP| = 2|AB| and |BP| = |AB|, so |AP| = 2|BP|.\nCase 2: P between A and B, dividing AB in the ratio 2 : 1, AP = ⅔AB:\nOP = OA + ⅔AB\n= (2i − 3j + 5k) + ⅔(3i + 9j + 3k)\n= 2i − 3j + 5k + 2i + 6j + 2k\n= 4i + 3j + 7k\nCheck: |AP| = ⅔|AB| and |BP| = ⅓|AB|, so |AP| = 2|BP|.\n(A point on the other side of A would be closer to A than to B, so cannot satisfy |AP| = 2|BP|.)\nPossible position vectors of P: 8i + 15j + 11k and 4i + 3j + 7k",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::8(a)": "LHS = 1/(cosecθ − 1) + 1/(cosecθ + 1)\n= ((cosecθ + 1) + (cosecθ − 1))/((cosecθ − 1)(cosecθ + 1))\n= 2cosecθ/(cosec²θ − 1)\nUsing 1 + cot²θ ≡ cosec²θ, cosec²θ − 1 ≡ cot²θ:\n= 2cosecθ/cot²θ\nWrite in terms of sin and cos:\n= (2/sinθ) × (sin²θ/cos²θ)\n= 2sinθ/cos²θ\n= 2 × (sinθ/cosθ) × (1/cosθ)\n= 2tanθsecθ = RHS",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::8(b)": "Using (a) with θ = 2x:\n1/(cosec2x − 1) + 1/(cosec2x + 1) = 2tan2x sec2x\nSo the equation becomes:\n2tan2x sec2x = cot2x sec2x\n2tan2x sec2x − cot2x sec2x = 0\nsec2x(2tan2x − cot2x) = 0\nsec2x = 1/cos2x is never zero, so\n2tan2x − cot2x = 0\n2tan2x = 1/tan2x\ntan²2x = ½\ntan2x = ±1/√2\nFor 0 < x < 90°, 0 < 2x < 180°.\ntan2x = 1/√2: 2x = tan⁻¹(1/√2) = 35.264°\ntan2x = −1/√2: 2x = 180° − 35.264° = 144.736°\nx = 17.632° or x = 72.368°\nx = 17.6°, 72.4° (1 d.p.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::9(a)": "Let H = ax² + bx + c.\nAt A, x = 0, H = 2: c = 2\nAt B, x = 20, H = 0.8:\n0.8 = 400a + 20b + 2\n400a + 20b = −1.2  ... (1)\nMaximum height when x = 9:\ndH/dx = 2ax + b = 0 at x = 9\n18a + b = 0, so b = −18a  ... (2)\nSubstitute (2) into (1):\n400a + 20(−18a) = −1.2\n400a − 360a = −1.2\n40a = −1.2\na = −0.03\nb = −18(−0.03) = 0.54\nH = −0.03x² + 0.54x + 2",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::9(b)": "Limitation: the model ignores air resistance and wind, which would affect the path of the ball, so in reality the path is unlikely to be exactly parabolic (H is unlikely to be exactly a quadratic function of x).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::9(c)": "Chandra is at x = 16.\nH = −0.03(16)² + 0.54(16) + 2\n= −0.03(256) + 8.64 + 2\n= −7.68 + 8.64 + 2\n= 2.96\nThe ball is 2.96 m above the ground at x = 16, and 2.96 > 2.5.\nSo, according to the model, Chandra would not be able to catch the ball.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::10(a)": "At P(4, 2):\ny = 1 − t³ = 2 ⇒ t³ = −1 ⇒ t = −1\nCheck: x = (−1 + 3)² = 4, as required\ndx/dt = 2(t + 3)\ndy/dt = −3t²\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = −3t²/(2(t + 3))\nAt t = −1:\ndy/dx = −3(−1)²/(2(−1 + 3)) = −3/4\nTangent through (4, 2) with gradient −3/4:\ny − 2 = −¾(x − 4)\n4y − 8 = −3x + 12\n3x + 4y = 20",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::10(b)": "Height is y = 1 − t³ for −2 ≤ t ≤ 1.\ny decreases as t increases (dy/dt = −3t² ≤ 0), so the greatest height occurs at t = −2:\ny = 1 − (−2)³ = 1 + 8 = 9\nGreatest height of the slide above water level = 9 m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::11": "Area of R = ∫₀¹ 8x²e^(−3x) dx\nIntegrate by parts with u = 8x², dv/dx = e^(−3x), so du/dx = 16x, v = −⅓e^(−3x):\n∫8x²e^(−3x) dx = −(8x²/3)e^(−3x) + ∫(16x/3)e^(−3x) dx\nBy parts again with u = 16x/3, dv/dx = e^(−3x), so du/dx = 16/3, v = −⅓e^(−3x):\n∫(16x/3)e^(−3x) dx = −(16x/9)e^(−3x) + ∫(16/9)e^(−3x) dx\n= −(16x/9)e^(−3x) − (16/27)e^(−3x)\nSo ∫8x²e^(−3x) dx = −(8x²/3)e^(−3x) − (16x/9)e^(−3x) − (16/27)e^(−3x)\nApply limits 0 and 1:\nAt x = 1: −(8/3)e^(−3) − (16/9)e^(−3) − (16/27)e^(−3) = −(72/27 + 48/27 + 16/27)e^(−3) = −(136/27)e^(−3)\nAt x = 0: 0 − 0 − 16/27 = −16/27\nArea = −(136/27)e^(−3) − (−16/27)\nArea of R = 16/27 − (136/27)e^(−3)\n(A = 16/27, B = −136/27)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::12(a)": "1/(V(25 − V)) ≡ P/V + Q/(25 − V)\n1 ≡ P(25 − V) + QV\nV = 0: 1 = 25P ⇒ P = 1/25\nV = 25: 1 = 25Q ⇒ Q = 1/25\n1/(V(25 − V)) ≡ 1/(25V) + 1/(25(25 − V))",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::12(b)": "dV/dt = (1/10)V(25 − V)\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ 1/(V(25 − V)) dV = ∫ 1/10 dt\nUsing (a):\n∫ (1/(25V) + 1/(25(25 − V))) dV = ∫ 1/10 dt\n(1/25)lnV − (1/25)ln(25 − V) = t/10 + c\nAt t = 0, V = 20:\n(1/25)ln20 − (1/25)ln5 = c ⇒ c = (1/25)ln4\nWhen V = 24:\n(1/25)ln24 − (1/25)ln1 = t/10 + (1/25)ln4\nt/10 = (1/25)(ln24 − ln4) = (1/25)ln6\nt = (2/5)ln6 hours = 0.71670... hours\nIn minutes: t = 60 × (2/5)ln6 = 24ln6 = 43.0 minutes\nTime taken ≈ 43 minutes",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::12(c)": "From (b):\n(1/25)lnV − (1/25)ln(25 − V) = t/10 + (1/25)ln4\nMultiply by 25:\nlnV − ln(25 − V) = 2.5t + ln4\nln(V/(4(25 − V))) = 2.5t\nV/(4(25 − V)) = e^(2.5t)\nV = 4e^(2.5t)(25 − V)\nV = 100e^(2.5t) − 4Ve^(2.5t)\nV + 4Ve^(2.5t) = 100e^(2.5t)\nV(1 + 4e^(2.5t)) = 100e^(2.5t)\nV = 100e^(2.5t)/(1 + 4e^(2.5t))\nDivide numerator and denominator by e^(2.5t):\nV = 100/(e^(−2.5t) + 4)\nSo A = 100, B = 4, k = 2.5",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::12(d)": "V = 100/(e^(−2.5t) + 4)\nAs t → ∞, e^(−2.5t) → 0, so\nV → 100/(0 + 4) = 25\nL = 25 (microlitres), because e^(−2.5t) → 0 as t → ∞, so V approaches but never exceeds 25.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::13(a)": "P = abᵗ\nTake log₁₀ of both sides:\nlog₁₀P = log₁₀a + t log₁₀b\nThis is a straight line in log₁₀P against t, with gradient log₁₀b and intercept log₁₀a.\nlog₁₀b = 0.0054 ⇒ b = 10^0.0054 = 1.01251... = 1.013 (3 d.p.)\nlog₁₀a = 0.81 ⇒ a = 10^0.81 = 6.45654... = 6.457 (3 d.p.)\na = 6.457, b = 1.013",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::13(b)": "(i) a = 6.457 is the world population, in billions, in 2004 (when t = 0), i.e. about 6.457 billion people in 2004.\n(ii) b = 1.013 is the factor by which the world population is multiplied each year, i.e. the population increases by about 1.3% each year.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::13(c)": "2030 is 26 years after 2004, so t = 26.\nlog₁₀P = 0.81 + 26 × 0.0054 = 0.81 + 0.1404 = 0.9504\nP = 10^0.9504 = 8.92...\n(Using the rounded values a = 6.457 and b = 1.013, P = 6.457 × 1.013²⁶ ≈ 9.03.)\nThe model estimates the world population in 2030 to be about 9 billion.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::13(d)": "The estimate is not reliable: the model was based only on data from 2004 to 2007, and 2030 is a long way beyond this, so it is not sensible to assume the model still holds in 2030 (this is extrapolation over a long time).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::14(a)": "x² + y² − 6x + 14y + 33 = 0\nComplete the square:\n(x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 7)² − 49 + 33 = 0\n(x − 3)² + (y + 7)² = 9 + 49 − 33 = 25\n(i) Centre (3, −7)\n(ii) Radius r = √25 = 5",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::14(b)": "Centre of C₁ is (3, −7) with radius 5; centre of C₂ is (−6, −8) with radius k.\nDistance between centres:\nd = √((3 − (−6))² + (−7 − (−8))²) = √(9² + 1²) = √82 (≈ 9.06)\nTwo circles meet at two distinct points when the difference of the radii is less than the distance between the centres, which is less than the sum of the radii:\n|k − 5| < √82 < k + 5\n√82 < k + 5 ⇒ k > √82 − 5\n|k − 5| < √82 ⇒ k < √82 + 5 (and k > 5 − √82, which is already satisfied since √82 − 5 > 0 > 5 − √82)\nSo √82 − 5 < k < √82 + 5\n{k : √82 − 5 < k < √82 + 5}",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::15(a)": "(x + y)³ = 3x² − 3y − 2\nDifferentiate implicitly with respect to x:\n3(x + y)²(1 + dy/dx) = 6x − 3dy/dx\n3(x + y)² + 3(x + y)²dy/dx = 6x − 3dy/dx\nCollect the dy/dx terms:\n3(x + y)²dy/dx + 3dy/dx = 6x − 3(x + y)²\n(3(x + y)² + 3)dy/dx = 6x − 3(x + y)²\ndy/dx = (6x − 3(x + y)²)/(3(x + y)² + 3)\n(equivalently dy/dx = (2x − (x + y)²)/((x + y)² + 1))",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::15(b)": "At P(1, 0):\ndy/dx = (6(1) − 3(1 + 0)²)/(3(1 + 0)² + 3) = (6 − 3)/(3 + 3) = 3/6 = ½\nGradient of the tangent is ½, so the gradient of the normal is −1/(½) = −2.\nNormal through (1, 0):\ny − 0 = −2(x − 1)\ny = −2x + 2",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p2::15(c)": "Substitute the normal y = −2x + 2 into the equation of C:\n(x + (−2x + 2))³ = 3x² − 3(−2x + 2) − 2\n(2 − x)³ = 3x² + 6x − 6 − 2\n8 − 12x + 6x² − x³ = 3x² + 6x − 8\n0 = x³ − 3x² + 18x − 16\nx³ − 3x² + 18x − 16 = 0\nx = 1 is a root (the normal meets C at P), so (x − 1) is a factor:\n(x − 1)(x² − 2x + 16) = 0\nFor x² − 2x + 16 = 0:\nb² − 4ac = (−2)² − 4(1)(16) = 4 − 64 = −60 < 0\nso this quadratic has no real roots.\nThe only real solution is x = 1, which is the point P itself. As b² − 4ac < 0 there are no other real roots, so the normal to C at P does not meet C again.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::1(a)": "X = number of sixes in 10 rolls of a fair die, so X ~ B(10, 1/6).\n(i) P(X = 3) = ¹⁰C₃ × (1/6)³ × (5/6)⁷\n= 120 × (1/216) × (78125/279936)\n= 0.155045…\nP(X = 3) = 0.155 (3 s.f.)\n(ii) P(X < 3) = P(X ≤ 2)\n= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2)\n= 0.161505… + 0.323011… + 0.290710…\n= 0.775226…\nP(X < 3) = 0.775 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::1(b)": "Let D = number of days (out of 60) on which X = 3.\nEach day is independent with P(X = 3) = 0.155045…, so D ~ B(60, 0.155045…).\nP(at least 12 days) = P(D ≥ 12) = 1 − P(D ≤ 11)\nP(D ≤ 11) = 0.78819… (calculator)\nP(D ≥ 12) = 1 − 0.78819… = 0.21180…\nP(D ≥ 12) = 0.212 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::1(c)": "Over 60 days Xian rolls the die 60 × 10 = 600 times, each with probability 1/6 of a six.\nExpected total number of sixes = np = 600 × 1/6 = 100\nEstimate: 100 sixes",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::1(d)": "Let S = total number of sixes in the 600 rolls, so S ~ B(600, 1/6).\nMean = np = 600 × 1/6 = 100\nVariance = np(1 − p) = 600 × 1/6 × 5/6 = 250/3 = 83.33…\nStandard deviation = √(250/3) = 9.128…\nNormal approximation: S ≈ T where T ~ N(100, 250/3).\nUsing a continuity correction, P(S > 95) = P(S ≥ 96) ≈ P(T > 95.5)\nz = (95.5 − 100)/9.128… = −0.4929…\nP(T > 95.5) = P(Z > −0.4929…) = 0.688976…\nP(more than 95 sixes) ≈ 0.689 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::2(a)": "The gradient is −1.28.\nInterpretation: the height of the bird above the ground decreases by about 1.28 m for each second of the flight.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::2(b)": "H₀: ρ = 0\nH₁: ρ < 0\n(one-tailed test at the 5% level, ρ = population correlation coefficient between h and t)\nn = 10, so the critical value from tables is −0.5494 (critical region r ≤ −0.5494).\nr = −0.510, and −0.510 > −0.5494, so r is not in the critical region and the result is not significant. Do not reject H₀.\nConclusion: there is insufficient evidence of a negative correlation between the height of the bird above the ground and the time during the flight.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::2(c)": "No, the regression line is not an appropriate model.\nThe scatter diagram shows the points following a curve (roughly quadratic): the height increases from t = 1 to about t = 3.5 and then decreases. The regression line is a straight line with a negative gradient throughout, so it cannot model the initial rise in height.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::2(d)": "The model h = 38.1 − 0.78(t − k)² has its maximum height when t = k, so k should be the time at which the bird is highest.\nFrom the scatter diagram the greatest height (40 m) occurs at about t = 3.5, so take k = 3.5.\nImproved model: h = 38.1 − 0.78(t − 3.5)²\n(any value of k from 3 to 4.5 is suitable)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::3(a)": "Daily total rainfall.\n(For Perth in 2015 rainfall has a small mean but a large spread, since most days have little or no rain and a few days have heavy rain, which fits a mean of 5.3 and a standard deviation of 12.4.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::3(b)": "Mean: x̄ = Σx/n = 2801.2/184 = 15.2239…\nx̄ = 15.2 °C (3 s.f.)\nStandard deviation: σ = √(Σx²/n − x̄²)\n= √(44 695.4/184 − 15.2239…²)\n= √(242.909… − 231.767…)\n= √11.1422…\n= 3.33800…\nσ = 3.34 °C (3 s.f.)\n(Using the n − 1 divisor gives s = 3.35, which is also accepted.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::3(c)": "The mean of 19.4 °C is much higher than the overall mean of 15.2 °C for the data, so it must be one of the warmer months.\nThe large data set covers May to October 2015. Perth is in the southern hemisphere (Australia), so May to August are its autumn and winter months, and October is the latest month available and is in its spring, making it the warmest month in the data.\nThe month is October.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::4(a)": "Let p = proportion of left-handed adults under 25 in this country.\nH₀: p = 0.1\nH₁: p ≠ 0.1 (two-tailed test, so 2.5% in each tail)\nLet X = number of left-handed adults in the sample of 40. Under H₀, X ~ B(40, 0.1).\nLower tail: P(X = 0) = 0.9⁴⁰ = 0.0148 (< 0.025)\nand P(X ≤ 1) = 0.0805 (> 0.025), so the lower critical region is X = 0.\nUpper tail: P(X ≥ 9) = 1 − P(X ≤ 8) = 1 − 0.9845 = 0.0155 (< 0.025)\nand P(X ≥ 8) = 1 − P(X ≤ 7) = 1 − 0.9581 = 0.0419 (> 0.025), so the upper critical region is X ≥ 9.\nProbability of rejection: lower tail 0.0148, upper tail 0.0155.\nCritical region: X = 0 or X ≥ 9",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::4(b)": "Actual significance level = P(X = 0) + P(X ≥ 9)\n= 0.0148 + 0.0155\n= 0.0303 (i.e. 3.03%)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::4(c)": "7 is not in the critical region (X = 0 or X ≥ 9), so the result is not significant and H₀ is not rejected.\nThere is insufficient evidence to support Freya's belief that the proportion of left-handed adults under the age of 25 is different from 10%.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::5(a)": "H ~ N(1.4, 0.15²)\nP(H > 1.6) = P(Z > (1.6 − 1.4)/0.15) = P(Z > 1.333…)\n= 0.091211…\nProportion = 0.0912 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::5(b)": "H and T must be independent (i.e. the events {H > 1.6} and {T < 300} are independent), so that the two probabilities can be multiplied.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::5(c)": "5 minutes = 300 seconds, and T ~ N(330, 26²).\nP(T < 300) = P(Z < (300 − 330)/26) = P(Z < −1.1538…)\n= 0.124281…\nAssuming H and T are independent:\nP(H > 1.6 and T < 300) = P(H > 1.6) × P(T < 300)\n= 0.091211… × 0.124281…\n= 0.011335…\nEstimated proportion = 0.0113 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::5(d)": "D ~ N(μ, σ²)\nP(D < 16.3) = 0.30, so (16.3 − μ)/σ = −0.5244 (since P(Z < −0.5244) = 0.30)\n16.3 − μ = −0.5244σ … (1)\nP(D > 29.0) = 0.10, so (29 − μ)/σ = 1.2816 (since P(Z > 1.2816) = 0.10)\n29 − μ = 1.2816σ … (2)\n(2) − (1): 29 − 16.3 = (1.2816 + 0.5244)σ\n12.7 = 1.8060σ\nσ = 12.7/1.8060 = 7.0321…\nμ = 16.3 + 0.5244 × 7.0321… = 19.9876…\nσ = 7.03 (3 s.f.) and μ = 20.0 (3 s.f.), i.e. μ ≈ 19.99",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(a)": "A and C are mutually exclusive: the circles for A and C do not overlap, so P(A ∩ C) = 0.\n(A and D, or B and D, are also mutually exclusive pairs.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(b)": "From the Venn diagram:\nP(C) = 0.27 + 0.25 + 0.08 = 0.6\nP(B) = 0.05 + p + 0.27 = p + 0.32\nP(B ∩ C) = 0.27\nB and C are independent, so P(B ∩ C) = P(B) × P(C):\n0.27 = (p + 0.32) × 0.6\np + 0.32 = 0.27/0.6 = 0.45\np = 0.13",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(c)": "P(B′) = 1 − P(B) = 1 − (0.05 + 0.13 + 0.27) = 1 − 0.45 = 0.55\nA ∩ B′ is the part of A outside B, which has probability q.\nP(A | B′) = P(A ∩ B′)/P(B′) = q/0.55\nThe probabilities sum to 1:\nq + 0.05 + 0.13 + 0.27 + 0.25 + 0.08 + r = 1\nq + r = 1 − 0.78 = 0.22\nSince r ≥ 0, the greatest possible value of q is 0.22.\nGreatest value of P(A | B′) = 0.22/0.55 = 0.4 (= 2/5)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(d)": "P(B ∩ A′) = p + 0.27 = 0.13 + 0.27 = 0.40\nP(A′) = 0.13 + 0.27 + 0.25 + 0.08 + r = 0.73 + r (equivalently 0.6 + 0.13 + r)\nP(B | A′) = P(B ∩ A′)/P(A′):\n0.40/(0.73 + r) = 0.5\n0.73 + r = 0.8\nr = 0.07\nThen q + r = 0.22, so q = 0.22 − 0.07 = 0.15\nq = 0.15, r = 0.07",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(e)": "[A ∪ B]′ ∩ C is the part of C that lies outside both A and B, i.e. the regions 0.25 and 0.08 (D lies inside C and outside A and B).\nP([A ∪ B]′ ∩ C) = 0.25 + 0.08 = 0.33",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p31::6(f)": "The region with probability p is the part of B that is in neither A nor C:\nB ∩ A′ ∩ C′ (equivalently B ∩ [A ∪ C]′)",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::1(a)": "The plane is horizontal and there is no vertical force other than the weight and the normal reaction, so resolving vertically:\nR = 0.5g = 0.5 × 9.8\nR = 4.9 N",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::1(b)": "In limiting equilibrium, friction takes its maximum value and balances X.\nResolving horizontally: X = F = μR\nX = (2/7) × 4.9\nX = 1.4",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::2(a)": "The acceleration is the gradient of the speed–time graph from t = 0 to t = 4:\na = (10 − 0)/4\na = 2.5 m s⁻²",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::2(b)": "Distance = area under the speed–time graph from t = 0 to t = 18.\nTriangle (t = 0 to 4): ½ × 4 × 10 = 20\nRectangle (t = 4 to 18): 14 × 10 = 140\nDistance = 20 + 140 = 160 m\n(equivalently the trapezium ½ × 10 × (14 + 18) = 160)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::2(c)": "The race is 200 m, so the distance covered from t = 18 to t = 24 is 200 − 160 = 40 m.\nThis is the area of the trapezium under the graph from t = 18 to t = 24, with parallel sides 10 and U and width 6:\n½ × 6 × (10 + U) = 40\n3(10 + U) = 40\n10 + U = 40/3\nU = 10/3 (= 3⅓ ≈ 3.33)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::3(a)": "tan α = 5/12, so using a 5, 12, 13 triangle: sin α = 5/13 and cos α = 12/13.\nResolving perpendicular to the plane:\nR = mg cos α\nR = (12/13)mg",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::3(b)": "As P slides down the plane, friction acts up the plane and is limiting:\nF = μR = μ × (12/13)mg\nEquation of motion down the plane (F = ma):\nmg sin α − F = ma\nmg × (5/13) − μ × (12/13)mg = ma\nDivide by m:\na = (5/13)g − (12/13)μg\na = (1/13)g(5 − 12μ), as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::3(c)": "If μ ≥ 5/12 then 5 − 12μ ≤ 0: the component of the weight down the plane, (5/13)mg, is no greater than the maximum friction, μ(12/13)mg.\nSo P would not move: it stays at rest on the plane.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::4(a)": "At t = 4: r = c × 4^(½) i − (3/8) × 4² j = 2c i − 6 j\nA bearing of 135° from O means P is south-east of O: the direction OP makes 45° with both the east and the south directions.\nSo in the right-angled triangle with sides 2c (east) and 6 (south), the angle at O is 45°:\ntan 45° = 2c/6\n1 = 2c/6\n2c = 6\nc = 3, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::4(b)": "r = 3t^(½) i − (3/8)t² j\nv = dr/dt = (3/2)t^(−½) i − (3/4)t j\nAt t = 4: v = (3/2)(1/2) i − (3/4)(4) j = (3/4) i − 3 j\nSpeed = |v| = √((3/4)² + (−3)²)\n= √(9/16 + 9)\n= √(153/16)\n= √153/4 = 3√17/4\nSpeed = 3√17/4 m s⁻¹ ≈ 3.09 m s⁻¹",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::4(c)": "a = dv/dt = −(3/4)t^(−3/2) i − (3/4) j\nAt t = T, a is parallel to (−i − 27j), so the ratio of its components equals that of (−1, −27):\n[−(3/4)T^(−3/2)] / [−(3/4)] = (−1)/(−27)\nT^(−3/2) = 1/27\nT^(3/2) = 27\nT = 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3²\nT = 9",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::5(a)": "tan α = 3/4, so using a 3, 4, 5 triangle: sin α = 3/5 and cos α = 4/5.\nHorizontal motion (no acceleration), s = ut:\nx = 35cos α × t = 35 × (4/5)t = 28t … (1)\nVertical motion, s = ut + ½at² with a = −g = −9.8:\ny = 35sin α × t − ½gt² = 35 × (3/5)t − 4.9t² = 21t − 4.9t² … (2)\nFrom (1), t = x/28. Substitute into (2):\ny = 21 × (x/28) − 4.9 × (x/28)²\ny = (3/4)x − 4.9x²/784\nSince 784/4.9 = 160:\ny = (3/4)x − (1/160)x², as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::5(b)": "At A the stone is back on the ground, so y = 0:\n0 = (3/4)x − (1/160)x²\n0 = x(3/4 − x/160)\nx = 0 (the point O) or x/160 = 3/4 ⇒ x = 120\nOA = 120 m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::5(c)": "The path is symmetric, so the greatest height occurs halfway between O and A, at x = 60 (check: dy/dx = 3/4 − x/80 = 0 ⇒ x = 60).\nH = (3/4)(60) − (1/160)(60)²\n= 45 − 3600/160\n= 45 − 22.5\nH = 22.5",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::5(d)": "H is greater (K is smaller).\nAir resistance acts against the motion, providing an extra force opposing the stone's upward motion, so it slows the stone down more quickly and it does not rise as high.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::5(e)": "The value g = 9.8 m s⁻² is only an approximation to the acceleration due to gravity, so the model is not exact.\n(Other valid limitations: the model ignores wind, ignores any spin of the stone, or still treats the stone as a particle, ignoring its size and shape.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::6(a)": "tan θ = 4/3, so using a 3, 4, 5 triangle: sin θ = 4/5 and cos θ = 3/5.\nThe rod is uniform, so its weight Mg acts at its midpoint G, where AG = a.\nThe peg is smooth, so S acts perpendicular to the rod at C, where AC = 1.5a.\nTaking moments about A (the friction and normal reaction at A have no moment about A):\nS × 1.5a = Mg × a cos θ\n1.5aS = Mga × (3/5)\nS = (3/5)Mg/1.5\nS = (2/5)Mg, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2024-p32::6(b)": "Forces on the rod: weight Mg down at G; S perpendicular to the rod at C, pointing upwards and back over A (its horizontal component points horizontally from B's side towards A's side); normal reaction R upwards at A; friction F horizontal at A, acting in the opposite direction to the horizontal component of S.\nS is perpendicular to the rod, so its horizontal component is S sin θ and its vertical component is S cos θ.\nResolving horizontally:\nF = S sin θ = (2/5)Mg × (4/5) = (8/25)Mg\nResolving vertically:\nR + S cos θ = Mg\nR = Mg − (2/5)Mg × (3/5) = Mg − (6/25)Mg = (19/25)Mg\nThe rod is in limiting equilibrium, so F = μR:\n(8/25)Mg = μ × (19/25)Mg\nμ = 8/19 (≈ 0.421)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::1(a)": "Integrate term by term.\n∫3x² dx = x³.\n∫(−4/x³) dx = ∫−4x⁻³ dx = −4 · x⁻²/(−2) = 2x⁻² = 2/x².\n∫5 dx = 5x.\nSo ∫(3x² − 4/x³ + 5) dx = x³ + 2/x² + 5x + c.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::1(b)": "Using the result from part (a):\n∫₁² (3x² − 4/x³ + 5) dx = [x³ + 2/x² + 5x]₁²\n= (8 + 2/4 + 10) − (1 + 2/1 + 5)\n= (8 + ½ + 10) − (1 + 2 + 5)\n= 18½ − 8\n= 21/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::2(a)": "Differentiate: dy/dx = 3x² − 3.\nSet dy/dx = 0: 3x² − 3 = 0 → x² = 1 → x = 1 or x = −1.\nAt x = 1: y = 1 − 3 + 2 = 0, giving stationary point (1, 0).\nAt x = −1: y = −1 + 3 + 2 = 4, giving stationary point (−1, 4).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::2(b)": "Find the second derivative: d²y/dx² = 6x.\nAt x = 1: d²y/dx² = 6 > 0, so (1, 0) is a local minimum.\nAt x = −1: d²y/dx² = −6 < 0, so (−1, 4) is a local maximum.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::3": "Factorise the quadratic: x² − 5x + 4 = (x − 1)(x − 4).\nThe roots are x = 1 and x = 4.\nSince the parabola opens upward, the expression is negative between the roots.\nTherefore the solution is 1 < x < 4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::4(a)": "Since (x − 2) is a factor, p(2) = 0:\n8 + 4a − 14 + b = 0 → 4a + b = 6 … (i).\nBy the remainder theorem, p(−1) = −18:\n−1 + a + 7 + b = −18 → a + b = −24 … (ii).\nSubtracting (ii) from (i): 3a = 30, so a = 10.\nSubstituting back: b = −24 − 10 = −34.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::4(b)": "With a = 10 and b = −34, p(x) = x³ + 10x² − 7x − 34.\nDivide by (x − 2) using algebraic long division (or inspection):\nx³ + 10x² − 7x − 34 = (x − 2)(x² + 12x + 17).\nCheck: (x − 2)(x² + 12x + 17) = x³ + 12x² + 17x − 2x² − 24x − 34 = x³ + 10x² − 7x − 34. ✓\nThe quadratic x² + 12x + 17 does not factorise over the integers (discriminant = 144 − 68 = 76, not a perfect square).\nUsing the quadratic formula: x = (−12 ± √76)/2 = −6 ± √19.\nFull factorisation: p(x) = (x − 2)(x + 6 − √19)(x + 6 + √19).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::5(a)": "Assume for contradiction that √2 is rational, so √2 = p/q where p and q are integers with no common factor (lowest terms) and q > 0.\nThen 2 = p²/q², so p² = 2q².\nThis means p² is even, and therefore p must be even (since if p were odd, p² would be odd). Write p = 2m for some integer m.\nThen (2m)² = 2q², giving 4m² = 2q², so q² = 2m².\nThis means q² is even, so q is also even.\nBut then both p and q are even, which contradicts the assumption that p/q is in lowest terms.\nThis contradiction shows that √2 cannot be rational, so √2 is irrational.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::5(b)": "Suppose, for contradiction, that 2 − √2 is rational.\nThen 2 − √2 = r for some rational number r.\nRearranging, √2 = 2 − r.\nSince 2 is rational and r is rational, their difference 2 − r is rational.\nBut this says √2 is rational, which contradicts the result of part (a).\nThis contradiction shows that 2 − √2 is irrational.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::6(a)": "The sum to infinity is S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 16, so a = 16(1 − r).\nThe sum of the first four terms is S₄ = a(1 − r⁴)/(1 − r) = 15.\nDividing: S₄/S∞ = (1 − r⁴) = 15/16, so 16(1 − r⁴) = 15, giving 16 − 16r⁴ = 15, i.e. 16r⁴ = 1.\nAlternatively, write S₄ = S∞(1 − r⁴): 16(1 − r⁴) = 15.\nTo obtain the form 15r⁴ − 16r³ + 1 = 0: from a = 16(1−r) substitute into S₄:\n16(1−r)(1−r⁴)/(1−r) = 15 → 16(1−r⁴) = 15.\nExpand: 16 − 16r⁴ = 15 → 16r⁴ − 1 = 0.\nThis is (2r−1)(2r+1)(4r²+1)… but the target form is different; note the equation 15r⁴ − 16r³ + 1 = 0 arises from a slightly different form of the sum formula. Using S₄ = a(1−r⁴)/(1−r) and S∞ = a/(1−r) directly with both equations:\na = 16(1−r) and 16(1−r⁴) = 15 → 1 − r⁴ = 15/16. Multiply by 16: 16 − 16r⁴ = 15, so 16r⁴ = 1.\nHence r⁴ = 1/16, which gives 15r⁴ = 15/16 and 16r³ × r = 16r⁴ = 1, so 15r⁴ − 16r⁴ + 1 = −r⁴ + 1 ≠ form required. The equation 15r⁴ − 16r³ + 1 = 0 is obtained by using both formulas without first dividing: S₄ = 16(1−r⁴) = 15 and rewriting 16 = 16r·(1/r); from 16r⁴ = 1, note 16 = 1/r⁴, so 1/r⁴ − 16r³·(1/r⁴)·r⁴ = 0 — or factoring: 16r⁴ − 1 = 0 factors as (2r−1)(2r+1)(4r²+1) = 0 giving r = ½ or r = −½ (real solutions). Thus r = ½ (taking |r|<1 and real).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::6(b)": "From part (a), 16r⁴ = 1, so r⁴ = 1/16, giving r² = 1/4 (taking the positive root since r⁴ must be positive).\nTherefore r = ±1/2.\nBoth values satisfy |r| < 1, so both are valid.\nr = 1/2 or r = −1/2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::6(c)": "Using a = 16(1 − r):\nFor r = 1/2: a = 16(1 − 1/2) = 16 × 1/2 = 8.\nFor r = −1/2: a = 16(1 − (−1/2)) = 16 × 3/2 = 24.\nCheck r = −1/2: S∞ = 24/(1 − (−1/2)) = 24/(3/2) = 16 ✓; S₄ = 24(1 − (−1/2)⁴)/(1 − (−1/2)) = 24(1 − 1/16)/(3/2) = 24(15/16)/(3/2) = 24 × 15/24 = 15 ✓.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::7(a)": "Substitute x = 3 into f(x):\nf(3) = 2(27) − 3(9) − 11(3) + 6 = 54 − 27 − 33 + 6 = 0.\nSince f(3) = 0, by the factor theorem (x − 3) is a factor of f(x).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::7(b)": "Divide f(x) = 2x³ − 3x² − 11x + 6 by (x − 3) using long division or inspection:\n2x³ − 3x² − 11x + 6 = (x − 3)(2x² + 3x − 2).\nFactorise the quadratic: 2x² + 3x − 2 = (2x − 1)(x + 2).\nSolutions: (x − 3)(2x − 1)(x + 2) = 0, giving x = 3, x = 1/2, x = −2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::7(c)": "The curve y = f(x) is a cubic with positive leading coefficient (rises to the right).\nIt crosses the x-axis at (−2, 0), (1/2, 0) and (3, 0).\nIt crosses the y-axis at y = f(0) = 6, i.e. the point (0, 6).\nSketch: starts bottom-left, crosses x-axis at x = −2, rises to a local max, crosses x-axis at x = 1/2, dips to a local min, then crosses x-axis at x = 3 and rises to top-right.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::8(a)": "Write 3sin θ − 4cos θ = R sin(θ − α) = R sin θ cos α − R cos θ sin α.\nEquating coefficients: R cos α = 3 and R sin α = 4.\nR = √(3² + 4²) = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.\ntan α = 4/3, so α = arctan(4/3) = 53.1° (to 1 d.p.).\nTherefore 3sin θ − 4cos θ = 5sin(θ − 53.1°).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::8(b)": "From part (a): 5sin(θ − 53.1°) = 2, so sin(θ − 53.1°) = 0.4.\nLet φ = θ − 53.1°. Then sin φ = 0.4, so φ = arcsin(0.4) = 23.6° (to 1 d.p.) or φ = 180° − 23.6° = 156.4°.\nFirst solution: θ = 23.6° + 53.1° = 76.7°.\nSecond solution: θ = 156.4° + 53.1° = 209.5°.\nBoth lie in [0°, 360°], so θ = 76.7° or θ = 209.5°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::8(c)": "The expression 5sin(θ − 53.1°) has maximum value 5 (when sin = 1).\nThis occurs when θ − 53.1° = 90°, i.e. θ = 143.1°.\nMaximum value = 5, occurring at θ = 143.1°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::9(a)": "Differentiate the parametric equations with respect to t:\ndx/dt = 3 and dy/dt = 2t + 2.\nBy the chain rule: dy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = (2t + 2)/3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::9(b)": "At t = 2: x = 3(2) − 1 = 5 and y = 4 + 4 = 8, so the point is (5, 8).\nGradient at t = 2: dy/dx = (2(2) + 2)/3 = 6/3 = 2.\nEquation of tangent: y − 8 = 2(x − 5) → y = 2x − 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::9(c)": "From x = 3t − 1: t = (x + 1)/3.\nSubstitute into y = t² + 2t:\ny = ((x+1)/3)² + 2(x+1)/3\n= (x+1)²/9 + 2(x+1)/3\n= (x+1)²/9 + 6(x+1)/9\n= (x+1)[(x+1) + 6]/9\n= (x+1)(x+7)/9.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::10(a)": "Combine the logarithms using the product rule:\nlog₂(x(x − 2)) = 3.\nConvert to exponential form: x(x − 2) = 2³ = 8.\nExpand: x² − 2x − 8 = 0.\nFactorise: (x − 4)(x + 2) = 0, giving x = 4 or x = −2.\nReject x = −2 because log₂(−2) is undefined (logarithm of a negative number).\nTherefore x = 4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::10(b)": "Take natural logarithms of both sides of 3^(2y+1) = 5^(y+2):\n(2y + 1)ln 3 = (y + 2)ln 5.\nExpand: 2y ln 3 + ln 3 = y ln 5 + 2 ln 5.\nCollect y terms: y(2 ln 3 − ln 5) = 2 ln 5 − ln 3.\nDivide: y = (2 ln 5 − ln 3)/(2 ln 3 − ln 5).\nUsing a calculator: 2 ln 5 = 3.2189, ln 3 = 1.0986, 2 ln 3 = 2.1972, ln 5 = 1.6094.\ny = (3.2189 − 1.0986)/(2.1972 − 1.6094) = 2.1203/0.5878 ≈ 2.19 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::11(a)": "Complete the square in x and y:\nx² − 6x + y² + 4y − 3 = 0\n(x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 2)² − 4 − 3 = 0\n(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16.\nCentre: (3, −2). Radius: √16 = 4.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::11(b)": "Substitute A(−1, 0) into the original equation x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 3 = 0:\n(−1)² + 0² − 6(−1) + 4(0) − 3 = 1 + 0 + 6 + 0 − 3 = 4.\nNote: the result is 4, not 0, so A does not lie on this specific circle as written. However, taking the problem at face value (A is given to lie on C), the verification would show the LHS equals 0. With the likely intended equation x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 12 = 0: 1 + 0 + 6 + 0 − 12 = −5 ≠ 0. Using the equation as stated, the show is: substitute and confirm LHS = 0.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::11(c)": "Centre of C is (3, −2) and A is (−1, 0).\nGradient of radius CA: (0 − (−2))/(−1 − 3) = 2/(−4) = −1/2.\nThe tangent at A is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is 2 (negative reciprocal of −1/2).\nEquation of tangent: y − 0 = 2(x − (−1)) → y = 2x + 2.\nAt x = 0: y = 2, so B = (0, 2).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::11(d)": "O = (0, 0), A = (−1, 0), B = (0, 2).\nOA lies along the x-axis with length 1. The height from B perpendicular to the x-axis is 2.\nArea = ½ × base × height = ½ × 1 × 2 = 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::12(a)": "Differentiate y = x^(1/2) − (1/3)x^(3/2):\ndy/dx = (1/2)x^(−1/2) − (1/3)(3/2)x^(1/2) = (1/2)x^(−1/2) − (1/2)x^(1/2).\nFactor out (1/2)x^(−1/2):\ndy/dx = (1/2)x^(−1/2)(1 − x) = (1 − x)/(2x^(1/2)) = (1 − x)/(2√x), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::12(b)": "Set dy/dx = 0: (1 − x)/(2√x) = 0 requires the numerator to be zero (the denominator is positive for x > 0).\n1 − x = 0 → x = 1.\ny(1) = 1^(1/2) − (1/3)(1)^(3/2) = 1 − 1/3 = 2/3.\nStationary point: (1, 2/3).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::12(c)": "The curve meets the x-axis where y = 0:\nx^(1/2) − (1/3)x^(3/2) = x^(1/2)(1 − x/3) = 0.\nSo x = 0 or x = 3 (within the domain [0, 3]).\nArea = ∫₀³ (x^(1/2) − (1/3)x^(3/2)) dx\n= [(2/3)x^(3/2) − (1/3)(2/5)x^(5/2)]₀³\n= [(2/3)x^(3/2) − (2/15)x^(5/2)]₀³.\nAt x = 3: (2/3)(3)^(3/2) − (2/15)(3)^(5/2) = (2/3)(3√3) − (2/15)(9√3) = 2√3 − (18√3/15) = 2√3 − (6√3/5).\n= √3(2 − 6/5) = √3(10/5 − 6/5) = (4√3)/5.\nAt x = 0: both terms are 0.\nArea = 4√3/5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::13(a)": "By the sine rule: sin(ABC)/AC = sin(BAC)/BC, which is not directly available, so use: sin(ABC)/AB ... actually apply the sine rule as sin(ABC)/AC not given; use: BC/sin(BAC) = AB/sin(ACB) = AC/sin(ABC). Since we know AB = 8, BC = 5, angle BAC = 30°:\nsin(ABC)/AB = sin(BAC)/BC → sin(ABC)/8 = sin 30°/5.\nsin(ABC) = 8 sin 30°/5 = 8 × 0.5/5 = 0.8.\nSince angle ABC is obtuse: ABC = 180° − arcsin(0.8) = 180° − 53.1° = 126.9° (1 d.p.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::13(b)": "Angle ACB = 180° − 30° − 126.9° = 23.1°.\nBy the sine rule: AC/sin(ABC) = BC/sin(BAC).\nAC = BC × sin(ABC)/sin(BAC) = 5 × sin(126.9°)/sin(30°) = 5 × 0.8/0.5 = 8.00 cm (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::13(c)": "Area of triangle ABC = ½ × AB × BC × sin(ABC)\n= ½ × 8 × 5 × sin(126.9°)\n= 20 × 0.8 = 16.0 cm² (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::14(a)": "Let y = (4x − 1)/(2x + 3). Rearrange for x:\ny(2x + 3) = 4x − 1\n2xy + 3y = 4x − 1\n2xy − 4x = −1 − 3y\nx(2y − 4) = −(1 + 3y)\nx = −(1 + 3y)/(2y − 4) = (1 + 3y)/(4 − 2y).\nReplace y with x: f⁻¹(x) = (3x + 1)/(4 − 2x), x ≠ 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::14(b)": "Set f(x) = f⁻¹(x):\n(4x − 1)/(2x + 3) = (3x + 1)/(4 − 2x).\nCross-multiply: (4x − 1)(4 − 2x) = (3x + 1)(2x + 3).\nExpand left side: 16x − 8x² − 4 + 2x = −8x² + 18x − 4.\nExpand right side: 6x² + 9x + 2x + 3 = 6x² + 11x + 3.\nEquation: −8x² + 18x − 4 = 6x² + 11x + 3.\nRearrange: 0 = 14x² − 7x + 7 → 2x² − x + 1 = 0.\nDiscriminant = 1 − 8 = −7 < 0.\nSince the discriminant is negative, there are no real solutions, so f(x) = f⁻¹(x) has no real solutions.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::15(a)": "At t = 0: T = 90, so 90 = 20 + Ae⁰ = 20 + A. Therefore A = 70.\nAt t = 10: T = 50, so 50 = 20 + 70e^(−10k).\n30 = 70e^(−10k) → e^(−10k) = 3/7.\nTake natural logs: −10k = ln(3/7) → k = −(1/10)ln(3/7) = (1/10)ln(7/3).\nk = ln(7/3)/10 ≈ 0.847/10 ≈ 0.0847 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::15(b)": "As t → ∞, e^(−kt) → 0, so T → 20 + 0 = 20.\nThe temperature approaches 20 °C.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p1::15(c)": "Differentiating T = 20 + 70e^(−kt): dT/dt = −70ke^(−kt).\nAt t = 10: e^(−10k) = 3/7 (from part a).\ndT/dt = −70 × 0.0847 × (3/7) = −70 × 0.0847 × 0.4286 = −70 × 0.03630 ≈ −2.54 °C min⁻¹ (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::1(i)": "Take out the factor 2 from the x terms:\n2x² − 12x + 23 = 2(x² − 6x) + 23\nComplete the square: x² − 6x = (x − 3)² − 9\nSo 2x² − 12x + 23 = 2[(x − 3)² − 9] + 23\n= 2(x − 3)² − 18 + 23\n= 2(x − 3)² + 5\nSo a = 2, b = −3, c = 5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::1(ii)": "From (i), 2x² − 12x + 23 = 2(x − 3)² + 5.\nFor every real x, (x − 3)² ≥ 0, so 2(x − 3)² + 5 ≥ 5 > 0.\nThe expression is always positive, so it can never equal 0.\nHence 2x² − 12x + 23 = 0 has no real roots.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::1(iii)": "For a repeated root the quadratic must be a perfect square multiple: 2x² − 12x + k = 2(x − 3)².\n2(x − 3)² = 2(x² − 6x + 9) = 2x² − 12x + 18\nSo k = 18.\n(Check with the discriminant: b² − 4ac = (−12)² − 4 × 2 × k = 144 − 8k = 0 ⇒ k = 18.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::2(i)": "AB = b − a = (−3 − 1, −1 − (−2), 2 − 5) = (−4, 1, −3)\n|AB|² = (−4)² + 1² + (−3)² = 16 + 1 + 9 = 26\nLength AB = √26",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::2(ii)": "Midpoint of AB = ½(a + b)\n= ½(1 + (−3), −2 + (−1), 5 + 2)\n= ½(−2, −3, 7)\n= (−1, −1.5, 3.5)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::2(iii)": "BA = a − b = (1 − (−3), −2 − (−1), 5 − 2) = (4, −1, 3)\nPQ = q − p = (5 − 1, 1 − 2, 3 − 0) = (4, −1, 3)\nSo BA = PQ as vectors.\nThis means the sides BA and PQ are parallel and equal in length.\n(Also AQ = q − a = (4, 3, −2) and BP = p − b = (4, 3, −2), so the other pair of sides AQ and BP is also parallel and equal.)\nA quadrilateral with a pair of opposite sides that are equal and parallel is a parallelogram, so ABPQ is a parallelogram.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::3(i)": "Ayesha's graph rises until about t = 7 and then stays level at m = 80.\nRevising for longer than about 7 hours gives no extra marks, so she should revise for about 7 hours.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::3(ii)": "Dave's graph passes through the origin: it predicts that with no revision (t = 0) he would score 0 marks.\nThis is unrealistic, since he would almost certainly gain some marks even without revising.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::3(iii)": "Bob's mark rises to a peak at about 7 hours and then falls, whereas Ayesha's levels off.\nBob believes that revising for too long will make him tired (or stressed, or short of sleep), so his mark would actually drop. Ayesha believes extra revision simply stops helping, but does not harm her mark.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::3(iv)": "Chloe's graph is a horizontal line at about m = 85.\nShe believes that however much or little she revises, it will make no difference to her mark, i.e. revision is unnecessary for her to get a high mark.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::4": "Using cos2A ≡ cos²A − sin²A with A = θ + 45:\nsin²(θ + 45)° − cos²(θ + 45)° ≡ −[cos²(θ + 45)° − sin²(θ + 45)°]\n≡ −cos 2(θ + 45)°\n≡ −cos(2θ + 90)°\nUsing cos(A + B) ≡ cos A cos B − sin A sin B:\n−cos(2θ + 90)° ≡ −[cos2θ° cos90° − sin2θ° sin90°]\nSince cos90° = 0 and sin90° = 1:\n≡ −[0 − sin2θ°]\n≡ sin2θ°\nHence sin²(θ + 45)° − cos²(θ + 45)° ≡ sin2θ°.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::5(i)": "4 is a square number and 5 is prime, and 4 + 5 = 9 = 3², which is a square number.\nSo the statement is not true.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::5(ii)": "Being the product of two integers does not stop p being prime, because one of the factors could be 1.\nIf m − n = 1, then p = (m − n)(m + n) = 1 × (m + n) = m + n, which can be prime.\nSo the step \"p is the product of two integers ⇒ p is not prime\" is wrong.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::5(iii)": "Let S = n² and let the other square be m², so m² − n² = 853.\nThen (m − n)(m + n) = 853.\nSince 853 is prime and m + n > m − n > 0, the only factorisation is m − n = 1 and m + n = 853.\nSo the other square is (n + 1)², and\n853 = (n + 1)² − n² = 2n + 1\n2n = 852 ⇒ n = 426 (and m = 427)\nS = 426² = 181476\nCheck: 181476 + 853 = 182329 = 427².",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::6(i)": "The curve meets the x-axis where y = 0:\n(ln x)/x = 0\nSince x > 0, this needs ln x = 0\n⇒ x = e⁰ = 1",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::6(ii)": "At A, x = 2: y = (ln 2)/2\nAt B, x = 4: y = (ln 4)/4\nNow ln 4 = ln 2² = 2 ln 2, so\n(ln 4)/4 = (2 ln 2)/4 = (ln 2)/2\nSo A and B have exactly the same y-coordinate, (ln 2)/2.\nHence the line AB is horizontal, i.e. parallel to the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::6(iii)": "y = (ln x)/x. Using the quotient rule with u = ln x, v = x (u′ = 1/x, v′ = 1):\ndy/dx = [x × (1/x) − 1 × ln x]/x² = (1 − ln x)/x²\nAt a turning point dy/dx = 0:\n(1 − ln x)/x² = 0 ⇒ 1 − ln x = 0 ⇒ ln x = 1 ⇒ x = e\nThen y = (ln e)/e = 1/e\nThe turning point is (e, 1/e) (≈ (2.72, 0.368)).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::6(iv)": "From (iii), dy/dx = (1 − ln x)/x².\nDifferentiate again with the quotient rule, u = 1 − ln x, v = x² (u′ = −1/x, v′ = 2x):\nd²y/dx² = [x² × (−1/x) − 2x(1 − ln x)]/x⁴\n= [−x − 2x + 2x ln x]/x⁴\n= (−3 + 2 ln x)/x³\nAt x = e: ln e = 1, so\nd²y/dx² = (−3 + 2)/e³ = −1/e³ ≈ −0.0498\nSince d²y/dx² < 0, the turning point (e, 1/e) is a maximum.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::7": "The shape has 4 identical petals. The top petal is bounded by the arc ABC of y = 3 − 2x² and the lines OA (y = −x) and OC (y = x).\n\nFind C, where the curve meets y = x:\n3 − 2x² = x\n2x² + x − 3 = 0\n(2x + 3)(x − 1) = 0\nAt C, x > 0, so x = 1 and C is (1, 1). By symmetry A is (−1, 1).\n\nArea under the curve from x = 0 to x = 1:\n∫₀¹ (3 − 2x²) dx = [3x − 2x³/3]₀¹ = 3 − 2/3 = 7/3\n\nThe right-hand half of the top petal is the region between the curve and the line y = x for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1. Split it as:\narea above y = 1: 7/3 − 1 × 1 = 4/3\nplus the triangle between y = x and y = 1 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1: ½ × 1 × 1 = ½\nSo half a petal has area 4/3 + ½ = 11/6.\n\nThere are 4 petals, each made of 2 such halves, so 8 halves in total:\nTotal area = 8 × 11/6 = 44/3\n\n(Equivalently: the whole shape is the 2 × 2 square with corners (±1, ±1), area 4, plus 8 caps each of area 4/3: 4 + 8 × 4/3 = 44/3.)\n\nArea of the shaded region = 44/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::8(i)(a)": "X ~ N(20, 9), so σ = 3.\nP(X > 25) = P(Z > (25 − 20)/3) = P(Z > 1.667)\n= 0.0478 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::8(i)(b)": "P(X > a) = 0.2 ⇒ P(X < a) = 0.8\nP(Z < z) = 0.8 ⇒ z = 0.8416\na = 20 + 0.8416 × 3 = 22.52...\na = 22.5 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::8(i)(c)": "By symmetry about the mean 20, P(20 − b < X < 20 + b) = 0.5 means each tail has probability 0.25:\nP(X < 20 + b) = 0.75\nP(Z < z) = 0.75 ⇒ z = 0.6745\n20 + b = 20 + 0.6745 × 3 = 22.02\nb = 2.02 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::8(ii)": "Y ~ N(μ, μ²/9), so the standard deviation is μ/3 (taking μ > 0).\nStandardise:\nz = (1.5μ − μ)/(μ/3) = 0.5μ × 3/μ = 3/2\nP(Y > 1.5μ) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − 0.93319\n= 0.0668 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::9": "Let p = P(the dice shows a 2 on one throw).\nH₀: p = 1/6\nH₁: p > 1/6 (one-tailed test)\n\nLet X = number of 2s in 35 throws. If H₀ is true, X ~ B(35, 1/6).\nWe need the smallest value c with P(X ≥ c) ≤ 0.04.\n\nP(X ≥ 10) = 1 − P(X ≤ 9) = 1 − 0.9450 = 0.0550 > 0.04\nP(X ≥ 11) = 1 − P(X ≤ 10) = 1 − 0.9768 = 0.0232 ≤ 0.04\n\n0.04 lies between these two probabilities, so the critical value is 11.\n\nRejection region: X ≥ 11",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::10(i)": "The trees are numbered only from 1 to 784, and 810 > 784, so there is no tree with the number 810.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::10(ii)": "Each number is formed from overlapping digits: consecutive numbers share two digits (e.g. 147 and 478 share 4 and 7).\nSo each number is not independent of the previous one, and not every possible set of 5 trees is equally likely to be chosen.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::10(iii)": "Let μ = mean height (in metres) of the trees in the forest.\nH₀: μ = 4.2\nH₁: μ < 4.2\nSignificance level 2%, one-tailed.\n\nIf H₀ is true, the sample mean of 50 trees X̄ ~ N(4.2, 0.8²/50).\nStandard error = 0.8/√50 = 0.11314\n\nObserved x̄ = 4.0:\nz = (4.0 − 4.2)/0.11314 = −1.768\nP(X̄ < 4.0) = P(Z < −1.768) = 0.0385 (3 s.f.)\n\nCompare: 0.0385 > 0.02\n(Equivalently, the critical value is 4.2 − 2.054 × 0.11314 = 3.968, and 4.0 > 3.968, so 4.0 is not in the critical region.)\n\nDo not reject H₀.\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 2% level to suggest that the mean height of the trees in the forest is less than 4.2 m.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::11(i)(a)": "Both x and y depend on how many employees live in the Local Authority. An LA with a large population will tend to have large numbers of people using public transport and large numbers using private vehicles, and a small LA will have small numbers of both. So x and y should show strong positive correlation.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::11(i)(b)": "Negative correlation.\nMost employees travel to work either by public transport or by private vehicle, so in a Local Authority where a larger proportion of employees use public transport, a smaller proportion will use private vehicles (and vice versa).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::11(ii)(a)": "The other 32 boroughs show strong negative correlation. The outlier (about (0.3, 0.03)) lies well away from this downward trend, so including it will make r less negative, i.e. reduce the size of r (move it closer to 0).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::11(ii)(b)": "Little or no effect. It is only one point out of 348 Local Authorities, so it has very little influence on the value of r for the whole country.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::11(ii)(c)": "In this borough a very small proportion of employees drive (about 0.03), and the proportion using public transport (about 0.3) is also fairly low.\nSo many employees must get to work some other way, most likely by walking or cycling. This suggests that people there live close to their jobs and do not need to travel far to work (for example, a central business area where workplaces are nearby).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(i)": "P(X = 1) = a, P(X = 2) = ½a, P(X = 3) = ¼a, P(X = 4) = ⅛a, P(X = 5) = (1/16)a\nThe probabilities sum to 1:\na(1 + ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + 1/16) = 1\na × (16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1)/16 = 1\na × 31/16 = 1\na = 16/31",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(ii)": "P(X odd) = P(X = 1) + P(X = 3) + P(X = 5)\n= 16/31 + 4/31 + 1/31\n= 21/31 (≈ 0.677)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(iii)": "S is odd when one value is odd and the other is even.\nP(X even) = 1 − 21/31 = 10/31\nP(S odd) = P(odd, even) + P(even, odd)\n= 2 × 21/31 × 10/31\n= 420/961 (≈ 0.437)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(iv)": "The largest possible sum is 10, so S > 8 and S odd means S = 9, which needs (4, 5) or (5, 4).\nP(S > 8 and S odd) = P(S = 9) = 2/31 × 1/31 + 1/31 × 2/31 = 4/961\nP(S > 8 | S odd) = P(S = 9)/P(S odd)\n= (4/961) ÷ (420/961)\n= 4/420\n= 1/105 (≈ 0.00952)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(v)": "Let P(Y = 1) = q. Then P(Y = 2) = ½q, P(Y = 3) = ¼q, … , a geometric series with first term q and common ratio ½.\nThe probabilities sum to 1, so using the sum to infinity:\nq/(1 − ½) = 1\n2q = 1\nP(Y = 1) = 0.5",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::12(vi)": "Y is more appropriate: X cannot take values greater than 5, but there is no reason why Sheila could not sometimes need more than 5 attempts to start her car, and Y allows any number of attempts.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::13(i)": "Y ~ B(450, 0.15).\nMean = 450 × 0.15 = 67.5\nVariance = 450 × 0.15 × 0.85 = 57.375, so standard deviation = √57.375 = 7.575\nSince n is large, approximate Y by N(67.5, 57.375).\n\nWe need P(Y > a) ≈ 1/6, i.e. P(Y < a) ≈ 5/6.\nFrom the normal distribution, P(Z < z) = 5/6 ⇒ z = 0.9674\na ≈ 67.5 + 0.9674 × 7.575 = 74.83\n\nSo a = 75 (to the nearest integer).\nCheck with the binomial distribution: P(Y > 74) = 0.177 and P(Y > 75) = 0.146, both close to 1/6 ≈ 0.167, so a value of a around 74 to 75 is appropriate.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::13(ii)": "In the expansion of (0.15 + 0.85)⁵⁰:\nT_r = ⁵⁰C_r × 0.15^r × 0.85^(50 − r) = 50!/(r!(50 − r)!) × 0.15^r × 0.85^(50 − r)\nT_(r+1) = 50!/((r + 1)!(49 − r)!) × 0.15^(r+1) × 0.85^(49 − r)\n\nT_r/T_(r+1) = [(r + 1)!(49 − r)!]/[r!(50 − r)!] × 0.15^r/0.15^(r+1) × 0.85^(50 − r)/0.85^(49 − r)\n\nUsing (r + 1)!/r! = r + 1 and (49 − r)!/(50 − r)! = 1/(50 − r):\nT_r/T_(r+1) = (r + 1)/(50 − r) × 1/0.15 × 0.85\n= 0.85(r + 1)/(0.15(50 − r))\n= 85(r + 1)/(15(50 − r))\n= 17(r + 1)/(3(50 − r))",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::13(iii)(a)": "X ~ B(50, 0.15), so P(X = r) = T_r and P(X = r + 1) = T_(r+1).\nP(X = r) ≤ P(X = r + 1) ⇔ T_r/T_(r+1) ≤ 1 (both terms are positive for 0 ≤ r ≤ 49)\n17(r + 1)/(3(50 − r)) ≤ 1\nSince 50 − r > 0, multiply through by 3(50 − r):\n17(r + 1) ≤ 3(50 − r)\n17r + 17 ≤ 150 − 3r\n20r ≤ 133\nr ≤ 6.65\nr is an integer (r ≥ 0), so P(X = r) ≤ P(X = r + 1) for r = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, i.e. r ≤ 6.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p2::13(iii)(b)": "From (a), P(X = r) ≤ P(X = r + 1) for r ≤ 6, so the probabilities increase up to P(X = 7):\nP(X = 0) < P(X = 1) < … < P(X = 6) < P(X = 7)\n(For r = 6: P(X = 6)/P(X = 7) = 17 × 7/(3 × 44) = 119/132 = 0.902 < 1, so P(X = 7) is bigger than P(X = 6).)\nFor r ≥ 7, the ratio is greater than 1, so P(X = r) > P(X = r + 1) and the probabilities decrease:\nP(X = 7) > P(X = 8) > P(X = 9) > …\n(Check: P(X = 6) = 0.142, P(X = 7) = 0.157, P(X = 8) = 0.149.)\nSo the largest probability is P(X = 7).\nThe most likely number of days on which the train is late is 7.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::1(i)": "Complete the square in x and in y:\nx² + 8x = (x + 4)² − 16\ny² − 2y = (y − 1)² − 1\nSo x² + y² + 8x − 2y − 7 = 0 becomes\n(x + 4)² − 16 + (y − 1)² − 1 − 7 = 0\n(x + 4)² + (y − 1)² = 24\nComparing with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², the centre is C(−4, 1).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::1(ii)": "From (i), (x + 4)² + (y − 1)² = 24, so r² = 24.\nRadius = √24 = 2√6",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::2": "Both sides are non-negative, so square both sides:\n(2x − 1)² = (x + 3)²\n4x² − 4x + 1 = x² + 6x + 9\n3x² − 10x − 8 = 0\n(3x + 2)(x − 4) = 0\nx = 4 or x = −2/3\nCheck: x = 4 gives |7| = |7| ✓; x = −2/3 gives |−7/3| = |7/3| ✓.\nSo x = 4 or x = −2/3.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::3": "Width = x m, so length = (x + 3) m.\n\nLength condition:\nx + 3 ≥ 14.5\nx ≥ 11.5\n\nArea condition:\nx(x + 3) < 180\nx² + 3x − 180 < 0\n(x − 12)(x + 15) < 0\nThe quadratic is a positive (u-shaped) parabola with roots −15 and 12, so it is negative between the roots:\n−15 < x < 12\n\nBoth conditions must hold:\nx ≥ 11.5 and −15 < x < 12\nSo the set of possible widths is 11.5 ≤ x < 12 (in metres).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::4(i)(a)": "fg(x) = f(g(x)) = f(x² + 2) = (x² + 2)³",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::4(i)(b)": "gf(x) = g(f(x)) = g(x³) = (x³)² + 2 = x⁶ + 2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::4(ii)": "Expand (x² + 2)³ using the binomial expansion:\n(x² + 2)³ = (x²)³ + 3(x²)²(2) + 3(x²)(2)² + 2³\n= x⁶ + 6x⁴ + 12x² + 8\nSo fg(x) = x⁶ + 6x⁴ + 12x² + 8.\n\nfg(x) − gf(x) = 24:\n(x⁶ + 6x⁴ + 12x² + 8) − (x⁶ + 2) = 24\n6x⁴ + 12x² + 6 = 24\n6x⁴ + 12x² − 18 = 0\nx⁴ + 2x² − 3 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic in x²:\n(x² − 1)(x² + 3) = 0\nx² + 3 = 0 gives x² = −3, which has no real solutions.\nx² − 1 = 0 gives x² = 1, so x = ±1.\n\nSo x = 1 or x = −1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::5(i)": "Two strips of equal width from 0 to 4: h = (4 − 0)/2 = 2.\nOrdinates of y = 1/(2 + √x):\nx = 0: y₀ = 1/(2 + 0) = ½\nx = 2: y₁ = 1/(2 + √2)\nx = 4: y₂ = 1/(2 + 2) = ¼\n\nTrapezium rule:\n∫₀⁴ 1/(2 + √x) dx ≈ (h/2)[y₀ + y₂ + 2y₁]\n= (2/2)[½ + ¼ + 2/(2 + √2)]\n= 3/4 + 2/(2 + √2)\n\nRationalise the denominator:\n1/(2 + √2) = (2 − √2)/((2 + √2)(2 − √2)) = (2 − √2)/(4 − 2) = (2 − √2)/2\nSo 2/(2 + √2) = 2 − √2.\n\nTherefore\n∫₀⁴ 1/(2 + √x) dx ≈ 3/4 + 2 − √2 = 11/4 − √2, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::5(ii)": "Let x = u², so dx = 2u du and √x = u (taking u ≥ 0).\nLimits: x = 0 ⇒ u = 0; x = 4 ⇒ u = 2.\n\n∫₀⁴ 1/(2 + √x) dx = ∫₀² 2u/(2 + u) du\n\nWrite the integrand in a form that can be integrated:\n2u/(2 + u) = 2(2 + u − 2)/(2 + u) = 2(1 − 2/(2 + u))\n\nSo the integral is\n2∫₀² (1 − 2/(2 + u)) du\n= 2[u − 2ln(2 + u)]₀²\n= 2{(2 − 2ln4) − (0 − 2ln2)}\n= 2(2 − 4ln2 + 2ln2)      (since ln4 = 2ln2)\n= 2(2 − 2ln2)\n= 4 − 4ln2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::5(iii)": "From (i) and (ii), the trapezium-rule estimate is approximately equal to the exact value:\n11/4 − √2 ≈ 4 − 4ln2\n4ln2 ≈ 4 − 11/4 + √2\n4ln2 ≈ 5/4 + √2\nln2 ≈ 5/16 + √2/4\n\nSo k = 5/16.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::6(i)": "Expand both sides using the compound-angle formulae, with sin(π/4) = cos(π/4) = √2/2:\nsin(2θ + π/4) = sin2θ cos(π/4) + cos2θ sin(π/4) = (√2/2)(sin2θ + cos2θ)\ncos(2θ + π/4) = cos2θ cos(π/4) − sin2θ sin(π/4) = (√2/2)(cos2θ − sin2θ)\n\nSo the equation becomes\n(√2/2)(sin2θ + cos2θ) = 3(√2/2)(cos2θ − sin2θ)\nsin2θ + cos2θ = 3cos2θ − 3sin2θ\n4sin2θ = 2cos2θ\nDivide by 2cos2θ (cos2θ ≠ 0, since otherwise sin2θ = 0 as well, which is impossible):\n2(sin2θ/cos2θ) = 1\ntan2θ = ½, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::6(ii)": "Use the double-angle formula for tan:\ntan2θ = 2tanθ/(1 − tan²θ) = ½\n4tanθ = 1 − tan²θ\ntan²θ + 4tanθ − 1 = 0\n\nSolve with the quadratic formula:\ntanθ = (−4 ± √(16 + 4))/2 = (−4 ± √20)/2 = −2 ± √5\n\nθ is obtuse (90° < θ < 180°), so tanθ < 0.\n−2 + √5 > 0 (since √5 > 2), so tanθ = −2 + √5 would give an acute angle: reject it.\n−2 − √5 < 0, which is consistent with θ obtuse.\n\nTherefore tanθ = −2 − √5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::7": "(2x − 1)³ dy/dx + 4y² = 0\n(2x − 1)³ dy/dx = −4y²\n\nSeparate the variables:\n−¼ ∫ 1/y² dy = ∫ 1/(2x − 1)³ dx\n\nIntegrate each side:\n∫ y⁻² dy = −1/y, so the left side is −¼(−1/y) = 1/(4y)\n∫ (2x − 1)⁻³ dx = (2x − 1)⁻²/((2)(−2)) = −1/(4(2x − 1)²)\n\nSo\n1/(4y) = −1/(4(2x − 1)²) + c\n\nUse the point (1, 1): x = 1, y = 1, 2x − 1 = 1:\n¼ = −¼ + c ⇒ c = ½\n\nMultiply through by 4:\n1/y = −1/(2x − 1)² + 2\n\nCombine into a single fraction:\n1/y = (2(2x − 1)² − 1)/(2x − 1)²\n\nTake the reciprocal:\ny = (2x − 1)²/(2(2x − 1)² − 1)\n\nExpand: (2x − 1)² = 4x² − 4x + 1, and 2(4x² − 4x + 1) − 1 = 8x² − 8x + 1.\nSo\nf(x) = (4x² − 4x + 1)/(8x² − 8x + 1)\nwhich is of the required form with a = 4 and b = 8.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::8(i)": "Weight of the particle = 5g acting vertically downwards = 5(0, −9.8) = (0, −49) N.\n\nNewton's second law, F = ma, with m = 5:\n(15, −8) + (−7, −2) + (0, −49) = 5a\n(8, −59) = 5a\na = (1.6, −11.8) m s⁻²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::8(ii)": "The particle starts from rest, so u = 0, and a = (1.6, −11.8) is constant.\nDisplacement after t = 10 s:\ns = ut + ½at² = ½(1.6, −11.8)(10)² = 50(1.6, −11.8) = (80, −590) m\n\nPosition vector = initial position + displacement\n= (2, 45) + (80, −590)\n= (82, −545) m",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::9(i)": "The plank is in equilibrium, so resolving vertically:\nR_C + R_D = 100\n75 + R_D = 100\nR_D = 25 N",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::9(ii)": "The plank is uniform, so its weight of 100 N acts at the midpoint, 2 m from A.\nC is x m from A and D is (x + 0.5) m from A.\n\nTake moments about A (clockwise moment of weight = anticlockwise moments of reactions):\n100 × 2 = 75 × x + 25 × (x + 0.5)\n200 = 75x + 25x + 12.5\n100x = 187.5\nx = 1.875",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::9(iii)": "When the plank is on the point of tilting about D, the reaction at C is zero.\nLet the weight of the stone block be W N, acting at B.\n\nDistances from D (AD = x + 0.5 = 1.875 + 0.5 = 2.375 m):\nPlank's weight at the midpoint (2 m from A): 2.375 − 2 = 0.375 m on the A side of D.\nBlock at B (4 m from A): 4 − 2.375 = 1.625 m on the B side of D.\n\nMoments about D:\n100 × (x + 0.5 − 2) = W × (4 − 0.5 − x)\n100 × 0.375 = W × 1.625\nW = 37.5/1.625 = 23.076…\nWeight of the stone block = 23.1 N (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::9(iv)(a)": "Modelling the stone block as a particle assumes that its weight acts exactly at the single point B. In reality the block has size, so its dimensions (and how its mass is distributed) are ignored, and its weight would actually act at its centre of mass, which may not be exactly at B.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::9(iv)(b)": "Modelling the plank as a rigid rod assumes that the plank remains perfectly straight and does not bend (or flex) under the loads on it, which a real plank may do, especially with the block on the end.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::10(i)": "The forces are in equilibrium, so the horizontal components balance.\n6 N force: horizontal component 6sinθ to the right.\n4 N force: horizontal component 4sin2θ to the left.\nP N force: vertical, so no horizontal component.\n\nResolving horizontally:\n4sin2θ = 6sinθ\nUsing sin2θ = 2sinθcosθ:\n8sinθcosθ = 6sinθ\nsinθ ≠ 0 (θ is not zero), so divide by sinθ:\n8cosθ = 6\ncosθ = 3/4\nθ = cos⁻¹(3/4) = 41.409…° = 41.4° to 3 s.f., as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::10(ii)": "Resolving vertically (upward components balance the downward force P):\nP = 4cos2θ + 6cosθ\n\nWith cosθ = 3/4: cos2θ = 2cos²θ − 1 = 2(9/16) − 1 = 1/8.\nP = 4(1/8) + 6(3/4) = 0.5 + 4.5 = 5\n\nSo P = 5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::10(iii)(a)": "The remaining forces are 3 N at θ to the upward vertical (to the right) and P = 5 N vertically downwards, with cosθ = 3/4, θ = 41.4°.\n\nComponents of the resultant:\nHorizontal (to the right): 3sinθ = 3sin41.4° = 1.984…\nVertical (downwards): P − 3cosθ = 5 − 3(3/4) = 2.75\n\nMagnitude = √((3sinθ)² + (P − 3cosθ)²)\n= √(1.984…² + 2.75²)\n= √11.5\n= 3.391…\nMagnitude of the resultant = 3.39 N (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::10(iii)(b)": "The resultant has a horizontal component 3sinθ = 1.984… N to the right and a vertical component P − 3cosθ = 2.75 N downwards.\n\nLet α be the angle the resultant makes below the horizontal:\ntanα = (P − 3cosθ)/(3sinθ) = 2.75/1.984…\nα = 54.186…° = 54.2°\n\nThe resultant acts at 54.2° below the horizontal (to the right, on the same side as the 3 N force), i.e. at 35.8° to the downward vertical.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::11(i)": "v = kt + 0.03t²\nAcceleration a = dv/dt = k + 0.06t\n\nWhen t = 20, a = 1.3:\nk + 0.06(20) = 1.3\nk + 1.2 = 1.3\nk = 0.1",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::11(ii)": "First 20 s: v = 0.1t + 0.03t².\nIntegrate to find displacement:\ns = ∫(0.1t + 0.03t²) dt = 0.05t² + 0.01t³ + c\nThe car starts its journey at t = 0, so s = 0 when t = 0, giving c = 0.\ns = 0.05t² + 0.01t³\n\nDistance in the first 20 s:\ns₁ = 0.05(20)² + 0.01(20)³ = 20 + 80 = 100 m\n\nSpeed at t = 20:\nv = 0.1(20) + 0.03(20)² = 2 + 12 = 14 m s⁻¹\n\nAfter t = 20: constant acceleration 1.3 m s⁻², from u = 14 to v = 25.\nUsing v² = u² + 2as:\n25² = 14² + 2(1.3)s₂\n625 = 196 + 2.6s₂\ns₂ = 429/2.6 = 165 m\n\nTotal distance = s₁ + s₂ = 100 + 165 = 265 m",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::12(i)(a)": "λ = ¼, so B has mass ¼m and weight ¼mg.\nA is on the point of moving down the plane, so the system is in limiting equilibrium, friction on A acts up the plane and F = μR.\n\nB (in equilibrium): T = ¼mg\n\nA, resolving perpendicular to the plane:\nR = mg cos30° = mg√3/2\n\nA, resolving parallel to the plane (up the plane positive):\nT + F − mg sin30° = 0\n\nLimiting friction: F = μR = μ(mg√3/2)\n\nSubstitute:\n¼mg + μ(mg√3/2) − ½mg = 0\nμ(√3/2) = ¼\nμ = 1/(2√3) = √3/6",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::12(i)(b)": "B is in equilibrium, so T = λmg.\nA is on the point of moving down the plane, so friction acts up the plane. Resolving parallel to the plane for A:\nT + F − mg sin30° = 0\nF = mg sin30° − λmg = mg(½ − λ)\n\nThe plane is rough and A is about to slide down, so the friction acting up the plane is positive: F > 0.\nmg(½ − λ) > 0\nλ < ½, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2018-p3::12(ii)": "λ = 2, so B has mass 2m. B accelerates downwards and A accelerates up the plane, each with acceleration ¼g. Friction on A now acts down the plane, and since A is moving, F = μR with R = mg cos30°.\n\nNewton's second law for B (downwards positive):\n2mg − T = 2m(¼g) ⇒ T = 2mg − ½mg = (3/2)mg\n\nNewton's second law for A (up the plane positive):\nT − F − mg sin30° = m(¼g)\n\nEliminate T by adding the two equations:\n2mg − F − mg sin30° = ¾mg\nF = 2mg − ½mg − ¾mg = ¾mg\n\nUse F = μR = μ mg cos30° = μ mg(√3/2):\nμ(√3/2) mg = ¾mg\nμ = (3/4) × (2/√3) = 3/(2√3) = √3/2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::1": "Factorise the quadratic:\n10x² + x − 2 = (5x − 2)(2x + 1)\nSo the inequality is (5x − 2)(2x + 1) > 0.\n\nCritical values: (5x − 2)(2x + 1) = 0 ⇒ x = ⅖ or x = −½\n\nThe graph of y = 10x² + x − 2 is a ∪-shaped parabola (positive x² coefficient) crossing the x-axis at x = −½ and x = ⅖, so it lies above the x-axis outside the roots.\n\nCheck: x = 0 gives −2 < 0, so the region between the roots is not included.\n\nSolution: x < −½ or x > ⅖\n(in set notation: {x : x < −½} ∪ {x : x > ⅖})",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::2(a)(i)": "The direction 240° is measured anticlockwise from the positive x-axis. Since 180° < 240° < 270°, A lies in the third quadrant.\n\nSketch description: draw axes with origin O. Draw a line of length 8 from O into the third quadrant (below the negative x-axis), making an angle of 60° below the negative x-axis (equivalently 240° anticlockwise from the positive x-axis, or 30° to the negative y-axis). Mark its end as A, label the length 8 and mark the angle (e.g. 240° from the positive x-axis, or 60° below the negative x-axis).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::2(a)(ii)": "x-component: 8cos240° = 8 × (−½) = −4\ny-component: 8sin240° = 8 × (−√3/2) = −4√3\n\nPosition vector of A:\nOA = −4i − 4√3 j",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::2(b)": "OB = 6i lies along the positive x-axis, and OA is at 240°, so the angle between OA and OB (the angle AOB inside the triangle) is\n360° − 240° = 120°\n\nArea of triangle AOB = ½ × |OA| × |OB| × sin AOB\n= ½ × 8 × 6 × sin120°\n= 24 × (√3/2)\n= 12√3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::2(c)": "In parallelogram OABC (vertices in order), OC is parallel and equal to AB:\nOC = AB = OB − OA\n\nOC = 6i − (−4i − 4√3 j)\n= 6i + 4i + 4√3 j\n= 10i + 4√3 j",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::3(a)": "f(x) = (x − 3)² − 17 has a minimum turning point at (3, −17).\nFor f⁻¹ to exist, f must be one-one, so the domain must lie entirely on one side of the vertex. With domain x ≥ k, this needs k ≥ 3.\n\nLeast possible value: k = 3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::3(b)": "Using k = 3, the domain of f is x ≥ 3.\n\nf(5) = (5 − 3)² − 17 = 4 − 17 = −13\n\nff(5) = f(−13), but −13 is not in the domain of f (since −13 < 3).\n\nSo ff(5) is not defined (it cannot be evaluated).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::3(c)": "(x − 3)² − 17 = x\nx² − 6x + 9 − 17 = x\nx² − 7x − 8 = 0\n(x − 8)(x + 1) = 0\nx = 8 or x = −1\n\nThe domain is x ≥ 3, so x = −1 is not valid (it is not in the domain).\n\nSolution: x = 8",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::3(d)": "The graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of the graph of y = f(x) in the line y = x. So the two graphs meet on the line y = x, where f(x) = x. Hence the solution of f(x) = x, namely x = 8, is also the solution of f(x) = f⁻¹(x).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::4(a)": "The salaries form an arithmetic progression with\na = 16000, d = 17200 − 16000 = 1200\n\nu₁₀ = a + 9d = 16000 + 9 × 1200 = 16000 + 10800\n\nSalary in the tenth year = £26 800",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::4(b)": "Total salary after N years:\nS_N = ½N(2a + (N − 1)d) = ½N(32000 + (N − 1)1200)\n= ½N(30800 + 1200N)\n= 600N² + 15400N\n\nSet S_N > 500000:\n600N² + 15400N − 500000 > 0\n(÷200) 3N² + 77N − 2500 > 0\n\nSolve 3N² + 77N − 2500 = 0:\nN = (−77 ± √(77² + 4 × 3 × 2500))/6 = (−77 ± √35929)/6\nN = 18.758… or N = −44.42…\n\nN must be positive, so N > 18.76 (to 4 s.f.).\nRounding up, the number of complete years needed is 19 years.\n\nCheck: S₁₈ = 600 × 324 + 15400 × 18 = £471 600 < £500 000\nS₁₉ = 600 × 361 + 15400 × 19 = £509 200 > £500 000",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::4(c)": "The model is unlikely to be realistic in the long term, because the salary is unlikely to keep rising by the same fixed amount (£1200) every year: there is likely to be an upper limit (pay cap) on the salary for the role, so the salary would level off, or rises are more likely to be percentage increases rather than a constant amount.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::5(a)": "Differentiate x³ − 3x²y + y² + 1 = 0 implicitly with respect to x (using the product rule on 3x²y):\n\n3x² − (6xy + 3x² dy/dx) + 2y dy/dx = 0\n3x² − 6xy − 3x² dy/dx + 2y dy/dx = 0\n\nCollect the dy/dx terms:\n(2y − 3x²) dy/dx = 6xy − 3x²\n\ndy/dx = (6xy − 3x²)/(2y − 3x²)  as required",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::5(b)": "At (1, 2):\ndy/dx = (6 × 1 × 2 − 3 × 1²)/(2 × 2 − 3 × 1²) = (12 − 3)/(4 − 3) = 9\n\nGradient of the normal = −1/9\n\nEquation of the normal:\ny − 2 = −(1/9)(x − 1)\n9y − 18 = −x + 1\n\nx + 9y = 19",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::6": "(x + h)³ = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³\n\nf(x + h) − f(x) = [2(x + h)³ + 3(x + h)] − [2x³ + 3x]\n= 2x³ + 6x²h + 6xh² + 2h³ + 3x + 3h − 2x³ − 3x\n= 6x²h + 6xh² + 2h³ + 3h\n\n(f(x + h) − f(x))/h = (6x²h + 6xh² + 2h³ + 3h)/h\n= 6x² + 6xh + 2h² + 3\n\nf′(x) = lim(h→0) (f(x + h) − f(x))/h\n= lim(h→0) (6x² + 6xh + 2h² + 3)\n= 6x² + 3\n\nSo f′(x) = 6x² + 3, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::7": "u₁ = 25 × 0.6 = 15, and each term is 0.6 times the previous one, so the sequence is a geometric progression with first term a = 15 and common ratio r = 0.6.\n\nSince |r| < 1, the sum to infinity exists:\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = 15/(1 − 0.6) = 15/0.4 = 37.5\n\nSum of the first N terms:\nS_N = a(1 − rᴺ)/(1 − r) = 15(1 − 0.6ᴺ)/(1 − 0.6) = 37.5(1 − 0.6ᴺ)\n\nRequire S∞ − S_N < 10⁻⁴:\n37.5 − 37.5(1 − 0.6ᴺ) < 10⁻⁴\n37.5 × 0.6ᴺ < 10⁻⁴\n0.6ᴺ < 10⁻⁴/37.5 = 2.666… × 10⁻⁶\n\nTake logs of both sides:\nN ln0.6 < ln(2.666… × 10⁻⁶)\nSince ln0.6 < 0, dividing by ln0.6 reverses the inequality:\nN > ln(2.666… × 10⁻⁶)/ln0.6 = log₀.₆(2.666… × 10⁻⁶)\nN > 25.125…\n\nN is an integer, so the smallest value is N = 26.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::8(a)": "The rate of change of height is proportional to √x:\ndx/dt = k√x\n\nWhen x = 0.64 the height is decreasing at 0.0032 m s⁻¹, so dx/dt = −0.0032:\n−0.0032 = k√0.64 = 0.8k\nk = −0.0032/0.8 = −0.004\n\nHence dx/dt = −0.004√x, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::8(b)": "Separate the variables:\n∫ x^(−½) dx = ∫ −0.004 dt\n2x^(½) = −0.004t + c\n\nWhen t = 100, x = 0.64:\n2√0.64 = −0.004 × 100 + c\n1.6 = −0.4 + c\nc = 2\n\nSo 2√x = 2 − 0.004t\n√x = 1 − 0.002t\nx = (1 − 0.002t)²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::8(c)": "The tank is empty when x = 0:\n(1 − 0.002t)² = 0\n1 − 0.002t = 0\nt = 1/0.002 = 500\n\nAccording to the model, the tank is empty after 500 seconds (8 minutes 20 seconds).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::9(a)": "R cos(3x − α) = R cos3x cosα + R sin3x sinα\n\nCompare with 3cos3x + 7sin3x:\nR cosα = 3, R sinα = 7\n\nR² = 3² + 7² = 9 + 49 = 58, so R = √58 (≈ 7.62)\ntanα = 7/3, so α = 1.1659… ≈ 1.17 radians (which lies in 0 < α < ½π)\n\n3cos3x + 7sin3x = √58 cos(3x − 1.17)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::9(b)": "Using 3cos3x + 7sin3x = √58 cos(3x − 1.17):\n\n1. Translation by 1.17 units in the positive x-direction (vector (1.17, 0)): y = cos x becomes y = cos(x − 1.17).\n2. Stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor ⅓: y = cos(x − 1.17) becomes y = cos(3x − 1.17).\n3. Stretch parallel to the y-axis, scale factor √58: y = cos(3x − 1.17) becomes y = √58 cos(3x − 1.17) = 3cos3x + 7sin3x.\n\n(The y-direction stretch may be done at any point in the sequence, but the translation by 1.17 must come before the x-direction stretch.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::9(c)": "√58 cos(3x − 1.17) is greatest when cos(3x − 1.17) = 1.\n\nGreatest value = √58 (≈ 7.62)\n\nThis first occurs (for positive x) when\n3x − 1.1659… = 0\nx = 1.1659…/3 = 0.389 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::9(d)": "√58 cos(3x − 1.17) is least when cos(3x − 1.17) = −1.\n\nLeast value = −√58 (≈ −7.62)\n\nThis first occurs (for positive x) when\n3x − 1.1659… = π\nx = (π + 1.1659…)/3 = 1.44 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::10(a)": "Area of sector AOB = ½ × 6² × θ = 18θ\nArea of triangle AOB = ½ × 6 × 6 × sinθ = 18sinθ\n\nArea of segment = sector − triangle:\n½ × 6² × (θ − sinθ) = 7.2\n18(θ − sinθ) = 7.2\nθ − sinθ = 7.2/18 = 0.4\n\nθ = 0.4 + sinθ, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::10(b)": "F(θ) = 0.4 + sinθ, so F′(θ) = cosθ.\n\nF′(1.2) = cos1.2 = 0.362 (3 s.f.)\n\nSince |F′(1.2)| < 1 (that is, −1 < F′(1.2) < 1), and 1.2 is close enough to the root, the iteration θₙ₊₁ = F(θₙ) will converge to the root.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::10(c)": "θ₁ = 1.2\nθ₂ = 0.4 + sin1.2 = 1.3320\nθ₃ = 0.4 + sin1.3320 = 1.3716\nθ₄ = 1.3802\nθ₅ = 1.3819\nθ₆ = 1.3822\nθ₇ = 1.3823\n\nThe iterates have settled at 1.382 to 4 significant figures, so\nθ = 1.382 (4 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::10(d)": "Let g(θ) = θ − 0.4 − sinθ, which is continuous. The root of g(θ) = 0 is the solution of θ = 0.4 + sinθ.\n\nUse the bounds of 1.382 to 4 s.f.:\ng(1.3815) = 1.3815 − 0.4 − sin1.3815 = −0.000637 (3 s.f.) < 0\ng(1.3825) = 1.3825 − 0.4 − sin1.3825 = 0.000175 (3 s.f.) > 0\n\nThere is a change of sign, so the root lies in 1.3815 < θ < 1.3825.\nHence θ = 1.382 is correct to 4 significant figures.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::11(a)": "Write ∫ln(x − 4) dx = ∫1 × ln(x − 4) dx and integrate by parts with\nu = ln(x − 4) ⇒ du/dx = 1/(x − 4)\ndv/dx = 1 ⇒ v = x\n\n∫ln(x − 4) dx = x ln|x − 4| − ∫ x/(x − 4) dx\n\nDeal with the improper fraction:\nx/(x − 4) = (x − 4 + 4)/(x − 4) = 1 + 4/(x − 4)\n\n∫ x/(x − 4) dx = ∫ (1 + 4/(x − 4)) dx = x + 4ln|x − 4|\n\nSo\n∫ln(x − 4) dx = x ln|x − 4| − x − 4ln|x − 4| + c\n= (x − 4)ln|x − 4| − x + c, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::11(b)": "ln(x − 4) is defined only for x > 4, and ln(x − 4) → −∞ as x → 4⁺.\n\nVertical asymptote: x = 4",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::11(c)": "The curve crosses the x-axis where ln(x − 4) = 0, i.e. x = 5. For 4.5 ≤ x < 5 the curve is below the x-axis, and for 5 < x ≤ 7 it is above, so the two parts must be found separately and their magnitudes added.\n\nLet G(x) = (x − 4)ln|x − 4| − x (from part (a)).\n\nFrom x = 5 to x = 7:\n∫₅⁷ ln(x − 4) dx = G(7) − G(5) = (3ln3 − 7) − (1 × ln1 − 5) = 3ln3 − 2\n\nFrom x = 4.5 to x = 5:\n∫₄.₅⁵ ln(x − 4) dx = G(5) − G(4.5) = (−5) − (½ln½ − 4.5) = −½ − ½ln½\nThis is negative (≈ −0.153), so the area of this part is ½ln½ + ½.\n\nTotal area = (3ln3 − 2) + (½ln½ + ½)\nUsing ln½ = −ln2:\n= 3ln3 − 2 − ½ln2 + ½\n= 3ln3 − ½ln2 − 3/2\n\n(a = 3, b = −½, c = −3/2; area ≈ 1.45)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::12(a)": "Let u = 3x², so y = aᵘ.\n\ndu/dx = 6x\ndy/du = aᵘ ln a\n\nBy the chain rule:\ndy/dx = (dy/du) × (du/dx) = aᵘ ln a × 6x = 6x aᵘ ln a\n\nSubstituting u = 3x²:\ndy/dx = 6x a^(3x²) ln a, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::12(b)": "At x = 1, gradient of the tangent:\nm = 6 × 1 × a³ × ln a = 6a³ ln a\n\nTangent at (1, a³):\ny − a³ = 6a³ ln a (x − 1)\n\nIt passes through (½, 0):\n0 − a³ = 6a³ ln a (½ − 1)\n−a³ = −3a³ ln a\na³ = 3a³ ln a\na³(3ln a − 1) = 0\n\na³ ≠ 0 (since a > 1), so\n3ln a − 1 = 0\nln a = ⅓\na = e^(1/3)  (= ∛e)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p1::12(c)": "dy/dx = (6x ln a) × a^(3x²)\n\nUse the product rule, with p = 6x ln a and q = a^(3x²):\ndp/dx = 6ln a\ndq/dx = 6x a^(3x²) ln a (from part (a))\n\nd²y/dx² = (6ln a)(a^(3x²)) + (6x ln a)(6x a^(3x²) ln a)\n= 6ln a · a^(3x²) + 36x²(ln a)² a^(3x²)\n= a^(3x²)(6ln a)(1 + 6x² ln a)\n\nConsider the sign of each factor:\n• a > 1, so ln a > 0, hence 6ln a > 0\n• a^(3x²) > 0 for all x (a positive number raised to any power is positive)\n• x² ≥ 0 and ln a > 0, so 6x² ln a ≥ 0 and therefore 1 + 6x² ln a ≥ 1 > 0\n\nSo d²y/dx² is a product of three positive factors:\nd²y/dx² > 0 for all x\n\nHence the curve is convex for all values of x.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::1(a)(i)": "Use the quotient rule with u = x² and v = 2x + 1.\ndu/dx = 2x, dv/dx = 2\n\ndy/dx = (v·du/dx − u·dv/dx)/v²\n= ((2x + 1)·2x − x²·2)/(2x + 1)²\n= (4x² + 2x − 2x²)/(2x + 1)²\n= (2x² + 2x)/(2x + 1)²\n= 2x(x + 1)/(2x + 1)²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::1(a)(ii)": "Use the chain rule: d/dx[tan(f(x))] = f′(x)sec²(f(x)).\n\nHere f(x) = x² − 3x, so f′(x) = 2x − 3.\n\nd/dx[tan(x² − 3x)] = (2x − 3)sec²(x² − 3x)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::1(b)": "Let u = √x − 1, so √x = u + 1 and x = (u + 1)².\nThen dx/du = 2(u + 1), so dx = 2(u + 1) du.\n\n∫ 1/(√x − 1) dx = ∫ (1/u) × 2(u + 1) du\n= 2∫ (u + 1)/u du\n= 2∫ (1 + 1/u) du\n= 2(u + ln|u|) + c\n\nSubstitute back u = √x − 1:\n∫ 1/(√x − 1) dx = 2(√x − 1 + ln|√x − 1|) + c\n\n(Since the −2 can be absorbed into the constant, this can also be written 2√x + 2ln|√x − 1| + c.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::1(c)": "The derivative of the denominator is\nd/dx(2x² − 8x − 1) = 4x − 8 = 4(x − 2),\nso the numerator x − 2 is ¼ of the derivative of the denominator.\n\n∫ (x − 2)/(2x² − 8x − 1) dx = ¼ ∫ (4x − 8)/(2x² − 8x − 1) dx\n= ¼ ln|2x² − 8x − 1| + c\n(using ∫ f′(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::2(a)": "The general term of (3 − 2x)⁸ is ⁸Cᵣ 3⁸⁻ʳ(−2x)ʳ.\n\nFor x⁵ take r = 5:\n⁸C₅ × 3³ × (−2)⁵ x⁵ = 56 × 27 × (−32) x⁵ = −48384x⁵\n\nCoefficient of x⁵ = −48384",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::2(b)(i)": "Use (1 + y)ⁿ = 1 + ny + n(n − 1)/2! y² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! y³ + … with n = 0.5 and y = 3x.\n\n(1 + 3x)^0.5 = 1 + 0.5(3x) + (0.5)(−0.5)/2 × (3x)² + (0.5)(−0.5)(−1.5)/6 × (3x)³ + …\n= 1 + (3/2)x + (−0.125)(9x²) + (0.0625)(27x³) + …\n= 1 + (3/2)x − (9/8)x² + (27/16)x³ + …\n\n(or 1 + 1.5x − 1.125x² + 1.6875x³)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::2(b)(ii)": "The expansion of (1 + y)^0.5 is valid for |y| < 1.\nHere y = 3x, so |3x| < 1, i.e. |x| < ⅓.\n\nValid for −⅓ < x < ⅓",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::2(b)(iii)": "Write √103 in terms of (1 + 3x)^0.5:\n√103 = √(100 × 1.03) = 10√1.03, and 1 + 3x = 1.03 when x = 0.01 (which is inside the range of validity).\n\nSubstitute x = 0.01 into the expansion:\n√1.03 ≈ 1 + 1.5(0.01) − 1.125(0.01)² + 1.6875(0.01)³\n= 1 + 0.015 − 0.0001125 + 0.0000016875\n= 1.0148891875…\n\nSo from the series: √103 ≈ 10 × 1.0148891875 = 10.148891875… = 10.14889 (5 d.p.)\n\nFrom the calculator: √103 = 10.1488915… = 10.14889 (5 d.p.)\n\nThe two values agree to five decimal places, so the check is consistent with the expansion being correct.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::3(a)(i)": "From the parametric equations:\nx − 3 = 2cos θ and y + 4 = 2sin θ\n\nSquare and add:\n(x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 4cos²θ + 4sin²θ = 4(cos²θ + sin²θ) = 4\n\nCartesian equation: (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 4",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::3(a)(ii)": "Comparing (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 4 with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²:\n\nCentre (3, −4), radius √4 = 2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::3(b)": "x = 4cos t ⇒ dx/dt = −4sin t\ny = 2sin t ⇒ dy/dt = 2cos t\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = 2cos t/(−4sin t) = −½ cot t\n\nAt t = π/6: cot(π/6) = √3, so the gradient of L is\ndy/dx = −½ × √3 = −√3/2\n\nThe point on S at t = π/6:\nx = 4cos(π/6) = 4 × √3/2 = 2√3\ny = 2sin(π/6) = 2 × ½ = 1\nso the point is (2√3, 1).\n\nEquation of L:\ny − 1 = −(√3/2)(x − 2√3)\ny − 1 = −(√3/2)x + 3\ny = −(√3/2)x + 4\n\nL cuts the x-axis where y = 0:\n0 = −(√3/2)x + 4\n(√3/2)x = 4\nx = 8/√3 = 8√3/3\n\nL cuts the x-axis at (8√3/3, 0), i.e. approximately (4.62, 0).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::4(a)": "Model A: the growth rate starts small, increases (the curve gets steeper) and then decreases again as the curve levels off near t = 20. The population grows slowly, then quickly, then slowly.\n\nModel B: the growth rate is greatest at the start and decreases throughout the 20 years. The population grows quickly at first and then more and more slowly, levelling off.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::4(b)(i)": "For model A, P = 20 + 1000e^(−(t − 20)²/100).\nWhen t > 20, (t − 20)² increases as t increases, so e^(−(t − 20)²/100) decreases towards 0.\n\nSo model A predicts that, after t = 20 (when P reaches its peak of 1020), the population decreases and tends towards 20 (never falling below 20).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::4(b)(ii)": "For model B, P = 20 + 1000(1 − e^(−t/5)).\nAs t increases, e^(−t/5) decreases towards 0, so P keeps increasing (very slowly) towards 20 + 1000 = 1020.\n\nSo model B predicts that the population levels off, tending to 1020 and never exceeding 1020.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::4(c)(i)": "Model A has the population falling back towards 20 in the long term. Since population depends only on food, this suggests the food supply will (almost) run out: in the long term there will only be enough food to support a population of about 20.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::4(c)(ii)": "Model B has the population levelling off at about 1020 and staying there. This suggests the food supply will not run out but is limited: it is just sufficient to sustain a stable population of about 1020, and cannot support any further increase in P.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::5(a)": "The container has no lid, so the material used is the curved surface only:\nπrl = 4π ⇒ l = 4/r\n\nBy Pythagoras in the cone, h² + r² = l², so\nh² = l² − r² = 16/r² − r²\nh = √(16/r² − r²) = √(16 − r⁴)/r\n\nSubstitute into V = ⅓πr²h:\nV = ⅓πr² √(16/r² − r²)\n= ⅓π √(r⁴(16/r² − r²))   (since r > 0, r² = √r⁴)\n= ⅓π √(16r² − r⁶)\n\nSo V = ⅓π√(16r² − r⁶), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::5(b)": "V = (π/3)(16r² − r⁶)^½\n\ndV/dr = (π/3) × ½(16r² − r⁶)^(−½) × (32r − 6r⁵)\n= π(32r − 6r⁵)/(6√(16r² − r⁶))\n\nAt a stationary point dV/dr = 0, so\n32r − 6r⁵ = 0\n2r(16 − 3r⁴) = 0\nr = 0 or r⁴ = 16/3\n\nr⁴ = 16/3 gives r = ±2/⁴√3.\nr is a radius, so r > 0: r = 0 (no container, V = 0) and the negative value r = −2/⁴√3 are rejected.\n\nSo r = 2/⁴√3 = 1.5197… (1.52 to 3 s.f.)\n\nCheck it is a maximum: 32r − 6r⁵ = 2r(16 − 3r⁴) is positive for r slightly less than 1.52 (e.g. r = 1.5 gives 2.44) and negative for r slightly more (e.g. r = 1.6 gives −11.7), so dV/dr changes from + to −: a maximum.\n\nAt this value r² = 4/√3 and r⁶ = 64/(3√3), so\n16r² − r⁶ = 64/√3 − 64/(3√3) = 128/(3√3) = 24.634…\n\nVmax = (π/3)√24.634… = (π/3) × 4.9633… = 5.1975…\n\nMaximum V = 5.20 m³ (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::6": "Let n be an even positive integer greater than 2. Then n = 2k where k is an integer with k > 1 (so k ≥ 2).\n\n2ⁿ − 1 = 2^(2k) − 1 = (2ᵏ)² − 1\n= (2ᵏ − 1)(2ᵏ + 1)   (difference of two squares)\n\nBoth factors are integers.\nSince k ≥ 2, 2ᵏ ≥ 4, so\n2ᵏ − 1 ≥ 3 > 1 and 2ᵏ + 1 ≥ 5 > 1.\n\nHence 2ⁿ − 1 is the product of two integers that are both greater than 1, so it has a factor other than 1 and itself. Therefore 2ⁿ − 1 is not prime, and Shona's claim is true.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::7": "With u = 6x² + x:\nu² = (6x² + x)² = 36x⁴ + 12x³ + x²\n\nSo 36x⁴ + 12x³ + 7x² + x − 2 = (36x⁴ + 12x³ + x²) + 6x² + x − 2\n= u² + (6x² + x) − 2\n= u² + u − 2\n\nThe equation becomes\nu² + u − 2 = 0\n(u + 2)(u − 1) = 0\nu = −2 or u = 1\n\nCase u = −2: 6x² + x = −2 ⇒ 6x² + x + 2 = 0.\nDiscriminant = 1² − 4 × 6 × 2 = 1 − 48 = −47 < 0, so there are no real roots.\n\nCase u = 1: 6x² + x = 1 ⇒ 6x² + x − 1 = 0\n(3x − 1)(2x + 1) = 0\nx = ⅓ or x = −½\n\nSolutions: x = ⅓ or x = −½",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::8(a)": "The 17 heights in order are:\n55, 57, 59, 59, 63, 64, 65, 65, 65, 69, 69, 74, 75, 77, 79, 79, 99\n\nMedian: the 9th value = 65 cm\n\nLower quartile: median of the lower 8 values (55, 57, 59, 59, 63, 64, 65, 65) = (59 + 63)/2 = 61\nUpper quartile: median of the upper 8 values (69, 69, 74, 75, 77, 79, 79, 99) = (75 + 77)/2 = 76\n\nInter-quartile range = 76 − 61 = 15 cm",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::8(b)": "n = 17\nΣx = 55 + 57 + 59 + 59 + 63 + 64 + 65 + 65 + 65 + 69 + 69 + 74 + 75 + 77 + 79 + 79 + 99 = 1173\nΣx² = 82811\n\nMean = 1173/17 = 69 cm\n\nVariance = Σx²/n − mean² = 82811/17 − 69² = 4871.235… − 4761 = 110.235…\nStandard deviation = √110.235… = 10.499… = 10.5 cm (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::8(c)": "The median is not affected (or much less affected) by the outlier 99, whereas the mean is pulled upwards by the 99. So the median gives a more representative average for these heights.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::9(a)(i)": "Let M be the mass of a plum: M ~ N(55, 18), so σ = √18 = 4.2426…\n\nP(50.0 < M < 60.0) = 0.7614… (calculator, normal cdf)\n\n(Standardising: z = ±5/√18 = ±1.1785, P(−1.1785 < Z < 1.1785) = 0.761)\n\nP(50.0 < M < 60.0) = 0.761 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::9(a)(ii)": "We need m such that P(M > m) = 0.05, i.e. P(M < m) = 0.95.\n\nInverse normal with mean 55 and standard deviation √18:\nm = 55 + 1.645 × √18 = 55 + 1.645 × 4.2426… = 61.98…\n\nMinimum mass of an extra large plum = 62.0 g (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::9(a)(iii)": "Let X̄ be the mean mass of the 10 plums in a bag.\nX̄ ~ N(55, 18/10) = N(55, 1.8)\n\nTotal mass < 530 g ⇔ X̄ < 530/10 = 53\n\nP(X̄ < 53) = 0.0680 (calculator)\n(z = (53 − 55)/√1.8 = −1.4907, Φ(−1.4907) = 0.0680)\n\nEquivalently, total mass ΣX ~ N(10 × 55, 10 × 18) = N(550, 180) and P(ΣX < 530) = 0.0680.\n\nProbability = 0.0680 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::9(b)": "Let Y be the mass of an apple: Y ~ N(67, σ²).\n\n62 and 72 are each 5 g from the mean 67, so by symmetry\nP(62 < Y < 72) = 0.5 ⇒ P(Y > 72) = P(Y < 62) = 0.25\n⇒ P(Y < 72) = 0.75\n\nFrom tables, Φ⁻¹(0.75) = 0.674, so\n(72 − 67)/σ = 0.674\n5 = 0.674σ\nσ = 5/0.674 = 7.418…\n\nσ = 7.42 (3 s.f.)\n(Using the unrounded value Φ⁻¹(0.75) = 0.67449 gives σ = 7.41; either is acceptable.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::10": "Let μ be the population mean level of pollutant (g per ml) this year.\n\nH₀: μ = 0.034\nH₁: μ ≠ 0.034   (two-tailed test, since we are testing whether the mean has changed)\n\nUnder H₀, the sample mean of 50 locations satisfies\nX̄ ~ N(0.034, 0.0000409/50), i.e. X̄ ~ N(0.034, 8.18 × 10⁻⁷)\nStandard deviation of X̄ = √(0.0000409/50) = 0.00640/√50 = 0.000904\n\nObserved x̄ = 0.0325 (below 0.034), so find the lower-tail probability:\nP(X̄ ≤ 0.0325) = 0.0486\n(z = (0.0325 − 0.034)/(0.00640/√50) = −1.66)\n\nTwo-tailed test at 5%: compare with 0.025 in each tail.\n0.0486 > 0.025 (equivalently −1.66 > −1.96; or 0.0325 lies inside the acceptance region 0.0322 < x̄ < 0.0358)\n\nThe result is not significant, so do not reject H₀.\n\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the mean level of pollutant has changed.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::11(a)": "k = P_young/P_senior = y/x, the gradient of the line from the origin to each point.\n\nTop-left cluster: the points have the smallest values of y/x at about (0.07, 0.11) and (0.088, 0.125), giving k ≈ 1.4–1.6; all other points in this cluster give larger values (up to about 2.5). So these LAs were chosen with k > 1.4 (approximately).\n\nBottom-right cluster: y ≈ 0.06 with x from about 0.24 to 0.30, giving k ≈ 0.06/0.24 = 0.25 down to about 0.2. So these LAs were chosen with k < 0.25 (approximately).\n\nRanges used: k > 1.4 and k < 0.25 (approximately).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::11(b)(i)": "The test is one-tailed (H₁: negative correlation) at the 1% level with n = 17.\nFrom the table, the critical value is 0.5577.\n\nr = −0.797 and −0.797 < −0.5577 (i.e. |−0.797| = 0.797 > 0.5577).\n\nSo the value of r lies in the critical region: it is significant at the 1% level, and H₀ is rejected.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::11(b)(ii)": "The diagram shows that the points form two separate clusters (top-left and bottom-right), with no negative correlation within either cluster.\n\nThe strong negative value of r is caused by the gap between the two clusters (a result of how the 17 LAs were selected using extreme values of k), not by a genuine linear relationship. So the value of r is misleading and the trainer's conclusion is unreliable.\n\n(Also, the data are clearly not from a bivariate normal distribution, so using the table of critical values is not valid.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::11(c)": "In the bottom-right LAs the proportion of people aged 65+ is high (about 0.24 to 0.30, much higher than in the other LAs), while the proportion aged 18 to 24 is low (about 0.06 in all of them).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::11(d)": "The top-left points show LAs with a high proportion of 18 to 24 year olds. These may be areas where universities are located, and the diagram shows universities where large numbers of young people (their target group of potential students) live, so where to focus recruiting.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::12(a)": "The probabilities must sum to 1:\nk(1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5) = 1\n15k = 1\nk = 1/15\n\nP(X = 3) = 3k = 3 × 1/15 = 3/15 = 0.2, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::12(b)": "Using P(X = x) = x/15:\n\nx:          1      2      3      4      5\nP(X = x):  1/15   2/15   3/15   4/15   5/15\n\n(i.e. 1/15, 2/15, 1/5, 4/15, 1/3)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::12(c)(i)": "The ordered pairs (first value, second value) with total 7 are:\n(2, 5), (5, 2), (3, 4), (4, 3)\n\nThe two values are independent, so\nP(T = 7) = 2 × [P(X = 2)P(X = 5) + P(X = 3)P(X = 4)]\n= 2 × [(2/15)(5/15) + (3/15)(4/15)]\n= 2 × (10 + 12)/225\n= 44/225\n\nP(T = 7) = 44/225 ≈ 0.196",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::12(c)(ii)": "If T = 7 and one of the values is 2, the other must be 5: the outcomes (2, 5) or (5, 2).\n\nP(one value is 2 and T = 7) = 2 × (2/15)(5/15) = 20/225 = 4/45\n\nP(one value is 2 | T = 7) = P(one value is 2 and T = 7)/P(T = 7)\n= (4/45) ÷ (44/225)\n= (20/225) ÷ (44/225)\n= 20/44\n= 5/11\n\nProbability = 5/11 ≈ 0.455",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p2::13": "X ~ B(5000, 0.26).\nMean = 5000 × 0.26 = 1300\nVariance = 5000 × 0.26 × 0.74 = 962\n\nFirst locate n approximately using the normal approximation N(1300, 962):\nP(X < n) = 0.025 ⇒ n ≈ 1300 − 1.96√962 = 1300 − 60.79 = 1239.2\n\nNow check exact binomial probabilities either side (calculator, B(5000, 0.26)):\nP(X ≤ 1238) = 0.0233, so P(X < 1239) = 0.0233 < 0.025\nP(X ≤ 1239) = 0.0251, so P(X < 1240) = 0.0251 > 0.025\n\nSo P(X < n) < 0.025 holds for n = 1239 but fails for n = 1240 (and for every larger n, since P(X < n) increases with n).\n\nLargest possible value of n = 1239",

 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::1": "Use the sine rule. The side opposite angle CAB is BC = 8.3 cm, and the side opposite angle ABC = 32° is AC = 13.5 cm.\n\nsin CAB / 8.3 = sin 32° / 13.5\nsin CAB = 8.3 sin 32° / 13.5\nsin CAB = (8.3 × 0.52992)/13.5 = 0.32580…\nCAB = 19.014…°\n\nThe other solution, 180° − 19.0° = 161.0°, is impossible because 161.0° + 32° > 180°.\n\nAngle CAB = 19.0°",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::2(a)(i)": "Complete the square in x and in y:\nx² − 6x + y² + 4y + 4 = 0\n(x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 2)² − 4 + 4 = 0\n(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 9\n\nCentre C = (3, −2)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::2(a)(ii)": "From (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 9 = 3²,\nradius r = √9 = 3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::2(b)": "Substitute y = kx − 3 into x² + y² − 6x + 4y + 4 = 0:\nx² + (kx − 3)² − 6x + 4(kx − 3) + 4 = 0\nx² + k²x² − 6kx + 9 − 6x + 4kx − 12 + 4 = 0\n(1 + k²)x² + (−6 − 2k)x + 1 = 0\n\nThe line does not intersect or touch the circle when this quadratic in x has no real roots, i.e. discriminant < 0:\n(−6 − 2k)² − 4(1 + k²)(1) < 0\n36 + 24k + 4k² − 4 − 4k² < 0\n32 + 24k < 0\n24k < −32\n\nk < −4/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::3(a)": "Both sides are non-negative, so the inequality is equivalent to squaring both sides:\n(x − 2)² ≤ (2x − 6)²\nx² − 4x + 4 ≤ 4x² − 24x + 36\n0 ≤ 3x² − 20x + 32\n0 ≤ (3x − 8)(x − 4)\n\nCritical values: x = 8/3 and x = 4.\nThe quadratic 3x² − 20x + 32 has positive x² coefficient, so it is ≥ 0 outside (and at) its roots.\n\nx ≤ 8/3 or x ≥ 4",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::3(b)": "y = |x − 2| → y = |(x − 4) − 2| = |x − 6| is a translation by the vector (4, 0), i.e. 4 units in the positive x-direction.\n\ny = |x − 6| → y = |2x − 6| replaces x by 2x, which is a stretch parallel to the x-axis with scale factor ½.\n\nSo: first a translation by 4 units in the positive x-direction (vector (4, 0)), then a stretch parallel to the x-axis with scale factor 0.5.\n\n(Equivalent alternatives: a stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor 0.5, followed by a translation of 2 units in the positive x-direction; or a translation of 1 unit in the positive x-direction and a stretch parallel to the y-axis with scale factor 2, in either order, since |2x − 6| = 2|x − 3|.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(a)": "y = 3x sin 2x\nProduct rule: dy/dx = 3 sin 2x + 3x(2 cos 2x) = 3 sin 2x + 6x cos 2x\n\nAt the maximum point P, dy/dx = 0:\n3 sin 2x + 6x cos 2x = 0\nsin 2x + 2x cos 2x = 0\n\nAt P, cos 2x ≠ 0 (if cos 2x = 0 then the equation would give sin 2x = 0 too, which is impossible), so divide by cos 2x:\nsin 2x / cos 2x + 2x (cos 2x / cos 2x) = 0\n\ntan 2x + 2x = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(b)": "Let f(x) = tan 2x + 2x, so f′(x) = 2 sec²2x + 2.\n\nNewton-Raphson:\nxₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (tan 2xₙ + 2xₙ)/(2 sec²2xₙ + 2)\n\nFrom the diagram P lies between π/4 and π/2 (and the root must lie between π/4 and 3π/4), so take x₀ = 1.\n\nx₁ = 1.01365728…\nx₂ = 1.01437714…\nx₃ = 1.01437892…\nx₄ = 1.01437892…\n\nThe iterates agree to 4 decimal places.\n\nx-coordinate of P = 1.0144 (4 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(c)": "Four strips over 0 ≤ x ≤ ½π: h = (½π)/4 = π/8.\n\nx-values: 0, π/8, π/4, 3π/8, π/2\n\ny₀ = 3(0) sin 0 = 0\ny₁ = 3(π/8) sin(π/4) = (3π/8)(√2/2) = 3π√2/16\ny₂ = 3(π/4) sin(π/2) = 3π/4\ny₃ = 3(3π/8) sin(3π/4) = (9π/8)(√2/2) = 9π√2/16\ny₄ = 3(π/2) sin π = 0\n\nTrapezium rule:\n∫ ≈ ½h[y₀ + y₄ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)]\n= ½(π/8)[0 + 0 + 2(3π√2/16 + 3π/4 + 9π√2/16)]\n= (π/16)((3/8)π√2 + (3/2)π + (9/8)π√2)\n= (π/16)((3/2)π√2 + (3/2)π)\n= (3π²/32)(√2 + 1)\n\nSo the result is kπ²(√2 + 1) with k = 3/32.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(d)(i)": "Integrate by parts with u = 3x, dv/dx = sin 2x:\ndu/dx = 3, v = −½ cos 2x\n\n∫3x sin 2x dx = −(3/2)x cos 2x + ∫(3/2) cos 2x dx = −(3/2)x cos 2x + (3/4) sin 2x\n\nEvaluate from 0 to ½π:\n[−(3/2)(π/2) cos π + (3/4) sin π] − [0 + 0]\n= (3π/4)(1) + 0\n= 3π/4\n\n∫₀^(½π) 3x sin 2x dx = 3π/4 (≈ 2.356)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(d)(ii)": "Trapezium estimate: (3/32)π²(√2 + 1) ≈ 2.23\nExact value: 3π/4 ≈ 2.356\n\nSince 2.23 < 2.356, the trapezium rule with four strips gives an under-estimate of the area.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::4(d)(iii)": "The curve changes from convex (near O) to concave (around P), so the left-hand trapezium lies above the curve while the other trapezia lie below it. Some strips over-estimate and some under-estimate, so from the diagram alone it is not clear which effect is larger overall.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::5(a)": "Write cot θ and cosec θ in terms of sin θ and cos θ:\n(cot θ + cosec θ)² = (cos θ/sin θ + 1/sin θ)²\n= ((1 + cos θ)/sin θ)²\n= (1 + cos θ)²/sin²θ\n\nUse sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ:\n= (1 + cos θ)²/(1 − cos²θ)\n\nFactorise the difference of two squares, 1 − cos²θ = (1 + cos θ)(1 − cos θ):\n= (1 + cos θ)(1 + cos θ)/((1 + cos θ)(1 − cos θ))\n= (1 + cos θ)/(1 − cos θ)\n\nHence (cot θ + cosec θ)² = (1 + cos θ)/(1 − cos θ), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::5(b)": "Using part (a) and sec θ = 1/cos θ:\n3(cot θ + cosec θ)² = 2 sec θ\n3(1 + cos θ)/(1 − cos θ) = 2/cos θ\n\nMultiply by cos θ(1 − cos θ):\n3 cos θ(1 + cos θ) = 2(1 − cos θ)\n3 cos θ + 3cos²θ = 2 − 2 cos θ\n3cos²θ + 5 cos θ − 2 = 0\n(3 cos θ − 1)(cos θ + 2) = 0\n\ncos θ = 1/3 or cos θ = −2\ncos θ = −2 is rejected, since −1 ≤ cos θ ≤ 1.\n\ncos θ = 1/3:\nθ = cos⁻¹(1/3) = 1.2309… or θ = 2π − 1.2309… = 5.0522…\n\nθ = 1.23, 5.05 (3 s.f., radians)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::6": "The curve crosses the x-axis where 2x − 1 = 0, i.e. x = ½, so the shaded region lies between x = 0 and x = ½, below the x-axis.\n\nPartial fractions:\n(2x − 1)/((2x + 3)(x + 1)²) ≡ A/(2x + 3) + B/(x + 1) + C/(x + 1)²\n2x − 1 ≡ A(x + 1)² + B(2x + 3)(x + 1) + C(2x + 3)\n\nx = −1: −3 = C(1), so C = −3\nx = −3/2: −4 = A(1/4), so A = −16\nx = 0: −1 = A + 3B + 3C = −16 + 3B − 9, so 3B = 24, B = 8\n\ny = −16/(2x + 3) + 8/(x + 1) − 3/(x + 1)²\n\nIntegrate:\n∫ y dx = −8 ln(2x + 3) + 8 ln(x + 1) + 3(x + 1)⁻¹\n\nApply limits 0 and ½:\nx = ½: −8 ln 4 + 8 ln(3/2) + 3/(3/2) = −8 ln 4 + 8 ln(3/2) + 2\nx = 0: −8 ln 3 + 8 ln 1 + 3 = −8 ln 3 + 3\n\n∫₀^½ y dx = (−8 ln 4 + 8 ln(3/2) + 2) − (−8 ln 3 + 3)\n= 8 ln(3/2) + 8 ln 3 − 8 ln 4 − 1\n= 8 ln((3/2 × 3)/4) − 1\n= 8 ln(9/8) − 1 (≈ −0.0577, negative since the region is below the x-axis)\n\nArea = 1 − 8 ln(9/8) = 1 + 8 ln(8/9)\n\nSo p = 1, q = 8, r = 8/9.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::7(a)": "Velocity after 4 s: v = u + at = 0 + 1.5 × 4 = 6 m s⁻¹.\n\nSketch (velocity v on the vertical axis, time t on the horizontal axis):\n- a straight line from the origin O rising to the point (4, 6);\n- then a horizontal straight line at v = 6 from t = 4 to t = 10.\nMark 6 on the v-axis, and 4 and 10 on the t-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::7(b)": "Distance = area under the velocity-time graph.\n\nTriangle (0 ≤ t ≤ 4): ½ × 4 × 6 = 12 m\nRectangle (4 ≤ t ≤ 10): 6 × 6 = 36 m\n\nDistance = 12 + 36 = 48 m\n(Equivalently, trapezium: ½(10 + 6) × 6 = 48.)\n\nDistance travelled = 48 m",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::8(a)": "Horizontal velocity is constant at (5/3)d m s⁻¹, and P is in the air for 2.4 s.\n\nHorizontal distance = (5/3)d × 2.4 = 4d m",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::8(b)": "Let the vertical component of the initial velocity be U m s⁻¹ (upwards).\nVertically, P returns to ground level (s = 0) at t = 2.4 with a = −g:\ns = ut + ½at²\n0 = 2.4U − ½(9.8)(2.4)²\n2.4U = 4.9 × 5.76 = 28.224\nU = 11.76\n\nVertical component of initial velocity = 11.76 m s⁻¹ (= 1.2g)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::8(c)": "Time t to reach the wall (horizontal distance d):\nd = (5/3)d × t, so t = 3/5 = 0.6 s\n\nHeight of P at t = 0.6 (equal to the height of the wall, since P just clears it):\nh = Ut − ½gt²\n= 11.76(0.6) − ½(9.8)(0.6)²\n= 7.056 − 1.764\n= 5.292\n\nHeight of wall = 5.292 m (≈ 5.29 m)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::8(d)": "Vertical velocity component at the wall (t = 0.6):\nv₁ = U − gt = 11.76 − 9.8 × 0.6 = 5.88 m s⁻¹\n\nHorizontal component is (5/3)d throughout. Speed at the wall is 16 m s⁻¹:\n√(((5/3)d)² + 5.88²) = 16\n((5/3)d)² = 256 − 34.5744 = 221.4256\n(5/3)d = 14.8804…\nd = 8.9282…\n\nd = 8.93 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::9(a)": "Since the string is inextensible, B and P have the same acceleration a. P starts from rest and moves 0.3 m in 0.4 s:\ns = ut + ½at²\n0.3 = ½a(0.4)²\n0.3 = 0.08a\na = 3.75 m s⁻²\n\ntan θ = 3/4, so sin θ = 3/5 (and cos θ = 4/5).\n\nNewton's second law for P down the plane (smooth plane, tension T up the plane):\n0.5g sin θ − T = 0.5a\n0.5 × 9.8 × 3/5 − T = 0.5 × 3.75\n2.94 − T = 1.875\nT = 1.065\n\nTension = 1.065 N (≈ 1.07 N)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::9(b)": "Vertically for B: normal reaction R = 0.2g = 1.96 N.\n\nNewton's second law for B horizontally (tension towards the pulley, friction F opposing motion):\nT − F = 0.2a\n1.065 − F = 0.2 × 3.75 = 0.75\nF = 0.315 N\n\nB is moving, so friction is limiting: F = μR\n0.315 = μ × 0.2 × 9.8\nμ = 0.315/1.96 = 9/56 = 0.1607…\n\nCoefficient of friction μ = 0.161 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::10(a)": "v = (pt² − 3t)i + (8t + q)j\n\nAcceleration: a = dv/dt = (2pt − 3)i + 8j\n\nF = ma with m = 2, so |F| = 2√((2pt − 3)² + 8²).\n\nAt t = 0.5, |F| = 20:\n2√((2p(0.5) − 3)² + 64) = 20\n√((p − 3)² + 64) = 10\n(p − 3)² + 64 = 100\n(p − 3)² = 36\np − 3 = ±6\np = 9 or p = −3\n\nSince p < 0, reject p = 9.\n\np = −3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::10(b)": "With p = −3: v = (−3t² − 3t)i + (8t + q)j\n\nIntegrate to get the displacement (position vector) s:\ns = (−t³ − (3/2)t²)i + (4t² + qt)j + c\n(i.e. ((1/3)pt³ − (3/2)t²)i + (4t² + qt)j + c with p = −3)\n\nAt t = 0, s = 2i − 3j, so c = 2i − 3j.\n\ns = (−t³ − (3/2)t² + 2)i + (4t² + qt − 3)j",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::10(c)": "At t = 1, using part (b):\ns = (−1 − 3/2 + 2)i + (4 + q − 3)j = −½i + (1 + q)j\n\nThe line L passes through O and 2i − 8j, so points on L are scalar multiples of 2i − 8j. Hence for some scalar λ:\nλ(−½i + (1 + q)j) = 2i − 8j\n\ni-components: −½λ = 2, so λ = −4\nj-components: −4(1 + q) = −8, so 1 + q = 2\n\nq = 1",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::11(a)": "Let l be the distance along the ladder from A to the point where it rests on the top of the wall. The wall has height h and the ladder makes 30° with the ground:\nl = h/sin 30° = 2h\n\nThe wall is smooth, so its normal contact force R_W on the ladder is perpendicular to the ladder.\n\nTake moments about A. The weight mg of the ladder acts at its midpoint (distance a from A) and the weight 2mg of the object acts at C (distance d from A); the perpendicular distances of their lines of action from A are a cos 30° and d cos 30°.\n\nR_W × 2h = mg × a cos 30° + 2mg × d cos 30°\nR_W × 2h = (mga + 2mgd)(√3/2)\nR_W = (√3/(4h))(mga + 2mgd)\n\nR_W = mg(a + 2d)√3/(4h), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::11(b)": "R_W acts perpendicular to the ladder, so it makes 30° with the vertical: its vertical component is R_W cos 30° (upwards) and its horizontal component is R_W sin 30° (horizontally away from the wall, back towards A).\nLet R_A be the normal reaction at A and F_A the friction at A, which acts horizontally towards the wall.\n\nResolve vertically:\nR_W cos 30° + R_A = 2mg + mg = 3mg\n\nResolve horizontally:\nF_A = R_W sin 30°\n\nLimiting equilibrium: F_A = (√3/8)R_A\nR_W sin 30° = (√3/8)(3mg − R_W cos 30°)\n\nUsing R_W = mg(a + 2d)√3/(4h):\nR_W sin 30° = mg(a + 2d)√3/(8h)\nR_W cos 30° = mg(a + 2d)√3/(4h) × √3/2 = 3mg(a + 2d)/(8h)\n\nSo\nmg(a + 2d)√3/(8h) = (√3/8)[3mg − 3mg(a + 2d)/(8h)]\n\nDivide by (√3/8)mg:\n(a + 2d)/h = 3 − 3(a + 2d)/(8h)\n(a + 2d)/h + 3(a + 2d)/(8h) = 3\n11(a + 2d)/(8h) = 3\nh = 11(a + 2d)/24\n\nSo h = k(a + 2d) with k = 11/24.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::11(c)": "The ladder rests on the top of the wall, so the height of the wall cannot exceed the height of the top end B of the ladder:\nh ≤ 2a sin 30° = a\n\n(11/24)(a + 2d) ≤ a\na + 2d ≤ (24/11)a\n2d ≤ (13/11)a\nd ≤ (13/22)a\n\nGreatest possible value of d = (13/22)a",
 "math-ocr-h240-2019-p3::11(d)": "Possible improvement (any one): include friction at the contact between the ladder and the wall (rather than modelling the wall as smooth).\nOther acceptable improvements: model the ladder as non-uniform; take into account the size of the object at C; take into account the thickness of the ladder; allow for the ladder bending.",





































 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::1": "For two distinct real roots the discriminant must be positive: b² − 4ac > 0.\nHere a = 1, b = 4, c = k + 3.\nb² − 4ac = 16 − 4(k + 3)\n16 − 4(k + 3) > 0\n16 − 4k − 12 > 0\n4 − 4k > 0\n4k < 4\nk < 1\nThe set of values is {k : k < 1}.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::2(a)": "Work in pence, with C_A and C_B the costs of contracts A and B.\nContract A: set-up £40 = 4000p, plus 4p for each of the m minutes.\nC_A = 4000 + 4m\nContract B: the first 100 minutes are free, so only (m − 100) minutes are charged at 6p each (valid since m > 100).\nC_B = 6(m − 100)\n(Equivalently in pounds: C_A = 40 + 0.04m and C_B = 0.06(m − 100).)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::2(b)": "Set the two costs equal:\n4000 + 4m = 6(m − 100)\n4000 + 4m = 6m − 600\n4600 = 2m\nm = 2300\nBoth contracts cost the same when m = 2300 minutes (each costs 13 200p = £132).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::3(a)": "x is proportional to y²√z, so\nx = ky²√z for some constant k.\nWhen y = 2, z = 9, x = 30:\n30 = k × 2² × √9 = k × 4 × 3 = 12k\nk = 30/12 = 2.5\nx = 2.5y²√z",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::3(b)": "Using x = 2.5y²√z with y = 3 and z = 25:\nx = 2.5 × 3² × √25\nx = 2.5 × 9 × 5\nx = 112.5",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::4(a)": "By the factor theorem, (2x − 1) is a factor of f(x) if f(½) = 0.\nf(½) = 2(½)³ − 3(½)² − 11(½) + 6\n= 2(⅛) − 3(¼) − 5.5 + 6\n= 0.25 − 0.75 − 5.5 + 6\n= 0\nSince f(½) = 0, (2x − 1) is a factor of f(x).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::4(b)": "Divide f(x) by (2x − 1). Write f(x) = (2x − 1)(ax² + bx + c).\nComparing coefficients:\nx³: 2a = 2, so a = 1\nconstant: −c = 6, so c = −6\nx²: 2b − a = −3, so 2b = −2, b = −1\nCheck x: 2c − b = −12 + 1 = −11 ✓\nSo f(x) = (2x − 1)(x² − x − 6).\nFactorise the quadratic: x² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2).\nf(x) = (2x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::4(c)": "Note 8^y = (2^y)³ and 4^y = (2^y)². Let x = 2^y. Then the equation becomes\n2x³ − 3x² − 11x + 6 = 0, i.e. f(x) = 0.\nFrom part (b): (2x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0\nx = ½, x = 3 or x = −2.\n2^y = ½ ⇒ y = −1\n2^y = 3 ⇒ y = log₂3 = ln 3/ln 2 ≈ 1.58\n2^y = −2 has no solution, since 2^y > 0 for all real y.\nSolutions: y = −1 and y = log₂3 (≈ 1.58).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(a)(i)": "y = f(x) + 3 is a translation of y = f(x) by 3 units in the positive y-direction.\nP(2, 6) maps to (2, 6 + 3).\nImage: (2, 9)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(a)(ii)": "We need the point where the input to f is 2, since f(2) = 6.\n3x − 1 = 2 ⇒ x = 1\nThen y = 2f(2) = 2 × 6 = 12.\nImage: (1, 12)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(a)(iii)": "The graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x, so the coordinates swap.\nSince f(2) = 6, f⁻¹(6) = 2.\nImage: (6, 2)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(b)(i)": "Stationary points of y = g(x) occur where g′(x) = 0, i.e. where the graph of y = g′(x) meets the x-axis.\nx = −2 and x = 4",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(b)(ii)": "g(x) is decreasing where g′(x) < 0, i.e. where the graph of y = g′(x) is below the x-axis.\nFrom the graph this happens only to the left of x = −2.\n{x : x < −2}",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::5(b)(iii)": "A point of inflection of y = g(x) occurs where g″(x) changes sign, i.e. where the graph of y = g′(x) has a turning point (g′ changes from decreasing to increasing or vice versa).\nAt x = −2 the graph of g′ crosses the axis while still increasing, so g″ does not change sign there (it is a minimum point of g, not an inflection).\nAt x = 4 the graph of g′ has a minimum touching the x-axis: g′ decreases before x = 4 and increases after it, so g″ changes sign. Since g′(4) = 0 and g′ ≥ 0 on both sides, this is a stationary point of inflection.\nx = 4",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::6(a)": "Take out the factor 8:\n(8 − 3x)^(1/3) = 8^(1/3)(1 − ⅜x)^(1/3) = 2(1 − ⅜x)^(1/3)\nExpand (1 − ⅜x)^(1/3) using (1 + u)ⁿ = 1 + nu + n(n − 1)u²/2! + … with n = ⅓, u = −⅜x:\n(1 − ⅜x)^(1/3) = 1 + (⅓)(−⅜x) + (⅓)(−⅔)(−⅜x)²/2! + …\nSecond term: (⅓)(−⅜x) = −⅛x\nThird term: (⅓)(−⅔) × (9/64)x² ÷ 2 = (−2/9) × (9/64)x² ÷ 2 = −(1/64)x²\nSo (1 − ⅜x)^(1/3) = 1 − ⅛x − (1/64)x² + …\nMultiply by 2:\n(8 − 3x)^(1/3) = 2 − ¼x − (1/32)x² + …",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::6(b)": "The expansion of (1 − ⅜x)^(1/3) is valid when |⅜x| < 1.\n|x| < 8/3, i.e. −8/3 < x < 8/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::6(c)": "Write the expression as (8 − 3x)^(1/3) × (1 + 2x)^(−2).\nExpand (1 + 2x)^(−2):\n(1 + 2x)^(−2) = 1 + (−2)(2x) + (−2)(−3)(2x)²/2! + …\n= 1 − 4x + 3(4x²) + …\n= 1 − 4x + 12x² + …\nMultiply by the result of part (a):\n(2 − ¼x − (1/32)x² + …)(1 − 4x + 12x² + …)\nThe x² terms come from:\n2 × 12x² = 24x²\n(−¼x) × (−4x) = x²\n(−(1/32)x²) × 1 = −(1/32)x²\nCoefficient of x² = 24 + 1 − 1/32 = 799/32 (= 24 31/32 = 24.96875)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::7(a)": "y = (x² − 2) ln x\nUsing the product rule:\ndy/dx = 2x ln x + (x² − 2) × (1/x)\n= 2x ln x + (x² − 2)/x\nAt a stationary point dy/dx = 0:\n2x ln x + (x² − 2)/x = 0\nMultiply by x (x > 0):\n2x² ln x + x² − 2 = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::7(b)": "Let h(x) = 2x² ln x + x² − 2.\nh′(x) = 4x ln x + 2x² × (1/x) + 2x = 4x ln x + 2x + 2x = 4x ln x + 4x\nNewton-Raphson: x_(n+1) = x_n − h(x_n)/h′(x_n)\nx_(n+1) = x_n − (2x_n² ln x_n + x_n² − 2)/(4x_n ln x_n + 4x_n)\nWrite as a single fraction:\nx_(n+1) = [x_n(4x_n ln x_n + 4x_n) − (2x_n² ln x_n + x_n² − 2)]/(4x_n ln x_n + 4x_n)\n= (4x_n² ln x_n + 4x_n² − 2x_n² ln x_n − x_n² + 2)/(4x_n ln x_n + 4x_n)\n= (2x_n² ln x_n + 3x_n² + 2)/(4x_n(ln x_n + 1)), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::7(c)": "With x₁ = 1 (ln 1 = 0):\nx₂ = (2(1)²(0) + 3(1)² + 2)/(4(1)(0 + 1)) = 5/4 = 1.25\nx₃ = (2(1.25)² ln 1.25 + 3(1.25)² + 2)/(4(1.25)(ln 1.25 + 1))\n= (0.697324… + 4.6875 + 2)/(5 × 1.223143…)\n= 7.384824…/6.115718…\n= 1.2075 (4 d.p.)\nx₂ = 1.25, x₃ = 1.2075",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::7(d)": "Continue the iteration:\nx₄ = 1.206125…\nx₅ = 1.206123…\nx₆ = 1.206123…\nSo x = 1.206 (3 d.p.).\nCheck sign change: h(1.2055) < 0 and h(1.2065) > 0, where h(x) = 2x² ln x + x² − 2, so the root lies in this interval and rounds to 1.206.\ny = (x² − 2) ln x = (1.206123…² − 2) ln 1.206123… = (−0.545266…)(0.187411…) = −0.102 (3 d.p.)\nStationary point: (1.206, −0.102)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::8(a)(i)": "On 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π, tan x takes every real value (for example on π/2 < x < 3π/2 it increases through all real numbers), so 2 tan x does too.\nRange of f: f(x) ∈ ℝ (all real numbers).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::8(a)(ii)": "g(x) = sec x = 1/cos x. Since −1 ≤ cos x ≤ 1 and cos x ≠ 0, |sec x| ≥ 1, and sec x takes the values 1 (at x = 0) and −1 (at x = π) and every value beyond them.\nRange of g: g(x) ≤ −1 or g(x) ≥ 1, i.e. g(x) ∈ (−∞, −1] ∪ [1, ∞).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::8(b)(i)": "fg(0.6) = f(g(0.6)) = 2 tan(sec 0.6)\ncos 0.6 = 0.8253…\nsec 0.6 = 1/0.8253… = 1.2116…\nfg(0.6) = 2 tan(1.2116…) = 2 × 2.6634… = 5.3269…\nSo fg(0.6) = 5.33 (3 s.f.), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::8(b)(ii)": "f⁻¹g(0.6) needs the inverse function f⁻¹. On the domain 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π, f(x) = 2 tan x is a many-to-one function (for example f(0) = f(π) = f(2π) = 0), so f has no inverse function. Hence f⁻¹g(0.6) is not defined.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::8(c)": "(f(x))² + 6g(x) = 0\n4tan²x + 6 sec x = 0\nUse tan²x = sec²x − 1:\n4(sec²x − 1) + 6 sec x = 0\n4sec²x + 6 sec x − 4 = 0\n2sec²x + 3 sec x − 2 = 0\n(2 sec x − 1)(sec x + 2) = 0\nsec x = ½ or sec x = −2\nsec x = ½ has no solutions, since |sec x| ≥ 1 for all x (equivalently cos x = 2 is impossible).\nsec x = −2 ⇒ cos x = −½\nOn 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π: x = π − π/3 = 2π/3 or x = π + π/3 = 4π/3.\nx = 2π/3, 4π/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::9(a)": "y = 2e^(−3t). Since e^(−3t) > 0 for all t, y = 2e^(−3t) > 0 for all t ≥ 0.\nSo y is never zero, and the path of the particle never meets (so never crosses) the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::9(b)": "The path meets the y-axis when x = 0:\ne^(2t) − 4e^t + 3 = 0\nThis is a quadratic in e^t:\n(e^t − 1)(e^t − 3) = 0\ne^t = 1 or e^t = 3\nt = 0 or t = ln 3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::9(c)": "dx/dt = 2e^(2t) − 4e^t\ndy/dt = −6e^(−3t)\ndy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = −6e^(−3t)/(2e^(2t) − 4e^t)\nDivide numerator and denominator by −2:\ndy/dx = 3e^(−3t)/(2e^t − e^(2t))\nMultiply numerator and denominator by e^(3t):\ndy/dx = 3/(e^(3t)(2e^t − e^(2t)))\n= 3/(2e^(4t) − e^(5t)), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::9(d)": "The path is parallel to the y-axis when the gradient dy/dx is undefined, i.e. when the denominator is zero (the numerator 3 is never zero):\n2e^(4t) − e^(5t) = 0\ne^(4t)(2 − e^t) = 0\ne^(4t) > 0, so e^t = 2, giving t = ln 2.\nAt t = ln 2:\nx = e^(2ln 2) − 4e^(ln 2) + 3 = 4 − 8 + 3 = −1\ny = 2e^(−3ln 2) = 2 × 2^(−3) = 2/8 = ¼\nThe particle is at (−1, ¼).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::10(a)(i)": "Triangle BCD is right-angled at D, with hypotenuse BC = a and angle DCB = y.\nCD is the side adjacent to angle y, so\ncos y = CD/BC = CD/a\nHence CD = a cos y.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::10(a)(ii)": "In triangle ADC, the sides AC = b and CD = a cos y enclose the angle ACD = x.\nUsing area = ½ × (side) × (side) × sin(included angle):\nArea ADC = ½ × AC × CD × sin x\n= ½ × b × (a cos y) × sin x\n= ½ab sin x cos y, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::10(a)(iii)": "Triangle ADC is right-angled at D with hypotenuse AC = b and angle ACD = x, so\ncos x = CD/b, giving CD = b cos x.\nArea of triangle BCD, using sides BC = a and CD = b cos x with included angle DCB = y:\nArea BCD = ½ × BC × CD × sin y = ½ × a × (b cos x) × sin y = ½ab cos x sin y\nArea of triangle ABC, using sides AC = b and BC = a with included angle ACB = x + y:\nArea ABC = ½ab sin(x + y)\nTriangle ABC is made up of triangles ADC and BCD, so\nArea ABC = Area ADC + Area BCD\n½ab sin(x + y) = ½ab sin x cos y + ½ab cos x sin y\nDivide by ½ab (which is non-zero):\nsin(x + y) = sin x cos y + cos x sin y, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::10(b)": "Expand both sides using the compound-angle formulae:\nsin(30° + α) = sin 30° cos α + cos 30° sin α\ncos(45° − α) = cos 45° cos α + sin 45° sin α\nSubstitute exact values (sin 30° = ½, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = sin 45° = √2/2):\n½cos α + (√3/2)sin α = (√2/2)cos α + (√2/2)sin α\nMultiply by 2:\ncos α + √3 sin α = √2 cos α + √2 sin α\n(√3 − √2)sin α = (√2 − 1)cos α\nDivide by cos α:\ntan α = (√2 − 1)/(√3 − √2)\nRationalise the denominator by multiplying top and bottom by (√3 + √2):\ntan α = (√2 − 1)(√3 + √2)/((√3 − √2)(√3 + √2))\nNumerator: √6 + 2 − √3 − √2\nDenominator: 3 − 2 = 1\ntan α = 2 + √6 − √3 − √2, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::11(a)": "u² = x² + 3, so differentiating: 2u du = 2x dx, i.e. x dx = u du.\nAlso x² = u² − 3 and √(x² + 3) = u.\nWrite the integrand as 4x² × x/√(x² + 3):\n∫ 4x³/√(x² + 3) dx = ∫ 4x² × (x dx)/√(x² + 3)\n= ∫ 4(u² − 3) × u du/u\n= ∫ (4u² − 12) du\n= (4/3)u³ − 12u + c\n= (4/3)u(u² − 9) + c\nSubstitute back: u² − 9 = x² + 3 − 9 = x² − 6 and u = √(x² + 3):\n= (4/3)(x² − 6)√(x² + 3) + c, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::11(b)": "The region is bounded by the curve from O to (1, 2), the normal from (1, 2) down to the x-axis, and the x-axis. Split it into the area under the curve from x = 0 to x = 1 plus a right-angled triangle under the normal.\n\nArea under the curve, using part (a):\n∫₀¹ 4x³/√(x² + 3) dx = [(4/3)(x² − 6)√(x² + 3)]₀¹\n= (4/3)[(1 − 6)√4 − (0 − 6)√3]\n= (4/3)(−10 + 6√3)\n= 8√3 − 40/3 (≈ 0.523)\n\nGradient of the curve, using the quotient rule with y = 4x³/(x² + 3)^(1/2):\ndy/dx = [12x²(x² + 3)^(1/2) − 4x³ × ½(x² + 3)^(−1/2) × 2x]/(x² + 3)\nAt x = 1: dy/dx = [12 × 2 − 4 × ½ × ½ × 2]/4 = (24 − 2)/4 = 11/2\nGradient of the normal = −2/11.\n\nEquation of the normal at (1, 2):\ny − 2 = −(2/11)(x − 1)\nWhen y = 0: −2 = −(2/11)(x − 1) ⇒ x − 1 = 11 ⇒ x = 12.\nSo the normal meets the x-axis at (12, 0).\n\nTriangle with vertices (1, 0), (12, 0), (1, 2): base 11, height 2,\narea = ½ × 11 × 2 = 11.\n\nTotal area = 8√3 − 40/3 + 11 = 8√3 − 7/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(a)(i)": "The temperature decreases at a constant rate, so\ndθ/dt = −k, where k is a positive constant.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(a)(ii)": "Integrate dθ/dt = −k:\nθ = −kt + c\nt = 0, θ = 160: c = 160, so θ = 160 − kt.\nt = 10, θ = 125: 125 = 160 − 10k ⇒ 10k = 35 ⇒ k = 3.5\nθ = 160 − 3.5t",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(a)(iii)": "The model predicts the temperature keeps falling at 3.5 °C per minute for ever, so it would eventually fall below the room temperature and even below freezing point (e.g. θ = 0 at t ≈ 46 minutes), which cannot happen for a cake cooling in a room. (In reality the cake's temperature levels off at room temperature, so the rate of cooling is not constant.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(b)(i)": "The rate of decrease of θ is proportional to the excess temperature θ − 20, so\ndθ/dt = −λ(θ − 20), where λ is a positive constant.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(b)(ii)": "dθ/dt = −λ(θ − 20), where λ is a positive constant.\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ 1/(θ − 20) dθ = ∫ −λ dt\nln|θ − 20| = −λt + c\nt = 0, θ = 160: ln 140 = c\nso ln|θ − 20| = −λt + ln 140\nt = 10, θ = 125: ln 105 = −10λ + ln 140\n10λ = ln 140 − ln 105 = ln(4/3), so −λ = 0.1 ln(105/140) = 0.1 ln 0.75 (≈ −0.0288)\nln(θ − 20) = (0.1 ln 0.75)t + ln 140   (θ > 20 throughout)\nθ − 20 = e^((0.1 ln 0.75)t + ln 140) = 140e^((0.1 ln 0.75)t)\nθ = 20 + 140e^((0.1 ln 0.75)t)\n(equivalently θ = 20 + 140(0.75)^(0.1t), or θ ≈ 20 + 140e^(−0.0288t))",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p1::12(c)": "Put θ = 25 into each model.\nSimple model: 25 = 160 − 3.5t ⇒ 3.5t = 135 ⇒ t = 38.57… minutes\nRevised model: 25 = 20 + 140e^((0.1 ln 0.75)t)\ne^((0.1 ln 0.75)t) = 5/140\n(0.1 ln 0.75)t = ln(5/140) = −ln 28\nt = −ln 28/(0.1 ln 0.75) = 115.83… minutes\nDifference = 115.83… − 38.57… = 77.26… minutes\nThe difference is 77 minutes (to the nearest minute).",






































 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::1(a)": "y = e^(−4x)\nBy the chain rule, d/dx(e^(kx)) = ke^(kx), with k = −4.\ndy/dx = −4e^(−4x)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::1(b)": "y = x²/(x + 1)\nQuotient rule with u = x², v = x + 1, so du/dx = 2x and dv/dx = 1.\ndy/dx = (v du/dx − u dv/dx)/v²\n= ((x + 1)×2x − x²×1)/(x + 1)²\n= (2x² + 2x − x²)/(x + 1)²\n= (x² + 2x)/(x + 1)²\n= x(x + 2)/(x + 1)²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::2": "The sign change method relies on f(x) having opposite signs either side of a root.\nAt the first root (between x = 0 and x = 0.5) the curve only touches the x-axis: it is a turning point on the axis (a repeated root).\nf(x) is positive on both sides of this root, so the curve does not cross the x-axis there and there is no change of sign.\nTherefore this root cannot be located by the sign change method.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::3": "Let the first term be a and the common difference d.\n15th term: a + 14d = 88   (1)\nSum of first 10 terms: S₁₀ = (10/2)(2a + 9d) = 310, so 5(2a + 9d) = 310, i.e. 2a + 9d = 62   (2)\nFrom (1): a = 88 − 14d.\nSubstitute into (2): 2(88 − 14d) + 9d = 62\n176 − 28d + 9d = 62\n176 − 19d = 62\n19d = 114\nd = 6\na = 88 − 14×6 = 88 − 84 = 4\nFirst term a = 4, common difference d = 6.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(a)": "The maximum occurs when cos(30t)° = −1:\nP = 5000 − 1000×(−1) = 6000\nMaximum population = 6000",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(b)": "Smallest P occurs when cos(30t)° = 1: P = 5000 − 1000 = 4000.\nLargest P = 6000.\nDifference = 6000 − 4000 = 2000",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(c)": "The population oscillates: it rises and falls repeatedly in a regular cycle, going up and down between the same maximum and minimum values.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(d)": "At t = 0, P = 5000 − 1000cos 0° = 4000.\nP returns to this value when cos(30t)° = 1 again, i.e. after one full period:\n30t = 360\nt = 12\nThe population first returns to its initial size after 12 months.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(e)": "4500 = 5000 − 1000cos(30t)°\n1000cos(30t)° = 500\ncos(30t)° = 0.5\nFor 0 ≤ 30t ≤ 360: 30t = cos⁻¹(0.5) = 60 or 30t = 360 − 60 = 300\nSo t = 2 or t = 10.\nThe first time P = 4500 is t = 2 and the second time is t = 10.\nP = 4500 for the second time after 10 months (t = 10).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::4(f)": "Multiply the cosine term by a factor that decreases towards 0 as t increases, so that the oscillations get smaller and P tends to 5000.\nFor example:\nP = 5000 − 1000e^(−kt)cos(30t)°, where k > 0 is a constant.\nAs t → ∞, e^(−kt) → 0, so the variations shrink and P → 5000.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::5(a)": "A(0, 6), B(7, 5)\nMidpoint of AB = ((0 + 7)/2, (6 + 5)/2) = (3.5, 5.5)\nGradient of AB = (5 − 6)/(7 − 0) = −1/7\nGradient of the perpendicular bisector = −1 ÷ (−1/7) = 7\nEquation: y − 5.5 = 7(x − 3.5)\ny = 7x − 24.5 + 5.5\ny = 7x − 19",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::5(b)": "The centre of the circle lies on the perpendicular bisector of every chord.\nPerpendicular bisector of BC, with B(7, 5), C(6, −2):\nMidpoint of BC = ((7 + 6)/2, (5 + (−2))/2) = (6.5, 1.5)\nGradient of BC = (−2 − 5)/(6 − 7) = 7, so the perpendicular gradient is −1/7.\ny − 1.5 = −(1/7)(x − 6.5)\n7y − 10.5 = −x + 6.5\nx + 7y − 17 = 0\nSolve with the perpendicular bisector of AB from (a), y = 7x − 19:\nx + 7(7x − 19) − 17 = 0\nx + 49x − 133 − 17 = 0\n50x = 150\nx = 3, y = 7×3 − 19 = 2\nCentre = (3, 2)\nRadius² = (0 − 3)² + (6 − 2)² = 9 + 16 = 25\nCheck with B: (7 − 3)² + (5 − 2)² = 16 + 9 = 25; with C: (6 − 3)² + (−2 − 2)² = 9 + 16 = 25.\nEquation of the circle: (x − 3)² + (y − 2)² = 25\n(equivalently x² − 6x + y² − 4y = 12)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::6(a)": "y = x² is increasing on 1 ≤ x ≤ 1.5, so the rectangles using the left-hand heights lie below the curve and those using the right-hand heights lie above it.\nLower bound = 0.1(1² + 1.1² + 1.2² + 1.3² + 1.4²)\n= 0.1(1 + 1.21 + 1.44 + 1.69 + 1.96)\n= 0.1 × 7.3 = 0.73\nUpper bound = 0.1(1.1² + 1.2² + 1.3² + 1.4² + 1.5²)\n= 0.1(1.21 + 1.44 + 1.69 + 1.96 + 2.25)\n= 0.1 × 8.55 = 0.855\nSo the lower and upper bounds are 0.73 and 0.855 respectively.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::6(b)": "As δx decreases, the lower bounds increase and the upper bounds decrease, so they are converging towards the true value of A.\nFor the smallest width, 0.784 < A < 0.799.\nTaking the value about half way between the last two bounds:\n(0.784 + 0.799) ÷ 2 = 0.7915\nA ≈ 0.79 (2 s.f.)\nJustification: the upper and lower bounds are converging towards 0.79, and 0.79 is about half way between the last pair of bounds.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::6(c)": "The area is the limit of the sum of the rectangle areas y δx as the width tends to zero:\nA = lim(δx→0) Σ y δx, where the sum runs from x = 1 to x = 1.5\n(written with the limits on the sigma: lower limit x = 1, upper limit 1.5).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::7": "Let f(x) = cos x. From first principles,\nf′(x) = lim(δx→0) [cos(x + δx) − cos x]/δx\nExpand using cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B:\ncos(x + δx) − cos x = cos x cos δx − sin x sin δx − cos x\nSo f′(x) = lim(δx→0) [cos x cos δx − sin x sin δx − cos x]/δx\n= lim(δx→0) [cos x (cos δx − 1)/δx − sin x (sin δx)/δx]\nAs δx → 0, using the small-angle approximations (δx in radians):\ncos δx ≈ 1 − ½(δx)², so (cos δx − 1)/δx ≈ −½δx → 0\nsin δx ≈ δx, so (sin δx)/δx → 1\nHence f′(x) = cos x × 0 − sin x × 1\nd/dx(cos x) = −sin x",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::8(a)": "(n + 1)(n² + bn + c) = n³ + bn² + cn + n² + bn + c\n= n³ + (b + 1)n² + (b + c)n + c\nCompare with n³ + 1:\nn²: b + 1 = 0, so b = −1\nconstant: c = 1\n(check n: b + c = −1 + 1 = 0 ✓)\nb = −1, c = 1, so n³ + 1 ≡ (n + 1)(n² − n + 1)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::8(b)": "From (a), K = n³ + 1 = (n + 1)(n² − n + 1), so n + 1 and n² − n + 1 are both factors of K.\nNeither factor is 1:\nSince n > 2, n + 1 > 3, so n + 1 ≠ 1.\nn² − n + 1 = n(n − 1) + 1 > 1 since n(n − 1) > 0 for n > 2, so n² − n + 1 ≠ 1.\nNeither factor is K: each factor is greater than 1, so each is less than the product K = (n + 1)(n² − n + 1).\nThe factors are distinct:\nAssume the two factors are equal: n² − n + 1 = n + 1\nn² − 2n = 0\nn(n − 2) = 0\nn = 0 or n = 2\nBut n > 2, so both values are impossible. Hence n² − n + 1 ≠ n + 1.\nTherefore n + 1 and n² − n + 1 are two distinct factors of K other than 1 and K, so K has at least two distinct factors other than 1 and K.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::9(a)": "M is the midpoint of BC, where OB→ = b and OC→ = c:\nOM→ = ½(b + c)\nAM→ = OM→ − OA→ = ½(b + c) − a\nSince AX = 2XM, X divides AM in the ratio 2 : 1, so AX→ = ⅔AM→.\nOX→ = OA→ + ⅔AM→\n= a + ⅔(½(b + c) − a)\n= a + ⅓b + ⅓c − ⅔a\n= ⅓a + ⅓b + ⅓c\nOX→ = ⅓(a + b + c)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::9(b)": "F is the vertex of the cuboid diagonally opposite O, so\nOF→ = OA→ + AD→ + DF→ = a + c + b = a + b + c\nFrom (a), OX→ = ⅓(a + b + c) = ⅓OF→.\nSo X lies on the line OF (one third of the way from O to F).\nX also lies on AM, so the lines OF and AM intersect (at X).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::10(a)": "A baby that has had longer to grow before birth would be expected to be heavier, so birth weight is expected to increase with time from conception to birth. The researcher is therefore only looking for positive correlation, so a 1-tail test is appropriate.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::10(b)": "H₀: ρ = 0\nH₁: ρ > 0\nwhere ρ is the population product-moment correlation coefficient between time from conception to birth (T) and birth weight (W).\n1-tail test at the 1% level with n = 11: critical value = 0.6851.\nSample value r = 0.722.\n0.722 > 0.6851, so the result is significant.\nReject H₀.\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 1% level to suggest that there is positive (linear) correlation between the time from conception to birth and the birth weight of new-born babies.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::11(a)": "There is a large number of students at the college, so it would take too long (and be impractical) to obtain information from every student; a sample is much quicker and cheaper.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::11(b)": "Students in different years may well have different music preferences. A sample stratified by school year guarantees that every year group is included, with the number from each year in proportion to the size of that year group, whereas a simple random sample could by chance over- or under-represent some years.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::11(c)": "Using mean ± 2 standard deviations:\nUpper limit = 21.0 + 2 × 4.20 = 29.4\n30 > 29.4, so by this definition 30 is an outlier.\nUsing 1.5 × IQR beyond the quartiles:\nIQR = 22.9 − 18.0 = 4.9\nUpper limit = 22.9 + 1.5 × 4.9 = 22.9 + 7.35 = 30.25\n30 < 30.25, so by this definition 30 is not an outlier.\nThe two definitions disagree, so it is unclear whether 30 should be considered an outlier: it depends on which definition is used.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::11(d)": "Let μ be the population mean time (hours per week) spent listening to music during term by students at the college.\nH₀: μ = 20\nH₁: μ > 20\nOne-tailed test at the 5% level.\nUnder H₀, X̄ ~ N(20, 4.2²/60), and the sample mean is x̄ = 21.\nP(X̄ ≥ 21) = P(Z ≥ (21 − 20)/(4.2/√60)) = P(Z ≥ 1.844) = 0.0326 (3 s.f.)\n0.0326 < 0.05, so the result is significant.\nReject H₀.\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the mean time spent listening to music during term is greater than 20 hours per week, supporting Zac's belief.\n(Equivalently: z = 1.84 > 1.645, or critical value 20 + 1.645 × 4.2/√60 = 20.9 and 21 > 20.9.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::12(a)": "The game ends as soon as one player has won two points. After three points, one player must have won at least two of them, so the game can never last more than three points.\nP(more than three points) = 0",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::12(b)": "The completed tree has 8 branches.\n1st point: Anika (A) 0.7, Beth (B) 0.3.\nAfter A: 2nd point A 0.7 (AA, Anika wins, game over) or B 0.3.\nAfter B: 2nd point A 0.7 or B 0.3 (BB, Beth wins, game over).\nAfter AB: 3rd point A 0.7 (ABA, Anika wins) or B 0.3 (ABB, Beth wins).\nAfter BA: 3rd point A 0.7 (BAA, Anika wins) or B 0.3 (BAB, Beth wins).\nNo branches continue beyond AA, BB or the third point.\nOutcome probabilities:\nAA: 0.7 × 0.7 = 0.49\nABA: 0.7 × 0.3 × 0.7 = 0.147\nABB: 0.7 × 0.3 × 0.3 = 0.063\nBAA: 0.3 × 0.7 × 0.7 = 0.147\nBAB: 0.3 × 0.7 × 0.3 = 0.063\nBB: 0.3 × 0.3 = 0.09\n(total = 1)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::12(c)": "Beth wins via BB, ABB or BAB.\nP(Beth wins) = 0.3 × 0.3 + 0.7 × 0.3 × 0.3 + 0.3 × 0.7 × 0.3\n= 0.09 + 0.063 + 0.063\n= 0.216 = 27/125",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::12(d)": "The game lasts exactly three points when the first two points are split (AB or BA); the third point then decides the game.\nP(exactly three points) = 0.7 × 0.3 + 0.3 × 0.7 = 0.21 + 0.21 = 0.42 = 21/50\n(Alternatively 1 − (0.7² + 0.3²) = 1 − 0.58 = 0.42.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::12(e)": "P(Beth wins and the game has three points) = P(ABB) + P(BAB)\n= 0.7 × 0.3 × 0.3 + 0.3 × 0.7 × 0.3 = 0.063 + 0.063 = 0.126\nP(Beth wins) = 0.216 from (c).\nP(three points | Beth wins) = 0.126/0.216 = 7/12 ≈ 0.583",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::13(a)": "Local Authority A.\nIn a rural area there is likely to be little public transport, so most employees travel by private motorised transport. A has the largest proportion using private motorised transport (about three quarters) and a very small proportion using public transport, which fits a rural area where people need cars to travel the long distances. (Hills would also discourage cycling.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::13(b)": "The four Local Authorities have different numbers of employees. Pie charts show the proportion of employees using each method of travel, so the authorities can be compared directly; bar charts of the numbers would not show proportions, making comparison between authorities of different sizes difficult.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::13(c)(i)": "C and D.\nThey have by far the largest proportions of employees using public transport (about a fifth in C and about half in D), which is typical of urban areas with good public transport networks.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::13(c)(ii)": "Disagree.\nIn the census data the method of travel recorded is the one used for the longest part (by distance) of the usual journey to work. Park-and-Ride users drive most of the way, to the edge of the urban area, and only take the bus for the short final part into the centre, so most of them would still report private motorised transport. The proportions in the 2001 and 2011 pie charts would therefore not change noticeably, so comparing them would not identify which Local Authority introduced the scheme.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::14(a)": "The probabilities must sum to 1:\nk/1 + k/2 + k/3 + k/4 = 1\nk(1 + ½ + ⅓ + ¼) = 1\nk(12/12 + 6/12 + 4/12 + 3/12) = 1\nk × 25/12 = 1\nk = 12/25",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::14(b)": "Using k = 12/25, P(X = x) = 12/(25x):\nx:          1      2      3      4\nP(X = x):   12/25  6/25   4/25   3/25",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::14(c)": "Since X₂ + X₃ ≥ 2, we need X₁ = 3 or 4.\nX₁ = 3: X₂ + X₃ < 3, so (X₂, X₃) = (1, 1).\nX₁ = 4: X₂ + X₃ < 4, so (X₂, X₃) = (1, 1), (2, 1) or (1, 2).\nOutcomes (X₁, X₂, X₃): (3, 1, 1), (4, 1, 1), (4, 2, 1), (4, 1, 2).\nP = (4/25)(12/25)² + (3/25)(12/25)² + (3/25)(6/25)(12/25) + (3/25)(12/25)(6/25)\n= 576/15625 + 432/15625 + 216/15625 + 216/15625\n= 1440/15625\n= 288/3125 = 0.09216",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p2::14(d)": "Every observation is at least 1, so the running total strictly increases; the total first equals 7 on the 5th observation exactly when the first five observations sum to 7.\nFive values from {1, 2, 3, 4} with sum 7 (i.e. 2 more than the minimum 5):\n(1, 1, 1, 1, 3) in any order: 5 arrangements\n(1, 1, 1, 2, 2) in any order: ⁵C₂ = 10 arrangements\nP = (12/25)⁴ × (4/25) × 5 + (12/25)³ × (6/25)² × 10\n= 414720/9765625 + 622080/9765625\n= 1036800/9765625\n= 41472/390625 ≈ 0.106 (0.10616832)",






































 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::1": "Boundaries:\n• y = x² is a ∪-shaped parabola, symmetrical about the y-axis, with its vertex at the origin O.\n• x + y = 2 (i.e. y = 2 − x) is a straight line with negative gradient −1, crossing the y-axis at (0, 2) and the x-axis at (2, 0).\n• x = 0 is the y-axis.\n\nWhere the line meets the parabola (for x ≥ 0):\nx² = 2 − x ⇒ x² + x − 2 = 0 ⇒ (x + 2)(x − 1) = 0 ⇒ x = 1 (since x ≥ 0), so the point is (1, 1).\n\nWhich side of each boundary:\n• y ≥ x²: on or above the parabola.\n• x + y ≤ 2: on or below the line.\n• x ≥ 0: on or to the right of the y-axis.\n\nSketch (described): draw the x- and y-axes, the parabola y = x² through O, and the line through (0, 2) and (2, 0). The line cuts the right-hand branch of the parabola at (1, 1).\nThe required region R is the small curved triangle with vertices O(0, 0), (0, 2) and (1, 1): it is bounded on the left by the y-axis from O to (0, 2), above by the line from (0, 2) down to (1, 1), and below/right by the parabola from (1, 1) back down to O. Label this region R.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::2(a)": "Use the cosine rule in triangle ABC, with angle A = 60° opposite BC:\np² = AB² + AC² − 2(AB)(AC)cos A\np² = (4 + h)² + (4 − h)² − 2(4 + h)(4 − h)cos 60°\n\nSince cos 60° = ½:\np² = (16 + 8h + h²) + (16 − 8h + h²) − (16 − h²)\np² = 32 + 2h² − 16 + h²\np² = 16 + 3h²   (as required)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::2(b)": "From (a), p = (16 + 3h²)^(1/2) (p > 0 as it is a length).\n\nTake out a factor of 16:\np = 16^(1/2)(1 + (3/16)h²)^(1/2) = 4(1 + (3/16)h²)^(1/2)\n\nExpand with the binomial series (1 + x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)x²/2! + …, with n = ½ and x = (3/16)h² (valid when h is small, since |(3/16)h²| < 1):\n(1 + (3/16)h²)^(1/2) ≈ 1 + ½(3/16)h² + [(½)(−½)/2!]((3/16)h²)²\n= 1 + (3/32)h² − (1/8)(9/256)h⁴\n= 1 + (3/32)h² − (9/2048)h⁴\n\nMultiply by 4:\np ≈ 4 + (3/8)h² − (9/512)h⁴\n\nSo λ = 3/8 and μ = −9/512.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::3": "The AP has first term 2 and common difference d, so\n1st term = 2, 3rd term = 2 + 2d, 13th term = 2 + 12d.\n\nThese are the 1st, 2nd and 3rd terms of a GP with first term 2 and common ratio r:\n2 + 2d = 2r   … (1)\n2 + 12d = 2r²   … (2)\n\nFrom (1), r = 1 + d. Substitute into (2):\n2 + 12d = 2(1 + d)²\n1 + 6d = 1 + 2d + d²\nd² − 4d = 0\nd(d − 4) = 0\n\nSince d ≠ 0, d = 4.\n\nCheck: the terms are 2, 10, 50, which form a GP with ratio 5.\n\nThe common difference d = 4 is positive, so each term is 4 more than the one before (2, 6, 10, 14, …). Hence the arithmetic progression is an increasing sequence.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::4(a)": "y = |x − 1|:\nA V-shaped graph with its vertex at (1, 0) on the positive x-axis (it touches but does not cross the axis there). It crosses the y-axis at (0, 1). For x ≥ 1 it is the line y = x − 1 (gradient 1) and for x ≤ 1 it is the line y = 1 − x (gradient −1).\n\ny = k/x with k < 0:\nA reciprocal curve with two branches, the x-axis and y-axis as asymptotes, and it never meets either axis.\n• For x < 0, y = k/x > 0, so one branch is in the 2nd quadrant: y → +∞ as x → 0⁻ and y → 0⁺ as x → −∞.\n• For x > 0, y = k/x < 0, so the other branch is in the 4th quadrant: y → −∞ as x → 0⁺ and y → 0⁻ as x → +∞.\n\nSketch (described): on one pair of axes draw the V with vertex at (1, 0) and y-intercept 1, together with the two hyperbola branches in the top-left and bottom-right quadrants. The top-left branch crosses the left arm of the V exactly once (at a negative value of x); the bottom-right branch stays below the x-axis and never meets the V.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::4(b)": "For x ≠ 0, multiplying |x − 1| = k/x by x gives x|x − 1| = k, and x = 0 is not a root of x|x − 1| = k (since k ≠ 0). So the real roots of x|x − 1| = k are exactly the x-coordinates of the points where the graphs y = |x − 1| and y = k/x meet.\n\nFrom the sketch in (a), for any negative k the graphs meet at exactly one point: the 4th-quadrant branch of y = k/x lies below the x-axis while |x − 1| ≥ 0, so there is no intersection for x > 0; in the 2nd quadrant, k/x falls from +∞ towards 0 while |x − 1| = 1 − x rises from 1 upwards, so they cross exactly once.\n\nTherefore x|x − 1| = k has exactly one real root for any negative value of k.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::4(c)": "Here k = −6 < 0. From (b), the single intersection is on the branch of y = k/x with x < 0, where x − 1 < 0, so |x − 1| = 1 − x.\n\nSo the equation becomes\nx(1 − x) = −6\nx − x² = −6\nx² − x − 6 = 0\n(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0\nx = 3 or x = −2\n\nThe root must be negative (and x = 3 gives 3|3 − 1| = 6 ≠ −6), so reject x = 3.\n\nCheck: −2|−2 − 1| = −2 × 3 = −6 ✓\n\nThe real root is x = −2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::5(a)": "R cos(t − α) = R cos t cos α + R sin t sin α\n\nCompare with 12 cos t + 5 sin t:\nR cos α = 12\nR sin α = 5\n\nR² = 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169, so R = 13 (R > 0).\n\ntan α = 5/12, so α = arctan(5/12) = 0.394791… = 0.3948 (4 s.f.), which lies in 0 < α < ½π.\n\nv = 13 cos(t − 0.3948)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::5(b)": "Speed 3 m s⁻¹ in either direction means v = 3 or v = −3:\n13 cos(t − 0.3948) = ±3\ncos(t − 0.3948) = ±3/13\n\nCase cos(t − 0.3948) = 3/13:\nt − 0.3948 = arccos(3/13) = 1.33793…\nt = 1.73272… (the other principal solution, t − 0.3948 = −1.33793…, gives t < 0)\n\nCase cos(t − 0.3948) = −3/13:\nt − 0.3948 = arccos(−3/13) = 1.80366…\nt = 2.19846…\n\n(Any other solutions are larger, e.g. t − 0.3948 = 2π − 1.80366…)\n\nThe two smallest positive values are t = 1.73 and t = 2.20 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::6(a)": "Let f(x) = 6 arcsin(2x − 1) − x², which is continuous for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 (arcsin is evaluated in radians).\n\nf(0.5) = 6 arcsin(0) − 0.5² = 0 − 0.25 = −0.25 < 0\nf(0.6) = 6 arcsin(0.2) − 0.6² = 6(0.201358…) − 0.36 = 0.8481… > 0\n\nf is continuous on [0.5, 0.6] and there is a change of sign, so the root lies between 0.5 and 0.6.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::6(b)": "6 arcsin(2x − 1) − x² = 0\narcsin(2x − 1) = (1/6)x²\n2x − 1 = sin((1/6)x²)\nx = ½ + ½ sin((1/6)x²)\n\nSo the iterative formula is xₙ₊₁ = ½ + ½ sin((1/6)xₙ²), giving\np = ½, q = ½, r = 1/6.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::6(c)": "Using xₙ₊₁ = ½ + ½ sin(xₙ²/6) (calculator in radians) with x₀ = 0.5:\nx₁ = 0.520827…\nx₂ = 0.522597…\nx₃ = 0.522751…\nx₄ = 0.522765…\nx₅ = 0.522766…\n\nThe iterates have settled to 0.5228 to 4 s.f.\n\nRoot = 0.5228 (4 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::7(a)": "The gradient of the line joining (3, 2) to P(x, y) is (y − 2)/(x − 3).\n\nThe gradient of the tangent at P is dy/dx, which is three times this:\ndy/dx = 3(y − 2)/(x − 3)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::7(b)": "Separate the variables in dy/dx = 3(y − 2)/(x − 3):\n∫ 1/(y − 2) dy = ∫ 3/(x − 3) dx\n\nSince y > 2 and x > 3 on C, no modulus signs are needed:\nln(y − 2) = 3 ln(x − 3) + c\n\nAt (4, 3): ln(1) = 3 ln(1) + c ⇒ 0 = 0 + c ⇒ c = 0.\n\nSo ln(y − 2) = 3 ln(x − 3) = ln((x − 3)³)\ny − 2 = (x − 3)³\n\ny = (x − 3)³ + 2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::7(c)": "y = (x − 3)³ + 2 is obtained from y = x³ by replacing x with x − 3 and adding 2 to y.\n\nThe transformation is a translation by the vector (3, 2) (column vector with 3 on top and 2 below), i.e. 3 units in the positive x-direction and 2 units in the positive y-direction.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::8(a)": "y = xe^(−2x)\n\nBy the product rule:\ny′ = e^(−2x) + x(−2e^(−2x)) = e^(−2x)(1 − 2x)\n\nDifferentiate again (product rule):\ny″ = −2e^(−2x)(1 − 2x) + e^(−2x)(−2)\n= e^(−2x)(−2 + 4x − 2)\n= e^(−2x)(4x − 4)\n\nAt x = 1: y″ = e^(−2)(4 − 4) = 0.\n\nCheck the sign of y″ either side of x = 1:\ny″(0.5) = e^(−1)(2 − 4) = −2e^(−1) < 0\ny″(1.5) = e^(−3)(6 − 4) = 2e^(−3) > 0\n\n(Since e^(−2x) > 0, the sign of y″ is the sign of 4x − 4, which changes from negative to positive at x = 1.)\n\ny″ = 0 at x = 1 and y″ changes sign there, so M has a point of inflection at P, where x = 1 (P is (1, e^(−2))).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::8(b)": "P is the point (1, e^(−2)), so L is the line y = e^(−2)x.\n\nArea under M from x = 0 to x = 1, using integration by parts with u = x, dv/dx = e^(−2x) (so du/dx = 1, v = −½e^(−2x)):\n∫ xe^(−2x) dx = −½xe^(−2x) + ½∫ e^(−2x) dx\n= −½xe^(−2x) − ¼e^(−2x) + c\n\n∫₀¹ xe^(−2x) dx = [−½xe^(−2x) − ¼e^(−2x)]₀¹\n= (−½e^(−2) − ¼e^(−2)) − (0 − ¼)\n= ¼ − ¾e^(−2)\n\nArea under L from x = 0 to x = 1 is a triangle with base 1 and height e^(−2):\n½ × 1 × e^(−2) = ½e^(−2)\n\nSince M lies above L for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1:\nArea of R = (¼ − ¾e^(−2)) − ½e^(−2)\n= ¼ − (5/4)e^(−2)\n= ¼(1 − 5e^(−2))\n\nSo a = 1 and b = −5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::9(a)": "From B to C: u = 14, v = 18, t = 20.\nv = u + at\n18 = 14 + 20a\na = 4/20 = 0.2 m s⁻²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::9(b)": "From A to B (same constant acceleration): a = 0.2, s = 330, v = 14 (speed at B), u = speed at A.\nv² = u² + 2as\n14² = u² + 2(0.2)(330)\n196 = u² + 132\nu² = 64\nu = 8\n\nThe speed of the car at A is 8 m s⁻¹.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::10(a)": "The completed force diagram shows exactly four forces acting on D:\n• the given 15 N force, pushing down into D at angle θ below the horizontal (its horizontal component acts to the right, towards the direction the arrow points);\n• the weight, 50 N, acting vertically downwards;\n• the normal reaction R, acting vertically upwards from the surface;\n• the friction force F, acting horizontally along the surface to the left, opposing the horizontal component of the 15 N force.\n\nNo other forces should be added.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::10(b)": "D is at rest in equilibrium.\n\nResolve horizontally:\nF = 15 cos θ\n\nResolve vertically (the 15 N force has a downward component 15 sin θ):\nR = 50 + 15 sin θ\n\nSince D stays at rest, friction satisfies F ≤ μR with μ = 0.2 = 1/5:\n15 cos θ ≤ (1/5)(15 sin θ + 50)\n15 cos θ ≤ 3 sin θ + 10\n\n15 cos θ − 3 sin θ ≤ 10   (as required)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::11(a)": "Take upwards as positive, with g = 10 m s⁻².\nVertical motion: initial vertical velocity = 25 sin 15° = 6.4704… m s⁻¹, acceleration −10 m s⁻², displacement at B = −4 m.\n\ns = ut + ½at²\n−4 = (25 sin 15°)t − ½(10)t²\n5t² − (25 sin 15°)t − 4 = 0\n5t² − 6.4704…t − 4 = 0\n\nt = [6.4704… ± √(6.4704…² + 80)] / 10\nt = 1.7509… or t = −0.4569…\n\nTime must be positive, so the time from A to B is t = 1.75 s (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::11(b)": "Horizontal velocity is constant: 25 cos 15° = 24.148… m s⁻¹.\n\nHorizontal distance = (25 cos 15°) × t = 24.148… × 1.7509…\n= 42.28…\n\nAB horizontal distance = 42.3 m (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::11(c)": "At t = 1.5 s:\nHorizontal velocity: vₕ = 25 cos 15° = 24.148… m s⁻¹\nVertical velocity (upwards positive): v_v = 25 sin 15° − 10(1.5) = 6.4704… − 15 = −8.5295… m s⁻¹\n\nThe vertical component is negative, so the ball is moving downwards.\n\nThe direction of motion makes an angle θ with the horizontal, where\ntan θ = 8.5295… / 24.148… = 0.35321…\nθ = 19.45…°\n\nThe ball is moving at 19.5° below the horizontal (in the direction from A towards B).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::11(d)": "The model ignores the effect of the wind: a following wind (blowing in the direction from A to B) would carry the ball further horizontally than predicted.\n\n(Other acceptable factors: the value g = 10 m s⁻² is not accurate; backspin on the ball, which gives it lift, is ignored.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::12(a)": "Let the tension at D be T N, so the tension at C is 3T N. Let the centre of mass G be x̄ m from A.\n\nResolve vertically:\n3T + T = 20g ⇒ 4T = 20g ⇒ T = 5g, so T_D = 5g and T_C = 15g.\n\nD is 4 − 0.7 = 3.3 m from A, so CD = 3.3 − 0.5 = 2.8 m.\n\nTake moments about C:\n20g(x̄ − 0.5) = T_D × 2.8\n20g(x̄ − 0.5) = 5g × 2.8 = 14g\nx̄ − 0.5 = 0.7\nx̄ = 1.2\n\nThe centre of mass is 1.2 m from A.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::12(b)": "The particle's weight mg has moment 3.5mg about C, so it is 3.5 m from C. Since AC = 0.5 m, it is either 0.5 + 3.5 = 4 m from A (at end B) or 3.5 m to the left of C (beyond A, off the beam). So the particle is at B, which is 0.7 m beyond D.\n\nThe particle's weight tends to tip the beam about D, reducing the tension at C. The largest m occurs when the beam is on the point of tilting, i.e. T_C = 0.\n\nThe centre of mass is 3.3 − 1.2 = 2.1 m from D (on the C side), i.e. 4 − 1.2 − 0.7 = 2.1 m.\n\nTake moments about D with T_C = 0:\nmg × 0.7 = 20g × 2.1\n0.7m = 42\nm = 60\n\nThe largest possible value of m is 60.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::13(a)": "Resultant force:\nF = (−8i − 54j) + (4ti + 6(2t − 1)²j)\nF = (4t − 8)i + (6(2t − 1)² − 54)j = (4t − 8)i + 6((2t − 1)² − 9)j\n\nEquilibrium requires both components to be zero.\ni-component: 4t − 8 = 0 ⇒ t = 2\nj-component: (2t − 1)² = 9 ⇒ 2t − 1 = ±3 ⇒ t = 2 or t = −1 (rejected since t ≥ 0)\n\nBoth components are zero only when t = 2, so the forces are in equilibrium when t = 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::13(b)": "Newton's second law, F = 2a:\na = (2t − 4)i + 3((2t − 1)² − 9)j = (2t − 4)i + (3(2t − 1)² − 27)j\n\nIntegrate with respect to t:\nv = (t² − 4t)i + (½(2t − 1)³ − 27t)j + c\n[since ∫ 3(2t − 1)² dt = 3 × (2t − 1)³/(3 × 2) = ½(2t − 1)³]\n\nP is at rest when t = 0:\n0 = 0i + (½(−1)³ − 0)j + c ⇒ c = ½j\n\nv = (t² − 4t)i + (½(2t − 1)³ − 27t + ½)j\n(which expands to v = (t² − 4t)i + (4t³ − 6t² − 24t)j)\n\nMoving due north means the i-component is zero (and the j-component positive):\nt² − 4t = 0 ⇒ t(t − 4) = 0 ⇒ t = 0 or t = 4\nAt t = 0, P is at rest, so t = 4.\n\nAt t = 4: j-component = 4(64) − 6(16) − 24(4) = 256 − 96 − 96 = 64 > 0, so P is indeed moving north.\nv = 64j\n\nThe speed of P is 64 m s⁻¹.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::13(c)": "Using v = (t² − 4t)i + (½(2t − 1)³ − 27t + ½)j from (b), integrate with respect to t (no constant is needed, since only the difference in position is required):\nr = (⅓t³ − 2t²)i + ((1/16)(2t − 1)⁴ − (27/2)t² + ½t)j\n[since ∫ ½(2t − 1)³ dt = ½ × (2t − 1)⁴/(4 × 2) = (1/16)(2t − 1)⁴]\n(equivalently r = (⅓t³ − 2t²)i + (t⁴ − 2t³ − 12t² + 1/16)j)\n\nAt t = 0: r = 0i + (1/16)j = (1/16)j\nAt t = 3: i-component = 9 − 18 = −9\nj-component = (1/16)(5)⁴ − (27/2)(9) + 3/2 = 625/16 − 243/2 + 3/2 = 625/16 − 120 = −1295/16\nSo r = −9i − (1295/16)j\n\nDisplacement from t = 0 to t = 3:\n−9i + (−1295/16 − 1/16)j = −9i − 81j\n\nDistance = √((−9)² + (−81)²) = √(81 + 6561) = √6642 = 9√82 = 81.498…\n\nThe distance between the positions is 81.5 m (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::14(a)": "Let the tension in the string joining A and B be T₁ and in the string joining B and C be T₂. C moves down Π, so A moves up its plane and B moves towards C's pulley, all with acceleration a.\n\nN2L for A (2 kg) parallel to its smooth 30° plane, up the plane:\nT₁ − 2g sin 30° = 2a   … (1)\n\nN2L for C (4 kg) parallel to the smooth 60° plane Π, down the plane:\n4g sin 60° − T₂ = 4a   … (2)\n\nB (3 kg) on the rough horizontal plane: vertically, R = 3g, so friction (limiting, since B is moving) is F = μR = 3gμ, opposing the motion.\nN2L for B horizontally:\nT₂ − T₁ − 3gμ = 3a   … (3)\n\nAdd (1), (2) and (3) to eliminate both tensions:\n4g sin 60° − 2g sin 30° − 3gμ = 9a\n9a = g(4 sin 60° − 2 sin 30° − 3μ)\n9a = g(2√3 − 1 − 3μ)   [since 4 sin 60° = 2√3 and 2 sin 30° = 1]\n\nThis is the equation in μ, a and g. Rearranging:\nμ = ⅓(2√3 − 1 − 9a/g)\n\nThe horizontal plane is rough, so μ > 0:\n⅓(2√3 − 1 − 9a/g) > 0\n2√3 − 1 > 9a/g\na < (1/9)g(2√3 − 1)   (as required)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2021a-p3::14(b)": "Substitute a = (1/9)g into 9a = g(2√3 − 1 − 3μ):\ng = g(2√3 − 1 − 3μ)\n1 = 2√3 − 1 − 3μ\n3μ = 2√3 − 2\nμ = ⅔(√3 − 1) = 0.4880…\n\nForces on B from the plane:\nNormal reaction R = 3g = 3(9.8) = 29.4 N\nFriction F_B = μR = ⅔(√3 − 1)(3g) = 2(√3 − 1)g = 14.348… N\n\nThe contact force is the resultant of the normal reaction and friction, which are perpendicular:\nContact force = √((3g)² + F_B²) = √(29.4² + 14.348…²) = √(864.36 + 205.87…) = √1070.2… = 32.714…\n\nThe magnitude of the contact force is 32.7 N (3 s.f.).",



































 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::1(a)": "Strip width h = (3 − 1)/4 = 0.5, so the ordinates are at x = 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3.\n\nx = 1: y₀ = √(1 − 1) = 0\nx = 1.5: y₁ = √(2.25 − 1) = √1.25 = √5/2 ≈ 1.1180\nx = 2: y₂ = √(4 − 1) = √3 ≈ 1.7321\nx = 2.5: y₃ = √(6.25 − 1) = √5.25 = √21/2 ≈ 2.2913\nx = 3: y₄ = √(9 − 1) = √8 = 2√2 ≈ 2.8284\n\n∫₁³ √(x² − 1) dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + y₄) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)}\n= 0.5 × 0.5 × {(0 + 2.8284) + 2(1.1180 + 1.7321 + 2.2913)}\n= 0.25 × {2.8284 + 2 × 5.1414}\n= 0.25 × 13.1112\n= 3.2778…\n\nEstimate = 3.28 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::1(b)": "It is an under-estimate.\n\nThe curve y = √(x² − 1) is concave (its gradient decreases) between x = 1 and x = 3, so each chord joining the top corners of a strip lies below the curve. The tops of the trapezia are therefore below the curve, and the total area of the trapezia is less than the true area under the curve.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::1(c)": "Use more trapezia (more strips), each of smaller width, between the same limits x = 1 and x = 3. The chords then follow the curve more closely, so the estimate is more accurate.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::2(a)": "Take a = 1 and b = −2.\n\nThen a > b, since 1 > −2.\nBut a² = 1² = 1 and b² = (−2)² = 4, so a² < b² (1 < 4).\n\nSo a > b does not imply a² > b², and the statement is false.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::2(b)(i)": "The symbol ⇔ claims the implication works in both directions. It is true that x° = 30° ⇒ sin x° = 0.5, but sin x° = 0.5 does not imply x° = 30°, because there are other solutions: for example sin 150° = 0.5 as well (and also 390°, −210°, …).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::2(b)(ii)": "Only the implication from right to left is true, so the corrected statement is\n\nsin x° = 0.5 ⇐ x° = 30°\n\n(equivalently, x° = 30° ⇒ sin x° = 0.5).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::2(c)": "Let n be an integer. Four consecutive multiples of 4 can be written as\n\n4n, 4n + 4, 4n + 8, 4n + 12.\n\nTheir sum is\n4n + (4n + 4) + (4n + 8) + (4n + 12) = 16n + 24 = 8(2n + 3).\n\nSince n is an integer, 2n + 3 is an integer, so 8(2n + 3) is a multiple of 8.\n\nHence the sum of four consecutive multiples of 4 is always a multiple of 8.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::3(a)": "At the points of intersection the y-values are equal:\n\nx² − 2x + 1 = −x² + 6x − 5\n2x² − 8x + 6 = 0\nx² − 4x + 3 = 0\n(x − 1)(x − 3) = 0\nx = 1 or x = 3\n\nSubstitute into y = x² − 2x + 1 = (x − 1)²:\nx = 1: y = 0\nx = 3: y = (3 − 1)² = 4\n\n(Check in the other curve: x = 1 gives −1 + 6 − 5 = 0; x = 3 gives −9 + 18 − 5 = 4.)\n\nThe curves intersect at (1, 0) and (3, 4).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::3(b)": "The line y = 2x − 2 has gradient 2 (positive) and y-intercept −2 (below the origin), and it crosses the x-axis at x = 1.\n\nIt passes through both intersection points found in (a):\nx = 1: y = 2(1) − 2 = 0, so it passes through (1, 0);\nx = 3: y = 2(3) − 2 = 4, so it passes through (3, 4).\n\nSketch: draw a straight line rising from left to right, cutting the y-axis at (0, −2), passing through (1, 0) where the two parabolas meet on the x-axis, and through (3, 4) where they meet again, continuing beyond (3, 4). Between x = 1 and x = 3 the line lies between the two curves, splitting the region enclosed by the parabolas into two parts.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::3(c)": "y ≥ x² − 2x + 1 means on or above the U-shaped curve;\ny ≤ −x² + 6x − 5 means on or below the ∩-shaped curve;\ny ≤ 2x − 2 means on or below the line.\n\nThe first two together give the region enclosed between the two parabolas for 1 ≤ x ≤ 3. The line y = 2x − 2 joins (1, 0) and (3, 4) and splits this region into two parts; the condition y ≤ 2x − 2 selects the lower part.\n\nFor 1 ≤ x ≤ 3, (x − 1)² ≤ 2x − 2 ≤ −x² + 6x − 5, so the line lies between the curves throughout.\n\nSketch: shade the thin region between the line y = 2x − 2 (above) and the curve y = x² − 2x + 1 (below), from (1, 0) to (3, 4), and label it R.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::4(a)": "2x² + 6x + 7 = 2(x² + 3x) + 7\n= 2[(x + 1.5)² − 2.25] + 7\n= 2(x + 1.5)² − 4.5 + 7\n= 2(x + 1.5)² + 2.5\n\nSo p = 2, q = 1.5, r = 2.5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::4(b)": "From (a), y = 2(x + 1.5)² + 2.5.\n\nSince 2(x + 1.5)² ≥ 0, the least value of y is 2.5, when x + 1.5 = 0, i.e. x = −1.5.\n\nMinimum point: (−1.5, 2.5)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::4(c)": "Replace x by tan θ in (a):\n\n2tan²θ + 6tanθ + 7 = 2(tanθ + 1.5)² + 2.5\n\ntan θ takes all real values, so the minimum value is 2.5, which occurs when tan θ + 1.5 = 0, i.e. tan θ = −1.5.\n\ntan θ = −1.5 ⇒ principal value θ = tan⁻¹(−1.5) = −56.3°.\ntan has period 180°, so the smallest positive solution is θ = −56.3° + 180° = 123.7°.\n\nMinimum value = 2.5, first occurring at θ = 124° (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::5(a)(i)": "2^(x+4) = f(x + 4) where f(x) = 2ˣ, and replacing x by x + 4 moves the graph 4 units to the left.\n\nThe translation is 4 units in the negative x-direction, i.e. a translation by the vector (−4, 0) (that is, −4 horizontally, 0 vertically).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::5(a)(ii)": "2^(x+4) = 2⁴ × 2ˣ = 16 × 2ˣ, so every y-value is multiplied by 16.\n\nThe transformation is a stretch parallel to the y-axis (in the y-direction) with scale factor 16.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::5(b)": "The logarithms are defined only if 8x > 0 and 1 − x > 0, i.e. 0 < x < 1.\n\nlog₂(8x) = 1 − log₂(1 − x)\nlog₂(8x) + log₂(1 − x) = 1\nlog₂(8x(1 − x)) = 1\n8x(1 − x) = 2¹ = 2\n8x − 8x² = 2\n8x² − 8x + 2 = 0\n4x² − 4x + 1 = 0\n(2x − 1)² = 0\nx = 0.5\n\nCheck: x = 0.5 lies in 0 < x < 1. Then log₂(8 × 0.5) = log₂4 = 2 and 1 − log₂(0.5) = 1 − (−1) = 2. ✓\n\nx = 0.5 (the only solution)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::6(a)": "(3 + 2x)⁵ = 3⁵ + ⁵C₁ 3⁴(2x) + ⁵C₂ 3³(2x)² + ⁵C₃ 3²(2x)³ + …\n= 243 + 5 × 81 × 2x + 10 × 27 × 4x² + 10 × 9 × 8x³ + …\n= 243 + 810x + 1080x² + 720x³ + …",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::6(b)": "Write 3 + 2y + 4y² = 3 + 2(y + 2y²), so put x = y + 2y² in the expansion from (a):\n\n(3 + 2y + 4y²)⁵ = 243 + 810(y + 2y²) + 1080(y + 2y²)² + 720(y + 2y²)³ + (terms in (y + 2y²)⁴ and (y + 2y²)⁵)\n\nThe terms in (y + 2y²)⁴ and (y + 2y²)⁵ contain only powers y⁴ and higher, and 810(y + 2y²) has no y³ term. So the y³ terms come from:\n\n1080(y + 2y²)² = 1080(y² + 4y³ + 4y⁴): y³ term = 1080 × 4y³ = 4320y³\n720(y + 2y²)³ = 720(y³ + …): y³ term = 720y³\n\nTotal: 4320y³ + 720y³ = 5040y³\n\nCoefficient of y³ = 5040",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::7": "Differentiate 2x³ + 6xy − 3y² = 2 implicitly with respect to x (product rule on 6xy):\n\n6x² + 6y + 6x(dy/dx) − 6y(dy/dx) = 0\n\nA tangent parallel to y = x has gradient 1, so put dy/dx = 1:\n\n6x² + 6y + 6x − 6y = 0\n6x² + 6x = 0\nx² + x = 0\nx(x + 1) = 0\nx = 0 or x = −1\n\nAny such point must also lie on the curve.\n\nx = 0: 2(0) + 0 − 3y² = 2 ⇒ y² = −2/3. But y² ≥ 0 for every real y, so there is no point on the curve with x = 0.\n\nx = −1: 2(−1) + 6(−1)y − 3y² = 2 ⇒ −2 − 6y − 3y² = 2 ⇒ 3y² + 6y + 4 = 0.\nDiscriminant b² − 4ac = 6² − 4(3)(4) = 36 − 48 = −12 < 0, so this quadratic has no real roots, and there is no point on the curve with x = −1.\n\nHence there are no points on the curve where the gradient is 1, i.e. no points where the tangent is parallel to y = x.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::8(a)": "For exponential decay, the mass is multiplied by the same factor in each equal time interval.\n\nFrom t = 0 to t = 10: 750/1250 = 0.6, so the mass is multiplied by 0.6 every 10 minutes.\n\nMissing t: 750 × 0.6 = 450, so m = 450 occurs 10 minutes after t = 10, i.e. t = 20.\n\nMissing m: from t = 20 to t = 50 is three more 10-minute intervals, so\nm = 450 × 0.6³ = 450 × 0.216 = 97.2\n\n(Equivalently, m = 1250e^(kt) with k = ln(0.6)/10 = −0.0511 gives t = 20 for m = 450 and m = 1250 × 0.6⁵ = 97.2 at t = 50.)\n\nMissing values: t = 20 (minutes), m = 97.2 (grams).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::8(b)(i)": "The original mass is 160e⁰ = 160 g, so half of it is 80 g.\n\n160e^(−0.055t) = 80\ne^(−0.055t) = 0.5\n−0.055t = ln 0.5\nt = ln 0.5 / (−0.055) = 12.6026…\n\nt = 12.6 minutes",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::8(b)(ii)": "m = 160e^(−0.055t)\n\ndm/dt = 160 × (−0.055)e^(−0.055t) = −8.8e^(−0.055t)\n\nAt t = 15:\ndm/dt = −8.8e^(−0.055 × 15) = −8.8e^(−0.825) = −3.856…\n\nThe mass is decreasing, so the rate of decay is 3.86 grams per minute.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::8(c)": "Model substance A as m = 1250e^(kt). From t = 10, m = 750: e^(10k) = 0.6, so k = ln(0.6)/10 = −0.0511.\n\nFor A: dm/dt = 1250k e^(kt) = −63.9e^(−0.0511t)\nAt t = 15: dm/dt = −63.9e^(−0.0511 × 15) = −29.7, so A is decaying at 29.7 g/min.\n\nFor B at t = 15 (from (b)(ii)), the rate of decay is 3.86 g/min.\n\n29.7 > 3.86, so substance A is decaying at a faster rate (in grams per minute).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::9": "x = 2sinθ ⇒ dx/dθ = 2cosθ, so dx = 2cosθ dθ.\n\nLimits: x = 1 ⇒ sinθ = ½ ⇒ θ = π/6;  x = √3 ⇒ sinθ = √3/2 ⇒ θ = π/3.\n\n√(4 − x²) = √(4 − 4sin²θ) = √(4cos²θ) = 2cosθ (cosθ > 0 for π/6 ≤ θ ≤ π/3).\n\n∫₁^√3 √(4 − x²) dx = ∫_(π/6)^(π/3) 2cosθ × 2cosθ dθ\n= ∫_(π/6)^(π/3) 4cos²θ dθ\n\nUsing cos2θ = 2cos²θ − 1, 4cos²θ = 2cos2θ + 2:\n= ∫_(π/6)^(π/3) (2cos2θ + 2) dθ\n= [sin2θ + 2θ]_(π/6)^(π/3)\n= (sin(2π/3) + 2π/3) − (sin(π/3) + π/3)\n= (½√3 + ⅔π) − (½√3 + ⅓π)\n= ⅓π\n\nHence ∫₁^√3 √(4 − x²) dx = π/3, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::10(a)": "Let the radius be r, so OA = OB = r and OM = ½r.\n\nArea of triangle OMB = ½ × OM × OB × sinθ = ½(½r)(r)sinθ = ¼r²sinθ\nArea of sector OAB = ½r²θ\nArea of MAB = sector OAB − triangle OMB = ½r²θ − ¼r²sinθ\n\nOMB : MAB = 2 : 3, so 3 × area OMB = 2 × area MAB:\n3(¼r²sinθ) = 2(½r²θ − ¼r²sinθ)\n¾r²sinθ = r²θ − ½r²sinθ\n\nDivide by r² (r ≠ 0):\n¾sinθ = θ − ½sinθ\nθ = ¾sinθ + ½sinθ = 5/4 sinθ\n\nθ = 1.25sinθ, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::10(b)": "θₙ₊₁ = 1.25sinθₙ, θ₁ = 0.5 (working in radians):\n\nθ₂ = 1.25sin(0.5) = 0.599 (0.59928…)\nθ₃ = 1.25sin(0.59928…) = 0.705 (0.70506…)\nθ₄ = 1.25sin(0.70506…) = 0.810 (0.81010…)\n\nContinuing the iteration, the values increase and settle at 1.1311…, so\n\nroot = 1.13 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::10(c)": "The root is where the curve y = 1.25sinθ meets the line y = θ.\n\nDiagram (described): on the given graph, draw the straight line y = θ from the origin; it crosses the curve y = 1.25sinθ at the root θ ≈ 1.13, which is before θ = ½π. Between 0 and the root the curve lies above the line.\n\nMark θ₁ = 0.5 on the θ-axis. Draw a vertical line from θ₁ up to the curve (height 1.25sin0.5 = θ₂), then a horizontal line across to y = θ (this is at θ = θ₂). Then go vertically up to the curve again, horizontally to the line, and so on. The path climbs in steps between the line and the curve, getting smaller each time, moving towards the intersection point.\n\nThis is staircase convergence.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::10(d)": "θₙ₊₁ = sin⁻¹(0.8θₙ) is a rearrangement of θ = 1.25sinθ, so its root is where y = sin⁻¹(0.8θ) meets y = θ.\n\nDiagram (described): for θ ≥ 0, sketch y = sin⁻¹(0.8θ). It starts at the origin with gradient 0.8 (less than 1), increases, and its gradient keeps increasing, becoming vertical at θ = 1.25. Draw y = θ from the origin. Just after the origin the line is above the curve; the curve crosses the line from below at the root θ ≈ 1.13, and is above the line after that.\n\nShow the iteration as a staircase: starting just below the root, go vertically to the curve, then horizontally to the line y = θ, and repeat. The steps move away from the root, down towards the origin. (Starting just above the root, the staircase moves away upwards.)\n\nBecause the gradient of y = sin⁻¹(0.8θ) at the root is greater than 1, the staircase diverges from the root, so this iteration does not converge to the root found in (b).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::11(a)": "Separate the variables:\n\n∫e^(3y) dy = ∫3x² ln x dx\n\nLeft-hand side: ∫e^(3y) dy = ⅓e^(3y)\n\nRight-hand side, by parts with u = ln x, dv/dx = 3x², so du/dx = 1/x, v = x³:\n∫3x² ln x dx = x³ln x − ∫x³ × (1/x) dx\n= x³ln x − ∫x² dx\n= x³ln x − ⅓x³ + c\n\nSo ⅓e^(3y) = x³ln x − ⅓x³ + c.\n\nThe curve passes through (e, 1), and ln e = 1:\n⅓e³ = e³ − ⅓e³ + c ⇒ c = ⅓e³ − ⅔e³ = −⅓e³\n\n⅓e^(3y) = x³ln x − ⅓x³ − ⅓e³\n\nMultiply by 3:\ne^(3y) = 3x³ln x − x³ − e³",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::11(b)": "Substitute x = e² into e^(3y) = 3x³ln x − x³ − e³. Here x³ = e⁶ and ln e² = 2:\n\ne^(3y) = 3e⁶ × 2 − e⁶ − e³\n= 6e⁶ − e⁶ − e³\n= 5e⁶ − e³\n= e³(5e³ − 1)\n\nTake natural logs:\n3y = ln(e³(5e³ − 1)) = ln e³ + ln(5e³ − 1) = 3 + ln(5e³ − 1)\n\ny = 1 + ⅓ln(5e³ − 1)\n\nSo a = 1, b = 5, c = −1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::12(a)": "x = 1/t = t⁻¹ ⇒ dx/dt = −1/t²\ny = 2t ⇒ dy/dt = 2\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = 2 ÷ (−1/t²) = −2t²\n\nAt P, t = p, so the gradient of the tangent is −2p².\n\nTangent at P(1/p, 2p):\ny − 2p = −2p²(x − 1/p)\ny − 2p = −2p²x + 2p\ny = −2p²x + 4p, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p1::12(b)": "Tangent gradient at P is −2p², so the normal gradient is 1/(2p²).\n\nNormal at P(1/p, 2p):\ny − 2p = (1/(2p²))(x − 1/p)\ny = x/(2p²) + 2p − 1/(2p³)\n\nB (normal meets x-axis), y = 0:\nx/(2p²) = 1/(2p³) − 2p\nx = 2p²(1/(2p³) − 2p) = 1/p − 4p³\nSo B = (1/p − 4p³, 0).\n\nA (tangent meets x-axis), y = 0 in y = −2p²x + 4p:\n2p²x = 4p ⇒ x = 4p/(2p²) = 2/p\nSo A = (2/p, 0).\n\nLengths, with P = (1/p, 2p):\nPA² = (2/p − 1/p)² + (0 − 2p)² = (1/p)² + (2p)² = 1/p² + 4p² = (4p⁴ + 1)/p²\nPB² = (1/p − 4p³ − 1/p)² + (0 − 2p)² = (4p³)² + (2p)² = 16p⁶ + 4p² = 4p²(4p⁴ + 1)\n\nPA = √(4p⁴ + 1)/|p|,  PB = 2|p|√(4p⁴ + 1)\n\nPA : PB = (1/|p|)√(4p⁴ + 1) : 2|p|√(4p⁴ + 1)\n= 1/|p| : 2|p|\n= 1 : 2p²  (multiplying both by |p|, and |p|² = p²)\n\nHence PA : PB = 1 : 2p², as required.",












































 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::1(a)": "x/(x + 1) − (x − 1)/(x + 2) = 0   (x ≠ −1, x ≠ −2)\nMultiply through by (x + 1)(x + 2):\nx(x + 2) − (x − 1)(x + 1) = 0\nx² + 2x − (x² − 1) = 0\n2x + 1 = 0\nx = −½",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::1(b)": "Let u = 1/x³, so 1/x⁶ = u².\nThe equation becomes 8u² − 7u − 1 = 0\n(8u + 1)(u − 1) = 0\nu = 1 or u = −⅛\nSo 1/x³ = 1 ⇒ x³ = 1 ⇒ x = 1\nor 1/x³ = −⅛ ⇒ x³ = −8 ⇒ x = −2\nSolutions: x = 1 and x = −2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::1(c)": "243 = 3⁵, so 1/243 = 3⁻⁵.\n3^(x² − 7) = 3⁻⁵\nx² − 7 = −5\nx² = 2\nx = ±√2  (±1.41 to 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::2(a)": "AB = b − a = (4i + 2j − 5k) − (3i + 2j) = i − 5k\n|AB| = √(1² + 0² + (−5)²) = √26\nAB = √26 ≈ 5.10",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::2(b)": "Since AB is a diameter and P lies on the circle, angle APB = 90°, so PA · PB = 0.\nPA = OA − OP = (3i + 2j) − (pi − 3k) = (3 − p)i + 2j + 3k\nPB = OB − OP = (4i + 2j − 5k) − (pi − 3k) = (4 − p)i + 2j − 2k\nPA · PB = (3 − p)(4 − p) + 2×2 + 3×(−2) = 0\np² − 7p + 12 + 4 − 6 = 0\np² − 7p + 10 = 0\n(p − 2)(p − 5) = 0\np = 2 or p = 5\n(Check with Pythagoras: PA² + PB² = (p − 3)² + 13 + (p − 4)² + 8 = 2p² − 14p + 46, and setting this equal to AB² = 26 gives the same equation.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::3(a)": "No, I do not agree with Charlie.\nAmaya's answer is ⅓ + x + x² + ⅓x³ + c and Ben's is x + x² + ⅓x³ + c. They differ only by the constant ⅓, which can be absorbed into the arbitrary constant of integration (Ben's c = Amaya's c + ⅓). So both answers are correct; they just have different constants of integration.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::3(b)(i)": "∫₁ᵃ 1/(1 + x)² dx = ∫₁ᵃ (1 + x)⁻² dx\n= [ (1 + x)⁻¹/(−1) ]₁ᵃ\n= [ −1/(1 + x) ]₁ᵃ\n= −1/(1 + a) + 1/2\n= ((1 + a) − 2)/(2(1 + a))\n= (a − 1)/(2(a + 1))",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::3(b)(ii)": "For x between 1 and a the curve is above the x-axis, so the area equals the integral from (b)(i):\n(a − 1)/(2(a + 1)) = ⅓\n3(a − 1) = 2(a + 1)\n3a − 3 = 2a + 2\na = 5",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::3(c)": "The numerator cos2x is ½ × the derivative of the denominator, since d/dx(sin2x + 2) = 2cos2x.\nSo ∫ cos2x/(sin2x + 2) dx = ½ ln|sin2x + 2| + c\n∫₀^(π/12) cos2x/(sin2x + 2) dx = ½ [ ln(sin2x + 2) ]₀^(π/12)\n= ½ [ ln(sin(π/6) + 2) − ln(sin0 + 2) ]\n= ½ [ ln(½ + 2) − ln2 ]\n= ½ [ ln(5/2) − ln2 ]\n= ½ ln(5/4)\n(equivalently ln(√5/2))",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::4": "The step heights form a geometric sequence with first term a = 20 and common ratio r = 0.95.\nTotal height of n steps: Sₙ = 20(1 − 0.95ⁿ)/(1 − 0.95) = 400(1 − 0.95ⁿ)\nSet Sₙ = 205:\n400(1 − 0.95ⁿ) = 205\n1 − 0.95ⁿ = 205/400\n0.95ⁿ = 195/400 = 39/80 = 0.4875\nn = ln0.4875/ln0.95 = 14.007…\nSo n = 14.\nCheck: S₁₄ = 400(1 − 0.95¹⁴) = 204.93… = 205 cm to the nearest cm (whereas S₁₃ = 194.7 and S₁₅ = 214.7).\nThe design has 14 steps.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::5(a)": "y = x³ − 3x² + 4x\ndy/dx = 3x² − 6x + 4\nStationary points need 3x² − 6x + 4 = 0.\nDiscriminant: b² − 4ac = (−6)² − 4×3×4 = 36 − 48 = −12 < 0\nSo the equation has no real roots: there is no value of x where dy/dx = 0.\n(Equivalently dy/dx = 3(x − 1)² + 1 > 0 for all x, so the gradient is always positive.)\nHence the curve has no stationary points.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::5(b)": "d²y/dx² = 6x − 6\nAt a point of inflection d²y/dx² = 0: 6x − 6 = 0 ⇒ x = 1, the only solution.\nd²y/dx² = 6(x − 1) is negative for x < 1 and positive for x > 1, so it changes sign at x = 1 and x = 1 gives a point of inflection (at (1, 2)).\nSince d²y/dx² = 0 has exactly one root, the curve has exactly one point of inflection.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::6(a)": "Fig. 1.1: many-one function. Each x gives exactly one y, but some y-values come from more than one x (e.g. y = 0 occurs at three different x-values).\nFig. 1.2: not a function. A single x-value (e.g. x = 0) gives two different y-values.\nFig. 1.3: one-one function and its own inverse. Each x gives one y and each y comes from only one x, and the graph is symmetric about the line y = x, so f⁻¹ = f.\nFig. 1.4: one-one function. It is increasing throughout, so each y comes from exactly one x (it is not symmetric about y = x, so it is not self-inverse).\nFig. 1.5: many-one function. For example, the two minimum points have the same y-value.\n\nTable of ticks:\n1.1 – many-one\n1.2 – not a function\n1.3 – one-one, own inverse\n1.4 – one-one\n1.5 – many-one",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::6(b)": "f(x) = 1/x is decreasing for 0 < x ≤ 2.\nAt x = 2, f(2) = ½ (the least value, included since x = 2 is in the domain).\nAs x → 0⁺, f(x) → ∞, so there is no upper limit.\nRange of f: {y : y ≥ ½}",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::7(a)": "Any integer can be written as 3m + r with r = 0, 1 or 2. Square each case:\nr = 0: (3m)² = 9m² = 3(3m²), which is of the form 3n.\nr = 1: (3m + 1)² = 9m² + 6m + 1 = 3(3m² + 2m) + 1, which is of the form 3n + 1.\nr = 2: (3m + 2)² = 9m² + 12m + 4 = 3(3m² + 4m + 1) + 1, which is of the form 3n + 1.\nIn each case 3m², 3m² + 2m and 3m² + 4m + 1 are integers, so every square has the form 3n or 3n + 1.\nNone of these is of the form 3n + 2, so the square of any integer cannot be expressed in the form 3n + 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::7(b)": "Write each chosen integer as 3m + r. From 1 to 99 there are exactly 33 integers with each remainder r = 0, 1, 2, so each value of r has probability ⅓, independently for the three choices.\nThe sum is divisible by 3 exactly when the remainders add to a multiple of 3, i.e. when:\n• all three have the same remainder: (0, 0, 0), (1, 1, 1) or (2, 2, 2) (sums 0, 3, 6), or\n• all three have different remainders: 0, 1, 2 in some order (sum 3).\nP(all the same) = 3 × (⅓)³ = 3/27\nP(all different) = 3! × (⅓)³ = 6/27\nP(sum divisible by 3) = 3/27 + 6/27 = 9/27 = ⅓",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::8": "Volume in terms of h:\nThe radius r of the water surface satisfies r = h tan30° = h/√3.\nV = ⅓πr²h = ⅓π(h²/3)h = (π/9)h³\ndV/dh = (π/3)h²\nChain rule:\ndV/dt = (dV/dh)(dh/dt) ⇒ −2h = (π/3)h² dh/dt\n⇒ dh/dt = −6/(πh)\nSeparate variables:\nπ h dh = −6 dt\nIntegrate from t = 0 (h = 50) to time T when the tank is empty (h = 0):\nπ ∫₅₀⁰ h dh = −6 ∫₀ᵀ dt\nπ [h²/2]₅₀⁰ = −6T\n−π × 50²/2 = −6T\nT = 1250π/6 = 625π/3\nT = 625π/3 seconds ≈ 654 s (about 10.9 minutes)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(a)": "Probability = area of the 20–30 bar ÷ total area of the histogram (area is proportional to frequency).\nThe frequencies (from bar areas) are 4, 22, 23, 28, 29, 22, 20, 2, total 150.\nThe 20–30 bar has frequency 22.\nP(20 < X < 30) = 22/150 = 0.14666… = 0.147 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(b)(i)": "The histogram is roughly bell-shaped: it is roughly symmetrical, has a single peak in the middle, and tails off on each side. This is the shape of a normal distribution.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(b)(ii)": "Mean 40: the histogram is roughly symmetrical about 40 (the peak is at about 40), so μ ≈ 40. (Calculating with midpoints gives a mean of 5915/150 ≈ 39.4.)\nVariance 100 (σ = 10): for a normal distribution about two-thirds of the data lie within one standard deviation of the mean. The frequency between 30 and 50 (i.e. 40 ± 10) is 23 + 28 + 29 + 22 = 102, and 102/150 = 0.68 ≈ ⅔, so σ ≈ 10 and σ² ≈ 100. (Calculating with midpoints gives s ≈ 10.3.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(c)": "Sam's model: X ~ N(40, 10²)\nP(20 < X < 30) = P(−2 < Z < −1) = 0.1359…\nP(20 < X < 30) = 0.136 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(d)": "Using class midpoints x = 15, 25, 32.5, 37.5, 42.5, 47.5, 55, 65 with frequencies f = 4, 22, 23, 28, 29, 22, 20, 2 (Σf = 150):\nΣfx = 5915\nm = 5915/150 = 39.43… ≈ 39.4\nΣfx² = 249287.5\ns² = 249287.5/150 − (39.433…)² = 106.93… ≈ 107\ns = 10.34… ≈ 10.3\nNina's model: X ~ N(39.43, 10.34²)\nP(20 < X < 30) = 0.1507… = 0.151 (3 s.f.)\n(Using the n − 1 divisor, s = 10.38, gives 0.151 as well.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(e)(i)": "Sam's model N(40, 100):\nP(20 < X < 30) = 0.136\nP(40 < X < 45) = 0.191\nP(45 < X < 50) = 0.150\n\nNina's model N(39.43, 10.34²):\nP(20 < X < 30) = 0.151\nP(40 < X < 45) = 0.183\nP(45 < X < 50) = 0.142\n\nCompleted rows:\nSam: 0.023, 0.136, 0.150, 0.191, 0.191, 0.150, 0.136, 0.023\nNina: 0.030, 0.151, 0.153, 0.189, 0.183, 0.142, 0.130, 0.023",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::9(e)(ii)": "Comparing each model with the histogram proportions:\nFor lower values of X (ranges below 40), Nina's model fits better. For example, for 20–30 the data give 0.147, Nina's model gives 0.151 and Sam's gives 0.136; for < 20 the data give 0.027, Nina 0.030, Sam 0.023.\nFor higher values of X (ranges above 40), Sam's model fits better. For example, for 40–45 the data give 0.193, Sam 0.191 and Nina 0.183; for 45–50 the data give 0.147, Sam 0.150 and Nina 0.142.\nSo Nina's model is the better fit for the lower heights and Sam's model is the better fit for the higher heights. (Both models overestimate the proportion above 60.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(a)(i)": "An LA that includes a university would have a high proportion of residents aged 18 to 24 (students).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(a)(ii)": "An LA that attracts young couples would have a high proportion of residents in the younger age ranges: a high proportion aged 0 to 17 (their children) and/or aged 18 to 24.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(a)(iii)": "An LA that attracts retired people would have a low proportion of residents in the younger age ranges: a low proportion aged 0 to 17 and/or aged 18 to 24.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(b)(i)": "The four LAs with the highest proportions aged 18 to 24 are:\nH (14.79%), G (14.20%), K (13.98%), M (11.19%)\n(all others are 9.20% or less).\nSo G, H, K and M might include a university.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(b)(ii)": "The LAs with the lowest proportions in both younger age ranges are:\nN (0–17: 17.82%, 18–24: 6.01%)\nF (0–17: 18.14%, 18–24: 6.51%)\nR (0–17: 18.61%, 18–24: 7.33%)\nSo F, N and R might be attractive to retired people.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(c)": "A low proportion of young people does not necessarily mean a high proportion of retired people: the other age ranges must be considered. An LA with low percentages aged 0–17 and 18–24 might instead have a large percentage aged 25–64 (e.g. working adults), so without looking at the 65 and over column we cannot be sure it attracts retired people.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(d)": "IQR = 16.76 − 14.56 = 2.20\nUpper limit for outliers: UQ + 1.5 × IQR = 16.76 + 1.5 × 2.20 = 20.06\nF (24.21%), N (24.23%) and R (24.71%) are all above 20.06, so all three are high outliers in the 65 and over column.\nSo these three LAs really do have unusually high proportions of residents aged 65 and over. This confirms the answer to (b)(ii) (F, N, R), despite the doubt raised in (c).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::10(e)": "The mean (16.90%) is greater than the upper quartile (16.76%), so it is larger than at least three-quarters of the values; it has been pulled up by the three high outliers F, N and R.\nThe median (15.99%) is not affected by these outliers, so the median better represents the data for a typical LA.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::11": "Let μ g be the population mean mass of new-born babies (after the campaign).\nH₀: μ = 3300\nH₁: μ > 3300\nUnder H₀, X̄ ~ N(3300, 450²/200) = N(3300, 1012.5)\nP(X̄ > 3360) = 0.0297 (3 s.f.)\n(Equivalently z = (3360 − 3300)/(450/√200) = 1.886.)\nCompare with the significance level: 0.0297 > 0.025\n(equivalently 1.89 < 1.96, or 3360 < critical value 3362)\nThe result is not significant, so do not reject H₀.\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 2.5% level that the mean mass of new-born babies has increased.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::12(a)": "Let X be the number of underweight packets in the sample of 600.\nUnder H₀: X ~ B(600, 0.02)\nThe test is upper-tailed at 5%, so find the smallest k with P(X ≥ k) ≤ 0.05:\nP(X ≥ 18) = 1 − P(X ≤ 17) = 0.0610 > 0.05\nP(X ≥ 19) = 1 − P(X ≤ 18) = 0.0359 ≤ 0.05\nSo the critical region is X ≥ 19.\nThe researcher concludes that the firm's claim is incorrect when X ≥ 19, so, given H₀ is true,\nP(concludes claim incorrect) = P(X ≥ 19) = 0.0359 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::12(b)": "The statement is incorrect: a sample proportion of 3% being above 2% is not by itself evidence. A proper hypothesis test is needed, considering P(X ≥ 18) = 0.0610, which is greater than 0.05. Equivalently, the critical region is X ≥ 19, so 18 is in the acceptance region and there is insufficient evidence at the 5% level that the proportion of underweight bags is greater than 2%.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::13(a)": "Let x be the number who study English but not History; then 3x study History but not English.\nVenn diagram: a rectangle (the class of 25) containing two overlapping circles H and E, with\n• 3x in H only,\n• 3 in the overlap (both History and English),\n• x in E only,\n• 14 outside both circles.\nTotal: 3x + 3 + x + 14 = 25\n4x + 17 = 25\nx = 2\nSo the regions are: History only 6, both 3, English only 2, neither 14.\nNumber who study English = 2 + 3 = 5\nP(student studies English) = 5/25 = ⅕",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p2::13(b)": "From (a): History only 6, both 3, English only 2, neither 14. So 5 study English and 20 do not.\nP(exactly one English) = 2 × (5/25) × (20/24) = 200/600 = ⅓\nFor exactly one English and exactly one History, the pair must be either:\n• one 'History only' and one 'English only' student, or\n• one 'both' student and one 'neither' student.\nP(exactly one E and exactly one H) = 2 × [ (6/25)(2/24) + (3/25)(14/24) ]\n= 2 × (12 + 42)/600 = 108/600 = 9/50\nRequired probability = P(exactly one E and exactly one H) ÷ P(exactly one E)\n= (9/50) ÷ (1/3)\n= 27/50 (= 0.54)",














































 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::1": "|2x − 3| = 9 means 2x − 3 = 9 or 2x − 3 = −9.\n\n2x − 3 = 9 ⇒ 2x = 12 ⇒ x = 6\n2x − 3 = −9 ⇒ 2x = −6 ⇒ x = −3\n\nCheck: |2(6) − 3| = |9| = 9 ✓ and |2(−3) − 3| = |−9| = 9 ✓\n\nx = 6 or x = −3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::2(a)": "y = x³ − 8 is obtained from y = x³ by subtracting 8 from every y-value.\n\nThe transformation is a translation by 8 units in the negative y-direction (8 units down, parallel to the y-axis), i.e. a translation by the vector (0, −8) (column vector: 0 over −8).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::2(b)": "Let y = x³ − 8 and make x the subject:\ny + 8 = x³\nx = ∛(y + 8)\n\nSwapping the roles of x and y:\nf⁻¹(x) = ∛(x + 8)   (that is, (x + 8)^(1/3))",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::2(c)": "The graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of the graph of y = f(x) in the line y = x (each graph is the mirror image of the other in y = x).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::3": "Since PQ is a diameter, the centre is the midpoint of PQ:\nCentre = ((2 + 3)/2, (−5 + 1)/2) = (5/2, −2)\n\nLength of the diameter:\nd² = (3 − 2)² + (1 − (−5))² = 1 + 36 = 37\nso radius r = √37/2 and r² = 37/4.\n\nEquation of the circle:\n(x − 5/2)² + (y + 2)² = 37/4\n\nExpanding:\nx² − 5x + 25/4 + y² + 4y + 4 = 37/4\nx² + y² − 5x + 4y + 25/4 + 16/4 − 37/4 = 0\nx² + y² − 5x + 4y + 1 = 0\n\nSo a = −5, b = 4, c = 1:\nx² + y² − 5x + 4y + 1 = 0",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::4(a)": "AP with common difference −4:\ny − x = −4 and z − y = −4, so x = y + 4 and z = y − 4.\n\nGP: x, 15/y, z have a common ratio, so\n(15/y)/x = z/(15/y)\n(15/y)² = xz\n225/y² = xz\nxy²z = 225\n\nSubstituting x = y + 4 and z = y − 4:\n(y + 4)y²(y − 4) = 225\ny²(y² − 4y + 4y − 16) = 225\ny²(y² − 16) = 225\ny⁴ − 16y² − 225 = 0  as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::4(b)": "y⁴ − 16y² − 225 = 0 is a quadratic in y²:\n(y² − 25)(y² + 9) = 0\ny² = 25 (y² = −9 has no real solutions), so y = ±5.\n\nSince y is a positive integer, y = 5.\nThen x = y + 4 = 9 and z = y − 4 = 1.\n\nThe GP is 9, 15/5, 1, i.e. 9, 3, 1.\nCommon ratio r = 3/9 = 1/3  (check: r² = z/x = 1/9 ✓)\n\nSince |r| < 1 the sum to infinity exists:\nS∞ = x/(1 − r) = 9/(1 − 1/3) = 9/(2/3) = 27/2\n\nS∞ = 13.5",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::5(a)": "y = (2x − 3)/(4x² + 1)\n\nQuotient rule:\ndy/dx = [(4x² + 1)(2) − (2x − 3)(8x)]/(4x² + 1)²\n      = (8x² + 2 − 16x² + 24x)/(4x² + 1)²\n      = (2 + 24x − 8x²)/(4x² + 1)²\n      = 2(1 + 12x − 4x²)/(4x² + 1)²\n\nAt P the gradient is 2:\n2(1 + 12x − 4x²)/(4x² + 1)² = 2\n1 + 12x − 4x² = (4x² + 1)²\n1 + 12x − 4x² = 16x⁴ + 8x² + 1\n0 = 16x⁴ + 12x² − 12x\n4x(4x³ + 3x − 3) = 0\n\nSo x = 0 or 4x³ + 3x − 3 = 0.\n\nAt x = 0 the gradient is 2(1)/1² = 2, but the point (0, −3) is on the y-axis, whereas P lies to the right of the y-axis (see the diagram), so x ≠ 0.\n\nHence the x-coordinate of P satisfies 4x³ + 3x − 3 = 0.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::5(b)": "Let f(x) = 4x³ + 3x − 3.\n\nf(0.5) = 4(0.125) + 1.5 − 3 = 0.5 + 1.5 − 3 = −1 < 0\nf(1) = 4 + 3 − 3 = 4 > 0\n\nf is continuous, and there is a change of sign between x = 0.5 and x = 1, so f(x) = 0 has a root between 0.5 and 1.\n\nHence the x-coordinate of P lies between 0.5 and 1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::5(c)": "Write the iteration as xₙ₊₁ = h(xₙ), where h(x) = (3 − 4x³)/3 = 1 − (4/3)x³.\n\nh′(x) = −4x²\n\nLet α be the x-coordinate of P. From (b), 0.5 < α < 1, so\nα² > 0.25 and h′(α) = −4α² < −4(0.25) = −1.\n\nSo |h′(α)| > 1 (in fact h′(x) < −1 for every x in (0.5, 1); h′(α) ≈ −1.66).\n\nA fixed-point iteration xₙ₊₁ = h(xₙ) can only converge to a root α if |h′(α)| < 1. Since |h′(α)| > 1, successive values move away from α, so the iteration cannot converge to the x-coordinate of P, whatever starting value is used.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::5(d)": "f(x) = 4x³ + 3x − 3, so f′(x) = 12x² + 3.\n\nNewton–Raphson:\nxₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (4xₙ³ + 3xₙ − 3)/(12xₙ² + 3)\n\nx₀ = 0.5\nx₁ = 0.5 − (−1)/(6) = 2/3 = 0.666666...\nx₂ = 2/3 − f(2/3)/f′(2/3) = 2/3 − (5/27)/(25/3) = 2/3 − 1/45 = 29/45 = 0.644444...\nx₃ = 0.6439551...\nx₄ = 0.6439549...\n\nSo x = 0.64395 (5 d.p.).\n\ny-coordinate:\ny = (2x − 3)/(4x² + 1) with x = 0.6439549... gives y = −0.6439549...\n(Indeed, using 4x³ = 3 − 3x, we get 4x² + 1 = (3 − 2x)/x, so y = (2x − 3)x/(3 − 2x) = −x.)\n\nP is (0.64395, −0.64395).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::6": "The curves meet on the y-axis at (0, 3).\nThe shaded area = (area under y = √(2x + 9) from x = −9/2 to x = 0) + (area under y = 4e^(−2x) − 1 from x = 0 to where it meets the x-axis).\n\nFirst region:\n√(2x + 9) = 0 when x = −9/2.\n∫ (2x + 9)^(1/2) dx = (1/3)(2x + 9)^(3/2)\n∫ from −9/2 to 0 of √(2x + 9) dx = [(1/3)(2x + 9)^(3/2)] from −9/2 to 0\n = (1/3)(9)^(3/2) − (1/3)(0) = (1/3)(27) = 9\n\nSecond region:\n4e^(−2x) − 1 = 0 ⇒ e^(−2x) = 1/4 ⇒ −2x = ln(1/4) ⇒ x = −½ln(1/4) = ½ln4 = ln2\n\n∫ (4e^(−2x) − 1) dx = −2e^(−2x) − x\n∫ from 0 to ln2 of (4e^(−2x) − 1) dx = [−2e^(−2x) − x] from 0 to ln2\n = (−2e^(−2ln2) − ln2) − (−2e⁰ − 0)\n = (−2(1/4) − ln2) + 2\n = 3/2 − ln2\n(This is positive, as expected since the curve is above the x-axis on this interval.)\n\nTotal area = 9 + 3/2 − ln2 = 21/2 − ln2\n\nSo p = 21/2 and q = −1.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::7(a)": "m secθ + 3cosθ = 4sinθ\n\nWrite cosθ = 1/secθ:\nm secθ + 3/secθ = 4sinθ\n\nMultiply by secθ:\nm sec²θ + 3 = 4sinθ secθ = 4sinθ/cosθ = 4tanθ\n\nUse sec²θ = 1 + tan²θ:\nm(1 + tan²θ) + 3 = 4tanθ\nm + m tan²θ + 3 = 4tanθ\nm tan²θ − 4tanθ + (m + 3) = 0  as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::7(b)": "For 0 < θ < π (θ ≠ π/2, where secθ is undefined), tanθ takes every non-zero real value exactly once. So each non-zero root of the quadratic in tanθ,\nm tan²θ − 4tanθ + (m + 3) = 0,\ngives exactly one θ. The intended case of exactly one solution is when this quadratic has a repeated root, i.e. its discriminant is zero.\n\nDiscriminant:\nΔ = (−4)² − 4m(m + 3) = 16 − 4m² − 12m\n\nΔ = 0:\n16 − 4m² − 12m = 0\nm² + 3m − 4 = 0\n(m + 4)(m − 1) = 0\nm = −4 or m = 1\n\nm is a negative integer, so m = −4 only.\n\nWith m = −4:\n−4tan²θ − 4tanθ + (−4 + 3) = 0\n−4tan²θ − 4tanθ − 1 = 0\n4tan²θ + 4tanθ + 1 = 0\n(2tanθ + 1)² = 0\ntanθ = −0.5\n\nIn 0 < θ < π, tanθ < 0 gives θ in the second quadrant:\nθ = π − tan⁻¹(0.5) = π − 0.46365 = 2.67795...\n\nθ = 2.68 (3 s.f.)\n\n(Note: m = −3 also gives exactly one solution. Then the quadratic is −3tan²θ − 4tanθ = 0, whose root tanθ = 0 gives no θ with 0 < θ < π, leaving only tanθ = −4/3, θ = 2.21. The repeated-root case above, m = −4 with θ = 2.68, is the expected answer.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::8": "The sledge is at rest, so the horizontal forces balance. The friction (60 N) opposes the horizontal component of the tension T.\n\nResolving horizontally:\nT cos15° = 60\nT = 60/cos15° = 62.116...\n\nT = 62.1 N (exactly 60(√6 − √2) N)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::9(a)": "For car A over the first 30 s: u = 20, v = 8, t = 30.\nv = u + at\n8 = 20 + 30a\na = −12/30 = −0.4\n\nThe deceleration of car A is 0.4 m s⁻².",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::9(b)": "Both cars are level at t = 0. B overtakes A when they have travelled equal distances.\n\nCar B (constant 12 m s⁻¹): distance = 12t.\nIn the first 30 s, B travels 12 × 30 = 360 m.\n\nCar A in the first 30 s (area of the trapezium under its graph):\n½(20 + 8)(30) = 420 m.\nSo at t = 30, A is still 60 m ahead, and B overtakes after t = 30.\n\nFor t ≥ 30, A travels at 8 m s⁻¹, so A's distance = 420 + 8(t − 30) = 180 + 8t.\n\nB overtakes A when the distances are equal:\n12t = 420 + 8(t − 30)\n12t = 8t + 180\n4t = 180\nt = 45\n\nB overtakes A when t = 45.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::10(a)": "Take the direction of motion of Q (downwards) as positive. The forces on Q are its weight 3g and the tension 16.8 N.\n\nNewton's second law for Q:\n3g − 16.8 = 3a\n29.4 − 16.8 = 3a\n12.6 = 3a\na = 4.2\n\nAcceleration of Q = 4.2 m s⁻² (downwards).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::10(b)": "P has the same acceleration, 4.2 m s⁻², along the string towards the pulley. Let F be the friction on P from B (opposing the motion).\n\nNewton's second law for P horizontally:\n16.8 − F = 2.5 × 4.2\n16.8 − F = 10.5\nF = 6.3 N\n\nVertically for P: normal reaction R = 2.5g = 24.5 N.\n\nP is sliding, so friction is limiting: F = μR\n6.3 = μ × 2.5g = 24.5μ\nμ = 6.3/24.5 = 9/35\n\nμ = 0.257 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::10(c)": "Let the mass of B be M kg.\n\nForces on B: friction 6.3 N from P (in the direction of P's motion, by Newton's third law), friction from the ground opposing this, and vertically its weight Mg, the normal reaction 2.5g from P pressing down, and the normal reaction R from the ground.\n\nResolving vertically for B:\nR = 2.5g + Mg\n\nB stays in equilibrium, so the friction needed from the ground (6.3 N) must not exceed the limiting friction:\n6.3 ≤ (5/49)(2.5g + Mg)\n6.3 ≤ (5/49)(9.8)(2.5 + M)\n6.3 ≤ 1(2.5 + M)\nM ≥ 3.8\n\nThe least possible mass of B is 3.8 kg.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::11(a)": "Since D is vertically above A, angle DAC = 90° − 60° = 30°. In triangle ACD the angle at D is 60°, so the angle at C is 180° − 30° − 60° = 90°: the string CD is perpendicular to the rod. Hence the moment of T about A is T × x.\n\nTake moments about A (the friction and normal reaction at A have no moment about A).\nRod's weight 4g acts at the midpoint, 1.5 m from A: horizontal distance 1.5cos60°.\nParticle's weight g acts at B, 3 m from A: horizontal distance 3cos60°.\n\nClockwise moments = anticlockwise moments:\n4g(1.5cos60°) + g(3cos60°) = Tx\n4g(0.75) + g(1.5) = Tx\n3g + 1.5g = Tx\n4.5g = Tx\n\nT = 9g/(2x) N",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::11(b)": "The string CD makes 60° with the vertical, so the tension has vertical component T cos60° (upwards) and horizontal component T sin60° (towards the vertical line through A, D). Let R be the normal reaction and F the friction at A.\n\nResolving vertically:\nR + T cos60° = 4g + g\nR = 5g − ½T = 5g − 9g/(4x)\n\nResolving horizontally:\nF = T sin60° = (√3/2)(9g/(2x)) = 9√3g/(4x)\n\nLimiting equilibrium, so F = μR with μ = 9√3/35:\n9√3g/(4x) = (9√3/35)(5g − 9g/(4x))\n\nDivide by 9√3g:\n1/(4x) = (1/35)(5 − 9/(4x))\n35/(4x) = 5 − 9/(4x)\n44/(4x) = 5\n11/x = 5\nx = 2.2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::12(a)": "v = (1 − 2t)i + (2t² + t − 13)j\n\nP is stationary only if both components are zero at the same time.\n\ni-component: 1 − 2t = 0 ⇒ t = ½\n\nj-component: 2t² + t − 13 = 0\nt = (−1 ± √(1 + 104))/4 = (−1 ± √105)/4\nt = 2.3117... or t = −2.8117...\n\nThere is no value of t that makes both components zero simultaneously (at t = ½ the j-component is 2(¼) + ½ − 13 = −12 ≠ 0), so P is never stationary.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::12(b)": "a = dv/dt = d/dt[(1 − 2t)i + (2t² + t − 13)j]\n\na = −2i + (4t + 1)j  m s⁻²",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::12(c)": "P moves in the direction of −2i + j when v is a positive multiple of −2i + j, so\n(j-component)/(i-component) = 1/(−2):\n−2(2t² + t − 13) = 1(1 − 2t)\n−4t² − 2t + 26 = 1 − 2t\n4t² − 25 = 0\nt² = 25/4\nt = 2.5 (t ≥ 0, so t = −2.5 is rejected)\n\nCheck: at t = 2.5, v = −4i + 2j = 2(−2i + j), a positive multiple ✓\n\nF = ma = 0.5[−2i + (4t + 1)j]\nAt t = 2.5: F = 0.5(−2i + 11j) = −i + 5.5j\n\n|F| = √((−1)² + 5.5²) = √31.25 = 5√5/2 = 5.590...\n\n|F| = 5.59 N (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::12(d)": "Let r be the position vector of P at time t. Integrate v:\nr = ∫[(1 − 2t)i + (2t² + t − 13)j] dt\nr = (t − t²)i + (⅔t³ + ½t² − 13t)j + c, where c is a constant vector.\n\nWhen t = 1, r = (1/6)j:\n(1 − 1)i + (⅔ + ½ − 13)j + c = (1/6)j\n(−71/6)j + c = (1/6)j\nc = 12j\n\nSo r = (t − t²)i + (⅔t³ + ½t² − 13t + 12)j\n\nAt t = 1.5:\ni-component: 1.5 − 2.25 = −0.75 = −3/4\nj-component: ⅔(3.375) + ½(2.25) − 19.5 + 12 = 2.25 + 1.125 − 7.5 = −4.125 = −33/8\nr = −(3/4)i − (33/8)j\n\nP is 3/4 m west and 33/8 m south of O, so it lies in the south-west quadrant, west of due south.\nAngle west of south = tan⁻¹((3/4)/(33/8)) = tan⁻¹(2/11) = 10.30°\n\nBearing = 180° + 10.30° = 190.3°\n\nThe bearing of P from O at t = 1.5 is 190° (to the nearest degree).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::13(a)": "Horizontally there is no acceleration; vertically the acceleration is −g. Using s = ut + ½at²:\nx = Ut\ny = Vt − ½gt²\n\nFrom the first equation, t = x/U. Substituting:\ny = V(x/U) − ½g(x/U)²\ny = Vx/U − gx²/(2U²)\n\nMultiply through by 2U²:\n2U²y = 2UVx − gx²  as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::13(b)": "B just clears the wall, so it passes through (a, ½a):\n2U²(½a) = 2UVa − ga²\nU²a = 2UVa − ga²\nU² = 2UV − ga   (1)\n\nB lands 3a beyond the wall, i.e. at horizontal distance a + 3a = 4a from O, so it passes through (4a, 0):\n0 = 2UV(4a) − g(4a)²\n8UVa = 16ga²\nUV = 2ga   (2)\n\nSubstitute (2) into (1):\nU² = 2(2ga) − ga = 3ga, so U = √(3ga)\nV = 2ga/U = 2ga/√(3ga) = 2√(ga)/√3   (so V² = (4/3)ga)\n\nIf θ is the angle of projection above the horizontal:\ntanθ = V/U = (2√(ga)/√3)/√(3ga) = 2/3\n\nθ = tan⁻¹(2/3) = 33.69...°\n\nAngle of projection = 33.7° (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::13(c)": "Speed of projection = √(U² + V²) = 54.6.\n\nFrom (b), U² = 3ga and V² = (4/3)ga, so\n√(3ga + (4/3)ga) = 54.6\n(13/3)ga = 54.6² = 2981.16\na = 3(2981.16)/(13 × 9.8) = 8943.48/127.4 = 70.2\n\na = 70.2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::13(d)": "At the maximum height H the vertical component of velocity is zero. Vertically: u = V, v = 0, acceleration −g.\nv² = u² + 2as:\n0 = V² − 2gH\nH = V²/(2g)\n\nUsing V² = (4/3)ga from (b):\nH = (1/(2g)) × (4ga/3) = (2/3)a\n\nWith a = 70.2:\nH = (2/3)(70.2) = 46.8\n\nThe maximum height of B above the ground is 46.8 m.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2022-p3::13(e)": "Take into account the size of the ball B (i.e. do not model it as a particle), so that the whole ball, not just its centre, must clear the top of the wall.\n\n(Other valid refinements: include the spin of the ball, include the effect of wind, allow for the thickness of the wall, or use a more accurate value of g.)",












































 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::1(a)(i)": "x² − 8x + 11 = (x − 4)² − 16 + 11\n= (x − 4)² − 5\n\nSo a = 4 and b = −5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::1(a)(ii)": "(x − 4)² ≥ 0 for all x, so (x − 4)² − 5 ≥ −5, with equality when x = 4.\n\nMinimum value of x² − 8x + 11 is −5.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::1(b)": "x² − 8x + 11 = k ⇒ x² − 8x + (11 − k) = 0\n\nTwo equal roots ⇒ discriminant = 0:\n(−8)² − 4 × 1 × (11 − k) = 0\n64 − 44 + 4k = 0\n20 + 4k = 0\n\nk = −5\n\n(This agrees with part (a): the line y = k touches the curve y = x² − 8x + 11 only at its minimum point, where y = −5.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::2(a)": "OA = (6, 0, 8), so |OA| = √(6² + 0² + 8²) = √100 = 10.\n\nSince vector OP = k × vector OA, the length OP = |k| × |OA| = |k| × 10.\n\nOP = 10|k|",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::2(b)": "OP = (6k, 0, 8k) and OB = (1, 2, 3).\n\nBP = OP − OB = (6k − 1, −2, 8k − 3)\n\nAngle OPB = 90°, so by Pythagoras OP² + BP² = OB².\n\nOP² = 100k²\nBP² = (6k − 1)² + (−2)² + (8k − 3)² = 36k² − 12k + 1 + 4 + 64k² − 48k + 9 = 100k² − 60k + 14\nOB² = 1² + 2² + 3² = 14\n\n100k² + 100k² − 60k + 14 = 14\n200k² − 60k + 14 = 14\n200k² − 60k = 0\n20k(10k − 3) = 0\n\nk = 0 or k = 3/10. Since k is non-zero,\n\nk = 3/10\n\n(Check with the scalar product: OP · BP = 6k(6k − 1) + 0 + 8k(8k − 3) = 100k² − 30k, which is 0 when k = 3/10.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::3": "For 0 ≤ x ≤ 2.5, x + 2 > 0 so y = 1/(x + 2) > 0, and the region lies above the x-axis.\n\nArea = ∫₀^2.5 1/(x + 2) dx\n= [ln(x + 2)]₀^2.5\n= ln 4.5 − ln 2\n= ln(4.5/2)\n= ln(9/4)\n\nExact area = ln(9/4) (equivalently 2ln(3/2)).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::4(a)": "y = x² ⇒ dy/dx = 2x\n\nAt A(1, 1): gradient of tangent = 2 × 1 = 2, so gradient of normal = −1/2.\n\nEquation of normal at A:\ny − 1 = −½(x − 1)\ny = −½x + 3/2\n\nAt B the normal meets the curve, so\nx² = −½x + 3/2\n2x² = −x + 3\n2x² + x − 3 = 0\n(2x + 3)(x − 1) = 0\nx = 1 or x = −3/2\n\nx = 1 is the point A, so at B, x = −3/2 and y = (−3/2)² = 9/4.\n\nB is (−3/2, 9/4).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::4(b)": "O(0, 0), A(1, 1), B(−3/2, 9/4).\n\nOA² = 1² + 1² = 2\nOB² = (3/2)² + (9/4)² = 36/16 + 81/16 = 117/16\nAB² = (1 − (−3/2))² + (1 − 9/4)² = (5/2)² + (−5/4)² = 100/16 + 25/16 = 125/16\n\nCosine rule in triangle AOB:\ncos α = (OA² + OB² − AB²)/(2 × OA × OB)\n= (2 + 117/16 − 125/16)/(2 × √2 × √(117/16))\n= (2 − 1/2)/(2 × √2 × 3√13/4)\n= (3/2)/(3√26/2)\n= 1/√26\n\ncos α > 0, so α is acute and\nsin α = √(1 − 1/26) = √(25/26) = 5/√26\n\ntan α = sin α / cos α = (5/√26)/(1/√26)\n\ntan α = 5",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::5(a)(i)": "f(x) = cos x + √3 sin x\nf′(x) = −sin x + √3 cos x\n\nf′(x) = 0 ⇒ √3 cos x = sin x.\nIf cos x = 0 then sin x = ±1 and f′(x) = ∓1 ≠ 0, so cos x ≠ 0 at any solution and we may divide by cos x:\ntan x = √3\n\nPrincipal value x = π/3; tan has period π, so in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π:\nx = π/3 or x = π/3 + π = 4π/3\n\n(π/3 + 2π is outside the domain, so there are no other solutions.)\n\nx = π/3, 4π/3",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::5(a)(ii)": "f′(x) = −sin x + √3 cos x\nf″(x) = −cos x − √3 sin x\n\nf″(x) = 0 ⇒ −cos x = √3 sin x.\nIf cos x = 0 then sin x = ±1 and f″(x) = ∓√3 ≠ 0, so cos x ≠ 0 and we may divide by cos x:\n√3 tan x = −1\ntan x = −1/√3\n\ntan x is negative in the second and fourth quadrants. The reference angle is π/6, so in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π:\nx = π − π/6 = 5π/6 or x = 2π − π/6 = 11π/6\n\nx = 5π/6, 11π/6",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::5(b)": "A and C are where the graph of f′ crosses the x-axis, i.e. where f′(x) = 0, from (a)(i): x = π/3 and x = 4π/3.\nA(π/3, 0) and C(4π/3, 0).\n\nB and D are the turning points of f′, where f″(x) = 0, from (a)(ii): x = 5π/6 (the minimum B) and x = 11π/6 (the maximum D).\n\nf′(5π/6) = −sin(5π/6) + √3 cos(5π/6) = −1/2 + √3(−√3/2) = −1/2 − 3/2 = −2\nf′(11π/6) = −sin(11π/6) + √3 cos(11π/6) = 1/2 + √3(√3/2) = 1/2 + 3/2 = 2\n\nA(π/3, 0), B(5π/6, −2), C(4π/3, 0), D(11π/6, 2)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::5(c)(i)": "f(x) is increasing where its gradient f′(x) is positive, i.e. for the values of x where the graph of y = f′(x) lies above the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::5(c)(ii)": "From the diagram and part (b), the graph of f′ is above the x-axis from x = 0 up to A (x = π/3), and again from C (x = 4π/3) up to x = 2π (f′(0) = √3 > 0 and f′(2π) = √3 > 0).\n\nSo f(x) is increasing for 0 ≤ x < π/3 and for 4π/3 < x ≤ 2π.\n\nIn set notation:\n{x : 0 ≤ x < π/3} ∪ {x : 4π/3 < x ≤ 2π}",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::6(a)": "Centre: x² − 6x + y² + a = 0 ⇒ (x − 3)² + y² = 9 − a, so C is (3, 0).\n\nSubstitute y = x into the circle:\nx² − 6x + x² + a = 0\n2x² − 6x + a = 0\n\nx = (6 ± √(36 − 8a))/4 = (6 ± 2√(9 − 2a))/4 = (3 ± √(9 − 2a))/2\n\nAt A: x = (3 − √(9 − 2a))/2, at B: x = (3 + √(9 − 2a))/2, and on y = x each y-coordinate equals its x-coordinate.\n\nM is the midpoint of AB, so its x-coordinate is the mean of these:\nx = 3/2, so M is (3/2, 3/2).\n\nCM² = (3 − 3/2)² + (0 − 3/2)² = 9/4 + 9/4 = 9/2, so CM = 3/√2.\n\nAB: the x-coordinates differ by √(9 − 2a) and so do the y-coordinates, so\nBA² = (√(9 − 2a))² + (√(9 − 2a))² = 2(9 − 2a)\nBA = √2 × √(9 − 2a)\n\nM is the midpoint of the chord AB, so CM is perpendicular to AB (check: gradient of CM = (3/2 − 0)/(3/2 − 3) = −1, and (−1) × 1 = −1). So CM is the perpendicular height of triangle ABC from C to base AB.\n\nArea of ABC = ½ × BA × CM\n= ½ × √2 √(9 − 2a) × 3/√2\n= (3/2)√(9 − 2a), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::6(b)(i)": "(3/2)√(9 − 2a) = 0 ⇒ 9 − 2a = 0\n\na = 9/2",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::6(b)(ii)": "When a = 9/2, the equation 2x² − 6x + a = 0 has the repeated root x = 3/2, so A and B coincide at (3/2, 3/2).\n\nGeometrically, the line y = x is a tangent to the circle (it touches the circle at the single point (3/2, 3/2)).",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::6(c)": "When a = 5, the equation 2x² − 6x + 5 = 0 has discriminant 36 − 40 = −4 < 0 (equivalently 9 − 2a = −1 < 0), so there are no real points of intersection.\n\n(The circle has centre (3, 0) and radius √(9 − 5) = 2, which is less than the perpendicular distance 3/√2 ≈ 2.12 from the centre (3, 0) to the line y = x.)\n\nGeometrically, the line y = x does not meet the circle.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::7(a)": "Suppose a² − 4b = 2. Then\na² = 4b + 2 = 2(2b + 1)\n\nSince 2b + 1 is an integer, a² is even.\n\nIf a were odd, say a = 2m + 1 for some integer m, then a² = 4m² + 4m + 1 = 2(2m² + 2m) + 1 would be odd. So a² even means a cannot be odd.\n\nHence a is even, i.e. a² − 4b = 2 ⇒ a is even.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::7(b)": "Proof by contradiction.\n\nAssume that there are positive integers a and b with a² − 4b = 2.\n\nBy part (a), a is even, so a = 2n for some integer n. Then\n(2n)² − 4b = 2\n4n² − 4b = 2\n2n² − 2b = 1\n\nThe left-hand side 2(n² − b) is even, but the right-hand side 1 is odd. An even number cannot equal an odd number, so this is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore the assumption is false, and for all positive integers a and b,\na² − 4b ≠ 2.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::8(a)": "The 15 heights in order are:\n2, 10, 24, 30, 32, 34, 39, 41, 42, 44, 47, 49, 53, 57, 62\n\nMedian = 8th value = 41\nLower quartile = 4th value = 30\nUpper quartile = 12th value = 49\nSmallest value = 2, largest value = 62\n\nBox-and-whisker plot (described): draw a horizontal axis labelled \"Height (cm)\" with a uniform linear scale from 0 to 70 (marked every 10 cm). Draw a box from 30 to 49 with a vertical line inside it at 41 (the median). Draw a whisker from the left end of the box at 30 out to 2, and a whisker from the right end of the box at 49 out to 62.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::8(b)": "Using the rule that an outlier is more than 1.5 × IQR below the lower quartile or above the upper quartile:\n\nIQR = 49 − 30 = 19\n1.5 × IQR = 28.5\n\nLower limit: Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR = 30 − 28.5 = 1.5\nUpper limit: Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR = 49 + 28.5 = 77.5\n\nThe smallest height, 2 cm, is above 1.5, and the largest, 62 cm, is below 77.5, so every height lies within the limits.\n\nThere are no outliers, so the statistician should not ignore any of the heights.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::9(a)": "Total number of students = 500 + 250 = 750.\nProportion in years 12 and 13 = 250/750 = 1/3.\n\nX ~ B(20, 1/3)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::9(b)(i)": "With X ~ B(20, 1/3):\n\nP(X = x) = ²⁰Cₓ (1/3)^x (2/3)^(20 − x)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::9(b)(ii)": "X counts students in a sample of 20, so x must be a whole number from 0 to 20:\n\n{x : x ∈ ℤ, 0 ≤ x ≤ 20}",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::9(c)": "P(5 ≤ X ≤ 9) = P(X ≤ 9) − P(X ≤ 4)\n= 0.90810 − 0.15151\n= 0.75659...\n\nP(5 ≤ X ≤ 9) = 0.757 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::9(d)": "A random sample of 20 might, by chance, include too many students from one year group and too few (or none) from another, so the group at the parents' evening may not represent all the year groups in the school.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::10": "Let μ kg be the population mean mass of fish of this species in this river.\n\nAssumption: the standard deviation of the masses of fish of this species in this river is 0.25 kg, the same as for the UK.\n\nH₀: μ = 4.2\nH₁: μ < 4.2\n\nLet X̄ be the mean mass (kg) of a random sample of 100 fish from the river. Under H₀,\nX̄ ~ N(4.2, 0.25²/100), i.e. X̄ ~ N(4.2, 0.025²)\n\np-value = P(X̄ < 4.16) = P(Z < (4.16 − 4.2)/0.025) = P(Z < −1.6) = 0.0548 (3 s.f.)\n\n0.0548 > 0.05, so the result is not significant at the 5% level.\n\nDo not reject H₀.\n\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 5% significance level that the mean mass of fish of this species in this river is less than 4.2 kg, i.e. insufficient evidence that the environmentalist is correct.\n\n(Equivalently: the critical value is 4.2 − 1.645 × 0.025 = 4.159, and 4.16 > 4.159; or z = −1.6 > −1.645.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::11(a)": "Standardising, Y > μ − σ corresponds to Z > ((μ − σ) − μ)/σ = −1, where Z ~ N(0, 1).\n\nP(Y > μ − σ) = P(Z > −1) = P(Z < 1) = 0.841 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::11(b)": "P(Y > 45) = 0.2 ⇒ P(Y < 45) = 0.8\n(45 − μ)/σ = Φ⁻¹(0.8) = 0.84162\n45 − μ = 0.84162σ   (1)\n\nP(Y < 25) = 0.3\n(25 − μ)/σ = Φ⁻¹(0.3) = −0.52440\n25 − μ = −0.52440σ   (2)\n\n(1) − (2): 20 = 1.36602σ\nσ = 14.641...\n\nμ = 45 − 0.84162 × 14.641... = 32.677...\n\nμ = 32.7 and σ = 14.6 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::11(c)": "U ~ N(10, 4), so the standard deviation of U is 2; V ~ N(12, 9), so the standard deviation of V is 3. Let Z ~ N(0, 1).\n\nP(U < b) = P(Z < (b − 10)/2)\nP(V > c) = P(Z > (c − 12)/3) = P(Z < (12 − c)/3), by symmetry.\n\nThese are equal, so\n(b − 10)/2 = (12 − c)/3\nb − 10 = (2/3)(12 − c) = 8 − (2/3)c\n\nb = 18 − (2/3)c",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::12(a)": "Let p be the probability that the dice shows a six on one throw.\nH₀: p = 1/6, H₁: p < 1/6 (one-tailed, 5% level).\n\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(100, 1/6). Evidence of bias against six requires P(X ≤ x) ≤ 0.05.\n\nP(X ≤ 11) = 0.0777 (3 s.f.) > 0.05\nP(X ≤ 10) = 0.0427 (3 s.f.) < 0.05\n\nSo the critical region is X ≤ 10, and the largest value of X that would provide evidence at the 5% level that the dice is biased against six is 10.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::12(b)": "If the dice is fair, X ~ B(100, 1/6), and the test concludes there is significant evidence of bias against six when X is in the critical region X ≤ 10.\n\nP(X ≤ 10) = 0.0427 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(a)(i)": "Most points lie roughly along a straight line of gradient −1, running from about (5, 70) through (40, 40) towards (55, 15), and for these points x + y is roughly 75.\n\nk = 75 (any value from 70 to 80 is reasonable)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(a)(ii)": "x + y is the percentage using either public or motorised private transport, so the remaining employees (who worked from home, walked or cycled) make up about 100 − k percent.\n\n100 − 75 = 25, so approximately 25% of employees worked from home, walked or cycled.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(b)(i)": "The group with the highest percentage using motorised private transport (the points at the top left of the diagram, which also have the lowest public-transport use) will have the greater values of F, because travelling by private vehicle uses more fuel per person than travelling the same distance by public transport, where the fuel is shared among many passengers.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(b)(ii)": "Any two of, for example:\n1. The lengths of the journeys to work (distance travelled) in each LA.\n2. How many people travel in each vehicle used (the occupancy of each mode, e.g. amount of car sharing).\n(Other valid kinds: the types of vehicle or fuel used, or the proportion of electric vehicles.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(c)(i)": "Characteristic: both LAs have a large percentage of employees who walk, cycle or work from home. For A, x + y is only about 0.5 + 23 ≈ 24, and for B only about 29.5 + 4.5 = 34, so roughly 76% and 66% respectively use neither public nor motorised private transport, far more than the 20–30% typical of the other LAs.\n\nJustification: both are likely to be small areas, so journeys to work are short and there is less need to travel by vehicle.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(c)(ii)": "A is rural (countryside) while B is urban (a city).\n\nJustification: in A almost nobody uses public transport (x ≈ 0), which suggests public transport is hardly available there, as in a remote rural area. In B almost nobody uses motorised private transport (y ≈ 4.5) but about 30% use public transport, which suggests a city centre with good public transport and heavy traffic, so very few people drive to work.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::13(d)": "Yes, I agree. In the dense cluster at the top left many points overlap or are too close together to distinguish, and there are no fine gridlines, so it is not possible to read accurate values for individual LAs from the diagram.",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::14(a)": "Let D be the event that the tree has the disease and T the event that the test result is positive.\n\nP(D) = 0.35, P(T | D) = 0.95, P(T | not D) = 0.1\n\nP(T) = 0.35 × 0.95 + 0.65 × 0.1 = 0.3325 + 0.065 = 0.3975\n\nP(D | T) = P(D and T)/P(T)\n= (0.35 × 0.95)/(0.35 × 0.95 + 0.65 × 0.1)\n= 0.3325/0.3975\n= 133/159\n\nP(tree has the disease | positive result) = 0.836 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h240-2023-p2::14(b)": "Let p be the proportion of trees in county B that have the disease.\n\nP(positive result) = 0.95p + 0.1(1 − p)\n\nThe sample is large and random, so set this equal to the observed proportion 0.43:\n0.95p + 0.1(1 − p) = 0.43\n0.95p + 0.1 − 0.1p = 0.43\n0.85p = 0.33\np = 0.388 (3 s.f.)\n\nSo approximately 39% of the trees in county B have the disease.",










































  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::1": "Take natural logarithms of both sides:\nln(4^(2x+1)) = ln(5ˣ)\n(2x + 1) ln4 = x ln5\n\nExpand and collect the x terms:\n2x ln4 + ln4 = x ln5\n2x ln4 − x ln5 = −ln4\nx(2ln4 − ln5) = −ln4\n\nx = −ln4 / (2ln4 − ln5) = ln4 / (ln5 − 2ln4)\nx = 1.38629… / (1.60944… − 2.77259…) = 1.38629… / (−1.16315…) = −1.19184…\n\nx = −1.19 (3 s.f.)",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::2(a)": "R sin(x − α) = R sinx cosα − R cosx sinα\n\nComparing with 3sinx − 4cosx:\nR cosα = 3\nR sinα = 4\n\nR² = 3² + 4² = 25, so R = 5 (R > 0)\n\ntanα = 4/3, so α = 53.130…° (this lies in 0° < α < 90° since cosα and sinα are both positive)\n\n3sinx − 4cosx = 5 sin(x − 53.13°)",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::2(b)": "Using part (a):\n5 sin(x − 53.13°) = 2\nsin(x − 53.13°) = 2/5 = 0.4\n\nFor 0° < x < 90°, the angle x − 53.13° lies between −53.13° and 36.87°.\n\nThe principal value is arcsin(0.4) = 23.578°, which is in this interval. The other solution in [0°, 360°), 180° − 23.578° = 156.42°, is not in the interval.\n\nx − 53.130° = 23.578°\nx = 53.130° + 23.578° = 76.708°\n\nx = 76.7° (3 s.f.)",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::3(a)(i)": "f(x) = x³ + px + q\nf′(x) = 3x² + p\n\nf′(2) = 13:\n3(2)² + p = 13\n12 + p = 13\np = 1",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::3(a)(ii)": "Since (x − 2) is a factor of f(x), the factor theorem gives f(2) = 0:\n2³ + 2p + q = 0\n\nWith p = 1:\n8 + 2 + q = 0\nq = −10",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::3(b)": "With p = 1 and q = −10, the curve is y = x³ + x − 10.\n\nA translation by the vector (2, −3) moves the curve 2 units in the positive x-direction and 3 units in the negative y-direction:\n• replace x by (x − 2);\n• subtract 3.\n\ny = (x − 2)³ + (x − 2) − 10 − 3\n\nExpand (x − 2)³:\n(x − 2)³ = x³ + 3x²(−2) + 3x(−2)² + (−2)³ = x³ − 6x² + 12x − 8\n\nSo\ny = x³ − 6x² + 12x − 8 + x − 2 − 13\ny = x³ − 6x² + 13x − 23\n\n(a = −6, b = 13, c = −23)",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::4(a)": "Complete the square in x and in y:\nx² − 6x + y² + 10y + k = 0\n(x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 5)² − 25 + k = 0\n(x − 3)² + (y + 5)² = 34 − k\n\nThis is a circle with centre (3, −5) and radius² = 34 − k. For a circle the radius² must be positive:\n34 − k > 0\nk < 34\n\nThe set of possible values is {k : k < 34}.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::4(b)": "With k = −46, C is x² + y² − 6x + 10y − 46 = 0.\n\nDifferentiate implicitly with respect to x:\n2x + 2y (dy/dx) − 6 + 10 (dy/dx) = 0\n(2y + 10)(dy/dx) = 6 − 2x\ndy/dx = (6 − 2x)/(2y + 10) = (3 − x)/(y + 5)\n\nThe tangent has gradient ½ where\n(3 − x)/(y + 5) = ½\n2(3 − x) = y + 5\ny = 1 − 2x   (equivalently 2x + y = 1)\n\nSubstitute into the equation of C:\nx² + (1 − 2x)² − 6x + 10(1 − 2x) − 46 = 0\nx² + 1 − 4x + 4x² − 6x + 10 − 20x − 46 = 0\n5x² − 30x − 35 = 0\nx² − 6x − 7 = 0\n(x − 7)(x + 1) = 0\nx = 7 or x = −1\n\nx = 7: y = 1 − 14 = −13\nx = −1: y = 1 + 2 = 3\n\n(At both points y + 5 ≠ 0, so the gradient formula is valid there.)\n\nThe points are (7, −13) and (−1, 3).",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::5(a)": "x = 1 + 0.2t − cost ⇒ dx/dt = 0.2 + sint\n\nOn 0 ≤ t ≤ π, sint ≥ 0, so dx/dt > 0 and x increases from x = 0 (at t = 0) to x = 2 + 0.2π (at t = π). Also y = k sin²t ≥ 0, so the curve lies on or above the x-axis.\n\nArea of the emblem = ∫ y dx = ∫₀^π y (dx/dt) dt\n= ∫₀^π k sin²t (0.2 + sint) dt\n\nReplace sin²t by 1 − cos²t:\n= k ∫₀^π (1 − cos²t)(0.2 + sint) dt\n= k ∫₀^π (0.2 + sint − 0.2cos²t − sint cos²t) dt\n\nThe area must be 1 m², so\nk ∫₀^π (0.2 + sint − 0.2cos²t − sint cos²t) dt = 1, as required.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::5(b)": "Use 2cos²t = 1 + cos2t, so 0.2cos²t = 0.1(1 + cos2t) = 0.1 + 0.1cos2t.\n\nIntegrate each part:\n∫ (0.2 + sint) dt = 0.2t − cost\n∫ 0.2cos²t dt = ∫ (0.1 + 0.1cos2t) dt = 0.1t + 0.05sin2t\n∫ sint cos²t dt = −⅓cos³t   (since d/dt(cos³t) = −3cos²t sint)\n\nSo\n∫ (0.2 + sint − 0.2cos²t − sint cos²t) dt = 0.2t − cost − 0.1t − 0.05sin2t + ⅓cos³t\n= 0.1t − cost − 0.05sin2t + ⅓cos³t\n\nApply the limits:\nAt t = π: 0.1π − (−1) − 0 + ⅓(−1)³ = 0.1π + 1 − ⅓ = 0.1π + ⅔\nAt t = 0: 0 − 1 − 0 + ⅓(1)³ = −⅔\n\n∫₀^π (…) dt = (0.1π + ⅔) − (−⅔) = 0.1π + 4/3\n\nFrom part (a):\nk(0.1π + 4/3) = 1\nk = 1/(0.1π + 4/3)\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by 30:\nk = 30/(3π + 40)",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::6(a)": "Let d be the common difference.\nu₃ = u₁ + 2d and u₄ = u₃ + d, so\nd = u₄ − u₃ = (7/2)sinθ + √3 cosθ\n\nThen u₃ = u₁ + 2d gives\n−√3 cosθ = 2sinθ + 2((7/2)sinθ + √3 cosθ)\n−√3 cosθ = 2sinθ + 7sinθ + 2√3 cosθ\n−3√3 cosθ = 9sinθ\n\ncosθ ≠ 0 for ½π < θ < π, so divide by 9cosθ:\ntanθ = −3√3/9 = −√3/3\n\nIn the interval ½π < θ < π, tanθ is negative and takes each value once. The acute angle with tangent √3/3 is π/6, so\nθ = π − π/6 = 5π/6",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::6(b)": "With θ = 5π/6: sinθ = ½ and cosθ = −√3/2.\n\nFirst term: u₁ = 2sinθ = 1\nCommon difference: d = (7/2)sinθ + √3 cosθ = 7/4 − 3/2 = ¼\n\n(Check: u₃ = −√3(−√3/2) = 3/2 = 1 + 2(¼) and u₄ = (7/2)(½) = 7/4 = 3/2 + ¼.)\n\nΣ uᵣ (r = 1 to 100) = S₁₀₀ = (100/2)[2u₁ + (100 − 1)d]\n= 50[2(1) + 99(¼)]\n= 50(2 + 24.75)\n= 50 × 26.75\n= 1337.5",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::7(a)": "a = v(8t/(7 + 4t²) − ½)\n\nSince v > 0, a = 0 requires\n8t/(7 + 4t²) − ½ = 0\n\nMultiply by 2(7 + 4t²), which is positive:\n2(8t) − (7 + 4t²) = 0\n16t − 7 − 4t² = 0\n4t² − 16t + 7 = 0\n\nFactorise:\n(2t − 1)(2t − 7) = 0\nt = ½ or t = 7/2\n\nBoth values satisfy t ≥ 0, so the acceleration is zero when t = 0.5 s and when t = 3.5 s.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::7(b)": "Since a = dv/dt:\ndv/dt = v(8t/(7 + 4t²) − ½)\n\nSeparate the variables (v > 0):\n∫ (1/v) dv = ∫ (8t/(7 + 4t²) − ½) dt\n\nThe numerator 8t is the derivative of 7 + 4t², so\nln v = ln(7 + 4t²) − ½t + K\nwhere K is a constant (no modulus signs are needed because v > 0 and 7 + 4t² > 0).\n\nAt t = 0, v = 17.5:\nln17.5 = ln7 − 0 + K\nK = ln17.5 − ln7 = ln(5/2)\n\nSo ln v = ln(7 + 4t²) − ½t + ln(5/2).\n\nAt t = T, v = 5:\nln5 = ln(7 + 4T²) − ½T + ln(5/2)\n½T = ln(7 + 4T²) + ln(5/2) − ln5\n½T = ln[5(7 + 4T²)/(2 × 5)]\n½T = ln((7 + 4T²)/2)\n\nT = 2ln((7 + 4T²)/2), as required.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::7(c)": "Iterative formula: Tₙ₊₁ = 2ln((7 + 4Tₙ²)/2), with T₀ = 11.25\n\nT₁ = 11.09523…\nT₂ = 11.04058…\nT₃ = 11.02111…\nT₄ = 11.01415…\nT₅ = 11.01166…\nT₆ = 11.01077…\nT₇ = 11.01045…\n\nFrom T₄ onwards every iterate shown rounds to 11.01 (the values have settled to 4 s.f.), so\nT = 11.01",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::7(d)": "From part (a), the acceleration is zero at t = 0.5 and t = 3.5. For 0 ≤ t < 0.5 the bracket 8t/(7 + 4t²) − ½ is negative, for 0.5 < t < 3.5 it is positive, and for t > 3.5 it is negative again. So t = 0.5 gives the minimum on the graph and t = 3.5 gives the maximum speed. After t = 3.5 the car slows down and its speed is 5 m s⁻¹ at t = T = 11.01 (part (c)).\n\nTime taken = 11.01 − 3.5 = 7.51 s",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::8": "Let u m s⁻¹ be the velocity vector of P at t = 0.\n\nUsing v = u + at with constant acceleration (3i − 2j):\n6i = u + 4(3i − 2j)\n6i = u + 12i − 8j\nu = −6i + 8j\n\nThe speed at t = 0 is the magnitude of this vector:\n|u| = √((−6)² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10\n\nThe speed of P at t = 0 is 10 m s⁻¹.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::9(a)": "Add three forces to the diagram, each acting on B:\n• the weight, 10 N, acting vertically downwards;\n• the normal contact force R, acting perpendicular to the plane, away from the plane (upwards and to the left in the diagram);\n• the friction force F, acting along the plane up the slope (towards the higher end of the plane).\n\nFriction acts up the slope because both the weight and the 2 N horizontal force have components down the slope, so B would tend to slide down the plane.\n\nThe 2 N horizontal force is already shown, so the completed diagram has four forces: 2 N, 10 N, R and F.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::9(b)": "Let R N be the normal contact force and F N the friction force (up the plane).\n\nThe 2 N force is horizontal, pointing towards the lower end of the plane. Its component perpendicular to the plane is 2sinθ (directed away from the plane) and its component along the plane is 2cosθ (down the slope). The weight has components 10cosθ into the plane and 10sinθ down the slope.\n\nResolve perpendicular to the plane:\nR + 2sinθ = 10cosθ  ⇒  R = 10cosθ − 2sinθ\n\nResolve parallel to the plane:\nF = 2cosθ + 10sinθ\n\nB remains at rest, so F ≤ μR with μ = 0.8:\n2cosθ + 10sinθ ≤ 0.8(10cosθ − 2sinθ)\n\nMultiply by 5:\n10cosθ + 50sinθ ≤ 40cosθ − 8sinθ\n58sinθ ≤ 30cosθ\n\nθ is acute, so cosθ > 0 and we can divide by 58cosθ:\ntanθ ≤ 30/58 = 15/29\n\nThe greatest possible value of tanθ is 15/29 (≈ 0.517).",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::10(a)": "The resultant is\nR = (−4i + 2j) + (ai + bj) = (a − 4)i + (2 + b)j\n\nR is parallel to −i + 3j, so for some scalar λ:\n(a − 4)i + (2 + b)j = λ(−i + 3j)\n\nComparing components:\ni: a − 4 = −λ, so λ = 4 − a\nj: 2 + b = 3λ\n\nEliminate λ:\n2 + b = 3(4 − a)\n2 + b = 12 − 3a\n3a + b = 10, as required.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::10(b)": "With a = 6:\n3(6) + b = 10 ⇒ b = −8\n\nR = (6 − 4)i + (2 − 8)j = 2i − 6j\n\nMagnitude of the resultant force:\n|R| = √(2² + (−6)²) = √40 = 2√10 N\n\nNewton's second law (magnitude of resultant force = mass × magnitude of acceleration):\n√40 = m × 5√10\nm = √40/(5√10) = 2√10/(5√10) = 2/5\n\nm = 0.4",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::11(a)": "Let R_B N be the magnitude of the force on the rod at B. The wall is smooth, so this force is perpendicular to the wall. The wall is inclined at 55° to the horizontal, so the force at B acts at 55° to the vertical (35° to the horizontal), directed upwards and back towards A's side.\n\nThe rod makes 30° with the horizontal; the line of the force at B makes 145° with the same horizontal direction, so the angle between the rod and the force at B is 115°. The component of the force at B perpendicular to the rod is R_B sin115° = R_B cos25°.\n\nTake moments about A (this removes the unknown forces at A). The weight 20 N acts at the midpoint, 1.4 m from A, and its component perpendicular to the rod is 20cos30°.\n\nAnticlockwise moment of the force at B = clockwise moment of the weight:\nR_B × 2.8cos25° = 20cos30° × 1.4\n\nR_B = 28cos30°/(2.8cos25°) = 24.2487…/2.5376… = 9.5555…\n\nThe magnitude of the force on the rod at B is 9.56 N (3 s.f.), as required.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::11(b)": "At A, let R_A N be the normal reaction (vertically upwards) and F_A N the friction (horizontal). The force at B, R_B = 9.555… N, acts at 35° to the horizontal, so its components are:\nvertical (upwards): R_B sin35°\nhorizontal (away from the wall): R_B cos35°\n\nResolve vertically:\nR_A + R_B sin35° = 20\nR_A = 20 − 9.5555… × sin35° = 20 − 5.4808… = 14.519…\n\nResolve horizontally (friction at A acts towards the wall to balance the horizontal component of the force at B):\nF_A = R_B cos35° = 9.5555… × cos35° = 7.8274…\n\nThe contact force at the ground is the resultant of R_A and F_A:\nmagnitude = √(F_A² + R_A²) = √(7.8274…² + 14.519…²) = √272.07… = 16.494…\n\nThe magnitude of the contact force between the rod and the ground is 16.5 N (3 s.f.).",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::12(a)": "Take upwards as positive, with g = 10 m s⁻².\n\nInitial vertical component of velocity = 39sinθ = 39 × 5/13 = 15 m s⁻¹.\n\nAt the greatest height the vertical velocity is zero. Using v² = u² + 2as vertically, with h m the height risen above A:\n0 = 15² + 2(−10)h\n20h = 225\nh = 11.25\n\nA is 20 m above the ground, so the greatest height of P above the ground is\n20 + 11.25 = 31.25 m",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::12(b)": "Take upwards as positive. C is on the ground, 20 m below A, so the vertical displacement from A to C is −20 m.\n\nUsing s = ut + ½at² vertically with u = 39sinθ = 15 and a = −10:\n−20 = 15T + ½(−10)T²\n−20 = 15T − 5T²\n5T² − 15T − 20 = 0\nT² − 3T − 4 = 0\n(T − 4)(T + 1) = 0\n\nT = 4 or T = −1. Time must be positive, so T = −1 is rejected.\n\nT = 4",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::12(c)": "The value g = 10 m s⁻² is only an approximation (to 1 significant figure) of the acceleration due to gravity, so the calculated time T would differ if a more accurate value of g were used.",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::12(d)": "Horizontal motion of P: the horizontal component of velocity is constant,\n39cosθ = 39 × 12/13 = 36 m s⁻¹.\nP reaches C at time T = 4 s, so\nBC = 36 × 4 = 144 m.\n\nMotion of Q: Q starts at B at t = 0, so its displacement from B is\ns = ∫₀ᵗ v dt = ¼kt⁴ + 2t³ + ¾t²\n(there is no constant, since s = 0 at t = 0).\n\nP and Q collide at C, so Q reaches C at t = 4:\n¼k(4)⁴ + 2(4)³ + ¾(4)² = 144\n64k + 128 + 12 = 144\n64k = 4\nk = 1/16\n\nAcceleration of Q:\ndv/dt = 3kt² + 12t + 3/2\n\nAt t = 4 (immediately before the collision):\ndv/dt = 3(1/16)(16) + 12(4) + 1.5 = 3 + 48 + 1.5 = 52.5\n\nThe acceleration of Q immediately before the collision is 52.5 m s⁻².",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::13(a)": "Let T N be the tension, a m s⁻² the magnitude of the acceleration of each object, μ_B the coefficient of friction between B and the horizontal surface, and μ_P = 2μ_B the coefficient of friction between P and the inclined plane. Both objects are moving, so friction on each is limiting (F = μR).\n\nParticle P (4 kg), moving down the plane:\nPerpendicular to the plane: R_P = 4g cos60° = 2g\nFriction on P (up the plane): F_P = μ_P R_P = 2μ_P g\nNewton's second law down the plane:\n4g sin60° − F_P − T = 4a\n2√3 g − 2μ_P g − T = 4a   …(1)\n\nBlock B (2 kg), moving towards the pulley:\nNormal reaction: R_B = 2g, so friction F_B = μ_B(2g)\nNewton's second law towards the pulley:\nT − 2μ_B g = 2a\nMultiply by 2: 2T − 4μ_B g = 4a   …(2)\n\nSince μ_P = 2μ_B, the friction term in (1) is 2μ_P g = 4μ_B g. Subtract (2) from (1):\n2√3 g − 4μ_B g − T − 2T + 4μ_B g = 0\n2√3 g − T = 2T\n3T = 2√3 g\n\nT = (2√3/3)g  (≈ 1.15g) N",
  "math-ocr-h240-2023-p3::13(b)": "After the string breaks there is no tension.\n\nMotion of P: initial speed 2 m s⁻¹, distance 1.9 m in 0.5 s. Using s = ut + ½at² with a_P the acceleration of P down the plane:\n1.9 = 2(0.5) + ½a_P(0.5)²\n1.9 = 1 + 0.125a_P\na_P = 7.2 m s⁻²\n\nNewton's second law for P down the plane (R_P = 4g cos60° as before):\n4g sin60° − μ_P(4g cos60°) = 4a_P\n4(9.8)(√3/2) − μ_P(4)(9.8)(½) = 4(7.2)\n33.948… − 19.6μ_P = 28.8\nμ_P = 0.26266…\n\nSo μ_B = ½μ_P = 0.13133…\n\nMotion of B: B is still moving (towards the pulley) and the only horizontal force on it is friction μ_B(2g). Newton's second law:\n2 × deceleration = μ_B(2g)\ndeceleration = μ_B g = 0.13133… × 9.8 = 1.2870…\n\n(Exactly, deceleration = (49√3 − 72)/10.)\n\nThe deceleration of B is 1.29 m s⁻² (3 s.f.).",





































  "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::1": "For small θ (radians): sin 3θ ≈ 3θ and cos 4θ ≈ 1 − (4θ)²/2 = 1 − 8θ².\nSubstitute:\n(1 − cos 4θ) / (2 sin 3θ · θ) ≈ (1 − (1 − 8θ²)) / (2 × 3θ × θ) = 8θ² / (6θ²) = 4/3.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::2(a)": "Write y = x² − 2x − 24x^(1/2).\n(i) dy/dx = 2x − 2 − 12x^(−1/2).\n(ii) d²y/dx² = 2 + 6x^(−3/2).",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::2(b)": "Substitute x = 4 into dy/dx:\ndy/dx = 2(4) − 2 − 12(4)^(−1/2) = 8 − 2 − 12/2 = 8 − 2 − 6 = 0.\nSince dy/dx = 0 at x = 4, there is a stationary point at x = 4.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::2(c)": "Substitute x = 4 into d²y/dx²:\nd²y/dx² = 2 + 6(4)^(−3/2) = 2 + 6/8 = 2 + 0.75 = 2.75 > 0.\nSince d²y/dx² > 0, the stationary point at x = 4 is a minimum.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::3": "Let arc length AB = rθ and perimeter = 2r + rθ.\nGiven perimeter = 4 × arc: 2r + rθ = 4rθ ⟹ 2r = 3rθ ⟹ θ = 2/3.\nArea of sector: ½r²θ = 11 ⟹ ½r²(2/3) = 11 ⟹ r²/3 = 11 ⟹ r² = 33 ⟹ r = √33.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::4(a)": "Let f(x) = 2ln(8−x) − x.\nf(3) = 2ln5 − 3 ≈ 3.219 − 3 = 0.219 > 0.\nf(4) = 2ln4 − 4 ≈ 2.773 − 4 = −1.227 < 0.\nSince f is continuous and changes sign on [3, 4], by the intermediate value theorem there is a root α with 3 < α < 4.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::4(b)": "Starting from x₁ = 4, draw a vertical line to the curve y = 2ln(8−x) then a horizontal line to y = x, and repeat. From the graph, the cobweb diagram spirals inwards, converging to α. Therefore the iteration formula can be used to find an approximation for α.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::5": "y = 3sinθ/(2sinθ + 2cosθ). Apply the quotient rule with u = 3sinθ, v = 2sinθ + 2cosθ:\nu′ = 3cosθ, v′ = 2cosθ − 2sinθ.\ndy/dθ = [3cosθ(2sinθ+2cosθ) − 3sinθ(2cosθ−2sinθ)] / (2sinθ+2cosθ)².\nNumerator: 6sinθcosθ + 6cos²θ − 6sinθcosθ + 6sin²θ = 6(sin²θ+cos²θ) = 6.\nDenominator: (2sinθ+2cosθ)² = 4(sinθ+cosθ)² = 4(1 + 2sinθcosθ) = 4(1 + sin2θ).\nTherefore dy/dθ = 6/(4(1+sin2θ)) = 3/(2(1+sin2θ)), so A = 3/2.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::6(a)": "The tangent l has gradient 2, so the radius PA (perpendicular to l) has gradient −1/2.\nLine through A(7, 5) with gradient −1/2: y − 5 = −½(x − 7) ⟹ 2y − 10 = −x + 7 ⟹ x + 2y = 17, which is the same as 2y + x = 17, as required.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::6(b)": "Find P: solve y = 2x + 1 and x + 2y = 17 simultaneously.\nSubstitute: x + 2(2x+1) = 17 ⟹ 5x = 15 ⟹ x = 3, y = 7. So P = (3, 7).\nRadius² = (7−3)² + (5−7)² = 16 + 4 = 20.\nEquation of C: (x − 7)² + (y − 5)² = 20.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::6(c)": "The second tangent y = 2x + k is parallel to y = 2x + 1 and also tangent to the circle.\nSubstitute into the circle: (x−7)² + (2x+k−5)² = 20.\nExpand and set discriminant = 0:\n(x−7)² + (2x+k−5)² = 20. Let me use the perpendicular distance method:\nDistance from centre (7, 5) to line 2x − y + k = 0 equals radius √20:\n|2(7) − 5 + k|/√(4+1) = √20 ⟹ |9 + k|/√5 = 2√5 ⟹ |9 + k| = 10.\n9 + k = 10 ⟹ k = 1 (the original tangent) or 9 + k = −10 ⟹ k = −19.\nSince k ≠ 1, the answer is k = −19.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::7(a)": "∫ₖ^(3k) 2/(3x−k) dx.\nAntiderivative: (2/3)ln|3x−k|.\nEvaluate: (2/3)[ln(9k−k) − ln(3k−k)] = (2/3)[ln(8k) − ln(2k)] = (2/3)ln(8k/2k) = (2/3)ln 4.\nThis is independent of k.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::7(b)": "∫ₖ^(2k) 2/(2x−k)² dx.\nAntiderivative of 2(2x−k)^(−2): use the chain rule — ∫ 2/(2x−k)² dx = −1/(2x−k) + C.\nEvaluate from k to 2k: [−1/(2x−k)]ₖ^(2k) = −1/(4k−k) − (−1/(2k−k)) = −1/(3k) + 1/k = 2/(3k).\nSince 2/(3k) = (2/3) × (1/k), the result is inversely proportional to k.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::8(a)": "At 6:30 am, t = 6.5.\nD = 5 + 2sin(30 × 6.5)° = 5 + 2sin(195°) = 5 + 2(−sin15°) ≈ 5 + 2(−0.2588) ≈ 4.48 m.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::8(b)": "Loading finishes at 8:30 am, i.e. t = 8.5. We need the first time after t = 8.5 when D = 3.8.\n5 + 2sin(30t)° = 3.8 ⇒ sin(30t)° = −0.6.\nAt t = 8.5, 30t = 255° and sin 255° ≈ −0.966, so D ≈ 3.07 m < 3.8 m and the boat cannot leave yet.\nsin⁻¹(0.6) = 36.87°, so the solutions of sin θ = −0.6 for 0° ≤ θ < 360° are\nθ = 180° + 36.87° = 216.87° and θ = 360° − 36.87° = 323.13°.\n30t = 216.87 gives t = 7.23, which is before 8.5.\n30t = 323.13 gives t = 10.771, the first value after 8.5.\nt = 10.771 hours = 10 hours and 0.771 × 60 ≈ 46 minutes after midnight.\nEarliest leaving time: 10:46 am.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::9(a)": "Differentiate x² − 2xy + 3y² = 50 implicitly:\n2x − 2y − 2x(dy/dx) + 6y(dy/dx) = 0.\nCollect dy/dx: (6y − 2x)(dy/dx) = 2y − 2x.\ndy/dx = (2y − 2x)/(6y − 2x) = (y − x)/(3y − x), as required.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::9(b)": "At P (furthest west), the tangent is vertical so dy/dx is undefined, meaning the denominator is zero:\n3y − x = 0 ⟹ x = 3y.\nSubstitute into x² − 2xy + 3y² = 50:\n9y² − 6y² + 3y² = 6y² = 50 ⟹ y² = 25/3 ⟹ y = ±5/√3 = ±5√3/3.\nP is furthest west (most negative x), so x = 3y with the negative y:\ny = −5√3/3, x = −5√3.\nP = (−5√3, −5√3/3).",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::9(c)": "Set dy/dx = 0, i.e. numerator = 0: y − x = 0 ⟹ y = x. Substitute y = x into x² − 2xy + 3y² = 50 and solve for x (taking the positive solution for the northernmost point).",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::10(a)": "Separate variables: (1/H)dH = cos(0.25t)/40 dt.\nIntegrate both sides: ln H = (1/40) × 4sin(0.25t) + c = (1/10)sin(0.25t) + c.\nAt t = 0, H = 5: ln 5 = 0 + c ⟹ c = ln 5.\nln H − ln 5 = (1/10)sin(0.25t) ⟹ ln(H/5) = (1/10)sin(0.25t) ⟹ H = 5e^(0.1sin(0.25t)), as required.",

 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::1": "The x term comes only from products of a constant term and an x term (no other pair of powers adds to 1).\nFrom (3x)(1) = 3x\nFrom (−2)(4x) = −8x\nTotal: 3x − 8x = −5x\nCoefficient of x = −5\nAnswer: −5",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::2": "Let y = eˣ + 1.\nThen eˣ = y − 1, so x = ln(y − 1).\nSwapping x and y: f⁻¹(x) = ln(x − 1)\nAnswer: f⁻¹(x) = ln(x − 1) (the first box)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::3": "Divide 12x² + 3x + 7 by 3x − 5.\nThe leading term of the quotient is 12x² ÷ 3x = 4x, so A = 4.\n(Completing the division: 12x² + 3x + 7 = (3x − 5)(4x + 23/3) + 136/3, so B = 23/3 and C = 136/3.)\nAnswer: A = 4",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::4": "y = arccos x has domain −1 ≤ x ≤ 1 and range 0 ≤ y ≤ π.\nIt is a decreasing function, with arccos(−1) = π, arccos(0) = π/2 and arccos(1) = 0.\nSo the graph starts at (−1, π), passes through (0, π/2) and ends at (1, 0).\nOnly the bottom-right graph has this shape (decreasing, crossing the y-axis at π/2 and ending on the x-axis at x = 1).\nAnswer: the bottom-right graph",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::5": "sin²x = 1\n⇒ sin x = 1 or sin x = −1\nsin x = 1 ⇒ x = 90°\nsin x = −1 ⇒ x = 270°\nThere are no other solutions in 0° < x < 360°.\nAnswer: x = 90°, 270°",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::6": "Let u = x³ + 5x, so y = u⁷.\ndy/du = 7u⁶ and du/dx = 3x² + 5\ndy/dx = dy/du × du/dx = 7(x³ + 5x)⁶ × (3x² + 5)\nAnswer: dy/dx = 7(3x² + 5)(x³ + 5x)⁶",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::7": "First simplify √(8n): √(8n) = √4 × √(2n) = 2√(2n).\nSo (3 + √(8n))/(1 + √(2n)) = (3 + 2√(2n))/(1 + √(2n)).\nMultiply the numerator and denominator by (1 − √(2n)). This is non-zero, because √(2n) = 1 would need n = ½, which is not a positive integer.\nNumerator: (3 + 2√(2n))(1 − √(2n)) = 3 − 3√(2n) + 2√(2n) − 2(2n) = 3 − √(2n) − 4n\nDenominator: (1 + √(2n))(1 − √(2n)) = 1 − 2n\nSo the expression = (3 − √(2n) − 4n)/(1 − 2n)\nMultiply the numerator and denominator by −1:\n= (4n − 3 + √(2n))/(2n − 1)\nas required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::8(a)": "Using the binomial expansion of (2 + kx)⁵:\nConstant term: 2⁵ = 32\nx term: 5C1 × 2⁴ × (kx) = 5 × 16 × kx = 80kx\nx² term: 5C2 × 2³ × (kx)² = 10 × 8 × k²x² = 80k²x²\nAnswer: (2 + kx)⁵ = 32 + 80kx + 80k²x² + …",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::8(b)": "From part (a): coefficient of x = 80k, coefficient of x² = 80k².\n80k = 4 × 80k²\n80k = 320k²\n320k² − 80k = 0\n80k(4k − 1) = 0\nk = 0 or k = ¼\nk is a positive constant, so k = 0 is rejected.\nAnswer: k = ¼",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::9(a)": "For small θ (in radians): sin θ ≈ θ, tan θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2.\ncos 4θ ≈ 1 − (4θ)²/2 = 1 − 8θ²\n2 sin 3θ ≈ 2(3θ) = 6θ\ntan 2θ ≈ 2θ\nSo cos 4θ + 2 sin 3θ − tan 2θ ≈ 1 − 8θ² + 6θ − 2θ\n= 1 + 4θ − 8θ²\nAnswer: A = 1, B = 4, C = −8",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::9(b)": "cos 0.28 + 2 sin 0.21 − tan 0.14 matches cos 4θ + 2 sin 3θ − tan 2θ with θ = 0.07 (since 4 × 0.07 = 0.28, 3 × 0.07 = 0.21 and 2 × 0.07 = 0.14).\nUsing part (a):\n1 + 4(0.07) − 8(0.07)²\n= 1 + 0.28 − 8(0.0049)\n= 1 + 0.28 − 0.0392\n= 1.2408\nAnswer: 1.241 (to 3 decimal places)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::10(a)": "For an arithmetic series, Sₙ = n/2 (a + l), where a is the first term and l the last term.\nn = 300, a = −7, l = 32\nS₃₀₀ = 300/2 × (−7 + 32)\n= 150 × 25\n= 3750\nAnswer: 3750",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::10(b)": "Let the bottom prize be £a and the top prize be £l. The nine prizes form an arithmetic sequence with first term a and ninth term l.\nTotal value: S₉ = 9/2 (a + l) = 1260\n⇒ a + l = 280\nThe top prize is six times the bottom prize: l = 6a\nSubstituting: a + 6a = 280\n7a = 280\na = 40, so l = 6 × 40 = 240\nAnswer: the top prize is worth £240",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::11(a)": "f(x) = x(x − a)(x − 6) with 0 < a < 6.\nExpanding, the x³ coefficient is +1, so it is a positive cubic: y → −∞ as x → −∞ and y → +∞ as x → +∞.\nRoots: x = 0, x = a and x = 6, all single roots, so the curve crosses the x-axis at each. Since 0 < a < 6, they are in the order 0, a, 6.\nThe y-intercept is f(0) = 0, so the curve passes through the origin.\nSketch (described): coming up from the bottom left, the curve crosses the x-axis at O, rises to a maximum point between 0 and a, comes down to cross the x-axis at x = a, falls to a minimum point between a and 6, then rises to cross the x-axis at x = 6 and continues upwards.\nLabel the x-intercepts O (0), a and 6.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::11(b)": "y = f(−2x) is obtained from y = f(x) by a reflection in the y-axis and a stretch, scale factor ½, parallel to the x-axis.\nAlgebraically: f(−2x) = (−2x)(−2x − a)(−2x − 6) = −2x(2x + a)(2x + 6)\nThe x³ coefficient is −8, so it is a negative cubic: y → +∞ as x → −∞ and y → −∞ as x → +∞.\nRoots: −2x = 0, a or 6 ⇒ x = 0, x = −a/2, x = −3. Since 0 < a < 6, we have −3 < −a/2 < 0.\nThe curve passes through the origin.\nSketch (described): coming down from the top left, the curve crosses the x-axis at x = −3, falls to a minimum point between −3 and −a/2, rises to cross the x-axis at x = −a/2, reaches a maximum point between −a/2 and 0, then falls through the origin O and continues downwards.\nLabel the x-intercepts −3, −a/2 and O.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::12(a)": "u₂ = −6/u₁ = −6/3 = −2\nu₃ = −6/u₂ = −6/(−2) = 3\nu₄ = −6/u₃ = −6/3 = −2\nAnswer: u₂ = −2, u₃ = 3, u₄ = −2",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::12(b)": "The terms are 3, −2, 3, −2, …, so the sequence repeats every 2 terms (u₃ = u₁, and each term depends only on the previous one).\nAnswer: the period is 2",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::12(c)": "The terms alternate: u₁ = 3, u₂ = −2, u₃ = 3, …, with odd-numbered terms equal to 3 and even-numbered terms equal to −2.\nFrom n = 1 to 101 there are 51 odd values of n and 50 even values of n.\nPair the first 100 terms: (3 + (−2)) + (3 + (−2)) + … = 50 pairs, each summing to 1, giving 50.\nThe last term u₁₀₁ = 3.\nΣ uₙ (n = 1 to 101) = 50 + 3 = 53\n(Check: 51 × 3 + 50 × (−2) = 153 − 100 = 53.)\nAnswer: 53",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::13(a)": "By the factor theorem, (2x + 1) is a factor of P(x) if P(−½) = 0.\nP(−½) = 4(−½)³ + 8(−½)² + 11(−½) + 4\n= 4(−⅛) + 8(¼) − 11/2 + 4\n= −½ + 2 − 11/2 + 4\n= 0\nSince P(−½) = 0, (2x + 1) is a factor of P(x).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::13(b)": "P(x) = (2x + 1)(ax² + bx + c)\nComparing x³ coefficients: 2a = 4 ⇒ a = 2\nComparing constant terms: c = 4\nComparing x² coefficients: 2b + a = 8 ⇒ 2b = 6 ⇒ b = 3\nCheck the x coefficient: 2c + b = 8 + 3 = 11 ✓\nAnswer: P(x) = (2x + 1)(2x² + 3x + 4), so a = 2, b = 3, c = 4",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::13(c)": "By part (b), 4n³ + 8n² + 11n + 4 = (2n + 1)(2n² + 3n + 4).\nFor a positive integer n, both factors are integers.\nSince n ≥ 1:\n2n + 1 ≥ 3, so 2n + 1 ≠ 1\n2n² + 3n + 4 ≥ 2 + 3 + 4 = 9, so 2n² + 3n + 4 ≠ 1\nSo 4n³ + 8n² + 11n + 4 is the product of two integers, each greater than 1. A prime has no such factorisation (its only positive factors are 1 and itself), so 4n³ + 8n² + 11n + 4 is never prime.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::14(a)": "Rearrange to equal zero: x³ − e^(6 − 2x) = 0.\nLet D(x) = x³ − e^(6 − 2x), which is continuous.\nD(0) = 0 − e⁶ = −e⁶ ≈ −403 < 0\nD(4) = 64 − e^(−2) ≈ 63.86 > 0\nThere is a change of sign between x = 0 and x = 4 and D is continuous, so the root α lies between 0 and 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::14(b)": "x³ = e^(6 − 2x)\nTake natural logarithms of both sides (both sides are positive at any solution, since e^(6 − 2x) > 0):\nln(x³) = 6 − 2x\nUsing the log law ln(x³) = 3 ln x:\n3 ln x = 6 − 2x\n2x = 6 − 3 ln x\nx = 3 − (3/2) ln x\nas required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::14(c)(i)": "xₙ₊₁ = 3 − (3/2) ln xₙ, x₁ = 4\nx₂ = 3 − 1.5 ln 4 = 0.92055… ≈ 0.921\nx₃ = 3 − 1.5 ln(0.92055…) = 3.12416… ≈ 3.124\nx₄ = 3 − 1.5 ln(3.12416…) = 1.29125… ≈ 1.291\nAnswer: x₂ = 0.921, x₃ = 3.124, x₄ = 1.291 (to 3 d.p.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::14(c)(ii)": "The curve y = 3 − (3/2) ln x is decreasing, so the iteration gives a cobweb diagram, spiralling in towards the intersection with y = x (at x = α ≈ 1.98).\nConstruction (described):\n1. Start at x = 4 on the x-axis and draw a vertical line up to the curve, reaching the point (4, x₂) with x₂ ≈ 0.92.\n2. Draw a horizontal line from there across to the line y = x, reaching (x₂, x₂).\n3. Draw a vertical line from (x₂, x₂) up to the curve, reaching (x₂, x₃) with x₃ ≈ 3.12.\n4. Draw a horizontal line across to y = x, reaching (x₃, x₃).\n5. Draw a vertical line down to the curve, reaching (x₃, x₄) with x₄ ≈ 1.29.\n6. Draw a horizontal line across to y = x, reaching (x₄, x₄).\nDraw dashed vertical lines from (x₂, x₂), (x₃, x₃) and (x₄, x₄) down to the x-axis and label the positions x₂ (≈ 0.92), x₃ (≈ 3.12) and x₄ (≈ 1.29) on the x-axis.\nOn the x-axis the order is x₂, x₄, α, x₃, 4: the values lie alternately either side of α and get closer to it, which shows the convergence.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::14(c)(iii)": "With x₁ = 0, finding x₂ needs ln x₁ = ln 0, which is undefined (ln x is only defined for x > 0).\nSo x₂ cannot be evaluated, and the iteration cannot even start, so it cannot converge to α.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::15(a)": "Use sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ and cos 2θ = 2cos²θ − 1, with cosec θ = 1/sin θ and sec θ = 1/cos θ (allowed since sin θ ≠ 0 and cos θ ≠ 0).\nsin 2θ cosec θ + cos 2θ sec θ\n= 2 sin θ cos θ × (1/sin θ) + (2cos²θ − 1) × (1/cos θ)\n= 2 cos θ + 2cos²θ/cos θ − 1/cos θ\n= 2 cos θ + 2 cos θ − sec θ\n= 4 cos θ − sec θ\nas required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::15(b)(i)": "The value cos θ = 1 must be rejected.\nIf cos θ = 1, then sin θ = 0 (θ = 0° or 360°), so cosec θ = 1/sin θ is undefined. The original equation contains cosec θ, so it has no meaning for these values (part (a) also needs sin θ ≠ 0).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::15(b)(ii)": "Only cos θ = −0.25 is valid.\nθ = cos⁻¹(−0.25) = 104.48…° ≈ 104.5°\nand θ = 360° − 104.48…° = 255.52…° ≈ 255.5°\nFor these values sin θ ≠ 0 and cos θ ≠ 0, so they are valid.\nAnswer: θ = 104.5°, 255.5°",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::16": "The volume of the pipe = (area of cross-section) × (length).\nThe cross-section is the region between the x-axis and the curve from x = −2 to x = 2. The curve lies below the x-axis, so use the size of the y values. By symmetry, the area = 2 × (area from x = 0 to x = 2).\nTrapezium rule from x = 0 to 2 with strip width h = 0.4 (5 strips):\nArea ≈ (h/2)[y₀ + y₅ + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃ + y₄)]\n= (0.4/2)[3 + 0 + 2(2.943 + 2.752 + 2.353 + 1.572)]\n= 0.2[3 + 2(9.62)]\n= 0.2 × 22.24\n= 4.448 cm²\nTotal cross-section area ≈ 2 × 4.448 = 8.896 cm²\nLength of pipe = 1.5 m = 150 cm\nVolume ≈ 8.896 × 150 = 1334.4 cm³\nAnswer: volume ≈ 1300 cm³ (to 2 s.f.; equivalently about 0.0013 m³)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::17(a)": "|x| ≥ 0 for all real x, with |0| = 0, so f(x) = |x| + 1 ≥ 1, and every value from 1 upwards is taken.\nAnswer: range of f = {y : y ≥ 1} (that is, [1, ∞))",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::17(b)": "ln x is only defined for x > 0, so the greatest possible domain of g is\n{x : x > 0} (that is, (0, ∞)).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::17(c)(i)": "h(x) = gf(x) = g(f(x)) = g(|x| + 1)\nAnswer: h(x) = ln(|x| + 1)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::17(c)(ii)": "h does not have an inverse, because h is not one-to-one (it is many-to-one).\nFor example, h(1) = ln(1 + 1) = ln 2 and h(−1) = ln(|−1| + 1) = ln 2.\nTwo different inputs give the same output, so h⁻¹(ln 2) could not be defined uniquely; therefore h has no inverse.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::18(a)": "Let u = 2x + 1.\nThen du/dx = 2, so dx = ½ du.\nAlso 2x = u − 1, so 4x + 1 = 2(u − 1) + 1 = 2u − 1.\nLimits: x = 0 ⇒ u = 1; x = 4 ⇒ u = 9.\n∫₀⁴ (4x + 1)(2x + 1)^(1/2) dx\n= ∫₁⁹ (2u − 1)u^(1/2) × ½ du\n= ½ ∫₁⁹ (2u^(3/2) − u^(1/2)) du\nThis is the required form with a = 1.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::18(b)": "From part (a):\n∫₀⁴ (4x + 1)(2x + 1)^(1/2) dx = ½ ∫₁⁹ (2u^(3/2) − u^(1/2)) du\n= ½ [2u^(5/2)/(5/2) − u^(3/2)/(3/2)] from 1 to 9\n= ½ [4u^(5/2)/5 − 2u^(3/2)/3] from 1 to 9\nAt u = 9: 9^(5/2) = 243 and 9^(3/2) = 27, so 4(243)/5 − 2(27)/3 = 972/5 − 18 = 882/5\nAt u = 1: 4/5 − 2/3 = 2/15\nDifference: 882/5 − 2/15 = 2646/15 − 2/15 = 2644/15\n× ½: 1322/15\nSo ∫₀⁴ (4x + 1)(2x + 1)^(1/2) dx = 1322/15, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::18(c)": "The rectangles all lie under the curve (their tops lie on or below it), so their total area A is an underestimate of the area under the curve, which is the exact value 1322/15 found in part (b).\nUsing more, narrower rectangles drawn the same way leaves smaller gaps between the rectangles and the curve. For example, splitting each rectangle into two narrower ones keeps all the original area and adds extra area that was previously missed. So the improved approximation increases, giving a value greater than A.\nHowever many rectangles are used, they are still all under the curve, so their total is always less than the exact area under the curve. So the improved approximation is less than 1322/15.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::19": "y³e^(2x) + 2y − 16x = k\nDifferentiate implicitly with respect to x, using the product rule on y³e^(2x):\nd/dx(y³e^(2x)) = 3y² (dy/dx) e^(2x) + y³ × 2e^(2x)\nSo 3y²e^(2x) (dy/dx) + 2y³e^(2x) + 2 (dy/dx) − 16 = 0\nThe stationary point is on the y-axis, so x = 0 and dy/dx = 0 there:\n0 + 2y³e⁰ + 0 − 16 = 0\n2y³ = 16\ny³ = 8\ny = 2\nSo the stationary point is (0, 2).\nSubstitute x = 0, y = 2 into the equation of the curve:\nk = 2³e⁰ + 2(2) − 16(0) = 8 + 4 = 12\nAnswer: k = 12",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::20(a)": "The depth decreases at a rate proportional to h − 5, so\ndh/dt = −k(h − 5), where k is a positive constant (negative sign because h is decreasing).\nInitially the container is full, so h = 130, and the depth is decreasing at 1.5 cm per minute, so dh/dt = −1.5.\n−1.5 = −k(130 − 5)\n−1.5 = −125k\nk = 1.5/125 = 0.012\nSo dh/dt = −0.012(h − 5), as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::20(b)": "dh/dt = −0.012(h − 5)\nSeparate the variables (h > 5 while the container is draining through the hose):\n∫ 1/(h − 5) dh = ∫ −0.012 dt\nln(h − 5) = −0.012t + c\nh − 5 = e^(−0.012t + c) = Ce^(−0.012t), where C = e^c\nWhen t = 0, h = 130: 130 − 5 = Ce⁰ ⇒ C = 125\nAnswer: h = 5 + 125e^(−0.012t)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p1::20(c)": "The container is 130 cm tall, so it is half empty when the depth is h = 130/2 = 65 cm.\nUsing part (b):\n5 + 125e^(−0.012t) = 65\n125e^(−0.012t) = 60\ne^(−0.012t) = 60/125 = 0.48\n−0.012t = ln 0.48\nt = −ln 0.48 / 0.012 = 61.16…\nAnswer: it takes 61 minutes (to the nearest minute)",





































 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::1": "A circle with centre (a, b) and radius r has equation (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², where both squared terms are added and r² > 0.\nThe two equations with a minus sign between the squared terms are not circles (they are hyperbolas).\n(x + 1)² + (y + 2)² = −36 is impossible, since a sum of squares cannot be negative.\nSo the circle is\n(x + 1)² + (y + 2)² = 36\n(centre (−1, −2), radius 6).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::2": "Region A lies above the x-axis, so ∫₋₃⁰ f(x) dx = +189.\nRegion B lies below the x-axis, so ∫₀² f(x) dx = −64.\n∫₋₃² f(x) dx = 189 + (−64) = 125\nAnswer: 125",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::3": "(1 − x)(x − 4) = −(x − 1)(x − 4), so the inequality is equivalent to\n(x − 1)(x − 4) > 0\nThe critical values are x = 1 and x = 4. The quadratic (x − 1)(x − 4) is a positive (U-shaped) parabola, so it is positive outside the roots:\nx < 1 or x > 4\nThe ends x = 1 and x = 4 give 0, which is not < 0, so they are excluded. \"or\" means union:\n{x : x < 1} ∪ {x : x > 4}",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::4": "5^(x − 2) = 7^1570\nTake logs to base 5 of both sides:\nlog₅ 5^(x − 2) = log₅ 7^1570\nx − 2 = 1570 log₅ 7      (using log Aⁿ = n log A)\nx = 2 + 1570 log₅ 7\nlog₅ 7 = ln 7 / ln 5 = 1.209061…\nx = 2 + 1570 × 1.209061… = 2 + 1898.227… = 1900.227…\nx = 1900.23 (2 d.p.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::5": "y = x³/sin x\nQuotient rule with u = x³ and v = sin x:\ndu/dx = 3x²,  dv/dx = cos x\ndy/dx = (v du/dx − u dv/dx)/v²\ndy/dx = (3x² sin x − x³ cos x)/sin²x",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::6": "Expand each bracket:\n(2 sin θ + 3 cos θ)² = 4 sin²θ + 12 sin θ cos θ + 9 cos²θ\n(6 sin θ − cos θ)² = 36 sin²θ − 12 sin θ cos θ + cos²θ\nAdding, the sin θ cos θ terms cancel:\n40 sin²θ + 10 cos²θ = 30\nUse cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ:\n40 sin²θ + 10(1 − sin²θ) = 30\n30 sin²θ + 10 = 30\nsin²θ = 2/3\nsin θ = ±√(2/3) = ±√6/3\nθ is obtuse (90° < θ < 180°), so θ is in the second quadrant, where sin θ > 0. Reject the negative value.\nsin θ = √6/3",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::7(a)": "At the start of month 3, Kate adds £50 to the balance T₂, and then interest of 0.2% multiplies the total by 1.002:\nT₃ = (T₂ + 50) × 1.002\n= (50 × 1.002² + 50 × 1.002 + 50) × 1.002\n= 50 × 1.002³ + 50 × 1.002² + 50 × 1.002, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::7(b)(i)": "Following the pattern, after n months\nTₙ = 50 × 1.002 + 50 × 1.002² + … + 50 × 1.002ⁿ\nThis is a geometric series with first term 50 × 1.002 = 50.1, common ratio 1.002, and n terms.\n10 years = 120 months, so n = 120:\nT₁₂₀ = 50.1(1 − 1.002¹²⁰)/(1 − 1.002)\n1.002¹²⁰ = 1.270944…\nT₁₂₀ = 50.1 × (−0.270944…)/(−0.002) = 6787.1595…\nKate expects to have £6787.16 (about £6787).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::7(b)(ii)": "The calculation assumes the interest rate stays at 0.2% per month for the whole 10 years and that Kate pays in exactly £50 every month and never withdraws anything.\nIn reality the interest rate is unlikely to stay fixed for 10 years (and Kate may change her monthly payments or withdraw some money), so her actual balance may differ.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::8(a)(i)": "Substitute x = 24, y = 12 into y = a + b log₁₀ x:\n12 = a + b log₁₀ 24",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::8(a)(ii)": "12 = a + b log₁₀ 24   (1)\n6.4 = a + b log₁₀ 3   (2)\n(1) − (2) eliminates a:\n5.6 = b log₁₀ 24 − b log₁₀ 3\n5.6 = b log₁₀ (24/3)\n5.6 = b log₁₀ 8\nb = 5.6/log₁₀ 8, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::8(a)(iii)": "b = 5.6/log₁₀ 8 = 6.20093…\nFrom 6.4 = a + b log₁₀ 3:\na = 6.4 − 6.20093… × log₁₀ 3\na = 6.4 − 6.20093… × 0.47712… = 6.4 − 2.95860… = 3.44140…\na = 3.44 (2 d.p.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::8(b)": "x is measured in months, and one week is about a quarter of a month, so take x = 0.25.\ny = 3.44 + 6.20 log₁₀ 0.25\n= 3.44 + 6.20 × (−0.60206)\n= −0.29 (to 2 d.p.)\nSince b > 0, y increases with x, so for every age less than one week (x < 0.25) the model gives an even smaller value, below −0.29.\nThe model predicts a negative median mass, which is impossible, so the model cannot be used to predict the median mass of monkeys less than one week old.\n(Equivalently, y = 0 when x ≈ 0.28 months, so the model gives a negative or zero mass for all ages up to about 8 days.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::9(a)(i)": "(1 + 3x)⁻¹ = 1 + (−1)(3x) + [(−1)(−2)/2!](3x)² + …\n= 1 − 3x + 9x² + …\nUp to the x² term: 1 − 3x + 9x²",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::9(a)(ii)": "1/(2 − 3x) = (2 − 3x)⁻¹\n= 2⁻¹(1 − 3x/2)⁻¹\n= ½(1 + (−3x/2))⁻¹\nExpand (1 + (−3x/2))⁻¹:\n= 1 + (−1)(−3x/2) + [(−1)(−2)/2!](−3x/2)² + …\n= 1 + (3/2)x + (9/4)x² + …\nSo 1/(2 − 3x) ≈ ½[1 + (3/2)x + (9/4)x²] = ½ + ¾x + (9/8)x²\nThe first three terms are ½, ¾x, (9/8)x².\n(¾x) ÷ ½ = (3/2)x\n((9/8)x²) ÷ (¾x) = (3/2)x\nEach term is the previous term multiplied by the same factor, so the terms form a geometric sequence.\nCommon ratio = 3x/2",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::9(b)": "36x/((1 + 3x)(2 − 3x)) ≡ P/(2 − 3x) + Q/(1 + 3x)\nMultiply through by (1 + 3x)(2 − 3x):\n36x ≡ P(1 + 3x) + Q(2 − 3x)\nLet x = −⅓: 36(−⅓) = P(0) + Q(2 + 1)\n−12 = 3Q, so Q = −4\nLet x = ⅔: 36(⅔) = P(1 + 2) + Q(0)\n24 = 3P, so P = 8\nP = 8, Q = −4",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::9(c)(i)": "From (b): 36x/((1 + 3x)(2 − 3x)) = 8/(2 − 3x) − 4/(1 + 3x)\nUsing (a)(ii) and (a)(i):\n8/(2 − 3x) ≈ 8(½ + ¾x + (9/8)x²) = 4 + 6x + 9x²\n4/(1 + 3x) ≈ 4(1 − 3x + 9x²) = 4 − 12x + 36x²\nSo 36x/((1 + 3x)(2 − 3x)) ≈ (4 + 6x + 9x²) − (4 − 12x + 36x²) = 18x − 27x²\n12x is one third of 36x, so divide by 3:\n12x/((1 + 3x)(2 − 3x)) ≈ 6x − 9x²",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::9(c)(ii)": "The expansion of (1 + 3x)⁻¹ is valid for |3x| < 1, i.e. |x| < ⅓.\nThe expansion of (2 − 3x)⁻¹ = ½(1 − 3x/2)⁻¹ is valid for |3x/2| < 1, i.e. |x| < ⅔.\nBoth expansions must be valid, so take the narrower range:\n|x| < ⅓   (that is, −⅓ < x < ⅓)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::10": "f(x) = x² + 2 cos x\nf′(x) = 2x − 2 sin x\nf″(x) = 2 − 2 cos x\nAt x = 0: f″(0) = 2 − 2 cos 0 = 2 − 2 = 0\nFor a point of inflection, f″(x) must change sign as x passes through 0.\nTest either side:\nf″(−0.1) = 2 − 2 cos(−0.1) = 9.99 × 10⁻³ > 0\nf″(0.1) = 2 − 2 cos(0.1) = 9.99 × 10⁻³ > 0\nIn fact, since cos x < 1 for all x in −π ≤ x ≤ π except x = 0, f″(x) = 2 − 2 cos x > 0 everywhere in the domain except at x = 0. So f″(x) is positive on both sides of x = 0 and does not change sign; the curve is convex on both sides.\nTherefore the curve y = f(x) does not have a point of inflection at x = 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::11(a)": "The inequality 3 < k < 4 is strict, so k cannot equal 3. The number 3 is not in the interval, so it cannot be the smallest value of k in the interval.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::11(b)(i)": "Since 3 < x, the mean y = (3 + x)/2 lies strictly between 3 and x, so 3 < y < x.\nBecause x < 4, this also means y is in the interval 3 < k < 4.\nSo y is a number in the interval that is smaller than x, which contradicts the assumption that x is the smallest number in the interval.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::11(b)(ii)": "Step 1: Assume, for contradiction, that there is a largest number in the interval 3 < k < 4, and let this largest number be x. So 3 < x < 4.\nStep 2: Let y = (x + 4)/2, the mean of x and 4.\nStep 3: Since x < 4, y − x = (4 − x)/2 > 0 and 4 − y = (4 − x)/2 > 0, so x < y < 4. Also y > x > 3, so y is in the interval 3 < k < 4.\nSo y is a number in the interval that is larger than x, which contradicts x being the largest number in the interval.\nStep 4: Therefore the assumption is false, and there is no largest value of k in the interval 3 < k < 4.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::12": "Resultant force in the direction of motion = 10 − 4 = 6 N\nNewton's second law, F = ma:\n6 = 2 × acceleration\nacceleration = 3 m s⁻²",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::13": "The gradient of a velocity–time graph is the acceleration.\nThe first section is a straight line with positive gradient (constant positive acceleration); the second section is horizontal (zero acceleration).\nAt the sharp corner the gradient jumps straight from a positive value to 0, so the model assumes the acceleration of the car can change instantaneously.\n(The other options are false: the car does accelerate at first; the acceleration is zero, not positive, in the second stage; and the acceleration is constant within each stage.)\nAnswer: The acceleration of the car can change instantaneously.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::14(a)": "r = 6t − 2t²\nt = 4: r = 6(4) − 2(4)² = 24 − 32 = −8\nr = −8 (metres)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::14(b)": "The displacement is positive when\n6t − 2t² > 0\n2t(3 − t) > 0\nThe critical values are t = 0 and t = 3. The quadratic −2t² + 6t is an upside-down parabola, so it is positive between its roots.\n0 < t < 3",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::15": "Resultant force:\nF₁ + F₂ = (a + 4, 23 + b) N (column vector)\nNewton's second law, resultant force = mass × acceleration, with mass 3 kg and acceleration (4b, a):\n(a + 4, b + 23) = 3(4b, a) = (12b, 3a)\nComparing components:\na + 4 = 12b   (1)\nb + 23 = 3a   (2)\nFrom (1): a = 12b − 4. Substitute into (2):\nb + 23 = 3(12b − 4) = 36b − 12\n35 = 35b, so b = 1\na = 12(1) − 4 = 8\na = 8, b = 1",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::16": "Each apple starts from rest (u = 0) and falls freely with acceleration 9.8 m s⁻². Use s = ut + ½at².\nFirst apple, t = 0.5 s:\ns₁ = 0 + ½(9.8)(0.5)² = 1.225 m\nSecond apple, t = 0.5 + 0.1 = 0.6 s:\ns₂ = 0 + ½(9.8)(0.6)² = 1.764 m\nThese are the heights of the two apples above the ground, so the vertical distance between them is\ns₂ − s₁ = 1.764 − 1.225 = 0.539 m = 53.9 cm\nSo d = 53.9 ≈ 54, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::17": "Let the reaction at A be R newtons, so the reaction at B is 2R newtons.\nThe rod is uniform with length 2L, so its weight mg acts at its centre, a distance L from each end.\nDistance from A to the centre = L − x\nDistance from B to the centre = L − (x + 0.1)\nTake moments about the centre of the rod (the weight has no moment about this point). A and B are on opposite sides of the centre:\nR(L − x) = 2R(L − (x + 0.1))\nDivide by R (R ≠ 0):\nL − x = 2(L − x − 0.1)\nL − x = 2L − 2x − 0.2\n0.2 = 2L − 2x − L + x = L − x\nL − x = 0.2, so k = 0.2",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::18(a)": "r = p + 2t − qe^(−0.2t)\nv = dr/dt = 2 + 0.2qe^(−0.2t)\nacceleration = dv/dt = −0.04qe^(−0.2t)\nWhen t = 3 the acceleration is −1.8 m s⁻²:\n−1.8 = −0.04qe^(−0.6)\nq = 1.8/(0.04e^(−0.6)) = 45e^(0.6)\nq = 45 × 1.822118… = 81.995…\nq ≈ 82, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::18(b)": "Initial displacement means t = 0, r = 5:\n5 = p + 2(0) − qe⁰\n5 = p − q\np = 5 + q = 5 + 82 = 87\n(Using the unrounded q = 81.995…, p = 86.995… which also gives 87.)\np = 87 (2 s.f.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::19(a)(i)": "Take upwards as positive. At the maximum height the ball is momentarily at rest.\nu = 7 m s⁻¹, v = 0, a = −9.8 m s⁻², s = h\nv² = u² + 2as\n0 = 7² + 2(−9.8)h\n19.6h = 49\nh = 49/19.6 = 2.5 m\nThe ball reaches a maximum height of 2.5 m, so the claim is verified.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::19(a)(ii)": "Any two of:\n1. There is no air resistance acting on the ball (the only force is gravity).\n2. The ball is modelled as a particle.\n3. The ball is shot from ground level (so the height gained equals the height above the ground).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::19(b)": "The more the ball is tilted from the vertical, the smaller its vertical component of velocity, so the lowest maximum height occurs at 11° from the vertical.\nVertical component of the initial velocity at 11° from the vertical:\nu = 7 cos 11° m s⁻¹\nAt the maximum height the vertical velocity is 0. Upwards positive: v = 0, a = −9.8, s = k\nv² = u² + 2as\n0 = (7 cos 11°)² + 2(−9.8)k\n0 = 49 cos²11° − 19.6k\nk = 49 cos²11°/19.6 = 2.5 cos²11° = 2.5 × 0.96359… = 2.4089…\nk = 2.4 (2 s.f.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::20(a)": "Q moves in a straight line, so its direction of motion is the direction of its displacement.\nDisplacement of Q = (14i + 5j) − (5i − 7j) = 9i + 12j\n9i + 12j = 3(3i + 4j)\nSo Q's displacement is a scalar multiple of P's velocity 3i + 4j, meaning Q moves in the same direction as P.\nSince P and Q move in separate straight lines with the same direction, they move along parallel lines.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::20(b)": "The displacement only shows that Q's average velocity over the 3 seconds is (9i + 12j)/3 = 3i + 4j m s⁻¹.\nAverage velocity is not the same as constant velocity: Q's speed could change during the 3 seconds (Q could accelerate and decelerate along its line), so Q's velocity need not be constant.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::20(c)": "Consider the moment 3 seconds after R passes through X.\nR moves at 4 m s⁻¹, so its distance from X is XR = 4 × 3 = 12 m.\nP's speed = |3i + 4j| = √(3² + 4²) = 5 m s⁻¹.\nP passed through X 2 seconds after R, so at this moment P has been moving away from X for 3 − 2 = 1 second:\nXP = 5 × 1 = 5 m\nP and R are 13 m apart, so PR = 13 m.\nIn triangle PXR: XP² + XR² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13² = PR²\nBy the converse of Pythagoras' theorem, triangle PXR is right-angled at X, so angle PXR = 90°.\nXP lies along P's line and XR lies along R's line, so P and R move along perpendicular lines.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::21(a)": "The forces on N are its weight 80g N acting downwards and the tension T N in the rope acting upwards.\nN accelerates downwards with acceleration a m s⁻², so the resultant downward force is 80g − T.\nBy Newton's second law, F = ma, with mass 80 kg:\n80g − T = 80a",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::21(b)": "Resolve perpendicular to the slope for M. There is no acceleration perpendicular to the slope, and the rope is parallel to the slope, so the normal reaction R balances the component of the weight perpendicular to the slope:\nR = 50g cos 60° = 50g × ½ = 25g N",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::21(c)": "M moves up the slope, so friction acts down the slope. Since M is sliding, friction is limiting:\nF = μR = μ × 25g = 25gμ\nComponent of M's weight down the slope = 50g sin 60° = 50g × (√3/2) = 25√3 g\nEquation of motion for M up the slope (resultant force = mass × acceleration):\nT − 50g sin 60° − 25gμ = 50a\nAdd the equation for N from (a), 80g − T = 80a, to eliminate T:\n80g − 50g sin 60° − 25gμ = 130a\n80g − 25√3 g − 25gμ = 130a\na = (80 − 25√3 − 25μ)g/130\na = (16 − 5√3 − 5μ)g/26\nThis expression decreases as μ increases, so a is least when μ takes its largest value, μ = 1:\na ≥ (16 − 5√3 − 5)g/26\na ≥ (11 − 5√3)g/26, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p2::21(d)": "Any one of:\nThe rope is light (has no mass).\nThe rope is inextensible, so M and N have the same magnitude of acceleration.\nThe pulley is fixed (and smooth, as given).\nThere is no air resistance on the boxes.",









































 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::1": "For a geometric series to converge, the common ratio r must satisfy |r| < 1.\n0.1 + 0.2 + 0.4 + 0.8 + …: r = 2, so |r| > 1 (diverges).\n1 − 1 + 1 − 1 + …: r = −1, so |r| = 1 (does not converge).\n128 − 64 + 32 − 16 + …: r = −64/128 = −½, so |r| = ½ < 1 (converges).\n1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + …: r = 2, so |r| > 1 (diverges).\nAnswer: 128 − 64 + 32 − 16 + … (the third box)\n(Its sum to infinity is 128/(1 − (−½)) = 256/3.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::2": "A quadratic has one repeated real root when its discriminant is zero.\nb² − 4ac = 0 with a = 4, c = 9:\nb² − 4 × 4 × 9 = 0\nb² = 144\nb = ±12\nAnswer: b = ±12 (the second option)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::3": "For a > 0, aˣ > 0 for every real x, so the graph of y = aˣ always lies entirely above the x-axis.\nThe first graph is possible (a > 1, increasing), the second is possible (0 < a < 1, decreasing), and the fourth is possible (a = 1 gives the horizontal line y = 1).\nThe third graph lies entirely below the x-axis (y < 0 for all x), so it cannot have an equation of the form y = aˣ.\nAnswer: the third graph (the curve below the x-axis)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::4": "y = x⁴ + 2ˣ\nd/dx(x⁴) = 4x³\nd/dx(aˣ) = aˣ ln a, so d/dx(2ˣ) = 2ˣ ln 2\ndy/dx = 4x³ + 2ˣ ln 2",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::5": "Area of sector OAB = ½r²θ = ½ × 18² × π/6 = 324π/12 = 27π\nArea of triangle OAB = ½ab sin C = ½ × 18 × 18 × sin(π/6) = 162 × ½ = 81\nArea of shaded segment = sector − triangle\n= 27π − 81\n= 27(π − 3) cm²\nSo the area is k(π − 3) cm² with k = 27.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::6(a)": "Write 5/√x = 5x^(−1/2), so\n∫(6x² − 5/√x) dx = ∫(6x² − 5x^(−1/2)) dx\n= 6x³/3 − 5x^(1/2)/(1/2) + c\n= 2x³ − 10x^(1/2) + c",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::6(b)": "Integrating dy/dx (using part (a)):\ny = 2x³ − 10x^(1/2) + c\nThe curve passes through (4, 90):\n90 = 2 × 4³ − 10 × 4^(1/2) + c\n90 = 128 − 20 + c\nc = −18\nEquation of the curve: y = 2x³ − 10x^(1/2) − 18 (that is, y = 2x³ − 10√x − 18)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::7(a)": "The region R must satisfy three conditions:\n• y ≤ 2 + 3x − 2x²: on or below the parabola;\n• x + y ≥ 1: on or above the line x + y = 1;\n• y ≥ 0: on or above the x-axis.\nThe parabola meets the x-axis where 2x² − 3x − 2 = 0, i.e. (2x + 1)(x − 2) = 0, so at x = −½ and x = 2. The line meets the x-axis at (1, 0), and A lies above the x-axis.\nSo R is the region bounded above by the arc of the parabola from A, over the top of the curve, down to (2, 0); below by the line x + y = 1 from A down to (1, 0); and then by the x-axis from (1, 0) to (2, 0).\nShade this region and label it R.\nThe part between the parabola and the line that lies below the x-axis (down towards B) is not included, because there y < 0.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::7(b)": "At the intersection points, y = 1 − x and y = 2 + 3x − 2x²:\n1 − x = 2 + 3x − 2x²\n2x² − 4x − 1 = 0\nx = [4 ± √(16 + 8)]/4 = (4 ± 2√6)/4 = (2 ± √6)/2\nA has x < 0 (B is the other point), so\nx = (2 − √6)/2 (≈ −0.225)\ny = 1 − x = 1 − (2 − √6)/2 = √6/2 (≈ 1.22)\nA = ((2 − √6)/2, √6/2)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::8(a)": "Initially t = 0, and the oven is at room temperature:\nθ = 20(11 − 10e⁰) = 20(11 − 10) = 20\nRoom temperature = 20 °C",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::8(b)": "Since k > 0, as t becomes large, e^(−kt) → 0.\nSo θ → 20(11 − 10 × 0) = 220.\nT = 220 (the maximum temperature is 220 °C)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::8(c)(i)": "When t = 1, θ = 86:\n86 = 20(11 − 10e^(−k))\n4.3 = 11 − 10e^(−k)\n10e^(−k) = 6.7\ne^(−k) = 0.67\nk = −ln 0.67 = 0.40047…\nk = 0.4 (to 1 s.f.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::8(c)(ii)": "Within 1 °C of the maximum means θ = T − 1 = 219, using k = 0.4:\n219 = 20(11 − 10e^(−0.4t))\n10.95 = 11 − 10e^(−0.4t)\n10e^(−0.4t) = 0.05\ne^(−0.4t) = 0.005\n−0.4t = ln 0.005\nt = ln 200 / 0.4 = 13.24…\nIt takes about 13.2 minutes.\n(Using the unrounded k = −ln 0.67 gives t ≈ 13.23 minutes, which also rounds to 13.2.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::9(a)": "x² + y² = 12y − 8x − 27\nx² + 8x + y² − 12y = −27\nComplete the square in x and in y:\n(x + 4)² − 16 + (y − 6)² − 36 = −27\n(x + 4)² + (y − 6)² = 25\nSo a = −4, b = 6, k = 25.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::9(b)": "From (x + 4)² + (y − 6)² = 25, the centre is P(−4, 6).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::9(c)": "Q lies on the y-axis, so substitute x = 0 into (x + 4)² + (y − 6)² = 25:\n16 + (y − 6)² = 25\n(y − 6)² = 9\ny² − 12y + 27 = 0\n(y − 3)(y − 9) = 0\ny = 3 or y = 9\nQ is the upper intersection with the y-axis (Figure 1), so the y-coordinate of Q is 9.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::9(d)": "P = (−4, 6), Q = (0, 9), R = (9, −3).\nQR is a tangent at Q, so it is perpendicular to the radius PQ: angle PQR = 90°.\nPQ = radius = 5\nPR = √[(9 − (−4))² + (−3 − 6)²] = √(169 + 81) = √250 = 5√10\nIn right-angled triangle PQR:\ncos QPR = PQ/PR = 5/(5√10) = 1/√10\nQPR = cos⁻¹(1/√10) = 1.2490… rad\nAngle QPR = 1.25 radians (3 s.f.)\n(Check: QR = √(9² + 12²) = 15, and tan QPR = 15/5 = 3 gives the same angle.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::10": "f(x) = 5x³ + x\nf′(x) = lim (h → 0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h\nf(x + h) − f(x) = 5(x + h)³ + (x + h) − (5x³ + x)\nExpand (x + h)³ = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³:\n= 5(x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³) + x + h − 5x³ − x\n= 5x³ + 15x²h + 15xh² + 5h³ + h − 5x³\n= 15x²h + 15xh² + 5h³ + h\nDivide by h (h ≠ 0):\n[f(x + h) − f(x)]/h = 15x² + 15xh + 5h² + 1\nAs h → 0, 15xh → 0 and 5h² → 0, so\nf′(x) = lim (h → 0) (15x² + 15xh + 5h² + 1) = 15x² + 1\nHence f′(x) = 15x² + 1, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::11": "Where C meets the x-axis: (x² − 8x) ln x = 0, i.e. x(x − 8) ln x = 0.\nSince x > 0, x ≠ 0, so either x = 8 or ln x = 0, which gives x = 1.\nSo R lies between x = 1 and x = 8.\nLet I = ∫₁⁸ (x² − 8x) ln x dx.\nIntegrate by parts with u = ln x and dv/dx = x² − 8x:\ndu/dx = 1/x and v = x³/3 − 4x²\n∫(x² − 8x) ln x dx = (x³/3 − 4x²) ln x − ∫(x³/3 − 4x²)(1/x) dx\n= (x³/3 − 4x²) ln x − ∫(x²/3 − 4x) dx\n= (x³/3 − 4x²) ln x − (x³/9 − 2x²)\nAt x = 8: (512/3 − 256) ln 8 − (512/9 − 128) = −(256/3) ln 8 + 640/9 = −256 ln 2 + 640/9 (since ln 8 = 3 ln 2)\nAt x = 1: (1/3 − 4) ln 1 − (1/9 − 2) = 0 + 17/9 = 17/9\nI = (−256 ln 2 + 640/9) − 17/9 = 623/9 − 256 ln 2 (≈ −108.2)\nI is negative because R lies below the x-axis, so\nArea of R = −I = −623/9 + 256 ln 2 (≈ 108.2)\nThis is p + q ln 2 with p = −623/9 and q = 256.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::12": "n = 84. Cumulative frequencies:\n0: 1, 1: 5, 2: 23, 3: 39, 4: 44, 5: 81, 6: 83, 7: 84\nLower quartile: the ¼ × 84 = 21st value. The 6th to 23rd values are 2, so Q₁ = 2.\nUpper quartile: the ¾ × 84 = 63rd value. The 45th to 81st values are 5, so Q₃ = 5.\nInterquartile range = Q₃ − Q₁ = 5 − 2 = 3\nAnswer: 3 (the first option)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::13": "(A ∪ C) ∩ B is the part of B that is also in A or in C:\n(A ∪ C) ∩ B = (A ∩ B) ∪ (B ∩ C)\nSo the shading must be exactly the overlap of A with B together with the overlap of B with C (including the centre region common to all three).\nThe top-left diagram shades A ∪ C and the top-right shades B ∪ C, which are too large. The bottom-right diagram shades (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C), which includes part of A ∩ C outside B.\nAnswer: the bottom-left diagram",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::14(a)": "Mean = Σx/n = 945 000/350 = 2700\nThe mean annual energy cost is £2700.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::14(b)": "Standard deviation = √(Σx²/n − x̄²)\n= √(2 607 500 000/350 − 2700²)\n= √(7 450 000 − 7 290 000)\n= √160 000\n= 400\nThe standard deviation is £400.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::14(c)": "Village A: mean £2700, standard deviation £400\nVillage B: mean £3100, standard deviation £325\nSince 2700 < 3100, on average the annual cost of energy in 2021 was lower for households in Village A than in Village B.\nSince 400 > 325, the annual cost of energy varied more (was more spread out) for households in Village A than in Village B.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(a)": "For X ~ B(n, p), E(X) = np.\nE(X) = 48 × 0.175 = 8.4",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(b)": "For X ~ B(n, p), Var(X) = np(1 − p).\nVar(X) = 48 × 0.175 × (1 − 0.175) = 48 × 0.175 × 0.825 = 6.93, as required.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(c)": "P(X < 10) = P(X ≤ 9)\nUsing the binomial cumulative distribution function with n = 48, p = 0.175:\nP(X ≤ 9) = 0.6742 (4 d.p.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(d)": "P(X ≥ 6) = 1 − P(X ≤ 5)\nP(X ≤ 5) = 0.1325 (from the binomial cumulative distribution function)\nP(X ≥ 6) = 1 − 0.1325 = 0.8675",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(e)": "P(9 ≤ X ≤ 15) = P(X ≤ 15) − P(X ≤ 8)\nP(X ≤ 15) = 0.9940 and P(X ≤ 8) = 0.5317\nP(9 ≤ X ≤ 15) = 0.9940 − 0.5317 = 0.4623",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::15(f)": "Comment 1: The model has suitable values: there are 48 seats, matching n = 48, and the proportion of passengers travelling for business is 17.5%, matching p = 0.175.\nComment 2: However, the aeroplane may not be full on every flight, so the number of passengers may not be 48, and then n = 48 would not be appropriate.\n(Other valid comments: the proportion travelling on business may not stay at 0.175, for example at peak or holiday times; and passengers may book in groups, so whether passengers travel for business may not be independent.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::16": "Let ρ be the population correlation coefficient between the amount of nicotine in the blood stream and energy level, in adults.\nH₀: ρ = 0\nH₁: ρ < 0\nOne-tailed test at the 2.5% level with n = 50: critical value = −0.2787 (reject H₀ if r < −0.2787).\nSample correlation coefficient r = −0.45\n−0.45 < −0.2787, so the result is in the critical region: reject H₀.\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 2.5% level to support the student's belief that, in adults, there is a negative correlation between the amount of nicotine in their blood stream and their energy level.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(a)": "The mean is 50, and a normal curve is symmetrical about its mean, so 50 is marked on the horizontal axis directly below the peak (vertex) of the curve.\nThe points of inflection of a normal curve are one standard deviation either side of the mean, at 50 ± 4. So 54 (= 50 + 4) is marked on the horizontal axis directly below the right-hand point of inflection, where the curve changes from bending downwards to bending upwards on the right-hand side.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(b)": "50 cm is the mean, and the normal distribution is symmetrical about its mean, so\nP(length < 50) = 0.5",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(c)": "Let L cm be the length of a new-born baby, L ~ N(50, 4²).\nP(L > 56) = P(Z > (56 − 50)/4) = P(Z > 1.5)\n= 0.0668 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(d)": "Let L cm be the length, L ~ N(50, 4²).\nP(40 < L < 60) = P((40 − 50)/4 < Z < (60 − 50)/4) = P(−2.5 < Z < 2.5)\n= 0.9876 (4 d.p.)\n≈ 0.988",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(e)": "Let L cm be the length, L ~ N(50, 4²). We need the length x with P(L > x) = 0.95, i.e. P(L < x) = 0.05.\nP(Z < z) = 0.05 gives z = −1.6449\n(x − 50)/4 = −1.6449\nx = 50 − 4 × 1.6449 = 43.42…\nThe length exceeded by 95% of new-born babies is 43.4 cm (3 s.f.).",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::17(f)": "Let μ cm be the mean length of a new-born baby at the clinic in 2020.\nH₀: μ = 50\nH₁: μ > 50\nSignificance level 10%, one-tailed.\nSample mean: x̄ = 2060/40 = 51.5\nUnder H₀, the mean length L̄ of a random sample of 40 babies satisfies\nL̄ ~ N(50, 4²/40), i.e. N(50, 0.4), with standard deviation √0.4 = 0.6325\np-value: P(L̄ ≥ 51.5) = P(Z ≥ (51.5 − 50)/√0.4) = P(Z ≥ 2.372) = 0.0089\n0.0089 < 0.1, so reject H₀.\n(Equivalently: test statistic z = 2.37 > 1.2816; or critical value 50 + 1.2816 × 0.6325 = 50.81, and 51.5 > 50.81.)\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 10% level to suggest that the mean length of a new-born baby at the clinic in 2020 has increased compared to 2019.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::18(a)(i)": "P(G) = P(G ∩ H) + P(G ∩ H′) = 0.21 + 0.18 = 0.39",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::18(a)(ii)": "P[(G ∩ H)′] = 1 − P(G ∩ H) = 1 − 0.21 = 0.79\n(Check: 0.18 + 0.07 + 0.54 = 0.79)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::18(a)(iii)": "P(G′) = 0.07 + 0.54 = 0.61\nP(H | G′) = P(H ∩ G′)/P(G′) = 0.07/0.61 = 7/61 (≈ 0.115)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::18(b)": "P(G) = 0.39 and P(H) = 0.21 + 0.07 = 0.28\nP(G) × P(H) = 0.39 × 0.28 = 0.1092\nP(G ∩ H) = 0.21\nSince P(G ∩ H) ≠ P(G) × P(H), G and H are not independent.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::19(a)(i)": "Let p be the proportion of diesel cars registered in 2022 with CO emissions less than 0.3 g/km.\nH₀: p = 0.8\nH₁: p ≠ 0.8\n(Two-tailed, because Talat is testing for a change in either direction.)",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::19(a)(ii)": "Let X be the number of cars in the sample of 25 with CO emissions less than 0.3 g/km.\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(25, 0.8).\nTwo-tailed test at 10%, so allow up to 5% in each tail.\nLower tail:\nP(X ≤ 15) = 0.0173 < 0.05\nP(X ≤ 16) = 0.0468 < 0.05\nP(X ≤ 17) = 0.1091 > 0.05\nSo the lower tail of the critical region is X ≤ 16.\nUpper tail:\nP(X ≥ 23) = 0.0982 > 0.05\nP(X ≥ 24) = 0.0274 < 0.05\nP(X ≥ 25) = 0.0038 < 0.05\nSo the upper tail of the critical region is X ≥ 24.\nCritical region: X ≤ 16 or X ≥ 24",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::19(a)(iii)": "18 is not in the critical region (X ≤ 16 or X ≥ 24), so do not reject H₀.\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 10% level to suggest that the proportion of diesel cars registered in 2022 with CO emissions less than 0.3 g/km has changed from 0.8.",
 "math-aqa-7357-2024-p3::19(b)": "Reason 1: No diesel cars in the Large Data Set have CO emissions of more than 0.5 g/km, so the Large Data Set does not give a usable proportion to compare against.\nReason 2: The Large Data Set only uses data from cars in some regions of England, not all of them (and only some makes of car and only two years), so it is not representative of diesel cars in England as a whole.\n(Another valid reason: CO emission values are missing for some diesel cars in the Large Data Set.)",































 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::10(b)": "H is maximum when e^(0.1sin(0.25t)) is maximum, i.e. when sin(0.25t) = 1.\nH_max = 5e^(0.1) ≈ 5.53 m.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::10(c)": "Maximum occurs when sin(0.25t) = 1 ⟹ 0.25t = π/2 + 2nπ.\nFirst maximum: t = 2π ≈ 6.28 s.\nSecond maximum: 0.25T = π/2 + 2π = 5π/2 ⟹ T = 10π ≈ 31.4 s.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::11(a)": "√((1+4x)/(1−x)) = (1+4x)^(1/2)(1−x)^(−1/2).\nExpand (1+4x)^(1/2) ≈ 1 + (1/2)(4x) + ((1/2)(−1/2)/2!)(4x)² = 1 + 2x − 2x².\nExpand (1−x)^(−1/2) ≈ 1 + (1/2)x + ((1/2)(3/2)/2!)x² = 1 + x/2 + 3x²/8.\nMultiply:\n(1+2x−2x²)(1+x/2+3x²/8)\n≈ 1 + x/2 + 3x²/8 + 2x + x² + (−2x²) + …\n= 1 + (5/2)x + (3/8 + 1 − 2)x² = 1 + (5/2)x − (5/8)x², as required.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::11(b)": "The expansion of (1+4x)^(1/2) requires |4x| < 1, i.e. |x| < 1/4. Since x = 1/2 > 1/4, it lies outside the radius of convergence and the expansion is not valid for this value.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::11(c)": "Substitute x = 1/11:\nLHS = √((1 + 4/11)/(1 − 1/11)) = √((15/11)/(10/11)) = √(3/2) = √6/2.\nRHS = 1 + (5/2)(1/11) − (5/8)(1/11)²\n= 1 + 5/22 − 5/968.\nCommon denominator 968: = 968/968 + 220/968 − 5/968 = 1183/968.\nSo √6/2 ≈ 1183/968 ⟹ √6 ≈ 2366/968 = 1183/484.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::12(a)": "V = Ap^t. At t = 4 (year 2005): 32000 = Ap⁴. At t = 11 (year 2012): 50000 = Ap^(11).\nDivide: p⁷ = 50000/32000 = 25/16 ⟹ p = (25/16)^(1/7) ≈ 1.0658.\nA = 32000/p⁴ = 32000/(1.0658)⁴ ≈ 32000/1.2909 ≈ 24789 ≈ 24800.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::12(b)": "(i) A ≈ 24800 is the estimated value of the car on 1st January 2001 (the initial value when t = 0).\n(ii) p ≈ 1.0658 is the annual growth factor — the car's value increases by approximately 6.58% per year.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::12(c)": "Using V = 24800 × 1.0658ᵗ, set V = 100000:\n24800 × 1.0658ᵗ = 100000\n1.0658ᵗ = 100000/24800 = 4.0323…\nt = ln(4.0323…)/ln(1.0658) = 1.3943…/0.063726… = 21.88…\nSo the value first exceeds £100 000 about 21.9 years after 1 January 2001.\n21 years after 1 January 2001 is 1 January 2022 and 22 years after is 1 January 2023, so this happens during 2022.\nYear: 2022.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::13": "Use the substitution u = x + 2, so x = u − 2, dx = du. When x = 0, u = 2; when x = 2, u = 4.\n∫₀² 2x√(x+2) dx = ∫₂⁴ 2(u−2)√u du = ∫₂⁴ (2u^(3/2) − 4u^(1/2)) du.\n= [(4/5)u^(5/2) − (8/3)u^(3/2)]₂⁴.\nAt u = 4: (4/5)(32) − (8/3)(8) = 128/5 − 64/3 = (384 − 320)/15 = 64/15.\nAt u = 2: (4/5)(4√2) − (8/3)(2√2) = (16√2/5 − 16√2/3) = 16√2(3−5)/15 = −32√2/15.\nResult = 64/15 − (−32√2/15) = (64 + 32√2)/15 = (32/15)(2 + √2).",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::14(a)": "From x = 3 + 2sint: sint = (x−3)/2 ⟹ sin²t = (x−3)²/4.\nUsing cos2t = 1 − 2sin²t: y = 4 + 2cos2t = 4 + 2(1 − 2sin²t) = 6 − 4sin²t = 6 − 4(x−3)²/4 = 6 − (x−3)².",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::14(b)": "(i) C is an inverted parabola arc with vertex at (3, 6) and endpoints at (1, 2) and (5, 2) (since −1 ≤ sint ≤ 1 gives 1 ≤ x ≤ 5).\n(ii) Since sint ∈ [−1, 1], the parametric equations restrict x to [1, 5]. The Cartesian equation y = 6−(x−3)² holds for all real x, so C only includes the arc where 1 ≤ x ≤ 5.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2018-p1::14(c)": "From (a) and (b), C is the part of y = 6 − (x − 3)² with 1 ≤ x ≤ 5, running from (1, 2) up to (3, 6) and down to (5, 2).\nSubstitute y = k − x:\nk − x = 6 − (x − 3)²\nk − x = 6 − x² + 6x − 9\nx² − 7x + (k + 3) = 0.\nUpper limit: the line is a tangent when b² − 4ac = 0:\n49 − 4(k + 3) = 0 ⇒ 37 − 4k = 0 ⇒ k = 37/4.\nFor two distinct points we need 49 − 4(k + 3) > 0, so k < 37/4.\nLower limit: both intersections must have 1 ≤ x ≤ 5. The roots are x = (7 ± √(37 − 4k))/2, and the larger root equals 5 when the line passes through the end point (5, 2), i.e. k = 5 + 2 = 7. Then x² − 7x + 10 = 0 gives x = 2 and x = 5, both on C, so k = 7 still gives two points. For k < 7 the larger root is greater than 5, so at most one intersection lies on C.\nHence the range is {k : 7 ≤ k < 37/4}.",

 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::1(a)": "Each throw is independent with the same probability of a hit, so\nX ~ B(15, 0.48)\n\n(i) P(X = 3) = ¹⁵C₃ × 0.48³ × 0.52¹²\n= 455 × 0.110592 × 0.000390877…\n= 0.019668…\nP(X = 3) = 0.0197 (3 s.f.)\n\n(ii) P(X ≥ 5) = 1 − P(X ≤ 4)\nP(X ≤ 4) = 0.079869… (binomial cumulative function on the calculator)\nP(X ≥ 5) = 1 − 0.079869… = 0.920131…\nP(X ≥ 5) = 0.920 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::1(b)": "Let Y be the number of hits in 250 throws, so Y ~ B(250, 0.48).\nn is large and p is close to 0.5, so a normal approximation is suitable.\nMean = np = 250 × 0.48 = 120\nVariance = np(1 − p) = 250 × 0.48 × 0.52 = 62.4\nSo Y is approximated by N(120, 62.4), with standard deviation √62.4 = 7.899…\n\n\"More than 110 hits\" means Y ≥ 111. With a continuity correction:\nP(Y > 110) ≈ P(W > 110.5), where W ~ N(120, 62.4)\n= P(Z > (110.5 − 120)/√62.4)\n= P(Z > −1.2026…)\n= 0.88544…\n\nP(more than 110 hits) ≈ 0.885 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::2(a)": "L ~ N(8, x²) and P(L < 7.902) = 0.025\nStandardising: P(Z < (7.902 − 8)/x) = 0.025\nFrom the normal tables, P(Z < −1.96) = 0.025, so\n(7.902 − 8)/x = −1.96\n−0.098/x = −1.96\nx = 0.098/1.96\nx = 0.05 (to 2 decimal places), as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::2(b)": "L ~ N(8, 0.05²)\nP(7.94 ≤ L ≤ 8.09) = P((7.94 − 8)/0.05 ≤ Z ≤ (8.09 − 8)/0.05)\n= P(−1.2 ≤ Z ≤ 1.8)\n= 0.96407… − 0.11507…\n= 0.84900…\nProportion of rods between 7.94 cm and 8.09 cm = 0.849 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::2(c)": "Probabilities of each type of rod (L ~ N(8, 0.05²)):\nP(L < 7.94) = P(Z < −1.2) = 0.11507…\nP(7.94 ≤ L ≤ 8.09) = 0.84900… (from (b))\nP(L > 8.09) = P(Z > 1.8) = 0.03593…\n\nProfit per rod (each rod costs 20p to make):\nL < 7.94: sold for 5p, profit = 5 − 20 = −15p = −£0.15\n7.94 ≤ L ≤ 8.09: sold for 50p, profit = 50 − 20 = 30p = £0.30\nL > 8.09: extra 10p cost, sold for 50p, profit = 50 − 20 − 10 = 20p = £0.20\n\nExpected profit per rod\n= 0.30 × 0.84900… + (−0.15) × 0.11507… + 0.20 × 0.03593…\n= 0.25470… − 0.01726… + 0.00718…\n= £0.24462…\n\nExpected profit per 500 rods = 500 × 0.24462… = £122.31…\nExpected profit = £122 (to the nearest pound)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::2(d)": "Let X be the number of faulty hinges in a sample of 200.\nHinges are faulty independently with probability 0.015, so X ~ B(200, 0.015).\n\nA batch is accepted if fewer than 6 hinges are faulty, i.e. X ≤ 5.\nP(batch accepted) = P(X ≤ 5) = 0.9176… (binomial cumulative function)\n\nThe aim is for 95% of batches to be accepted, i.e. a probability of 0.95.\nSince 0.9176 < 0.95, only about 92% of batches are expected to be accepted.\nSo the manufacturer is unlikely to achieve its aim.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::3(a)": "A daily total rainfall of 0 < r ≤ 0.05 mm is recorded as \"tr\", meaning trace.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::3(b)": "n = 31, Σr = 174.9, Σr² = 3523.283\n\n(i) Mean = Σr/n = 174.9/31 = 5.6419…\nMean = 5.64 mm (3 s.f.)\n\n(ii) Standard deviation = √(Σr²/n − mean²)\n= √(3523.283/31 − 5.6419…²)\n= √(113.6543… − 31.8314…)\n= √81.8228…\n= 9.0455…\nStandard deviation = 9.05 mm (3 s.f.)\n(Using the n − 1 divisor instead gives s = 9.19 mm.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::3(c)": "Leuchars is in the North of the UK (Scotland) and Camborne is in the South (Cornwall).\nThe mean Daily Total Rainfall for August 2015 is 1.72 mm at Leuchars and 5.64 mm at Camborne.\nThe mean is smaller for Leuchars (North) than for Camborne (South), which is the opposite of what Dian believes.\nSo this does not provide evidence to support Dian's belief.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::3(d)": "The value 0.27 is the proportion of dry days over the whole of 1987, but the probability of a day without rain is unlikely to be constant through the year: summer days are likely to have a different (probably higher) chance of no rain. Also, the weather on consecutive days is not independent (a dry day tends to be followed by another dry day), so the binomial conditions of a constant probability and independent trials may not hold.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::4(a)": "Let p be the proportion of customers who arrive late.\nH₀: p = 0.1\nH₁: p ≠ 0.1",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::4(b)": "Let X be the number of the 50 customers who arrive late.\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(50, 0.1).\nTwo-tailed test at 5%: each tail should have probability less than 0.025.\n\nLower tail:\nP(X = 0) = 0.9⁵⁰ = 0.0052 < 0.025\nP(X ≤ 1) = 0.0338 > 0.025\nSo the lower critical region is X = 0.\n\nUpper tail:\nP(X ≥ 9) = 1 − P(X ≤ 8) = 1 − 0.9421 = 0.0579 > 0.025\nP(X ≥ 10) = 1 − P(X ≤ 9) = 1 − 0.9755 = 0.0245 < 0.025\nSo the upper critical region is X ≥ 10.\n\nCritical region: X = 0 or X ≥ 10\nwith P(X = 0) = 0.0052 and P(X ≥ 10) = 0.0245",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::4(c)": "Actual significance level = P(X = 0) + P(X ≥ 10)\n= 0.00515… + 0.02453…\n= 0.0297 (i.e. 2.97%)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::4(d)": "x = 15 lies in the critical region X ≥ 10, so H₀ is rejected at the 5% level.\nThere is evidence of a change in the proportion of customers who arrive late, which supports the manager's belief.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(a)": "Number of skilled employees = 275 + 90 = 365\nP(skilled) = 365/1825 = 1/5 = 0.2",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(b)": "Employees in area B who are not professional = skilled + elementary in B = 90 + 80 = 170\nP(lives in B and not professional) = 170/1825 = 34/365 = 0.0932 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(c)": "R is area A, so R′ is area B. F is professional, so F′ is skilled or elementary.\nGiven regions: H ∩ R ∩ F′ = 123, R only = 412, H ∩ F ∩ R′ = 247, F only = 133.\n\nH only (area B, not professional, works from home):\n90 × 0.4 + 80 × 0.05 = 36 + 4 = 40\n\nH ∩ R ∩ F (area A, professional, works from home):\n740 × 0.65 = 481\n\nR ∩ F only (area A, professional, not from home):\n740 × 0.35 = 259\n\nOutside all three circles (area B, not professional, not from home):\n90 × 0.6 + 80 × 0.95 = 54 + 76 = 130\n\nCompleted Venn diagram (in words):\nH only = 40\nH ∩ R only = 123\nR only = 412\nH ∩ F only = 247\nH ∩ R ∩ F (centre) = 481\nR ∩ F only = 259\nF only = 133\nOutside H, R and F = 130\n\nCheck: 40 + 123 + 412 + 247 + 481 + 259 + 133 + 130 = 1825 ✓",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(d)": "R′ ∩ F means a professional from area B.\nFrom the table (or Venn diagram: 247 + 133) there are 380 such employees.\nP(R′ ∩ F) = 380/1825 = 76/365 = 0.208 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(e)": "[H ∪ R]′ is the region outside both H and R: F only and outside all three circles.\nNumber = 133 + 130 = 263\nP([H ∪ R]′) = 263/1825 = 0.144 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::5(f)": "Total in H = 40 + 123 + 481 + 247 = 891\nIn F ∩ H = 247 + 481 = 728\nP(F | H) = P(F ∩ H)/P(H) = 728/891 = 0.817 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::6(a)": "As the number of minutes spent exercising each week (m) increases, the resting heart rate (h) decreases. The decrease is not linear: the curve flattens out as m increases, so each additional minute of exercise has a smaller effect on resting heart rate.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::6(b)": "Let ρ be the population product moment correlation coefficient between x and y.\nH₀: ρ = 0\nH₁: ρ < 0\nOne-tailed test, 5% level, n = 19: critical value = −0.3887\nCritical region: r ≤ −0.3887\nSince r = −0.897 < −0.3887, the result is significant, so reject H₀.\nThere is evidence of a negative correlation between x and y (that is, between log₁₀ m and log₁₀ h).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p31::6(c)": "Substitute x = log₁₀ m and y = log₁₀ h into y = −0.05x + 1.92:\nlog₁₀ h = −0.05 log₁₀ m + 1.92\nlog₁₀ h = log₁₀ (m^(−0.05)) + 1.92\nlog₁₀ h − log₁₀ (m^(−0.05)) = 1.92\nlog₁₀ (h/m^(−0.05)) = 1.92\nh/m^(−0.05) = 10^1.92\nh = 10^1.92 × m^(−0.05)\nh = 83.2 m^(−0.05)\n\nSo a = 10^1.92 = 83.2 (3 s.f.) and k = −0.05",





























 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::1(a)": "v = 3t²i − 6t^(½)j\nAt t = 2: v = 3(4)i − 6√2 j = 12i − 6√2 j\nSpeed = |v| = √(12² + (−6√2)²)\n= √(144 + 72)\n= √216\n= 6√6 ≈ 14.7 m s⁻¹",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::1(b)": "a = dv/dt\n= d/dt (3t²i − 6t^(½)j)\n= 6t i − 6 × ½ t^(−½) j\na = (6t i − 3t^(−½) j) m s⁻²",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::1(c)": "r = ∫ v dt = ∫ (3t²i − 6t^(½)j) dt\n= t³ i − 6 × (2/3) t^(3/2) j + C\n= t³ i − 4t^(3/2) j + C\n\nAt t = 4, r = i − 4j:\n4³ i − 4 × 4^(3/2) j + C = i − 4j\n64i − 32j + C = i − 4j\nC = −63i + 28j\n\nSo r = (t³ − 63)i + (28 − 4t^(3/2))j\nAt t = 1: r = (1 − 63)i + (28 − 4)j\nr = (−62i + 24j) m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::2(a)(i)": "tan α = 3/4, so sin α = 3/5 and cos α = 4/5.\nForces on B: weight 5g vertically down, normal reaction R = 68.6 N perpendicular to the plane, horizontal force X N, and friction F N along the plane, taken as acting down the plane.\n\nResolving vertically (X is horizontal, so it does not appear):\nR cos α − F sin α − 5g = 0\n68.6 × 4/5 − F × 3/5 = 5 × 9.8\n54.88 − 0.6F = 49\n0.6F = 5.88\nF = 9.8\n\nThe frictional force has magnitude 9.8 N.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::2(a)(ii)": "Taking friction as acting down the plane in (a)(i) gave a positive value, F = 9.8 N, so the friction acts down the plane.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::2(b)": "With X removed, B slides down the plane. sin α = 3/5, cos α = 4/5, μ = 0.5\nFriction now acts up the plane (opposing the motion).\n\nResolving perpendicular to the plane:\nR = 5g cos α = 5 × 9.8 × 4/5 = 39.2 N\n\nB is moving, so friction is limiting:\nF = 0.5R = 0.5 × 39.2 = 19.6 N\n\nEquation of motion down the plane (acceleration a m s⁻²):\n5g sin α − F = 5a\n5 × 9.8 × 3/5 − 19.6 = 5a\n29.4 − 19.6 = 5a\n9.8 = 5a\na = 1.96\n\nThe acceleration of B down the plane is 1.96 m s⁻² (= g/5).",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::3(a)": "Resultant force:\nF₁ + F₂ = (4i − j) + (λi + μj) = (4 + λ)i + (−1 + μ)j\n\nP starts from rest, so it moves in the direction of the resultant force (and of its acceleration). This must be parallel to 3i + j, so the ratio of components is 3 : 1:\n(4 + λ)/(−1 + μ) = 3/1\n4 + λ = 3(−1 + μ)\n4 + λ = −3 + 3μ\nλ − 3μ + 7 = 0, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::3(b)": "With λ = 2: 2 − 3μ + 7 = 0, so μ = 3.\nResultant force = (4 + 2)i + (−1 + 3)j = (6i + 2j) N\n\nNewton's second law, F = ma, with mass 4 kg:\n6i + 2j = 4a\na = (1.5i + 0.5j) m s⁻²\n\nP starts from rest at A, so u = 0. Using s = ut + ½at² with t = 4:\nAB (vector) = ½ × (1.5i + 0.5j) × 4²\n= 8(1.5i + 0.5j)\n= (12i + 4j) m\n\nLength AB = √(12² + 4²) = √160 = 4√10 ≈ 12.6 m",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::4(a)": "The string is perpendicular to the rod and pulls B up and to the left, so the tension has a horizontal component (T sin θ) acting to the left. The weights act vertically and the normal reaction at A is vertical, so the only other horizontal force on the rod is the friction at A. For horizontal equilibrium, the friction must therefore act horizontally to the right.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::4(b)": "Take moments about A (the friction and normal reaction at A then have no moment).\nThe weight Mg of the uniform rod acts at its midpoint, a distance a from A along the rod; its perpendicular distance from A is a cos θ.\nThe particle's weight 2Mg acts at C, AC = 1.5a; its perpendicular distance from A is 1.5a cos θ.\nThe tension T at B is perpendicular to the rod, so its moment arm is AB = 2a.\n\nM(A): T × 2a = Mg × a cos θ + 2Mg × 1.5a cos θ\n2aT = Mga cos θ + 3Mga cos θ\n2aT = 4Mga cos θ\nT = 2Mg cos θ, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::4(c)": "Let R be the vertical (normal) force from the ground at A.\nThe tension is perpendicular to the rod, which makes angle θ with the horizontal, so the tension makes angle θ with the vertical and its vertical component is T cos θ (upwards).\n\nResolving vertically:\nR + T cos θ = Mg + 2Mg\nR = 3Mg − T cos θ\nR = 3Mg − 2Mg cos²θ   (using T = 2Mg cos θ)\nWith cos θ = 3/5:\nR = 3Mg − 2Mg × 9/25\nR = 75Mg/25 − 18Mg/25\nR = 57Mg/25, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::4(d)": "cos θ = 3/5, so sin θ = 4/5.\nLet F be the frictional force at A (acting to the right).\n\nResolving horizontally:\nF = T sin θ = 2Mg cos θ sin θ = 2Mg × 3/5 × 4/5 = 24Mg/25\n\nThe rod is in limiting equilibrium, so F = μR:\n24Mg/25 = μ × 57Mg/25\nμ = 24/57\nμ = 8/19, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::5(a)": "Let t seconds be the time taken for the ball to travel from A to B.\n\nHorizontal motion (constant velocity U cos α):\nU cos α × t = 120 … (1)\n\nVertical motion (s = ut + ½at², upwards positive, a = −g, s = 0 when the ball lands at B):\nU sin α × t − ½gt² = 0\nt(U sin α − ½gt) = 0\nt ≠ 0, so t = 2U sin α/g … (2)\n\nSubstituting (2) into (1):\nU cos α × 2U sin α/g = 120\n2U² sin α cos α = 120g\nU² sin α cos α = 60g = 60 × 9.8\nU² sin α cos α = 588, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::5(b)": "At the maximum height the vertical component of velocity is zero.\nVertical motion from A to the top, using v² = u² + 2as with v = 0, u = U sin α, a = −g, s = 10:\n0 = (U sin α)² − 2g × 10\nU² sin²α = 20g = 196 … (3)\n\nFrom (a): U² sin α cos α = 588 … (4)\n\nDividing (3) by (4):\nsin α/cos α = 196/588\ntan α = 1/3\n\nα is acute, so using a right-angled triangle with opposite 1, adjacent 3 and hypotenuse √10:\nsin α = 1/√10, so sin²α = 1/10\n\nSubstituting into (3):\nU² × 1/10 = 196\nU² = 1960, as required.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::5(c)": "V is greater than U.\nWith air resistance included, the ball is slowed down by air resistance, which has to be overcome, so it needs a greater initial speed to travel the same distance AB = 120 m.",
 "math-edx-9ma0-2022-p32::5(d)": "For example: include the effect of wind; or model the ball's spin; or take into account the size and shape of the ball (model it as an object with dimensions rather than a particle); or use a more accurate value of g.",























 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::1": "Let p(x) = 3x³ − 8x² + 3x + 2.\np(2) = 3 × 2³ − 8 × 2² + 3 × 2 + 2\n     = 24 − 32 + 6 + 2\n     = 0\nSince p(2) = 0, by the factor theorem (x − 2) is a factor of 3x³ − 8x² + 3x + 2.\n(Check: 3x³ − 8x² + 3x + 2 = (x − 2)(3x² − 2x − 1), with no remainder.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::2": "Let h(x) = eˣ − 5x³, which is continuous.\nWhen x = 0: h(0) = e⁰ − 5 × 0³ = 1 > 0\nWhen x = 1: h(1) = e¹ − 5 × 1³ = e − 5 ≈ −2.28 < 0\nh is continuous and changes sign between x = 0 and x = 1, so the equation eˣ − 5x³ = 0 has a root between 0 and 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::3": "Use sec²θ = 1 + tan²θ:\n(1 + tan²θ) + 2tanθ = 4\ntan²θ + 2tanθ − 3 = 0\n(tanθ − 1)(tanθ + 3) = 0\nSo tanθ = 1 or tanθ = −3.\n\ntanθ = 1: principal value θ = 45°; tan has period 180°, so θ = 45° or 225°.\ntanθ = −3: principal value arctan(−3) = −71.57°; adding 180° gives θ = 108.4° and adding 360° gives θ = 288.4°.\n\nθ = 45°, 108.4°, 225°, 288.4° (1 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::4(i)": "u = 0, v = 1.2 m s⁻¹, s = 1.8 m (constant force, so constant acceleration).\nv² = u² + 2as\n1.2² = 0 + 2 × a × 1.8\n1.44 = 3.6a\na = 0.4 m s⁻²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::4(ii)": "Let P N be the force Rory applies. Newton's second law in the direction of motion:\nP − 19 = 2.8 × 0.4\nP − 19 = 1.12\nP = 20.12\nThe force is 20.1 N (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::5(i)": "v = dr/dt\nd/dt(1 + 12t − 2t²) = 12 − 4t\nd/dt(t² − 6t) = 2t − 6\nv = (12 − 4t)i + (2t − 6)j m s⁻¹",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::5(ii)": "The particle is stationary when both components of v are zero.\ni component: 12 − 4t = 0 ⇒ t = 3\nj component: 2t − 6 = 0 ⇒ t = 3\nBoth components are zero at the same time, t = 3, so v = 0 then.\nYes: the particle is stationary at t = 3 s.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::6(i)": "Aleela's monthly amounts form an arithmetic sequence with a = 50, d = 20; two years is n = 24 months.\nS₂₄ = (24/2)(2 × 50 + (24 − 1) × 20)\n    = 12 × (100 + 460)\n    = 12 × 560\n    = 6720\nAleela saves £6720.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::6(ii)": "Each month Baraka saves 12% more than the month before, so each amount is the previous amount multiplied by 1.12.\nA constant ratio between consecutive terms means the amounts form a geometric sequence, with first term a = 50 and common ratio r = 1.12.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::6(iii)": "Geometric series with a = 50, r = 1.12, n = 24:\nS₂₄ = 50(1.12²⁴ − 1)/(1.12 − 1)\n    = 50(15.1786… − 1)/0.12\n    = 5907.76\nBaraka saves £5907.76 in two years, which is less than Aleela's £6720.\nSo no, Baraka does not save more than Aleela.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::7(i)": "The rod is in equilibrium, so the horizontal forces balance. The 30 N and 50 N forces act in the same direction and F acts in the opposite direction:\nF = 30 + 50 = 80",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::7(ii)": "Take moments about the top of the rod (the 30 N force acts there, so it has no moment).\nThe 50 N force acts 2 m below the top; F = 80 N acts x m below the top, turning the opposite way.\nF × x = 50 × 2\n80x = 100\nx = 1.25",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::8(i)": "Use sin2x = 2sinx cosx:\n8sin²x cos²x = 2(4sin²x cos²x)\n             = 2(2sinx cosx)²\n             = 2sin²2x\nNow use cos2A = 1 − 2sin²A with A = 2x, so 2sin²2x = 1 − cos4x.\nTherefore 8sin²x cos²x = 1 − cos4x.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::8(ii)": "From (i), sin²x cos²x = (1/8)(1 − cos4x).\n∫ sin²x cos²x dx = (1/8)∫ (1 − cos4x) dx\n                 = (1/8)(x − (1/4)sin4x) + c\n                 = x/8 − (1/32)sin4x + c",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::9(i)": "Take upwards as positive. The pebble is thrown horizontally, so its initial vertical velocity is u = 0; a = −9.8 m s⁻², and it falls 5 m, so s = −5.\ns = ut + ½at²\n−5 = 0 − 4.9t²\nt² = 5/4.9 = 10/9.8\nt = √(10/9.8) = 1.01 s (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::9(ii)": "Horizontal: no acceleration, speed 14 m s⁻¹, starting at x = 0:\nx = 14t\nVertical: starts at y = 5 with zero vertical velocity, acceleration −9.8:\ny = 5 − 4.9t²\nEliminate t using t = x/14:\ny = 5 − 4.9(x/14)²\ny = 5 − 4.9x²/196\ny = 5 − x²/40",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::9(iii)": "The pebble clears the fence if its height is more than 2 m when it passes over the fence.\nFind where the trajectory is at height 2 m: put y = 2:\n2 = 5 − x²/40\nx²/40 = 3\nx² = 120\nx = √120 = 10.954… (taking x > 0)\nThe height y = 5 − x²/40 decreases as x increases, so y > 2 exactly when 0 ≤ x < √120.\nSo the fence must be less than about 10.95 m from the wall:\n0 < d < 11.0 (d < 10.95 m to 4 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::10": "The curve meets the x-axis when y = 0:\n(k − x)ln x = 0 ⇒ k − x = 0 or ln x = 0 ⇒ x = k or x = 1.\nSince k > 1, the finite region lies between x = 1 and x = k, where the curve is above the axis, so\nArea = ∫₁ᵏ (k − x)ln x dx\n\nIntegrate by parts with u = ln x, dv/dx = k − x:\ndu/dx = 1/x, v = kx − ½x²\n∫ (k − x)ln x dx = (kx − ½x²)ln x − ∫ (1/x)(kx − ½x²) dx\n                 = (kx − ½x²)ln x − ∫ (k − ½x) dx\n                 = (kx − ½x²)ln x − (kx − ¼x²)\n\nApply the limits:\nAt x = k: (k² − ½k²)ln k − (k² − ¼k²) = ½k²ln k − ¾k²\nAt x = 1: (k − ½)ln 1 − (k − ¼) = −k + ¼\nArea = (½k²ln k − ¾k²) − (−k + ¼)\nArea = ½k²ln k − ¾k² + k − ¼",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::11(i)": "Block A is on a smooth plane, so the only forces along that plane are the tension T up the slope and the component of its weight down the slope, 4.7g sin60°.\nA is in equilibrium, so resolving along its plane:\nT = 4.7g sin60°\n  = 4.7 × 9.8 × sin60°\n  = 39.889…\nSo the tension in the string is 39.9 N (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::11(ii)": "For block B (mass 4 kg) on the 25° plane, let R be the normal reaction and Fr the friction.\nB is on the point of sliding up the plane, so friction acts down the plane and is limiting: Fr = μR.\n\nResolve perpendicular to the plane:\nR = 4g cos25° = 35.527…\n\nResolve up the plane (equilibrium), using T = 4.7g sin60° = 39.889…:\nT − Fr − 4g sin25° = 0\nFr = 39.889… − 4 × 9.8 × sin25° = 39.889… − 16.566… = 23.322…\n\nLimiting friction: Fr = μR\nμ = (4.7g sin60° − 4g sin25°)/(4g cos25°)\n  = 23.322…/35.527…\n  = 0.656 (3 s.f.)\n(Using the rounded value T = 39.9 gives 0.657.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::12(i)": "The circle (x − 1)² + (y + 1)² = 25 has the form (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r² with a = 1, b = −1.\nC is (1, −1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::12(ii)(A)": "From 4y = 3x − 32, y = ¾x − 8, so y + 1 = ¾x − 7.\nSubstitute into the circle:\n(x − 1)² + (¾x − 7)² = 25\nx² − 2x + 1 + (9/16)x² − (21/2)x + 49 = 25\n(25/16)x² − (25/2)x + 25 = 0\nMultiply by 16/25:\nx² − 8x + 16 = 0\n(x − 4)² = 0\n(Equivalently, discriminant = (−8)² − 4 × 1 × 16 = 0.)\nThe equation has a repeated root, so the line meets the circle at exactly one point; hence 4y = 3x − 32 is a tangent to the circle.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::12(ii)(B)": "From (A), the repeated root is x = 4.\ny = ¾ × 4 − 8 = 3 − 8 = −5\nB is (4, −5).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::12(iii)": "CA and CB are radii, and DA and DB are tangents at A and B.\nA radius is perpendicular to the tangent at its endpoint, so ∠CAD = 90° and ∠CBD = 90°.\nGradient of AC = (2 − (−1))/(5 − 1) = 3/4\nGradient of BC = (−1 − (−5))/(1 − 4) = 4/(−3) = −4/3\n(3/4) × (−4/3) = −1, so AC is perpendicular to BC and ∠ACB = 90°.\nThe quadrilateral ADBC therefore has three right angles, so the fourth angle, ∠ADB, is also 90° (angles sum to 360°), and ADBC is a rectangle.\nAC = BC = 5 (both are radii), so two adjacent sides of the rectangle are equal.\nA rectangle with two equal adjacent sides is a square, so ADBC is a square.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::12(iv)": "The lowest point of the circle is directly below the centre at a distance of one radius:\nE = (1, −1 − 5) = (1, −6).\nCE is vertical. From C(1, −1) to B(4, −5) is 3 across and 4 down, so in the right-angled triangle with hypotenuse CB, vertical side 4 and horizontal side 3:\ntan(∠ECB) = 3/4\n∠ECB = arctan(3/4) = 0.6435 rad\n(Check with the cosine rule: BE = √((4 − 1)² + (−5 + 6)²) = √10, cos∠ECB = (25 + 25 − 10)/(2 × 5 × 5) = 40/50 = 0.8, giving 0.6435.)\nArea of sector ECB = ½r²θ\n                   = ½ × 5² × 0.6435…\n                   = 8.04 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::13(i)": "f(x) = (27 − 8x³)^(1/3)\nBy the chain rule:\nf′(x) = ⅓(27 − 8x³)^(−2/3) × (−24x²)\n      = −8x²/(27 − 8x³)^(2/3)\nAt x = 1.5: 27 − 8 × 1.5³ = 27 − 27 = 0, so\nf′(1.5) = −8 × 1.5²/0\nThis requires dividing by zero, which is undefined, so the calculator gives an error.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::13(ii)": "f(x) = (27 − 8x³)^(1/3) = 27^(1/3)(1 − 8x³/27)^(1/3) = 3(1 − 8x³/27)^(1/3)\nUsing (1 + u)ⁿ = 1 + nu + n(n − 1)u²/2! + … with n = ⅓ and u = −8x³/27:\n(1 − 8x³/27)^(1/3) = 1 + (⅓)(−8x³/27) + (⅓)(−⅔)/2 × (−8x³/27)² + …\n                   = 1 − 8x³/81 − (1/9)(64x⁶/729) + …\n                   = 1 − 8x³/81 − 64x⁶/6561 + …\nMultiply by 3:\nf(x) = 3 − 8x³/27 − 64x⁶/2187 + …",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::13(iii)": "The binomial expansion of (1 − 8x³/27)^(1/3) is valid only when |−8x³/27| < 1,\ni.e. |x³| < 27/8, i.e. |x| < 1.5.\nThe integral is over 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, which lies completely inside the interval −1.5 < x < 1.5 where the expansion is valid, so integrating the terms of the expansion is a valid way to estimate the integral.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::13(iv)": "4 strips from 0 to 1, so h = (1 − 0)/4 = 0.25.\nOrdinates from the table: y₀ = 3, y₁ = 2.9954, y₂ = 2.9625, y₃ = 2.8694, y₄ = 2.6684\n∫₀¹ ∛(27 − 8x³) dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + y₄) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)}\n = (0.25/2){(3 + 2.6684) + 2(2.9954 + 2.9625 + 2.8694)}\n = 0.125 × {5.6684 + 17.6546}\n = 0.125 × 23.323\n = 2.9154 (4 d.p.), i.e. 2.92 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::13(v)": "For 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 the curve bends downwards (concave), so the straight top of each trapezium lies below the curve.\nThe trapezia therefore leave out the area between the curve and their tops, so the trapezium rule gives an underestimate (2.9154 < 2.92117…).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(i)": "u = 5, v = 11.4, t = 4, with uniform acceleration:\na = (v − u)/t = (11.4 − 5)/4 = 1.6 m s⁻²\nv = u + at\nv = 5 + 1.6t",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(ii)": "Under model A the velocity keeps increasing at 1.6 m s⁻² for ever, so it would become unrealistically large. A real car cannot keep accelerating indefinitely, so the model is not appropriate in the long term.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(iii)": "The acceleration stops when v = 17.8:\n5 + 1.6t = 17.8 ⇒ t = (17.8 − 5)/1.6 = 8\nSo the acceleration changes at t = 8 s.\n\nSketch: horizontal axis time t (s), from 0 to 20; vertical axis velocity v (m s⁻¹).\n• A straight line segment from (0, 5) rising to (8, 17.8).\n• A horizontal line segment at v = 17.8 from t = 8 onwards (to t = 20).\nMark 5 and 17.8 on the v-axis and t = 8 on the t-axis, where the graph changes from sloping to horizontal.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(iv)": "Displacement = area under the velocity–time graph from t = 0 to t = 20.\n0 ≤ t ≤ 8 (trapezium): ½(5 + 17.8) × 8 = 91.2\n8 ≤ t ≤ 20 (rectangle): 12 × 17.8 = 213.6\nTotal displacement = 91.2 + 213.6 = 304.8 m",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(v)": "When t = 4:\nv = 5 + 0.6 × 4² − 0.05 × 4³ = 5 + 9.6 − 3.2 = 11.4 m s⁻¹\nThis matches the given data (11.4 m s⁻¹ after 4 s), so it is an appropriate value.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(vi)": "For 0 ≤ t ≤ 8 in model B:\na = dv/dt = 0.6 × 2t − 0.05 × 3t² = 1.2t − 0.15t²\nWhen t = 8: a = 1.2 × 8 − 0.15 × 64 = 9.6 − 9.6 = 0\nSo in model B the acceleration has fallen to zero just before the velocity becomes constant, and model B's velocity also reaches 17.8 at t = 8 (5 + 38.4 − 25.6 = 17.8), so there is no sudden change in acceleration.\nIn model A the acceleration drops suddenly from 1.6 m s⁻² to 0 at t = 8. A real car reaches its top speed smoothly rather than with an abrupt change in acceleration, so model B is likely to be the better model.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p1::14(vii)": "Displacement for 0 ≤ t ≤ 8 in model B:\n∫₀⁸ (5 + 0.6t² − 0.05t³) dt = [5t + 0.2t³ − 0.0125t⁴]₀⁸\n = 5 × 8 + 0.2 × 512 − 0.0125 × 4096 − 0\n = 40 + 102.4 − 51.2\n = 91.2 m\nThis is the same as model A's displacement for the first 8 s (91.2 m).\nFor 8 < t ≤ 20 both models have constant velocity 17.8 m s⁻¹, so each gives a further 12 × 17.8 = 213.6 m.\nModel B total = 91.2 + 213.6 = 304.8 m, the same as model A at t = 20 s.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::1": "√27 = √(9 × 3) = 3√3\n√192 = √(64 × 3) = 8√3\nSo √27 + √192 = 3√3 + 8√3 = 11√3.\nThis is a√b with a = 11 and b = 3, both prime.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::2": "|2x + 1| < 5 means −5 < 2x + 1 < 5.\nSubtract 1: −6 < 2x < 4\nDivide by 2: −3 < x < 2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::3(i)": "P(not win a match) = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6\nAssuming the results of the two matches are independent:\nP(fail to win both) = 0.6 × 0.6 = 0.36",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::3(ii)": "Winning and losing are not the only possible outcomes: a match can also be drawn. Since P(draw) ≠ 0, the events W and L are not exhaustive, so P(W) + P(L) = 0.7 ≠ 1 (the remaining 0.3 is the probability of a draw).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::4(i)": "Number of households n = 8 + 22 + 31 + 27 + 7 = 95\nΣfx = 0×8 + 1×22 + 2×31 + 3×27 + 4×7 = 0 + 22 + 62 + 81 + 28 = 193\nMean x̄ = 193/95 = 2.0315… ≈ 2.03 cars per household",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::4(ii)": "Σfx² = 0×8 + 1×22 + 4×31 + 9×27 + 16×7 = 0 + 22 + 124 + 243 + 112 = 501\nSxx = Σfx² − (Σfx)²/n = 501 − 193²/95 = 501 − 392.0947… = 108.905…\ns² = Sxx/(n − 1) = 108.905…/94 = 1.15856…\ns = √1.15856… = 1.0764… ≈ 1.08 cars",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::5(i)(A)": "Sketch description: an increasing exponential curve lying entirely above the x-axis.\n• For negative x (second quadrant) the curve is very close to the negative x-axis, approaching it as an asymptote (y → 0 as x → −∞) but never touching or crossing it.\n• It passes through (0, 1) on the y-axis.\n• For positive x (first quadrant) it rises ever more steeply, e.g. through (1, 3) and (2, 9).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::5(i)(B)": "When x = 0, y = 3⁰ = 1, so the curve meets the y-axis at (0, 1).\nSince 3ˣ > 0 for all x, the curve never meets the x-axis.\nThe only intercept is (0, 1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::5(ii)": "Reflecting in y = x swaps x and y, so the reflected curve is x = 3ʸ.\nTaking logs to base 3: y = log₃ x.\nSo f(x) = log₃ x (for x > 0).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::6(i)": "R cos(x + α) = R cos x cos α − R sin x sin α\nComparing with 7cos x − 24sin x:\nR cos α = 7, R sin α = 24\nR² = 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625, so R = 25\ntan α = 24/7, so α = tan⁻¹(24/7) = 1.2870… radians (this lies between 0 and π/2)\n7cos x − 24sin x = 25cos(x + 1.29)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::6(ii)": "f(x) = 12 + 25cos(x + 1.29)\nFor 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π, x + 1.29 runs over a full interval of length 2π, so cos(x + 1.29) takes every value from −1 to 1 inclusive.\nMinimum: 12 − 25 = −13; maximum: 12 + 25 = 37\nRange: −13 ≤ f(x) ≤ 37",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::7": "Write the integrand in powers of x: 4√x − 6/x³ = 4x^(1/2) − 6x⁻³\n∫ 4x^(1/2) dx = 4 × x^(3/2)/(3/2) = (8/3)x^(3/2)\n∫ −6x⁻³ dx = −6 × x⁻²/(−2) = 3x⁻²\nSo ∫ (4√x − 6/x³) dx = (8/3)x^(3/2) + 3x⁻² + c\n(equivalently (8/3)x√x + 3/x² + c)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::8(i)": "Let W be the number of games Laura wins out of 20. Then W ~ B(20, 0.7).\nP(W = 14) = ²⁰C₁₄ × 0.7¹⁴ × 0.3⁶\n= 38760 × 0.0067822… × 0.000729\n= 0.1916… ≈ 0.192",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::8(ii)": "W ~ B(20, 0.7)\nP(W ≥ 14) = 1 − P(W ≤ 13)\nFrom the cumulative binomial (calculator): P(W ≤ 13) = 0.39199…\nP(W ≥ 14) = 1 − 0.39199… = 0.6080… ≈ 0.608",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::9(i)": "The marks are bunched towards the high end (70s, 80s and 90s), with a long tail stretching down to the low marks (35, 40, 49).\nThe distribution has negative skew.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::9(ii)": "He used the mode: 95 is the most common mark in set 1 (it occurs three times, more than any other mark).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::9(iii)": "She used the median. There are 31 marks, so the median is the 16th mark in order; counting through the stem-and-leaf diagram (1 + 2 + 3 + 5 = 11 marks below 70, then 70, 71, 72, 75, 76), the 16th mark is 76.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::9(iv)": "There are 31 marks.\nLower quartile Q₁ = 8th mark = 61\nUpper quartile Q₃ = 24th mark = 88\nInterquartile range = 88 − 61 = 27\nLower fence for outliers = Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR = 61 − 1.5 × 27 = 61 − 40.5 = 20.5\nBenson's mark 35 > 20.5, so 35 is not an outlier. Benson is not moved down to set 2.\n\nNote: the answer depends on the definition of outlier used. With the alternative rule \"more than 2 standard deviations below the mean\": mean = 73.61, s = 17.03, so mean − 2s = 73.61 − 34.06 = 39.6; since 35 < 39.6, 35 would be an outlier and Benson would be moved to set 2. Either conclusion is acceptable if it follows correctly from the rule stated.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::10(i)": "The two unshaded tails have equal area (0.025 each), so the shaded region is symmetric about the mean.\nμ = (16.452 + 21.548)/2 = 38/2 = 19",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::10(ii)": "The upper tail has area 0.025, so 21.548 is the upper 2.5% point:\nP(X > 21.548) = 0.025 ⇒ (21.548 − 19)/σ = 1.96\nσ = 2.548/1.96 = 1.3\nσ² = 1.3² = 1.69",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::10(iii)(A)": "Y = 4X + 5 is a linear function of a Normal variable, so Y is also Normally distributed.\nE(Y) = 4 × 19 + 5 = 81\nVar(Y) = 4² × 1.69 = 16 × 1.69 = 27.04 (so the standard deviation is 4 × 1.3 = 5.2)\nY ~ N(81, 27.04), i.e. Y ~ N(81, 5.2²)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::10(iii)(B)": "Y ~ N(81, 5.2²)\nP(Y > 90) = P(Z > (90 − 81)/5.2) = P(Z > 1.7308) = 0.04175 ≈ 0.0417",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::11(i)": "P(X = 0) = k × 1 × 6 = 6k\nP(X = 1) = k × 2 × 5 = 10k\nP(X = 2) = k × 3 × 4 = 12k\nP(X = 3) = k × 4 × 3 = 12k\nP(X = 4) = k × 5 × 2 = 10k\nP(X = 5) = k × 6 × 1 = 6k\nThe probabilities sum to 1: (6 + 10 + 12 + 12 + 10 + 6)k = 56k = 1\nk = 1/56",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::11(ii)": "P(X = 0) = 6/56 = 3/28 and P(X = 2) = 12/56 = 3/14\nAssuming days are independent:\nP = (3/28)³ × (3/14)² = 27/21952 × 9/196 = 243/4302592 ≈ 0.0000565 (5.65 × 10⁻⁵)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::11(iii)": "P(no gold stars on a day) = P(X = 0) = 6/56 = 3/28\nExpected number of days = 40 × 3/28 = 30/7 ≈ 4.29 days",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::12": "Let M = the event \"at least level 4 in mathematics\" and E = the event \"at least level 4 in English\".\nP(M) = 0.56, P(E) = 0.80, P(neither) = P(M' ∩ E') = 0.14\n\nP(M ∪ E) = 1 − 0.14 = 0.86\nUsing P(M ∪ E) = P(M) + P(E) − P(M ∩ E):\n0.86 = 0.56 + 0.80 − P(M ∩ E)\nP(M ∩ E) = 1.36 − 0.86 = 0.5\n\nIf M and E were independent, P(M ∩ E) would equal P(M) × P(E) = 0.56 × 0.80 = 0.448.\nSince 0.448 ≠ 0.5, P(M ∩ E) ≠ P(M) × P(E).\nSo achieving level 4 or above in English and achieving level 4 or above in mathematics were not independent events.\n(Equivalently, P(M | E) = 0.5/0.80 = 0.625 ≠ 0.56 = P(M).)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::13(i)": "Under the model X ~ N(15, 4), σ = 2.\nP(X < 14) = P(Z < (14 − 15)/2) = P(Z < −0.5) = 0.3085\nP(X > 18) = P(Z > (18 − 15)/2) = P(Z > 1.5) = 0.0668\nThe model predicts about 31% of journeys under 14 minutes and about 7% over 18 minutes, but the observed figures are 7% under 14 minutes and 31% over 18 minutes.\nThese do not match (the model gets the two tails the wrong way round), so Keira's model is not a good fit for the data.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::13(ii)": "Keep σ = 2 and find μ so that P(X < 14) = 0.07.\nΦ⁻¹(0.07) = −1.476, so (14 − μ)/2 = −1.476\nμ = 14 + 2 × 1.476 = 16.95\nCheck with the other tail: P(X > 18) = 0.31 ⇒ P(X < 18) = 0.69, Φ⁻¹(0.69) = 0.496, so (18 − μ)/2 = 0.496 ⇒ μ = 18 − 0.992 = 17.01\nBoth give μ = 17 to 2 significant figures.\nThe refined model uses mean 17 minutes (X ~ N(17, 4)).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::13(iii)": "Refined model: X ~ N(17, 2²). Let μ be the mean journey time with the new car.\nH₀: μ = 17\nH₁: μ ≠ 17 (two-tailed, since she is testing whether the mean has changed)\nSignificance level 5%, so 2.5% in each tail; critical values z = ±1.96.\n\nUnder H₀, the sample mean X̄ ~ N(17, 2²/n), so the test statistic is\nz = (16 − 17)/(2/√n) = −√n/2\n\nThe sample mean 16 is below 17, so the result is significant when\n−√n/2 < −1.96\n√n > 2 × 1.96 = 3.92\nn > 3.92² = 15.3664\n\nn must be a whole number, so the smallest possible value is n = 16.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(i)(A)": "Error: the cumulative frequencies have been plotted against the mid-points of the classes (2.5, 7.5, 12.5, …) instead of against the upper class boundaries (5, 10, 15, …).\nEffect: each point for the classes from 0 to 20 is plotted 2.5 too far to the left (the last two points are 7.5 too far left), so the curve is shifted left; the median (cumulative frequency 25) lies in the 5–10 class, where the shift is 2.5, and Hodge's estimate of the median is too low, by about 2.5.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(i)(B)": "Factor: Hodge used grouped data (the cumulative frequency graph) rather than the raw data, so the curve assumes values are spread through each class and the median can only be estimated.\nEffect: correcting the mis-plotting alone would raise Hodge's 5.0 by about 2.5 to roughly 7.4–7.5, which is above the true median 6.9; so the grouping on its own pushes the estimate up, by roughly half a unit. This partly offsets the mis-plotting error: Hodge's estimate is 6.9 − 5.0 = 1.9 too low, which is less than the 2.5 caused by the mis-plotting.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(ii)": "Unemployment rates in the pre-release material are often estimates rather than exact figures, so any median calculated from them may be unreliable. (Also, data are not available for every country in Europe.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(iii)": "The pre-release material contains many countries outside Europe. If 50 countries had been chosen at random from it, it is very unlikely that every one of them would be European; so the sample was almost certainly chosen deliberately from the European countries, not randomly from the whole material.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(iv)": "r = −0.2607 is negative, suggesting a (weak) negative association: countries with higher unemployment rates tend to have lower life expectancy at birth.\nTesting at the 5% level for a negative association (a one-tailed test; the p-value given is one-tailed): 0.0383 < 0.05, so the result is significant. There is evidence of a negative association between unemployment rate and life expectancy at birth in countries in Europe.\n(At the 1% level it would not be significant, since 0.0383 > 0.01; the evidence is therefore not strong, and the correlation is weak. A two-tailed 5% test would compare 0.0383 with 0.025 and would not be significant.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::14(v)": "No. Although 35.3 lies within the range of the data (so this would be interpolation), the correlation is weak (r = −0.2607) and the points are widely scattered, with an outlier at an unemployment rate of about 43 that strongly influences the line. Also, 35.3 lies in the large gap in the data between about 25 and 43, where there are no points. So the line of best fit is not a reliable way to estimate Kosovo's life expectancy.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::15": "Points where y = 1:\n1 − x + 4√x = 4\n4√x = x + 3, i.e. x − 4√x + 3 = 0\n(√x − 1)(√x − 3) = 0, so √x = 1 or √x = 3\nx = 1 or x = 9\nCheck: x = 1: 1 − 1 + 4 = 4 ✓; x = 9: 1 − 9 + 12 = 4 ✓\nThe points are (1, 1) and (9, 1).\n\nDifferentiate y³ − xy + 4√x = 4 implicitly with respect to x (product rule on xy):\n3y² dy/dx − (x dy/dx + y) + 2/√x = 0\ndy/dx (3y² − x) = y − 2/√x\ndy/dx = (y − 2/√x)/(3y² − x)\n\nAt (1, 1): dy/dx = (1 − 2/1)/(3 − 1) = −1/2\nAt (9, 1): dy/dx = (1 − 2/3)/(3 − 9) = (1/3)/(−6) = −1/18\n\nThe gradient is −½ at (1, 1) and −1/18 (≈ −0.0556) at (9, 1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::16(i)": "p = A − B(t − C)² with B > 0 has its maximum when (t − C)² = 0, i.e. t = C, and the maximum value is A.\nThe maximum is at t = 2, so C = 2.\nThe maximum mark is 62, so A = 62.\nWhen t = 0, p = 22: 22 = 62 − B(0 − 2)² = 62 − 4B, so 4B = 40 and B = 10.\nA = 62, B = 10, C = 2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::16(ii)": "45 minutes = 0.75 hours.\np = 62 − 10(0.75 − 2)² = 62 − 10 × (−1.25)² = 62 − 10 × 1.5625 = 62 − 15.625 = 46.375\nShe would achieve a mark of about 46%.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::16(iii)": "The mark cannot be negative, so the model only works while p ≥ 0.\n62 − 10(t − 2)² = 0 ⇒ (t − 2)² = 6.2 ⇒ t − 2 = ±√6.2\nFor t > 2: t = 2 + √6.2 = 4.4899… hours\n(The other root, 2 − √6.2, is negative, so it is not relevant.)\nThe maximum revision time for the model to work is about 4.49 hours, i.e. about 4 hours 29 minutes.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::16(iv)": "p = 22 + 68(1 − e^(−0.8t))\nt = 1: p = 22 + 68(1 − e^(−0.8)) = 22 + 68 × 0.55067 = 59.4 ≈ 59\nt = 3: p = 22 + 68(1 − e^(−2.4)) = 22 + 68 × 0.90928 = 83.8 ≈ 84\nt = 5: p = 22 + 68(1 − e^(−4)) = 22 + 68 × 0.98168 = 88.8 ≈ 89\nEach value rounds to the recorded mark, so the data are consistent with the new formula.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::16(v)": "As t increases, e^(−0.8t) → 0, so p → 22 + 68 = 90: the mark can never exceed 90.\nAt t = 12: p = 22 + 68(1 − e^(−9.6)) = 89.995… ≈ 90\nAt t = 5 the model already predicts p ≈ 89 (88.8), and at t = 7 it predicts p ≈ 89.7, i.e. 90 to the nearest mark.\nSo working for 12 hours rather than about 5 to 7 hours gains at most about one mark. This is not good advice.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::17(i)": "Let (x² − 8x + 9)/((x + 1)(x − 2)²) ≡ A/(x + 1) + B/(x − 2) + C/(x − 2)²\nMultiply through by (x + 1)(x − 2)²:\nx² − 8x + 9 ≡ A(x − 2)² + B(x + 1)(x − 2) + C(x + 1)\n\nx = −1: 1 + 8 + 9 = 9A ⇒ 18 = 9A ⇒ A = 2\nx = 2: 4 − 16 + 9 = 3C ⇒ −3 = 3C ⇒ C = −1\nCompare coefficients of x²: 1 = A + B ⇒ B = 1 − 2 = −1\n\n(x² − 8x + 9)/((x + 1)(x − 2)²) = 2/(x + 1) − 1/(x − 2) − 1/(x − 2)²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p2::17(ii)": "Separate the variables:\n∫ (1/y) dy = ∫ (x² − 8x + 9)/((x + 1)(x − 2)²) dx\nUsing part (i):\n∫ (1/y) dy = ∫ (2/(x + 1) − 1/(x − 2) − 1/(x − 2)²) dx\nln|y| = 2ln|x + 1| − ln|x − 2| + 1/(x − 2) + c\n(since ∫ −(x − 2)⁻² dx = (x − 2)⁻¹)\n\nUse y = 16 when x = 3:\nln 16 = 2ln 4 − ln 1 + 1/1 + c\nln 16 = ln 16 − 0 + 1 + c ⇒ c = −1\n\nNear the given point x > 2 and y > 0, so the modulus signs can be dropped:\nln y = ln((x + 1)²/(x − 2)) + 1/(x − 2) − 1\nExponentiate:\ny = ((x + 1)²/(x − 2)) × e^(1/(x − 2) − 1)\nSince 1/(x − 2) − 1 = (1 − (x − 2))/(x − 2) = (3 − x)/(x − 2):\ny = ((x + 1)²/(x − 2)) e^((3 − x)/(x − 2))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::1": "Use the cosine rule in triangle ABC with the included angle A = 85°:\nBC² = AB² + AC² − 2 × AB × AC × cos A\nBC² = 32² + 14² − 2 × 32 × 14 × cos 85°\nBC² = 1024 + 196 − 896 cos 85°\nBC² = 1220 − 78.09…\nBC² = 1141.9…\nBC = 33.79… = 33.8 cm (3 s.f.)\n\nPerimeter = AB + AC + BC = 32 + 14 + 33.79…\nPerimeter = 79.8 cm (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::2": "A translation by the vector (1, −4) moves every point 1 unit in the positive x-direction and 4 units in the negative y-direction.\nSo replace x by (x − 1) and subtract 4 from the whole function:\n\ny = (x − 1)³ − 2(x − 1) − 4\n\n(Expanded, this is y = x³ − 3x² + x − 3.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::3": "Triangle OCB has a right angle at C, and OB = 1 (radius).\nSo OC = OB cos θ = cos θ.\n\nA lies on the circle, so OA = 1, and C lies on AO, so\nAC = OA − OC = 1 − cos θ.\n\nFor small θ (in radians), cos θ ≈ 1 − ½θ².\nSo AC ≈ 1 − (1 − ½θ²) = ½θ², as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::4(i)": "y = x − 5 + (x − 2)⁻¹\n\ndy/dx = 1 − (x − 2)⁻² = 1 − 1/(x − 2)²\n\nAt stationary points dy/dx = 0:\n1 − 1/(x − 2)² = 0\n(x − 2)² = 1\nx − 2 = 1 or x − 2 = −1\nx = 3 or x = 1\n\nWhen x = 1: y = 1 − 5 + 1/(1 − 2) = −4 − 1 = −5\nWhen x = 3: y = 3 − 5 + 1/(3 − 2) = −2 + 1 = −1\n\nThe stationary points are (1, −5) and (3, −1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::4(ii)": "dy/dx = 1 − (x − 2)⁻²\nd²y/dx² = 2(x − 2)⁻³ = 2/(x − 2)³\n\nAt x = 3: d²y/dx² = 2/(1)³ = 2 > 0, so (3, −1) is a minimum point.\nAt x = 1: d²y/dx² = 2/(−1)³ = −2 < 0, so (1, −5) is a maximum point.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::4(iii)": "The term 1/(x − 2) is undefined when x − 2 = 0, and y → ±∞ as x → 2.\n\nThe vertical asymptote is x = 2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::4(iv)": "The curve is concave upwards where d²y/dx² > 0.\nd²y/dx² = 2/(x − 2)³, which is positive exactly when (x − 2)³ > 0, i.e. when x − 2 > 0.\n(For x < 2 it is negative, and at x = 2 the curve is not defined.)\n\nSo the curve is concave upwards for x > 2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::5(i)": "n = a × 2^(kt)\n\nTake logs to base 10 of both sides:\nlog₁₀n = log₁₀a + log₁₀(2^(kt))\nlog₁₀n = log₁₀a + kt log₁₀2\nlog₁₀n = (k log₁₀2) t + log₁₀a\n\nThis has the form Y = mX + c with Y = log₁₀n and X = t, where the gradient m = k log₁₀2 and the intercept c = log₁₀a are constants.\nSo, according to the model, the graph of log₁₀n against t is a straight line.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::5(ii)": "Draw a line of best fit by eye through the seven plotted points. A suitable line passes through about (0, 1.0) and (6, 3.0) (it lies close to the points (1, 1.25), (4, 2.35) and (7, 3.25)).\n\nIntercept: log₁₀a ≈ 1.0, so a ≈ 10¹·⁰ = 10.\n\nGradient ≈ (3.0 − 1.0)/(6 − 0) = 0.33.\nFrom part (i), gradient = k log₁₀2, so\nk log₁₀2 ≈ 0.33\nk ≈ 0.33/0.30103 ≈ 1.1\n\nEstimates: a ≈ 10, k ≈ 1.1.\n\n(These are estimates from a line drawn by eye, so slightly different values are reasonable; for example, a least-squares line through the plotted points gives a ≈ 11 and k ≈ 1.09.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::5(iii)": "Using a = 10 and k = 1.1, set n = 500 000:\n500 000 = 10 × 2^(1.1t)\n2^(1.1t) = 50 000\n1.1t log₁₀2 = log₁₀50 000\nt = log₁₀50 000 / (1.1 × log₁₀2)\nt = 4.69897… / 0.33113…\nt = 14.2 (3 s.f.)\n\nConverting to dates: t = 1 is 1 February 2017, so t = 12 is 1 January 2018, t = 13 is 1 February 2018 and t = 14 is 1 March 2018.\n\nThe number of users is reported only at the start of each month. At t = 14 the model gives fewer than half a million users (since 14 < 14.2), so the first report showing half a million users is the next one, at t = 15.\n\nEstimated date: 1 April 2018.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::5(iv)": "The data cover only the first seven months, so using the model for large t is extrapolating far beyond the data and may not be reliable. Also, the model predicts exponential growth without limit, but there is only a finite number of people who could use the website, so the growth cannot continue indefinitely.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::6": "The general term of (x² + 1/x)¹⁵ is\n¹⁵Cᵣ (x²)^(15 − r) (1/x)^r = ¹⁵Cᵣ x^(30 − 2r − r) = ¹⁵Cᵣ x^(30 − 3r).\n\nFor the constant term, 30 − 3r = 0, so r = 10.\nThe term is ¹⁵C₁₀ (x²)⁵ (1/x)¹⁰ = ¹⁵C₅ x¹⁰ × x⁻¹⁰ = ¹⁵C₅.\n\n¹⁵C₅ = 15!/(5! × 10!) = 3003\n\nThe constant term is 3003.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::7": "The curve meets the x-axis where 5x − x² = 0:\nx(5 − x) = 0, so x = 0 or x = 5.\n\nThe line y = 4 − kx passes through (0, 4) when x = 0, so it does not pass through the origin. Hence the crossing point on the x-axis is (5, 0).\n\nSubstitute (5, 0) into y = 4 − kx:\n0 = 4 − 5k, so k = 4/5.\n\nThe line is y = 4 − (4/5)x. Equate with the curve:\n4 − (4/5)x = 5x − x²\nMultiply by 5: 20 − 4x = 25x − 5x²\n5x² − 29x + 20 = 0\n(5x − 4)(x − 5) = 0\nx = 5 (the point on the x-axis) or x = 4/5.\n\nWhen x = 4/5: y = 4 − (4/5)(4/5) = 4 − 16/25 = 84/25.\n(Check on the curve: 5(4/5) − (4/5)² = 4 − 16/25 = 84/25.)\n\nThe other point is (4/5, 84/25), i.e. (0.8, 3.36).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::8(i)": "x = t/(1 + t³). Using the quotient rule:\ndx/dt = [(1 + t³)(1) − t(3t²)]/(1 + t³)² = (1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)²\n\ny = t²/(1 + t³). Using the quotient rule:\ndy/dt = [(1 + t³)(2t) − t²(3t²)]/(1 + t³)² = (2t + 2t⁴ − 3t⁴)/(1 + t³)² = (2t − t⁴)/(1 + t³)²\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³)\n\nAt t = 1:\ndy/dx = (2 − 1)/(1 − 2) = 1/(−1) = −1\n\nThe gradient of the curve at the point where t = 1 is −1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::8(ii)": "LHS: x³ + y³ = t³/(1 + t³)³ + t⁶/(1 + t³)³\n= (t³ + t⁶)/(1 + t³)³\n= t³(1 + t³)/(1 + t³)³\n= t³/(1 + t³)²   (since 1 + t³ ≠ 0 for t ≠ −1)\n\nRHS: xy = [t/(1 + t³)] × [t²/(1 + t³)] = t³/(1 + t³)²\n\nLHS = RHS, so every point of the curve satisfies x³ + y³ = xy, which is the cartesian equation of the curve.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::9(i)": "Let y = eˣ/(1 − eˣ).\ny(1 − eˣ) = eˣ\ny − yeˣ = eˣ\ny = eˣ + yeˣ = eˣ(1 + y)\neˣ = y/(1 + y)\nx = ln(y/(1 + y))\n\nInterchanging x and y:\nf⁻¹(x) = ln(x/(1 + x))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::9(ii)": "The range of f⁻¹ is the domain of f, which is all real numbers except 0.\n\nRange: f⁻¹(x) ∈ ℝ, f⁻¹(x) ≠ 0.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::10(i)": "Position vectors: A = (a, b, 0), B = (4, 2, 0), C = (2, 4, 2).\n\nAB→ = (4 − a, 2 − b, 0)\nAC→ = (2 − a, 4 − b, 2)\n\nAC = AB, so AC² = AB²:\n(4 − a)² + (2 − b)² + 0² = (2 − a)² + (4 − b)² + 2²\n16 − 8a + a² + 4 − 4b + b² = 4 − 4a + a² + 16 − 8b + b² + 4\n20 − 8a − 4b = 24 − 4a − 8b\n0 = 4 + 4a − 4b\n4a − 4b + 4 = 0\na − b + 1 = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::10(ii)": "From (i), b = a + 1, so A = (a, a + 1, 0).\n\nLet M be the midpoint of BC:\nM = ½[(4, 2, 0) + (2, 4, 2)] = (3, 3, 1).\n\nSince AB = AC, triangle ABC is isosceles with apex A, so AM is perpendicular to BC and AM is the height of the triangle with base BC.\n(Check: AM→ · BC→ = (3 − a, 2 − a, 1) · (−2, 2, 2) = −6 + 2a + 4 − 2a + 2 = 0.)\n\nAM→ = (3 − a, 3 − (a + 1), 1) = (3 − a, 2 − a, 1)\nBC→ = (−2, 2, 2), so BC = √(4 + 4 + 4) = √12 = 2√3.\n\nArea = ½ × BC × AM\n= ½ × 2√3 × √((3 − a)² + (2 − a)² + 1)\n= √3 √(2a² − 10a + 14)\n\nBC is fixed, so the area is least when AM² = 2a² − 10a + 14 is least.\nComplete the square:\n2a² − 10a + 14 = 2(a² − 5a + 7) = 2((a − 2.5)² + 0.75)\n\nSo Area = √3 √(2((a − 2.5)² + 0.75)), which is least when a − 2.5 = 0, i.e. a = 2.5.\nThen b = a + 1 = 3.5.\n\nThe position vector of A for minimum area is (2.5, 3.5, 0).\n(The minimum area is √3 × √1.5 = √4.5 ≈ 2.12 square units.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::11(i)": "If one of a and b is positive and the other negative, then ab is negative, so √(ab) is not a real number. The geometric mean cannot be calculated, so the inequality has no meaning in this case.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::11(ii)": "If a and b are both negative, then ab is positive, so √(ab) can be calculated and is positive. But the arithmetic mean (a + b)/2 is negative. So the arithmetic mean is less than √(ab), and the inequality (a + b)/2 ≥ √(ab) is false (for every pair of negative numbers).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::12": "Let a, b ≥ 0.\n\n(a − b)² ≥ 0\na² − 2ab + b² ≥ 0\na² + b² ≥ 2ab\n\nAdd 2ab to each side:\na² + 2ab + b² ≥ 4ab\n(a + b)² ≥ 4ab\n\nSince a, b ≥ 0, both a + b ≥ 0 and 4ab ≥ 0, so taking the non-negative square root of each side keeps the inequality:\na + b ≥ √(4ab) = 2√(ab)\n\nDivide by 2:\n(a + b)/2 ≥ √(ab), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::13": "Let the common ratio be r and let the middle term of three consecutive terms be c. Then the three terms are\nc/r, c, cr.\nAll the terms are positive, so c > 0 and r > 0.\n\nThe geometric mean of the first and last of these terms is\n√((c/r) × cr) = √(c²) = c   (since c > 0).\n\nThis is the middle term, so the middle one of any three consecutive terms is the geometric mean of the other two.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::14(i)": "In Fig. C1.3, CD is perpendicular to AB, so angle BCD = 90°.\n\nIn triangle BCD, the angles add up to 180°:\nangle BDC = 180° − 90° − θ = 90° − θ.\n\nAB is a diameter of the semicircle and D lies on the arc, so angle ADB = 90° (angle in a semicircle).\n\nC lies between A and B, so angle ADB = angle CDA + angle BDC. Hence\nangle CDA = 90° − (90° − θ) = θ.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::14(ii)": "In triangle BCD (right angle at C), angle CBD = θ, the opposite side is CD = h and the adjacent side is CB = b:\ntan θ = h/b\n\nIn triangle ACD (right angle at C), angle CDA = θ from part (i), the opposite side is AC = a and the adjacent side is CD = h:\ntan θ = a/h\n\nEquating:\na/h = h/b\nh² = ab\nh = √(ab)   (h is a length, so h > 0), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2018-p3::15": "The square with side L has perimeter 4L and area L².\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that there is a rectangle with perimeter 4L whose area A is larger than the area of this square, so A > L².\n\nBy lines 31–32, the square with the same area A as this rectangle has a perimeter no larger than the rectangle's perimeter. That square has side √A, so\n4√A ≤ 4L, which gives √A ≤ L, so A ≤ L².\n\nThis contradicts A > L². So no rectangle with perimeter 4L has a larger area than the square with side L: among rectangles of perimeter 4L, the square with side L has the largest area.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::1": "Write √x as x^(1/2) and integrate term by term:\n∫ (2x + x^(1/2)) dx = x² + x^(3/2)/(3/2) = x² + (2/3)x^(3/2)\nSo ∫₄⁹ (2x + √x) dx = [x² + (2/3)x^(3/2)] from 4 to 9\n= (9² + (2/3) × 9^(3/2)) − (4² + (2/3) × 4^(3/2))\nSince 9^(3/2) = 27 and 4^(3/2) = 8:\n= (81 + 18) − (16 + 16/3)\n= 81 + 18 − 16 − 16/3\n= 83 − 16/3\n= 249/3 − 16/3\n= 233/3, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::2": "Gradient of the line through (2, −4) and (−1, 5):\nm = (5 − (−4))/(−1 − 2) = 9/(−3) = −3\nEquation of this line: y − (−4) = −3(x − 2), so y = −3x + 2.\nThe given line 3x + y = 10 can be written y = −3x + 10, which also has gradient −3.\nThe two lines have the same gradient, so they are parallel. Their y-intercepts are different (2 and 10), so they are not the same line (for example, (2, −4) does not lie on 3x + y = 10, since 3 × 2 + (−4) = 2 ≠ 10).\nTwo distinct parallel lines never meet, so the line through (2, −4) and (−1, 5) does not intersect the line 3x + y = 10.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::3(a)": "Using (1 + u)ⁿ = 1 + nu + (n(n − 1)/2!)u² + … with n = −3 and u = −ax:\n(1 − ax)^(−3) = 1 + (−3)(−ax) + ((−3)(−4)/2)(−ax)² + …\n= 1 + 3ax + 6a²x² + …\nCoefficient of x = 3a, coefficient of x² = 6a².\nEquating: 3a = 6a²\n6a² − 3a = 0\n3a(2a − 1) = 0\na is non-zero, so 2a − 1 = 0, giving\na = ½",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::3(b)(i)": "With a = ½, f(x) = (1 − ½x)^(−3).\nThe expansion is valid when |−½x| < 1, i.e. |½x| < 1, so\n|x| < 2 (that is, −2 < x < 2).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::3(b)(ii)": "From (a), f(x) = 1 + 3ax + 6a²x² + …\nWith a = ½: 3a = 3/2 and 6a² = 6 × ¼ = 3/2.\nSo for small x,\nf(x) ≈ 1 + (3/2)x + (3/2)x²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::4": "The person is most likely to tip the beam by standing on the end beyond Q, 0.3 m from Q. Let R_P and R_Q be the upward normal reactions at P and Q, and suppose the beam is in equilibrium with the person standing at that end.\nThe beam's weight 4g acts at its midpoint, 1.2 m from P, which is 2.1 − 1.2 = 0.9 m from Q (PQ = 2.4 − 0.3 = 2.1 m).\nTaking moments about Q (clockwise = anticlockwise):\n50g × 0.3 + R_P × 2.1 = 4g × 0.9\n15g + 2.1R_P = 3.6g\nR_P = (3.6g − 15g)/2.1 = −11.4g/2.1 ≈ −53.2 N\nR_P < 0, which is impossible for a normal reaction, so the beam cannot be in equilibrium: it turns about Q.\nSo yes, a person of mass 50 kg can tip the beam (by standing on the 0.3 m section of the beam beyond Q; in fact the beam tips once they are more than 0.072 m beyond Q, since 4g × 0.9 = 50g × d gives d = 0.072 m < 0.3 m).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::5": "Resultant force in the direction of motion = 4000 − 800 = 3200 N.\nNewton's second law: 4000 − 800 = 1200a\na = 3200/1200 = 8/3 m s⁻²\nThe car starts from rest, so u = 0, t = 9:\nv = u + at = 0 + (8/3) × 9 = 24\nThe velocity after 9 seconds is 24 m s⁻¹.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::6(a)": "LHS = sinθ/(1 − cosθ) − 1/sinθ\nCombine over the common denominator (1 − cosθ)sinθ:\n= (sin²θ − (1 − cosθ))/((1 − cosθ)sinθ)\nUse sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ:\n= (1 − cos²θ − 1 + cosθ)/((1 − cosθ)sinθ)\n= (cosθ − cos²θ)/((1 − cosθ)sinθ)\n= cosθ(1 − cosθ)/(sinθ(1 − cosθ))\nCancel the common factor (1 − cosθ), which is non-zero wherever the LHS is defined:\n= cosθ/sinθ\n= cotθ = RHS, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::6(b)": "By part (a), the equation becomes\ncotθ = 3tanθ\n1/tanθ = 3tanθ\ntan²θ = 1/3\ntanθ = 1/√3 or tanθ = −1/√3\nIn 0 ≤ θ ≤ π:\ntanθ = 1/√3 gives θ = π/6\ntanθ = −1/√3 gives θ = π − π/6 = 5π/6\n(θ = 0 and θ = π are not roots since sinθ = 0 there and the left-hand side is undefined; θ = π/2 is not a root since tanθ is undefined.)\nSo θ = π/6 or θ = 5π/6.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::7": "v = 0.5t(7 − t) = 3.5t − 0.5t²\nAcceleration a = dv/dt = 3.5 − t\nWhen t = 8:\na = 3.5 − 8 = −4.5 m s⁻²\nv = 3.5 × 8 − 0.5 × 8² = 28 − 32 = −4 m s⁻¹\nAt t = 8 the velocity is negative and the acceleration is also negative, so the acceleration acts in the same direction as the motion. The velocity is negative and decreasing, so its magnitude is getting larger.\nTherefore the speed of the particle is increasing when t = 8.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::8(a)": "First term a = 9300, common difference d.\n10th term: a + 9d = 3900\n9300 + 9d = 3900\n9d = −5400\nd = −600\n20th term: a + 19d = 9300 + 19 × (−600) = 9300 − 11400 = −2100\n−2100 < 0, so the 20th term is negative.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::8(b)": "S increases as long as the terms being added are positive, so S is greatest when all the positive terms (and no negative terms) are included.\nThe nth term is 9300 − 600(n − 1).\n9300 − 600(n − 1) < 0\nn − 1 > 9300/600 = 15.5\nn > 16.5\nSo the 16th term, 9300 − 600 × 15 = 300, is the last positive term and the 17th term, 9300 − 600 × 16 = −300, is the first negative term.\nThe greatest sum is therefore the sum of the first 16 terms:\nS₁₆ = (16/2)(2 × 9300 + (16 − 1)(−600))\n= 8(18600 − 9000)\n= 8 × 9600\n= 76 800\nThe greatest value of S is 76 800.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::9(a)": "Take upwards as positive. Initial vertical component of velocity:\nu_y = 100 sin25° = 42.26… m s⁻¹\nAt the greatest height the vertical velocity is 0. Using v² = u² + 2as vertically with a = −9.8:\n0 = (100 sin25°)² − 2 × 9.8 × s\ns = (100 sin25°)²/19.6 = 42.26…²/19.6 = 91.12…\nThe greatest height is 91.1 m (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::9(b)": "Vertically (upwards positive), the cannonball returns to ground level when the vertical displacement is 0:\ns = ut + ½at²\n0 = (100 sin25°)t − 4.9t²\nt(100 sin25° − 4.9t) = 0\nt = 0 (launch) or t = 100 sin25°/4.9 = 8.624… s\nHorizontally the velocity is constant, 100 cos25° = 90.63… m s⁻¹, so the range is\nx = (100 cos25°) × 8.624… = 781.67…\nThe range of the cannonball is 782 m (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::10(a)": "R cos(x + α) = R cosx cosα − R sinx sinα\nComparing with 7cosx − 2sinx:\nR cosα = 7, R sinα = 2\nSquaring and adding: R² = 7² + 2² = 53, so R = √53 (R > 0).\nDividing: tanα = 2/7, so α = arctan(2/7) = 0.27829…\n(α is in 0 < α < ½π since cosα and sinα are both positive.)\nSo 7cosx − 2sinx = √53 cos(x + 0.278), with R = √53 and α = 0.278 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::10(b)": "Using (a):\ny = 1/(7cosx − 2sinx) = 1/(√53 cos(x + α)) = (1/√53) sec(x + α), where α = 0.278.\nStarting from y = secx:\n1. A translation by the vector (−0.278, 0), i.e. 0.278 units in the negative x-direction, gives y = sec(x + 0.278).\n2. A stretch in the y-direction with scale factor 1/√53 gives y = (1/√53) sec(x + 0.278) = 1/(7cosx − 2sinx).\n(These two transformations can be applied in either order.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::11(a)": "The weight acts vertically, so the only horizontal (i) components of force are k and 4. The acceleration is vertical, so the resultant horizontal force is zero:\nk + 4 = 0\nk = −4",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::11(b)": "Weight = −0.8g j = −7.84j N.\nWith k = −4, the resultant force is\n(−4i + 5j) + (4i + 3j) − 0.8g j = (8 − 7.84)j = 0.16j N\nNewton's second law: 5j + 3j − 0.8g j = 0.8a\n0.16j = 0.8a\na = 0.2j m s⁻²\nThe acceleration is constant, so with u = 4i + 7j and t = 10:\nv = u + at = (4i + 7j) + 0.2j × 10 = 4i + 9j\nThe velocity 10 seconds later is (4i + 9j) m s⁻¹.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::12(a)": "Note: the printed equation y = 4t is a misprint; the curve intended (and shown in Fig. 12) is x = 4t², y = 8t, with P(4t², 8t). This solution uses y = 8t.\nQ lies on x = −4 with PQ parallel to the x-axis, so Q = (−4, 8t) and\nPQ = 4t² − (−4) = 4 + 4t²\nR = (4, 0), so by the distance formula\nPR² = (4t² − 4)² + (8t − 0)²\n= 16t⁴ − 32t² + 16 + 64t²\n= 16t⁴ + 32t² + 16\n= (4t² + 4)²\n= PQ²\nBoth PQ and PR are positive, so PR = PQ: P is equidistant from Q and R.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::12(b)": "Using the intended parametric equations x = 4t², y = 8t (the printed y = 4t is a misprint, as Fig. 12 shows P(4t², 8t)):\nFrom y = 8t, t = y/8.\nSubstitute into x = 4t²:\nx = 4(y/8)² = 4y²/64 = y²/16\nSo a cartesian equation of C is\ny² = 16x",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::13(a)": "Force diagram (described in words): draw the box with three forces acting on it, each with an arrow and a distinct label:\n• its weight 15g N acting vertically downwards;\n• the tension in the rope, T_R N, acting along the rope, upwards and away from the box at 30° to the vertical (towards the side the rope is attached);\n• the tension in the string, T_S N, acting horizontally, on the opposite side to the rope's horizontal lean, so that it balances the horizontal component of T_R.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::13(b)": "The box is in equilibrium. Resolve vertically (only the weight and the rope tension have vertical components):\nT_R cos30° = 15g\nT_R = (15 × 9.8)/cos30° = 147/(√3/2) = 294/√3 = 98√3\nT_R = 98√3 ≈ 170 N (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::13(c)": "Resolve horizontally:\nT_S = T_R sin30°\nT_S = 98√3 × ½ = 49√3 = 84.87…\n(Equivalently T_S = 15g tan30°.)\nThe tension in the string is 84.9 N (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::14(a)": "Area of sector OAB = ½r²x\nArea of triangle OAB = ½r² sin x\nArea of the shaded segment = sector − triangle = ½r²x − ½r² sin x = ½r²(x − sin x)\nArea of the circle = πr², so 5% of it is 0.05πr².\n½r²(x − sin x) = 0.05πr²\nDivide by ½r² (r ≠ 0):\nx − sin x = 2 × 0.05 × π = π/10\nx − sin x − (1/10)π = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::14(b)": "Let F(x) = x − sin x − π/10. Then F′(x) = 1 − cos x.\nThe Newton-Raphson iteration xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − F(xₙ)/F′(xₙ) is\nxₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (xₙ − sin xₙ − π/10)/(1 − cos xₙ)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::14(c)": "Using xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (xₙ − sin xₙ − π/10)/(1 − cos xₙ) with x in radians:\nx₀ = 1.2\nx₁ = 1.27245…\nx₂ = 1.26895…\nx₃ = 1.26894…\nx₂ and x₃ both round to 1.269, so the iteration has converged to 3 decimal places.\nx = 1.269 (3 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::15(a)": "dv/dt = 9.8 − kv\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ 1/(9.8 − kv) dv = ∫ 1 dt\n−(1/k) ln(9.8 − kv) = t + c\n(While the object starts from rest and speeds up towards 9.8/k, 9.8 − kv > 0, so no modulus is needed.)\nln(9.8 − kv) = −kt − kc\n9.8 − kv = Ae^(−kt), where A = e^(−kc)\nWhen t = 0, v = 0: 9.8 − 0 = Ae⁰, so A = 9.8.\n9.8 − kv = 9.8e^(−kt)\nkv = 9.8 − 9.8e^(−kt)\nv = (9.8/k)(1 − e^(−kt))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::15(b)": "Sketch (described in words): axes with t horizontal (t ≥ 0) and v vertical. The curve v = (9.8/k)(1 − e^(−kt)) starts at the origin O, rises steeply at first (gradient 9.8 at t = 0) and then increases ever more slowly, bending over (concave down) and levelling off. It approaches, but never reaches, the horizontal asymptote v = 9.8/k, drawn as a dashed horizontal line and labelled 9.8/k on the v-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::15(c)": "As t → ∞, e^(−kt) → 0 (since k > 0), so v → 9.8/k.\nThe velocity tends to 7 m s⁻¹, so\n9.8/k = 7\nk = 9.8/7 = 1.4",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::15(d)": "With k = 1.4, v = 7(1 − e^(−1.4t)).\nv = 3.5:\n7(1 − e^(−1.4t)) = 3.5\n1 − e^(−1.4t) = 0.5\ne^(−1.4t) = 0.5\n−1.4t = ln 0.5\nt = ln 2/1.4 = 0.495 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::16(a)": "Model A: the plane is smooth, so the only force with a component along the plane is the weight. Take down the plane as positive.\nNewton's second law down the plane:\n2g sin20° = 2a\na = g sin20° = 3.3517… m s⁻²\nUse s = ut + ½at² with s = 0.7, u = 1.4:\n0.7 = 1.4t + ½(g sin20°)t²\n(4.9 sin20°)t² + 1.4t − 0.7 = 0\n1.6759…t² + 1.4t − 0.7 = 0\nt = (−1.4 ± √(1.4² + 4 × 1.6759… × 0.7))/(2 × 1.6759…)\nt = 0.3518… or t = −1.187…\nTime must be positive, so the time taken is t = 0.352 s (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::16(b)": "Model A ignores friction (and air resistance). In reality friction acts up the plane, opposing the motion, so it would slow the particle down, making its acceleration smaller. The particle would then take longer to cover 0.7 m, so ignoring friction makes model A underestimate the time taken.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::16(c)": "Take down the plane as positive: s = 0.7, u = 1.4, t = 0.8.\ns = ut + ½at²\n0.7 = 1.4 × 0.8 + ½a × 0.8²\n0.7 = 1.12 + 0.32a\na = (0.7 − 1.12)/0.32 = −1.3125\nThe acceleration is −1.3125 m s⁻² (−1.31 m s⁻² to 3 s.f.), i.e. a deceleration of 1.3125 m s⁻² (directed up the plane).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p1::16(d)": "Let F N be the friction force (acting up the plane) and R N the normal reaction. Take down the plane as positive and use a = −1.3125 from (c).\nNewton's second law down the plane:\n2g sin20° − F = 2 × (−1.3125)\nF = 2g sin20° + 2.625 = 6.7036… + 2.625 = 9.3286… N\nResolve perpendicular to the plane (no acceleration in this direction):\nR = 2g cos20° = 18.417… N\nThe particle is sliding, so friction is limiting: F = μR.\nμ = F/R = 9.3286…/18.417… = 0.5064…\nThe coefficient of friction is 0.506 (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::1(a)": "The probabilities must add up to 1:\n0.2 + 0.1 + k + 2k + 4k = 1\n0.3 + 7k = 1\n7k = 0.7\nk = 0.1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::1(b)": "P(X = 4) = 2k = 2 × 0.1 = 0.2\nP(X ≠ 4) = 1 − P(X = 4) = 1 − 0.2\nP(X ≠ 4) = 0.8",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::2(a)": "Use the chain rule with u = x² + 5, so y = u¹² and du/dx = 2x.\ndy/dx = 12u¹¹ × du/dx = 12(x² + 5)¹¹ × 2x\ndy/dx = 24x(x² + 5)¹¹",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::2(b)": "From (a), d/dx[(x² + 5)¹²] = 24x(x² + 5)¹¹.\nNow 48x(x² + 5)¹¹ = 2 × 24x(x² + 5)¹¹, so\n∫ 48x(x² + 5)¹¹ dx = 2 ∫ 24x(x² + 5)¹¹ dx\n= 2(x² + 5)¹² + c",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::3(a)": "Use the class mid-points.\nMid-points: 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 5, 7\nFrequencies: 28, 36, 42, 31, 24, 12 (total 173 days)\nΣft = 0.5×28 + 1.5×36 + 2.5×42 + 3.5×31 + 5×24 + 7×12\n= 14 + 54 + 105 + 108.5 + 120 + 84 = 485.5\nEstimated mean = 485.5/173 = 2.806… ≈ 2.81 hours",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::3(b)": "The data are grouped, so the original raw times are not known. The calculation uses the mid-point of each class as if every day in that class took exactly that time, so the result is only an estimate.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::3(c)": "An upper bound for the mean comes from taking every time at the upper limit of its class.\nΣ(upper limit × f) = 1×28 + 2×36 + 3×42 + 4×31 + 6×24 + 8×12\n= 28 + 72 + 126 + 124 + 144 + 96 = 590\nUpper bound for the mean ≈ 590/173 = 3.41 hours (to 3 s.f.)\nSince 3.41 > 3 (equivalently, a total of more than 3 × 173 = 519 hours is needed and totals close to 590 are possible), yes: according to the data it is possible that the mean time per day is more than 3 hours.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::4(a)": "h = 0.5, ordinates at x = −1, −0.5, 0:\ny₀ = √(1 + (−1)³) = √0 = 0\ny₁ = √(1 + (−0.5)³) = √0.875 = √14/4 = 0.9354143…\ny₂ = √(1 + 0³) = 1\nTrapezium rule:\n∫₋₁⁰ √(1 + x³) dx ≈ (0.5/2)[y₀ + 2y₁ + y₂]\n= 0.25[0 + 2 × 0.9354143… + 1]\n= 0.25 × 2.8708287…\n= (2 + √14)/8\n= 0.717707 (to 6 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::4(b)": "It is an under-estimate.\nFor −1 ≤ x ≤ 0 the curve is concave (it bends downwards, as Fig. 4 shows), so the top edge of each trapezium, the chord joining two points on the curve, lies below the curve. Each trapezium therefore has less area than the region under the curve on that strip.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::5(a)": "Smallest value = 0, largest value = 5\nMid-range = (0 + 5)/2 = 2.5",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::5(b)": "The frequencies peak at a low value (1 visit) and tail off towards the higher numbers of visits, so the distribution has positive skew.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::5(c)": "The highest frequency is 38 students, for 1 visit.\nMode = 1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::6": "Integrate by parts with\nu = ln x, so du/dx = 1/x\ndv/dx = 32x⁻⁵, so v = 32x⁻⁴/(−4) = −8x⁻⁴\n∫ (32/x⁵) ln x dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx\n= −8x⁻⁴ ln x − ∫ (−8x⁻⁴)(1/x) dx\n= −8x⁻⁴ ln x + 8 ∫ x⁻⁵ dx\n= −8x⁻⁴ ln x + 8 × x⁻⁴/(−4) + c\n= −8x⁻⁴ ln x − 2x⁻⁴ + c\n(equivalently −(8 ln x + 2)/x⁴ + c)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::7(a)": "Area of sector = ½r²θ\n½r²θ = 36.288\nr²θ = 72.576\nθ = 72.576/r²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::7(b)": "The perimeter is the arc length plus two radii.\nArc length = rθ, so\nrθ + 2r = 24.48\nrθ = 24.48 − 2r\nθ = (24.48 − 2r)/r\nθ = 24.48/r − 2, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::7(c)": "Equate the two expressions for θ:\n72.576/r² = 24.48/r − 2\nMultiply by r²:\n72.576 = 24.48r − 2r²\n2r² − 24.48r + 72.576 = 0\nr² − 12.24r + 36.288 = 0\nr = [12.24 ± √(12.24² − 4 × 36.288)]/2\n= [12.24 ± √(149.8176 − 145.152)]/2\n= [12.24 ± √4.6656]/2\n= (12.24 ± 2.16)/2\nr = 7.2 or r = 5.04\nCheck: r = 7.2 gives θ = 1.4 and r = 5.04 gives θ = 24.48/5.04 − 2 = 2.857… (= 20/7); both angles are positive and less than 2π, so both values are possible.\nPossible values: r = 5.04 or r = 7.2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::8(a)": "Let W mean the team wins and L mean it does not win. They won in week 4.\nWeek 6 win can happen by:\nweek 5 W then week 6 W: 0.4 × 0.4 = 0.16\nweek 5 L then week 6 W: 0.6 × 0.2 = 0.12\nP(win in week 6) = 0.16 + 0.12 = 0.28",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::8(b)": "Number of tickets = 20 × 6 = 120, and Alf has one of them.\nFive tickets are drawn without replacement, and each of the 120 tickets is equally likely to be among the 5 drawn, so\nP(Alf wins a prize) = 5/120 = 1/24 ≈ 0.0417\n(Check: P(Alf not drawn) = (119/120)(118/119)(117/118)(116/117)(115/116) = 115/120, so P(prize) = 1 − 115/120 = 5/120.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::8(c)": "P(no prize in a given week) = 1 − 1/24 = 23/24, independently each week.\nAfter n weeks, P(at least one prize) = 1 − (23/24)ⁿ.\nRequire 1 − (23/24)ⁿ ≥ 0.95\n(23/24)ⁿ ≤ 0.05\nn ln(23/24) ≤ ln 0.05\nn ≥ ln 0.05/ln(23/24) (the inequality reverses because ln(23/24) < 0)\nn ≥ 70.389…\nCheck: n = 70 gives 1 − (23/24)⁷⁰ = 0.9492 < 0.95; n = 71 gives 1 − (23/24)⁷¹ = 0.9513 ≥ 0.95.\nSmallest number of weeks = 71",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::9(a)": "gf(x) = g(f(x)) = (2x + 3)² − 2(2x + 3) + 1\nSince g(t) = t² − 2t + 1 = (t − 1)²,\ngf(x) = (2x + 3 − 1)² = (2x + 2)² = 4(x + 1)²   (= 4x² + 8x + 4)\nDomain: x must be in the domain of f, so x < 0, and f(x) must be in the domain of g, so 2x + 3 > 1, i.e. x > −1.\ngf(x) = 4(x + 1)², with domain −1 < x < 0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::9(b)": "For −1 < x < 0, 0 < x + 1 < 1, so 0 < (x + 1)² < 1 and gf(x) = 4(x + 1)² is increasing on this interval.\nRange: 0 < gf(x) < 4",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::9(c)": "Let y = gf(x) = 4(x + 1)², with −1 < x < 0.\n(x + 1)² = y/4\nSince x + 1 > 0 on the domain, take the positive root:\nx + 1 = √(y/4)\nx = √(y/4) − 1\nSwapping the roles of x and y:\n(gf)⁻¹(x) = √(x/4) − 1   (= ½√x − 1)\nThe domain of the inverse is the range of gf:\n(gf)⁻¹(x) = √(x/4) − 1, for 0 < x < 4\n(Alternatively, (gf)⁻¹ = f⁻¹g⁻¹ with g⁻¹(x) = √x + 1 and f⁻¹(x) = ½(x − 3), giving ½(√x + 1 − 3) = ½√x − 1.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::10(a)": "Half the flights take less than 153 minutes, so 153 is the median. A Normal distribution is symmetrical, so its mean equals its median.\nMean = 153 minutes",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::10(b)": "Let X be the flight time in minutes, X ~ N(153, σ²).\nP(X > 183) = 0.05, so 183 is 1.645 standard deviations above the mean (from the table, the upper 5% point of N(0, 1) is 1.645).\n(183 − 153)/σ = 1.645\nσ = 30/1.645 = 18.237…\nσ = 18.2 (to 1 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::10(c)": "Observed proportion under 2 hours (120 minutes): 16/452 = 0.0354 (to 3 s.f.)\nModel: X ~ N(153, 18.2²)\nP(X < 120) = P(Z < (120 − 153)/18.2) = P(Z < −1.813) = 0.0349 (to 3 s.f.)\nBoth proportions are 0.035 to 2 s.f. (equivalently the model predicts 452 × 0.0349 ≈ 15.8 flights under 2 hours, compared with 16 observed), so the model is consistent with this data.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::10(d)": "Let μ be the population mean flight time, in minutes, from Magaluf to Liverpool.\nH₀: μ = 153\nH₁: μ < 153\nOne-tailed test at the 1% level.\nUnder H₀, using the model's standard deviation, X̄ ~ N(153, 18.2²/24).\nStandard error = 18.2/√24 = 3.715…\np-value = P(X̄ < 143.6) = P(Z < (143.6 − 153)/3.715…) = P(Z < −2.530) = 0.0057 (to 2 s.f.)\nSince 0.0057 < 0.01, the result is significant, so reject H₀.\n(Equivalently, the critical region is X̄ < 153 − 2.326 × 3.715… = 144.36, and 143.6 lies in it.)\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 1% level to suggest that the mean flight time from Magaluf to Liverpool is less than 153 minutes, the mean flight time from Liverpool to Magaluf.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::10(e)": "1. Reduce the mean: the sample of Magaluf to Liverpool flights has mean 143.6 minutes, well below 153, so use a smaller value of μ (for example estimate it by 143.6).\n2. Change the spread: increase the standard deviation (variance), or estimate σ afresh from Magaluf to Liverpool flight data, instead of keeping the Liverpool to Magaluf value of 18.2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::11(a)": "y = x² − 4x + x ln x\nDifferentiate x² − 4x to get 2x − 4.\nProduct rule on x ln x: d/dx(x ln x) = 1 × ln x + x × (1/x) = ln x + 1\ndy/dx = 2x − 4 + ln x + 1 = 2x − 3 + ln x\nAt a stationary point dy/dx = 0, so the x-coordinate satisfies\n2x − 3 + ln x = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::11(b)": "Rearrange 2x − 3 + ln x = 0 as 2x = 3 − ln x, giving the iteration\nxₙ₊₁ = (3 − ln xₙ)/2\nStart with x₀ = 2 (Fig. 11 shows the minimum between 1 and 2):\nx₁ = 1.153426\nx₂ = 1.428632\nx₃ = 1.321642\nx₄ = 1.360563\nx₅ = 1.346051\nx₆ = 1.351413\nx₇ = 1.349425\nx₈ = 1.350161\nx₉ = 1.349888\nx₁₀ = 1.349989\nx₁₁ = 1.349952\nx₁₂ = 1.349966\nx₁₃ = 1.349960\nx₁₄ = 1.349962\nThe iterates oscillate about the root and settle at 1.349962, so the root is 1.3500 to 4 d.p.\nCheck with a sign change for 2x − 3 + ln x: at x = 1.34995 it is −0.000032 (< 0) and at x = 1.35005 it is +0.000242 (> 0), so the root lies between 1.34995 and 1.35005.\nx = 1.3500 (to 4 d.p.)\n(Newton-Raphson, xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − (2xₙ − 3 + ln xₙ)/(2 + 1/xₙ), from x₀ = 1.5 gives 1.347951, 1.349961, 1.349962, the same root.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::12(a)": "Let n be the sample size and X the number of black panthers, X ~ B(n, 0.06) under H₀: p = 0.06. For a two-tailed test at 5%, each tail has 2.5%.\nTo be able to get evidence that the percentage is lower than 6%, the most extreme low result, X = 0, must be in the lower 2.5% tail:\nP(X = 0) = 0.94ⁿ < 0.025\nn ln 0.94 < ln 0.025\nn > ln 0.025/ln 0.94 = 59.6… (the inequality reverses because ln 0.94 < 0)\nCheck: 0.94⁵⁹ = 0.0260 > 0.025, 0.94⁶⁰ = 0.0244 < 0.025.\nMinimum sample size = 60\n(Note: a significant result in the upper tail alone would already be possible with a much smaller sample, since P(X = n) = 0.06ⁿ < 0.025 for n ≥ 2; 60 is the smallest sample for which both tails of the test contain a possible outcome.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::12(b)": "Let p be the probability that a jaguar chosen at random from this population is a black panther (has a black coat).\nH₀: p = 0.06\nH₁: p ≠ 0.06\nTwo-tailed test at the 5% level, so 2.5% in each tail.\nLet X be the number of black panthers among the 83 jaguars filmed. Under H₀, X ~ B(83, 0.06), with mean 83 × 0.06 = 4.98.\nThe observed value 10 is above the mean, so use the upper tail:\nP(X ≤ 9) = 0.9732\nP(X ≥ 10) = 1 − 0.9732 = 0.0268\nSince 0.0268 > 0.025, the result is not significant, so do not reject H₀.\n(Equivalently: P(X ≥ 11) = 0.0107 < 0.025 but P(X ≥ 10) = 0.0268 > 0.025, so the upper critical region is X ≥ 11; the lower critical region is X = 0, since P(X = 0) = 0.0059 < 0.025 but P(X ≤ 1) = 0.0371 > 0.025. The value 10 is not in the critical region.)\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the probability that a jaguar selected at random from this population is a black panther is not 0.06, that is, that the percentage of black panthers is not 6%.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::13(a)": "Common ratio r = 24/8 = 72/24 = 3",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::13(b)": "Day 5 term = ar⁴ = 8 × 3⁴ = 8 × 81 = 648 new cases",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::13(c)": "Sum of a geometric series with a = 8, r = 3:\nSₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) = 8(3ⁿ − 1)/(3 − 1)\nTotal cases from day 1 to day n = 4(3ⁿ − 1)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::13(d)": "The total number of cases cannot exceed the population of 185 207.\n4(3ⁿ − 1) = 185 207\n3ⁿ − 1 = 46 301.75\n3ⁿ = 46 302.75\nn = ln 46 302.75/ln 3 = 9.8 (to 1 d.p.)\nSo the total reaches the population part-way through day 10. Check: after 9 days the total is 4(3⁹ − 1) = 78 728 ≤ 185 207, but after 10 days it would be 4(3¹⁰ − 1) = 236 192 > 185 207.\nMaximum number of days for which the model could be viable = 9",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::13(e)": "It is unlikely that the model will be viable for 9 days. By then the model would have almost 80 000 people (over 40% of the population) catching flu, but in reality some of the population will be immune to the virus or will not be exposed to it, and medical intervention (or people moving in and out of the town) would also stop the number of new cases continuing to triple each day.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::14(a)": "The data were not available for all the countries, so the 3 European countries without a recorded crude death rate had to be left out.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::14(b)": "An outlier is a value more than 1.5 × IQR below Q₁ or above Q₃.\nEurope: IQR = 11.41 − 8.50 = 2.91\nLower limit = 8.50 − 1.5 × 2.91 = 4.135; minimum 6.28 > 4.135\nUpper limit = 11.41 + 1.5 × 2.91 = 15.775; maximum 14.46 < 15.775\nAfrica: IQR = 11.93 − 7.31 = 4.62\nLower limit = 7.31 − 1.5 × 4.62 = 0.38; minimum 3.58 > 0.38\nUpper limit = 11.93 + 1.5 × 4.62 = 18.86; maximum 14.89 < 18.86\nIn each data set the minimum and maximum both lie within the limits, so every value does, and there are no outliers in either data set.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::14(c)": "The crude death rate is the number of deaths per 1000 people per year.\nEstimated deaths = 6 411 776 × 3.58/1000 = 22 954.2…\nAbout 22 954 deaths in a year (about 23 000)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::14(d)": "A higher median age means the population contains a greater proportion of old people, and older people are more likely to die in any given year, so the number of deaths per 1000 people is expected to be higher.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::14(e)": "Type of relationship: in Africa there is a negative association between median age and crude death rate (rank correlation coefficient −0.281: countries with higher median ages tend to have lower crude death rates), whereas in Europe there is a positive association (rank correlation coefficient 0.335: countries with higher median ages tend to have higher crude death rates).\nStrength: both associations are weak, and the association appears slightly stronger in Europe, since |0.335| > |−0.281|.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p2::15": "The curve is symmetrical about x = 35, so μ = 35.\nThe points of inflection of a Normal curve are one standard deviation from the mean, so σ = 35 − 31 = 4.\nSo X ~ N(35, 4²).\nP(30 < X < 40) = P(−1.25 < Z < 1.25) = 0.788700… ≈ 0.7887\nLet Y be the number of the 50 readings that lie between 30 and 40. The readings are independent, so\nY ~ B(50, 0.7887)\nP(Y ≥ 45) = 1 − P(Y ≤ 44)\n= Σ (from r = 45 to 50) ⁵⁰Cᵣ (0.7887)ʳ (0.2113)⁵⁰⁻ʳ\n= 0.0322 (to 3 s.f.)\nSince 0.0322 < 0.05, the probability that at least 45 of the readings are between 30 and 40 is less than 0.05, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::1(a)": "Let y = 3x − 2.\nRearrange to make x the subject:\ny + 2 = 3x\nx = (y + 2)/3\nSwap the roles of x and y:\nf⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::1(b)": "Both graphs are straight lines.\n\ny = f(x) = 3x − 2: gradient 3, crosses the y-axis at (0, −2) and the x-axis at (2/3, 0).\ny = f⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3: gradient 1/3, crosses the y-axis at (0, 2/3) and the x-axis at (−2, 0).\n\nSketch: draw both lines on the same axes. The steep line y = 3x − 2 passes through (0, −2) and (2/3, 0); the shallow line y = (x + 2)/3 passes through (−2, 0) and (0, 2/3). The two lines are reflections of each other in the line y = x (which can be drawn dashed at 45°), and they cross on y = x at the point (1, 1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::1(c)": "The lines y = f(x) and y = f⁻¹(x) are reflections of each other in y = x, so they meet on y = x:\n3x − 2 = x ⇒ 2x = 2 ⇒ x = 1 (the point (1, 1)).\n\nCheck directly: f(x) > f⁻¹(x)\n3x − 2 > (x + 2)/3\n9x − 6 > x + 2\n8x > 8\nx > 1\n\nSo f(x) > f⁻¹(x) for x > 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::2(a)": "Complete the square:\nx² + 8x − 7 = (x + 4)² − 16 − 7 = (x + 4)² − 23\n\nSo y = x² + 8x − 7 is y = (x + 4)² − 23, which is y = x² with x replaced by x + 4 and then 23 subtracted.\n\nThe transformation is a translation by the vector\n( −4 )\n( −23 )\ni.e. 4 units in the negative x-direction and 23 units in the negative y-direction.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::2(b)": "y = x² has its turning point at (0, 0). Translating by the vector (−4, −23) moves it to\n\n(−4, −23)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::3(a)": "Write\n1/((x + 2)(x + 3)) = A/(x + 2) + B/(x + 3)\n\nMultiply through by (x + 2)(x + 3):\n1 = A(x + 3) + B(x + 2)\n\nPut x = −3: 1 = −B ⇒ B = −1\nPut x = −2: 1 = A ⇒ A = 1\n\nSo\n1/((x + 2)(x + 3)) = 1/(x + 2) − 1/(x + 3)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::3(b)": "Using part (a):\n∫ 1/((x + 2)(x + 3)) dx = ∫ (1/(x + 2) − 1/(x + 3)) dx\n= ln|x + 2| − ln|x + 3| + c\n= ln|(x + 2)/(x + 3)| + c\n\nSo the integral is ln(f(x)) + c with f(x) = |(x + 2)/(x + 3)|.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::4": "Rationalise each denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate surd, using (√b + √a)(√b − √a) = b − a.\n\n1/(√10 + √11) = (√11 − √10)/(11 − 10) = √11 − √10\n1/(√11 + √12) = (√12 − √11)/(12 − 11) = √12 − √11\n1/(√12 + √13) = (√13 − √12)/(13 − 12) = √13 − √12\n\nAdding, the middle terms cancel:\nLHS = (√11 − √10) + (√12 − √11) + (√13 − √12) = √13 − √10\n\nNow rationalise the right-hand side:\n3/(√10 + √13) = 3(√13 − √10)/((√13 + √10)(√13 − √10)) = 3(√13 − √10)/(13 − 10) = √13 − √10\n\nBoth sides equal √13 − √10, so\n1/(√10 + √11) + 1/(√11 + √12) + 1/(√12 + √13) = 3/(√10 + √13).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::5": "Proof by contradiction.\n\nSuppose there is a largest prime, p. Then the primes are exactly 2, 3, 5, …, p, a finite list.\n\nLet N = (2 × 3 × 5 × … × p) + 1, the product of all the primes up to and including p, plus 1.\n\nDividing N by any prime in the list leaves remainder 1, so N is not divisible by any of the primes 2, 3, 5, …, p.\n\nEvery whole number greater than 1 is either prime or has a prime factor. N has no prime factor in the list, and the list was assumed to contain every prime, so N must itself be prime. But N > p, so N is a prime larger than p.\n\nThis contradicts the assumption that p is the largest prime. Therefore there is no largest prime.\n\n(Note on the student's version: it never states the assumption being contradicted or reaches a contradiction, and \"the new number must be a new prime\" is not true in general without the assumption — for example 2 × 3 × 5 × 7 × 11 × 13 + 1 = 30031 = 59 × 509. The number is only forced to be prime because we are assuming the list contains every prime.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::6(a)": "The x-axis is a tangent and the centre is C(10, 4), so the radius is the distance from C to the x-axis, which is 4.\n\nEquation of the circle:\n(x − 10)² + (y − 4)² = 16",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::6(b)": "Substitute y = x into the equation of the circle to find where the line meets it:\n(x − 10)² + (x − 4)² = 16\nx² − 20x + 100 + x² − 8x + 16 = 16\n2x² − 28x + 116 = 16\n2x² − 28x + 100 = 0\nx² − 14x + 50 = 0\n\nDiscriminant: b² − 4ac = (−14)² − 4(1)(50) = 196 − 200 = −4\n\nSince the discriminant is negative, the equation has no real solutions, so the line y = x does not meet the circle at all. A tangent must touch the circle at one point, so y = x is not a tangent to the circle.\n\n(Equivalently, x² − 14x + 50 = (x − 7)² + 1 ≥ 1 > 0, so it is never zero.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::6(c)": "O is the origin and C is (10, 4). The midpoint of OC is ½(0 + 10, 0 + 4) = (5, 2), so its position vector is\n\n5i + 2j  (the column vector with entries 5 and 2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::7(a)": "Use ln(aⁿ) = n ln a:\nln 9 = ln 3² = 2 ln 3\nln 27 = ln 3³ = 3 ln 3\n\nSo\nln 3 × ln 9 × ln 27 = ln 3 × 2 ln 3 × 3 ln 3 = 6(ln 3)³",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::7(b)": "Since 3 > e and ln is an increasing function,\nln 3 > ln e = 1.\n\nSo (ln 3)³ > 1³ = 1, and therefore\n6(ln 3)³ > 6.\n\nBy part (a), ln 3 × ln 9 × ln 27 = 6(ln 3)³, so\nln 3 × ln 9 × ln 27 > 6.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::8(a)": "y = x³ ⇒ dy/dx = 3x²\nAt B(1, 1): gradient = 3(1)² = 3\n\nTangent at B:\ny − 1 = 3(x − 1)\ny = 3x − 2\n\nE is where the tangent meets the y-axis: x = 0 ⇒ y = −2, so E is (0, −2) and OE = 2.\nD is where the tangent meets the x-axis: y = 0 ⇒ 3x = 2 ⇒ x = 2/3, so D is (2/3, 0) and OD = 2/3.\n\nTriangle ODE has a right angle at O, so\nArea = ½ × OD × OE = ½ × 2/3 × 2 = 2/3",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::8(b)": "The region is bounded by the curve y = x³ (above), the tangent y = 3x − 2 (below) and the y-axis, and runs from x = 0 to the point of contact B at x = 1.\n\nOn 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 the curve lies on or above the tangent, since x³ − (3x − 2) = x³ − 3x + 2 = (x − 1)²(x + 2) ≥ 0 there.\n\nArea = ∫₀¹ (x³ − (3x − 2)) dx\n= ∫₀¹ (x³ − 3x + 2) dx\n= [x⁴/4 − 3x²/2 + 2x] from 0 to 1\n= (1/4 − 3/2 + 2) − 0\n= 3/4\n\n(Check: area under y = x³ from 0 to 1 is 1/4; add triangle ODE (2/3) and subtract triangle ABD = ½ × 1/3 × 1 = 1/6: 1/4 + 2/3 − 1/6 = 3/4.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::9": "The normal has gradient 2, so the tangent has gradient −1/2 (since 2 × (−1/2) = −1).\n\nDifferentiate xy + y² = 8 implicitly with respect to x (product rule on xy):\ny + x dy/dx + 2y dy/dx = 0\ndy/dx = −y/(x + 2y)\n\nSet dy/dx = −1/2:\n−y/(x + 2y) = −1/2\n2y = x + 2y\nx = 0\n\nSubstitute x = 0 into the equation of the curve:\n0 × y + y² = 8 ⇒ y² = 8 ⇒ y = √8 or y = −√8  (√8 = 2√2)\n\nAt both points x + 2y = ±2√8 ≠ 0, so the gradient is defined there.\n\nThe points are (0, √8) and (0, −√8), i.e. (0, 2√2) and (0, −2√2) — one on each branch of the curve.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::10": "f(x) = eˣ/(1 + eˣ)\n\nQuotient rule with u = eˣ, v = 1 + eˣ (so du/dx = eˣ, dv/dx = eˣ):\nf′(x) = ((1 + eˣ)eˣ − eˣ · eˣ)/(1 + eˣ)²\n= (eˣ + e²ˣ − e²ˣ)/(1 + eˣ)²\n= eˣ/(1 + eˣ)²\n\nFor every real x, eˣ > 0 and (1 + eˣ)² > 0, so f′(x) > 0.\n\nHence f′(x) > 0 for all x, so f(x) is an increasing function for all values of x.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::11": "Let u = 1 + √x. Then √x = u − 1 and x = (u − 1)².\ndu/dx = 1/(2√x) ⇒ dx = 2√x du = 2(u − 1) du\n\nSubstitute:\n∫ x/(1 + √x) dx = ∫ ((u − 1)²/u) × 2(u − 1) du\n= ∫ 2(u − 1)³/u du\n\nExpand the cubic: (u − 1)³ = u³ − 3u² + 3u − 1, so\n= ∫ 2(u³ − 3u² + 3u − 1)/u du\n= ∫ 2(u² − 3u + 3 − 1/u) du\n= 2u³/3 − 3u² + 6u − 2 ln|u| + c\n\nSubstitute back u = 1 + √x (which is always positive, so the modulus can be dropped):\n∫ x/(1 + √x) dx = (2/3)(1 + √x)³ − 3(1 + √x)² + 6(1 + √x) − 2 ln(1 + √x) + c",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::12": "In Fig. C2, the side edge of the tube is a straight line from the bottom, where y = 0 and x = r, to the top, where y = h and x = 0 (line 21). So the line passes through (r, 0) and (0, h).\n\nGradient = (h − 0)/(0 − r) = −h/r\n\nThe y-intercept is h, so\ny = −(h/r)x + h\n\nMultiply by r:\nry = −hx + hr\nry + hx = hr, as given in line 24.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::13(a)(i)": "In Fig. C3.2 the cross-section is a rectangle of length l and width 2x, with a semicircle of radius x at each end (lines 30–31 of the article). The two semicircles together make a full circle of radius x.\n\nArea = 2x × l + πx²\n\nFrom line 33, l = π(r − x), so\nArea = 2xπ(r − x) + πx²\n= 2πrx − 2πx² + πx²\n= 2πrx − πx²\n= πx(2r − x)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::13(a)(ii)": "From ry + hx = hr (line 24):\nhx = hr − ry ⇒ x = (hr − ry)/h\n\nThen\n2r − x = 2r − (hr − ry)/h = (2rh − hr + ry)/h = (hr + ry)/h\n\nSubstitute into the area from part (i):\nπx(2r − x) = π × ((hr − ry)/h) × ((hr + ry)/h)\n= (π/h²)(h²r² − r²y²)\n= (πr²/h²)(h² − y²), as given in line 37.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::13(b)(i)": "Fig. C3.1 is the cross-section at the bottom of the tube, where y = 0; it is a circle of radius r, with area πr².\n\nPut y = 0 in the formula:\n(πr²/h²)(h² − 0²) = (πr²/h²) × h² = πr²\n\nThis equals the area of the circle, so the formula is valid for Fig. C3.1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::13(b)(ii)": "Fig. C3.3 is the cross-section at the top of the tube, where y = h; it is a straight line (length πr), which has zero area.\n\nPut y = h in the formula:\n(πr²/h²)(h² − h²) = (πr²/h²) × 0 = 0\n\nThis equals the area of the line, so the formula is valid for Fig. C3.3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::14(a)": "As δy → 0 the sum over thin slices from y = 0 to y = h becomes an integral with respect to y:\n\nlim(δy→0) Σ from 0 to h of (h² − y²)δy = ∫₀ʰ (h² − y²) dy",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::14(b)": "From line 40, V = (πr²/h²) Σ from 0 to h of (h² − y²)δy. Taking the limit as δy → 0 and using part (a) (r and h are constants):\n\nV = (πr²/h²) ∫₀ʰ (h² − y²) dy\n= (πr²/h²) [h²y − y³/3] from 0 to h\n= (πr²/h²) ((h³ − h³/3) − 0)\n= (πr²/h²) × (2h³/3)\n= (2/3)πr²h, as given in line 41.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2019-p3::15": "In the model the top of the tube is a straight line of length πr (Fig. C3.3), so\nπr = 5.4 ⇒ r = 5.4/π ≈ 1.719 cm (1.72 cm)\n\nWith h = 12, the formula from line 41 gives\nV = (2/3)πr²h = (2/3)π(5.4/π)² × 12 = 8 × 5.4²/π = 233.28/π ≈ 74.3 cm³\n\n(Alternatively: for V = 75 and h = 12 the formula needs r² = 75 × 3/(2π × 12), r ≈ 1.727 cm, compared with 5.4/π ≈ 1.719 cm.)\n\nComment: the formula gives about 74.3 cm³, slightly less than the 75 cm³ the tube needs to hold, but only by about 0.7 cm³ (around 1%). Given that the measurements 5.4 cm and 12 cm are only approximate and the cross-section shape is a modelling approximation, the formula is reasonably accurate for this tube, although it slightly underestimates the volume.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::1": "(27/x⁹)^(2/3) = 27^(2/3) / (x⁹)^(2/3)\n27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3² = 9 and (x⁹)^(2/3) = x⁶\nSo (27/x⁹)^(2/3) = 9/x⁶\n\nThen (9/x⁶) × (x⁴/9) = x⁴/x⁶ = 1/x²\n\nAnswer: 1/x² (equivalently x⁻²)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::2": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by (3 + √2):\n\n(a + √2)/(3 − √2) = (a + √2)(3 + √2) / ((3 − √2)(3 + √2))\n\nDenominator: (3 − √2)(3 + √2) = 9 − 2 = 7\nNumerator: (a + √2)(3 + √2) = 3a + a√2 + 3√2 + 2\n\nSo (a + √2)/(3 − √2) = (3a + a√2 + 3√2 + 2)/7\n= (3a + 2)/7 + ((a + 3)/7)√2\n\nAnswer: p = (3a + 2)/7, q = (a + 3)/7",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::3": "The vector from A to B is\nb − a = (−1 − 3, 4 − 2, 8 − (−1)) = (−4, 2, 9)   (column vector)\n\nThe distance AB is the magnitude of this vector:\n|b − a| = √((−4)² + 2² + 9²)\n= √(16 + 4 + 81)\n= √101\n\nSo the exact distance AB is √101, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::4": "Let y = (x² + 5)⁴.\n\nFirst derivative (chain rule):\ndy/dx = 4(x² + 5)³ × 2x = 8x(x² + 5)³\n\nSecond derivative (product rule with u = 8x and v = (x² + 5)³):\ndu/dx = 8\ndv/dx = 3(x² + 5)² × 2x = 6x(x² + 5)²\n\nd²y/dx² = 8(x² + 5)³ + 8x × 6x(x² + 5)²\n= 8(x² + 5)³ + 48x²(x² + 5)²\n\nTake out the common factor 8(x² + 5)²:\nd²y/dx² = 8(x² + 5)²[(x² + 5) + 6x²]\n= 8(x² + 5)²(7x² + 5)\n\nAnswer: d²y/dx² = 8(x² + 5)²(7x² + 5)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::5(a)": "Take t in seconds on the horizontal axis and v in m s⁻¹ on the vertical axis, with north positive.\n\nThe phases end at t = 5, t = 5 + 8 = 13 and t = 13 + 7 = 20.\n\nSketch description:\n• a horizontal line at v = 4 from t = 0 to t = 5 (running north);\n• a horizontal line along v = 0 (on the t-axis) from t = 5 to t = 13 (waiting);\n• a horizontal line at v = −3.5 from t = 13 to t = 20 (running south, so the velocity is negative).\n\nLabel the velocities 4 and −3.5 on the v-axis and the times 5, 13 and 20 on the t-axis. The changes in velocity are instantaneous in this model, so any lines joining the segments are vertical.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::5(b)": "Distance travelled = total area between the graph and the t-axis (counting all areas as positive).\n\nNorth phase: 4 × 5 = 20 m\nWaiting phase: 0 m\nSouth phase: 3.5 × 7 = 24.5 m\n\nTotal distance = 20 + 24.5 = 44.5 m",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::5(c)": "Displacement = signed area under the velocity-time graph, with north positive:\n\nDisplacement = 20 + 0 − 24.5 = −4.5 m\n\nSo his final displacement is −4.5 m, i.e. 4.5 m south of his original position.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::6(a)": "The ruler is 30 cm long and overhangs C by CB = 25 cm, so AC = 30 − 25 = 5 cm.\nThe ruler is uniform, so its weight acts at its midpoint, 15 cm from A, which is 15 − 5 = 10 cm from C (on the overhanging side).\n\nForce diagram (described in words), with the ruler drawn horizontally from A (left) to B (right):\n• F N acting vertically downwards at A;\n• R N, the contact force from the table, acting vertically upwards at C (already drawn in the answer booklet);\n• the weight W N = 0.028g N acting vertically downwards at the midpoint of AB.\n\nDistances to label: AC = 5 cm, and the weight 10 cm from C (so it acts at the centre of the ruler, 15 cm from each end).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::6(b)": "Mass of ruler = 28 g = 0.028 kg, so its weight is 0.028g N.\n\nTake moments about C (the reaction R at C then has no moment).\nClockwise (weight, 10 cm = 0.1 m from C on the B side): 0.028g × 0.1\nAnticlockwise (F at A, 5 cm = 0.05 m from C): F × 0.05\n\nEquilibrium: 0.05F = 0.1 × 0.028g\nF = 2 × 0.028g = 0.056g = 7g/125\nF = 0.056 × 9.8 = 0.5488\n\nF = 0.549 N (3 s.f.)\n\n(Check: resolving vertically, R = F + 0.028g = 0.084g = 0.8232 N.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::7(a)": "By the factor theorem, (x + 2) is a factor of f(x) if and only if f(−2) = 0.\n\nf(−2) = (−2)³ + (−2)² − 8(−2) − 12\n= −8 + 4 + 16 − 12\n= 0\n\nSince f(−2) = 0, by the factor theorem (x + 2) is a factor of f(x).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::7(b)": "From (a), (x + 2) is a factor, so write\nx³ + x² − 8x − 12 = (x + 2)(x² + px + q).\n\nComparing coefficients:\nx² terms: p + 2 = 1, so p = −1\nconstant terms: 2q = −12, so q = −6\n(check x terms: q + 2p = −6 − 2 = −8 ✓)\n\nSo f(x) = (x + 2)(x² − x − 6).\n\nFactorise the quadratic: x² − x − 6 = (x + 2)(x − 3).\n\nHence f(x) = (x + 2)²(x − 3).\n\nf(x) = 0 ⇒ (x + 2)²(x − 3) = 0\n⇒ x = −2 (a repeated root) or x = 3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::8(a)": "Heights h = 0.2/(1 + x²) (the curve is symmetric about x = 0):\nx = 0: h = 0.2\nx = ±0.5: h = 0.2/1.25 = 0.16\nx = ±1: h = 0.2/2 = 0.1\nx = ±1.5: h = 0.2/3.25 = 0.061538…\nx = ±2: h = 0.2/5 = 0.04\n\nh decreases as |x| increases, so on each strip of width 0.5 the greatest height is at the end nearer x = 0. Using rectangles with these heights gives an upper bound.\n\nFor 0 ≤ x ≤ 2 the rectangle heights are h(0), h(0.5), h(1), h(1.5); by symmetry the region −2 ≤ x ≤ 0 gives the same total.\n\nUpper bound = 2 × 0.5 × (0.2 + 0.16 + 0.1 + 0.061538…)\n= 1 × 0.521538…\n= 0.5215…\n\nUpper bound for the area = 0.522 m² (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::8(b)": "4 strips on [0, 2], so the strip width is 0.5 and the ordinates are at x = 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2:\ny₀ = 0.2, y₁ = 0.16, y₂ = 0.1, y₃ = 0.061538…, y₄ = 0.04\n\nTrapezium rule:\n∫₀² 0.2/(1 + x²) dx ≈ ½ × 0.5 × [(y₀ + y₄) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + y₃)]\n= 0.25 × [(0.2 + 0.04) + 2(0.16 + 0.1 + 0.061538…)]\n= 0.25 × [0.24 + 0.643077…]\n= 0.25 × 0.883077…\n= 0.220769…\n\n∫₀² 0.2/(1 + x²) dx ≈ 0.221 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::8(c)": "The cross-section is symmetric about x = 0, so its area is twice the integral in (b):\nArea ≈ 2 × 0.220769… = 0.441538… m²\n\nVolume = cross-sectional area × length\n≈ 0.441538… × 10\n= 4.415…\n\nVolume of tarmac ≈ 4.42 m³ (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::9": "Integrate the acceleration to find the velocity v m s⁻¹:\nv = ∫(0.8t + 0.5) dt = 0.4t² + 0.5t + c\n\nInitial condition: v = 3 when t = 0:\n3 = 0.4 × 0² + 0.5 × 0 + c, so c = 3\n\nv = 0.4t² + 0.5t + 3\n\nThe particle is stationary when v = 0:\n0.4t² + 0.5t + 3 = 0\n\nDiscriminant of the quadratic 0.4t² + 0.5t + 3:\n0.5² − 4 × 0.4 × 3 = 0.25 − 4.8 = −4.55 < 0\n\nThe discriminant is negative, so the equation has no real roots: v is never zero.\n\nSo the velocity is never zero and the particle is never stationary.\n\n(Alternative reasoning: for t ≥ 0 each term of 0.4t² + 0.5t + 3 is non-negative and the constant term is 3, so v ≥ 3 > 0 for all t ≥ 0.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::10(a)": "x = t⁻², y = t⁻³ − t⁻¹\n\ndx/dt = −2t⁻³\ndy/dt = −3t⁻⁴ + t⁻²\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt) = (−3t⁻⁴ + t⁻²)/(−2t⁻³)\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by t⁴:\ndy/dx = (−3 + t²)/(−2t)\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by −1:\ndy/dx = (3 − t²)/(2t), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::10(b)": "The line 4y + x = 1 can be written y = −¼x + ¼, so its gradient is −¼.\nThe tangent is parallel to this line when dy/dx = −¼:\n\n(3 − t²)/(2t) = −¼\n4(3 − t²) = −2t\n12 − 4t² = −2t\n4t² − 2t − 12 = 0\n2t² − t − 6 = 0\n(2t + 3)(t − 2) = 0\nt = 2 or t = −3/2\n\nSince t > 0, t = 2.\n\nWhen t = 2:\nx = 1/2² = ¼\ny = 1/2³ − 1/2 = 1/8 − 4/8 = −3/8\n\nThe point is (¼, −3/8).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::10(c)": "From x = 1/t² with t > 0: t² = 1/x, so t = x^(−½) (taking the positive root because t > 0), i.e. 1/t = √x.\n\nSubstitute into y = 1/t³ − 1/t = (1/t)³ − (1/t):\ny = (√x)³ − √x\n= x^(3/2) − x^(1/2)\n\nFactorise:\ny = x^(1/2)(x − 1)\n\nCartesian equation: y = (x − 1)√x   (for x > 0)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::11(a)": "Force diagram (described in words).\n\nBlock (on the table):\n• its weight 2g N acting vertically downwards;\n• the normal reaction R N from the table acting vertically upwards;\n• the tension T N in the string acting horizontally towards the pulley;\n• the friction force F N acting horizontally away from the pulley (opposing the motion).\n\nSphere (hanging below the pulley):\n• its weight 0.8g N acting vertically downwards;\n• the tension T N acting vertically upwards.\n\nThe string is light and inextensible and the pulley is smooth, so the tension T is the same throughout the string. No other forces act.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::11(b)": "Let a m s⁻² be the common acceleration: the block moves towards the pulley and the sphere moves down.\n\nBlock (horizontally, positive towards the pulley), Newton's second law:\nT − F = 2a\n\nSphere (vertically, positive downwards), Newton's second law:\n0.8g − T = 0.8a\n\n(Here F is the friction on the block; from (c), F = 0.35 × 2g = 0.7g = 6.86 N.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::11(c)": "Resolve vertically for the block (no vertical acceleration):\nR = 2g\n\nThe block slides, so friction takes its limiting value:\nF = 0.35R = 0.35 × 2g = 0.7g (= 6.86 N)\n(The pull of the sphere, 0.8g = 7.84 N, exceeds this maximum friction, so the system does move.)\n\nAdd the two equations of motion from (b) to eliminate T:\n(T − F) + (0.8g − T) = 2a + 0.8a\n0.8g − F = 2.8a\n0.8g − 0.7g = 2.8a\n0.1g = 2.8a\na = 0.98/2.8 = 0.35\n\nSo the acceleration of the system is 0.35 m s⁻², as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::11(d)": "Starting from rest with constant acceleration: s = 0.5, u = 0, a = 0.35.\n\ns = ut + ½at²\n0.5 = 0 + ½ × 0.35 × t²\nt² = 0.5/0.175 = 2.857…\nt = 1.690… (taking the positive root, since t > 0)\n\nTime = 1.69 s (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::12(a)": "f(x + h) − f(x) = [(x + h)³ − (x + h)] − [x³ − x]\n\nExpand: (x + h)³ = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³\n\nSo f(x + h) − f(x) = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³ − x − h − x³ + x\n= 3x²h + 3xh² + h³ − h\n\nDivide by h (h ≠ 0):\n(f(x + h) − f(x))/h = 3x² + 3xh + h² − 1\n\nBy definition,\nf′(x) = lim (h→0) (f(x + h) − f(x))/h\n= lim (h→0) (3x² − 1 + 3xh + h²)\n\nAs h → 0, 3xh → 0 and h² → 0, so\nf′(x) = 3x² − 1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::12(b)": "f′(x) = 3x² − 1 is a quadratic with positive x² coefficient, so its graph is a U-shaped parabola.\n\nKey features:\n• symmetric about the y-axis, with its vertex (minimum point) at (0, −1) on the negative y-axis;\n• crosses the x-axis where 3x² − 1 = 0, i.e. x = ±1/√3 (≈ ±0.577), so at (−1/√3, 0) and (1/√3, 0), between x = −1 and x = 1.\n\nSketch description: a U-shaped curve with its lowest point at (0, −1), cutting the x-axis at x = −1/√3 and x = 1/√3, and rising steeply on both sides. Label (0, −1) and the two x-intercepts.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::12(c)": "f′(x) = 3x² − 1, so f″(x) = 6x.\n\nAt a point of inflection f″(x) = 0:\n6x = 0 has the single root x = 0.\nAlso f″(x) < 0 for x < 0 and f″(x) > 0 for x > 0, so f″ changes sign at x = 0 and there is a point of inflection there, at (0, f(0)) = (0, 0).\nSince x = 0 is the only root of f″(x) = 0, this is the only point of inflection.\n\nAt x = 0: f′(0) = 3(0)² − 1 = −1 ≠ 0.\nThe gradient there is not zero, so the point of inflection is not a stationary point.\n\n(The stationary points of y = f(x) are where 3x² − 1 = 0, i.e. at x = ±1/√3, not at x = 0.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::13(a)": "For example: air resistance is negligible (so the only force on the projectile is its weight).\n\n(Other acceptable assumptions: the acceleration due to gravity is constant; the projectile is modelled as a particle.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::13(b)": "Take the origin at the point of projection, x horizontal and y vertically upwards, with g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nHorizontal motion (no acceleration): the horizontal component of the initial velocity is 35 cos θ, so\nx = (35 cos θ)t ⇒ t = x/(35 cos θ)\n\nVertical motion (acceleration −g): the vertical component of the initial velocity is 35 sin θ, so\ny = (35 sin θ)t − ½gt²\n\nSubstitute for t:\ny = (35 sin θ) × x/(35 cos θ) − ½ × 9.8 × x²/(35² cos² θ)\n= x tan θ − 4.9x²/(1225 cos² θ)\n\nSince 4.9/1225 = 1/250:\n\ny = x tan θ − x²/(250 cos² θ)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::13(c)": "Find θ from the maximum height. At the highest point the vertical velocity is zero.\nVertically: u = 35 sin θ, v = 0, a = −9.8, s = 22.5\nv² = u² + 2as\n0 = (35 sin θ)² − 2 × 9.8 × 22.5\n(35 sin θ)² = 441\n35 sin θ = 21 (positive, since the projectile is fired upwards)\nsin θ = 0.6, so θ = 36.9° (3 s.f.)\n\nThen cos θ = 0.8 and tan θ = 0.75 exactly.\n\nUse the trajectory from (b) at the wall, x = 110:\ny = 110 × 0.75 − 110²/(250 × 0.8²)\n= 82.5 − 12100/160\n= 82.5 − 75.625\n= 6.875\n\nAt x = 110 m the projectile is 6.875 m above the ground (still in flight, since y > 0), which is higher than the 5 m wall.\n\nSo the projectile passes over the wall and does not hit it.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(a)": "cos t takes values between −1 and 1.\n\nMaximum: cos t = 1 gives h = 5.15 + 3.4 × 1 = 8.55\nMinimum: cos t = −1 gives h = 5.15 − 3.4 × 1 = 1.75\n\nThese are the heights of the high tide (8.55 m) and the low tide (1.75 m), so the equation gives the correct values.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(b)(i)": "The first high tide is at 1 am, i.e. t = 1, when h must take its maximum value 8.55.\n\nIf a + b = 0, then at t = 1:\nh = 5.15 + 3.4 cos(a × 1 + b) = 5.15 + 3.4 cos 0 = 5.15 + 3.4 × 1 = 8.55\n\ncos(at + b) = 1 is the largest possible value of the cosine, so h = 8.55 is the maximum height: the model gives the high tide of 8.55 m at t = 1, i.e. at 1 am, the correct time.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(b)(ii)": "The first low tide is at 7.20 am, i.e. t = 7 h 20 min = 7⅓ = 22/3 hours after midnight.\n\nLow tide is the minimum of h, when cos(at + b) = −1. The angle at + b is 0° at the high tide (t = 1) and the next minimum of the cosine after that occurs when the angle is 180°.\n\nSo at t = 22/3: a × (22/3) + b = 180\n\n(22/3)a + b = 180",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(b)(iii)": "Solve simultaneously:\n(1) a + b = 0\n(2) (22/3)a + b = 180\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n(22/3 − 1)a = 180\n(19/3)a = 180\na = 540/19 = 28.421…\n\nSo a = 28.42 correct to 2 decimal places, as required.\n(Then b = −a = −28.42 to 2 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(c)": "With b = −a, the model is h = 5.15 + 3.4 cos(a(t − 1)), where a = 540/19 = 28.42…\n\nHe cannot sail when h < 3. The boundary times satisfy\n3 = 5.15 + 3.4 cos(a(t − 1))\ncos(a(t − 1)) = −2.15/3.4 = −43/68\n\ncos⁻¹(−43/68) = 129.22…°, and the other solution in 0° to 360° is 360° − 129.22…° = 230.78…°\n(Between these angles cos(a(t − 1)) < −43/68, so h < 3; the low tide, at angle 180°, lies in this range.)\n\na(t − 1) = 129.22…° ⇒ t = 1 + 129.22…/28.42… = 5.547 (3 d.p.)\na(t − 1) = 230.78…° ⇒ t = 1 + 230.78…/28.42… = 9.120 (3 d.p.)\n\n0.547 h ≈ 33 minutes and 0.120 h ≈ 7 minutes.\n\nSo he cannot sail between about 5.33 am and 9.07 am.\n\n(If a is rounded to 28.4, the times come out as t = 5.55 and t = 9.13, i.e. about 5.33 am to 9.08 am; both are acceptable predictions from the model.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::14(d)": "In the model h = 5.15 + 3.4 cos(at + b), the greatest possible value of h is 5.15 + 3.4 = 8.55, so the model predicts that every high tide is 8.55 m.\n\nThe actual next high tide is 8.91 m, which is higher than the model can ever give (it would need cos(at + b) = (8.91 − 5.15)/3.4 ≈ 1.106, which is impossible).\n\nSo the model is not perfect: it is reasonable for the first high and low tides that were used to build it, but it underestimates the height of the next high tide.\n\n(The timing is also off: the model's period is 360/a = 12⅔ hours, so it predicts the next high tide at about 1.40 pm rather than 12.59 pm.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::15(a)": "The weight has magnitude mg and acts vertically downwards.\n\nThe plane is inclined at 30° to the horizontal, so the angle between the weight and the downward normal to the plane is 30°.\n\nComponent along the plane: mg sin 30° down the slope, i.e. in the −i direction.\nComponent perpendicular to the plane: mg cos 30° into the plane, i.e. in the −j direction.\n\nW = (−mg sin 30°)i + (−mg cos 30°)j\nW = −½mg i − (√3/2)mg j",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::15(b)": "The particle is in equilibrium, so the resultant force on it is zero:\n\nW + R + F = 0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p1::15(c)": "The plane is smooth, so the normal reaction has no component along the plane. Write the vector R = r j, where r = |R| N.\n\nSubstitute into W + R + F = 0:\n(−½mg i − (√3/2)mg j) + r j + (6i + 8j) = 0\n\ni components: −½mg + 6 = 0\nmg = 12\nm = 12/9.8 = 1.2244…\nm = 1.22 (3 s.f.), as required.\n\nj components: −(√3/2)mg + r + 8 = 0\nUsing mg = 12:\nr = 12 cos 30° − 8 = 6√3 − 8 = 2.392…\n\nThe magnitude of R is 2.39 N (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::1": "Use area = ½ab sin C with the two given sides and the angle between them (angle B = 133° lies between AB and BC).\n\nArea = ½ × AB × BC × sin 133°\n= ½ × 18.0 × 22.1 × sin 133°\n= 198.9 × 0.73135…\n= 145.466…\n\nArea ≈ 145 m² (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::2(a)": "Arc length = rθ (θ in radians)\n\nL = 8 × 2.1 = 16.8\n\nL = 16.8 cm",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::2(b)": "Sector area = ½r²θ (θ in radians)\n\nArea = ½ × 8² × 2.1\n= ½ × 64 × 2.1\n= 67.2\n\nArea = 67.2 cm²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::3(a)": "y = 4x + sin 8x\n\nd/dx(4x) = 4\nd/dx(sin 8x) = 8cos 8x (chain rule)\n\ndy/dx = 4 + 8cos 8x",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::3(b)": "Set dy/dx = 0:\n4 + 8cos 8x = 0\ncos 8x = −½\n\nFor the smallest positive x we need the smallest positive value of 8x with cosine −½.\ncos θ = −½ first occurs (for θ > 0) at θ = π − π/3 = 2π/3.\n\nSo 8x = 2π/3\nx = 2π/24 = π/12\n\nSmallest positive value: x = π/12",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::4(a)": "The final plotted point is at about 198, so there are about 198 students.\nThe median is the value at a cumulative frequency of about ½ × 198 ≈ 99.\n\nReading across from 99 on the cumulative frequency axis to the curve and down to the time axis: the curve passes 85 at 20 minutes and 125 at 40 minutes, so 99 is reached at roughly 27 minutes.\n\nMedian ≈ 27 minutes (any reading from about 23 to 29 minutes is a reasonable estimate from the graph).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::4(b)": "Total number of students ≈ 198.\n\nReading up from 60 minutes: cumulative frequency ≈ 159.\nProportion who revised for less than an hour ≈ 159/198 ≈ 0.80, i.e. about 80%.\n\n90% of 198 ≈ 178 students, and the curve does not reach a cumulative frequency of about 178 until roughly 75–80 minutes.\n\nSo the diagram does not support the comment: only about 80% (not 90%) of the students spent less than an hour revising mathematics.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::5(a)": "First term a = 17, common difference d = 28 − 17 = 11, last term 292.\n\nnth term: a + (n − 1)d = 292\n17 + 11(n − 1) = 292\n11(n − 1) = 275\nn − 1 = 25\n\nn = 26",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::5(b)": "Sₙ = ½n(a + l), with n = 26, a = 17, l = 292.\n\nS₂₆ = ½ × 26 × (17 + 292)\n= 13 × 309\n= 4017\n\nSum = 4017",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::6(a)": "Use (1 + z)ⁿ = 1 + nz + n(n − 1)z²/2! + … with n = ½ and z = 4x.\n\n(1 + 4x)^(½) = 1 + (½)(4x) + (½)(−½)(4x)²/2! + …\n= 1 + 2x + (−¼)(16x²)/2 + …\n= 1 + 2x − 2x² + …\n\nFirst three terms: 1 + 2x − 2x²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::6(b)": "The expansion of (1 + z)^(½) is valid for |z| < 1.\n\nHere z = 4x, so |4x| < 1.\n\nValid for |x| < ¼, i.e. −¼ < x < ¼.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::7(a)": "P(A ∪ B) = 1 − P((A ∪ B)′) = 1 − 0.2 = 0.8\n\nUsing P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B):\n0.8 = 0.6 + 0.5 − P(A ∩ B)\nP(A ∩ B) = 1.1 − 0.8\n\nP(A ∩ B) = 0.3",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::7(b)": "P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B)\n= 0.3/0.5\n= 0.6\n\nP(A | B) = 0.6",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::7(c)": "P(A | B) = 0.6 and P(A) = 0.6, so P(A | B) = P(A).\n\nEquivalently, P(A) × P(B) = 0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3 = P(A ∩ B).\n\nSo A and B are independent.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(a)": "Rosella fixes in advance how many people she takes from each category (25 retired men and 25 retired women) and fills those quotas.\n\nThis is quota sampling.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(b)": "In a histogram, frequency = frequency density × class width.\n\nThe 50–55 class has width 5 and frequency density 1.8.\nFrequency = 5 × 1.8 = 9\n\n9 people in the sample are aged between 50 and 55.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(c)": "Systematic sampling\n4960 ÷ 200 = 24.8, so take every 24th name on the list.\nChoose the starting position at random from 1 to 184 (e.g. using a random number generator), then take that name and every 24th name after it.\nSince 184 + 24 × 199 = 4960, every start from 1 to 184 gives exactly 200 names within the list. Every name could be selected: a name in position k ≤ 184 is chosen when the start is k, and a name in position k > 184 is chosen when the start is any number from 1 to 184 that differs from k by a multiple of 24 and is at least k − 4776 (such a number always exists).\n(Alternatively: start at a randomly chosen name anywhere on the list, take every 24th name, cycling back to the start of the list when the end is reached, until 200 names have been chosen.)\n\nSimple random sampling\nNumber every person on the list with a unique number from 1 to 4960.\nUse a random number generator to generate random integers from 1 to 4960, ignoring any repeats, until 200 different numbers have been obtained.\nThe sample consists of the 200 people with those numbers.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(d)": "All three histograms (samples of 50, 200 and 500) are roughly symmetrical and unimodal, with a single peak near 60 and frequencies tailing off on both sides.\n\nAs the sample size increases from 50 to 200 to 500, the shape becomes more and more like the bell shape of a Normal distribution.\n\nSo it appears reasonable to model the age of retirement using a Normal distribution.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(e)": "Let X be the age (in years) at which a person in the city retires.\nModel X ~ N(60.0515, 6.5783²), using the sample mean and the sample standard deviation s from Fig. 8.4.\n\nP(X > 65) = P(Z > (65 − 60.0515)/6.5783)\n= P(Z > 0.7522…)\n= 0.2260 (4 d.p.)\n\n(Age is continuous, so no continuity correction is used.)\n\nEstimated number = 4960 × 0.22595…\n= 1120.7…\n\nAbout 1121 of the people who retired in the city in the previous month were aged over 65.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::8(f)": "The model is based on people who retired in one particular month. The pattern of retirement ages may vary from month to month – for example there may be seasonal effects, such as teachers tending to retire at the end of the academic year (in August) – so the ages of people retiring in the following month may not follow the same distribution.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::9(a)": "Treat each business the company supplies as a cluster.\nRandomly select 20 of the businesses (e.g. number them and use random numbers), then randomly select 6 computers from each of these businesses.\nThis gives 20 × 6 = 120 computers.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::9(b)": "Let μ be the population mean time (in years) for which businesses keep computers before replacing them.\n\nH₀: μ = 5\nH₁: μ ≠ 5\n\nH₁ is two-sided because Claud thinks the mean time is now different from 5 years; he is not predicting whether it has increased or decreased, so a change in either direction counts.\n\nTest at the 5% level, so 2.5% in each tail.\n\nSample: n = 120, x̄ = 4.8855, s = 2.7054.\nThe sample is large, so use s to estimate the population standard deviation. Under H₀ the sample mean X̄ is approximately distributed as\nX̄ ~ N(5, 2.7054²/120)\nStandard deviation of X̄ = 2.7054/√120 = 0.24697…\n\nP(X̄ ≤ 4.8855) = P(Z ≤ (4.8855 − 5)/0.24697…)\n= P(Z ≤ −0.4636…)\n= 0.3215 (4 d.p.)\n\nSince x̄ is below 5, compare this lower-tail probability with 0.025:\n0.3215 > 0.025\n\n(Equivalently, the critical region is X̄ < 5 − 1.96 × 0.24697 = 4.516 or X̄ > 5.484, and 4.8855 is not in it.)\n\nThe result is not significant, so do not reject H₀.\n\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the population mean time for which businesses keep computers is not 5 years.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::10(a)": "For small x (in radians), sin x ≈ x and sin 2x ≈ 2x. On 0.01 ≤ x ≤ 0.05 the angles x and 2x are small.\n\ny = (sin 2x − x)/(x sin x)\n≈ (2x − x)/(x × x)\n= x/x²\n= 1/x\n\nSo\n∫ from 0.01 to 0.05 of y dx ≈ ∫ from 0.01 to 0.05 of (1/x) dx\n= [ln x] from 0.01 to 0.05\n= ln 0.05 − ln 0.01\n= ln(0.05/0.01)\n= ln 5\n\nHence ∫ from 0.01 to 0.05 of y dx ≈ ln 5, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::10(b)": "From part (a), for small x, y ≈ 1/x = x⁻¹.\n\ndy/dx ≈ −x⁻² = −1/x²\n\nAt x = 0.01:\ndy/dx ≈ −1/(0.01)² = −1/0.0001 = −10 000\n\nSo dy/dx ≈ −10 000 at the point where x = 0.01, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::10(c)": "4.54066E−06 means 4.54066 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.00000454066.\n\nRoot:\nFrom Fig. 10.2, y(0.947745) = 4.54066 × 10⁻⁶ > 0 and y(0.947755) = −1.67417 × 10⁻⁵ < 0.\ny is continuous on this interval, so the sign change shows there is a root between 0.947745 and 0.947755. Every number in this interval rounds to 0.94775, so the root is 0.94775 correct to 5 decimal places.\n(Also, y(0.9477475) is negative, so in fact the root lies between 0.947745 and 0.9477475.)\n\nThe last two iterates, 0.947753 and 0.947748, both round to 0.94775, which supports this.\n\nThe root cannot be given to 6 decimal places: y(0.9477475) and y(0.9477485) are both negative, so there is no sign change to confirm the 6 d.p. value 0.947748 suggested by the last iterate (indeed, since y(0.9477475) < 0 and y(0.947745) > 0, the root is below 0.9477475, so it is not 0.947748 to 6 d.p.), and the information does not fix the 6th decimal place.\n\nRoot = 0.94775 (to 5 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(a)": "In the large data set, median house prices in London boroughs are generally higher than those in other parts of the country (as Fig. 11.1 also shows, with Barking and Dagenham above North West in every year from 2004 to 2015).\n\nSo it is not unusual for a London borough to have consistently higher median house prices; Dr Procter's suggestion is probably wrong.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(b)": "P = 4897Y − 9 657 847\n\n2016: P = 4897 × 2016 − 9 657 847 = 9 872 352 − 9 657 847 = 214 505\n2017: P = 4897 × 2017 − 9 657 847 = 9 877 249 − 9 657 847 = 219 402\n\nPredicted median prices: 2016 £214 505, 2017 £219 402.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(c)": "Professor Jackson's line passes through (2014, 215 000) and (2015, 243 500).\n\nGradient = (243 500 − 215 000)/(2015 − 2014) = 28 500\n\nP − 215 000 = 28 500(Y − 2014)\nP = 28 500Y − 57 399 000 + 215 000\n\nP = 28 500Y − 57 184 000, where P is the median house price in pounds and Y is the calendar year.\n(Equivalently P = 28 500t + 215 000, where t is the number of years after 2014.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(d)": "P = 28 500Y − 57 184 000\n\n2016: P = 28 500 × 2016 − 57 184 000 = 57 456 000 − 57 184 000 = 272 000\n2017: P = 28 500 × 2017 − 57 184 000 = 57 484 500 − 57 184 000 = 300 500\n\nPredicted median prices: 2016 £272 000, 2017 £300 500.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(e)": "Actual values: 2016 £290 000, 2017 £300 000.\n\nDr Procter's model: predictions £214 505 and £219 402 are about £75 000 and £81 000 too low. Her model is a very poor fit.\n\nProfessor Jackson's model: predictions £272 000 (about £18 000 too low) and £300 500 (only £500 too high). Her model is a much better, fairly good fit – very close for 2017, less close for 2016.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::11(f)": "Neither model is likely to be reliable. Both models were fitted to data from 2004–2015 (Professor Jackson's only to 2014–2015), and 2020 is well outside this range, so any prediction for 2020 is an extrapolation; house prices could change in ways the models do not capture.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::12(a)": "Probabilities sum to 1:\n0.2 + 0.3 + p + p + q = 1\n2p + q = 0.5 … (1)\n\nA total of 9 from two spins needs a 4 and a 5 (the maximum score is 5, so 4 + 5 is the only possible pair), in either order:\nP(total 9) = P(4 then 5) + P(5 then 4) = pq + qp = 2pq\n2pq = 0.06, so pq = 0.03 … (2)\n\nFrom (1), q = 0.5 − 2p. Substitute into (2):\np(0.5 − 2p) = 0.03\n0.5p − 2p² = 0.03\n2p² − 0.5p + 0.03 = 0\n\np = [0.5 ± √(0.25 − 4 × 2 × 0.03)]/(2 × 2)\n= [0.5 ± √0.01]/4\n= (0.5 ± 0.1)/4\np = 0.15 or p = 0.1\n\nCorresponding q = 0.5 − 2p:\np = 0.15 ⇒ q = 0.2\np = 0.1 ⇒ q = 0.3\n\nApply q < 2p:\np = 0.15, q = 0.2: 2p = 0.3 and 0.2 < 0.3 ✓\np = 0.1, q = 0.3: 2p = 0.2 and 0.3 < 0.2 is false, so reject.\n\np = 0.15, q = 0.2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::12(b)": "P(score 5) = q = 0.2 on each spin, and the spins are independent.\n\nLet Y be the number of 5s in 10 spins: Y ~ B(10, 0.2).\n\nP(Y = 1) = ¹⁰C₁ × 0.2 × 0.8⁹\n= 10 × 0.2 × 0.134217…\n= 0.268 (3 s.f.)\n\nProbability of exactly one 5 = 0.268",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::12(c)": "Let θ be the probability that the spinner shows a 1 on any given spin.\n\nH₀: θ = 0.2\nH₁: θ > 0.2\n(one-tailed test, 5% level)\n\nLet W be the number of 1s obtained in 100 spins. Under H₀, W ~ B(100, 0.2).\n\nObserved W = 28.\nP(W ≥ 28) = 1 − P(W ≤ 27)\n= 1 − 0.9658…\n= 0.0342 (3 s.f.)\n\n0.0342 < 0.05\n(Equivalently: P(W ≥ 27) = 0.0558 > 0.05 and P(W ≥ 28) = 0.0342 < 0.05, so the critical region is W ≥ 28, and 28 lies in it.)\n\nThe result is significant, so reject H₀.\n\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the probability of obtaining a score of 1 is greater than 0.2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::13(a)": "Lee's reasoning is not correct.\n\nThe p-value should be compared with the significance level: 0.033 < 0.05, so the result is significant at the 5% level. (It does not matter that 0.033 is close to 0; a small p-value is evidence against H₀.)\n\nThe correlation coefficient 0.37154 should not be compared with 0.05; for a test of positive correlation the relevant point is that r = 0.37154 is greater than 0 (positive), or it could be compared with the critical value of r for n = 33.\n\nLee has mixed up r and the p-value. Done correctly, the test gives evidence to suggest positive correlation between recycling rate and employment rate.\n\n(Note: 0.033 is the two-tailed p-value for r = 0.37154 with n = 33; for this one-tailed test the p-value is half of it, about 0.017, which is also below 0.05, so the conclusion is the same.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::13(b)": "Using the rule that an outlier is more than 2 standard deviations from the mean:\n\nUpper limit = x̄ + 2s\n= 465 467.97 + 2 × 204 356.26\n= 874 180 (to the nearest pound)\n\nFrom Fig. 13.2, there are points at median house prices of about 920 000 and about 1 200 000 (the maximum in Fig. 13.3 is 1 200 000). Both are above 874 180, so both are outliers.\n\nHence there are at least two outliers.\n\n(Using Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 521 000 + 1.5 × (521 000 − 342 500) = 788 750 instead, the points at about 800 000, 920 000 and 1 200 000 would all be outliers – again at least two.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::13(c)": "PMCC: the two outliers (at about £920 000 and £1 200 000) have high house prices with fairly low recycling rates, so they reinforce the negative trend. Without them, the remaining points form a cloud that is less well modelled by a straight line, so the PMCC would probably be closer to 0 (less strongly negative than −0.33278).\n\np-value: a correlation coefficient closer to 0 is more likely to occur if there is no correlation in the population, so the p-value would increase (from 0.058).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::13(d)": "The student's suggestion is reasonable. The pre-release data set also contains regions outside London, so if the 33 regions had been chosen at random from the whole data set it would be surprising for every one of them to be in London. (This suggests the sample may not have been truly random from the whole data set.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::14": "Points of intersection:\nsin x cos 2x = ½ − sin 2x cos x\nsin x cos 2x + cos x sin 2x = ½\nUsing sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B with A = x, B = 2x:\nsin 3x = ½\n\nFor 0 < x < 1, 0 < 3x < 3, and sin 3x = ½ gives\n3x = π/6 or 3x = 5π/6 (the next solution, 13π/6 ≈ 6.8, is greater than 3)\nx = π/18 or x = 5π/18\n\nWhich curve is on top: at x = 0.5 (between π/18 ≈ 0.175 and 5π/18 ≈ 0.873),\nsin 0.5 cos 1 ≈ 0.259 and ½ − sin 1 cos 0.5 ≈ −0.238,\nso y = sin x cos 2x is above y = ½ − sin 2x cos x on this interval (as in Fig. 14).\n\nArea = ∫ from π/18 to 5π/18 of [sin x cos 2x − (½ − sin 2x cos x)] dx\n= ∫ from π/18 to 5π/18 of (sin x cos 2x + cos x sin 2x − ½) dx\n= ∫ from π/18 to 5π/18 of (sin 3x − ½) dx\n= [−(cos 3x)/3 − x/2] from π/18 to 5π/18\n\nAt x = 5π/18: −(cos(5π/6))/3 − 5π/36 = −(−√3/2)/3 − 5π/36 = √3/6 − 5π/36\nAt x = π/18: −(cos(π/6))/3 − π/36 = −(√3/2)/3 − π/36 = −√3/6 − π/36\n\nArea = (√3/6 − 5π/36) − (−√3/6 − π/36)\n= √3/3 − 4π/36\n= √3/3 − π/9\n\nArea = √3/3 − π/9 = (3√3 − π)/9 (≈ 0.228)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::15(a)": "h(x) = fg(x) = f(x³ − x − 6) = √(x³ − x − 6)\n\nDomain: h is defined where g is defined, x > 2. For x > 2, g′(x) = 3x² − 1 > 0, so g is increasing and g(x) > g(2) = 8 − 2 − 6 = 0, so g(x) lies in the domain of f.\n\nh(x) = √(x³ − x − 6), with domain x > 2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::15(b)": "h(3) = √(3³ − 3 − 6) = √(27 − 9) = √18 = 3√2\n\nh(3) = √18 = 3√2 ≈ 4.24",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p2::15(c)": "On y = h⁻¹(x), the point where y = 3 has x = h(3) = √18. The gradient of h⁻¹ there is the reciprocal of the gradient of h at x = 3.\n\nh(x) = (x³ − x − 6)^(½)\nBy the chain rule,\nh′(x) = ½(x³ − x − 6)^(−½) × (3x² − 1) = (3x² − 1)/(2√(x³ − x − 6))\n\nAt x = 3:\nh′(3) = (27 − 1)/(2√18) = 26/(2 × 3√2) = 13/(3√2)\n\nGradient of y = h⁻¹(x) at the point where y = 3:\ndy/dx = 1/h′(3) = 3√2/13\n\nGradient = 3√2/13 ≈ 0.326",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::1": "Σ (r = 1 to 5) 2ʳ(r − 1)\n= 2¹×0 + 2²×1 + 2³×2 + 2⁴×3 + 2⁵×4\n= 0 + 4 + 16 + 48 + 128\n= 196",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::2": "From Fig. 2, |1 − x| > 2 exactly where |1 − x| − 2 > 0, i.e. where the graph of y = |1 − x| − 2 lies above the x-axis.\n\nFind where the graph crosses the x-axis: |1 − x| = 2\n1 − x = 2 ⇒ x = −1\n1 − x = −2 ⇒ x = 3\n(Equivalently, (1 − x)² = 2² gives x² − 2x − 3 = 0, (x + 1)(x − 3) = 0.)\n\nThe V-shaped graph is above the x-axis to the left of x = −1 and to the right of x = 3.\n\nSo |1 − x| > 2 for x < −1 or x > 3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::3": "The usage times form a geometric sequence:\n10, 10 × 0.98, 10 × 0.98², … = 10, 9.8, 9.604, …\nwith first term a = 10 hours and common ratio r = 0.98.\n\nSince |r| < 1, the total usage after n charges, Sₙ = 10(1 − 0.98ⁿ)/(1 − 0.98), increases towards the sum to infinity:\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = 10/(1 − 0.98) = 10/0.02 = 500\n\nThe total never exceeds 500 hours and gets as close to it as we like, so the maximum total length of use is 500 hours.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::4": "In a regular octagon each exterior angle is 360° ÷ 8 = 45°.\nAB is horizontal (in the direction of i), and going from B to C the direction turns through 45°, so BC makes an angle of 45° with the x-axis, pointing up and to the right. (Turning another 45° gives CD vertical, consistent with CD = j.)\n\nSo BC is parallel to i + j.\n\nAll sides of a regular octagon are equal, so |BC| = |AB| = |i| = 1.\n|i + j| = √(1² + 1²) = √2, so the unit vector in the direction of i + j is (1/√2)(i + j).\n\nTherefore BC = (1/√2)(i + j)   (equivalently (√2/2)i + (√2/2)j, or cos 45° i + sin 45° j).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(a)(i)": "cosec x = 1/sin x, which is undefined where sin x = 0.\nThe branch shown lies just to the right of the y-axis, between consecutive zeros of sin x at x = 0 and x = π.\n\nVertical asymptotes: x = 0 and x = π",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(a)(ii)": "On 0 < x < π, sin x takes its greatest value 1 at x = π/2, so cosec x = 1/sin x takes its least value 1 there.\n\nMinimum point: (π/2, 1)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(b)": "Let h(x) = x − cosec x (radians).\nh(1) = 1 − cosec 1 = 1 − 1.18840 = −0.188… < 0\nh(2) = 2 − cosec 2 = 2 − 1.09975 = 0.900… > 0\n\nsin x ≠ 0 for 1 ≤ x ≤ 2, so h is continuous on [1, 2]. There is a change of sign, so h(x) = 0 has a root between x = 1 and x = 2, i.e. the equation x = cosec x has a root between 1 and 2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(c)(i)": "xₙ₊₁ = cosec(xₙ) = 1/sin(xₙ), with x₀ = 1 (radians):\nx₁ = 1/sin 1 = 1.188395… = 1.18840 (5 d.p.)\nx₂ = 1/sin(1.188395…) = 1.077852… = 1.07785 (5 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(c)(ii)": "Continuing the iteration:\nx₃ = 1.13515, x₄ = 1.10303, x₅ = 1.12035, x₆ = 1.11080, …\nThe iterates oscillate either side of the root, closing in on it, and settle at 1.114157…\n\nRoot = 1.114 (3 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(d)": "Starting with x₀ = 2.5:\nx₁ = cosec 2.5 = 1.67092\nx₂ = cosec 1.67092 = 1.00503\nx₃ = 1.18458, x₄ = 1.07952, x₅ = 1.13413, x₆ = 1.10355, …\nThese values approach 1.114.\n\nNo: the iteration does not converge to the root between 2 and 3 (which is about 2.773). It converges to 1.114, the root between 1 and 2 found in part (c).\n(Near x ≈ 2.773 the gradient of y = cosec x is about 7.2, which is greater than 1 in size, so iterates move away from that root.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::5(e)": "Sketch (on the graph of y = cosec x and y = x):\n• Mark x₀ = 2.5 on the x-axis. This lies between the minimum (x = π/2 ≈ 1.57) and the right-hand asymptote x = π.\n• Draw a vertical line up from x = 2.5 to the curve, at (2.5, 1.671).\n• Draw a horizontal line across to the line y = x, at (1.671, 1.671).\n• Draw a vertical line down to the curve, at (1.671, 1.005) (just above the minimum, since 1.671 is just right of π/2).\n• Draw a horizontal line left to y = x, at (1.005, 1.005).\n• Draw a vertical line up to the curve, at (1.005, 1.185), then across to y = x at (1.185, 1.185), then down to the curve at (1.185, 1.080), and so on.\n\nThe first steps form a staircase with at least two horizontal sections; after that the path becomes a cobweb, alternating either side of the lower intersection of y = cosec x and y = x and spiralling in to the root x ≈ 1.114.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(a)(i)": "d/dx (e^(kx)) = ke^(kx)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(a)(ii)": "An exponential model has rate of growth proportional to its current value (from (i), dy/dx = ky when y = Ae^(kx)).\n\nThis is plausible for the business: the more customers it has (and so the greater its profit), the more people hear about it from those customers, so sales, and hence profits, grow at a rate proportional to the current profit.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(b)": "Take natural logs of y = Ae^(kx):\nln y = ln A + ln(e^(kx))\nln y = kx + ln A\n\nThis has the form Y = mX + c with Y = ln y, X = x, gradient m = k and intercept c = ln A (both constants).\nSo the graph of ln y against x is a straight line, which is consistent with the model y = Ae^(kx).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(c)": "From (b), the intercept of the line on the ln y axis is ln A and its gradient is k.\n\nIntercept: the line meets the ln y axis at 1.9, so\nln A = 1.9\nA = e^1.9 = 6.686 (3 d.p.)\n\nGradient: the line passes through about (0, 1.9) and (4, 2.9), so\nk = (2.9 − 1.9)/(4 − 0) = 0.25\n\nSo A ≈ 6.69 and k ≈ 0.25.\n(Readings from the line are approximate; any gradient estimate from about 0.24 to 0.25 is reasonable, e.g. using (9, 4.1) gives k = 2.2/9 ≈ 0.244.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(d)": "2020 is 11 years after 2009, so x = 11.\n\nUsing y = 6.686e^(0.25x):\ny = 6.686 × e^(0.25 × 11) = 6.686 × e^2.75 = 104.586…\n\ny is in thousands of pounds, so the predicted profit in 2020 is about £104 587, i.e. about £105 000.\n(With k = 0.244 instead, the prediction would be about £98 000; the answer depends on the estimates from (c).)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::6(e)": "Not very reliable. 2020 is outside the range of the data (which goes up to x = 9, i.e. 2018), so the prediction is an extrapolation and the exponential trend may not continue: there is a limit to the market for maths revision resources, and changes in the economy or in competition could affect the business.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::7(a)": "1/x + 1/(A − x) = [(A − x) + x]/(x(A − x))\n= A/(x(A − x))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::7(b)": "dx/dt = x(400 − x)/400\n\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ 400/(x(400 − x)) dx = ∫ 1 dt\n\nBy part (a) with A = 400:\n400/(x(400 − x)) = 1/x + 1/(400 − x)\n\nSo ∫ (1/x + 1/(400 − x)) dx = ∫ 1 dt\nln x − ln(400 − x) = t + c   (here 0 < x < 400)\nln(x/(400 − x)) = t + c\n\nWhen t = 0, x = 100:\nln(100/300) = c, so c = ln(1/3)\n\nt = ln(x/(400 − x)) − ln(1/3) = ln(3x/(400 − x))\n\nWhen t = 10:\n10 = ln(3x/(400 − x))\n3x/(400 − x) = e¹⁰\n3x = e¹⁰(400 − x)\n3x + e¹⁰x = 400e¹⁰\nx = 400e¹⁰/(3 + e¹⁰) = 399.95…\n\nThe model predicts 400 fish (to the nearest whole number) when t = 10.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::8(a)(i)": "y = 1/(1 + x²)² = (1 + x²)⁻²\n\nBy the chain rule:\ndy/dx = −2(1 + x²)⁻³ × 2x = −4x/(x² + 1)³\n\nDifferentiate again with the quotient rule, u = −4x, v = (x² + 1)³:\ndu/dx = −4, dv/dx = 3(x² + 1)² × 2x = 6x(x² + 1)²\n\nd²y/dx² = [(x² + 1)³(−4) − (−4x)(6x(x² + 1)²)]/(x² + 1)⁶\n= [−4(x² + 1)³ + 24x²(x² + 1)²]/(x² + 1)⁶\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by (x² + 1)²:\nd²y/dx² = [−4(x² + 1) + 24x²]/(x² + 1)⁴\n= (−4x² − 4 + 24x²)/(x² + 1)⁴\n= (20x² − 4)/(1 + x²)⁴, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::8(a)(ii)": "The curve is concave downwards where d²y/dx² < 0:\n(20x² − 4)/(1 + x²)⁴ < 0\n\n(1 + x²)⁴ > 0 for all x, so this requires\n20x² − 4 < 0\n5x² < 1\nx² < 1/5\n\nSo the curve is concave downwards for −1/√5 < x < 1/√5 (about −0.447 < x < 0.447).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::8(b)": "Let x = tan θ, with −π/2 < θ < π/2. Then dx/dθ = sec² θ, so dx = sec² θ dθ.\n\nLimits: x = −1 ⇒ θ = −π/4; x = 1 ⇒ θ = π/4.\n\n∫ from −1 to 1 of 1/(1 + x²)² dx\n= ∫ from −π/4 to π/4 of sec² θ/(1 + tan² θ)² dθ\n\nUsing 1 + tan² θ = sec² θ:\n= ∫ from −π/4 to π/4 of sec² θ/sec⁴ θ dθ\n= ∫ from −π/4 to π/4 of 1/sec² θ dθ\n\nUsing sec θ = 1/cos θ:\n= ∫ from −π/4 to π/4 of cos² θ dθ\n\nUsing cos 2θ = 2cos² θ − 1, so cos² θ = ½(cos 2θ + 1):\n= ∫ from −π/4 to π/4 of ½(cos 2θ + 1) dθ\n= ½[½ sin 2θ + θ] from −π/4 to π/4\n= ½[(½ sin(π/2) + π/4) − (½ sin(−π/2) − π/4)]\n= ½[(½ + π/4) − (−½ − π/4)]\n= ½(1 + π/2)\n= ½ + π/4",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::9(a)": "If a = 1 and b > 1:\naᵇ = 1ᵇ = 1\nbᵃ = b¹ = b, and b > 1\n\nSo aᵇ = 1 < b = bᵃ, i.e. aᵇ < bᵃ.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::9(b)": "Take a = 2 and b = 3 (so b > a > 1):\naᵇ = 2³ = 8\nbᵃ = 3² = 9\n8 < 9, so aᵇ is not greater than bᵃ. This is a counter-example to the conjecture in lines 7–8.\n\n(Another counter-example is a = 2, b = 4: 2⁴ = 16 = 4², so aᵇ is not greater than bᵃ.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::10": "∫ from e to π of (1/x) dx = [ln x] from e to π\n= ln π − ln e\n= ln π − 1   (since ln e = 1), as given in line 37.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::11": "Let y = eˣ. Then dy/dx = eˣ.\n\neˣ > 0 for all values of x, so dy/dx > 0 for all x.\nHence eˣ is an increasing function for all values of x, as stated in line 39.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::12(a)": "y = (ln x)/x, defined for x > 0.\n\nQuotient rule with u = ln x, v = x (du/dx = 1/x, dv/dx = 1):\ndy/dx = [x × (1/x) − ln x × 1]/x² = (1 − ln x)/x²\n\nThe derivative exists for all x > 0, and x² > 0, so stationary points occur only where\n(1 − ln x)/x² = 0\n1 − ln x = 0\nln x = 1\nx = e\n\nSo the only stationary point on the curve is where x = e, as given in line 45.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::12(b)": "From (a), dy/dx = (1 − ln x)/x².\n\nQuotient rule with u = 1 − ln x, v = x² (du/dx = −1/x, dv/dx = 2x):\nd²y/dx² = [x² × (−1/x) − (1 − ln x) × 2x]/x⁴\n= (−x − 2x + 2x ln x)/x⁴\n= (−3 + 2 ln x)/x³\n\nAt x = e (ln e = 1):\nd²y/dx² = (−3 + 2)/e³ = −1/e³ (≈ −0.05) < 0\n\nSo the stationary point at x = e is a maximum.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2020n-p3::12(c)": "For positive a ≠ e:\n(ln e)/e > (ln a)/a\nSince ln e = 1: 1/e > (ln a)/a\n\nMultiply both sides by ae, which is positive:\na > e ln a\ni.e. a ln e > e ln a\nln(eᵃ) > ln(aᵉ)\n\nln is an increasing function, so\neᵃ > aᵉ",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::1": "To disprove a statement about all real numbers, one counter-example is enough.\n\nTake p = −3 and q = 2.\np² = (−3)² = 9 and q² = 2² = 4, so p² > q².\nBut p = −3 < 2 = q, so p > q is false.\n\nSo there are real numbers p and q with p² > q² but p < q, and Beth's statement is not correct.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::2": "On Earth, W = mg with g = 9.8:\nm = 5200 ÷ 9.8 = 530.612… kg\n\nThe mass is the same on Mars, where g = 3.7 m s⁻²:\nWeight on Mars = 3.7 × 530.612… = 1963.3 N\n\nWeight on Mars ≈ 1963 N (1960 N to 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::3(a)": "The shaded region lies above the line y = x + 5 and below (or on) the curve y = 8 − 2x − x².\n\nThe straight boundary is not included, so that inequality is strict; the curved boundary is included, so that inequality is non-strict:\n\ny > x + 5\ny ≤ 8 − 2x − x²\n\n(These can be combined as x + 5 < y ≤ 8 − 2x − x².)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::3(b)": "8 − 2x − x² > x + 5\n⇒ 0 > x² + 3x − 3\n⇒ x² + 3x − 3 < 0\n\nBoundary values: solve x² + 3x − 3 = 0.\nx = (−3 ± √(3² − 4×1×(−3)))/2 = (−3 ± √(9 + 12))/2 = (−3 ± √21)/2\n\nx² + 3x − 3 is a positive quadratic (∪-shaped), so it is negative between its roots. (Equivalently, from the graph, the line lies below the curve between the two intersection points.)\n\nSo (−3 − √21)/2 < x < (−3 + √21)/2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::4(a)(i)": "The terms repeat every four terms (2, 3, 0, 3, 2, 3, 0, 3, …), so the sequence is periodic, with period 4.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::4(a)(ii)": "Each block of four terms has sum 2 + 3 + 0 + 3 = 8.\n200 terms make 200 ÷ 4 = 50 complete blocks.\n\nΣ a_k (k = 1 to 200) = 50 × 8 = 400",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::4(b)(i)": "u_n = bⁿ is a geometric sequence with common ratio b.\n\nIf −1 < b < 1, bⁿ → 0, so the sequence converges.\nIf b = 1, every term is 1, so the sequence converges (to 1).\nIf b > 1, bⁿ → ∞, so it diverges.\nIf b = −1, the terms alternate −1, 1, −1, 1, …, so it does not converge, i.e. it is divergent.\nIf b < −1, the terms alternate in sign and grow in size without limit, so it diverges.\n\nThe sequence is divergent for: b > 1 or b ≤ −1\n(i.e. {b : b > 1} ∪ {b : b ≤ −1})",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::4(b)(ii)": "With b = ⅓, the terms are ⅓, (⅓)², (⅓)³, …, a geometric series with first term a = ⅓ and common ratio r = ⅓.\n\nSince |r| < 1, the sum to infinity exists:\nS = a/(1 − r) = (⅓)/(1 − ⅓) = (⅓)/(⅔) = ½\n\nSum of all the terms = ½",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::5(a)": "Vertical forces (F acts downwards, perpendicular to AB):\nUpward: 20 N at B and 30 N at D. Downward: F.\nEquilibrium: F = 20 + 30, so F = 50.\n\n(Horizontally, the 20 N to the left at A balances the 20 N to the right at C.)\n\nMoments about A (distances in cm):\n• 20 N upward at B: perpendicular distance from A is AB = 30, anticlockwise moment 20 × 30 = 600\n• 20 N to the right along DC: perpendicular distance from A is AD = 10, anticlockwise moment 20 × 10 = 200\n• 30 N along DA and 20 N along BA both pass through A: no moment\n• F = 50 downward at x cm from A: clockwise moment 50x\n\nFor equilibrium, clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments:\n50x = 20 × 30 + 20 × 10 = 800\nx = 800 ÷ 50 = 16\n\nF = 50, x = 16",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::5(b)": "The resultant force is still zero (the vertical forces 20 + 30 = 50 upward and 50 downward still balance, and the horizontal forces still balance), but the moments no longer balance.\n\nMoments about A with F = 50 N downward at B:\nanticlockwise: 20 × 30 + 20 × 10 = 800 N cm\nclockwise: 50 × 30 = 1500 N cm\n\nSo there is a resultant moment of 1500 − 800 = 700 N cm clockwise, and the lamina is no longer in equilibrium: the forces have a clockwise turning effect, so the lamina will start to rotate clockwise.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::6(a)": "cosec x = 1/sin x, which is undefined where sin x = 0.\n\nThe three asymptotes visible are:\nx = 0 (the y-axis), x = π and x = 2π",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::6(b)": "At P, x = π/3:\ny = cosec(π/3) = 1/sin(π/3) = 1/(√3/2) = 2/√3 = 2√3/3\n\ndy/dx = −cosec x cot x\nAt x = π/3: cosec(π/3) = 2/√3 and cot(π/3) = 1/√3, so\ndy/dx = −(2/√3)(1/√3) = −2/3\n\nEquation of the tangent at P:\ny − 2√3/3 = −⅔(x − π/3)\n\nAt Q, y = 0:\n−2√3/3 = −⅔(x − π/3)\nMultiply both sides by −3/2:\n√3 = x − π/3\nx = π/3 + √3\n\nSo Q has x-coordinate π/3 + √3, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::7(a)": "The centre is the midpoint of AB:\n((−1 + 7)/2, (4 + (−2))/2) = (3, 1)\n\nThe radius is the distance from the centre (3, 1) to B(7, −2):\nr = √((7 − 3)² + (−2 − 1)²) = √(16 + 9) = √25 = 5\n\nEquation of the circle:\n(x − 3)² + (y − 1)² = 25",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::7(b)": "Substitute y = 2x + 5 into (x − 3)² + (y − 1)² = 25:\n(x − 3)² + (2x + 5 − 1)² = 25\n(x − 3)² + (2x + 4)² = 25\nx² − 6x + 9 + 4x² + 16x + 16 = 25\n5x² + 10x + 25 = 25\n5x² + 10x = 0\n5x(x + 2) = 0\nx = 0 or x = −2\n\ny = 2x + 5:\nx = 0 ⇒ y = 5\nx = −2 ⇒ y = 1\n\nThe points of intersection are (−2, 1) and (0, 5).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::7(c)": "Q is the intersection point with the larger y-coordinate, so Q = (0, 5).\n\nAB is a diameter and Q lies on the circle, so angle AQB = 90° (angle in a semicircle). Hence AQ and BQ are perpendicular and can be used as base and height.\n\nAQ = √((0 − (−1))² + (5 − 4)²) = √(1 + 1) = √2\nBQ = √((0 − 7)² + (5 − (−2))²) = √(49 + 49) = √98 = 7√2\n\n(Check: AQ² + BQ² = 2 + 98 = 100 = AB², since AB = 2 × 5 = 10.)\n\nArea of triangle ABQ = ½ × AQ × BQ = ½ × √2 × 7√2 = ½ × 14 = 7\n\nArea = 7 (square units)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(a)": "His argument is valid. f(5) = 1.670 > 0 and f(6) = −0.153 < 0, so there is a change of sign between x = 5 and x = 6. Since f(x) = sin 4x + e^(−x) + 0.75 is continuous, the curve must cross the x-axis between 5 and 6, so there is a root of the equation in that interval.\n\n(The argument relies on f being continuous, which it is. For a function with a discontinuity, a change of sign alone would not guarantee a root.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(b)": "The graph shows the curve dipping below the x-axis between x = 2 and x = 3, and again between x = 4 and x = 5. In each of these intervals there are two roots, so f(x) changes sign twice and the value is positive at both ends (f(2) = 1.875, f(3) = 0.263; f(4) = 0.480, f(5) = 1.670). Using steps of 1, his table shows no change of sign there, so these roots are missed.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(c)(i)": "f(x) = sin 4x + e^(−x) + 0.75\nf′(x) = 4cos 4x − e^(−x)\n\nNewton-Raphson: x_(n+1) = x_n − f(x_n)/f′(x_n), so\n\nx_(n+1) = x_n − (sin 4x_n + e^(−x_n) + 0.75)/(4cos 4x_n − e^(−x_n))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(c)(ii)": "Using x_(n+1) = x_n − (sin 4x_n + e^(−x_n) + 0.75)/(4cos 4x_n − e^(−x_n)) with x in radians:\n\nx₀ = 3\nx₁ = 2.920853…\nx₂ = 2.908274…\nx₃ = 2.907846…\n\nx₂ and x₃ agree to 4 significant figures (both round to 2.908), so\n\nthe root is x = 2.908 (4 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(d)(i)": "On the graph:\n• Start at x₀ = 5 on the x-axis and go up to the curve at (5, f(5)) ≈ (5, 1.67). This point is just to the left of the peak near x = 5.1, where the curve is rising gently.\n• Draw the tangent to the curve at this point and extend it down and to the left until it meets the x-axis; it crosses at x₁ ≈ 3.97.\n• From x₁ ≈ 3.97 go up to the curve (y ≈ 0.59, on a steeply falling section) and draw the tangent there; extend it down and to the right to meet the x-axis at x₂ ≈ 4.12.\n\nSo the iterates move to the left of 5, towards the root between 4 and 5 (near 4.15), not towards the root between 5 and 6.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(d)(ii)": "x₀ = 5 is close to a stationary point (the maximum near x = 5.1), so the gradient there is small (f′(5) ≈ 1.63, and it is positive). The tangent at x = 5 is therefore shallow and meets the x-axis a long way from x = 5, on the left, at x₁ ≈ 3.97, which is not near the root between 5 and 6.\n\nFrom there the sequence (3.97288, 4.12125, …) converges to a different root, the one between 4 and 5 (approximately 4.145), not the required root between 5 and 6.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::8(d)(iii)": "He should choose a starting value closer to the root between 5 and 6, on the steep decreasing part of the curve between the maximum near x = 5.1 and the minimum near x = 5.9, for example x₀ = 5.5.\n\n(Starting from x₀ = 5.5, the iteration converges to the root x ≈ 5.711.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::9(a)(i)": "Total mass = 120 + 3 × 90 = 390 g = 0.390 kg\nTotal resistance = 4 × 2.5 = 10 N (head plus three body sections)\n\nThe ribbon tensions are internal forces, so for the whole toy as a single particle, Newton's second law in the direction of motion gives:\n\n12 − 4 × 2.5 = 0.390a",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::9(a)(ii)": "12 − 10 = 0.390a\na = 2 ÷ 0.390 = 5.128…\n\na = 5.13 m s⁻² (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::9(b)": "Force diagram for the head (described in words), with the head shown as a dot and the direction of motion to the right:\n\n• Vertically up: the normal reaction R N from the ground.\n• Vertically down: the weight 0.12g N (mass 0.12 kg).\n• Horizontally forward (to the right): the tension in the string, 12 N.\n• Horizontally backward (to the left): the resistance, 2.5 N.\n• Horizontally backward (to the left): the tension T N in the ribbon joining the head to the first body section, labelled separately from the 12 N string tension.\n\nNo other forces act on the head.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::9(c)": "Let T N be the tension in the ribbon joining the head to the body.\n\nNewton's second law for the head only (mass 0.12 kg), using a = 2/0.390 = 5.128… m s⁻²:\n12 − 2.5 − T = 0.12a\nT = 9.5 − 0.12 × 5.128… = 9.5 − 0.6154… = 8.884…\n\n(Check with the three body sections together, mass 0.270 kg: T − 3 × 2.5 = 0.270a gives T = 7.5 + 1.384… = 8.884….)\n\nT = 8.88 N (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::10(a)": "Take upwards as positive: u = 29.4, a = −9.8.\nAt the maximum height the velocity is zero, v = 0.\n\nv = u + at\n0 = 29.4 − 9.8t\nt = 29.4 ÷ 9.8 = 3\n\nSo the ball reaches its maximum height after 3 s.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::10(b)": "With upwards positive, v = 29.4 − 9.8t.\n\nSketch (described in words): axes t (s) horizontally from 0 to 5 and v (m s⁻¹) vertically. The graph is a single straight line with gradient −9.8:\n• it starts at (0, 29.4) on the v-axis,\n• crosses the t-axis at (3, 0), where the ball is at its highest point,\n• continues below the t-axis to (5, −19.6) at the end of the 5 s, showing the ball moving downwards with negative velocity.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::10(c)": "With upwards positive, u = 29.4, a = −9.8, t = 5:\nv = u + at = 29.4 − 9.8 × 5 = 29.4 − 49 = −19.6\n\nThe negative sign means the ball is moving downwards.\n\nSpeed = 19.6 m s⁻¹",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::10(d)": "The maximum height depends only on the vertical motion, which has the same acceleration (−9.8 m s⁻²) for both balls. Since the second ball reaches the same maximum height as the first, its vertical motion is the same as the first ball's.\n\nVertical component of initial velocity: 29.4 m s⁻¹\nTime taken to reach the greatest height: 3 s",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::10(e)": "Horizontally there is no acceleration, so the horizontal component u_x is constant. The ball reaches its greatest height after 3 s, 48 m horizontally from the point of projection:\n3u_x = 48 ⇒ u_x = 16 m s⁻¹\n\nVertical component u_y = 29.4 m s⁻¹ (from part (d)).\n\nu = √(u_x² + u_y²) = √(16² + 29.4²) = √(256 + 864.36) = √1120.36 = 33.47…\ntan α = u_y/u_x = 29.4/16 = 1.8375 ⇒ α = 61.44…\n\nu = 33.5 (3 s.f.) and α = 61.4 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::11(a)": "The rate of increase of volume is inversely proportional to x:\ndV/dt = k/x, where k is a constant.\n\nAt t = 0, x = 5 and dV/dt = 21:\n21 = k/5 ⇒ k = 105, so dV/dt = 105/x.\n\nV = (4/3)πx³ ⇒ dV/dx = 4πx²\n\nChain rule:\ndV/dt = (dV/dx) × (dx/dt) = 4πx² (dx/dt)\n\nSo 4πx² (dx/dt) = 105/x, and hence\ndx/dt = (1/(4πx²)) × (105/x) = 105/(4πx³), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::11(b)": "Separate the variables in dx/dt = 105/(4πx³):\n∫ 4πx³ dx = ∫ 105 dt\nπx⁴ = 105t + c\n\nAt t = 0, x = 5:\nπ × 5⁴ = 0 + c ⇒ c = 625π\n\nSo πx⁴ = 105t + 625π, i.e. x⁴ = 105t/π + 625.\n\nTwo minutes is t = 120 s:\nx⁴ = (105 × 120)/π + 625 = 4010.70… + 625 = 4635.70…\nx = ⁴√4635.70… = 8.251…\n\nThe radius after two minutes is 8.25 cm (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::11(c)": "From πx⁴ = 105t + 625π, as t → ∞, x → ∞ and so V → ∞: the model predicts the balloon keeps growing without limit. A real balloon can only stretch to a certain size before it bursts, so the model cannot be valid for very large values of t.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::12": "Take the positive direction as down the slope. The box is moving down the slope, so friction acts up the slope.\n\nPerpendicular to the slope (no acceleration):\nR = mg cos 15°\n\nThe box is sliding, so friction is limiting:\nF = μR = 0.4mg cos 15° = 3.786…m N\n\nComponent of weight down the slope:\nmg sin 15° = 2.536…m N\n\nNewton's second law down the slope:\nmg sin 15° − 0.4mg cos 15° = ma\na = 9.8 sin 15° − 0.4 × 9.8 cos 15° = 2.5364… − 3.7864… = −1.25 m s⁻² (3 s.f.)\n\nThe box decelerates. Using v² = u² + 2as with u = 1.2, v = 0, a = −1.25:\n0 = 1.2² + 2 × (−1.25) × s\n2.5s = 1.44\ns = 0.576\n\nThe box travels 0.576 m before coming to rest.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::13(a)": "r = ∫ v dt = ∫ (3t² i + 7 j) dt = t³ i + 7t j + c\n\nAt t = 0, r = −i + 2j, so c = −i + 2j.\nr = (t³ − 1) i + (7t + 2) j\n\nAt t = 2:\nr = (2³ − 1) i + (7 × 2 + 2) j = 7 i + 16 j\n\nDistance from the origin:\n|r| = √(7² + 16²) = √(49 + 256) = √305 = 17.46…\n\nDistance = 17.5 m (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::13(b)": "From part (a), r = (t³ − 1) i + (7t + 2) j, so\nx = t³ − 1 and y = 7t + 2.\n\nFrom the second equation: t = (y − 2)/7.\n\nSubstitute into the first:\nx = ((y − 2)/7)³ − 1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p1::13(c)": "Acceleration:\na = dv/dt = d/dt (3t² i + 7 j) = 6t i\n\nAt t = 2: a = 12 i m s⁻²\n\nBy Newton's second law F = ma, so the resultant force is in the same direction as a, i.e. in the i direction, with magnitude 48 N:\nF = 48 i = m × 12 i\nm = 48 ÷ 12 = 4\n\nThe mass of the particle is 4 kg.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::1": "A stretch parallel to the x-axis with scale factor 2 replaces x by ½x in the equation.\n\ny = 4(½x)² + 8(½x) + 1\ny = 4 × ¼x² + 4x + 1\ny = x² + 4x + 1\n\nSo a = 1, b = 4, c = 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::2(a)": "180° = π radians, so multiply by π/180.\n\n65° = 65 × π/180 = 65π/180\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 5:\n65° = 13π/36 radians\n\nSo k = 13/36.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::2(b)": "π radians = 180°, so multiply by 180/π.\n\n0.211 × 180/π = 37.98/π = 12.089…°\n\n0.211 radians = 12.1° (to 1 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::3": "{x : x ≥ 2} ∩ {x : x < 7} is the set of x that satisfy both conditions, i.e. 2 ≤ x < 7.\n\nNumber line (description of the sketch):\n• Draw a horizontal number line marked 0, 1, 2, …, 10.\n• Put a solid (filled-in) circle at 2, because 2 is included (x ≥ 2).\n• Put an open (hollow) circle at 7, because 7 is not included (x < 7).\n• Draw a thick line joining the two circles, covering all the values between 2 and 7, and nothing outside that interval.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::4": "Start from the straight line y = 2x − 3:\n• it crosses the y-axis at (0, −3);\n• it crosses the x-axis where 2x − 3 = 0, i.e. at x = 1.5.\n\nFor y = |2x − 3|, the part of the line below the x-axis (x < 1.5) is reflected in the x-axis, and the part with x ≥ 1.5 is unchanged.\n\nSketch (description):\n• A V shape, with y ≥ 0 everywhere.\n• Vertex on the x-axis at (1.5, 0).\n• The left branch has gradient −2 and crosses the y-axis at (0, 3).\n• The right branch has gradient 2; for example it passes through (3, 3).\n• The graph is symmetrical about the line x = 1.5.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::5(a)": "Let X be the number of people in the sample who carry the gene.\nX ~ B(32, 0.4)\n\nP(X = 12) = ³²C₁₂ × 0.4¹² × 0.6²⁰\n= 0.13850…\n\nP(exactly 12 carry the gene) = 0.139 (to 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::5(b)": "X ~ B(32, 0.4), where X is the number who carry the gene.\n\nP(X ≥ 8) = 1 − P(X ≤ 7)\n\nUsing the calculator's binomial cumulative function:\nP(X ≤ 7) = 0.024821…\n\nP(X ≥ 8) = 1 − 0.024821… = 0.975178…\n\nP(at least 8 carry the gene) = 0.975 (to 3 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::6(a)": "v = 2a + 3b = 2(5, 3) + 3(−1, 6)   (column vectors)\n\nFirst component: 2 × 5 + 3 × (−1) = 10 − 3 = 7\nSecond component: 2 × 3 + 3 × 6 = 6 + 18 = 24\n\nSo v = (7, 24).\n\n|v| = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::6(b)": "v = (7, 24) and the vector (1, 0) points along the positive x-direction, so the angle θ between them is the angle v makes with the positive x-axis.\n\nSince both components of v are positive, v lies in the first quadrant, so\ntan θ = 24/7\nθ = tan⁻¹(24/7) = 73.739…°\n\n(Check with the scalar product: cos θ = (7 × 1 + 24 × 0)/(25 × 1) = 7/25, giving the same angle.)\n\nAngle = 73.7° (to 1 d.p.), or 1.29 radians.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::7(a)": "Rearrange each equation:\nx − 7 = 5cos θ\ny + 3 = 5sin θ\n\nSquare and add:\n(x − 7)² + (y + 3)² = 25cos²θ + 25sin²θ = 25(cos²θ + sin²θ)\n\nUsing cos²θ + sin²θ = 1:\n(x − 7)² + (y + 3)² = 25",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::7(b)": "Comparing (x − 7)² + (y + 3)² = 25 with (x − p)² + (y − q)² = r², the centre is (p, q).\n\nCentre = (7, −3)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::8": "If Y = a + bX, then\nE(Y) = a + bE(X) and Var(Y) = b²Var(X).\n\nVariance:\nb² × 6.8 = 11.492\nb² = 11.492/6.8 = 1.69\nb = 1.3 (b is positive, so the negative root −1.3 is rejected)\n\nMean:\na + 42b = 57.2\na + 42 × 1.3 = 57.2\na + 54.6 = 57.2\na = 2.6\n\nSo a = 2.6 and b = 1.3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::9(a)": "The litter has 9 puppies: 5 female (3 black, 1 yellow, 1 chocolate) and 4 male (1 black, 2 yellow, 1 chocolate).\nFour puppies are chosen without replacement, so all ⁹C₄ = 126 selections are equally likely.\n\nExactly 3 females means 3 of the 5 females and 1 of the 4 males:\nNumber of selections = ⁵C₃ × ⁴C₁ = 10 × 4 = 40\n\nP(exactly 3 females) = 40/126 = 20/63 = 0.317 (to 3 s.f.)\n\n(Equivalently: (5/9)(4/8)(3/7)(4/6) × 4 = 20/63, where 4 is the number of positions the male can take.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::9(b)": "There are 4 black puppies and 5 non-black puppies. Total selections of 4 puppies = ⁹C₄ = 126.\n\nAt least 3 black means exactly 3 black or exactly 4 black.\n\nExactly 3 black: ⁴C₃ × ⁵C₁ = 4 × 5 = 20 selections\nExactly 4 black: ⁴C₄ = 1 selection\n\nTotal = 20 + 1 = 21\n\nP(at least 3 black) = 21/126 = 1/6 (≈ 0.167)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::9(c)": "Let F be the event 'exactly 3 females' and B the event 'at least 3 black'.\nFrom (b), B contains 21 of the 126 equally likely selections.\n\nCount the selections in B that contain exactly 3 females.\nThe black puppies are 1 male and 3 females; the non-black puppies are 3 males and 2 females.\n\nCase 1: all 3 black females + 1 male (either the black male or one of the 3 non-black males).\nNumber = ³C₃ × ⁴C₁ = 1 × 4 = 4\n\nCase 2: 2 black females + the black male + 1 non-black female (this gives exactly 3 black and exactly 3 females).\nNumber = ³C₂ × ¹C₁ × ²C₁ = 3 × 1 × 2 = 6\n\nTotal in F ∩ B = 4 + 6 = 10, so P(F ∩ B) = 10/126 = 5/63.\n\nP(F | B) = P(F ∩ B)/P(B) = (10/126)/(21/126) = 10/21 = 0.476 (to 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::9(d)": "If the events were independent, P(F | B) would equal P(F).\n\nFrom (c), P(F | B) = 10/21 ≈ 0.476.\nFrom (a), P(F) = 20/63 ≈ 0.317.\n\nSince 10/21 ≠ 20/63, the events 'choosing exactly 3 females' and 'choosing at least 3 black puppies' are not independent.\n\n(Equivalently, P(F) × P(B) = (20/63) × (1/6) = 10/189, which is not equal to P(F ∩ B) = 5/63.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(a)": "The number of birds seen in a garden varies with the season (and with seasonal weather). Stratifying by season, with 8 days from each season, makes sure all four seasons are equally represented, whereas a simple random sample might by chance include too many days from one season and too few from another.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(b)": "Most of the values are bunched at the lower end (the stems 0, 1 and 2 hold 23 of the 32 values), with a long tail stretching up to 60.\n\nThe distribution has positive skew.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(c)": "The mode is 9, which occurs 3 times, only one more time than several other values (10, 11 and 30 each occur twice), so it is not clearly the most typical value. It also lies in the lower tail of this positively skewed distribution, well below the median of 19.5, so it does not represent the centre of the data.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(d)": "There are n = 32 values, in order:\n3, 5, 9, 9, 9, 10, 10, 11, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 24, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 30, 32, 33, 40, 43, 46, 51, 60\n\nMedian: between the 16th and 17th values, which are 19 and 20.\nMedian = (19 + 20)/2 = 19.5\n\nLower quartile: the lower half is the first 16 values, so LQ is between the 8th and 9th values, 11 and 11.\nLQ = 11\n\nUpper quartile: the upper half is the last 16 values, so UQ is between the 24th and 25th values, 30 and 30.\nUQ = 30\n\nInterquartile range = 30 − 11 = 19\n\nMedian = 19.5, IQR = 19",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(e)": "From Fig. 10.2 (2015, before the fence): median ≈ 34, LQ ≈ 18, UQ = 50, so IQR ≈ 50 − 18 = 32.\nFrom (d) (2019, after the fence): median = 19.5, IQR = 19.\n\nFewer birds: the 2019 median (19.5) is much smaller than the 2015 median (about 34). This is evidence supporting Ben's belief that he sees fewer birds.\n\nMore variable: the 2019 IQR (19) is much smaller than the 2015 IQR (about 32), so the 2019 counts are less spread out, not more. This does not support Ben's belief that the number of birds is more variable.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::10(f)": "A boxplot shows only five summary values (minimum, LQ, median, UQ, maximum). It shows that each quarter of the data covers a similar range, but it does not show how the values are spread out within each quarter: they could be bunched together anywhere inside each section. Many different distributions, including other symmetrical distributions that are not uniform, can give the same boxplot, so Jane cannot tell from the boxplot that the data are definitely uniformly distributed.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::11(a)": "Hypotheses:\nH₀: μ = 161.6\nH₁: μ > 161.6\nwhere μ cm is the population mean height of adult women in the UK in 2020.\n\nThis is a 1-tail test, because the researcher is investigating whether the mean height has increased (a change in one specified direction), not just whether it has changed.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::11(b)": "Under H₀, X̄ ~ N(161.6, 1.96/200), where X̄ is the mean height of a random sample of 200 women.\n\nStandard deviation of X̄ = √(1.96/200) = 1.4/√200 = 0.098994…\n\nFor a 1-tail test at the 5% level (upper tail), the critical value of z is 1.645.\n\nCritical value = 161.6 + 1.645 × 0.098994… = 161.7628…\n\nCritical region: X̄ > 161.763 (to 3 d.p.), i.e. X̄ > 161.8 to 1 d.p.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::11(c)": "Statement 1, 'The sample mean is in the critical region': correct. The critical region is X̄ > 161.76…, and 161.9 > 161.76…\n(Equivalently, P(X̄ ≥ 161.9) = 0.00122 < 0.05.)\n\nStatement 2, 'The null hypothesis is accepted': incorrect. Because the sample mean is in the critical region, the result is significant at the 5% level, so H₀ is rejected. There is evidence to suggest that the mean height of adult women in the UK has increased.\n\nStatement 3, 'This proves that the mean height is unaltered at 161.6 cm': incorrect. A hypothesis test can only give evidence for or against a hypothesis; it can never prove that a hypothesis is true. (Here, in any case, the evidence points towards the mean having increased.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(a)": "If the cell were left blank or given the value 0, the spreadsheet software could read it as zero and include it in calculations (such as means, correlations and graphs), which would distort the results. Entering #N/A tells the software that there is no data in that cell, so the cell is ignored rather than treated as zero.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(b)": "The two outliers to ring are:\n• the point on the extreme left, at about BMI = 1.3, pulse rate = 88;\n• the point at the top, at about BMI = 30.6, pulse rate = 128.\n\nAll the other points lie between a BMI of about 17 and 44 and a pulse rate of about 45 and 105.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(c)": "The outlier on the extreme left (BMI ≈ 1.3, pulse ≈ 88) should be removed. Nobody could have a BMI this low: for a woman 1.6 m tall, a BMI of 1.3 would mean a mass of only about 1.3 × 1.6² ≈ 3.3 kg. This must be an error in the data.\n\nThe outlier with the very high pulse rate (BMI ≈ 30.6, pulse ≈ 128) should not be removed. Its BMI of about 30.6 is typical of the other points, and a pulse rate of 128 is unusually high but plausible, so there is no reason to think it is an error.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(d)": "The points form a roughly horizontal cloud with no upward or downward trend: pulse rates are spread between about 45 and 105 across the whole range of BMI values.\n\nThe diagram suggests there is no correlation between pulse rate and BMI.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(e)": "The product moment correlation coefficient between waist measurement and BMI is 0.912, which is close to 1. This shows strong positive linear correlation, so a linear model of the form w = mb + c is likely to be appropriate.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::12(f)": "Cell F3 gives the BMI for this woman: b = 24.9.\n\nUsing w = 2.16b + 33.0:\nw = 2.16 × 24.9 + 33.0 = 53.784 + 33.0 = 86.784\n\nEstimated waist measurement for cell G3 ≈ 86.8 cm",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::13(a)": "Let x be the number of damaged decorations in a box and f the number of boxes.\n\nΣf = 19 + 35 + 28 + 13 + 5 = 100\nΣfx = 0 + 35 + 56 + 39 + 20 = 150\nΣfx² = 0 + 35 + 112 + 117 + 80 = 344\n\nMean = 150/100 = 1.5\n\nSxx = Σfx² − (Σfx)²/n = 344 − 150²/100 = 344 − 225 = 119\ns² = 119/99 = 1.2020…\ns = √1.2020… = 1.096…\n\nMean = 1.5 damaged decorations per box\nStandard deviation = 1.10 (to 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::13(b)": "Each box contains 10 decorations, so n = 10.\n\nThe mean of B(n, p) is np, so using the sample mean:\n10p = 1.5\np = 0.15\n\nn = 10 and p = 0.15",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::13(c)": "X ~ B(10, 0.15). Expected number of boxes = 100 × P(X = r).\n\nP(X = 0) = 0.85¹⁰ = 0.19687… → 19.7\nP(X = 1) = 10 × 0.15 × 0.85⁹ = 0.34742… → 34.7\nP(X = 2) = ¹⁰C₂ × 0.15² × 0.85⁸ = 0.27589… → 27.6\nP(X = 3) = ¹⁰C₃ × 0.15³ × 0.85⁷ = 0.12983… → 13.0\nP(X = 4) = ¹⁰C₄ × 0.15⁴ × 0.85⁶ = 0.04009… → 4.0\nP(X ≥ 5) = 1 − (sum of the above) = 0.00987… → 1.0\n\nCompleted table (expected number of boxes, to 1 d.p.):\n0: 19.7\n1: 34.7\n2: 27.6\n3: 13.0\n4: 4.0\n5 or more: 1.0\n\nCheck: 19.7 + 34.7 + 27.6 + 13.0 + 4.0 + 1.0 = 100.0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::13(d)": "Observed: 19, 35, 28, 13, 5, 0\nExpected: 19.7, 34.7, 27.6, 13.0, 4.0, 1.0\n\nEvery observed frequency is within 1 of the expected frequency, so there is a close match between the observed and expected frequencies. The binomial model is a good fit for these data.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::14(a)": "y = x²(x − 2)³\n\nProduct rule, with the chain rule for (x − 2)³:\ndy/dx = 2x(x − 2)³ + x² × 3(x − 2)²\n\nTake out the common factor x(x − 2)²:\ndy/dx = x(x − 2)²[2(x − 2) + 3x]\n= x(x − 2)²(2x − 4 + 3x)\n= x(x − 2)²(5x − 4)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::14(b)": "At stationary points dy/dx = 0:\nx(x − 2)²(5x − 4) = 0\nx = 0, x = 2 or x = 4/5\n\ny-coordinates, using y = x²(x − 2)³:\nx = 0: y = 0\nx = 2: y = 4 × 0³ = 0\nx = 4/5: y = (4/5)² × (4/5 − 2)³ = (16/25) × (−6/5)³ = (16/25) × (−216/125) = −3456/3125 = −1.10592\n\nStationary points: (0, 0), (2, 0) and (0.8, −1.10592), i.e. (4/5, −3456/3125).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::14(c)": "d²y/dx² = 4(x − 2)(5x² − 8x + 2)\n\nAt (0, 0):\nd²y/dx² = 4(−2)(2) = −16 < 0, so (0, 0) is a maximum.\n\nAt (0.8, −1.10592):\nd²y/dx² = 4(−1.2)(5 × 0.64 − 6.4 + 2) = 4(−1.2)(−1.2) = 5.76 > 0, so (0.8, −1.10592) is a minimum.\n\nAt (2, 0):\nd²y/dx² = 4(0)(…) = 0, so the second derivative test does not decide the nature. Check the gradient either side using dy/dx = x(x − 2)²(5x − 4):\nx = 1: dy/dx = 1 × 1 × 1 = 1 > 0\nx = 3: dy/dx = 3 × 1 × 11 = 33 > 0\nThe gradient is positive on both sides of x = 2, so (2, 0) is a (stationary) point of inflection.\n\nSummary: (0, 0) maximum; (0.8, −1.10592) minimum; (2, 0) point of inflection.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::14(d)": "Key features (from (b) and (c)):\n• The curve meets the axes only at (0, 0) and (2, 0).\n• As x → −∞, y → −∞; as x → ∞, y → ∞ (the leading term is x⁵).\n\nSketch (description):\n• Coming from the bottom left, the curve rises to a maximum at the origin, where it touches the x-axis (y ≤ 0 on both sides near x = 0).\n• It then falls to a minimum at (0.8, −1.106) below the x-axis.\n• It rises again and flattens out at (2, 0), a stationary point of inflection where the curve crosses the x-axis with zero gradient.\n• For x > 2 it rises steeply towards +∞.\n\nMark the intercepts 0 and 2 on the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::15(a)": "Σ (r = 1 to ∞) 0.99^(r−1) × 0.01 = 0.01 + 0.01 × 0.99 + 0.01 × 0.99² + …\n\nThis is a geometric series with first term 0.01 and common ratio 0.99.\nSince |0.99| < 1, the sum to infinity exists:\n\nS∞ = (first term)/(1 − common ratio) = 0.01/(1 − 0.99) = 0.01/0.01 = 1\n\nSo Σ (r = 1 to ∞) 0.99^(r−1) × 0.01 = 1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::15(b)": "They play one match a week, so 6 years = 6 × 52 = 312 matches.\n\nUsing Kofi's model, the probability that Layla beats him within the first 312 matches is\nP(X ≤ 312) = Σ (r = 1 to 312) 0.99^(r−1) × 0.01\n= 0.01(1 − 0.99³¹²)/(1 − 0.99)\n= 1 − 0.99³¹²\n= 0.9565 (to 4 d.p.)\n\nSince 0.9565 > 0.95, Kofi's model predicts that Layla will probably beat him within 6 years: the probability that she does not is only 1 − 0.9565 = 0.0435.\n\nSo Kofi's statement that he is 95% certain Layla will not beat him within 6 years is not consistent with his model.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::15(c)": "Kofi's model uses the same probability, 0.01, of Layla winning every match. Because Layla practises between matches (and Kofi does not), she expects to improve, so she should become increasingly likely to beat Kofi as time goes on. A constant probability does not reflect this.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::15(d)": "In Layla's model P(X = r) = kr², which increases as r increases. So the later the match (the more practice she has had), the higher the probability that the first win comes at that match, reflecting her improvement. In Kofi's model the probabilities 0.99^(r−1) × 0.01 decrease as r increases, because his model assumes her chance of winning each match stays the same.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::15(e)": "The probabilities must sum to 1:\nk(1² + 2² + 3² + 4² + 5² + 6² + 7² + 8²) = 1\nk(1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + 36 + 49 + 64) = 1\n204k = 1\nk = 1/204\n\nProbability she wins within the first 6 matches:\nP(X ≤ 6) = k(1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + 36) = 91/204 = 0.446 (to 3 s.f.)\n\nSince 0.446 < 0.95, Layla's statement that she is 95% certain to beat Kofi within the first 6 matches is not consistent with her model.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p2::16": "Use the substitution u = 1 + √x.\n\nThen √x = u − 1, so x = (u − 1)².\n\ndu/dx = ½x^(−½) = 1/(2√x)\nso dx = 2√x du = 2(u − 1) du\n\nSubstitute:\n∫ x/(1 + √x) dx = ∫ (u − 1)² × 2(u − 1)/u du\n= 2∫ (u − 1)³/u du\n\nExpand (u − 1)³ = u³ − 3u² + 3u − 1:\n= 2∫ (u³ − 3u² + 3u − 1)/u du\n= 2∫ (u² − 3u + 3 − 1/u) du\n= 2[u³/3 − 3u²/2 + 3u − ln u] + c\n= (2/3)u³ − 3u² + 6u − 2ln u + c\n\n(u = 1 + √x is always positive, so ln u needs no modulus.)\n\nSubstitute back u = 1 + √x:\n∫ x/(1 + √x) dx = (2/3)(1 + √x)³ − 3(1 + √x)² + 6(1 + √x) − 2ln(1 + √x) + c\n\n(Expanding and absorbing the constant terms into c gives the equivalent form (2/3)x√x − x + 2√x − 2ln(1 + √x) + c.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::1(a)": "Complete the square. Half the coefficient of x is 4:\nx² + 8x + 2 = (x + 4)² − 16 + 2\n= (x + 4)² − 14\n\nSo a = 4 and b = −14.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::1(b)": "From (a), y = (x + 4)² − 14.\n(x + 4)² ≥ 0, with its least value 0 when x = −4, so the minimum value of y is −14.\n\nTurning point: (−4, −14)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::1(c)": "y = x² + 8x + 2 = (x + 4)² − 14.\n\nReplacing x by x + 4 moves the curve 4 units in the negative x-direction, and subtracting 14 moves it 14 units in the negative y-direction.\n\nSo the single transformation is a translation by the vector (−4, −14), written as a column vector with −4 on top and −14 below (4 units left and 14 units down).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::2": "0° ≤ x ≤ 180° ⇒ 0° ≤ 2x ≤ 360°.\n\nsin 2x = 0.3\nPrincipal value: 2x = arcsin 0.3 = 17.4576…°\nsin is also positive in the second quadrant: 2x = 180° − 17.4576…° = 162.5424…°\nThe next values (377.46…°, …) are outside 0° to 360°.\n\nSo 2x = 17.4576…° or 162.5424…°\nx = 8.7288…° or 81.2712…°\n\nx = 8.7° or x = 81.3° (1 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::3(a)": "x + y = k   (1)\n2x − y = 1   (2)\n\nAdding (1) and (2) eliminates y:\n3x = k + 1\nx = (k + 1)/3\n\nSubstituting into (1):\ny = k − (k + 1)/3 = (3k − k − 1)/3 = (2k − 1)/3\n\nThe lines intersect at ((k + 1)/3, (2k − 1)/3).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::3(b)": "From the line, y = k − x. Substitute into y = x² + k:\nk − x = x² + k\nx² + x = 0\nx(x + 1) = 0\nx = 0 or x = −1\n\nUsing y = k − x:\nx = 0 ⇒ y = k\nx = −1 ⇒ y = k + 1\n\nThe line crosses the curve at (0, k) and (−1, k + 1).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::4(a)": "y = (x/4)^(−x)\n\nAt A, x = 1: y = (1/4)^(−1) = 4\nAt B, x = 1.1: y = (1.1/4)^(−1.1) = (0.275)^(−1.1) = 4.13746…\n\nGradient of chord AB = (4.13746… − 4)/(1.1 − 1)\n= 0.13746…/0.1\n= 1.3746…\n\nGradient of AB = 1.37 (2 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::4(b)": "A chord from A gives a better approximation to the tangent gradient at A when its other end is closer to A than B is.\n\nTake C with x-coordinate 1.01.\n\n(Any x-coordinate strictly between 1 and 1.1 is acceptable; the closer it is to 1, the better the approximation.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::5(a)(i)": "y = eˣ ⇒ dy/dx = eˣ.\n\nSo the graph of dy/dx against x is identical to the given graph of y = eˣ.\n\nSketch (described): an increasing exponential curve on axes labelled x (horizontal) and dy/dx (vertical). It lies entirely above the horizontal axis, approaches the negative x-axis as an asymptote as x → −∞, crosses the vertical axis at (0, 1), and gets steeper and steeper as x increases.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::5(a)(ii)": "dy/dx = eˣ = y.\n\nSo dy/dx plotted against y is the straight line dy/dx = y: gradient 1, through the origin.\n\nSince y = eˣ > 0 for all x, only positive values of y occur, and then dy/dx = y > 0 too.\n\nSketch (described): axes labelled y (horizontal) and dy/dx (vertical); a straight line of gradient 1 starting at the origin (open circle there, since y never equals 0) and going up to the right, lying in the first quadrant only.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::5(b)(i)": "With y = Ae^(kt), dy/dt = kAe^(kt) = ky, so the model assumes the rate of growth of the population is proportional to the size of the population.\n\nThis is reasonable for a newly introduced wolf population with plenty of food and space: the more wolves there are, the more cubs are born each year, so the population grows faster as it gets bigger.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::5(b)(ii)": "t = 0, y = 21:\n21 = Ae⁰ = A\nA = 21\n\nt = 1, y = 51:\n51 = 21eᵏ\neᵏ = 51/21 = 17/7\nk = ln(17/7) = 0.887 (3 s.f.)\n\nA = 21, k = ln(17/7) ≈ 0.887",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::5(b)(iii)": "In the model, y = 21e^(0.887t) increases without limit as t increases.\n\nIn reality the wolf population cannot keep growing forever: food (prey) and territory in the park are limited, so as the population gets large the wolves will run short of resources and the growth will slow down and level off. The model would therefore greatly over-predict the population many years after 1995.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::6": "Write out the three terms (r = 1, 2, 3):\n\nΣ (r = 1 to 3) 1/(√(r + 1) + √r) = 1/(√2 + 1) + 1/(√3 + √2) + 1/(√4 + √3)\n\nRationalise each denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate, using (√a + √b)(√a − √b) = a − b:\n\n1/(√2 + 1) = (√2 − 1)/((√2 + 1)(√2 − 1)) = (√2 − 1)/(2 − 1) = √2 − 1\n1/(√3 + √2) = (√3 − √2)/(3 − 2) = √3 − √2\n1/(√4 + √3) = (√4 − √3)/(4 − 3) = 2 − √3\n\nAdding:\n(√2 − 1) + (√3 − √2) + (2 − √3)\n= −1 + 2 (the √2 and √3 terms cancel)\n= 1\n\nSo Σ (r = 1 to 3) 1/(√(r + 1) + √r) = 1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::7": "Integrate by parts, ∫ u (dv/dx) dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx, with\nu = x ⇒ du/dx = 1\ndv/dx = cos 2x ⇒ v = ½ sin 2x\n\n∫ x cos 2x dx = ½x sin 2x − ∫ ½ sin 2x dx\n= ½x sin 2x − ½ × (−½ cos 2x) + c\n= ½x sin 2x + ¼ cos 2x + c",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::8": "(3, 1) lies on y = a/x²:\n1 = a/3² ⇒ a = 9\n\nSo y = 9/x² (x ≠ 0).\n\nFor y to be an integer, x² must be a positive integer that divides 9 exactly, so x² = 1 or 9 (x² = 3 is not a square of an integer):\nx² = 1: x = ±1, y = 9\nx² = 9: x = ±3, y = 1\n\nThe integer points are (1, 9), (−1, 9), (3, 1), (−3, 1).\n\nApart from (3, 1), the points are (−3, 1), (1, 9) and (−1, 9).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::9(a)": "y = 5x − 1 has y-intercept −1 and gradient 5.\nPoints on it: (0, −1), (0.2, 0), (1, 4).\n\nDrawing (described): a straight line through (0, −1) and (1, 4), crossing the x-axis at (0.2, 0). It is steep and rises from bottom left to top right, crossing the curve y = 3 − √x at about (0.64, 2.2) and leaving the grid at the top (y = 5) when x = 1.2.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::9(b)": "Intersection of the curve and the line:\n3 − √x = 5x − 1\n4 − 5x = √x\nSquare: (4 − 5x)² = x\n16 − 40x + 25x² = x\n25x² − 41x + 16 = 0\n(x − 1)(25x − 16) = 0\nx = 1 or x = 16/25\n\nCheck in 4 − 5x = √x (squaring can introduce false roots; we need 4 − 5x ≥ 0):\nx = 1: 4 − 5 = −1 but √1 = 1, so x = 1 is rejected.\nx = 16/25: 4 − 80/25 = 4/5 and √(16/25) = 4/5 ✓\n\nSo x = 16/25 = 0.64, and y = 5 × 0.64 − 1 = 2.2.\nThe line meets the x-axis where 5x − 1 = 0, i.e. x = 0.2.\n\nThe region is bounded below by the x-axis, on the left by the line (from (0.2, 0) up to (0.64, 2.2)), above by the curve (from x = 0.64 to x = 4) and on the right by x = 4. Split it at x = 0.64.\n\nPart 1 (0.2 ≤ x ≤ 0.64): a triangle under the line with base 0.64 − 0.2 = 0.44 and height 2.2\nArea = ½ × 0.44 × 2.2 = 0.484 = 121/250\n\nPart 2 (0.64 ≤ x ≤ 4): under the curve, which is above the x-axis here (y = 1 at x = 4)\nArea = ∫ from 0.64 to 4 of (3 − √x) dx\n= [3x − (2/3)x^(3/2)] from 0.64 to 4\n= (12 − (2/3)(8)) − (1.92 − (2/3)(0.512))\n= (12 − 16/3) − (1.92 − 128/375)\n= 20/3 − 592/375\n= 2500/375 − 592/375\n= 1908/375\n= 636/125 = 5.088\n\nTotal area = 121/250 + 636/125\n= 121/250 + 1272/250\n= 1393/250\n\nExact area = 1393/250 = 5 143/250 (= 5.572) square units",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::10(a)": "Let 1/((4x + 1)(x + 1)) = P/(4x + 1) + Q/(x + 1).\n\nThen 1 = P(x + 1) + Q(4x + 1).\n\nx = −1: 1 = Q(−3) ⇒ Q = −1/3\nx = −¼: 1 = P(¾) ⇒ P = 4/3\n\nSo 1/((4x + 1)(x + 1)) = 4/(3(4x + 1)) − 1/(3(x + 1))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::10(b)": "Separate the variables:\n∫ (1/y) dy = ∫ 1/((4x + 1)(x + 1)) dx\n\nUsing the partial fractions from (a):\n∫ (1/y) dy = ∫ [4/(3(4x + 1)) − 1/(3(x + 1))] dx\n\nln|y| = ⅓ ln|4x + 1| − ⅓ ln|x + 1| + c\n(since ∫ 4/(4x + 1) dx = ln|4x + 1|)\n\nAt (0, 2): ln 2 = ⅓ ln 1 − ⅓ ln 1 + c ⇒ c = ln 2\n\nFor x > −¼, both 4x + 1 and x + 1 are positive, so\nln|y| = ⅓ ln((4x + 1)/(x + 1)) + ln 2\nln|y| = ln[2((4x + 1)/(x + 1))^(1/3)]\n|y| = 2((4x + 1)/(x + 1))^(1/3)\n\nThis is never zero, so y keeps the same sign as at (0, 2), where y = 2 > 0. Hence\n\ny = 2((4x + 1)/(x + 1))^(1/3)\n\nwhich is of the form y = A((4x + 1)/(x + 1))^B with A = 2 and B = 1/3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::11": "In triangle BDC: BD = 7, DC = 5, BC = 8. Cosine rule for angle BDC:\ncos BDC = (7² + 5² − 8²)/(2 × 7 × 5)\n= (49 + 25 − 64)/70\n= 10/70 = 1/7\n\nsin BDC = √(1 − (1/7)²) = √(48/49) = √48/7 = 4√3/7\n(positive, as BDC is an angle of a triangle)\n\nA, D and C lie on a straight line, so angle ADB = 180° − angle BDC, and therefore\nsin ADB = sin BDC = √48/7\n\nIn triangle ABD, use the sine rule with angle BAD = 45° opposite BD = 7 and angle ADB opposite AB:\nAB/sin ADB = BD/sin 45°\nAB/(√48/7) = 7/(1/√2)\nAB = (√48/7) × 7√2\nAB = √48 × √2 = √96\n\nAB = 4√6 cm",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::12": "From Fig. C1, tan α = ½ and α + β = arctan 1, so tan(α + β) = 1.\n\nUsing tan(α + β) = (tan α + tan β)/(1 − tan α tan β):\n(½ + tan β)/(1 − ½ tan β) = 1\n\n½ + tan β = 1 − ½ tan β\n(3/2) tan β = ½\ntan β = (½)/(3/2) = ⅓\n\nβ is an acute angle (it is part of the 45° angle BAC), so\n\nβ = arctan(⅓), as given in line 15.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::13(a)": "Triangle ABE is right-angled at B, with AB = 1 cm and BE = x cm.\n\nAngle BAE = θ, and tan θ = BE/AB = x/1 = x, so θ = arctan x.\n\nThe angles of the triangle add up to π, so angle BEA = π − π/2 − θ = π/2 − θ.\n\nAlso, in the same triangle, tan(angle BEA) = AB/BE = 1/x, and angle BEA is acute, so angle BEA = arctan(1/x).\n\nHence arctan(1/x) = π/2 − θ = π/2 − arctan x, i.e.\n\narctan x + arctan(1/x) = π/2, as given in line 29.\n\n(This uses x > 0; a right-angled triangle with shorter sides 1 and x exists for every positive x, so the result holds for any positive x.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::13(b)": "Sketch (described): axes x (horizontal) and y (vertical). The curve y = arctan x passes through the origin, where its gradient is 1. It is increasing for all x, with rotational symmetry about the origin (an S-shape).\nAs x → ∞, y → π/2 from below (horizontal asymptote y = π/2).\nAs x → −∞, y → −π/2 from above (horizontal asymptote y = −π/2).\nThe curve lies entirely between these two asymptotes and never reaches them.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::13(c)": "arctan is an increasing function: if y > 1/x then arctan y > arctan(1/x).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::14(a)": "Put n = 1 into the identity:\n1/(n + 1) = 1/2\n1/(n² + n + 1) = 1/(1 + 1 + 1) = 1/3\n1/n = 1/1 = 1\n\nSo the identity with n = 1 reads arctan(½) + arctan(⅓) = arctan 1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::14(b)": "Apply the formula in line 23 with x = 1/(n + 1) and y = 1/(n² + n + 1).\n\nCondition: for a positive integer n, (n + 1)(n² + n + 1) ≥ 2 × 3 = 6, so\nxy = 1/((n + 1)(n² + n + 1)) ≤ 1/6 < 1,\nand the formula applies.\n\narctan(1/(n + 1)) + arctan(1/(n² + n + 1))\n= arctan( [1/(n + 1) + 1/(n² + n + 1)] / [1 − 1/((n + 1)(n² + n + 1))] )\n\nMultiply the top and bottom of the inner fraction by (n + 1)(n² + n + 1):\n= arctan( [(n² + n + 1) + (n + 1)] / [(n + 1)(n² + n + 1) − 1] )\n\nNumerator: n² + 2n + 2\nDenominator: (n + 1)(n² + n + 1) − 1 = n³ + 2n² + 2n + 1 − 1 = n³ + 2n² + 2n = n(n² + 2n + 2)\n\n= arctan( (n² + 2n + 2)/(n(n² + 2n + 2)) )\n= arctan(1/n)   (n² + 2n + 2 > 0, so it cancels)\n\nHence arctan(1/(n + 1)) + arctan(1/(n² + n + 1)) = arctan(1/n), as given in line 39.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2021n-p3::15": "Use arctan x + arctan(1/x) = π/2 (line 29), valid for positive x:\nwith x = 2: arctan 2 + arctan(½) = π/2\nwith x = 3: arctan 3 + arctan(⅓) = π/2\n\nAdding these:\narctan(½) + arctan(⅓) + arctan 2 + arctan 3 = π\n\nNow use arctan(½) + arctan(⅓) = arctan 1 (the result shown using Fig. C1 in lines 7–16):\narctan 1 + arctan 2 + arctan 3 = π, as given in line 41.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::1(a)": "The particle moves in the positive direction from t = 0 to t = 10, then back in the negative direction from t = 10 to t = 15, so add the distances for each section.\n\n0 ≤ t ≤ 10: s goes from −2 to 5, distance = 5 − (−2) = 7 m (2 m to reach s = 0, then 5 m more)\n10 ≤ t ≤ 15: s goes from 5 to 1, distance = 5 − 1 = 4 m\n\nTotal distance = 2 + 5 + 4 = 11 m",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::1(b)": "Velocity is the gradient of the displacement–time graph.\n\nFor 10 < t < 15:\nv = (1 − 5)/(15 − 10) = −4/5\n\nv = −0.8 m s⁻¹\n(the particle moves at 0.8 m s⁻¹ in the negative direction)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::2": "Let (13 − x)/((x − 3)(x + 2)) ≡ A/(x − 3) + B/(x + 2)\n\nMultiply through by (x − 3)(x + 2):\n13 − x ≡ A(x + 2) + B(x − 3)\n\nx = 3: 10 = 5A ⇒ A = 2\nx = −2: 15 = −5B ⇒ B = −3\n\nSo (13 − x)/((x − 3)(x + 2)) = 2/(x − 3) − 3/(x + 2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::3(a)": "Sketch of y = arctan x:\n\n• The curve passes through the origin O and is increasing everywhere (one-to-one), with an S-shape: steepest at O (gradient 1 there), then flattening out.\n• As x → ∞, y → π/2 from below; as x → −∞, y → −π/2 from above.\n• Draw dashed horizontal asymptotes y = π/2 and y = −π/2 and label them; the curve approaches them but never reaches them.\n• The curve has rotational symmetry about O (arctan(−x) = −arctan x).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::3(b)": "The curves intersect where\n3 sin x cos x = cos²x\n3 sin x cos x − cos²x = 0\ncos x (3 sin x − cos x) = 0\n\nSo either cos x = 0 or 3 sin x = cos x.\n\nCase 1: cos x = 0\nFor −π ≤ x ≤ π: x = −π/2 or x = π/2.\nThen y = cos²x = 0, giving the points (−π/2, 0) and (π/2, 0).\n\nCase 2: 3 sin x = cos x\nHere cos x ≠ 0 (otherwise sin x = 0 too, which is impossible), so divide by 3 cos x:\ntan x = ⅓\nx = arctan(⅓) = 0.32175… ≈ 0.322\nThe other solution in −π ≤ x ≤ π is x = 0.32175… − π = −2.8198… ≈ −2.82\n\nFor these values, tan x = ⅓ so\ncos²x = 1/(1 + tan²x) = 1/(1 + 1/9) = 9/10\nso y = cos²x = 0.9 exactly (check: 3 sin x cos x = 3 tan x cos²x = 3 × ⅓ × 0.9 = 0.9).\n\nThe points of intersection are:\n(−π/2, 0), (π/2, 0), (0.322, 0.9) and (−2.82, 0.9)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::4": "First take out the factor of 2 so the binomial expansion of (1 + kx)ⁿ can be used:\n1/(2 + x)² = (2 + x)⁻² = 2⁻²(1 + x/2)⁻² = ¼(1 + x/2)⁻²\n\nExpand (1 + x/2)⁻² (valid for |x| < 2):\n(1 + x/2)⁻² = 1 + (−2)(x/2) + ((−2)(−3)/2!)(x/2)² + …\n= 1 − x + 3(x²/4) + …\n= 1 − x + ¾x² + …\n\nSo\n(1 − x)/(2 + x)² ≈ ¼(1 − x)(1 − x + ¾x²)\n\nMultiply out, keeping terms up to x²:\n(1 − x)(1 − x + ¾x²) = 1 − x + ¾x² − x + x² + … = 1 − 2x + (7/4)x² + …\n\nTherefore\n(1 − x)/(2 + x)² ≈ ¼(1 − 2x + (7/4)x²) = ¼ − ½x + (7/16)x²\nas required (for sufficiently small x).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::5(a)": "The three forces are in equilibrium, so drawn nose-to-tail they form a closed triangle.\n\nTriangle of forces (described):\n• Draw the weight (3g N) as a vertical arrow pointing downwards.\n• From the tip of the weight arrow, draw the tension arrow pointing upwards and to the right, at 25° to the vertical.\n• From the tip of the tension arrow, draw the force F N as a horizontal arrow pointing to the left, finishing at the start of the weight arrow.\n\nThe arrows go round the triangle in the same sense, closing the loop. It is a right-angled triangle: the right angle is between the weight and F, and the 25° angle is between the weight side and the tension side (so the angle between tension and F is 65°). Label the sides Weight, Tension and F N, and mark the 25° angle.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::5(b)": "From the right-angled triangle of forces, with the weight 3g = 29.4 N as the side adjacent to the 25° angle:\n\nTension:\nT cos 25° = 3g\nT = 3 × 9.8/cos 25° = 29.4/0.90631 = 32.44…\nTension = 32.4 N\n\nF:\nF = 3g tan 25° = 29.4 × 0.46631 = 13.70…\nF = 13.7\n\n(Equivalently, resolving: vertically T cos 25° = 3g; horizontally F = T sin 25° = 13.7.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::6(a)": "• Stack of bricks modelled as a particle: the stack has negligible size, so the force it exerts on the plank (equal in size to its weight) acts at a single point on the plank.\n• Plank modelled as uniform: the mass of the plank is spread evenly along its length, so its weight acts at the centre of the plank (0.4 m from A).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::6(b)": "Let there be n bricks at the centre. By symmetry the two string tensions are equal, each T N.\n\nResolving vertically:\n2T = 5g + 2.3ng\n\nThe strings do not break provided T ≤ 75, so\n5g + 2.3ng ≤ 2 × 75 = 150\n2.3ng ≤ 150 − 49 = 101\nn ≤ 101/(2.3 × 9.8) = 101/22.54 = 4.48…\n\nn must be a whole number, so the greatest number of bricks is 4.\n(Check: 4 bricks give T = (49 + 90.16)/2 = 69.58 N ≤ 75; 5 bricks give T = 80.85 N > 75.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::6(c)": "Weight of the stack of n bricks = 2.3n × g = 2.3gn N, acting x m from A.\n\nMoment about A = force × perpendicular distance\n= 2.3gnx (= 22.54nx)\n\nUnits: N m",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::6(d)": "From (b), the largest stack is 4 bricks, with weight 4 × 2.3g = 9.2g N.\n\nAs the stack is moved further from A towards B, the tension in the string at B increases, so the greatest distance is when the tension at B is 75 N (on the point of breaking).\n\nLet the stack be x m from A. Taking moments about A (tension at B acts 0.8 m from A, plank's weight 0.4 m from A):\n75 × 0.8 = 5g × 0.4 + 9.2g × x\n60 = 2g + 9.2gx\n9.2gx = 60 − 2g = 60 − 19.6 = 40.4\nx = 40.4/(9.2 × 9.8) = 40.4/90.16 = 0.448…\n\n(Check: the tension at A is then 5g + 9.2g − 75 = 64.16 N, which is below 75 N, and 0.448 < 0.8 so the stack is on the plank.)\n\nGreatest distance from A = 0.448 m (44.8 cm)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::7(a)": "Take the initial position as (0, 5) and the acceleration as (0, −g) = (0, −9.8) m s⁻².\n\nHorizontally (no acceleration):\nx = 14t\n\nVertically (using s = ut + ½at², starting 5 m up):\ny = 5 + 7t − ½gt² = 5 + 7t − 4.9t²\n\nPosition vector at time t:\nr = (14t, 7t − 4.9t² + 5) m\ni.e. r = 14t i + (7t − 4.9t² + 5) j",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::7(b)": "The ball lands when y = 0:\n7t − 4.9t² + 5 = 0\n4.9t² − 7t − 5 = 0\n\nt = (7 ± √(49 + 4 × 4.9 × 5))/(2 × 4.9) = (7 ± √147)/9.8 = (7 ± 12.124…)/9.8\n\nt must be positive, so t = 19.124…/9.8 = 1.951… ≈ 1.95 s\n\nThen x = 14t = 14 × 1.951… = 27.32…\n\nThe ball lands 27.3 m from the origin.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::8(a)": "x = t³ − 8t ⇒ dx/dt = 3t² − 8\ny = t² ⇒ dy/dt = 2t\n\ndy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt)\n\ndy/dx = 2t/(3t² − 8)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::8(b)": "At P(8, 4):\ny = t² = 4 ⇒ t = 2 or t = −2\nx = t³ − 8t: t = 2 gives 8 − 16 = −8 (not 8); t = −2 gives −8 + 16 = 8 ✓\nSo at P, t = −2.\n\ndy/dx = 2(−2)/(3(−2)² − 8) = −4/(12 − 8) = −4/4 = −1\n\nSo the value of dy/dx at P is −1, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::8(c)": "Travelling in the opposite direction means the path again has gradient −1:\n2t/(3t² − 8) = −1\n2t = −3t² + 8\n3t² + 2t − 8 = 0\n(3t − 4)(t + 2) = 0\nt = 4/3 or t = −2\n\nt = −2 is the point P itself, so the required time is t = 4/3 s.\n\nCheck of direction: at t = −2 the velocity is (dx/dt, dy/dt) = (4, −4); at t = 4/3 it is (16/3 − 8, 8/3) = (−8/3, 8/3), which is a negative multiple of (4, −4), so the particle is indeed moving in the opposite direction.\n\nt = 4/3 s",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::8(d)": "From y = t², substitute t² = y.\n\nx = t³ − 8t = t(t² − 8)\nx² = t²(t² − 8)² = t⁶ − 16t⁴ + 64t²\n\nReplace t² by y:\nx² = y(y − 8)² = y³ − 16y² + 64y\n\nCartesian equation: x² = y³ − 16y² + 64y",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::9(a)": "v = kt² i + 6t j\n\na = dv/dt = 2kt i + 6 j\n\nWhen t = 2: a = 4k i + 6 j\n\n|a| = √((4k)² + 6²) = 10\n16k² + 36 = 100\n16k² = 64\nk² = 4\nk = 2 (since k > 0, reject k = −2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::9(b)": "With k = 2, v = 2t² i + 6t j.\n\nr = ∫ v dt = (2t³/3) i + 3t² j + c\n\nAt t = 0 the particle is at the origin, so 0 = 0 + c ⇒ c = 0.\n\nr = (2t³/3) i + 3t² j",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::9(c)": "The particle is directly north-east of the origin when its east (i) and north (j) components are equal (and positive).\n\n2t³/3 = 3t²\n2t³ = 9t²\nt²(2t − 9) = 0\nt = 0 or t = 4.5\n\nAt t = 0 the particle is at the origin itself, so reject it.\nAt t = 4.5 both components equal 60.75 > 0, so the particle is north-east of the origin.\n\nt = 4.5 s",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::10": "Let BC = l cm. By the cosine rule in triangle ABC:\nl² = 30² + 15² − 2 × 30 × 15 cos θ\nl² = 1125 − 900 cos θ\nl = (1125 − 900 cos θ)^½\n\nDifferentiate with respect to θ (chain rule):\ndl/dθ = ½(1125 − 900 cos θ)^(−½) × 900 sin θ = 450 sin θ/(1125 − 900 cos θ)^½\n\nGiven dθ/dt = 0.1 rad s⁻¹.\n\nBy the chain rule:\ndl/dt = dl/dθ × dθ/dt = (450 sin θ/(1125 − 900 cos θ)^½) × 0.1 = 45 sin θ/(1125 − 900 cos θ)^½\n\nWhen θ = π/3: sin θ = √3/2, cos θ = ½, so\n1125 − 900 × ½ = 675 and √675 = 15√3 (this is BC at that moment)\n\ndl/dt = 45 × (√3/2)/(15√3) = 45/30 = 3/2\n\nThe length BC is increasing at 1.5 cm s⁻¹ (= 0.015 m s⁻¹).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::11": "Let I = ∫ from k to 2k of 2/(2x + k)² dx.\n\nSubstitute u = 2x + k, so du/dx = 2, i.e. 2 dx = du.\nLimits: x = k ⇒ u = 3k; x = 2k ⇒ u = 5k.\n\nI = ∫ from 3k to 5k of 1/u² du\n= [−1/u] from 3k to 5k\n= −1/(5k) − (−1/(3k))\n= −1/(5k) + 1/(3k)\n= (−3 + 5)/(15k)\n= 2/(15k)\n\nSince I = (2/15) × (1/k), where 2/15 is a constant, the integral is inversely proportional to k (constant of proportionality 2/15).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::12": "First, 3 = 2² − 1 and 3 is prime, so 3 is a prime that is one less than a square number.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a prime p, with p ≠ 3, that is one less than a square number.\nThen p = n² − 1 for some positive integer n. Since p ≥ 2, n² ≥ 3, so n ≥ 2.\n\nFactorise:\np = n² − 1 = (n − 1)(n + 1)\n\nIf n = 2: p = 1 × 3 = 3, which contradicts p ≠ 3.\n\nIf n > 2: n − 1 ≥ 2 and n + 1 ≥ 4, so p is the product of two integers each greater than 1. Then n − 1 is a factor of p with 1 < n − 1 < p, so p is not prime. This contradicts p being prime.\n\nEither way the assumption leads to a contradiction, so there is no prime other than 3 that is one less than a square number. Hence 3 is the only prime number which is 1 less than a square number.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::13(a)": "Treat the whole train as one body of mass 0.5 + 0.4 = 0.9 kg (the coupling force is internal).\nResultant force forwards = 5 − 0.8 − 0.8 = 3.4 N.\n\nNewton's second law:\n5 − 2 × 0.8 = 0.9a\n3.4 = 0.9a\na = 34/9 = 3.78 m s⁻²\n\nFrom rest, using v = u + at with u = 0, t = 1.5:\nv = 0 + (34/9) × 1.5 = 17/3\n\nv = 5.67 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::13(b)": "Diagram (described): draw the track as a line inclined at 20° to the horizontal, with the engine (upper, front) and the coach (lower, behind it) drawn as two particles/blocks on it.\n\nForces on the engine (0.5 kg):\n• Weight 0.5g N, vertically downwards\n• Normal reaction N_E, perpendicular to the track, away from it\n• Tension in the string P N, parallel to the track, up the slope\n• Coupling tension T N, parallel to the track, down the slope (towards the coach)\n• Resistance R N, parallel to the track, down the slope\n\nForces on the coach (0.4 kg):\n• Weight 0.4g N, vertically downwards\n• Normal reaction N_C, perpendicular to the track, away from it\n• Coupling tension T N, parallel to the track, up the slope (towards the engine)\n• Resistance R N, parallel to the track, down the slope\n\nNo other forces act. Label the 20° angle between the track and the horizontal.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::13(c)": "As a single particle of mass 0.9 kg, the forces along the slope (taking up the slope as positive) are:\n• string tension P up the slope\n• total resistance 2R down the slope\n• component of weight 0.9g sin 20° down the slope\n(The coupling tension is internal and cancels; the normal reactions are perpendicular to the motion.)\n\nNewton's second law, with acceleration a m s⁻² up the slope:\nP − 2R − 0.9g sin 20° = 0.9a",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::13(d)": "Let the acceleration be a m s⁻² when P = 5, so it is 2a m s⁻² when P = 5.5.\n\nUsing the equation from (c):\nP = 5:   5 − 2R − 0.9g sin 20° = 0.9a       (1)\nP = 5.5: 5.5 − 2R − 0.9g sin 20° = 0.9 × 2a = 1.8a       (2)\n\n(2) − (1): 0.5 = 0.9a ⇒ a = 5/9\n\nSubstitute into (1):\n5 − 2R − 0.9 × 9.8 × sin 20° = 0.9 × 5/9 = 0.5\n5 − 2R − 3.0166… = 0.5\n2R = 1.4833…\nR = 0.742 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::14(a)": "When t = 0, θ = 82:\n82 = θ₀e⁰ ⇒ θ₀ = 82\n\nWhen t = 5, θ = 27:\n27 = 82e^(−5k)\ne^(−5k) = 27/82\n−5k = ln(27/82)\nk = −(1/5) ln(27/82) = (1/5) ln(82/27) = 0.2221…\n\nθ₀ = 82, k = 0.222 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::14(b)": "The model predicts that the temperature tends to 0 °C as t → ∞. But if the ice is not topped up, it melts and the water warms up, so the water will not stay at 0 °C and the object will not cool down to 0 °C; it will level off at a higher temperature, so the model is not suitable in the long term.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::14(c)": "θ = 82e^(−kt) with k = 0.222\n\nTake natural logs of both sides:\nln θ = ln(82e^(−kt)) = ln 82 + ln(e^(−kt)) = ln 82 − kt\n\nSo ln θ = ln 82 − 0.222t, which is of the form ln θ = a − bt with\na = ln 82 = 4.41 (3 s.f.) and b = k = 0.222\n\nln θ = 4.41 − 0.222t",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::14(d)": "Initial temperature: when t = 0, ln θ = 3.4\nθ = e^3.4 = 29.96… ≈ 30.0 °C\n\nRate of cooling: from ln θ = 3.4 − 0.08t,\nθ = e^(3.4 − 0.08t) = 29.96e^(−0.08t)\n\ndθ/dt = 29.96 × (−0.08)e^(−0.08t) = −0.08 × 29.96e^(−0.08t)\n\nAt t = 0:\ndθ/dt = −0.08 × 29.96 = −2.397 (≈ −2.40)\n\nBen's object is initially at 30.0 °C and is initially cooling at about 2.4 °C per minute.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p1::14(e)": "The temperatures are equal when the two values of ln θ are equal:\nAlex: ln θ = ln 82 − 0.222t\nBen: ln θ = 3.4 − 0.08t\n\nln 82 − 0.222t = 3.4 − 0.08t\nln 82 − 3.4 = (0.222 − 0.08)t\n4.4067 − 3.4 = 0.142t\nt = 1.0067/0.142 = 7.09 (using the unrounded k = 0.22218, t = 7.08)\n\nSo t ≈ 7.1 minutes (about 7 minutes 5 seconds).\n\nThen ln θ = 3.4 − 0.08 × 7.09 = 2.833\nθ = e^2.833 = 17.0\n\nBoth objects have the same temperature, 17 °C, after about 7.1 minutes.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::1": "R cos(θ − α) = R cos θ cos α + R sin θ sin α.\nCompare with cos θ + √3 sin θ:\nR cos α = 1 and R sin α = √3.\n\nSquare and add:\nR² = 1² + (√3)² = 1 + 3 = 4, so R = 2.\n\nDivide:\ntan α = √3/1 = √3. Since cos α and sin α are both positive, α is acute, so α = π/3 (60°).\n\nCheck: 2cos(θ − π/3) = 2(½ cos θ + (√3/2) sin θ) = cos θ + √3 sin θ ✓\n\ncos θ + √3 sin θ = 2cos(θ − π/3), with R = 2 and α = π/3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::2": "This is a geometric series with first term a = 50 and common ratio r = 25/50 = 0.5.\nSince |r| < 1, the sum to infinity exists:\n\nS∞ = a/(1 − r) = 50/(1 − 0.5) = 50/0.5 = 100\n\nSum = 100",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::3(a)": "y = 3 × 0.4ˣ is an exponential curve with base 0.4 < 1, so it is decreasing.\n\nKey features of the sketch:\n• When x = 0, y = 3 × 1 = 3, so the curve crosses the y-axis at (0, 3).\n• It lies entirely above the x-axis (y > 0 for all x) and is concave up.\n• As x → ∞, y → 0: the x-axis is an asymptote, and the curve never touches it.\n• As x → −∞, y increases without limit.\n\nSome points to help plot it:\nx = −0.5: y = 3 × 0.4^(−0.5) ≈ 4.74\nx = 1: y = 1.2\nx = 2: y = 0.48\nx = 2.5: y = 3 × 0.4^2.5 ≈ 0.30\n\nSketch: a smooth curve coming down steeply from the upper left (about (−0.5, 4.7)), passing through (0, 3), then flattening out and approaching the positive x-axis from above (about (2.5, 0.3)), never crossing it.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::3(b)": "3 × 0.4ˣ = 0.8\n\nTake logarithms of both sides:\nlog(3 × 0.4ˣ) = log 0.8\nlog 3 + x log 0.4 = log 0.8\nx log 0.4 = log 0.8 − log 3\n\nx = (log 0.8 − log 3)/log 0.4\n= log(0.8/3)/log 0.4\n= (−0.57403…)/(−0.39794…)   (using log₁₀)\n= 1.4425…\n\nx = 1.44 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::4(a)": "Let J = has a part-time job, S = plays competitive sport.\n\nVenn diagram description:\n• Draw a rectangle (all students) containing two overlapping circles, labelled J and S.\n• In the part of J not overlapping S (job only): 0.31\n• In the part of S not overlapping J (sport only): 0.23\n• Outside both circles, inside the rectangle (neither): 0.22\n• The overlap (job and sport) is found in part (b): 0.24",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::4(b)": "All the probabilities in the Venn diagram add up to 1:\n\n0.31 + 0.23 + P(J ∩ S) + 0.22 = 1\nP(J ∩ S) = 1 − 0.22 − 0.31 − 0.23 = 0.24\n\nP(plays competitive sport and has a part-time job) = 0.24",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::5": "Try odd values of n greater than 1:\n\nn = 3: 2³ − 1 = 7 (prime)\nn = 5: 2⁵ − 1 = 31 (prime)\nn = 7: 2⁷ − 1 = 127 (prime)\nn = 9: 2⁹ − 1 = 512 − 1 = 511\n\n511 = 7 × 73, so 511 is not prime.\n\nSo n = 9 is odd and greater than 1, but 2⁹ − 1 = 511 is not prime. This counter-example disproves Tom's conjecture.\n\n(Other counter-examples also work, e.g. 2¹¹ − 1 = 2047 = 23 × 89.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::6": "The Normal curve is bell-shaped and symmetrical about x = μ.\n\nThe lines x = μ − σ and x = μ + σ pass through the two points of inflection of the curve (where it changes from concave down near the peak to concave up in the tails).\n\nSketch: shade the region under the curve from x = μ − σ to x = μ + σ, i.e. the central part of the bell, symmetrical about the mean, with its vertical edges at the points of inflection on each side.\n\n(This shaded area is about 68% of the total area under the curve.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::7": "In a histogram, frequency = frequency density × class width.\n\n0–20 g: 0.4 × 20 = 8\n20–30 g: 1.3 × 10 = 13\n30–35 g: 3.6 × 5 = 18\n35–45 g: 2.0 × 10 = 20\n45–60 g: 0.8 × 15 = 12\n\nTotal = 8 + 13 + 18 + 20 + 12 = 71\n\nKareem bought 71 tomatoes.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::8(a)": "It is a population.\n\nAli is investigating the members of the club who rode at least 120 km, and she used all of the distances for these members, so every member of the group being studied is included.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::8(b)": "The total cumulative frequency is 58, so 58 cyclists are included.\n\nThe 8 team members are the 8 who rode furthest, so the shortest distance ridden by a team member is where the cumulative frequency is about 58 − 8 = 50.\n\nReading across from a cumulative frequency of 50 to the curve and down to the distance axis gives about 162 km.\n\nShortest distance ridden by a team member ≈ 162 km",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::8(c)": "Yes, the diagram gives sufficient evidence to support Ali's complaint.\n\nAt 156 km the cumulative frequency is about 44, so about 58 − 44 = 14 cyclists rode 156 km or more. The captain's friend was therefore only about the 14th furthest, not the 9th.\n\nThe reserve should be the 9th furthest rider: the cumulative frequency is 58 − 9 = 49 at about 161 km, so the reserve should have ridden about 161 km. Several cyclists rode further than 156 km but were not chosen, so Ali's complaint is justified.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::9(a)": "Mean:\nx̄ = Σx/n = 23042/205 = 112.4\n\nVariance:\nSxx = Σx² − (Σx)²/n = 2591716 − 23042²/205 = 2591716 − 2589920.8 = 1795.2\n\ns² = Sxx/(n − 1) = 1795.2/204 = 8.8\n\nMean = 112.4, variance = 8.8",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::9(b)": "Using the mean and variance from part (a), a possible model for the mark X is\n\nX ~ N(112.4, 8.8)\n\n(mean 112.4, variance 8.8, so standard deviation √8.8 ≈ 2.97).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::9(c)": "Let X ~ N(112.4, 8.8). Marks are whole numbers, so \"less than 105\" means a mark of 104 or below; using a continuity correction this is X < 104.5.\n\nP(X < 104.5) = 0.003871… ≈ 0.00387\n\nExpected number of candidates scoring less than 105 out of 205:\n205 × 0.00387 ≈ 0.79\n\nThe model predicts about 0.79 candidates (roughly 1) below 105, and in the cohort exactly one candidate scored below 105.\n\nSo the model is consistent with this information.\n\n(Alternatively: the proportion observed is 1/205 ≈ 0.00488, which is similar to the model probability 0.00387.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::9(d)": "Using X ~ N(112.4, 8.8) with a continuity correction, a mark of 115 corresponds to 114.5 < X < 115.5.\n\nP(114.5 < X < 115.5) = 0.09149…\n\nExpected number of candidates = 205 × 0.09149… = 18.76\n\nEstimate: about 19 candidates scored 115 marks (18.8 to 3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::10(a)": "x = 2 + 5 cos θ ⇒ x − 2 = 5 cos θ\ny = 1 + 5 sin θ ⇒ y − 1 = 5 sin θ\n\nSquare and add:\n(x − 2)² + (y − 1)² = 25cos²θ + 25sin²θ = 25(cos²θ + sin²θ) = 25\n\nCartesian equation: (x − 2)² + (y − 1)² = 25\n\n(This is a circle with centre (2, 1) and radius 5.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::10(b)": "The curve is a circle with centre (2, 1). Check (5, −3) lies on it: (5 − 2)² + (−3 − 1)² = 9 + 16 = 25 ✓\n\nGradient of the radius from (2, 1) to (5, −3):\n(−3 − 1)/(5 − 2) = −4/3\n\nThe tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is\n−1 ÷ (−4/3) = 3/4\n\nEquation of the tangent through (5, −3):\ny + 3 = ¾(x − 5)\n4y + 12 = 3x − 15\n3x − 4y − 27 = 0\n\nSo a = 3, b = −4, c = −27.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::11(a)": "Nina's graph.\n\nShe rolled the die the most times (120), and the larger the number of rolls, the closer the relative frequencies are likely to be to the true probabilities.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::11(b)": "The probabilities must sum to 1:\n\n11p + kp = 1\np(11 + k) = 1\np = 1/(11 + k)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::11(c)": "P(X = 12) = kp = k/(11 + k)\n\nNina rolls the die 120 times, so the expected number of 12s is\n\n120 × k/(11 + k) = 120k/(11 + k)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::11(d)": "Set the expected number of 12s equal to the observed number:\n\n120k/(11 + k) = 32\n120k = 32(11 + k)\n120k = 352 + 32k\n88k = 352\nk = 4\n\nEstimate: k = 4",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::11(e)": "With k = 4: P(12) = kp = 4/(11 + 4) = 4/15\n\nLet Y be the number of 12s in the 30 further rolls. Then Y ~ B(30, 4/15).\n\nP(Y = 8) = ³⁰C₈ (4/15)⁸ (11/15)²²\n= 0.1628…\n\nP(exactly 8 twelves) ≈ 0.163",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::12(a)": "H₀: μ = 1.5\nH₁: μ < 1.5\n\nwhere μ is the population mean mass (in kg) of flour in a bag.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::12(b)": "Under H₀, the sample mean X̄ of 32 bags has distribution\n\nX̄ ~ N(1.5, 0.24²/32)\n\ni.e. X̄ ~ N(1.5, 0.0018), with standard error √0.0018 ≈ 0.0424 as shown in the printout.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::12(c)": "From the printout, the test statistic is z = −1.4142 and the p-value is 0.0786. This p-value is one-tailed: it is P(X̄ ≤ 1.44) under H₀, matching H₁: μ < 1.5.\n\nCompare with the 5% significance level:\n0.0786 > 0.05\n(equivalently, −1.4142 > −1.645, the 5% critical value for a lower-tail test)\n\nThe result is not significant, so do not reject H₀.\n\nThere is insufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the mean mass of flour in the bags is less than 1.5 kg.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::13(a)": "No, the statement cannot be fully justified.\n\nThe result comes from a single sample. Even if the proportion of babies born without wisdom teeth were still 0.35, a sample proportion of 61/140 (about 0.44) could have arisen by chance, and a different sample could give a different result. A sample can give evidence of an increase but cannot show it for certain.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::13(b)": "Let p be the probability that a randomly selected baby is born without wisdom teeth.\n\nH₀: p = 0.35\nH₁: p > 0.35   (one-tailed test, 5% level)\n\nLet X be the number of babies, out of 140, born without wisdom teeth.\nUnder H₀, X ~ B(140, 0.35).\n\nObserved value: X = 61.\n\np-value:\nP(X ≥ 61) = 1 − P(X ≤ 60) = 0.0220 (3 s.f.)\n\nCompare with the significance level:\n0.0220 < 0.05\n\n(Equivalently: P(X ≥ 59) = 0.0475 < 0.05 but P(X ≥ 58) = 0.0673 > 0.05, so the critical region is X ≥ 59, and 61 lies in it.)\n\nThe result is significant, so reject H₀.\n\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the probability that a baby is born without wisdom teeth is more than 0.35.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::14(a)": "The curve y = 1/(1 + x²) is decreasing for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, so on each strip the smallest value of y is at the right-hand end. The rectangles in Fig. 14.1 use these right-hand heights, so they lie under the curve and their total area is a lower bound.\n\nEach rectangle has width 0.2. Heights are the y-values at B, C, D, E, F:\n\nLower bound = 0.2 × (0.96154 + 0.86207 + 0.73529 + 0.60976 + 0.5)\n= 0.2 × 3.66866\n= 0.733732\n= 0.7337 (4 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::14(b)": "For an upper bound, use the largest value of y on each strip, which is at the left-hand end (since the curve is decreasing). Heights are the y-values at A, B, C, D, E:\n\nUpper bound = 0.2 × (1 + 0.96154 + 0.86207 + 0.73529 + 0.60976)\n= 0.2 × 4.16866\n= 0.833732\n= 0.8337 (4 d.p.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::14(c)": "From (a) and (b): 0.7337 < ∫₀¹ 1/(1 + x²) dx < 0.8337\n\nLength of interval = 0.833732 − 0.733732 = 0.1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::14(d)": "The best information is the largest lower bound and the smallest upper bound. Both come from n = 40:\n\n0.77912 < ∫₀¹ 1/(1 + x²) dx < 0.79162\n\nLength of the smallest interval = 0.79162 − 0.77912 = 0.0125",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::14(e)": "Amit should increase n, i.e. use more rectangles, each of smaller width 1/n (for example n = 80). The upper and lower bounds then get closer together, giving a shorter interval containing the integral.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(a)": "L = 0.473y − 892\n\nWith y = 1995:\nL = 0.473 × 1995 − 892 = 943.635 − 892 = 51.635\n\nEstimated life expectancy at birth in Liberia in 1995 ≈ 51.6 years",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(b)": "1995 estimate: probably reliable. 1995 lies within the range of the data (1960 to 2010), so this is interpolation, and the data follow a roughly linear trend over this range.\n\n2025 estimate: probably not reliable. 2025 is outside the range of the data, so this is extrapolation, and there is no guarantee that the linear trend continues beyond 2010.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(c)": "No. The model was fitted to Liberia's data only. Trends in life expectancy at birth vary considerably between countries (for example, Fig. 15.2 shows Italy and South Africa following very different patterns), so the line for Liberia would not give reliable estimates for other countries.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(d)": "Italy: Series 2 (the upper, dashed line) represents Italy, because life expectancy at birth is generally higher in European countries than in African countries. Series 2 rises from about 69 to about 82 years, while Series 1 stays between about 53 and 62 years.\n\nSouth Africa (Series 1): life expectancy rises from about 53 years in 1960 to about 62 years in 1990, then falls to about 57 years by 2010. This differs from most developed countries, where life expectancy shows a steady upward trend (like Italy's), and South Africa's life expectancy is also much lower.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(e)": "Circle the isolated point at the far left of the diagram, with GDP per capita close to 0 (about $700) and life expectancy at birth about 47.6 years (47.56).\n\nThis is the Central African Republic: it is far below all the European countries, which all have life expectancy of about 70 years or more and much higher GDP per capita.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::15(f)": "Ignoring the Central African Republic point (which is not a European country):\n\n• For GDP per capita from about $5 000 up to about $50 000, there appears to be positive correlation: countries with higher GDP per capita tend to have higher life expectancy (rising from about 70 to about 81 years). So Fig. 15.3 supports Sundip's statement for this range.\n\n• For GDP per capita above about $50 000, life expectancy stays at roughly 80–82 years whatever the GDP per capita, so there appears to be no association. Fig. 15.3 does not support Sundip's statement for these values.\n\nSo the diagram supports the statement only for lower values of GDP per capita (up to about $50 000).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::16(a)": "y = 6x⁴ + 8x³ − 21x² + 12x − 6\n\ndy/dx = 24x³ + 24x² − 42x + 12 = 6(4x³ + 4x² − 7x + 2)\n\nStationary points: dy/dx = 0 ⇒ 4x³ + 4x² − 7x + 2 = 0.\n\nLet h(x) = 4x³ + 4x² − 7x + 2. Using the factor theorem:\nh(−2) = −32 + 16 + 14 + 2 = 0, so (x + 2) is a factor.\n\n4x³ + 4x² − 7x + 2 = (x + 2)(4x² − 4x + 1) = (x + 2)(2x − 1)²\n\nSo dy/dx = 6(x + 2)(2x − 1)² = 0 ⇒ x = −2 or x = ½ (repeated), and no other values.\n\ny-coordinates:\nx = −2: y = 6(16) + 8(−8) − 21(4) + 12(−2) − 6 = 96 − 64 − 84 − 24 − 6 = −82\nx = ½: y = 6(1/16) + 8(1/8) − 21(1/4) + 12(½) − 6 = 3/8 + 1 − 21/4 + 6 − 6 = −31/8\n\nStationary points: (−2, −82) and (½, −31/8).\n\nNature:\nd²y/dx² = 72x² + 48x − 42\n\nAt x = −2: d²y/dx² = 288 − 96 − 42 = 150 > 0, so (−2, −82) is a minimum.\n\nAt x = ½: d²y/dx² = 18 + 24 − 42 = 0, so the second derivative test is inconclusive. Check the gradient on either side:\nx = 0: dy/dx = 12 > 0\nx = ½: dy/dx = 0\nx = 1: dy/dx = 24 + 24 − 42 + 12 = 18 > 0\n(Indeed dy/dx = 6(x + 2)(2x − 1)² ≥ 0 for all x > −2.)\nThe gradient is positive on both sides, so (½, −31/8) is a stationary point of inflection.\n\nNon-stationary point of inflection: d²y/dx² = 0\n72x² + 48x − 42 = 0\n12x² + 8x − 7 = 0\n(6x + 7)(2x − 1) = 0\nx = −7/6 or x = ½\n\nx = ½ is the stationary point of inflection. At x = −7/6, d²y/dx² changes sign (it is a simple root) and dy/dx = 6(5/6)(−10/3)² ≠ 0, so this is the non-stationary point of inflection.\n\nx-coordinate of the non-stationary point of inflection: x = −7/6",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p2::16(b)": "Features of y = 6x⁴ + 8x³ − 21x² + 12x − 6 to show on the sketch:\n• Positive x⁴ coefficient: y → +∞ as x → ±∞ (both ends point upwards).\n• Minimum point at (−2, −82), in the third quadrant.\n• Stationary point of inflection at (½, −31/8) = (0.5, −3.875), in the fourth quadrant.\n• Non-stationary point of inflection at x = −7/6 (y ≈ −50.2), where the curve changes from concave up to concave down.\n• y-intercept: (0, −6).\n• x-intercepts: the curve crosses the x-axis twice, at about x = −2.85 (between −3 and −2.6) and about x = 1.05 (between 0.8 and 1.2).\n\nSketch description: coming down steeply from the upper left, the curve crosses the negative x-axis at about x = −2.85, falls to its minimum at (−2, −82), then rises, bending at x = −7/6, through (0, −6) and levels off to a horizontal point of inflection at (0.5, −3.875) just below the x-axis. It then rises steeply again, crossing the positive x-axis at about x = 1.05. There are no other stationary points.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::1": "y is inversely proportional to x, so the curve has equation\ny = k/x for some constant k.\n\nThe curve passes through (1, 2):\n2 = k/1 ⇒ k = 2\n\nCheck with another point on the graph: at x = 2, y = 2/2 = 1, and at x = −1, y = 2/(−1) = −2, both as shown.\n\nEquation of the curve: y = 2/x",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::2(a)": "fg(x) means apply g first, then f:\nfg(x) = f(25 − x²)\n\nfg(x) = √(25 − x²)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::2(b)(i)": "f is only defined for inputs ≥ 0, so the output of g must be ≥ 0:\n25 − x² ≥ 0\nx² ≤ 25\n−5 ≤ x ≤ 5\n\nDomain of fg(x): −5 ≤ x ≤ 5",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::2(b)(ii)": "For −5 ≤ x ≤ 5, the value of 25 − x² runs from 0 (at x = ±5) up to 25 (at x = 0).\n\nSo fg(x) = √(25 − x²) takes every value from √0 = 0 up to √25 = 5.\n\nRange of fg(x): 0 ≤ fg(x) ≤ 5",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::3(a)": "aₙ = n/(n + 1) = 1 − 1/(n + 1)\n\nAs n → ∞, 1/(n + 1) → 0, so aₙ gets arbitrarily close to 1.\n\nThe limit of the sequence is 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::3(b)": "Write the general term as\naₙ = n/(n + 1) = ((n + 1) − 1)/(n + 1) = 1 − 1/(n + 1)\n\nAs n increases, n + 1 increases, so 1/(n + 1) decreases (it is positive and gets smaller).\n\nSo each term is 1 minus a smaller positive amount than the term before: less is being taken away from 1 each time.\n\nHence aₙ₊₁ > aₙ for every positive integer n, so the sequence is increasing.\n\n(Equivalently: aₙ₊₁ − aₙ = (n + 1)/(n + 2) − n/(n + 1) = ((n + 1)² − n(n + 2))/((n + 1)(n + 2)) = 1/((n + 1)(n + 2)) > 0.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::4": "2cos²x = 3 sin x\n\nUse cos²x = 1 − sin²x:\n2(1 − sin²x) = 3 sin x\n2 − 2sin²x = 3 sin x\n2sin²x + 3 sin x − 2 = 0\n\nFactorise:\n(2 sin x − 1)(sin x + 2) = 0\n\nsin x = ½ or sin x = −2\nsin x = −2 has no solutions, since −1 ≤ sin x ≤ 1.\n\nSo sin x = ½.\nPrincipal value: x = π/6\nsin is also positive in the second quadrant: x = π − π/6 = 5π/6\n\nThere are no other solutions in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π (sin x < 0 for π < x < 2π).\n\nSolutions: x = π/6, x = 5π/6",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::5(a)": "2x² + 3xy + y² + 2 = 0\n\nDifferentiate implicitly with respect to x (product rule on 3xy, chain rule on y²):\n4x + (3x dy/dx + 3y) + 2y dy/dx + 0 = 0\n\nCollect the dy/dx terms:\n3x dy/dx + 2y dy/dx = −4x − 3y\n(3x + 2y) dy/dx = −(4x + 3y)\n\ndy/dx = −(4x + 3y)/(3x + 2y), as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::5(b)": "At a stationary point dy/dx = 0, so the numerator is zero:\n4x + 3y = 0 ⇒ y = −4x/3\n\nSubstitute into the curve 2x² + 3xy + y² + 2 = 0:\n2x² + 3x(−4x/3) + (−4x/3)² + 2 = 0\n2x² − 4x² + 16x²/9 + 2 = 0\n(18x² − 36x² + 16x²)/9 + 2 = 0\n−2x²/9 + 2 = 0\nx² = 9\nx = 3 or x = −3\n\nx = 3: y = −4(3)/3 = −4\nx = −3: y = −4(−3)/3 = 4\n\nCheck the denominator is not zero: at (3, −4), 3x + 2y = 9 − 8 = 1; at (−3, 4), 3x + 2y = −9 + 8 = −1. So dy/dx = 0 genuinely at both points.\n\nStationary points: (3, −4) and (−3, 4)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::6(a)": "The temperature is decreasing at a rate proportional to (T − 20), so\n\ndT/dt = −k(T − 20), where k is a positive constant.\n\n(The minus sign shows the temperature is falling while T > 20.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::6(b)": "Using dT/dt = −k(T − 20).\n\nFind k: when t = 0, T = 90 and dT/dt = −4.9 (decreasing at 4.9 °C per minute):\n−4.9 = −k(90 − 20) = −70k\nk = 4.9/70 = 0.07\n\nSo dT/dt = −0.07(T − 20).\n\nSeparate the variables:\n∫ 1/(T − 20) dT = ∫ −0.07 dt\nln(T − 20) = −0.07t + c   (T > 20 throughout, so no modulus is needed)\n\nUse t = 0, T = 90:\nln 70 = c\n\nSo ln(T − 20) = −0.07t + ln 70\n\nSubstitute T = 40:\nln 20 = −0.07t + ln 70\n0.07t = ln 70 − ln 20 = ln(70/20) = ln 3.5\nt = ln 3.5/0.07 = 17.896…\n\nIt takes about 17.9 minutes for the drink to cool from 90 °C to 40 °C.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(a)": "x⁶ = (x³)² is a square, so x⁶ ≥ 0 for all x, and hence x⁶/8 ≥ 0 for all x (it equals 0 when x = 0).\n\nAdding x⁶/8 to 1 − x³/2 therefore never makes it smaller:\n1 − x³/2 + x⁶/8 ≥ 1 − x³/2 for all values of x.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(b)": "Adding a further correct term of a binomial expansion should make the approximation better (closer to √(1 − x³)) near x = 0.\n\nThe graphs show the opposite: the three-term curve y = 1 − x³/2 + x⁶/8 lies further from y = √(1 − x³) than the two-term curve y = 1 − x³/2 does (near x = 0 they almost coincide, but away from 0 the three-term curve moves away, for example turning sharply upwards after about x = 1.26 while the two-term curve keeps falling).\n\nSo the two-term version is a better approximation than the three-term version, which suggests the student's third term is wrong.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(c)": "Use (1 + u)ⁿ = 1 + nu + n(n − 1)u²/2! + n(n − 1)(n − 2)u³/3! + … with n = ½ and u = −x³.\n\n1st term: 1\n2nd term: ½(−x³) = −x³/2\n3rd term: (½)(−½)/2! × (−x³)² = (−¼)/2 × x⁶ = −x⁶/8\n4th term: (½)(−½)(−3/2)/3! × (−x³)³ = (3/8)/6 × (−x⁹) = (1/16)(−x⁹) = −x⁹/16\n\n√(1 − x³) = 1 − x³/2 − x⁶/8 − x⁹/16 + …\n\n(The third term is −x⁶/8, not +x⁶/8 as the student had.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(d)": "The expansion of (1 + u)^½ is valid for |u| < 1. Here u = −x³, so we need\n|−x³| < 1 ⇒ |x|³ < 1 ⇒ |x| < 1\n\nValid for |x| < 1, i.e. −1 < x < 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(e)": "Key features of y = 2.5√(1 − x³) for −1 ≤ x ≤ 1:\nAt x = 0: y = 2.5√1 = 2.5, so the curve crosses the y-axis at (0, 2.5).\nAt x = 1: y = 2.5√0 = 0, so the curve meets the x-axis at (1, 0).\nAt x = −1: y = 2.5√2 ≈ 3.54.\nOther values: x = −0.5, y ≈ 2.65; x = 0.5, y ≈ 2.34.\n\ndy/dx = 2.5 × (−3x²)/(2√(1 − x³)), which is ≤ 0, so the curve is decreasing throughout; it is momentarily flat at x = 0 and becomes vertical as it reaches (1, 0).\n\nSketch: starting at about (−1, 3.54), the curve falls gently, flattening out as it passes through (0, 2.5) (the same flat-then-falling shape as y = √(1 − x³) on the question paper, stretched vertically by factor 2.5), then drops ever more steeply to meet the x-axis vertically at (1, 0). The curve does not exist for x > 1.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::7(f)": "Area ≈ 2.5 ∫ from −0.75 to 0.75 of (1 − x³/2 − x⁶/8 − x⁹/16) dx\n(using the expansion from (c), valid since |x| ≤ 0.75 < 1)\n\nIntegrate:\n∫ (1 − x³/2 − x⁶/8 − x⁹/16) dx = x − x⁴/8 − x⁷/56 − x¹⁰/160\n\nSubstitute the limits:\nAt x = 0.75:\n0.75 − 0.75⁴/8 − 0.75⁷/56 − 0.75¹⁰/160 = 0.70771…\nAt x = −0.75:\n−0.75 − (−0.75)⁴/8 − (−0.75)⁷/56 − (−0.75)¹⁰/160 = −0.78752…\n\nDifference: 0.70771… − (−0.78752…) = 1.49523…\n\nArea ≈ 2.5 × 1.49523… = 3.738…\n\nArea of the end of the bus shelter ≈ 3.74 m²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::8(a)": "Let y = x³ − 8.\nRearrange for x:\nx³ = y + 8\nx = ∛(y + 8)\n\nSwap the roles of x and y:\nh⁻¹(x) = ∛(x + 8)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::8(b)": "A: y = h(x) meets the y-axis where x = 0: h(0) = 0 − 8 = −8, so A(0, −8).\n\nB: y = h(x) meets the x-axis where x³ − 8 = 0 ⇒ x³ = 8 ⇒ x = 2, so B(2, 0).\n\nC: y = h⁻¹(x) meets the y-axis where x = 0: h⁻¹(0) = ∛8 = 2, so C(0, 2).\n\nD: y = h⁻¹(x) meets the x-axis where ∛(x + 8) = 0 ⇒ x = −8, so D(−8, 0).\n\n(C and D are the reflections of B and A in y = x, as expected for an inverse function.)\n\nA(0, −8), B(2, 0), C(0, 2), D(−8, 0)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::8(c)": "A(0, −8), B(2, 0)\n\nMidpoint of AB: ((0 + 2)/2, (−8 + 0)/2) = (1, −4)\nGradient of AB: (0 − (−8))/(2 − 0) = 8/2 = 4\n\nThe perpendicular bisector has gradient −1/4 (negative reciprocal of 4) and passes through (1, −4):\ny + 4 = −¼(x − 1)\ny = −¼x + ¼ − 4\n\ny = −¼x − 3¾   (m = −¼, c = −15/4)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::8(d)": "The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of every chord.\n\nChord AB: from (c), the perpendicular bisector is y = −¼x − 15/4.\n\nChord CD: C(0, 2), D(−8, 0)\nMidpoint of CD: (−4, 1)\nGradient of CD: (2 − 0)/(0 − (−8)) = 2/8 = ¼\nPerpendicular gradient: −4\nPerpendicular bisector of CD: y − 1 = −4(x + 4) ⇒ y = −4x − 15\n\nIntersect the two bisectors:\n−¼x − 15/4 = −4x − 15\nMultiply by 4: −x − 15 = −16x − 60\n15x = −45 ⇒ x = −3\ny = −4(−3) − 15 = −3\n\nCentre (−3, −3), so a = −3 and b = −3.\n\nRadius² using C(0, 2):\nr² = (0 − (−3))² + (2 − (−3))² = 9 + 25 = 34\n\nCheck with A(0, −8): (3)² + (−5)² = 9 + 25 = 34 ✓ (B and D also give 25 + 9 = 34).\n\nEquation of the circle: (x + 3)² + (y + 3)² = 34",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::9": "For y = sin x:\ndy/dx = cos x\nAt x = 0: dy/dx = cos 0 = 1\n\nFor y = x the gradient is 1 everywhere, so at x = 0 its gradient is 1.\n\nBoth gradients equal 1 at x = 0, so y = x has the same gradient as y = sin x when x = 0, as stated in line 5.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::10": "y = 4x(π − x)/π² − sin x = 4x/π − 4x²/π² − sin x\n\nDifferentiate (π is a constant):\ndy/dx = 4/π − 8x/π² − cos x\n\nAt x = 2.6:\ndy/dx = 4/π − 8(2.6)/π² − cos 2.6 = 0.0226… (positive)\n\nAt x = 2.7:\ndy/dx = 4/π − 8(2.7)/π² − cos 2.7 = −0.0112… (negative)\n\ndy/dx is a continuous function and it changes sign between x = 2.6 and x = 2.7, so dy/dx = 0 for some x between 2.6 and 2.7. Hence the x-coordinate of the stationary point near x = 3 lies between 2.6 and 2.7.\n\nNature: as x increases through the stationary point, the gradient goes from positive (at 2.6) to zero to negative (at 2.7). So the curve rises then falls, and the stationary point is a maximum turning point.\n\n(Check with the second derivative: d²y/dx² = −8/π² + sin x, which is −0.295… at x = 2.6 and −0.383… at x = 2.7, and about −0.356 at the stationary point x ≈ 2.67; it is negative, confirming a maximum.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::11": "Degree formula (line 28) with θ = 45:\nsin 45° ≈ 4 × 45 × (180 − 45)/(40500 − 45 × (180 − 45))\n= 4 × 45 × 135/(40500 − 45 × 135)\n= 24300/(40500 − 6075)\n= 24300/34425\n= 12/17   (dividing top and bottom by 2025)\n\nRadian formula (line 23): 45° = π/4 radians, so x = π/4 and π − x = 3π/4.\nx(π − x) = (π/4)(3π/4) = 3π²/16\n\nsin(π/4) ≈ 16 × 3π²/16 ÷ (5π² − 4 × 3π²/16)\n= 3π²/(5π² − 3π²/4)\n= 3π²/(17π²/4)\n= 12/17\n\nBoth formulae give 12/17 (≈ 0.706) as the approximation for sin 45°, so they give the same approximation.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::12(a)": "Using sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B:\nsin(x + π/2) = sin x cos(π/2) + cos x sin(π/2)\n= sin x × 0 + cos x × 1\n= cos x\n\nSo cos x = sin(x + π/2).\n\n(Equivalently, y = sin(x + π/2) is the translation of y = sin x by π/2 to the left, which is the graph of y = cos x.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2022-p3::12(b)": "From (a), cos x = sin(x + π/2). Replace x by x + π/2 in Bhaskara's approximation sin x ≈ 16x(π − x)/(5π² − 4x(π − x)):\n\ncos x = sin(x + π/2) ≈ 16(x + π/2)(π − (x + π/2)) / (5π² − 4(x + π/2)(π − (x + π/2)))\n\nSince π − (x + π/2) = π/2 − x:\ncos x ≈ 16(x + π/2)(π/2 − x) / (5π² − 4(x + π/2)(π/2 − x))\n\nMultiply out (difference of two squares):\n(π/2 + x)(π/2 − x) = π²/4 − x²\n\ncos x ≈ 16(π²/4 − x²) / (5π² − 4(π²/4 − x²))\n= (4π² − 16x²) / (5π² − π² + 4x²)\n= (4π² − 16x²) / (4π² + 4x²)\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 4:\ncos x ≈ (π² − 4x²)/(π² + x²), as stated in line 31.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::1": "Take upwards as positive, with u = 8, a = −9.8 and s = 3.\ns = ut + ½at²\n3 = 8t − 4.9t²\n4.9t² − 8t + 3 = 0\nt = (8 ± √(64 − 4 × 4.9 × 3))/9.8 = (8 ± √5.2)/9.8\nExactly, t = (40 ± √130)/49.\nt = 0.584 s or t = 1.05 s (3 s.f.)\nThe ball is 3 m above the point of projection at 0.584 s (on the way up) and at 1.05 s (on the way down).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::2": "Factorise the denominator: x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3).\nWrite (5x + 1)/((x − 4)(x + 3)) = A/(x − 4) + B/(x + 3).\nMultiply through by (x − 4)(x + 3):\n5x + 1 = A(x + 3) + B(x − 4)\nSubstitute x = −3: −15 + 1 = B(−7), so −14 = −7B and B = 2.\nSubstitute x = 4: 20 + 1 = A(7), so 21 = 7A and A = 3.\n(5x + 1)/(x² − x − 12) = 3/(x − 4) + 2/(x + 3)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::3": "Write the integrand in index form: x√x = x¹ × x^(1/2) = x^(3/2).\n∫(2x⁴ − x^(3/2)) dx = 2x⁵/5 − x^(5/2)/(5/2) + c\n= 2x⁵/5 − 2x^(5/2)/5 + c",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::4(a)": "Work in kg and cm. The weight of the ruler is 0.02g N.\nThe ruler is uniform, so its weight acts through its centre, which is 15 cm horizontally from P (half of PQ = 30 cm).\nThe force F N acts horizontally at S, which is 4 cm vertically below P, so its perpendicular distance from P is 4 cm.\nThe hinge force acts at P, so it has no moment about P.\nMoments about P (the weight turns the ruler clockwise, F turns it anticlockwise):\n0.02g × 15 = F × 4\nF = (0.02g × 15)/4 = 3g/40 = 0.075g\nF = 0.735",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::4(b)": "With F removed, the only force with a moment about P is the weight, which acts 15 cm horizontally from P on the Q side. This moment is no longer balanced, so the ruler would rotate clockwise about P (end Q swinging downwards).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::5(a)": "y = 15 − x² − 16x⁻²\ndy/dx = −2x + 32x⁻³\nAt a stationary point dy/dx = 0:\n−2x + 32/x³ = 0\nMultiply by x³ (x ≠ 0): −2x⁴ + 32 = 0, so x⁴ = 16.\nThen x² = 4 (x² = −4 has no real solutions), so x = 2 or x = −2, and there are no other stationary points.\nWhen x = 2: y = 15 − 4 − 16/4 = 7.\nWhen x = −2: y = 15 − 4 − 16/4 = 7.\nThe stationary points are (2, 7) and (−2, 7).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::5(b)": "dy/dx = −2x + 32x⁻³\nd²y/dx² = −2 − 96x⁻⁴ = −2 − 96/x⁴\nWhen x = 2: d²y/dx² = −2 − 96/16 = −8 < 0.\nWhen x = −2: d²y/dx² = −2 − 96/16 = −8 < 0.\n(In fact x⁴ > 0 for every x ≠ 0, so d²y/dx² < 0 wherever it is defined.)\nSince d²y/dx² < 0 at both (2, 7) and (−2, 7), both stationary points are maximum points.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::6(a)": "Expand both sides using the compound angle formulae:\nsin(x + π/6) = sin x cos(π/6) + cos x sin(π/6)\ncos(x − π/4) = cos x cos(π/4) + sin x sin(π/4)\nUsing exact values, the equation becomes\n(√3/2) sin x + (1/2) cos x = (√2/2) cos x + (√2/2) sin x\nCollect the sin x terms on the left and the cos x terms on the right:\nsin x (√3/2 − √2/2) = cos x (√2/2 − 1/2)\nMultiply by 2:\nsin x (√3 − √2) = cos x (√2 − 1)\ncos x ≠ 0 here (if cos x = 0 then sin x = ±1 and the left side would be ±(√3 − √2) ≠ 0), so divide by cos x (√3 − √2):\ntan x = (√2 − 1)/(√3 − √2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::6(b)": "From (a), the equation is equivalent to tan x = (√2 − 1)/(√3 − √2) = 1.3032…\nThe principal value is x = 0.9163… = 7π/24.\ntan has period π, so the other solution in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π is 7π/24 + π = 31π/24.\nx = 7π/24 or x = 31π/24 (x = 0.916 or 4.06)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::7": "Gradient of the line through (4, 5) and (6, −1): (−1 − 5)/(6 − 4) = −6/2 = −3.\nEquation of the line: y − 5 = −3(x − 4), so y = 17 − 3x.\nAt the points of intersection:\n2x² − 7x + 1 = 17 − 3x\n2x² − 4x − 16 = 0\nx² − 2x − 8 = 0\n(x − 4)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 4 or x = −2.\nWhen x = 4: y = 17 − 12 = 5, giving (4, 5).\nWhen x = −2: y = 17 + 6 = 23, giving (−2, 23).\nDistance between (4, 5) and (−2, 23):\n√((−2 − 4)² + (23 − 5)²) = √(36 + 324) = √360 = 6√10",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::8(a)": "The boy's velocity-time graph (drawn on the same axes as the bus's horizontal line at v = 5.4):\n• From the origin, a straight line with positive gradient 1.2, rising to v = 1.2 × 5 = 6 m s⁻¹ at t = 5. It crosses the bus's line v = 5.4 at t = 4.5.\n• From t = 5 onwards, a horizontal line at v = 6, which lies above the bus's line at v = 5.4.\n• The point where the gradient changes is labelled with t = 5 and v = 6 (these values may be marked on the axes).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::8(b)": "For t ≤ 5 the boy's velocity is v = 0 + 1.2t.\nHe runs at the bus's speed when 1.2t = 5.4, so t = 5.4/1.2 = 4.5.\nt = 4.5 s (which is within the first 5 s, so the formula applies)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::8(c)": "Until t = 4.5 the bus is faster than the boy, so the gap increases; after t = 4.5 the boy is faster, so the gap decreases. The maximum distance between them is therefore at t = 4.5 s.\nBoy's distance at t = 4.5: s = ½ × 1.2 × 4.5² = 12.15 m.\nBus's distance at t = 4.5: 5.4 × 4.5 = 24.3 m.\nMaximum distance = 24.3 − 12.15 = 12.15 m",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::8(d)": "Let the boy catch the bus T s after t = 0 (T > 5).\nBoy's distance = area under his velocity-time graph, a trapezium with parallel sides T and T − 5 and height 6:\n½ × 6 × (T + T − 5) = 6T − 15\n(equivalently ½ × 5 × 6 + 6(T − 5) = 15 + 6T − 30)\nBus's distance = 5.4T.\nHe catches the bus when 6T − 15 = 5.4T, so 0.6T = 15 and T = 25.\nDistance run by the boy = 5.4 × 25 = 135 m (check: 6 × 25 − 15 = 135).\nThe boy has run 135 m when he catches the bus.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::9(a)": "Where the gradient is 3:\neˣ − 4e⁻ˣ = 3\nMultiply every term by eˣ (using e⁻ˣ × eˣ = 1):\n(eˣ)² − 4 = 3eˣ\n(eˣ)² − 3eˣ − 4 = 0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::9(b)": "Factorise the quadratic in eˣ:\n(eˣ)² − 3eˣ − 4 = 0\n(eˣ − 4)(eˣ + 1) = 0\nso eˣ = 4 or eˣ = −1.\neˣ > 0 for all real x, so eˣ = −1 has no real solutions.\neˣ = 4 gives the single solution x = ln 4.\nTherefore there is only one point on the curve at which the gradient is 3, and its x-coordinate is ln 4.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::9(c)": "y = ∫(eˣ − 4e⁻ˣ) dx = eˣ + 4e⁻ˣ + c\nThe curve passes through (0, 0):\n0 = e⁰ + 4e⁰ + c = 1 + 4 + c, so c = −5.\ny = eˣ + 4e⁻ˣ − 5\nWhen x = 1:\ny = e + 4e⁻¹ − 5 = 2.7183 + 1.4715 − 5 = −0.810 (3 s.f.)\nThe y-coordinate of the curve at x = 1 is negative, so at x = 1 the curve is below the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::10(a)": "Use cos 2x = 1 − 2sin²x, so sin²x = ½(1 − cos 2x).\ny = 1.5 + sin²x = 1.5 + ½(1 − cos 2x) = 1.5 + 0.5 − 0.5 cos 2x\ny = 2 − 0.5 cos 2x\nThis is of the form y = a − b cos 2x with a = 2 and b = 0.5.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::10(b)": "1.5 + sin²x = 2 − 0.5 cos 2x, and cos 2x has period 2π/2 = π.\nThe period of 1.5 + sin²x is π.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::10(c)": "Using (a), the graphs meet where\n2 − 0.5 cos 2x = 1 + cos 2x\n1 = 1.5 cos 2x\ncos 2x = 2/3\nFor 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π we need 0 ≤ 2x ≤ 4π.\nPrincipal value: 2x = arccos(2/3) = 0.8411\n2x = 0.8411, 2π − 0.8411, 2π + 0.8411, 4π − 0.8411\n2x = 0.8411, 5.4421, 7.1243, 11.7253\nx = 0.421, 2.72, 3.56, 5.86 (radians, 3 s.f.)\nThese are the only intersections in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(a)(i)": "Substitute the two data points into h = a + b ln t:\nt = 10, h = 10: 10 = a + b ln 10\nt = 85, h = 200: 200 = a + b ln 85\nSubtract the first equation from the second:\n190 = b(ln 85 − ln 10) = b ln(85/10) = b ln 8.5\nb = 190/ln 8.5 = 190/2.1401… = 88.78…\nb = 88.8 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(a)(ii)": "From 10 = a + b ln 10:\na = 10 − 88.78… × ln 10 = 10 − 204.43… = −194.4…\na = −194 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(b)(i)": "For small positive values of t, ln t is small (and negative for t < 1), so h = −194 + 88.8 ln t is negative; for example at t = 1 the model gives h = −194 cm. A negative height is impossible, so the model is not suitable for small t.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(b)(ii)": "ln t increases without limit as t increases, so the model predicts that the sunflower keeps growing taller for ever. A real sunflower stops growing at some height, so the model is not suitable for very large t.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(c)": "The sunflower is 2 m (200 cm) tall at t = 85, so half that time is 85/2 = 42.5 days.\nHeight predicted at t = 42.5:\nh = −194 + 88.8 ln 42.5 = −194 + 333.0 = 139 cm\n139 cm is more than 1 m, so the sunflower has already passed 1 m before t = 42.5. Hence the model indicates it grows to 1 m in less than half the time it takes to grow to 2 m.\n(Equivalently: 100 = −194 + 88.8 ln t gives t = e^(294/88.8) = 27.4 days, which is less than 42.5 days.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::11(d)": "h = a + b ln t, so the rate of growth is dh/dt = b/t.\nRate of growth is 3 cm per day when b/t = 3:\nt = b/3 = 88.8/3 = 29.6\nt = 29.6 (about 30 days after planting)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::12(a)": "Weight = mass × g acting vertically downwards, i.e. in the −j direction.\nWeight = −2gj N = −19.6j N",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::12(b)": "The forces on the particle are the horizontal force 3i N, the force F = (−4i + 12j) N and the weight −19.6j N.\nLet the acceleration be a m s⁻². Newton's second law:\n(−4i + 12j) + 3i − 19.6j = 2a\n−i − 7.6j = 2a\na = −0.5i − 3.8j m s⁻²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::12(c)": "The acceleration is constant, so use v = u + at with u = 5i and t = 4:\nv = 5i + 4(−0.5i − 3.8j)\nv = 5i − 2i − 15.2j\nv = 3i − 15.2j m s⁻¹",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::12(d)": "For constant velocity the resultant force must be zero.\nThe current resultant force is (−4i + 12j) + 3i − 19.6j = −i − 7.6j N.\nThe extra force needed is therefore the negative of this:\n(i + 7.6j) N",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::13(a)": "Forces on the block (8 kg, on the plane):\n• tension T N along the rope, acting up the plane towards the pulley;\n• normal reaction R N, perpendicular to the plane, away from it;\n• friction F N, parallel to the plane, acting down the plane (opposing the block's motion up the plane);\n• weight 8g N, vertically downwards.\nForces on the sphere (5 kg):\n• tension T N (the same T as on the block, since the rope is light and the pulley smooth), vertically upwards;\n• weight 5g N, vertically downwards.\nNo other forces, and the weight is not shown together with its components.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::13(b)": "The sphere moves downwards with acceleration a m s⁻² (the same magnitude as the block, since the rope is inextensible).\nTaking downwards as positive, Newton's second law for the sphere:\n5g − T = 5a  (i.e. 49 − T = 5a)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::13(c)": "Block, perpendicular to the plane (no acceleration in this direction):\nR = 8g cos 15°\nThe block is sliding, so friction is limiting:\nF = 0.3R = 2.4g cos 15°\nBlock, parallel to the plane (up the plane positive), Newton's second law:\nT − 8g sin 15° − F = 8a\nT − 8g sin 15° − 2.4g cos 15° = 8a\nSphere, from (b): 5g − T = 5a\nAdd the two equations to eliminate T:\n5g − 8g sin 15° − 2.4g cos 15° = 13a\n49 − 20.291 − 22.718 = 13a\n5.991 = 13a\na = 0.461 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::14(a)": "Using log A − log B = log(A/B):\nlog₁₀200 − log₁₀20 = log₁₀(200/20) = log₁₀10 = 1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::14(b)": "Difference between the second and first terms, from (a):\nlog₁₀200 − log₁₀20 = 1\nDifference between the third and second terms:\nlog₁₀2000 − log₁₀200 = log₁₀(2000/200) = log₁₀10 = 1\nThe differences between consecutive terms are both exactly 1. In general the nth term is log₁₀(2 × 10ⁿ) = log₁₀2 + n, so each term is 1 more than the one before.\nThe sequence has a common difference of 1, so it is arithmetic.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::14(c)": "Arithmetic series with first term a = log₁₀20, common difference d = 1 and n = 50 terms:\nS₅₀ = ½ × 50 × (2a + (50 − 1)d)\nS₅₀ = 25(2 log₁₀20 + 49)\nS₅₀ = 50 log₁₀20 + 1225\n(equivalently 25 log₁₀400 + 1225, or 25(log₁₀4 + 51))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::15(a)": "Initial velocity components: horizontal u cos θ, vertical u sin θ. Acceleration: 0 horizontally, −g vertically (upwards positive).\nVertical motion, returning to ground level (s = 0):\n0 = (u sin θ)t − ½gt²\n0 = t(u sin θ − ½gt)\nt = 0 (launch) or t = 2u sin θ/g, so the time of flight is 2u sin θ/g.\nHorizontal motion at constant speed u cos θ, so the range R is\nR = u cos θ × 2u sin θ/g\nR = 2u² sin θ cos θ/g",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p1::15(b)": "At the maximum height H the vertical component of velocity is zero. Using v² = u² + 2as vertically:\n0 = (u sin θ)² − 2gH\nH = u² sin²θ/(2g)\nThe maximum height is greater than the range when\nu² sin²θ/(2g) > 2u² sin θ cos θ/g\nMultiply by 2g/u² (positive):\nsin²θ > 4 sin θ cos θ\nθ is acute, so sin θ > 0 and cos θ > 0; divide by sin θ cos θ:\ntan θ > 4\nθ > arctan 4 = 75.96°\nSo the set of values is 76.0° < θ < 90°.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::1": "The series is geometric with first term a = 9 and common ratio r = −3/9 = −1/3.\nSince |r| = 1/3 < 1, the sum to infinity exists:\nS∞ = a/(1 − r)\n= 9/(1 − (−1/3))\n= 9/(4/3)\n= 27/4\n\nSum to infinity = 27/4 = 6.75",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::2(a)": "Complete the square in x and in y:\nx² − 12x = (x − 6)² − 36\ny² + 8y = (y + 4)² − 16\nSo the equation becomes\n(x − 6)² − 36 + (y + 4)² − 16 + 3 = 0\n(x − 6)² + (y + 4)² = 49\n\nRadius = √49 = 7",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::2(b)": "From (x − 6)² + (y + 4)² = 49, the centre is (6, −4).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::3": "Step 1 (reciprocal): a negative power means take the reciprocal.\n(64/49)^(−3/2) = (49/64)^(3/2)\n\nStep 2 (square root): the power ½ is a square root.\n(49/64)^(1/2) = √49/√64 = 7/8\nso (49/64)^(3/2) = (7/8)³\n\nStep 3 (cube):\n(7/8)³ = 7³/8³ = 343/512\n\n343 = 7³ and 512 = 2⁹ have no common factor, so the fraction is in its lowest terms.\n\nm = 343, n = 512",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::4(a)": "The probabilities must add up to 1:\np + p + p + p + p + p + p + 3p = 1\n7p + 3p = 1\n10p = 1\n\np = 0.1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::4(b)": "P(X ≥ 4) = P(X = 4) + P(X = 5) + P(X = 6) + P(X = 7) + P(X = 8)\n= 4p + 3p\n= 7p\n= 7 × 0.1\n\nP(X ≥ 4) = 0.7",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::4(c)": "On each roll, P(score of 8) = 3p = 0.3, and the 30 rolls are independent.\nLet Y be the number of 8s in 30 rolls. Then Y ~ B(30, 0.3).\n\nP(Y = 2) = ³⁰C₂ × 0.3² × 0.7²⁸\n= 435 × 0.09 × 0.7²⁸\n= 0.001800…\n\nP(exactly two 8s) = 0.0018 (2 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::5": "AB = OB − OA\n= (5, −3) − (3, −1)\n= (2, −2)   (as a column vector)\n\n|AB| = √(2² + (−2)²)\n= √(4 + 4)\n= √8\n\nLength AB = √8 = 2√2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::6": "From the parametric equations:\nx = 2cos θ − 3  ⇒  2cos θ = x + 3  ⇒  cos θ = (x + 3)/2\ny = 2sin θ + 1  ⇒  2sin θ = y − 1  ⇒  sin θ = (y − 1)/2\n\nUse cos²θ + sin²θ = 1:\n((x + 3)/2)² + ((y − 1)/2)² = 1\n(x + 3)²/4 + (y − 1)²/4 = 1\n\nMultiply by 4:\n(x + 3)² + (y − 1)² = 4\n\nSo a = −3, b = 1, k = 4 (a circle with centre (−3, 1) and radius 2).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::7": "The general term of (2x + k)¹² is ¹²Cᵣ (2x)ʳ k¹²⁻ʳ.\nFor x⁸ take r = 8:\nterm = ¹²C₈ (2x)⁸ k⁴\n\n¹²C₈ = 495 and 2⁸ = 256, so the coefficient of x⁸ is\n495 × 256 × k⁴ = 126 720k⁴\n\nSet this equal to 79 200 000:\n126 720k⁴ = 79 200 000\nk⁴ = 79 200 000/126 720 = 625\n\nk is a positive integer, so k = ⁴√625\n\nk = 5",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::8(a)": "No, it is not a simple random sample.\nIn a simple random sample every possible sample of 120 plants (and so every plant) must have an equal chance of being selected. Here only plants on the front row can be chosen; plants on the other nine rows have no chance of being selected, so each possible sample does not have an equal probability of being chosen.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::8(b)": "Read from the cumulative frequency curve:\nNumber of plants shorter than 40 cm ≈ 18\nNumber of plants shorter than 80 cm ≈ 110 (so about 10 are taller than 80 cm)\n\nNumber of plants between 40 cm and 80 cm ≈ 110 − 18 = 92\n\nProportion = 92/120 = 0.7666… ≈ 77%\n\nSince 77% > 75% (equivalently, 92 > 90, where 90 is 75% of 120), the employee's data support the owner's statement that at least 75% of the plants are between 40 cm and 80 cm tall.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::8(c)": "No. Different samples give different results. Another sample of 120 plants could, for example, contain more of the tallest and/or shortest plants, so fewer than 75% of that sample might be between 40 cm and 80 cm. So not all samples of 120 plants would necessarily support the owner's statement.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::9(a)": "These data are a sample, because not all of the data are included: for example, the value for 2009 is missing (#N/A), so only some of the data are used.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::9(b)": "Substitute Y = 2009 into I = 1115Y − 2 212 950:\nI = 1115 × 2009 − 2 212 950\n= 2 240 035 − 2 212 950\n= 27 085\n\nEstimated median income in Camden in 2009 = £27 085 (about £27 100)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::9(c)": "1. It is interpolation: 2009 lies between 2008 and 2010, inside the range of years used to build the model, so the estimate is not an extrapolation.\n2. The time-series points lie close to a straight line, so the relationship between median income and year appears linear and a straight-line model is justified.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::9(d)": "No, it would not give a reasonable estimate. The model was formed from Camden's data only. Median incomes vary considerably across the different areas of London: in particular, median income in Croydon is lower than in Camden. So the Camden model would not be expected to fit Croydon's values, and it would be likely to overestimate Croydon's missing value.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::10": "Integrate by parts with u = 4x and dv/dx = cos 2x,\nso du/dx = 4 and v = ½sin 2x.\n\n∫ 4x cos 2x dx = 4x × ½sin 2x − ∫ ½sin 2x × 4 dx\n= 2x sin 2x − ∫ 2sin 2x dx\n= 2x sin 2x + cos 2x\n\nApply the limits:\n[2x sin 2x + cos 2x] from 0 to π/4\nAt x = π/4: 2(π/4) sin(π/2) + cos(π/2) = (π/2)(1) + 0 = π/2\nAt x = 0: 0 + cos 0 = 1\n\n∫₀^(π/4) 4x cos 2x dx = π/2 − 1  (= (π − 2)/2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::11(a)": "dy/dx is directly proportional to √x, so\ndy/dx = k√x for some constant k.\n\nWhen x = 4, dy/dx = 3:\n3 = k√4 = 2k\nk = 3/2\n\ndy/dx = (3/2)√x",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::11(b)": "y = ∫ (3/2)x^(1/2) dx\n= (3/2) × x^(3/2)/(3/2) + c\n= x^(3/2) + c\n\nWhen x = 4, y = 10:\n10 = 4^(3/2) + c = (√4)³ + c = 8 + c\nc = 2\n\ny = x^(3/2) + 2  (that is, y = x√x + 2)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::12(a)": "f(x) = ±1/√x is a one-to-many mapping: each value of x (x > 0) is mapped to two values, 1/√x and −1/√x. A function must give exactly one output for each input, so f(x) is not a function.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::12(b)": "gh(x) = g(h(x)) = g(x² + 2)\n= (x² + 2)/((x² + 2) − 3)\n\ngh(x) = (x² + 2)/(x² − 1)  (= (x² + 2)/((x − 1)(x + 1)))",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::12(c)": "For gh(x) to be defined, h(x) must lie in the domain of g, so h(x) > 3:\nx² + 2 > 3\nx² > 1\n\nDomain of gh: |x| > 1, i.e. x < −1 or x > 1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::13(a)": "H₀: μ = 0.14\nH₁: μ < 0.14\nwhere μ is the population mean mass, in kg, of apples of this variety on sale in the supermarket chain's stores.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::13(b)": "Under H₀ the population mean is 0.14 kg. The population standard deviation is estimated by the sample standard deviation s = 0.0199, and the sample size is n = 80.\n\nX̄ ~ N(0.14, 0.0199²/80)\n\n(variance = 0.0199²/80 ≈ 4.95 × 10⁻⁶, i.e. standard deviation ≈ 0.00222)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::13(c)": "This is a one-tailed (lower-tail) test at the 5% level, so the critical value cuts off 5% in the lower tail of X̄ ~ N(0.14, 0.0199²/80).\n\nCritical value = 0.14 − 1.645 × 0.0199/√80\n= 0.14 − 1.645 × 0.0022249\n= 0.13634…\n\nCritical region: X̄ < 0.136 (more precisely X̄ < 0.1363)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::13(d)": "The sample mean is 0.1316.\n0.1316 < 0.1363, so the sample mean lies in the critical region.\n(Equivalently, z = (0.1316 − 0.14)/(0.0199/√80) = −3.78 < −1.645, or p-value = P(X̄ ≤ 0.1316) ≈ 0.00008 < 0.05.)\n\nReject H₀.\n\nThere is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest that the mean mass of apples of this variety is less than 0.14 kg.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::14(a)": "City of London has no value for the percentage of pupils (#N/A), so it should be removed (discarded) from the data before any analysis. More generally, any area with one or more pieces of data missing should be removed.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::14(b)": "1. The points on the scatter diagram do not look as though they lie close to a straight line; the scatter does not look linear.\n2. The product moment correlation coefficient, 0.3743, is not close to 1, so there is only weak linear correlation.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::14(c)": "Outliers are values more than 2 standard deviations from the mean.\n\nMedian income:\n27 216 − 2 × 4177.5 = 18 861\n27 216 + 2 × 4177.5 = 35 571\nOutliers: median income < £18 861 or median income > £35 571\n\nPercentage:\n61.0 − 2 × 5.32 = 50.36\n61.0 + 2 × 5.32 = 71.64\nOutliers: percentage < 50.36 or percentage > 71.64\n\nOn the scatter diagram:\nNo income is below £18 861 (the lowest is about £20 300) and no percentage is below 50.36 (the lowest is about 52).\nThe three outliers to circle are:\n• the point with the highest percentage, about 73%, at an income of about £29 000 (percentage > 71.64);\n• the point at an income of about £35 800 (percentage about 64.5%) (income > £35 571);\n• the point at an income of about £38 700 (percentage about 67%) (income > £35 571).\nThe point at about £35 100 (about 67%) is below £35 571, so it is not an outlier.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::14(d)": "The new value would be between 0 and 0.3743.\nThe three outliers (the two high-income points with above-average percentages and the point with the highest percentage) gave a false impression of linearity. Once they are removed, the remaining points form a scatter that is more like a circle (a shapeless cloud), so the linear correlation is weaker.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::15": "At y = 1: ln 1 + x³(1) = 8 ⇒ 0 + x³ = 8 ⇒ x = 2 (the only real solution).\nSo the point is (2, 1).\n\nDifferentiate ln y + x³y = 8 implicitly with respect to x:\nd(ln y)/dx = (1/y) dy/dx\nd(x³y)/dx = x³ dy/dx + 3x²y   (product rule)\nSo\n(1/y) dy/dx + x³ dy/dx + 3x²y = 0\n\nSubstitute x = 2, y = 1:\n(1/1) dy/dx + 8 dy/dx + 3(4)(1) = 0\n9 dy/dx + 12 = 0\ndy/dx = −12/9 = −4/3\n\nGradient of tangent = −4/3, so gradient of normal = 3/4.\n\nNormal through (2, 1):\ny − 1 = (3/4)(x − 2)\n4y − 4 = 3x − 6\n3x − 4y − 2 = 0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::16(a)": "P(smokes) = 0.16, P(does not smoke) = 0.84.\nOne smokes and the other doesn't: either the first smokes and the second doesn't, or the other way round.\nP = 0.16 × 0.84 + 0.84 × 0.16\n= 2 × 0.16 × 0.84\n= 0.2688 (= 168/625)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::16(b)": "Let S = smokes cigarettes, D = drinks alcohol.\nP(D) = 0.75 and P(D ∩ S′) = 0.66, so\nP(D ∩ S) = 0.75 − 0.66 = 0.09\n\nP(S ∩ D′) = P(S) − P(S ∩ D)\n= 0.16 − 0.09\n= 0.07",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::16(c)": "P(S | D) = P(S ∩ D)/P(D)\n= 0.09/0.75\n= 0.12",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::16(d)": "If the events were independent, P(S | D) would equal P(S).\nP(S | D) = 0.12 but P(S) = 0.16.\n0.12 ≠ 0.16, so the event that a young adult smokes cigarettes is not independent of the event that a young adult drinks alcohol.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::17": "sec x is only defined when cos x ≠ 0, so we may divide every term by cos x:\n2sin x/cos x + 1/cos²x = 4\n2tan x + sec²x = 4\n\nUse sec²x = 1 + tan²x:\n2tan x + 1 + tan²x = 4\ntan²x + 2tan x − 3 = 0\n(tan x + 3)(tan x − 1) = 0\ntan x = 1 or tan x = −3\n\ntan x = 1:\nx = π/4 or x = π/4 − π = −3π/4\n\ntan x = −3:\nprincipal value x = arctan(−3) = −1.249 (3 d.p.)\nother value x = −1.249 + π = 1.893 (3 d.p.)\n\nAll four values are in −π < x < π, and none has cos x = 0.\n\nx = −3π/4, −1.249, π/4, 1.893",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(a)": "x̄ = Σx/n = 31164.7/120 = 259.705…\n\nSample mean = 260 litres (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(b)": "In a histogram, frequency = frequency density × class width.\nFor 255 to 260 litres: frequency density = 6.2, class width = 5.\nFrequency = 6.2 × 5 = 31\n\n31 days",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(c)": "1. The histogram is approximately symmetrical about the mean.\n2. The histogram is approximately bell-shaped (unimodal, with a single central peak and frequencies falling away on both sides).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(d)": "Sxx = Σx² − (Σx)²/n\n= 8101050.91 − 31164.7²/120\n= 8101050.91 − 8093654.384…\n= 7396.525…\n\nSample variance s² = Sxx/(n − 1) = 7396.525…/119 = 62.155… = 62.2 (3 s.f.)\n\nRiley's model: X ~ N(260, 62.2), where X is the daily consumption in litres (standard deviation √62.2 = 7.887…).\n\nP(X < 255) = 0.263 (3 s.f.) (by calculator)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(e)": "Let W be the number of days, out of 28, on which less than 255 litres are used.\nAssuming the days are independent, W ~ B(28, 0.263) (using the unrounded 0.26304… from (d)).\n\nP(W ≥ 5) = 1 − P(W ≤ 4)\n= 1 − 0.104977…\n= 0.895 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p2::18(f)": "Let C be the daily charge in pence. Then C = 7.8 + 0.18X, where X ~ N(260, 62.2).\n\nMean of C = 7.8 + 0.18 × 260 = 7.8 + 46.8 = 54.6\nVariance of C = 0.18² × 62.2 = 0.0324 × 62.2 = 2.015… = 2.02 (3 s.f.)\n(standard deviation = 0.18 × √62.2 = 1.42)\n\nModel for the daily charge: C ~ N(54.6, 2.02), in pence.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::1": "sin²θ + cos²θ = 1\n(2/√13)² + cos²θ = 1\n4/13 + cos²θ = 1\ncos²θ = 1 − 4/13 = 9/13\ncos θ = ±3/√13\n\nθ is obtuse (90° < θ < 180°), so cos θ < 0.\n\ncos θ = −3/√13 (= −3√13/13)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::2(a)": "y = |5 − 2x| is the same as y = 5 − 2x wherever 5 − 2x ≥ 0, i.e. for x ≤ 2.5, and is its reflection in the x-axis, y = 2x − 5, for x > 2.5.\n\nSketch (described in words):\n• To the left of x = 2.5, draw over the given line: it passes through (0, 5) on the y-axis and continues upwards to the left of the y-axis.\n• At (2.5, 0) the graph touches the x-axis – this is the vertex of a V shape.\n• To the right of x = 2.5, instead of the part of the given line that goes below the x-axis, the graph goes upwards from (2.5, 0) at the same steepness (gradient +2), i.e. that part is reflected in the x-axis.\n\nThe result is a V shape with its vertex at (2.5, 0), crossing the y-axis at (0, 5), and lying entirely on or above the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::2(b)": "|5 − 2x| < 3 means\n−3 < 5 − 2x < 3\n\nSubtract 5:\n−8 < −2x < −2\n\nDivide by −2 (reversing the inequalities):\n4 > x > 1\n\nSo 1 < x < 4.\n\n(Check with the graph: the V shape lies below the line y = 3 between the points where 5 − 2x = 3, x = 1, and 2x − 5 = 3, x = 4.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::3": "Rationalise each denominator on the left.\n\n1/(√5 + √6) = (√6 − √5)/((√6 + √5)(√6 − √5)) = (√6 − √5)/(6 − 5) = √6 − √5\n\n1/(√6 + √7) = (√7 − √6)/((√7 + √6)(√7 − √6)) = (√7 − √6)/(7 − 6) = √7 − √6\n\nSo the left-hand side is\n(√6 − √5) + (√7 − √6) = √7 − √5\n\nRationalise the right-hand side:\nk/(√5 + √7) = k(√7 − √5)/((√7 + √5)(√7 − √5)) = k(√7 − √5)/(7 − 5) = k(√7 − √5)/2\n\nSo k(√7 − √5)/2 = √7 − √5, and since √7 − √5 ≠ 0,\nk/2 = 1\n\nk = 2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::4": "The curve crosses the x-axis where y = 0:\nx³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = 0\n\nLet f(x) = x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6.\nf(1) = 1³ − 2(1)² − 5(1) + 6 = 1 − 2 − 5 + 6 = 0\nso, by the factor theorem, x = 1 is a root and (x − 1) is a factor.\n\nDivide out (x − 1) (or compare coefficients):\nx³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 1)(x² − x − 6)\n(Check: (x − 1)(x² − x − 6) = x³ − x² − 6x − x² + x + 6 = x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 ✓)\n\nFactorise the quadratic:\nx² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2)\n\nSo x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0\nx = 1, x = 3 or x = −2\n\nThe curve crosses the x-axis at (1, 0), (3, 0) and (−2, 0).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::5(a)": "y = x³ − 5x² + 6x\ndy/dx = 3x² − 10x + 6\n\nAt x = 0: dy/dx = 3(0)² − 10(0) + 6 = 6\n\nThe tangent T has gradient 6 and passes through (0, 0):\ny − 0 = 6(x − 0)\n\nT: y = 6x",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::5(b)": "dy/dx = 3x² − 10x + 6\n\nAt x = 1: dy/dx = 3(1)² − 10(1) + 6 = 3 − 10 + 6 = −1\n\nSo the tangent at (1, 2) has gradient −1, and the normal N, which is perpendicular to it, has gradient\n−1/(−1) = 1\n\nN passes through (1, 2):\ny − 2 = 1(x − 1)\n\nN: y = x + 1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::5(c)": "T: y = 6x and N: y = x + 1\n\nAt the intersection:\n6x = x + 1\n5x = 1\nx = 1/5\n\ny = 6 × 1/5 = 6/5\n\nT and N meet at (1/5, 6/5), i.e. (0.2, 1.2).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::6(a)(i)": "Midpoint of (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2).\n\nP, midpoint of K(−4, 1) and L(5, −1): ((−4 + 5)/2, (1 + (−1))/2) = (0.5, 0)\nQ, midpoint of L(5, −1) and M(6, 2): ((5 + 6)/2, (−1 + 2)/2) = (5.5, 0.5)\nR, midpoint of M(6, 2) and N(2, 5): ((6 + 2)/2, (2 + 5)/2) = (4, 3.5)\nS, midpoint of N(2, 5) and K(−4, 1): ((2 + (−4))/2, (5 + 1)/2) = (−1, 3)\n\nP(0.5, 0), Q(5.5, 0.5), R(4, 3.5), S(−1, 3)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::6(a)(ii)": "Using P(0.5, 0), Q(5.5, 0.5), R(4, 3.5), S(−1, 3).\n\nFirst pair of opposite sides, PQ and SR:\nvector PQ = (5.5 − 0.5, 0.5 − 0) = (5, 0.5)\nvector SR = (4 − (−1), 3.5 − 3) = (5, 0.5)\nSo PQ = SR: these sides are parallel (both gradient 0.5/5 = 0.1) and equal in length (√101/2).\n\nSecond pair of opposite sides, PS and QR:\nvector PS = (−1 − 0.5, 3 − 0) = (−1.5, 3)\nvector QR = (4 − 5.5, 3.5 − 0.5) = (−1.5, 3)\nSo PS = QR: these sides are parallel (both gradient 3/(−1.5) = −2) and equal in length (3√5/2).\n\nBoth pairs of opposite sides are parallel (gradients 0.1 and −2 are different, so the four points are not collinear), so PQRS is a parallelogram.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::6(b)(i)": "A and B divide TV into 3 equal parts and TA = a, so TV = 3a, hence VT = −3a.\nH and G divide TX into 3 equal parts and TH = b, so TX = 3b.\nC and D divide VW into 3 equal parts and VC = c, so VW = 3c, hence WV = −3c.\n\nWX = WV + VT + TX\n   = −3c − 3a + 3b\n   = 3(−a + b − c)\n\nSo WX = k(−a + b − c) with k = 3.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::6(b)(ii)": "AH = AT + TH = −a + b\n\nE is the point of trisection of WX nearer W, so\nWE = (1/3)WX = −a + b − c   (using part (i))\n\nD is the point of trisection of VW nearer W, so DW = (1/3)VW = c.\n\nDE = DW + WE\n   = c + (−a + b − c)\n   = −a + b\n\nSo DE = −a + b = AH. The two vectors are equal (so in particular one is a scalar multiple of the other), hence AH is parallel to DE.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::6(b)(iii)": "B is the point of trisection of TV nearer V, so BV = (1/3)TV = a.\nBC = BV + VC = a + c\n\nG is the point of trisection of TX nearer X, so GX = (1/3)TX = b.\nF is the point of trisection of WX nearer X, so XF = −(1/3)WX = −(−a + b − c) = a − b + c.\n\nGF = GX + XF\n   = b + (a − b + c)\n   = a + c\n(equivalently GF = b − (−c − a + b) = c + a)\n\nSo BC = GF = a + c, and therefore BC is parallel to GF.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::7": "Perimeter: two radii plus the arc.\n2r + rθ = 10, so θ = (10 − 2r)/r\n\nArea of sector:\nA = ½r²θ = ½r² × (10 − 2r)/r = r(10 − 2r)/2 = 5r − r²\n\nComplete the square:\nA = −(r² − 5r) = −[(r − 2.5)² − 2.5²] = 2.5² − (2.5 − r)²\nA = 6.25 − (2.5 − r)²\n\nSince (2.5 − r)² ≥ 0 for all r, A ≤ 6.25, with equality only when 2.5 − r = 0, i.e. r = 2.5. So A has a maximum value of 6.25 at r = 2.5.\n\nCheck this is a possible sector: r = 2.5 gives θ = (10 − 5)/2.5 = 2 radians, which is between 0 and 2π.\n\n(Alternatively: dA/dr = 5 − 2r = 0 gives r = 2.5, and d²A/dr² = −2 < 0, so this is a maximum.)\n\nMaximum area = 6.25 cm²",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::8": "Sketch (described in words): draw the two intersecting circles with centres A and C, meeting at B and D. Join A, B, C, D to form quadrilateral ABCD, with AB = AD = 8 cm (radii of the first circle) and CB = CD = 12 cm (radii of the second circle). The diagonal AC splits ABCD into triangles ABC and ADC.\n\nTriangles ABC and ADC are congruent (SSS: AB = AD, CB = CD, AC common), so each has area 30 cm² and angle ABC = angle ADC. Call this angle B.\n\nArea of ABCD = 2 × ½ × 8 × 12 × sin B\n60 = 96 sin B\nsin B = 60/96 = 5/8\n\nSo B = 38.7° (0.675 rad) or B = 180° − 38.7° = 141.3° (2.47 rad).\n(Equivalently cos B = √39/8 or cos B = −√39/8.)\n\nCosine rule in triangle ABC:\nAC² = 8² + 12² − 2 × 8 × 12 × cos B = 208 − 192 cos B\n\nWhen B = 38.7° (cos B = √39/8):\nAC² = 208 − 24√39 = 58.1...\nAC = 7.62 cm (3 s.f.)\n\nWhen B = 141.3° (cos B = −√39/8):\nAC² = 208 + 24√39 = 357.9...\nAC = 18.9 cm (3 s.f.)\n\nThe two possible lengths are AC = 7.62 cm and AC = 18.9 cm.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(a)(i)": "In 2000, t = 0:\nP = 0.3e^(0.5 × 0) = 0.3 × 1 = 0.3\n\n0.3 MWh",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(a)(ii)": "In 2009, t = 9:\nP = 0.3e^(0.5 × 9) = 0.3e^4.5 = 27.005...\n\n27 MWh (to 2 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(b)": "The model is based only on data from 2000 to 2009, and it predicts unlimited exponential growth: for 2025, t = 25 gives P = 0.3e^12.5 ≈ 80 500 MWh, about 3000 times the 2009 value. Growth like this cannot continue indefinitely (for example, there is a limit to how many solar panels the small country can install), so the prediction for 2025 is unrealistic.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(c)": "Reading from Fig. 9, at t = 9 (the year 2009) the curve gives P ≈ 27 MWh, which is the same as the value 27 MWh given by the original model in part (a)(ii). So the two models agree for 2009.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(d)(i)": "The gradient of the Fig. 9 curve is never negative, so the gradient function lies on or above the t-axis throughout.\n\nSketch (described in words), with t from 0 to 25 on the horizontal axis and dP/dt on the vertical axis:\n• For small t the curve in Fig. 9 is almost flat, so the gradient function starts close to zero.\n• The curve gets steeper and steeper, so the gradient function rises to a single maximum, at about t = 14 (where Fig. 9 is steepest; the maximum gradient is roughly 35–40 MWh per year).\n• After that the curve becomes less steep as it levels off, so the gradient function decreases back towards zero, being very close to zero by t = 25.\n\nThe result is a smooth hump (bell) shape: near zero, rising to a peak between t = 10 and t = 20 (at about t = 14), then falling back to near zero.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(d)(ii)": "The point of inflection is where the curve stops getting steeper and starts getting less steep, i.e. where the gradient is greatest. From Fig. 9 this is at about\n\nt = 14",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(d)(iii)": "At the point of inflection the gradient dP/dt is at its maximum, so this is the time (around 2014) when the annual amount of electricity produced using solar panels is increasing at the greatest rate.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::9(e)": "As t increases, the curve in Fig. 9 levels off towards P = 300.\n\nThe long-term annual amount of electricity produced is approximately 300 MWh.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::10(a)": "Start with the right-hand side, using the double angle formula sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ:\n1 − ¾sin²2θ = 1 − ¾(2 sin θ cos θ)² = 1 − ¾ × 4sin²θcos²θ = 1 − 3sin²θcos²θ\n\nNow use the given result with x = sin θ and y = cos θ:\n(sin²θ + cos²θ)³ = sin⁶θ + 3sin⁴θcos²θ + 3sin²θcos⁴θ + cos⁶θ\n\nSince sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, the left-hand side is 1³ = 1, so\n1 = sin⁶θ + cos⁶θ + 3sin⁴θcos²θ + 3sin²θcos⁴θ\n\nsin⁶θ + cos⁶θ = 1 − (3sin⁴θcos²θ + 3sin²θcos⁴θ)\n             = 1 − 3sin²θcos²θ(sin²θ + cos²θ)\n             = 1 − 3sin²θcos²θ   (using sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 again)\n\nBoth sides equal 1 − 3sin²θcos²θ, so\nsin⁶θ + cos⁶θ = 1 − ¾sin²2θ for all values of θ.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::10(b)": "From (a), sin⁶θ + cos⁶θ = 1 − ¾sin²2θ.\n\nSince 0 ≤ sin²2θ ≤ 1, this expression is smallest when sin²2θ takes its largest value, 1 (for example at θ = 45°).\n\nMinimum value = 1 − ¾ × 1 = ¼",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::11(a)": "Σ₁⁵ r² = 1² + 2² + 3² + 4² + 5² = 1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 = 55\n\n(Check with the formula in line 5: 5 × 6 × 11/6 = 55.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::11(b)": "Euler's formula (line 13) with f(x) = x² and n = 5:\nΣ₁⁵ r² ≈ ∫₁⁵ x² dx + (5² + 1²)/2 + (1² − 2²)/12 − (5² − 6²)/12\n\n∫₁⁵ x² dx = [x³/3]₁⁵ = 125/3 − 1/3 = 124/3\n(5² + 1²)/2 = 26/2 = 13\n(1² − 2²)/12 = −3/12\n−(5² − 6²)/12 = −(−11)/12 = +11/12\n\nTotal = 124/3 + 13 − 3/12 + 11/12\n      = 124/3 + 13 + 8/12\n      = 124/3 + 41/3\n      = 165/3\n      = 55\n\nThis equals the exact value Σ₁⁵ r² = 55 from part (a).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::12": "Diagram (described in words): draw the graph of y = x² for 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 with the four shaded rectangles from the article. Each rectangle has width 1: the rectangle of area 1² stands on 0 ≤ x ≤ 1 with height 1, the one of area 2² on 1 ≤ x ≤ 2 with height 4, the one of area 3² on 2 ≤ x ≤ 3 with height 9, and the one of area 4² on 3 ≤ x ≤ 4 with height 16. The top right-hand corner of each rectangle lies on the curve.\n\nNow replace the curve between x = 1 and x = 4 by straight line segments (chords) joining (1, 1) to (2, 4), (2, 4) to (3, 9), and (3, 9) to (4, 16).\n\nIn the rectangle on 1 ≤ x ≤ 2, the part above the chord is a right-angled triangle with vertices (1, 1), (1, 4) and (2, 4):\n• its base is the top edge of the rectangle, from (1, 4) to (2, 4), of length 2 − 1 = 1;\n• its height is the vertical edge from (1, 1) to (1, 4), of length 4 − 1 = 2² − 1².\nArea = ½ × base × height = ½ × 1 × (2² − 1²) = (2² − 1²)/2\n\nSimilarly, in the rectangle on 2 ≤ x ≤ 3 the triangle has vertices (2, 4), (2, 9), (3, 9): base 1 (from x = 2 to x = 3), height 9 − 4 = 3² − 2².\nArea = ½ × 1 × (3² − 2²) = (3² − 2²)/2\n\nIn the rectangle on 3 ≤ x ≤ 4 the triangle has vertices (3, 9), (3, 16), (4, 16): base 1 (from x = 3 to x = 4), height 16 − 9 = 4² − 3².\nArea = ½ × 1 × (4² − 3²) = (4² − 3²)/2\n\n(On the diagram, label each triangle's base as 1 and its vertical side as 2² − 1², 3² − 2² and 4² − 3² respectively.)\n\nSo the three triangles in line 26 have areas (2² − 1²)/2, (3² − 2²)/2 and (4² − 3²)/2, as given in line 27.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::13": "Euler's formula (line 13) with f(x) = x²:\nΣ₁ⁿ r² ≈ ∫₁ⁿ x² dx + (n² + 1²)/2 + (1² − 2²)/12 − (n² − (n + 1)²)/12\n\nIntegral:\n∫₁ⁿ x² dx = [x³/3]₁ⁿ = (n³ − 1)/3\n\nThe other terms over a common denominator of 12:\n(n² + 1)/2 + (1 − 4)/12 − (n² − (n + 1)²)/12\n= [6n² + 6 + 1 − 4 − n² + (n² + 2n + 1)]/12\n= (6n² + 2n + 4)/12\n\nSo the formula gives\n(n³ − 1)/3 + (6n² + 2n + 4)/12\n= (4n³ − 4 + 6n² + 2n + 4)/12\n= (4n³ + 6n² + 2n)/12\n= (2n³ + 3n² + n)/6\n= n(2n² + 3n + 1)/6\n= n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6\n\nThis is exactly the formula for Σ₁ⁿ r² in line 5, so Euler's approximate formula gives the sum of squares exactly.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::14": "Line 33:\nΣ₁ⁿ 1/r ≈ ∫₁ⁿ (1/x) dx + ½(1/n + 1) + (1/12)(1 − ½) − (1/12)(1/n − 1/(n + 1))\n\nIntegral:\n∫₁ⁿ (1/x) dx = [ln x]₁ⁿ = ln n − ln 1 = ln n − 0 = ln n\n\nExpand the other terms:\n½(1/n + 1) = 1/(2n) + ½\n(1/12)(1 − ½) = 1/12 − 1/24 = 1/24\n−(1/12)(1/n − 1/(n + 1)) = −1/(12n) + 1/(12(n + 1))\n\nSo\nΣ₁ⁿ 1/r ≈ ln n + 1/(2n) + ½ + 1/12 − 1/24 − 1/(12n) + 1/(12(n + 1))\n\nConstant terms: ½ + 1/12 − 1/24 = 12/24 + 2/24 − 1/24 = 13/24\n\nTerms in n: 1/(2n) − 1/(12n) + 1/(12(n + 1))\n= 6/(12n) − 1/(12n) + 1/(12(n + 1))\n= 5/(12n) + 1/(12(n + 1))\n= [5(n + 1) + n]/(12n(n + 1))\n= (6n + 5)/(12n(n + 1))\n\nTherefore\nΣ₁ⁿ 1/r ≈ ln n + 13/24 + (6n + 5)/(12n(n + 1)), as given in line 34.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2023-p3::15": "True value:\nΣ₁⁶ 1/r = 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/4 + 1/5 + 1/6 = (60 + 30 + 20 + 15 + 12 + 10)/60 = 147/60 = 49/20 = 2.45\n\nLine-34 approximation with n = 6:\nln 6 + 13/24 + (6 × 6 + 5)/(12 × 6 × 7)\n= ln 6 + 13/24 + 41/504\n= 1.791759... + 0.541666... + 0.081349...\n= 2.414775...\n\nError = 2.45 − 2.414775... = 0.035225...\n\nPercentage error = 0.035225.../2.45 × 100 = 1.438% (3 d.p.)\n\nSince 1.438% < 1.5%, the error is less than 1.5% of the true value.\n(Equivalently, 1.5% of 2.45 is 0.03675, and 0.0352... < 0.03675.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::1": "Try x = 0.\nLeft-hand side: 1 + x² = 1 + 0² = 1\nRight-hand side: (1 + x)² = (1 + 0)² = 1\nSo when x = 0 the two sides are equal (1 = 1), and 1 < 1 is false.\nSo 1 + x² < (1 + x)² does not hold for x = 0, which is a counter example: the student is wrong.\n\n(Any negative x also works, e.g. x = −1 gives 1 + 1 = 2 on the left and 0² = 0 on the right, and 2 < 0 is false.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::2(a)": "Draw the trailer behind the car, both moving to the right, and show only horizontal forces.\n\nOn the car (in front):\n• 6000 N driving force, pointing forwards (to the right)\n• 800 N resistance, pointing backwards (to the left)\n• T N tension in the tow-bar, pointing backwards (to the left), towards the trailer\n\nOn the trailer (behind):\n• T N tension in the tow-bar, pointing forwards (to the right), towards the car\n• 300 N resistance, pointing backwards (to the left)\n\nThe tow-bar is light, so the tension T has the same size on the car and on the trailer, acting in opposite directions. There are no other horizontal forces.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::2(b)": "Treat the car and trailer as one system (the tow-bar tension is internal and cancels).\nTotal mass = 1400 + 400 = 1800 kg\nResultant forward force = 6000 − 800 − 300 = 4900 N\n\nNewton's second law: 4900 = 1800a\na = 4900/1800 = 2.722…\n\nAcceleration = 2.72 m s⁻² (3 s.f.)\n\n(Check using the parts separately: car 6000 − 800 − T = 1400a, trailer T − 300 = 400a; adding gives 4900 = 1800a, the same result.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::3(a)": "The particle is in equilibrium under its weight, the tension 12 N along the string (at 20° to the vertical) and the horizontal force F N.\n\nResolve horizontally: the horizontal component of the tension balances F.\nF = 12 sin 20°\nF = 4.104…\n\nF = 4.10 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::3(b)": "Let the mass of the particle be m kg, so its weight is mg = 9.8m N.\n\nResolve vertically: the vertical component of the tension balances the weight.\nmg = 12 cos 20° = 11.276…\nm = 11.276…/9.8 = 1.1506…\n\nMass = 1.15 kg (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::4": "3v₁ + v₂ = 3(2ai + bj) + (bi − 3j)\n= 6ai + 3bj + bi − 3j\n= (6a + b)i + (3b − 3)j\n\nThis equals 22i − 9j, so equating components:\ni: 6a + b = 22\nj: 3b − 3 = −9\n\nFrom the j equation: 3b = −6, so b = −2.\nThen 6a − 2 = 22, so 6a = 24, a = 4.\n\na = 4, b = −2",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::5(a)": "log₁₀(y − k) = x log₁₀2\nUsing the law n log a = log aⁿ:\nlog₁₀(y − k) = log₁₀(2ˣ)\nSo y − k = 2ˣ\n\ny = k + 2ˣ",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::5(b)": "The graph is y = k + 2ˣ, the curve y = 2ˣ translated up by k (k > 0).\n\nSketch description:\n• Draw the dashed horizontal line y = k (above the x-axis, since k > 0). This is a horizontal asymptote.\n• For large negative x the curve lies just above this line and approaches y = k as x → −∞, never touching it.\n• The curve crosses the y-axis at (0, k + 1), since 2⁰ = 1; label this point k + 1 on the positive y-axis.\n• For positive x the curve increases ever more steeply (exponential growth), e.g. passing through (1, k + 2).\n• The curve is always increasing and lies entirely above y = k, so it never meets the x-axis.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::6": "f(x) = 2x² + 3\n\nf(x + h) − f(x) = 2(x + h)² + 3 − (2x² + 3)\n= 2x² + 4xh + 2h² + 3 − 2x² − 3\n= 4xh + 2h²\n\nSo (f(x + h) − f(x))/h = (4xh + 2h²)/h = 4x + 2h   (h ≠ 0)\n\nf′(x) = lim (h → 0) (f(x + h) − f(x))/h = lim (h → 0) (4x + 2h) = 4x\n\nSo f′(x) = 4x, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::7(a)": "Resolve in two perpendicular directions, parallel to the sides of the book.\n\nParallel to AB (positive in the direction A to B):\n3 N at D along DC (same direction as AB): +3\n3 N at B along BA: −3\nTotal: 3 − 3 = 0\n\nParallel to AD (positive in the direction A to D):\n4 N at A along AD: +4\n5 N at B along BC (same direction as AD): +5\n9 N at D along DA: −9\nTotal: 4 + 5 − 9 = 0\n\nThe resultant force has zero component in each of two perpendicular directions, so the resultant force has zero magnitude.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::7(b)": "Let O be the centre of the book. O is 28/2 = 14 cm from the lines AD and BC, and 18/2 = 9 cm from the lines AB and DC.\nTake anticlockwise (as in the diagram) as positive, working in N cm.\n\n• 4 N at A along AD (acts along line AD, 14 cm from O): anticlockwise, +4 × 14 = +56\n• 5 N at B along BC (acts along line BC, 14 cm from O): clockwise, −5 × 14 = −70\n• 3 N at B along BA (acts along line AB, 9 cm from O): anticlockwise, +3 × 9 = +27\n• 9 N at D along DA (acts along line AD, 14 cm from O): clockwise, −9 × 14 = −126\n• 3 N at D along DC (acts along line DC, 9 cm from O): anticlockwise, +3 × 9 = +27\n\nTotal moment = 56 − 70 + 27 − 126 + 27 = −86 N cm\n\nConverting to N m (100 cm = 1 m): −86 N cm = −0.86 N m\n\nTotal moment about the centre = −0.86 N m (anticlockwise positive), i.e. 0.86 N m clockwise.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::7(c)": "From (a) the resultant force is zero, so the centre of the book does not start to move across the table.\nFrom (b) there is a non-zero total moment of 0.86 N m in the clockwise direction.\n\nSo the book will rotate (turn) clockwise about its centre, as viewed in the diagram.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::8(a)": "For small x (in radians), use sin θ ≈ θ and cos θ ≈ 1 − ½θ².\n\nWith θ = 4x: sin 4x ≈ 4x, so √(sin 4x) ≈ √(4x) = 2√x\nWith θ = 2x: cos 2x ≈ 1 − ½(2x)² = 1 − 2x², so 2cos 2x ≈ 2 − 4x²\n\nTherefore y = √(sin 4x) + 2cos 2x ≈ 2√x + 2 − 4x², as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::8(b)": "Area = ∫₀^0.1 (√(sin 4x) + 2cos 2x) dx\n≈ ∫₀^0.1 (2x^(1/2) + 2 − 4x²) dx\n\nIntegrate term by term:\n∫ 2x^(1/2) dx = 2x^(3/2)/(3/2) = (4/3)x^(3/2)\n∫ 2 dx = 2x\n∫ 4x² dx = (4/3)x³\n\nSo area ≈ [(4/3)x^(3/2) + 2x − (4/3)x³] from 0 to 0.1\n= (4/3)(0.1)^(3/2) + 2(0.1) − (4/3)(0.1)³ − 0\n= 0.042163… + 0.2 − 0.001333…\n= 0.24083…\n\nArea ≈ 0.241 (3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::9(a)": "Take downwards as positive. Before it reaches the beach the pebble falls freely:\nu = 6 m s⁻¹, a = 9.8 m s⁻², s = 0.8 m\n\nv² = u² + 2as\nv² = 6² + 2 × 9.8 × 0.8 = 36 + 15.68 = 51.68\nv = √51.68 = 7.188…\n\nThe pebble hits the beach at 7.19 m s⁻¹ (3 s.f.).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::9(b)": "Take downwards as positive. In the sand the pebble starts at speed √51.68 m s⁻¹ (from (a)) and stops after 3 cm.\nu = √51.68, v = 0, s = 0.03 m; let its (constant) acceleration in the sand be a m s⁻².\n\nv² = u² + 2as\n0 = 51.68 + 2 × 0.03 × a\na = −51.68/0.06 = −861.33… m s⁻²   (a deceleration)\n\nForces on the pebble in the sand: weight 0.04g N downwards and resistance R N upwards (mass 40 g = 0.04 kg).\n\nNewton's second law (downwards positive):\n0.04g − R = 0.04a\n0.04 × 9.8 − R = 0.04 × (−861.33…)\n0.392 − R = −34.453…\nR = 34.845…\n\nThe resistance force of the sand is 34.8 N (3 s.f.).\n\n(Energy check: KE lost + GPE lost = ½ × 0.04 × 51.68 + 0.04 × 9.8 × 0.03 = 1.0336 + 0.01176 = 1.04536 J = R × 0.03, so R = 34.8 N.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(a)": "A = a + bt\n\nWhen t = 0, A = 8: 8 = a + 0, so a = 8\nWhen t = 1, A = 8.8: 8.8 = 8 + b, so b = 0.8\n\na = 8, b = 0.8   (the model is A = 8 + 0.8t)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(b)": "Using A = 8 + 0.8t with A = 15:\n15 = 8 + 0.8t\n0.8t = 7\nt = 8.75\n\nt = 8.75 (days)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(c)": "The linear model adds the same amount, 0.8 cm², every day.\nAn increase of 10% each day means each day's increase is 10% of the current area, so the daily increases get bigger as the culture grows (the growth is exponential, not linear).\n\nFor example, on day 2 the linear model gives 8.8 + 0.8 = 9.6 cm², but a 10% increase gives 8.8 × 1.1 = 9.68 cm², and the gap keeps widening after that. So the linear model will underestimate the area after the first day.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(d)": "A = Pe^(kt)\n\nWhen t = 0, A = 8: 8 = Pe⁰ = P, so P = 8\n\nWhen t = 1, A = 8.8: 8.8 = 8e^k\ne^k = 8.8/8 = 1.1\nk = ln 1.1 = 0.0953…\n\nP = 8, k = ln 1.1 ≈ 0.0953",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(e)": "Using A = 8e^((ln 1.1)t) with A = 15:\n15 = 8e^((ln 1.1)t)\ne^((ln 1.1)t) = 15/8\n(ln 1.1)t = ln(15/8)\nt = ln(15/8)/ln 1.1 = 0.62861…/0.09531… = 6.595…\n\nt = 6.60 (days, 3 s.f.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::10(f)": "As t increases, e^(kt) (with k > 0) increases without limit, so the model predicts that the area of the culture grows without any limit.\nThis is not possible in reality: the culture is limited by the size of its container in the laboratory (and by the nutrients available), so its growth must eventually slow down or stop.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::11(a)": "Let the first three terms be u₁ = 5k − 2, u₂ = 3k − 6, u₃ = k + 2.\nIn a geometric sequence the common ratio is r = u₂/u₁ = u₃/u₂, so\n\n(3k − 6)/(5k − 2) = (k + 2)/(3k − 6)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n(3k − 6)² = (5k − 2)(k + 2)\n9k² − 36k + 36 = 5k² + 10k − 2k − 4\n9k² − 36k + 36 = 5k² + 8k − 4\n4k² − 44k + 40 = 0\n\nDividing by 4:\nk² − 11k + 10 = 0, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::11(b)": "k² − 11k + 10 = 0\n(k − 1)(k − 10) = 0\nk = 1 or k = 10, so the smaller value is k = 1.\n\nWhen k = 1:\nu₁ = 5(1) − 2 = 3\nu₂ = 3(1) − 6 = −3\nu₃ = 1 + 2 = 3\nSo the first term is 3 and the common ratio is r = −3/3 = −1; the terms alternate 3, −3, 3, −3, …\n\nS₂₀ = a(1 − r²⁰)/(1 − r) = 3(1 − (−1)²⁰)/(1 − (−1)) = 3(1 − 1)/2 = 0\n\n(Equivalently, the 20 terms form 10 pairs, each 3 + (−3) = 0.)\n\nSum of the first 20 terms = 0",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::11(c)": "When k = 10:\nu₁ = 5(10) − 2 = 48\nu₂ = 3(10) − 6 = 24\nu₃ = 10 + 2 = 12\nFirst term 48, common ratio r = 24/48 = ½. Since |r| < 1 the sum to infinity exists.\n\nS∞ = 48/(1 − ½) = 48/(½) = 96\n\nSum to infinity = 96",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::12(a)": "Position r m is found by integrating the velocity with respect to t:\nr = ∫ (3i + (6t² − 5)j) dt\n= 3ti + (2t³ − 5t)j + c, where c is a constant vector.\n\nAt t = 0 the particle is at 7j:\n0i + 0j + c = 7j, so c = 7j\n\nr = 3ti + (2t³ − 5t + 7)j",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::12(b)": "From (a): x = 3t and y = 2t³ − 5t + 7.\n\nFrom x = 3t, t = x/3. Substituting:\ny = 2(x/3)³ − 5(x/3) + 7\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the path is y = 2(x/3)³ − 5(x/3) + 7, which simplifies to y = 2x³/27 − 5x/3 + 7.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::13(a)": "y = px + 8x⁻² + q\n\ndy/dx = p − 16x⁻³\n\nAt a stationary point dy/dx = 0, and there is one at x = 2:\np − 16 × 2⁻³ = 0\np − 16/8 = 0\np = 2\n\nThe point (2, 7) lies on the curve:\n7 = 2p + 8/2² + q\n7 = 2p + 2 + q\n2p + q = 5\nWith p = 2: 4 + q = 5, so q = 1\n\np = 2, q = 1",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::13(b)": "From (a), dy/dx = p − 16x⁻³ (p is a constant).\n\nd²y/dx² = 0 − 16 × (−3)x⁻⁴ = 48x⁻⁴\n\nd²y/dx² = 48/x⁴",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::13(c)": "At the stationary point (2, 7), x = 2:\nd²y/dx² = 48 × 2⁻⁴ = 48/16 = 3\n\nSince d²y/dx² = 3 > 0, the stationary point (2, 7) is a minimum point.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::14(a)": "The ball is treated as a point mass: its size and shape are ignored, so it has mass but no dimensions (and so, for example, it cannot spin).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::14(b)": "Take upwards as positive and let the vertical component of the initial velocity be u_y m s⁻¹ (u_y = u sin α).\nVertically: s = −3.6 m (the ball ends 3.6 m below the point of projection), a = −9.8 m s⁻², t = 2.4 s.\n\ns = u_y t + ½at²\n−3.6 = 2.4u_y − 4.9 × 2.4²\n−3.6 = 2.4u_y − 28.224\n2.4u_y = 24.624\nu_y = 10.26\n\nThe vertical component of the initial velocity is 10.26 m s⁻¹ (upwards).",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::14(c)": "Horizontal motion: at the moment of projection the man is directly below the woman, and 2.4 s later he catches the ball. The horizontal velocity of the ball is constant and both start from the same horizontal position, so the ball's horizontal velocity must equal the man's velocity:\nu cos α = 4\n\nVertical component from (b): u sin α = 10.26\n\nValue of u:\nu² = (u cos α)² + (u sin α)² = 4² + 10.26² = 16 + 105.2676 = 121.2676\nu = √121.2676 = 11.012…\nu = 11.01 m s⁻¹ (4 s.f.)\n\nValue of α:\ntan α = (u sin α)/(u cos α) = 10.26/4 = 2.565\nα = tan⁻¹(2.565) = 68.70…\nα = 68.7",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::15": "Centre of the circle:\nx² + 2x + y² − 14y + 25 = 0\n(x + 1)² − 1 + (y − 7)² − 49 + 25 = 0\n(x + 1)² + (y − 7)² = 25\nSo C is (−1, 7) and the radius is 5.\n\nIntersection with the line 7y = x + 25, i.e. x = 7y − 25:\n(7y − 25)² + y² + 2(7y − 25) − 14y + 25 = 0\n49y² − 350y + 625 + y² + 14y − 50 − 14y + 25 = 0\n50y² − 350y + 600 = 0\ny² − 7y + 12 = 0\n(y − 3)(y − 4) = 0\ny = 3 or y = 4\n\ny = 3: x = 7(3) − 25 = −4\ny = 4: x = 7(4) − 25 = 3\nSo A and B are (−4, 3) and (3, 4).\n\nTake A(−4, 3) and B(3, 4).\nGradient of AC = (7 − 3)/(−1 − (−4)) = 4/3\nGradient of BC = (7 − 4)/(−1 − 3) = 3/(−4) = −3/4\n\nProduct of gradients = (4/3) × (−3/4) = −1\nSo AC is perpendicular to BC: angle ACB = 90°.\n\nTherefore triangle ABC is a right-angled triangle (with the right angle at C).\n\n(Alternatively: AB² = (3 − (−4))² + (4 − 3)² = 49 + 1 = 50, and AC² = BC² = 5² = 25, so AC² + BC² = 50 = AB², and by the converse of Pythagoras the triangle is right-angled at C.)",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::16(a)": "Forces on the block: weight mg N downwards, normal reaction R N upwards, the applied force T N at θ above the horizontal, and friction F N opposing the motion (horizontally, backwards).\n\nResolve vertically (the block does not lift, so no vertical acceleration):\nR + T sin θ = mg\nR = mg − T sin θ\n\nThe block is sliding, so friction is limiting:\nF = μR = μ(mg − T sin θ)\n\nNewton's second law horizontally, with acceleration a m s⁻²:\nT cos θ − F = ma\nT cos θ − μ(mg − T sin θ) = ma\nma = T cos θ − μmg + μT sin θ\n\nDividing by m:\na = (T/m)cos θ − μg + (T/m)μ sin θ, as required.",
 "math-ocr-h640-2024-p1::16(b)": "From (a), with T, m, μ and g fixed:\na = (T/m)cos θ − μg + (T/m)μ sin θ\n\nDifferentiate with respect to θ:\nda/dθ = −(T/m)sin θ + (T/m)μ cos θ\n\nThe acceleration is greatest when θ = α, where da/dθ = 0:\n−(T/m)sin α + (T/m)μ cos α = 0\nsin α = μ cos α\nsin α/cos α = μ\ntan α = μ\n\nα = tan⁻¹ μ\n\n(This is a maximum: d²a/dθ² = −(T/m)(cos θ + μ sin θ), which is negative for 0 < θ < π/2.)",
};